A multivalent mRNA monkeypox virus vaccine (BNT166) protects mice and macaques from orthopoxvirus disease.,"In response to the 2022 outbreak of mpox driven by unprecedented human-to-human monkeypox virus (MPXV) transmission, we designed BNT166, aiming to create a highly immunogenic, safe, accessible, and scalable next-generation vaccine against MPXV and related orthopoxviruses. To address the multiple viral forms and increase the breadth of immune response, two candidate multivalent mRNA vaccines were evaluated pre-clinically: a quadrivalent vaccine (BNT166a; encoding the MPXV antigens A35, B6, M1, H3) and a trivalent vaccine (BNT166c; without H3). Both candidates induced robust T cell responses and IgG antibodies in mice, including neutralizing antibodies to both MPXV and vaccinia virus. In challenge studies, BNT166a and BNT166c provided complete protection from vaccinia, clade I, and clade IIb MPXV. Furthermore, immunization with BNT166a was 100% effective at preventing death and at suppressing lesions in a lethal clade I MPXV challenge in cynomolgus macaques. These findings support the clinical evaluation of BNT166, now underway (NCT05988203).","Adam Zuiani, Charles L. Dulberger, Nilushi S De Silva, M. Marquette, Yu-Jung Lu, G. Palowitch, A. Dokic, Ricardo Sanchez-Velazquez, Katja Schlatterer, Sanjay Sarkar, S. Kar, Bhavna Chawla, A. Galeev, Claudia Lindemann, Daniel A. Rothenberg, Huitian Diao, A. Walls, Theresa A. Addona, Federico J. Mensa, Annette B. Vogel, L. Stuart, R. G. van der Most, John R. Srouji, Ö. Türeci, Richard B. Gaynor, U. Şahin, A. Poran",10.1016/j.cell.2024.01.017,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2024.01.017,Cell,125,2024,no,The study population consists of mice and cynomolgus macaques rather than human subjects.,yes,The study reports effectiveness outcomes such as prevention of death and suppression of lesions in animal challenge models.,yes,The paper describes primary pre-clinical research conducted in animal models.,no,"This is explicitly a pre-clinical study involving animals (mice and macaques), not a human study.",no,"The study does not include human participants of any age or status, focusing instead on mice and macaques.",no,"No human vaccination timing is reported, as the study is pre-clinical.",no,"The study evaluates mRNA vaccines (BNT166a/c), which are not vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,This paper describes pre-clinical research into an mRNA vaccine candidate (BNT166) using mice and macaques. It fails the inclusion criteria because it is not a human study and it investigates an mRNA vaccine rather than a vaccinia-based vaccine.,"Population Focus, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""two candidate multivalent mRNA vaccines were evaluated pre-clinically: a quadrivalent vaccine (BNT166a; encoding the MPXV antigens A35, B6, M1, H3) and a trivalent vaccine (BNT166c; without H3). Both candidates induced robust T cell responses and IgG antibodies in mice,""
  - ""immunization with BNT166a was 100% effective at preventing death and at suppressing lesions in a lethal clade I MPXV challenge in cynomolgus macaques.""",,"  - ""immunization with BNT166a was 100% effective at preventing death and at suppressing lesions in a lethal clade I MPXV challenge in cynomolgus macaques.""",,"  - ""two candidate multivalent mRNA vaccines were evaluated pre-clinically: a quadrivalent vaccine (BNT166a; encoding the MPXV antigens A35, B6, M1, H3) and a trivalent vaccine (BNT166c; without H3). Both candidates induced robust T cell responses and IgG antibodies in mice,""
