"COVID-19 and COVID-19 Vaccination in Pregnancy: Epidemiology, Pathophysiology, and Clinical Management","Pregnant individuals represent a high-risk cohort for severe manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection, with epidemiological data indicating elevated rates of maternal morbidity, preterm birth, preeclampsia, and perinatal complications relative to non-infected pregnant populations. Pathophysiological studies highlight altered immune responses and placental ACE2 expression as potential mediators of increased susceptibility and adverse outcomes. Accumulating evidence from large prospective cohorts and meta-analyses demonstrates that administration of COVID-19 vaccines during pregnancy does not increase the incidence of major congenital anomalies, spontaneous abortion, or stillbirth when compared to unvaccinated controls. Moreover, vaccination is associated with significant reductions in the risk of severe maternal COVID-19, hospitalization, and adverse neonatal outcomes. Immunogenicity studies confirm efficient transplacental transfer of SARS-CoV-2 antibodies, conferring passive immunity to the neonate. Collectively, these findings substantiate the safety and efficacy of COVID-19 vaccination in pregnancy, supporting its role as a critical intervention to mitigate maternal and perinatal morbidity during the ongoing pandemic","Michał Jastrzębski, Maja Piątek, Aleksandra Hejnosz, Mateusz Szczerba, Karol Knysak, A. Maj, Krystyna Wilkowska, Wojciech Kaczorowski, M. Michałowski, Maria Sudomir",10.12775/qs.2025.46.66635,https://doi.org/10.12775/qs.2025.46.66635,Quality in Sport,0,2025,yes,"The study focuses on pregnant individuals, who are not healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The paper discusses effects on severe maternal COVID-19 and neonatal outcomes, but these are related to SARS-CoV-2, not mpox.",yes,"The text references evidence from prospective cohorts and meta-analyses, suggesting it is a synthesis or summary of primary research.",yes,The work discusses human clinical outcomes like maternal morbidity and preterm birth.,yes,The study specifically addresses pregnant women and neonates.,yes,The study evaluates vaccination during pregnancy.,no,"The study focuses exclusively on COVID-19 vaccines (targeting SARS-CoV-2), not vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study focuses on COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant populations. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not involve vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines, nor does it report outcomes related to mpox infection.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,,,"  - ""administration of COVID-19 vaccines during pregnancy""",,"  - ""evidence from large prospective cohorts and meta-analyses demonstrates""",,,,"  - ""efficient transplacental transfer of SARS-CoV-2 antibodies,""",,"  - ""administration of COVID-19 vaccines during pregnancy""",,"  - ""administration of COVID-19 vaccines during pregnancy""",
"Global Transmission, Prevention, Control, and Treatment of Mpox Virus in 2025: A Comprehensive Review from Infection Mechanisms to Vaccine Development","The World Health Organization (WHO) declared the mpox (MPX) outbreak a public health emergency of international concern (PHEIC) on 23 July 2022, and 14 August 2024, respectively, underscoring the confirmed and concerning global spread of the disease. A gap exists in our fundamental understanding of the mpox virus (MPXV), despite its genetic relatedness to the variola virus (VARV). This knowledge deficit is evident in the performance of current medical countermeasures; vaccines and antiviral therapies adapted from smallpox programs demonstrate only partial efficacy and are constrained by issues of safety and suboptimal effectiveness against MPXV. In this context, the development of MPX-specific vaccines and antiviral drugs has become a critical priority in the global effort to combat MPX. However, MPXV employs multiple strategies to evade host immune responses, such as producing specific and poxvirus homologous proteins that suppress both innate immunity (including the six principal innate immune signaling pathways and antiviral strategies, notably the interferon [IFN] pathway) and adaptive immunity, thereby complicating vaccine and drug development. Insights from research on vaccinia virus (VACV) and VARV may inform the investigation of MPXV pathogenesis and immune evasion mechanisms. Drawing on relevant scientific literature, this review systematically examines key aspects of MPX infection, pathogenicity, and immune evasion, as well as the coordination between innate and adaptive immune responses. Furthermore, this review elucidates the current application and deployment landscape of the three principal therapeutics and three major vaccines for MPX, aiming to provide a theoretical foundation for future research and development of vaccines and targeted antiviral agents.","Quan Quan, Nan Wu, Ying-Hua Luo, Yanjun Tang, Yan-Zhi Liu, Xi-Chun Huang, Junhua Li, Wan-Xia Ren, Chenghao Jin",10.3390/vaccines13101071,https://doi.org/10.3390/vaccines13101071,Vaccines,4,2025,maybe,"The study is a general review of global transmission and vaccine development, suggesting it is not strictly limited to healthy non-pregnant adults, though it does not explicitly define its population scope.",yes,The review discusses vacuum efficacy and safety outcomes.,no,"The paper is described as a 'comprehensive review'. While it mentions 'systematically examines,' it does not present itself as a formal systematic review or meta-analysis, nor as primary research.",yes,The review focuses on human disease transmission and medical countermeasures.,no,"The abstract does not mention the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it focuses on general infection mechanisms and vaccine development.",no,"There is no evidence in the provided text that the study addresses vaccination timing specifically during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The study specifically evaluates vaccines adapted from smallpox programs and current mpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This paper is a general narrative review focusing on the mechanisms and global landscape of mpox vaccines and therapeutics. It fails to meet the inclusion criteria because it does not focus on or include the required target populations of pregnant women, children, or adolescents, and it is not a primary study or systematic review.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Drawing on relevant scientific literature, this review systematically examines key aspects of MPX infection, pathogenicity, and immune evasion,""
