Immune Response to MVA-BN and Prior Smallpox Vaccination in People With HIV or at Risk for HIV Acquisition,"Abstract Little is known about the serologic response elicited by the Modified Vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic (MVA-BN) vaccination (JYNNEOS) against mpox in elderly individuals with or without HIV. In this study, we measured levels of antibody against orthopoxvirus in selected participants (n = 114) with HIV or at risk for HIV acquisition from the Multicenter AIDS Cohort Study/Women's Interagency HIV Study Combined Cohort Study, who had no prior reported history of mpox. Participants reported MVA-BN vaccination history via questionnaires. The median age was 64 years, 24.6% were female, and 46% were HIV seropositive. Twenty out of 114 participants received at least 1 dose of MVA-BN. We found that MVA-BN induced sustained IgG levels regardless of HIV status, even up to 1 year. Birth before 1973 was correlated with higher IgG. MVA-BN-unvaccinated individuals with HIV had lower IgG than those without HIV. Although limited by small sample size, our study is among the first to assess anti-orthopoxvirus antibodies specifically in a vulnerable, older population and stratified by HIV status.","Yijia Li, Michael B. Townsend, Shanshan Li, Quinn E Testa, Tom Medvec, Elizabeth A Thompson, Frank J Palella, M. Mimiaga, James B Brock, M. Alcaide, Anandi N. Sheth, M. Floris-Moore, Aruna Chandran, Audrey L. French, Phyllis C Tien, Daniel J Merenstein, Michael Augenbraun, Anjali Sharma, Caitlin A Moran, Charles R Rinaldo, B. Macatangay, P. Satheshkumar, Ken S Ho",10.1093/ofid/ofaf497,https://doi.org/10.1093/ofid/ofaf497,Open Forum Infectious Diseases,0,2025,yes,The study focus is not limited to healthy adults as it specifically includes and stratifies by HIV status.,no,"The study reports antibody levels (immunogenicity) rather than clinical effectiveness outcomes like prevention of infection, hospitalization, or death.",yes,This is a primary research study measuring antibodies in a cohort.,yes,The study was conducted on humans.,no,"Participants in the study are described as an 'older population' with a median age of 64 years, and the study does not include children, adolescents, or pregnant women.",no,"While participants received historical smallpox vaccines in the past, the MVA-BN vaccinations relevant to the current mpox response were administered to this elderly cohort as adults.",yes,The study examines both the MVA-BN (JYNNEOS) vaccine and historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"The study assesses immunogenicity of MVA-BN in an elderly cohort (median age 64) of people with HIV. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not include children, adolescents, or pregnant women and lacks the specified clinical effectiveness outcomes.","Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""measured levels of antibody against orthopoxvirus in selected participants (n = 114) with HIV or at risk for HIV acquisition""
