Mpox neutralising antibodies at 6 months from mpox infection or MVA-BN vaccination: a comparative analysis.,"Over 80 000 cases of mpox (formerly known as monkeypox) infection have been confirmed since May 2022.1Thornhill JP Barkati S Walmsley S et al.Monkeypox Virus Infection in Humans across 16 Countries - April-June 2022.N Engl J Med. 2022; 387: 679-691Crossref PubMed Scopus (945) Google Scholar, 2Girometti N Byrne R Bracchi M et al.Demographic and clinical characteristics of confirmed human monkeypox virus cases in individuals attending a sexual health centre in London, UK: an observational analysis.Lancet Infect Dis. 2022; 22: 1321-1328Summary Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (256) Google Scholar Infection elicits both cell-mediated and humoral immunity.3Agrati C Cossarizza A Mazzotta V et al.Immunological signature in human cases of monkeypox infection in 2022 outbreak: an observational study.Lancet Infect Dis. 2023; 23: 320-330Summary Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (36) Google Scholar Modified Vaccinia Ankara Bavarian Nordic (MVA-BN) vaccine has been used during the outbreak for infection control, showing an effectiveness of 66–78%.4Deputy NP Deckert J Chard AN et al.Vaccine Effectiveness of JYNNEOS against Mpox Disease in the United States.N Engl J Med. 2023; 388: 2434-2443Crossref PubMed Scopus (26) Google Scholar, 5Bertran M Andrews N Davison C et al.Effectiveness of one dose of MVA-BN smallpox vaccine against mpox in England using the case-coverage method: an observational study.Lancet Infect Dis. 2023; 23: 828-835Summary Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (29) Google Scholar Second episodes of mpox and cases of breakthrough infections among fully vaccinated individuals have been reported.6Zeggagh J Ferraris O Salmona M Tarantola A Molina JM Delaugerre C Second clinical episode of hMPX virus in a man having sex with men.Lancet. 2023; 4011610Summary Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (7) Google Scholar, 7Raccagni AR Canetti D Mileto D et al.Two individuals with potential monkeypox virus reinfection.Lancet Infect Dis. 2023; 23: 522-524Summary Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (14) Google Scholar, 8Thy M Peiffer-Smadja N Mailhe M et al.Breakthrough Infections after Postexposure Vaccination against Mpox.N Engl J Med. 2022; 387: 2477-2479Crossref PubMed Scopus (25) Google Scholar This opens questions regarding the durability and strength of protection following infection and vaccination. The aim of this study is to compare the neutralising antibody response at 6 months from mpox infection or MVA-BN vaccination. Individuals with mpox infection (May–Nov, 2022) or who received MVA-BN vaccination (August–November, 2022) at the Infectious Diseases Unit of San Raffaele Scientific Institute, Milan, Italy, were included. The plaque reduction neutralisation test (PRNT) was used to establish mpox neutralising antibody titres at 6 months from infection or first vaccination (baseline). Titres were grouped as low (L-mpox ≤1/10), medium (M-mpox 1/20–1/80), or high (H-mpox ≥1/160). Multivariable multinomial logistic regression and classification tree analysis were applied (appendix p 4). Overall, 180 people were included: 95 with mpox infection and 85 vaccinated with MVA-BN. All were males and 178 (99%) were men who have sex with men. The median age was 37·2 years (IQR 32·2–41·8) and previous smallpox vaccination was reported by 18 (10%) people; 65 (36%) were people living with HIV and eight (4%) were immunodepressed for other causes. A comparison of the baseline characteristics of mpox infected and MVA-BN-vaccinated individuals is given in the appendix (p 7). Overall, samples for PRNT testing were obtained following 6·91 months (IQR 6·41–7·37) from baseline. Neutralising mpox antibody titres are shown in the appendix (p 8). Individuals who received MVA-BN vaccination had significantly lower concentrations of mpox neutralising antibodies compared with those with mpox infection (p<0·0001). The most common value of antibodies among those with infection was 1/80 (29 [31%]) and all had detectable neutralising antibodies. Among people who received MVA-BN vaccination the most common value was 1/10 (29 [34%]) and ten (12%) had no detectable antibodies (<1:10). At multivariable regression, vaccinated people, rather than those with mpox infection, had a significantly higher risk of having L-mpox antibody titres (adjusted odds ratio [aOR] 319·84; 95% CI 43·44–2354·85; p<0·0001) and M-mpox titres (aOR 9·02; 95% CI 2·44–33·31; p<0·0001). People living with HIV had a higher risk of having L-mpox titres (aOR 3·99; 95% CI 1·01–15·75; p=0·050) but not M-mpox (aOR 1·63, 95% CI 0·68–3·89; p=0·27), compared with those not living with HIV. Older people had a lower risk of having L-mpox titres (aOR 0·92; 95% CI 0·85–1·00; p=0·060) or M-mpox (aOR 0·94; 95% CI 0·89–0·99; p=0·020). People with or without immunodepression had a similar risk of L-mpox titres (aOR 0·40; 95% CI=0·02–6·66; p=0·52) or M-mpox (aOR 0·59, 95% CI 0·10–3·30; p=0·55). The univariable analysis is detailed in the appendix (p 4). A classification tree analysis is presented in the figure. The highest probability of H-mpox antibodies was among people with mpox infection aged at least 42 years (31 [17%] of 180, L-mpox 0, M-mpox 11 [35%] of 31, H-mpox 20 [65%] of 31); the highest of L-mpox antibodies was among vaccinated people living with HIV (15 [8%] of 180, L-mpox ten [67%] of 15, M-mpox four [27%] of 15, H-mpox one [7%] of 15). In conclusion, people vaccinated with MVA-BN were found to develop frequently low or medium mpox neutralising antibodies when compared with infected individuals, as reported in 2023.9Zaeck LM Lamers MM Verstrepen BE et al.Low levels of monkeypox virus-neutralizing antibodies after MVA-BN vaccination in healthy individuals.Nat Med. 2023; 29: 270-278Crossref PubMed Scopus (68) Google Scholar, 10Cohn H Bloom N Cai GY et al.Mpox vaccine and infection-driven human immune signatures: an immunological analysis of an observational study.Lancet Infect Dis. 2023; (published online July 17.)https://doi.org/10.1016/S1473-3099(23)00352-3Summary Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (5) Google Scholar One in ten vaccinated individuals showed no detectable neutralising antibodies at 6 months, whereas every person with mpox infection developed antibodies. People living with HIV, when compared with people without HIV, developed more frequently low or medium neutralising antibody responses, which might be related to premature cellular senescence. Study limitations are noted in the appendix (p 9). Although we identified people with low or no detectable antibody titres, MVA-BN vaccination has also been shown in the real world setting to be a powerful tool for mpox prevention. All in all, neutralising antibody responses at 6 months are much lower in people vaccinated with MVA-BN compared with those with previous mpox infection. NM and ARR visited the individuals and contributed to writing the article. SN designed the study and contributed to writing the report. MB executed the laboratory assays. DM and MRG coordinated the virological activities and contributed to the reviewing of the manuscript. SD did the statistical analyses and contributed to the writing of the manuscript. RL collected the clinical data. CC visited the individuals and contributed to the review of the manuscript. AC and CUF coordinated clinical activities and contributed to the review of the manuscript. All authors have read and agreed to the published version of the manuscript. All authors had direct access and verified the underlying data reported in the manuscript; had full access to all the data in the study and accept responsibility to submit for publication; and have seen and approved of the final text. We declare no competing interests. ARR and SN directly accessed and verified the underlying data reported in the manuscript and all authors potentially had access to all data. SN and DM were responsible for the decision to submit the manuscript. NM and ARR contributed equally. SN and DM contributed equally. Individuals included in the study signed written informed consent to include case details and personal information in the published version of the manuscript in all formats. AC has received personal fees for advisory boards, speaker panels, and educational materials from Gilead Sciences, ViiV Healthcare, Janssen-Cilag, Merck Sharp & Dohme, and Theratechnlogies. SN has received personal fees for advisory boards, speaker panels, and educational materials from Gilead Sciences, ViiV Healthcare, Janssen-Cilag, Merck Sharp & Dohme. ARR, SD, RL, CC, DM, MRG, MB, and NM declare no competing interests. All data collected in the study are available on reasonable request, after approval of a proposal, to the corresponding author ([email protected]), including deidentified single participant data. The data can be requested from publication onwards. The data are not publicly available for ethical reasons. Download .pdf (.71 MB) Help with pdf files Supplementary appendix","Nicolò Moschetta, A. Raccagni, Micol Bianchi, Sara Diotallevi, R. Lolatto, C. Candela, Caterina Uberti Foppa, M. Gismondo, A. Castagna, S. Nozza, D. Mileto",10.1016/s1473-3099(23)00571-6,https://doi.org/10.1016/s1473-3099(23)00571-6,Lancet. Infectious Diseases (Print),11,2023,yes,"The study included 65 people living with HIV and 8 who were otherwise immunodepressed, thus it was not exclusively limited to healthy adults.",no,"The study reports neutralising antibody titres (immunogenicity) using the PRNT assay rather than clinical effectiveness outcomes such as the prevention of mpox infection, hospitalization, or death.",yes,The study is a primary research study (observational comparative analysis).,yes,The research was conducted on human subjects.,no,"The study participants were all adult males, with a median age of 37.2 years, and did not include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"The MVA-BN vaccine was administered in 2022 to adult participants. Although 10% reported historical smallpox vaccination, the focus and primary vaccination analyzed (MVA-BN) occurred during adulthood.",yes,The study investigates MVA-BN and historical vaccinia-based vaccines.,Exclude,The study evaluates neutralising antibody titres in a cohort of adult males (median age 37.2) following mpox infection or MVA-BN vaccination. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not include target populations such as children or pregnant women and reports immunogenicity rather than clinical effectiveness or safety outcomes.,"Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""65 (36%) were people living with HIV and eight (4%) were immunodepressed for other causes.""",,"  - ""The plaque reduction neutralisation test (PRNT) was used to establish mpox neutralising antibody titres at 6 months from infection or first vaccination (baseline).""",,,,"  - ""Overall, 180 people were included: 95 with mpox infection and 85 vaccinated with MVA-BN. All were males and 178 (99%) were men who have sex with men.""",,"  - ""Overall, 180 people were included: 95 with mpox infection and 85 vaccinated with MVA-BN. All were males and 178 (99%) were men who have sex with men.""
