Hospitalization records during monkeypox outbreaks: a scientific report from the available documents,"Monkeypox virus (MPXV) is a rare zoonotic virus belonging to the Orthopoxvirus family. As of September 2022, over 43 600 con-ﬁ rmed monkeypox (MPX) cases have been reported in more than 90 different countries beyond African territories. The ﬁ rst documented human deaths of MPX in recent outbreaks outside of Africa have been reported in Brazil, India, Ecuador, Spain, and Cuba. Human MPX is a self-limiting neglected tropical disease with two clinical stages of illness: ﬁ rst stage, fever, myalgia, chills, sore throat; second stage, lymphadenopathy associated with skin eruption after fever [1] . According to available documents, we found that nine of 34 laboratory-con ﬁ rmed cases of MPX during the 2003 outbreak in the USA required hospitalization; two cases of MPX required ICU admission due to encephalitis as well as retropharyngeal abscess [2] . Furthermore, complications, for example, bacterial superinfection and keratitis with corneal ulceration were also reported from hospitalized patients suffering MPX [3] . Unfortunately, MPXV outbreak is spreading in several countries around the world, while there is limited information on the epidemiological changes and clinical aspects of its outbreak. Herein, we discuss hospitalization due to MPXV 2022 outbreak using previous observations beyond the African continent.","M. Karbalaei, M. Keikha",10.1097/JS9.0000000000000110,https://doi.org/10.1097/JS9.0000000000000110,International Journal of Surgery,0,2023,yes,"The study focuses on patients with MPX infection requiring hospitalization and ICU care, rather than exclusively healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study reports on disease complications like encephalitis, bacterial superinfection, and keratitis, as well as hospitalization and death. However, it does not report on vaccine-related safety or effectiveness outcomes.",maybe,The paper describes itself as a 'scientific report' based on 'available documents'. It appears to be a secondary review or commentary on previously published outbreak data.,yes,The study discusses human cases and outbreaks.,no,"The text does not specify that the participants include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it speaks generally about laboratory-confirmed cases in the USA and other countries.",no,The study does not mention vaccination or the timing of vaccine administration; it focuses on clinical stages and hospitalization due to infection.,no,The study does not involve or mention any vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines. It is entirely focused on the clinical presentation and hospitalization records of MPX cases.,Exclude,This paper is a scientific report/review of hospitalization records and clinical complications during monkeypox outbreaks. It is excluded because it does not involve vaccination and does not specifically focus on the target populations of interest like pregnant women or children.,"Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""complications, for example, bacterial superinfection and keratitis with corneal ulceration were also reported from hospitalized patients""",,"  - ""human deaths of MPX in recent outbreaks outside of Africa have been reported in Brazil, India, Ecuador, Spain, and Cuba.""
