Optimal deployment of limited vaccine supplies to combat mpox,"Mpox outbreaks in Central Africa have been declared a public health emergency of international concern by the World Health Organization. Fortunately, real-world effectiveness studies of the MVA-BN vaccine indicate that it has an effectiveness of 74% after one dose, and 82% after two doses against mpox. However, given the very limited supply of vaccines in Central Africa, there remain questions around the optimal deployment of limited MVA-BN doses. In this study, we consider whether more mpox cases might be averted by following the traditional two-dose vaccine regimen (4 week dosing interval), or by giving a single dose of MVA-BN to as many individuals as possible. We find that the optimal strategy depends on both, (i) the degree to which a subpopulation might be at higher risk of mpox, or severe mpox, infections, and (ii) how long ago the first dose was administered to the most at-risk subpopulation.","Matthew T. Berry, Raina Macintyre, D. Cromer, Adam Hacker, Miles P. Davenport, D. Khoury",10.1101/2024.11.03.24316551,https://doi.org/10.1101/2024.11.03.24316551,medRxiv,1,2024,maybe,"The study mentions at-risk subpopulations requiring vaccine deployment optimization, which typically extends beyond healthy non-pregnant adults in the context of Clade I mpox outbreaks.",yes,The study evaluates the effectiveness outcome of mpox cases averted by different vaccination strategies.,no,"The study appears to be a mathematical modeling or decision analysis study rather than a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or evidence synthesis.",yes,The study is a theoretical or modeling study of human vaccine deployment rather than an animal or laboratory-only study.,no,"The abstract does not specify the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it refers general 'at-risk' clusters and the general population of Central Africa.",no,"There is no information provided regarding vaccination during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,"The study involves the MVA-BN vaccine, which is a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This study is a mathematical modeling analysis investigating the optimal deployment of MVA-BN vaccine doses rather than a primary clinical study or systematic review. Furthermore, it does not explicitly focus on or report results for pregnant women, neonates, or pediatric populations.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""the degree to which a subpopulation might be at higher risk of mpox, or severe mpox, infections,""",,"  - ""In this study, we consider whether more mpox cases might be averted by following the traditional two-dose vaccine regimen (4 week dosing interval), or by giving a single dose of MVA-BN to as many individuals as possible.""",,"  - ""In this study, we consider whether more mpox cases might be averted by following the traditional two-dose vaccine regimen (4 week dosing interval), or by giving a single dose of MVA-BN to as many individuals as possible.""",,"  - ""In this study, we consider whether more mpox cases might be averted by following the traditional two-dose vaccine regimen (4 week dosing interval), or by giving a single dose of MVA-BN to as many individuals as possible.""",,"  - ""In this study, we consider whether more mpox cases might be averted by following the traditional two-dose vaccine regimen (4 week dosing interval), or by giving a single dose of MVA-BN to as many individuals as possible.""
