Evaluation of pre-exposure vaccine effectiveness against mpox during the 2022–2023 mpox outbreak in the Madrid region (Spain): a test-negative design,"Abstract Introduction An appropriate vaccination approach is critical to control the current outbreak of mpox but there is little research providing information on its effectiveness, particularly under circumstances of limited vaccine availability. Methods Pre-exposure vaccination campaign in the Madrid region, with the modified vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic was indicated in the risk groups from 18 July 2022. To evaluate the vaccine effectiveness (VE) of a dose of third-generation smallpox vaccine against mpox in the context of pre-exposure prophylaxis, a population-based indirect cohort study (Broome method) also known as test-negative design (TND) was conducted in the Madrid region (6 751 251 inhabitants). Logistic regression was used to obtain the adjusted VE in the TND with its time–response relationship and for the sensitivity analysis the conditional logistic regression for matched case–control groups. Results By epidemiological surveillance, 1690 suspected episodes of mpox were detected of which 799 were cases and 891 controls. The overall adjusted effectiveness of the pre-exposure vaccination against mpox considering an induction period of 14 days, was 86.4% (95% CI 62.2% to 95.1%). VE increases, with a statistically significant time–response effect, being greater than 77.4% with a 95% confidence level from week 7. The VE at eighth week of vaccination was 99% (95% CI 81.7% to 99.9%). Conclusions The effectiveness of single-dose pre-exposure vaccination against mpox seems very high. Therefore, it appears as a reliable measure to minimise the spread of mpox. The progressive increase in the effectiveness could justify the delay in the administration of the second dose in situations of shortage of vaccines or prioritisation in the vaccination of the maximum number of subjects at risk. Further studies evaluating the long-term effectiveness of the full vaccination would be appropriate.","J. F. Barbas del Buey, J. Íñigo Martínez, M. Alonso García, A. Aragón Peña, Laura Montero Morales, Araceli Arce Arnáez",10.1136/bmjph-2023-000620,https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjph-2023-000620,BMJ public health,4,2024,maybe,"The study does not specify an age cutoff for adult risk groups, nor does it explicitly state it is limited to healthy non-pregnant adults. However, in the context of the 2022-2023 Madrid outbreak, it is likely the majority were adults.",yes,The study specifically evaluates vaccine effectiveness against mpox infection.,yes,The study is a population-based indirect cohort study (test-negative design).,yes,The study is based on human epidemiological surveillance and suspected clinical episodes.,no,"The abstract does not mention including pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it refers generally to 'risk groups'. There is no evidence these specific sub-populations were analyzed.",no,"Because the study does not explicitly confirm the presence of the target populations (pregnant women or children), it cannot be confirmed they received the vaccine during the specified periods.",yes,"The study uses the modified vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic, which is a third-generation vaccinia-based smallpox/mpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This study evaluated the effectiveness of the MVA-BN vaccine against mpox in Madrid using a test-negative design. While it provides important effectiveness data, it focuses on general risk groups and does not include or provide sub-analysis for the required populations of pregnant women, neonates, or children.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""indicated in the risk groups from 18 July 2022.""",,"  - ""evaluate the vaccine effectiveness (VE) of a dose of third-generation smallpox vaccine against mpox""",,"  - ""a population-based indirect cohort study (Broome method) also known as test-negative design (TND)""",,"  - ""1690 suspected episodes of mpox were detected""",,"  - ""indicated in the risk groups from 18 July 2022.""",,"  - ""indicated in the risk groups from 18 July 2022.""",,"  - ""modified vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic""
  - ""third-generation smallpox vaccine""",
Orthopoxvirus vaccines and vaccination,"Immunization procedures against Variola virus, from the historical perspective most often first credited to Edward Jenner in the late 18th century, helped finally to eradicate smallpox from the world. Since its eradication, the study of this disease and its pathology has been given little attention; however, with the emergence of Monkeypox virus into the human population and the potential use of smallpox as a bioterrorist weapon, the need for an option to vaccinate the world’s population is once again a reality. The vaccines used during the eradication program were live, attenuated Vaccinia virus preparations of varying virulence that caused a significant number of adverse reactions in naïve subjects. Currently, immunosuppressed individuals, persons with certain skin diseases, and people with cardiovascular complications are contraindicated against receiving this type of vaccine. A new vaccine is needed. Until now, the only known correlate of immunity to the smallpox vaccine conveying protection has been the development of a scar at the site of vaccination. Characterizing the protective immune response established upon vaccination with Dryvax®, at both the innate and adaptive levels, would greatly enhance our understanding of the human immune response to the vaccine, and thus generate information for the production and evaluation of new and safer third- and fourth-generation vaccines.","L. Handley, J. Mackey, R. Buller, C. Bellone",10.1007/978-3-7643-7557-7_16,https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-7643-7557-7_16,,5,2007,maybe,The paper is a general review of orthopoxvirus vaccines and doesn't specify a study population beyond commenting on individuals with contraindications such as immunosuppressed patients. It does not clearly mention a specific study focus on non-healthy adults or any of the target groups.,no,"The paper mentions adverse reactions associated with historical vaccinia preparations and discusses the need for characterizing the protective immune response, but it does not report data-driven effectiveness or safety outcomes from a structured study.",no,"The text appears to be a narrative review, commentary, or book chapter overviewing the history and status of vaccines. It is not a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,The paper discusses human vaccination history and human immune responses.,no,"The text contains no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as study participants. It mentions immunosuppressed individuals and those with skin or cardiovascular diseases as contraindicated groups.",no,"There is no mention of participants receiving vaccines during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence in the context of a study.",yes,"The paper specifically discusses historical smallpox vaccines, live attenuated Vaccinia virus preparations, and Dryvax.",Exclude,This paper is a narrative overview or book chapter regarding the history and future of orthopoxvirus vaccination and does not involve primary research or a systematic review. It fails to include the required target populations of pregnant women or children and does not report clinical study outcomes.,"Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Currently, immunosuppressed individuals, persons with certain skin diseases, and people with cardiovascular complications are contraindicated against receiving this type of vaccine.""",,"  - ""The vaccines used during the eradication program were live, attenuated Vaccinia virus preparations of varying virulence that caused a significant number of adverse reactions in naïve subjects.""
