Sexual and Gender Minorities' Vaccine Uptake and Behavioral Change in Response to the Mpox Outbreak in the United States: August 2022 Through November 2022.,"BACKGROUND
In the summer of 2022, the United States faced a nationwide outbreak of mpox, with cases concentrated in sexual and gender minorities who have sex with men. Understanding rates of mpox vaccine uptake and concomitant behavioral change is essential to guide the implementation of targeted public health responses to the potential reemergence of mpox.
METHODS
Between August 2022 and November 2022, 8551 individuals recruited via geosocial networking apps completed a brief survey that assessed mpox vaccine uptake, intentions to get a mpox vaccine, and behavioral change.
RESULTS
In August, 17.4% of participants reported having received at least 1 dose of the mpox vaccine. By November, this prevalence estimate was 35.0%. Black participants were significantly less likely to be vaccinated, and vaccine hesitancy increased among Black participants over time. Among those who had not yet received a vaccination, the intention to get vaccinated decreased over time. We observed trends that coincided with the evolving outbreak, such as decreased worry about mpox and less engagement in risk reduction behaviors over time.
CONCLUSIONS
Despite a 2-fold increase in mpox vaccine uptake between August 2022 and November 2022 in sexual and gender minorities who have sex with men, disparities in vaccine uptake were observed among Black participants. Findings will guide the implementation of public health responses to the potential reemergence of mpox and other viral infectious diseases (eg, meningitis) with a specific focus on optimizing vaccine uptake in Black communities.","Christian Grov, Fatima Zohra, C. Mirzayi, Matthew Stief, Alexa B. D’Angelo, Michelle H Dearolf, D. Westmoreland, Pedro B. Carneiro, Denis Nash, Adam W. Carrico",10.1093/cid/ciad793,https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciad793,Clinical Infectious Diseases,11,2024,maybe,"The study focuses on sexual and gender minorities recruited via geosocial networking apps. While it does not explicitly state all are healthy, it focuses on general vaccine uptake in an adult population (GBMSM) and does not specify focus on non-healthy groups outside this demographic.",no,"The study only reports vaccine uptake, intentions, and behavioral changes. It does not report safety (adverse events) or clinical effectiveness (prevention of infection, hospitalization, or death).",yes,"The study is a cross-sectional survey/cohort study using serial samples, which is a primary research study.",yes,The study involves human participants recruited via apps.,no,"The study focuses on adult sexual and gender minorities (GBMSM) recruited via dating apps; there is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Participants received vaccines as adults during the 2022 outbreak; there is no evidence of vaccination during pregnancy, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The study involves the mpox vaccine (likely JYNNEOS) used during the 2022 US outbreak.,Exclude,This study is a survey-based evaluation of vaccine uptake and behavioral changes among sexual and gender minorities. It fails several mandatory criteria as it focuses on an adult population rather than children or pregnant women and does not report clinical safety or effectiveness outcomes.,"Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""8551 individuals recruited via geosocial networking apps completed a brief survey that assessed mpox vaccine uptake, intentions to get a mpox vaccine, and behavioral change.""
