"COMPARATIVE EFFECTIVENESS OF mRNA, INACTIVATED, AND VIRAL-VECTOR VACCINES IN PREVENTING INFECTIOUS DISEASES","The review compared three key vaccine platforms, mRNA, inactivated and viral vectors, to control infectious disease using COVID-19 as a prototype, but also with further insights into polio, RSV and Ebola. After 18 months of follow-up with subgroup analyses and summary safety profiles of 18,000 participants in a multicenter prospective cohort, vaccine efficacy was highest with mRNA vaccines: 71% against infection, 84% against severe disease and 88% against death, versus inactivated and viral vector vaccines. Inactivated vaccines, with less effective protection against infection, still offered great and important protection against hospitalization and death, particularly in younger people, and retained the safety and operation benefits of safety, simplicity and coverage. Viral vector vaccines offered partial protection but were most appealing in an outbreak by virtue of single dose administration with limitations owing to isolated cases of thrombosis. Age-stratified analyses revealed reduced efficacy in older participants, particularly inactivated vaccines, which reinforced the importance of booster dosing and mixed schedules. Bad events in all the platforms were generally mild or moderate, and serious adverse events like myocarditis or thrombosis remained extremely rare. These observations indicate that despite the high bar of efficacy posed by mRNA technology, diversification of platforms is essential to enable global vaccine equity, response to outbreaks, and pandemic preparedness.","Arfeen Muhammad Siddiq, Sara Munir, Hira Naeem, Farwa Yousuf, Muskan Imran Ali, Namra Alvi, Adeeba Shakeel, Zohaib Ahmed",10.62019/pt0qa993,https://doi.org/10.62019/pt0qa993,Journal of Medical &amp; Health Sciences Review,1,2025,yes,"The study includes subgroups such as 'younger people' and 'older participants', indicating it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on effectiveness (infection, severe disease, death) and safety (myocarditis, thrombosis).",yes,The study is described as a multicenter prospective cohort and comparison review.,yes,"The study involved 18,000 human participants.",maybe,"While the study mentions 'younger people', it does not explicitly state the inclusion of children/adolescents (<18) or pregnant women.",maybe,It is unclear if the 'younger people' mentioned received the vaccine during childhood or adolescence specifically as defined by the criteria.,no,"The study focuses on COVID-19, polio, RSV, and Ebola vaccines; it does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"The paper focuses on vaccine platforms for COVID-19, polio, RSV, and Ebola, failing the mandatory requirement to study mpox or smallpox vaccines. Although it reports on safety and effectiveness in a large human cohort, the interventions are irrelevant to the target disease.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""particularly in younger people,""
