Safety and Immunogenicity of Varicella-Zoster Vaccination in Pediatric Heart Transplant Recipients.,"BACKGROUND
Pediatric heart transplant recipients are at increased risk of morbidity and mortality from vaccine-preventable infections due to long-term immunosuppression. Although live vaccines have traditionally been contraindicated post-transplant, emerging evidence supports their administration in selected solid organ recipients. This study evaluated the safety and immunogenicity of varicella-zoster virus (VZV) vaccination in pediatric heart transplant recipients.
METHODS
We retrospectively reviewed all pediatric heart transplant recipients who received a post-transplant VZV vaccination between November 2019 and April 2025 at our center. Baseline demographics, immune parameters, immunosuppressive regimens, serologic responses, and adverse events were analyzed in relation to the 2019 International Pediatric Transplant Association (IPTA) consensus guidelines.
RESULTS
Forty patients received at least one dose of VZV vaccination post-transplantation, of which 30 patients completed a two-dose vaccination series. Seroconversion was achieved in 97% of patients with eligible follow-up serology (n=36) and persisted in 80% at a median follow-up of 20 months. Adverse events occurred in 37.5% of patients, predominantly mild localized self-limiting rashes. One patient developed mild varicella, and one disseminated vaccine-strain VZV infection following re-transplantation shortly after vaccination. Notably, 53% were receiving mycophenolate mofetil, 25% had tacrolimus target levels of ≥ 8ug/mL, 55% had CD4 counts below age-specific reference, and 20% had detectable EBV loads.
CONCLUSIONS
Live-attenuated VZV vaccination appears safe and immunogenic in selected pediatric heart transplant recipients. These findings support cautious expansion of current vaccination criteria to include heart transplant recipients, those on MMF therapy, and patients with lower CD4 counts, with careful consideration of immunosuppressive stability at the time of vaccination.","Dyan Zhang, L. Rosenthal, M. Seifert-Hansen, L. Scavuzzo, A. Marrato, A. Dipchand",10.1016/j.healun.2026.03.010,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.healun.2026.03.010,The Journal of Heart and Lung Transplantation,0,2026,yes,"The study focuses on pediatric heart transplant recipients, not healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports safety outcomes including adverse events and complications like disseminated vaccine-strain infection.,yes,The study is a retrospective review of patient records.,yes,The study involves human pediatric patients.,yes,The study specifically looks at children and adolescents undergoing heart transplants.,yes,Participants received the vaccination during their childhood (post-transplant).,no,"The study evaluates the varicella-zoster virus (VZV) vaccine, which is not a vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the safety and immunogenicity of the varicella-zoster virus (VZV) vaccine in pediatric patients. While it includes a target population of children, it falls outside the scope of this review because it does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Pediatric heart transplant recipients""",,"  - ""Adverse events occurred in 37.5% of patients,""",,"  - ""retrospectively reviewed""",,"  - ""Pediatric heart transplant recipients""",,"  - ""Pediatric heart transplant recipients""",,"  - ""Pediatric heart transplant recipients""
  - ""retrospectively reviewed""",,"  - ""varicella-zoster virus (VZV) vaccination""",
Current Status of Vaccine Development for Monkeypox Virus.,"Monkeypox virus (MPXV) of poxviridae family causes a zoonotic disease called monkeypox (Mpox). MPXV cases have a fatality ratio ranging from 0 to 11% globally and have been more prevalent in children. There are three generations of smallpox vaccines that protect against MPXV. First and second generation of the vaccinia virus (VACV) vaccine protects MPXV. However, various adverse side effects were associated with the first and second generations of vaccines. In contrast, the Modified Vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic (MVA-BN) replication-incompetent vaccine shows fewer adverse effects and a significant amount of neutralizing antibodies in mammalian cells. A third-generation Modified Vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic (MVA-BN) was approved to prevent Mpox in 2019. Recently, MVA-BN-based Imvanex, Imvamune, and JYNNEOS vaccines have also been administered against MPXV. Globally, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared a global health emergency in May 2022 due to increased MPXV cases. Various computational studies have also designed a multi-epitope-based vaccine against the MPXV. In the multi-epitope-based vaccine, different epitopes like B-cell, Cytotoxic T Lymphocyte (CTL), CD8+, and CD4+ epitopes were derived from MPXV proteins. Further, these epitopes were linked with the help of various linkers to design a multi-epitope vaccine against MPXV. In summary, we have provided an overview of the current status of the vaccine against MPXV.
© 2024. The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG.","Amber Rastogi, Manoj Kumar",10.1007/978-3-031-57165-7_18,https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-57165-7_18,Advances in Experimental Medicine and Biology,5,2024,maybe,"While the study mentions children broadly, it's a general review and doesn't specify which ages are covered in its summary of vaccine development.",yes,The paper discusses adverse side effects and vaccine effectiveness for prevention.,no,"The paper describes itself as an ""overview of the current status"". This is a narrative review/overview rather than a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",no,"The paper is a review article and includes discussions on computational studies, rather than being a clinical study involving human participants.",no,"The paper mentions that Mpox is prevalent in children, but it does not include children, pregnant women, or neonates as study participants.",no,"As there are no study participants described, there is no specific vaccination timing recorded for the target groups.",yes,The study discusses historical three generations of smallpox vaccines and Modified Vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic (MVA-BN).,Exclude,"The paper is a narrative overview of monkeypox vaccine development and doesn't involve primary research or a systematic review. It lacks a specific study population and does not include data on pregnant women, children, or neonates as participants, though it recognizes children as a demographic affected by the virus.","Study Design, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""have been more prevalent in children.""",,"  - ""various adverse side effects were associated""
