  - ""Modified Vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic (MVA-BN) replication-incompetent vaccine shows fewer adverse effects""",
Clinical manifestations and outcomes of human mpox infection from 1970 to 2023: A systematic literature review,"Mpox is an important emerging infectious disease spreading rapidly with a changing geographical, clinical, epidemiological, virological, and clinical manifestations. The identification of clade I virus, the variant associated with severe disease and higher risk of death outside Africa in August 2024 is alarming. It is vital to document its changing epidemiology, clinical manifestations, and outcome to better understand how to prevent and treat the disease. We aimed to describe the clinical features and disease outcome of patients with mpox from 1970 to 2023. We performed a systematic search of peer-reviewed publications and grey literature using PubMed, MEDLINE, CINAHL, and EMBASE, published between 1973 and 2023. Thirty-seven articles met the inclusion criteria. We pooled data from 19 studies with at least 10 cases to enhance the appreciation of the review findings. A clear distinction was observed regarding the age, gender, sexual orientation, and HIV status distribution of mpox cases before and after May 2022. From May 2022 onwards, a high prevalence of mpox, almost exclusively, was observed among individuals identifying as men who have sex with men (MSM) 20 – 40 years with high-risk sexual behaviour and/or HIV immunosupression. No change in the common clinical manifestations – mucocutaneous rash, fever, lymphadenopathy, headache, and generalized malaise, typically during prodrome. However, certain symptoms such as myalgia/arthralgia, oropharyngitis, proctitis, and depression/anxiety were typically reported from May 2022 onwards. We could not establish from the review if the observed symptomatology change was real or resulting from an observation bias. Notably, a clear change in symptomatology was observed among individuals with immunosuppression who do not necessarily experience the prodromal stage and present with significantly more and larger mucocutaneous lesions that sometimes coalesced to form ulcers. Our pooled data report a CFR of 8.3 % before May 2022 and 1.2 % from May 2022 onwards. We show that mental health in patients with mpox has not received the attention required. There is a need for a strategic and strong approach to revamp mpox services to improve clinical suspicion and treatment, protect individuals most at risk including healthcare workers in high risk areas.","C. Abejegah, K. Obohwemu, Mselenge Mdegela",,,,3,,yes,"The study includes individuals with HIV immunosuppression, meaning it is not exclusively limited to healthy adults.",no,The study reports on outcomes of the infection itself (such as CFR and clinical symptoms) rather than the safety or effectiveness of vaccines.,yes,The study is a systematic literature review.,yes,The study focuses on human mpox infection.,maybe,"While the study covers a broad datespan (1970-2023) where children were historically affected, the abstract does not explicitly confirm the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, or children in its findings, focusing instead on adult MSM populations post-2022.",no,The study does not report on vaccination or its timing; it focuses on infection manifestations and outcomes.,no,There is no mention of vaccines in the abstract; the research is about the clinical features of the infection.,Exclude,"This is a systematic review focused on the clinical manifestations and outcomes of human mpox infection from 1970 to 2023. It does not evaluate vaccines, vaccine safety, or vaccine effectiveness, thus failing multiple mandatory criteria related to interventions and outcomes.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""HIV immunosupression.""",,"  - ""CFR of 8.3 % before May 2022 and 1.2 % from May 2022 onwards.""
  - ""clinical features and disease outcome of patients with mpox""",,,,,,"  - ""individuals identifying as men who have sex with men (MSM) 20 – 40 years""",,"  - ""clinical features and disease outcome of patients with mpox""",,"  - ""clinical features and disease outcome of patients with mpox""",
Smallpox vaccination: a conundrum of risks and outcomes.,"Smallpox is a disfiguring and often fatal illness. As the name suggests, people with smallpox become covered with pocks, blister-like pustules that contain billions of highly contagious virus particles (Fig. 1) [2]. Pustules can also erupt in the mouth, the throat, the trachea, and the esophagus. When the victim coughs or sneezes, the virus is dispersed in potentially lethal droplets. In many victims, the virus invades the eyes, causing blindness. Death ensues in up to 60% of victims a few days after the appearance of the rash. In 5–10% of the victims, the lesions become hemorrhagic. This form of smallpox called ‘black pox’ or ‘sledgehammer pox’ invariably results in fatal bleeding in 5–7 days. Another presentation of the disease, characterized by severe toxemia and flat confluent lesions that do not progress to the pustular stage, carries more than 95% mortality. If the sufferer survives, the skin lesions dry up, form crusts, and slough off, leaving deep scars. The survivor is protected for life against a second infection. When smallpox was endemic it was predominantly a childhood illness, less lethal among children than adults, conferring protection for survivors in later life.","H. Milgrom, E. Palmer, D. Leung",10.1097/01.ALL.0000168782.28841.71,https://doi.org/10.1097/01.ALL.0000168782.28841.71,Current Opinion in Allergy and Clinical Immunology,2,2005,yes,"The text discusses smallpox in general, including children.",maybe,The abstract discusses smallpox mortality and pathology but does not obviously report vaccine effectiveness or safety outcomes from a primary study.,no,The snippet appears to be from a review article (Current Opinion in Allergy and Clinical Immunology) rather than primary research or a systematic review.,yes,The text discusses human smallpox infection.,maybe,"Children are mentioned in the context of historical endemic smallpox, though it's unclear if this is a primary study on them.",maybe,There is no information in the text regarding specific participants being vaccinated during childhood or adolescence; it discusses childhood illness more generally.,maybe,"The title mentions smallpox vaccination, but the abstract text provided focuses on pathology rather than a specific vaccine type used in a study.",Exclude,"This article appears to be a review or commentary on smallpox pathology and risks rather than primary research or a systematic review reporting vaccine outcomes. Because it is not a primary or evidence synthesis study, it fails the mandatory study design criterion.",Study Design,Study Design,0.1,"  - ""When smallpox was endemic it was predominantly a childhood illness, less lethal among children than adults, conferring protection for survivors in later life.""",,,,,,"  - ""When smallpox was endemic it was predominantly a childhood illness, less lethal among children than adults, conferring protection for survivors in later life.""",,"  - ""When smallpox was endemic it was predominantly a childhood illness, less lethal among children than adults, conferring protection for survivors in later life.""",,"  - ""When smallpox was endemic it was predominantly a childhood illness, less lethal among children than adults, conferring protection for survivors in later life.""",,,
Comparison of Adverse Events in Pregnant Persons Receiving COVID-19 and Influenza Vaccines: A Disproportionality Analysis Using Combined Data from US VAERS and EudraVigilance Spontaneous Report Databases,"Although multiple post-licensure studies demonstrated that coronavirus disease-2019 (COVID-19) vaccines are safe for use during pregnancy, none of them have identified a signal of disproportionate reporting. To assess the disproportionality in reported adverse events among pregnant persons receiving COVID-19 vaccination compared with influenza vaccines in spontaneous reporting databases. Individual case safety reports (ICSRs) with COVID-19 vaccines (Pfizer, AstraZeneca, Moderna and Johnson & Johnson) and influenza vaccines were retrieved from spontaneous reporting databases in the Vaccine Adverse Event Report System (VAERS) and the EudraVigilance (EV) system between 1 December 2020 and 31 October 2023. Both datasets were combined through a common data model. Pregnancy-associated ICSRs were identified using adaptations to the European Medicines Agency (EMA) algorithm based on age groups and key medical conditions. We compared the disproportionate reporting of High-Level Terms (HLT) after COVID-19 vaccines of interest (e.g. mRNA vaccine) with another COVID-19 viral vector-based/protein subunit and influenza vaccines during pregnancy. The proportional reporting ratio (PRR) with 95% confidence intervals (CIs) was calculated using a combined dataset. PRR met the predefined criteria (PRR ≥ 2, lower 95% CI ≥ 2 and N ≥ 3), confirming a potential signal of disproportionate reporting (SDR). A total of 22,383 pregnancy-related ICSRs were included. Five associations met the PRR threshold: inborn errors of steroid synthesis 35.1 (95% CI 7.8–158.3); non-site-specific embolism and thrombosis 15.9 (95% CI 3.1–82.2); general signs and symptoms not elsewhere classified (NEC) 11.17 (95% CI 3.3–38.1); peripheral nervous system disorders congenital NEC 4.2 (95% CI 2.3–7.7); and vascular anomalies congenital NEC 3.7 (95% CI 2.4–5.6), all associated with viral vector-based/protein subunit. Despite this analysis, several statistical disproportionalities were identified during pregnancy; the case-by-case analysis shows that embolism and thrombosis require prioritized investigation through proper causal inference studies.","Leonardo Roque-Pereira, M. M. Sisay, C. Ogar, C. E. Durán, E. V. van Puijenbroek, Daniel Weibel, Katia M C Verhamme, M. Sturkenboom",10.1007/s40264-025-01561-6,https://doi.org/10.1007/s40264-025-01561-6,Drug Safety,2,2025,yes,The study includes pregnant persons.,yes,The study reports adverse events (safety outcomes).,yes,"The study is a disproportionality analysis of spontaneous reporting databases, which is a primary research design (observational/case series-like).",yes,The study uses data from human reporting databases (VAERS and EudraVigilance) related to pregnant persons.,yes,The study specifically includes pregnant persons.,yes,The participants received the vaccines during pregnancy.,no,The study only involves COVID-19 and influenza vaccines. It does not mention vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This study focuses on adverse events in pregnant individuals who received COVID-19 or influenza vaccines. It does not investigate the required vaccine types, specifically vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""To assess the disproportionality in reported adverse events among pregnant persons receiving COVID-19 vaccination compared with influenza vaccines""
  - ""A total of 22,383 pregnancy-related ICSRs were included.""",,"  - ""To assess the disproportionality in reported adverse events among pregnant persons receiving COVID-19 vaccination compared with influenza vaccines""",,"  - ""To assess the disproportionality in reported adverse events among pregnant persons receiving COVID-19 vaccination compared with influenza vaccines""",,"  - ""To assess the disproportionality in reported adverse events among pregnant persons receiving COVID-19 vaccination compared with influenza vaccines""
  - ""A total of 22,383 pregnancy-related ICSRs were included.""",,"  - ""To assess the disproportionality in reported adverse events among pregnant persons receiving COVID-19 vaccination compared with influenza vaccines""
