  - ""ICSRs) with COVID-19 vaccines (Pfizer, AstraZeneca, Moderna and Johnson & Johnson) and influenza vaccines were retrieved""",
Maternal and Pediatric Use of Vaccines for Mpox: A Living Systematic Review and Meta-analysis of Safety and Effectiveness.,"Vaccinia-based vaccines appear generally safe in pregnancy and children. However, evidence on the safety and effectiveness of third-generation mpox vaccines is still scarce. High-quality prospective studies and strengthened pharmacovigilance are urgently needed to inform policy and clinical decision making.","Agustín Ciapponi, J. Ballivian, M. Berrueta, J. M. Sambade, Noelia Castellana, Ariel Bardach, Martín Brizuela, Julieta Caravario, D. Comandé, Esteban Couto, A. Mazzoni, E. Parker, Florencia Salva, K. Stegelmann, Xu Xiong, Andy Stergachis, Flor M. Munoz, Pierre Buekens",10.1007/s40264-026-01657-7,https://doi.org/10.1007/s40264-026-01657-7,Drug Safety,1,2026,yes,"The study focus is not limited to healthy non-pregnant adults, as it specifically investigates pregnancy and pediatric use.",yes,The study explicitly aims to report safety and effectiveness outcomes.,yes,The title confirms this is a systematic review and meta-analysis.,yes,"The study focuses on the maternal and pediatric use of vaccines, which refers to human subjects.",yes,The research includes pregnant women ('maternal') and children ('pediatric').,yes,The study reviews vaccination during pregnancy and childhood.,yes,The study specifically mentions vaccinia-based vaccines and third-generation mpox vaccines.,Include,This is a systematic review and meta-analysis focused on the safety and effectiveness of vaccinia-based mpox vaccines in pregnant women and children. It satisfies all inclusion criteria by targeting the specified populations and vaccines.,,,5,"  - ""Vaccinia-based vaccines appear generally safe in pregnancy and children.""",,"  - ""evidence on the safety and effectiveness of third-generation mpox vaccines is still scarce.""",,,,,,"  - ""Vaccinia-based vaccines appear generally safe in pregnancy and children.""",,"  - ""Vaccinia-based vaccines appear generally safe in pregnancy and children.""",,"  - ""Vaccinia-based vaccines appear generally safe in pregnancy and children.""
  - ""evidence on the safety and effectiveness of third-generation mpox vaccines is still scarce.""",
Safety of mRNA COVID-19 vaccines during pregnancy,"Vaccination with the Pfizer-BioNTech COVID-19 vaccine [tozinameran; Comirnaty; BNT162b2] or the Moderna COVID-19 vaccine [elasomeran; SpikeVax; mRNA-1273] appears to be safe during pregnancy, according to findings of a study published in The Lancet Regional Health – Europe. This nationwide study conducted in Switzerland investigated early adverse events (AEs) and perinatal outcomes after one or more doses of BNT162b2 or mRNA-1273 in 1012 pregnant women enrolled in the Swiss COVI-PREG vaccine registry between March and December 2021. Questionnaires on AEs and outcomes were sent to gynaecologists before or at the time of vaccination, one month after vaccination, and the end of pregnancy (up to 5 days after delivery). Most (81%) women reported injection-site pain after vaccination. The most frequently reported systemic AEs were fatigue, headache and muscle pain. Severe early AEs were reported in 0.4% of women, and included deep vein thrombosis with pulmonary embolism, premature rupture of membranes, herpes zoster, and hospitalisation due to fever. Overall, 0.9% of women vaccinated before 14 weeks experienced early spontaneous abortion, and 0.4% of those vaccinated before 20 weeks experienced late spontaneous abortion. Preterm delivery was reported in 6.4% of women vaccinated before 37 weeks, but no stillbirths were reported. Overall, 4.7% of neonates required admission to the neonatal intensive care unit, but no neonatal deaths were reported. ""This prospective study has provided further evidence that mRNA vaccination against SARS-CoV-2 during pregnancy seems safe in terms of early adverse events, pregnancy, and neonatal outcomes,"" concluded the investigators.","S. Ellington, C. Olson",10.1007/s40278-022-16844-1,https://doi.org/10.1007/s40278-022-16844-1,Reactions weekly,28,2022,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women.,yes,The study reports on early adverse events and perinatal outcomes.,yes,The paper describes a nationwide prospective study utilizing a vaccine registry.,yes,The study involves human participants (pregnant women).,yes,The participants are pregnant women.,yes,Participants received the vaccine during pregnancy.,no,"The vaccines studied are Pfizer-BioNTech (BNT162b2) and Moderna (mRNA-1273) COVID-19 vaccines, which are mRNA vaccines against SARS-CoV-2, not vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the safety of mRNA COVID-19 vaccines (Pfizer and Moderna) in 1012 pregnant women. While it involves pregnant women and reports safety outcomes, it is focused on SARS-CoV-2 vaccination rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""1012 pregnant women""",,"  - ""investigated early adverse events (AEs) and perinatal outcomes""",,"  - ""investigated early adverse events (AEs) and perinatal outcomes after one or more doses of BNT162b2 or mRNA-1273 in 1012 pregnant women enrolled in the Swiss COVI-PREG vaccine registry""
