A Case of Neonatal Monkeypox Treated With Oral Tecovirimat.,"Since the Monkeypox virus outbreak erupted in May 2022, infection has been reported across all ages. Few cases exist in the medical literature about Monkeypox infection in neonates, and little is known about its clinical manifestations, disease course, or side effects of available antiviral agents in this age group. In this report, we describe the case of a 10-day-old neonate from the southern United States who presented with fevers and generalized papulopustular rash. She was treated empirically as a febrile neonate but mpox infection was suspected early because of the characteristic exanthem and its similarity to her mother's rash that she had developed a few days before the patient's presentation. Oral tecovirimat was initiated on the third day of admission and mpox was later confirmed by polymerase chain reaction analysis. The patient tolerated oral tecovirimat well and experienced a favorable outcome without lasting effects of infection.","Sandra Castejon-Ramirez, John K. Pennington, Hayley Beene, Nicholas Hysmith, S. Ost",10.1542/peds.2023-061198,https://doi.org/10.1542/peds.2023-061198,Pediatrics,7,2023,yes,"The study focuses on a 10-day-old neonate, which means it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports on the safety (toleration) and effectiveness (favorable outcome) of treatment following mpox infection.,yes,The study is a case report of a single patient.,yes,The study involves a human patient.,yes,The participant is a 10-day-old neonate.,no,The study describes a neonate treated for an active infection with an antiviral agent; there is no report of vaccination occurring during pregnancy or the neonatal period.,no,The study involves the antiviral tecovirimat rather than a vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccine.,Exclude,"This case report describes the clinical course and antiviral treatment of a 10-day-old neonate with mpox. Although it involves a target population, it is excluded because it evaluates the antiviral medicine tecovirimat rather than a vaccine.","Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""we describe the case of a 10-day-old neonate from the southern United States who presented with fevers and generalized papulopustular rash.""",,"  - ""The patient tolerated oral tecovirimat well and experienced a favorable outcome without lasting effects of infection.""",,"  - ""we describe the case of a 10-day-old neonate from the southern United States who presented with fevers and generalized papulopustular rash.""",,"  - ""we describe the case of a 10-day-old neonate from the southern United States who presented with fevers and generalized papulopustular rash.""",,"  - ""we describe the case of a 10-day-old neonate from the southern United States who presented with fevers and generalized papulopustular rash.""",,"  - ""Oral tecovirimat was initiated on the third day of admission and mpox was later confirmed by polymerase chain reaction analysis.""
