"Demographics and Health Beliefs of Black Gay, Bisexual, and Other Sexual Minority Men Receiving a Mpox Vaccination in the United States","An outbreak of mpox virus (MPV) among humans in the United States (U.S.) was described in May 2022. This outbreak disproportionately affects Black and Hispanic sexual minority men (SMM) and these groups have lower rates of vaccination compared to Whites. Between July and August 2022, a partnership between a nonprofit and two local health departments successfully designed and implemented a community intervention to administer MPV vaccines to Black SMM in the Washington D.C. metropolitan area. We administered a quantitative survey to 178 respondents across four vaccinations clinics. We found that study participants had high socioeconomic status, high levels of anticipated MPV stigma, and were relatively skeptic about MPV. We demonstrated how a partnership between an urban nonprofit and government agency can facilitate quick and effective dissemination of a community intervention in a relatively low cost manner.","A. Ogunbajo, Alexa Euceda, Jamilia Smith, Raven Ekundayo, Justise Wattree, Mitchell Brooks, D. Hickson",10.1007/s11524-022-00712-9,https://doi.org/10.1007/s11524-022-00712-9,Journal of urban health,13,2023,no,"The study is exclusively focused on Black sexual minority men, which usually refers to adults. No inclusion of non-adult or pregnant populations is mentioned.",no,"The study reports on demographics, stigma, and health beliefs rather than clinical safety or effectiveness outcomes.",yes,The study is a primary research effort using a quantitative survey design.,yes,The study involves human participants responding to a survey.,no,"The study identifies its population specifically as 'men', failing to include the required populations of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Participants are described as sexual minority men receiving vaccines during an adult outbreak response, not during childhood or pregnancy.",yes,The study involves the administration of MPV (mpox virus) vaccines.,Exclude,This study focuses on the health beliefs and demographics of adult Black sexual minority men. It does not include the target populations of children or pregnant women and does not report clinical safety or effectiveness outcomes.,"Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""outbreak disproportionately affects Black and Hispanic sexual minority men (SMM)""",,"  - ""study participants had high socioeconomic status, high levels of anticipated MPV stigma, and were relatively skeptic about MPV.""",,"  - ""administered a quantitative survey to 178 respondents""",,"  - ""administered a quantitative survey to 178 respondents""",,"  - ""outbreak disproportionately affects Black and Hispanic sexual minority men (SMM)""
