Smallpox and Pregnancy: From Eradicated Disease to Bioterrorist Threat,"Health care personnel must be prepared for the threat of bioterrorism. Our objective is to educate primary care providers, obstetricians in particular, in the prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of smallpox. Smallpox poses a particularly serious threat because of its high case-fatality rate in unvaccinated populations (no one younger than 25 years has been vaccinated, and older persons have little remaining residual immunity). Routine nonemergency smallpox vaccination is restricted to laboratory staff working with smallpox-related viruses. Under these circumstances, contraindications to vaccination are pregnancy, immunodeficiency, exfoliative skin conditions (eczema), and allergy to vaccine components. In case of an intentional release of the smallpox virus, those directly exposed and their close contacts must be vaccinated and isolated. Under such emergency circumstances, pregnant women exposed to the variola virus should be vaccinated because of the lethality of the disease during pregnancy. Currently, there is a limited supply of vaccine available.","V. Suarez, G. Hankins",10.1016/S0029-7844(02)02048-3,https://doi.org/10.1016/S0029-7844(02)02048-3,Obstetrics and Gynecology,35,2002,yes,The focus of the paper is specifically on the implications of smallpox and vaccination for pregnant women.,no,The paper does not report specific results or outcomes from a study; it provides general medical guidance and recommendations.,no,"The study is an educational/review article aimed at clinicians, not a primary research study or systematic review.",yes,The paper discusses human health and clinical practices for pregnant women.,no,"While the target population (pregnant women) is the subject of the discussion, the paper does not actually include them as participants in a study because it is a professional commentary.",no,The study does not involve participants receiving the vaccine; it only discusses the indications for when they should potentially receive it in an emergency.,yes,The study discusses smallpox vaccination and its components.,Exclude,This article is a clinical educational review meant to instruct obstetricians on smallpox management rather than a research study. It does not include study participants or report effectiveness/safety data from a specific trial or cohort.,"Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Our objective is to educate primary care providers, obstetricians in particular, in the prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of smallpox.""
