"Mpox in immunocompromised patients: with more data, more questions","Mpox (previously monkeypox) emerged while the world was still grappling with the COVID-19 pandemic and was declared a public health emergency by theWorld Health Organization in July of 2022. Despite challenges related to testing, vaccination, and access to treatment that persist in low-income countries,1 the features of mpox in immunocompetent individuals are well-characterized. In a report of 528 cases of mpox infection, 98% of patients were gay or bisexual men, and sexual contact was the suspected route of transmission in 95% of cases.2 Nopatients died, but 13% required hospitalization primarily for pain management; a few patients were hospitalized for ocular lesions, reduced oral intake, myocarditis, or acute kidney injury. Most patients presented with a rash, with the majority exhibiting ≤10 lesions; 11% had more widespread lesions. Although 41% of patients were infected with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), over 95% were virally suppressed on antiretroviral therapy (ART). Additionally, no concurrent immunocompromising conditions were reported, suggesting that this is a relatively immunocompetent cohort. Thus, not surprisingly, < 5% of individuals requiredmpox-specific antiviral therapy, highlighting the self-limited nature of mpox in immunocompetent individuals. By contrast, the natural history of mpox in immunocompromised individuals is much more grim, as demonstrated by a study from the Centers for Disease Control (CDC) focusing on 57 patients hospitalized with severe mpox.3 Most individuals were infected with HIV, but less than 10% were receiving ART; over 90% had CD4 counts < 200 cells/mm3, with around 70% exhibiting CD4 counts < 50 cells/mm3. Approximately 5% of patients had a solid organ transplant (SOT), and 3.5% had a hematologic malignancy. All patients had severe dermatologic manifestations, and most had severe mucosal lesions. Furthermore, dissemination of mpox to other organs was common, with 21%, 21%, and 7% experiencing pulmonary, ocular, or central nervous system involvement, respectively. Most patients received mpox-directed antivirals, includingmonotherapy or combination therapywith tecovirimat (93%), vaccinia immune globulin intravenous (VIGIV) (51%), or cidofovir (23%). Case details of two patients suggest that mpox lesions may progress despite antivirals. Most ominously, 30% of patients received ICU-level care, and 21% died; death was attributable to mpox in 5/12 cases. These findings provide a sobering view of the status and severity of mpox in immunocompromised patients who, unlike immunocompetent individuals, are unable to generate a neutralizing IgG and poxvirus-specific Th1 response to infection,4,5 placing them at risk for dissemination and death. In the current issue of Transplant Infectious Diseases, two case reports provide additional insights on the natural history of mpox disease in immunocompromised patients, one receiving cytotoxic chemotherapy for Hodgkin’s lymphoma one who had undergone a kidney transplant in the context of well-controlled HIV. Both patients were men who have sex with men and had engaged in anal intercourse without barrier protection in the weeks preceding the onset of lesions. The patient with lymphoma, who was undergoing chemotherapy when he was infected, developed large, disfiguring, and necrotic facial lesions that progressed despite the rapid initiation of tecovirimat. He also received 28 days of tecovirimat (longer than the standard14days)with cidofovir andVIGIV, given theprogressionof his lesions. The patient with a kidney transplant developed mpox despite childhood vaccination against smallpox, suggesting waning immunity. He exhibited over 50 lesions and received 14 days of tecovirimat, which was initiated 12 days after symptom onset. Although his lesions began to improve 2 days after therapy, at least 1 lesion developed while he was on tecovirimat and remained positive for orthopoxvirus after completion of therapy; resistance testing is underway. Although both patients survived, the photographs demonstrate that the patient with lymphoma had a much more severe course than the patient with a kidney transplant. This observation is likely a reflection of the lower net state of immunosuppression of the kidney transplant recipient, who had been transplanted 5 years prior to his illness, had no recent rejection episodes, and was receiving low-dose tacrolimus and azathioprine. Both these cases highlight the differences in the manifestations of this infection in patients with immunodeficiency, as well as the challenges in managing these patients in what is a data vacuum. Although over 80,000 mpox cases have been diagnosed worldwide,6 with over 29,000 in the United States to date,7 data from randomized controlled trials (RCTs) of antiviral therapy are lacking. Additionally, the mpox pandemic appears to be under control, with only a handful of new cases emerging in the fall of 2022. Nonetheless, it is expected that new cases will continue to be diagnosed, particularly in low-income countries,1 where access to testing, treatment, and prevention is limited, and where much of the mpox-infected population has advancedHIV infection. Thus, there is an ongoing need to generate additional data to optimize the management of immunocompromised patients with mpox. First, all cases of mpox infection in immunocompromised patients should continue to be reported so that we can better understand mpox’s capacity to cause dissemination and death in these patients. Second, all providers caring for immunocompromised patients with mpox infection in settings where resources are available should attempt to enroll these patients into biorepositories, to better","R. Sigler, G. Haidar",10.1111/tid.14023,https://doi.org/10.1111/tid.14023,Transplant Infectious Disease,3,2023,yes,"The study focuses on immunocompromised individuals, including patients with HIV, solid organ transplants, and malignancies. These populations are not exclusively healthy adults.",yes,"The article discusses the natural history, severity, outcomes (dissemination, ICU care, death), and breakthrough infection despite historical vaccination.",yes,This is an editorial or commentary summarizing existing data (like a CDC study) and highlighting two specific case reports appearing in the same journal issue.,yes,The data discussed refers to human patients infected with mpox.,no,"The paper does not explicitly mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as current participants. The patients discussed are men who have sex with men and those with childhood vaccinations (identifying as adults now). No pediatric or obstetric subjects are reported.",yes,"One patient received the vaccine during childhood, which meets the timing criterion.",yes,The study mentions 'childhood vaccination against smallpox'.,Exclude,"This editorial discusses the severity of mpox in immunocompromised adults, citing a CDC study and two case reports of adult men. While it mentions one patient who received a smallpox vaccine in childhood, it does not include current pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as required by the target population criteria.",Target Population,Target Population,0.1,"  - ""the natural history of mpox in immunocompromised individuals is much more grim, as demonstrated by a study from the Centers for Disease Control (CDC) focusing on 57 patients hospitalized with severe mpox.3""
