Poxvirus vaccines: the evolution of an 18th-century vaccine to the 21st century.,"The unprecedented global spread of monkeypox (mpox) has reinvigorated the pursuit of next-generation poxvirus vaccines. Given the success of the mRNA-lipid nanoparticle (LNP) vaccine platform to vaccinate against SARS-CoV-2, candidate poxvirus mRNA vaccines, as recently reported by Zuiani et al. and Mucker et al., demonstrate success in nonhuman primate (NHP) models and provide insights into the basis of the protective immune response.","Yuhong Xiao, Stuart N. Isaacs",10.1016/j.tips.2024.10.015,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tips.2024.10.015,TIPS - Trends in Pharmacological Sciences,2,2024,no,The study does not involve human subjects; it focuses on nonhuman primate models.,yes,The study mentions 'success' and 'protective immune response' in animal models regarding mpox vaccines.,no,This is a commentary or review article (Trends in Pharmacological Sciences) summarizing research by other authors (Zuiani et al. and Mucker et al.) and is not a primary human research study or a systematic review.,no,The study specifically evaluates vaccines in 'nonhuman primate (NHP) models' rather than humans.,no,"The study does not include any human participants, let alone pregnant women, neonates, or children.",no,The study focuses on NHP models and does not involve human vaccination during the specified life stages.,maybe,"The study discusses 'poxvirus mRNA vaccines' and the evolution of historical vaccines, though it focuses on next-generation candidates.",Exclude,"This article is a commentary or trend review focusing on next-generation mRNA poxvirus vaccines using data from nonhuman primate (NHP) models. It fails the inclusion criteria because it is not a human study (C4) and does not include the target populations of pregnant women, children, or neonates (C5).","Population Focus, Study Design, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""candidate poxvirus mRNA vaccines, as recently reported by Zuiani et al. and Mucker et al., demonstrate success in nonhuman primate (NHP) models""",,"  - ""candidate poxvirus mRNA vaccines, as recently reported by Zuiani et al. and Mucker et al., demonstrate success in nonhuman primate (NHP) models""
  - ""provide insights into the basis of the protective immune response.""",,"  - ""candidate poxvirus mRNA vaccines, as recently reported by Zuiani et al. and Mucker et al., demonstrate success in nonhuman primate (NHP) models""",,"  - ""candidate poxvirus mRNA vaccines, as recently reported by Zuiani et al. and Mucker et al., demonstrate success in nonhuman primate (NHP) models""",,"  - ""candidate poxvirus mRNA vaccines, as recently reported by Zuiani et al. and Mucker et al., demonstrate success in nonhuman primate (NHP) models""",,"  - ""candidate poxvirus mRNA vaccines, as recently reported by Zuiani et al. and Mucker et al., demonstrate success in nonhuman primate (NHP) models""",,"  - ""The unprecedented global spread of monkeypox (mpox) has reinvigorated the pursuit of next-generation poxvirus vaccines.""
