"Smallpox vaccine: contraindications, administration, and adverse reactions.","Since the terrorist attacks of September 11, 2001, and the anthrax exposures in the following weeks, concern that smallpox could be used as a biologic weapon has increased. Public health departments and the U.S. military have begun the process of vaccinating soldiers and civilian first-responders. Smallpox vaccination carries some serious risks: approximately one in 1 million primary vaccinees and one in 4 million revaccinees will die from adverse vaccine reactions. The most serious side effects of smallpox vaccine include progressive vaccinia, postvaccinial central nervous system disease, and eczema vaccinatum. Some of these reactions can be treated with vaccinia immune globulin or cidofovir. Proper patient screening and site care are essential. Family physicians must learn to screen potential vaccinees for contraindications (e.g., immunodeficiency, immunosuppression, certain skin and eye diseases, pregnancy, lactation, allergy to the vaccine or its components, moderate or severe intercurrent illness) and to treat vaccine-associated adverse reactions.","D. Maurer, Brian C. Harrington, M. Lane",,,American Family Physician,25,2003,yes,"The paper discusses contraindications for various populations including those with immunodeficiency, skin diseases, and pregnancy. It also mentions vaccination of soldiers and first-responders.",yes,The paper reports on serious side effects like progressive vaccinia and death rates from adverse reactions.,no,"The paper appears to be a clinical review or guidance article rather than a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,The paper discusses human vaccination programs and clinical contraindications in humans.,no,"While pregnancy is mentioned as a contraindication, the paper does not include pregnant women or children as participants in a study; it is a general review of administration and risks.",no,"There is no evidence of a study population receiving vaccines during the specified periods (pregnancy, childhood); the text only lists these as contraindications for vaccination.",yes,The paper specifically focuses on the smallpox vaccine.,Exclude,"This paper is a clinical review of smallpox vaccine contraindications and adverse reactions rather than a primary research study or systematic review. It identifies pregnancy and other conditions as contraindications but does not include these groups as study participants, failing the target population and study design criteria.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""Family physicians must learn to screen potential vaccinees for contraindications (e.g., immunodeficiency, immunosuppression, certain skin and eye diseases, pregnancy, lactation, allergy to the vaccine or its components, moderate or severe intercurrent illness)""
  - ""Public health departments and the U.S. military have begun the process of vaccinating soldiers and civilian first-responders.""",,"  - ""The most serious side effects of smallpox vaccine include progressive vaccinia, postvaccinial central nervous system disease, and eczema vaccinatum.""
