1048Pregnancy Registry for Varicella-Zoster Virus-Containing Vaccines: 18-Year Summary of Pregnancy Outcomes,"18-Year Summary of Pregnancy Outcomes English Willis, MD; Ann Marko, BSN, RN; Mona Marin, MD; Sonja Rasmussen, MD; Stephanie R. Bialek, MD, MPH; Ann Redfield, MSN, RN; Maureen Mcgee, BSN, RN; Adrian Dana, MD; Clinical Safety and Risk Management, Merck Research Laboratories, Merck and Co., Inc., North Wales, PA; Division of Viral Diseases, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, Atlanta, GA; Influenza Division, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, Altanta, GA","E. Willis, Ann Marko, M. Marin, S. Rasmussen, S. Bialek, Ann R. Redfield, Maureen Mcgee, Adrian Dana",10.1093/OFID/OFU052.756,https://doi.org/10.1093/OFID/OFU052.756,Open Forum Infectious Diseases,3,2014,yes,"The study focuses on pregnancy outcomes, which involves pregnant women rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"While the study reports safety outcomes (pregnancy outcomes), these are for the Varicella-Zoster vaccine, not mpox or smallpox vaccines.",yes,"The study is a summary of a pregnancy registry, which constitutes a primary research study/case series.",yes,"The study involves a pregnancy registry and clinical safety management by medical professionals, indicating it is a human study.",yes,The study specifically includes pregnant women as participants in a registry.,yes,"The study tracks pregnancy outcomes after vaccination, implying vaccination occurred during or immediately preceding pregnancy.",no,"The study evaluates Varicella-Zoster Virus-containing vaccines, which are not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study is a summary of pregnancy outcomes from a registry of patients who received Varicella-Zoster vaccines. It is excluded because it does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines, failing both the vaccine type and relevant outcome criteria for this review.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
"Reduced Odds of Mpox-Associated Hospitalization Among Persons Who Received JYNNEOS Vaccine — California, May 2022–May 2023","The effectiveness of 1 dose of JYNNEOS vaccine (modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine, Bavarian Nordic) against hospitalization for mpox (caused by Monkeypox virus), has been demonstrated; however, the impact of 2 doses on hospitalization risk, especially among persons infected with HIV, who are at higher risk for severe disease, is an important factor in evaluating vaccine effectiveness against mpox disease severity and Monkeypox virus infection. Surveillance data collected by the California Department of Public Health were used to evaluate whether receipt of 2 doses of JYNNEOS vaccine reduced the odds of hospitalization among persons with mpox. The odds of hospitalization among persons with mpox who had received 1 or 2 JYNNEOS doses were 0.27 (95% CI = 0.08-0.65) and 0.20 (95% CI = 0.01-0.90), respectively, compared with unvaccinated mpox patients. In mpox patients with HIV infection, the odds of hospitalization among those who had received 1 JYNNEOS vaccine dose was 0.28 (95% CI = 0.05-0.91) times that of those who were unvaccinated. No mpox-associated hospitalizations were identified among persons infected with HIV who had received 2 JYNNEOS vaccine doses. To optimize durable immunity, all eligible persons at risk for mpox, especially those infected with HIV, should complete the 2-dose JYNNEOS series.","Samuel Schildhauer, Kayla Saadeh, Josh Vance, J. Quint, Tarek Salih, Timothy Lo, Awa Keinde, Edwin Chojolan, Esther E Gotlieb, Marisa Ramos, Eric Chapman, Philip Peters, Jessica Watson, Kelly A Johnson, Eric C Tang, Kathleen Jacobson, Robert E Snyder",10.15585/mmwr.mm7236a4,https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm7236a4,MMWR. Morbidity and mortality weekly report,24,2023,yes,The study is not limited to healthy adults as it specifically includes and analyzes mpox patients with HIV infection.,yes,The study reports on mpox effectiveness in terms of reducing the odds of hospitalization.,yes,The study is a primary research analysis of state-level surveillance data.,yes,The study uses data from human patients in California.,no,"There is no evidence in the text that the study includes pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; the population is described generally as 'persons with mpox' and specifically notes HIV-infected individuals.",no,"There is no evidence that participants were vaccinated during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The study involves the JYNNEOS (modified vaccinia Ankara) vaccine.,Exclude,"This study evaluates the effectiveness of the JYNNEOS vaccine against mpox hospitalizations using California surveillance data. While it includes populations with HIV infection, it does not explicitly include or report data for pregnant women, children, or adolescents as required by the screening criteria.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""In mpox patients with HIV infection, the odds of hospitalization among those who had received 1 JYNNEOS vaccine dose was 0.28 (95% CI = 0.05-0.91) times that of those who were unvaccinated.""
