Safety of mRNA Vaccines Administered During the First Twenty-Four Months of the International COVID-19 Vaccination Program,"Two mRNA-based COVID vaccines were granted emergency use authorization in both children and adults in 2021 after a drastically accelerated approval process that allowed the manufacturers to essentially fast-track their vaccines. We analyzed data from regulatory surveillance and self-reporting systems like Defense Medical Epidemiology Database(DMED); EudraVigilance; Eurostat; German health insurers; the Israeli Minister of Health; the Natural Cycles app; Public Health Scotland; the United Kingdom’s (UK) Office for National Statistics (ONS); UK’s yellow card reporting system; the vaccine adverse event reporting system (VAERS); and v-safe entries to look for long-term adverse events of these vaccines that cannot be captured in the expedited clinical safety trials. In this observational study, we analyzed non-foreign VAERS data for selected symptoms reported after COVID, influenza, and pertussis vaccines and calculated rates per vaccine dose administered as well as proportion of total reports received. We also looked at DMED data and compared annual incidence rates of selected conditions by taking into account the total number of military personnel for each study year. Our data show, among other trends, increases in adverse event reports if we compare COVID vaccines to influenza and pertussis vaccines and statistically significant higher numbers of hospital encounters in military personnel, as well as increases in incidences of thromboembolic conditions, such as menstrual abnormalities, myocarditis, and cerebrovascular events after the implementation of COVID vaccine mandates, compared to the five years prior. We verified these observations using data from EudraVigilance; the UK’s ONS; German health providers; and Eurostat. Our meta-analysis of both national and international vaccine adverse events emphasizes the importance of re-evaluating public health policies that promote universal mass vaccination and multiple boosters for all demographic groups. In combination with anecdotal evidence, limitations of the safety trials, and the decreased lethality of new strains our research demonstrates that the cost (both monetary and humanitarian) of vaccinating otherwise healthy people, and especially children, may outweigh the benefits.","E. Romero, S. Fry, B. Hooker",10.56098/ijvtpr.v3i1.70,https://doi.org/10.56098/ijvtpr.v3i1.70,"International Journal of Vaccine Theory, Practice, and Research",1,2023,yes,"The study explicitly includes children and military personnel, rather than being limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on safety outcomes including menstrual abnormalities, myocarditis, and cerebrovascular events.",yes,The study is an observational study and meta-analysis of data from surveillance and self-reporting systems.,yes,The study uses data from human reporting systems such as VAERS and DMED.,yes,The study specifically mentions children and adolescents as a focus of the analysis regarding vaccine authorization and safety.,yes,The study evaluates outcomes for children who received vaccines as authorized in 2021.,no,"The study focuses exclusively on mRNA-based COVID-19 vaccines, as well as influenza and pertussis vaccines for comparison; it does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the safety of mRNA COVID-19 vaccines in children and adults using surveillance data from sources like VAERS and DMED. While it includes target populations like children and reports relevant safety outcomes, it focuses on mRNA COVID-19 vaccines rather than the required vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Two mRNA-based COVID vaccines were granted emergency use authorization in both children and adults in 2021""
