Mpox (Monkeypox) Infection During Pregnancy,"Pregnant individuals are susceptible to maternal mpox (monkeypox) infection and are eligible for treatment. Although transplacental transmission of mpox is possible, this case did not result in fetal infection. BACKGROUND: An mpox (formerly “monkeypox”) outbreak began in 2022, leading to infection in special populations, including pregnant individuals. CASE: We present a case of an individual who presented with a labial ulcer and subsequent papular rash at 31 weeks of gestation. She was diagnosed with mpox infection and was treated with tecovirimat. She had an uncomplicated induction of labor at 39 2/7 weeks of gestation and delivered a healthy neonate. The neonate had a positive immunoglobulin G test result for orthopoxvirus but did not have skin lesions or positive molecular test results suggestive of infection. CONCLUSION: Transplacental transmission of mpox is possible, but, in this case, the neonate did not have clinical findings suggestive of active or antenatal mpox infection. Treatment with tecovirimat in gestational cases of mpox may be beneficial.","Mindy M. Sampson, Gray Magee, Evan Schrader, K. Dantuluri, Areej Bukhari, Catherine L. Passaretti, Lorene A. Temming, Michael Leonard, J. Philips, David A. Weinrib",10.1097/AOG.0000000000005170,https://doi.org/10.1097/AOG.0000000000005170,Obstetrics and Gynecology,9,2023,yes,"The study focuses on a pregnant individual and her neonate, not healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study reports on clinical outcomes of the mpox infection and fetal health, but it does not report on vaccine-related safety or effectiveness as the patient was treated with tecovirimat rather than vaccinated.",yes,This is a case report of a single patient.,yes,The study is a clinical case report of a human patient.,yes,The study participants include a pregnant woman and a neonate.,no,The patient focused on in the case report received tecovirimat treatment for an active infection; there is no mention of the patient receiving an mpox or smallpox vaccine during pregnancy or any other relevant period.,no,"The study describes treatment with tecovirimat, an antiviral, rather than a vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This is a case report of a pregnant individual treated with tecovirimat for mpox infection. The study does not involve the administration of any mpox or smallpox vaccines, failing the vaccination-related criteria.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccination Timing,0.1,"  - ""We present a case of an individual who presented with a labial ulcer and subsequent papular rash at 31 weeks of gestation.""
