Multiple gene-deletion vaccinia virus Tiantan strain against mpox,"Monkeypox virus (MPXV) is an important zoonotic pathogenic virus, which poses serious threats to public health. MPXV infection can be prevented by immunization against the variola virus. Because of the safety risks and side effects of vaccination with live vaccinia virus (VACV) strain Tian Tan (VTT), we constructed two gene-deleted VTT recombinants (TTVAC7 and TTVC5). The immunogenicity and protective effects of the gene-deleted VTT vaccine were assessed using BALB/C mice challenged with VTT and New Zealand rabbits challenged with MPXV. The results demonstrated strong humoral and cellular immune responses. The VTT-specific and neutralizing antibody titers, specific T cell levels, and degree of dendritic cell maturation of the mice, in addition to the MPXV neutralizing antibody titers and IFN-γ, IL-6, and TNF-α levels of the rabbits were markedly higher in the groups immunized with TTVAC7 and TTVC5 than the control groups (p < 0.05). Moreover, immunization with TTVAC7 and TTVC5 reduced morbidities caused by VACV and MPXV infection. The weight change, lung histological score, and residual virus of the mouse model (p < 0.05). Similarly, the temperature change, pock number, lung histological score, and residual virus of the rabbit model were significantly reduced in the groups immunized with TTVAC7 and TTVC5 (p < 0.05). Collectively, these results demonstrate that TTVAC7 and TTVC5 may be used as potential live attenuated vaccines against MPXV infection.","Yilong Zhu, Dapeng Li, Renshuang Zhao, Minghua Chen, Yue Li, Xia Yang, Hongyue Mao, Xiao Li, Yiquan Li, Chao Shang, Xianzhu Xia",10.1186/s12985-025-02629-6,https://doi.org/10.1186/s12985-025-02629-6,Virology Journal,3,2025,no,The study does not involve human subjects; it is an animal study using mice and rabbits.,yes,The study reports immunogenicity and protective effects against morbidity in animal models.,yes,The study is a primary research laboratory study evaluating vaccine efficacy in animals.,no,"This is an animal study conducted on BALB/C mice and New Zealand rabbits, not a human study.",no,"The study does not include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as it uses animal models.",no,"Participants were not humans; therefore, no vaccination during human pregnancy or childhood was assessed.",yes,The study involves the vaccinia virus Tiantan strain (VTT) recombinants.,Exclude,This study evaluates gene-deleted vaccinia virus recombinants using BALB/C mice and New Zealand rabbit models. It is excluded from the review because it is an animal-only study and does not include human participants such as pregnant women or children.,"Population Focus, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""The immunogenicity and protective effects of the gene-deleted VTT vaccine were assessed using BALB/C mice challenged with VTT and New Zealand rabbits challenged with MPXV.""",,"  - ""The immunogenicity and protective effects of the gene-deleted VTT vaccine were assessed using BALB/C mice challenged with VTT and New Zealand rabbits challenged with MPXV.""",,"  - ""The immunogenicity and protective effects of the gene-deleted VTT vaccine were assessed using BALB/C mice challenged with VTT and New Zealand rabbits challenged with MPXV.""",,"  - ""The immunogenicity and protective effects of the gene-deleted VTT vaccine were assessed using BALB/C mice challenged with VTT and New Zealand rabbits challenged with MPXV.""",,"  - ""The immunogenicity and protective effects of the gene-deleted VTT vaccine were assessed using BALB/C mice challenged with VTT and New Zealand rabbits challenged with MPXV.""",,"  - ""The immunogenicity and protective effects of the gene-deleted VTT vaccine were assessed using BALB/C mice challenged with VTT and New Zealand rabbits challenged with MPXV.""",,"  - ""we constructed two gene-deleted VTT recombinants (TTVAC7 and TTVC5).""",
A 2-week old infant with Mpox in Uganda: a case report,"The World Health Organization declared Mpox (formerly known as monkey pox) as a public health emergency of international concern in August 2024. Whereas Mpox primarily affects adults and children, neonatal case reports are rare. We present a case of Mpox in a 2-week-old neonate without prior known history of exposure, and whose mother’s DNA polymerase chain reaction test was negative for Mpox. A 2-week-old African female neonate presented to our emergency unit with 1-week history of high-grade fever and 4-day history of vesicular rash. The rash initially manifested as an erythematous macular rash on the forehead, then gradually transformed into a maculopapular rash, and finally into a vesicular rash that spread cephalocaudally, affecting even the palms and soles. There was no known history of exposure to a person with a similar rash. A real-time polymerase chain reaction test was done, which yielded a positive result for Mpox. The mother’s Mpox polymerase chain reaction test was negative. No other family members were tested. The patient was managed in an isolation care center with emollients for skin care, exposure to control fevers, and prophylactic antibiotics to prevent secondary bacterial skin infections. The patient subsequently improved and was discharged in good condition from the care center after 11 days of hospitalization. This case highlights the need to increase the index of suspicion for Mpox even in neonates who present with no clear history of exposure and negative maternal Mpox polymerase chain reaction.","Michael Derrick Ngobi, Lucy Amaniyo, Evelyn Kembabazi, Alice Aine, David Mukunya, Vimal Konduri, Patience Atuhaire, S. B. Kitaka, Victor Musiime",10.1186/s13256-025-05547-x,https://doi.org/10.1186/s13256-025-05547-x,Journal of Medical Case Reports,0,2025,yes,"The study focus is a 2-week-old neonate, which is not a healthy non-pregnant adult.",yes,The study reports on mpox infection (positive PCR) and hospitalization duration (11 days).,yes,The study is explicitly identified as a case report.,yes,The study describes a clinical case of a human neonate.,yes,The participant is a 2-week-old neonate.,no,"There is no mention of the participant receiving any vaccine; management was limited to emollients, fever control, and antibiotics.",no,The study does not involve or mention any vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines; it focus only on infection management.,Exclude,"This case report describes an mpox infection in a 2-week-old neonate. However, it does not involve any vaccination or evaluate vaccine outcomes, failing several mandatory criteria for inclusion.","Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""A 2-week-old African female neonate presented to our emergency unit""",,"  - ""A real-time polymerase chain reaction test was done, which yielded a positive result for Mpox.""
  - ""The patient subsequently improved and was discharged in good condition from the care center after 11 days of hospitalization.""",,,,"  - ""A 2-week-old African female neonate presented to our emergency unit""",,"  - ""A 2-week-old African female neonate presented to our emergency unit""",,"  - ""managed in an isolation care center with emollients for skin care, exposure to control fevers, and prophylactic antibiotics""",,"  - ""managed in an isolation care center with emollients for skin care, exposure to control fevers, and prophylactic antibiotics""",
Maternal and perinatal outcomes of pregnancies complicated by poxvirus infection,"Objective . To ascertain the maternal and perinatal outcomes of pregnancies complicated by poxviruses infections Materials and Methods . Medline, Embase and Cochrane databases were searched. Maternal death, fetal and neonatal death, preterm birth and vertical transmission were observed. Sub-group analyses according to the severity of maternal infection, gestational age at infection, vaccination status and pregnancy status were also performed. Random effect meta-analyses of proportion and risk assessment were used to analyse the data.","L. D. Valle, Sara Mscheretti, Francesca Di, Danilo Italo, Pio Buca, M. Liberati, F. D'antonio",10.1002/uog.26147,https://doi.org/10.1002/uog.26147,Ultrasound in Obstetrics and Gynecology,2,2022,yes,"The study focuses on outcomes derived from pregnancies, meaning the population is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on maternal and perinatal outcomes including death, preterm birth, and vertical transmission, and analyzes these in the context of vaccination status.",yes,"The authors conducted a systematic search of databases and performed random effect meta-analyses, which classifies this as an evidence synthesis study.",yes,"The study analyzes pregnancy and perinatal data (maternal/fetal/neonatal deaths), which are human outcomes.",yes,The participants are specifically pregnant women and their neonates.,maybe,"The study includes vaccination status as a subgroup analysis. While it is highly likely participants were vaccinated during childhood (particularly for smallpox) or during/around pregnancy, the abstract does not specify the exact timing for the vaccinated cohorts.",yes,The context of 'poxviruses infections' and 'vaccination status' implies the use of vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.,Include,"This study is a systematic review and meta-analysis focusing on the risks of poxvirus infection during pregnancy. It evaluates maternal and perinatal outcomes and specifically analyzes vaccination status as a modifier of these outcomes, making it highly relevant to the evaluation of poxvirus vaccines in pregnant populations.",,,4.6,"  - ""pregnancies complicated by poxviruses infections""",,"  - ""Maternal death, fetal and neonatal death, preterm birth and vertical transmission were observed.""
  - ""Sub-group analyses according to the severity of maternal infection, gestational age at infection, vaccination status and pregnancy status were also performed.""",,"  - ""Medline, Embase and Cochrane databases were searched.""
