"Safety of COVID-19 vaccines among pregnant individuals in Quebec, Canada: a population-based retrospective cohort study from the Canadian Immunization Research Network","Objective To estimate the association between maternal COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy and adverse neonatal and maternal outcomes. Design Population-based retrospective cohort study using a hospitalisation database linked with other health administrative databases. Setting Province of Quebec, Canada, from 1 May 2021 to 30 June 2023. Participants All singleton pregnancies resulting in a live birth or stillbirth at ≥20 weeks of gestation, excluding those with a conception date <42 weeks before the end of the study period. Primary outcome measures We used robust Poisson regression models to estimate adjusted risk ratios (aRRs) for chorioamnionitis, postpartum haemorrhage, caesarean delivery, preterm birth, very preterm birth, small for gestational age (SGA), maternal and neonatal admission to intensive care unit (ICU, NICU) and severe neonatal morbidity. We used a Cox regression model with a time-varying exposure variable to estimate adjusted HRs (aHRs) for stillbirth. Propensity score weighting was used to adjust for potential confounding. Results Among 140 073 singleton pregnancies resulting in live birth or stillbirth, 61 282 individuals (43.8%) received at least one dose of messenger RNA COVID-19 vaccine during pregnancy. Vaccination during pregnancy was not associated with an increased risk of chorioamnionitis (aRR 0.99, 95% CI 0.95 to 1.04), postpartum haemorrhage (aRR 1.03, 95% CI 0.99 to 1.06), very preterm birth (aRR 1.04, 95% CI 0.89 to 1.21) and stillbirth (aHR 1.14, 95% CI 0.94 to 1.39). Vaccination during pregnancy was significantly associated with a reduced risk of caesarean delivery (aRR 0.94, 95% CI 0.92 to 0.96), maternal ICU admission (aRR 0.80, 95% CI 0.65 to 0.98), SGA (aRR 0.94, 95% CI 0.91 to 0.98), NICU admission (aRR 0.91, 95% CI 0.85 to 0.96), preterm birth (aRR 0.94, 95% CI 0.90 to 0.99) and severe neonatal morbidity (aRR 0.91, 95% CI 0.85 to 0.98). Conclusions Our findings suggest that COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy was not associated with an increased risk of adverse outcomes. Ongoing surveillance of the safety of maternal COVID-19 vaccination is essential as doses continue to be recommended for this group.","Marilou Kiely, Ella Diendéré, I. Dialahy, Gentiane Perrault-Sullivan, N. Brousseau, Shu Qin Wei, D. Talbot, Amélie Boutin, Caroline Quach, S. Jorgensen, I. Boucoiran",10.1136/bmjopen-2025-106494,https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2025-106494,BMJ Open,1,2025,yes,The study focuses on pregnant individuals rather than non-pregnant healthy adults.,yes,"The study reports on various adverse neonatal and maternal outcomes, which are safety measures.",yes,"This is a primary research study, specifically a retrospective cohort study.",yes,The study involves a population of human pregnant individuals.,yes,"The study specifically includes a cohort of 140,073 pregnant individuals.",yes,Study participants received the vaccine during pregnancy.,no,"The study investigates the mRNA COVID-19 vaccine, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"While the study focuses on a target population of pregnant individuals, it evaluates the safety of mRNA COVID-19 vaccines rather than the required vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines. Since the vaccine type does not meet the inclusion criteria, the study is excluded.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Objective To estimate the association between maternal COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy and adverse neonatal and maternal outcomes.""
