Update on Mpox: a brief narrative review.,"Mpox (formerly Monkeypox), a neglected tropical disease once confined to Central and West Africa, emerged as a global epidemic outbreak in May, 2022 with 87,529 cases reported as of May, 23, 2023. It predominantly affected men (96.2%) who have sex with men (84-100%), although other transmission routes have been reported, including occupational exposure and vertical transmission. Concomitant HIV infection has been recorded in 21-46.9% and pre-exposure prophylaxis against HIV infection has been reported in 11-57% of published cases. The current outbreak clinical presentation differs from endemic cases with prodromal symptoms that could be absent: the number of lesions is generally low, with skin lesions predominantly localised in the ano-genital areas and frequent lesions present in different stages of progression (i.e., asynchronous). Asymptomatic Mpox infection can occur in 1.8-6.5% of at-risk subjects. People living with HIV with severe immunodeficiency (less than 100 CD4+ lymphocytes per microliter) are at risk of more severe clinical manifestations and death. According to a systematic review and meta-analysis, the hospitalisation rate is around 6% and the observed case-fatality rate is less than 0.1%. Tecovirimat is the drug of choice for treating severe cases although there is no evidence of efficacy from randomised controlled trials. Immunization with a live non-replicating vaccine (JYNNEOS) effectively reduces the disease's incidence.","S. Antinori, G. Casalini, Andrea Giacomelli, A. Rodriguez-Morales",10.53854/liim-3103-1,https://doi.org/10.53854/liim-3103-1,Le infezioni in medicina,14,2023,yes,"The study includes individuals with HIV and mentions routes like vertical transmission, so it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on hospitalization rates, case-fatality rates, and the effectiveness of the JYNNEOS vaccine in reducing incidence.",no,"The paper identifies itself as a brief narrative review, which is not a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,The study discusses clinical presentations and immunization in humans.,no,"The text mentions vertical transmission but does not include pregnant women, neonates, or children as specific participants in the research described.",no,"The study does not specify that vaccination occurred during pregnancy, childhood, or the neonatal period.",yes,"The study discusses JYNNEOS, which is a vaccinia-based vaccine.",Exclude,"This paper is a narrative review and does not meet the required study design or target population criteria, as it focuses on the general epidemic and does not include data on participants from the mandatory groups such as pregnant women or children. While it mentions JYNNEOS vaccine effectiveness, it lacks primary data or a systematic synthesis of the specified target populations.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""other transmission routes have been reported, including occupational exposure and vertical transmission.""",,"  - ""hospitalisation rate is around 6% and the observed case-fatality rate is less than 0.1%.""
  - ""Immunization with a live non-replicating vaccine (JYNNEOS) effectively reduces the disease's incidence.""",,,,"  - ""Immunization with a live non-replicating vaccine (JYNNEOS) effectively reduces the disease's incidence.""",,"  - ""other transmission routes have been reported, including occupational exposure and vertical transmission.""",,"  - ""Immunization with a live non-replicating vaccine (JYNNEOS) effectively reduces the disease's incidence.""",,"  - ""Immunization with a live non-replicating vaccine (JYNNEOS) effectively reduces the disease's incidence.""",
Vaccination for Monkeypox Virus Infection in Humans: A Review of Key Considerations,"Monkeypox virus infection in humans (MVIH) is currently an evolving public health concern given that >3000 MVIH cases have been reported in >50 countries globally, and the World Health Organization declared monkeypox a global health emergency on 23 July 2022. Adults (≥16 years old) usually have mild disease in contemporary studies, with a pooled case fatality rate of 0.03% (1/2941 cases). In comparison, poorer outcomes have been reported in children <16 years old (pooled case fatality rate 19% (4/21 cases)), immunocompromised patients, and pregnant women, with high rates of fetal demise in this group. Monkeypox-specific treatments include oral or intravenous tecovirimat, intravenous or topical cidofovir, oral brincidofovir, and vaccinia immunoglobulin, but the overall risk–benefit balance of monkeypox-specific treatment is unclear. Two effective vaccines exist for the prevention of MVIH: modified vaccinia Ankara and ACAM2000. Most probably, vaccination will be a key strategy for mitigating MVIH given the current rapid global spread of monkeypox, the existence of efficacious vaccines, and the uncertain risk–benefit profile of current antivirals. Priority groups for vaccination should include healthcare workers at high risk for occupational exposure, immunocompromised patients, and children. Vaccination strategies include pre-exposure vaccination, post-exposure prophylaxis, and ring vaccination of close contacts.",K. See,10.3390/vaccines10081342,https://doi.org/10.3390/vaccines10081342,Vaccines,55,2022,yes,"The study discusses outcomes and considerations for children and pregnant women, not just healthy adults.",maybe,"The paper mentions case fatality rates for children and adults, and mentions the existence of effective vaccines, but does not provide original data on vaccine effectiveness or safety outcomes from a primary study.",no,The title and content indicate this is a narrative review ('A Review of Key Considerations') rather than primary research or a systematic review/meta-analysis.,yes,The study focuses on humans.,yes,The text discusses children and pregnant women.,maybe,"While the review discusses vaccination strategies for children, it does not present a primary cohort or case series of individuals vaccinated during these periods; it reviews existing literature and general considerations.",yes,"The paper mentions modified vaccinia Ankara and ACAM2000, which are vaccinia-based vaccines.",Exclude,"This paper is a narrative review rather than a primary research study or systematic review. While it discusses high-risk populations including children and pregnant women, its design as a general review of considerations excludes it from the review scope.",Study Design,Study Design,0.1,"  - ""poorer outcomes have been reported in children <16 years old (pooled case fatality rate 19% (4/21 cases)), immunocompromised patients, and pregnant women, with high rates of fetal demise in this group.""
