"Differential Diagnosis, Prevention, and Treatment of mpox (Monkeypox): A Review for Dermatologists","The current 2022 mpox (monkeypox) outbreak has been officially recognized as a public health emergency. The mpox clinical symptoms include high fever, fatigue, chills, headache, swollen lymph nodes, muscle aches, and a disseminated painful rash. However, recent cases of mpox have shown a shift in clinical symptoms, with anogenital skin lesions emerging as the predominant feature. Due to the predominant skin manifestations of mpox, dermatologists could be crucial in detecting new mpox cases and educating frontline healthcare professionals about mpox. The mpox virus is continuously evolving and has several variants. Genome sequencing has revealed that the Clade IIb variant is responsible for the 2022 mpox outbreak. Mpox spread may occur through animal-to-human and human-to-human transmission; however, unlike coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19), long-range airborne transmission has not been reported. Healthcare professionals are at higher risk of becoming infected since they are usually in close contact with both the patients and potentially contaminated fomites (e.g., examination table, gowns, gloves). Both public and healthcare professionals should take preventive and avoidance measures to limit the spread. Mpox is usually self-limiting and may require only symptomatic treatment; however, it may cause severe complications in special populations such as immunocompromised individuals. For severe infection, clinicians may consider antiviral drugs (off-label), tecovirimat and brincidofovir, originally approved for smallpox treatment. Two smallpox vaccines, ACAM2000^® and JYNNEOS^TM, can be used as pre-exposure prophylaxis against mpox. JYNNEOS^TM, which carries approval for mpox use, has less adverse effect potential than ACAM2000^®, and may also be used as post-exposure prophylaxis, preferably within 4 days of exposure.","Aditya K. Gupta, M. Talukder, T. Rosen, V. Piguet",10.1007/s40257-023-00778-4,https://doi.org/10.1007/s40257-023-00778-4,American Journal of Clinical Dermatology,42,2023,maybe,The paper does not present primary data on specific populations; it is a clinical review for dermatologists discussing symptoms and treatments in general terms.,maybe,"The paper mentions that vaccines can be used for prophylaxis and mentions adverse effect potential generally, but does not report specific outcome data from a study.",no,"The title and content identify this as a 'Review', not a primary research study (like an RCT or cohort study) or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,The paper discusses human disease and clinical management.,no,"There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as participants in a study described in this text.",no,The paper discusses vaccines in general terms for pre-exposure or post-exposure prophylaxis but does not track vaccination during specific life stages like pregnancy or childhood within a study framework.,yes,"The study mentions ACAM2000 and JYNNEOS, which are vaccinia-based vaccines.",Exclude,"This paper is a general narrative review for dermatologists and does not contain primary research or a systematic review (Study Design failure). Furthermore, it does not specifically include or report data on the required target populations such as pregnant women or children.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,,,"  - ""Two smallpox vaccines, ACAM2000^® and JYNNEOS^TM, can be used as pre-exposure prophylaxis against mpox.""",,,,,,,,"  - ""Two smallpox vaccines, ACAM2000^® and JYNNEOS^TM, can be used as pre-exposure prophylaxis against mpox.""",,"  - ""Two smallpox vaccines, ACAM2000^® and JYNNEOS^TM, can be used as pre-exposure prophylaxis against mpox.""",
Preliminary Findings of mRNA Covid-19 Vaccine Safety in Pregnant Persons,"Abstract Background Many pregnant persons in the United States are receiving messenger RNA (mRNA) coronavirus disease 2019 (Covid-19) vaccines, but data are limited on their safety in pregnancy. Methods From December 14, 2020, to February 28, 2021, we used data from the “v-safe after vaccination health checker” surveillance system, the v-safe pregnancy registry, and the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS) to characterize the initial safety of mRNA Covid-19 vaccines in pregnant persons. Results A total of 35,691 v-safe participants 16 to 54 years of age identified as pregnant. Injection-site pain was reported more frequently among pregnant persons than among nonpregnant women, whereas headache, myalgia, chills, and fever were reported less frequently. Among 3958 participants enrolled in the v-safe pregnancy registry, 827 had a completed pregnancy, of which 115 (13.9%) resulted in a pregnancy loss and 712 (86.1%) resulted in a live birth (mostly among participants with vaccination in the third trimester). Adverse neonatal outcomes included preterm birth (in 9.4%) and small size for gestational age (in 3.2%); no neonatal deaths were reported. Although not directly comparable, calculated proportions of adverse pregnancy and neonatal outcomes in persons vaccinated against Covid-19 who had a completed pregnancy were similar to incidences reported in studies involving pregnant women that were conducted before the Covid-19 pandemic. Among 221 pregnancy-related adverse events reported to the VAERS, the most frequently reported event was spontaneous abortion (46 cases). Conclusions Preliminary findings did not show obvious safety signals among pregnant persons who received mRNA Covid-19 vaccines. However, more longitudinal follow-up, including follow-up of large numbers of women vaccinated earlier in pregnancy, is necessary to inform maternal, pregnancy, and infant outcomes.","T. Shimabukuro, Shin Y. Kim, Tanya R. Myers, P. Moro, T. Oduyebo, L. Panagiotakopoulos, P. Marquez, C. Olson, Ruiling Liu, Karen T Chang, S. Ellington, V. Burkel, A. Smoots, C. J. Green, C. Licata, Bicheng Zhang, M. Alimchandani, A. Mba-Jonas, S. Martin, J. Gee, D. Meaney-Delman",10.1056/NEJMoa2104983,https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2104983,New England Journal of Medicine,716,2021,yes,"The study focuses on pregnant persons, which are not healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports safety outcomes including pregnancy loss, preterm birth, and small size for gestational age.",yes,"The study uses data from surveillance systems and a pregnancy registry, representing a primary research observational cohort/surveillance study.",yes,The study is a human study involving pregnant participants.,yes,The study specifically includes pregnant women as participants.,yes,Participants received the vaccine during pregnancy.,no,"The study evaluates mRNA Covid-19 vaccines, not vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the safety of mRNA Covid-19 vaccines in pregnant persons. While it includes the target population of pregnant women and reports relevant safety outcomes, it fails the inclusion criteria because it does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""35,691 v-safe participants 16 to 54 years of age identified as pregnant.""",,"  - ""Adverse neonatal outcomes included preterm birth (in 9.4%) and small size for gestational age (in 3.2%); no neonatal deaths were reported.""",,"  - ""we used data from the “v-safe after vaccination health checker” surveillance system, the v-safe pregnancy registry, and the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS)""",,"  - ""35,691 v-safe participants 16 to 54 years of age identified as pregnant.""",,"  - ""35,691 v-safe participants 16 to 54 years of age identified as pregnant.""",,"  - ""35,691 v-safe participants 16 to 54 years of age identified as pregnant.""",,"  - ""messenger RNA (mRNA) coronavirus disease 2019 (Covid-19) vaccines,""",
Impact of prenatal COVID-19 vaccination on delivery and neonatal outcomes: Results from a New York City cohort,"Research suggest prenatal vaccination against coronavirus disease-19 (COVID-19) is safe. However, previous studies utilized retrospectively collected data or examined late pregnancy vaccinations. We investigated the associations of COVID-19 vaccination throughout pregnancy with delivery and neonatal outcomes. We included 1,794 mother-neonate dyads enrolled in the Generation C Study with known prenatal COVID-19 vaccination status and complete covariate and outcome data. We used multivariable quantile regressions to estimate the effect of prenatal COVID-19 vaccination on birthweight, delivery gestational age, and blood loss at delivery; and Poisson generalized linear models for Caesarean delivery (CD) and Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) admission. Using the above methods, we estimated effects of trimester of vaccine initiation on these outcomes. In our sample, 13.7% (n = 250) received at least one prenatal dose of any COVID-19 vaccine. Vaccination was not associated with birthweight (β = 12.42 g [-90.5, 114.8]), gestational age (β = 0.2 days [-1.1, 1.5]), blood loss (β = -50.6 ml [-107.0, 5.8]), the risks of CD (RR = 0.8; [0.6, 1.1]) or NICU admission (RR = 0.9 [0.5, 1.7]). Trimester of vaccine initiation was also not associated with these outcomes. Our findings suggest that there is no associated risk between prenatal COVID-19 vaccination and adverse delivery and neonatal outcomes in a cohort sample from NYC.","E. Ibroci, Xiaoqin Liu, W. Lieb, R. Jessel, F. Gigase, Kyle Chung, M. Graziani, M. Lieber, S. Ohrn, J. Lynch, Juliana Castro, Christina L Marshall, R. Tubassum, Farida Mutawakil, E. Kaplowitz, S. Ellington, Nina M Molenaar, R. Sperling, E. Howell, T. Janevic, Siobhan M. Dolan, J. Stone, L. D. de Witte, V. Bergink, Anna-Sophie Rommel",10.1016/j.vaccine.2022.09.095,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2022.09.095,Vaccine,11,2022,yes,"The study focuses on mother-neonate dyads, specifically pregnant women and their infants.",yes,"The study reports on neonatal and delivery outcomes such as birthweight, gestational age, Caesarean delivery, and NICU admission.",yes,This is a primary research study involving a cohort (Generation C Study).,yes,"The study involves a human cohort of 1,794 mother-neonate dyads.",yes,The study participants are pregnant women and neonates.,yes,Participants received the vaccine during their pregnancy.,no,"The study investigates COVID-19 vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study investigates the impact of prenatal COVID-19 vaccination on neonatal and delivery outcomes in a NYC cohort. However, it does not evaluate vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines, which is a requirement for this review.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""included 1,794 mother-neonate dyads""",,"  - ""associations of COVID-19 vaccination throughout pregnancy with delivery and neonatal outcomes.""",,"  - ""prenatal COVID-19 vaccination""",,"  - ""included 1,794 mother-neonate dyads""",,"  - ""included 1,794 mother-neonate dyads""",,"  - ""prenatal COVID-19 vaccination""",,"  - ""prenatal COVID-19 vaccination""",
"An open-label, non-randomized study investigating the safety and efficacy of smallpox vaccine, LC16, as post-exposure prophylaxis for mpox","ABSTRACT Mpox is an acute exanthematous disease caused by the monkeypox virus. Since May 2022, it has spread as a community-acquired infection, mainly in Europe and the United States, and urgent measures to prevent this infection were also required in Japan. In this study, we investigated the post-exposure prophylaxis of mpox and safety after inoculating the smallpox vaccine. Participants in close contact with patients with mpox were inoculated with “Freeze-dried cell culture Smallpox Vaccine LC16,” within 14 days after close contact. Six cases were registered, and all the participants were inoculated. No mpox symptoms or related complications were observed in the participants for 21 days after the close contact. Adverse events due to inoculation, such as rash, fever, lymphadenopathy, and local reaction at the inoculation site (comprising erythema, swelling, induration, and pain) were observed in the participants; however, all inoculation-related events were non-severe and non-serious, and the participants recovered during the 28-day observation period. The findings of this study suggest that inoculation with LC16 is an effective post-exposure prophylaxis in individuals who had close contact with patients with mpox. Further large-scale studies are warranted to validate these findings.","N. Tomita, J. Terada-Hirashima, Y. Uemura, Yosuke Shimizu, H. Iwasaki, Rina Yano, Tetsuya Suzuki, S. Saito, N. Okumura, W. Sugiura, N. Ohmagari, M. Ujiie",10.1080/21645515.2023.2242219,https://doi.org/10.1080/21645515.2023.2242219,Human Vaccines & Immunotherapeutics,13,2023,no,The study only involves 6 cases of close contacts and does not provide evidence of including any individuals other than non-pregnant adults.,yes,The study reports on clinical effectiveness (prevention of mpox symptoms) and safety (adverse events).,yes,"This is described as an open-label, non-randomized study, which qualifies as primary research.",yes,The study involves human participants (registered cases) who were inoculated.,no,"The abstract does not state that pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents were included in the 6 registered cases.",no,"The abstract does not specify that vaccination occurred during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood/adolescence.",yes,"The vaccine used is the 'Freeze-dried cell culture Smallpox Vaccine LC16', which is a vaccinia-based vaccine.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the LC16 smallpox vaccine as post-exposure prophylaxis in six individuals with close contact to mpox patients. However, it does not provide any evidence that the study population includes pregnant women, children, or adolescents, thus failing key population inclusion criteria.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Six cases were registered, and all the participants were inoculated.""",,"  - ""No mpox symptoms or related complications were observed in the participants for 21 days after the close contact.""
  - ""Adverse events due to inoculation, such as rash, fever, lymphadenopathy, and local reaction at the inoculation site (comprising erythema, swelling, induration, and pain) were observed in the participants;""
  - ""all inoculation-related events were non-severe and non-serious,""",,"  - ""investigated the post-exposure prophylaxis of mpox and safety after inoculating the smallpox vaccine.""",,"  - ""Six cases were registered, and all the participants were inoculated.""",,"  - ""Six cases were registered, and all the participants were inoculated.""",,"  - ""Participants in close contact with patients with mpox were inoculated with “Freeze-dried cell culture Smallpox Vaccine LC16,” within 14 days after close contact.""",,"  - ""Participants in close contact with patients with mpox were inoculated with “Freeze-dried cell culture Smallpox Vaccine LC16,” within 14 days after close contact.""",
Adverse Events Post Smallpox-Vaccination: Insights from Tail Scarification Infection in Mice with Vaccinia virus,"Adverse events upon smallpox vaccination with fully-replicative strains of Vaccinia virus (VACV) comprise an array of clinical manifestations that occur primarily in immunocompromised patients leading to significant host morbidity/mortality. The expansion of immune-suppressed populations and the possible release of Variola virus as a bioterrorist act have given rise to concerns over vaccination complications should more widespread vaccination be reinitiated. Our goal was to evaluate the components of the host immune system that are sufficient to prevent morbidity/mortality in a murine model of tail scarification, which mimics immunological and clinical features of smallpox vaccination in humans. Infection of C57BL/6 wild-type mice led to a strictly localized infection, with complete viral clearance by day 28 p.i. On the other hand, infection of T and B-cell deficient mice (Rag1 −/−) produced a severe disease, with uncontrolled viral replication at the inoculation site and dissemination to internal organs. Infection of B-cell deficient animals (µMT) produced no mortality. However, viral clearance in µMT animals was delayed compared to WT animals, with detectable viral titers in tail and internal organs late in infection. Treatment of Rag1 −/− with rabbit hyperimmune anti-vaccinia serum had a subtle effect on the morbidity/mortality of this strain, but it was effective in reduce viral titers in ovaries. Finally, NUDE athymic mice showed a similar outcome of infection as Rag1 −/−, and passive transfer of WT T cells to Rag1 −/− animals proved fully effective in preventing morbidity/mortality. These results strongly suggest that both T and B cells are important in the immune response to primary VACV infection in mice, and that T-cells are required to control the infection at the inoculation site and providing help for B-cells to produce antibodies, which help to prevent viral dissemination. These insights might prove helpful to better identify individuals with higher risk of complications after infection with poxvirus.","B. E. Mota, Nadia F. Gallardo-Romero, G. Trindade, M. Keckler, K. Karem, Darin S. Carroll, M. A. Campos, L. Vieira, F. D. da Fonseca, P. Ferreira, C. A. Bonjardim, I. Damon, E. Kroon",10.1371/journal.pone.0018924,https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0018924,PLoS ONE,22,2011,yes,The study involves mice rather than healthy human adults.,yes,"The study reports on disease severity, mortality, and viral dissemination in animal models.",yes,The study is a primary experimental research study using murine models.,no,The study is conducted in a 'murine model' and is not a human study.,no,"The research is conducted on mice and does not include human pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"The participants are mice, so vaccination did not occur during human pregnancy or childhood stages.",yes,The study uses historical smallpox vaccination strains of Vaccinia virus (VACV).,Exclude,"This research is a laboratory study utilizing a murine model to examine the immune response to smallpox vaccination. Because it is an animal-based study rather than a human study, it fails to meet several inclusion criteria related to target human populations and study design.","Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""a murine model of tail scarification,""",,"  - ""infection of T and B-cell deficient mice (Rag1 −/−) produced a severe disease,""
  - ""Infection of B-cell deficient animals (µMT) produced no mortality.""",,"  - ""a murine model of tail scarification,""",,"  - ""a murine model of tail scarification,""",,"  - ""Infection of C57BL/6 wild-type mice""
