Death-associated with human monkeypox outbreak 2022: the current perspectives – correspondence,"Dear Editor, Monkeypox virus (MPXV) is a neglected tropical-zoonotic viral disease that is endemic in Central and Western Africa. However, unprecedented outbreaks of monkeypox were recorded across all six WHO regions beyond the African regions in May 2022. As of November 6, 2022, the WHO reports a total of 78,229 (77,301 in nonendemic areas and 928 in endemic-African areas) laboratory-confirmed cases of monkeypox as well as 41 deaths (28 cases outside Africa) across 109 various countries (https://www. cdc.gov/poxvirus/monkeypox/response/2022/world-map.html). MPXV is thought to be a self-limiting illness that only affects African populations; however, there has been a sudden increase in the number of MPXV infections since May 2022, which is largely linked to men who have sex with men. The majority of MPXVinfested individuals experienced vesiculopustular lesions in the anogenital region following fever and lymphadenopathy. According to recent comprehensive studies, MPXV transmission occurs through direct contact with contaminated cutaneous lesions. In addition, there is a need for further investigation regarding the impact of body fluids (eg, breastmilk, semen, vaginal fluid, amniotic fluid, or blood) in boosting the MPXV transmission cycle. On July 23, 2022, WHO declared the MPXV outbreak of 2022 a public health emergency of international concern as a consequence of the unexpected sudden rise in MPXV cases across the globe, the lack of effective immunity resulting from the smallpox vaccine cession, as well as the absence of exclusive therapeutic agents. Scientists proposed a novel nomenclature to distinguish the resurgentMPXV2022 (as clade 3) from the previous sublineages, that is, the Congo Basin lineage as clade 1, as well as the West African lineage as clade 2. Recent studies have shown that the R0 of MPXV clade 3 is substantially higher than 1, elucidating the sustained transmission of MPXV clade 3 in high-risk populations, for example, young esbian, gay, bisexual, and transgender individuals, immunocompromised individuals, pregnant women, as well as pediatrics. From the beginning until June 2022, WHO reports 72 human deaths due to monkeypox, all of which occurred in Africa (64 deaths occurred in the Democratic Republic of Congo). However, 41MPXV-related deaths will occur in the first 11months of 2022, there are 41 MPXV-related deaths. According to a review of the literature, the reasons for deaths caused by MPXV 2022 have gained little attention, which is critical for better clinical practice. Herein, we pooled data from previous observational studies to discuss the reasons for deaths among MPXV-infected patients. Previous research suggested that children under the age of 10 accounted for one-third of MPXV-related deaths from the 1970s to the 1990s. D’Antonio et al. reveal MPXV infection during pregnancy is linkedwith intrauterine fetal death. In anothermetaanalysis, DeWitt et al. suggested that being a child is considered a key risk factor for MPXV death. Furthermore, MPXV-related deaths have been documented in high-risk individuals, such as infants, young children below 10, pregnant women, and those who were immunocompromised in previous MPXV outbreaks before 2022. There are different virus behaviors after the reemergence of the monkeypox outbreak in 2022; for example, all documented death cases due to monkeypox in nonendemic regions were middle-aged men.MPXV-associated deaths in 2022 have a risk factor, for example, lymphoma as well as septic shock in Brazil and encephalitis in Spain, as well as respiratory failure and acute renal failure in a 41-year-old with HIV/tuberculosis coinfection from Peru. According to a large surveillance in the WHO European Region, a large portion of MPOXV-related hospitalization and death cases involved people who had not received smallpox vaccination, were HIV positive, and had rash, lymphadenopathy, and systemic symptoms. Miller reported the clinical characteristics of severe MPXV in hospitalized patients in the USA; they found that male gender, HIV infection, and STI coinfection are the most risky comorbidities associated with death. All MPXV-related deaths occurred among unvaccinated individuals. According to the data presented, immune disorders, a lack of vaccination, HIV infection, cutaneous ulcers, bacterial superinfection, septic shock, and respiratory complications all contribute to monkeypox morbidity and mortality. During the course of an infection, MPXV has extended viremia to human organs, which may result in invasion of the central nervous system, lungs, and kidney. In this situation, serious MPXV consequences such as encephalitis and organ failure are possible. The superimposition of bacterial soft-tissue coinfection can potentially result in septic shock,which has been linked to deadly sepsis. Further research is required to understand MPXV-related mechanisms of death. According to the literature, varied case fatality rates (CFRs) related to Congo Basin and Central African clades have been observed (ranging from 2.8 to 11%). According to our findings, the current CFR is around 0.08% (Table 1). MPXVDepartment of Medical Microbiology, School of Medicine, Zahedan University of Medical Sciences, Zahedan, Iran","M. Keikha, Mehdi Zandhaghighi, Shahram Shahraki Zahedani",10.1097/JS9.0000000000000123,https://doi.org/10.1097/JS9.0000000000000123,International Journal of Surgery,7,2023,yes,The study discusses deaths in children and pregnant women.,yes,The paper reports outcomes related to death and hospitalization.,no,"The document is a correspondence piece that summarizes/pools data from other studies. It is not a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or a formal systematic review/meta-analysis conducted by the authors, but rather a commentary on general trends.",yes,The study discusses human deaths and infections.,yes,The text references children and pregnant women.,no,"While the paper mentions vaccination status, it does not detail individuals receiving vaccines specifically during pregnancy or childhood in a way that contributes primary data to this review.",yes,The paper mentions 'smallpox vaccination' in the context of deaths.,Exclude,This article is a correspondence piece that reviews existing literature rather than presenting primary research. It fails the study design criteria and does not provide specific data on outcomes for individuals vaccinated during the relevant windows (pregnancy/childhood).,"Study Design, Vaccination Timing",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""Previous research suggested that children under the age of 10 accounted for one-third of MPXV-related deaths""
