Defining and emulating target trials of the effects of postexposure vaccination using observational data,"Postexposure vaccination has the potential to prevent or modify the course of clinical disease among those exposed to a pathogen. However, due to logistical constraints, postexposure vaccine trials have been difficult to implement in practice. In place of trials, investigators have used observational data to estimate the efficacy or optimal timing window for postexposure vaccines, but the relationship between these analyses and those that would be conducted in a trial is often unclear. Here, we define several possible target trials for postexposure vaccination and show how, under certain conditions, they can be emulated using observational data. We emphasize the importance of the incubation period and the timing of vaccination in trial design and emulation. As an example, we specify a protocol for postexposure vaccination against mpox and provide a step-by-step description of how to emulate it using data from a healthcare database or contact tracing program. We further illustrate some of the benefits of the target trial approach through simulation.","M. Lipsitch, C. Boyer",10.1101/2023.05.03.23289471,https://doi.org/10.1101/2023.05.03.23289471,medRxiv,1,2023,maybe,"The study does not specify a population, as it is a methodological protocol and simulation.",no,"The study does not report clinical outcomes (safety or effectiveness) from a trial or observation, but instead focuses on the methodology of outcome estimation via simulation.",no,"The study is a methodological paper utilizing simulations to describe trial emulation, which is not among the accepted primary research designs (RCT, cohort, etc.) or evidence synthesis.",no,"This is a computational/methodological paper based on simulation, not a study involving human subjects.",no,"The study does not include human participants, and there is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, or children.",no,"As a methodological/simulation study, there are no participants receiving vaccinations during the specified periods.",yes,The protocol example specifically addresses mpox vaccination.,Exclude,"This is a methodological paper focused on the simulation and emulation of target trials for mpox postexposure vaccination. It does not contain clinical data or human participants, and therefore does not include the required target populations of pregnant women or children.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""specify a protocol for postexposure vaccination against mpox and provide a step-by-step description of how to emulate it using data from a healthcare database or contact tracing program.""",,"  - ""we define several possible target trials for postexposure vaccination and show how, under certain conditions, they can be emulated using observational data.""
