"Safety of Monovalent BNT162b2 (Pfizer-BioNTech), mRNA-1273 (Moderna), and NVX-CoV2373 (Novavax) COVID-19 Vaccines in US Children Aged 6 months to 17 years","Importance Active monitoring of health outcomes after COVID-19 vaccination provides early detection of rare outcomes that may not be identified in prelicensure trials. Objective To conduct near-real-time monitoring of health outcomes following COVID-19 vaccination in the United States (US) pediatric population aged 6 months to 17 years. Design We evaluated 21 pre-specified health outcomes; 15 were sequentially tested through near-real-time surveillance, and 6 were monitored descriptively within a cohort of vaccinated children. We tested for increased rate of each outcome following vaccination compared to a historical comparator cohort. Setting This population-based study was conducted under the US Food and Drug Administration public health surveillance mandate using three commercial claims databases. Participants Children aged 6 months to 17 years were included if they received a monovalent COVID-19 vaccine dose before early 2023 and had continuous enrollment in a medical health insurance plan from the start of an outcome-specific clean window to the COVID-19 vaccination dose. Exposure Exposure was defined as receipt of a monovalent BNT162b2, mRNA-1273, or NVX-CoV2373 COVID-19 vaccine dose. The primary analysis evaluated dose 1 and dose 2 combined, and secondary analyses evaluated each dose separately. Follow-up time was censored at death, disenrollment, end of risk window, end of study period, or a subsequent dose administration. Main Outcomes Twenty-one prespecified health outcomes. Results The study included 4,102,016 enrollees aged 6 months to17 years. Thirteen of 15 outcomes sequentially tested did not meet the threshold for a statistical signal. In the primary analysis, myocarditis or pericarditis signals were detected following BNT162b2 vaccine in children aged 12-17 years old and seizures/convulsions signals were detected following vaccination with BNT162b2 and mRNA-1273 in children aged 2-4/5 years. However, in a post-hoc sensitivity analysis, the seizures/convulsions signal was sensitive to background rates selection and was not observed when 2022 background rates were selected instead of 2020 rates. Conclusions and Relevance Of the two signaled outcomes, the myocarditis or pericarditis signals are consistent with previously published reports. The new signal detected for seizures/convulsions among younger children should be further investigated in a robust epidemiological study with better confounding adjustment.","P. Lloyd, M. Hu, A. Shoaibi, Yuhui Feng, H. Wong, E. R. Smith, K. Amend, Annemarie Kline, D. Beachler, J. Gruber, Mahasweta Mitra, John D. Seeger, Charlalynn Harris, A. Secora, J. Obidi, Jing Wang, Jennifer Song, C. McMahill-Walraven, Christian Reich, Rowan McEvoy, Rose Do, Y. Chillarige, Robin Clifford, Danielle D. Cooper, R. Forshee, S. Anderson",10.1101/2023.10.13.23296903,https://doi.org/10.1101/2023.10.13.23296903,medRxiv,0,2023,yes,The study focuses on the pediatric population aged 6 months to 17 years.,yes,The study evaluates safety outcomes such as myocarditis and seizures following vaccination.,yes,"The study is a population-based surveillance study using claims databases, which is a primary cohort-style research design.",yes,The study involves human children followed through medical health insurance plans.,yes,The participants are children and adolescents aged 6 months to 17 years.,yes,Participants received the vaccine during childhood or adolescence (ages 6 months to 17 years).,no,"The study specifically evaluates COVID-19 vaccines (BNT162b2, mRNA-1273, NVX-CoV2373), not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the safety of COVID-19 vaccines in a pediatric cohort aged 6 months to 17 years. While it meets the target population and outcome criteria by monitoring adverse events, it fails the intervention criterion as it does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""US) pediatric population aged 6 months to 17 years.""",,"  - ""myocarditis or pericarditis signals were detected""",,"  - ""population-based study was conducted under the US Food and Drug Administration public health surveillance mandate using three commercial claims databases.""",,"  - ""population-based study was conducted under the US Food and Drug Administration public health surveillance mandate using three commercial claims databases.""",,"  - ""Children aged 6 months to 17 years""",,"  - ""US) pediatric population aged 6 months to 17 years.""",,,
"Maternal and Neonatal Outcomes after Vaccination with SARS-CoV-2: A
Systematic Review and Meta-analysis of Cohort Studies","SARS-CoV-2 infection among pregnant women causes maternal and neonatal complications. Professional societies endorse the vaccination among pregnant women. This review of the
cohort studies aims to assess the short-term maternal and neonatal outcomes among vaccinated vs.
non-vaccinated pregnant women with SARS-CoV-2 vaccination. We searched Cochrane Central
Registry of Controlled Trials, Scopus, Google Scholar, and PubMed databases. The observational
cohort studies published from July 2021 to December 2022 were included. The eligibility criteria
were assessed. The studies documenting maternal and neonatal outcomes and the relative risk, and
95% confidence interval were considered. Joanna Briggs Institute data extraction form was used,
and the quality assessment of the included study was conducted using the Newcastle-Ottawa quality assessment scale. The quality of the grading was summarised with GradePro software. Data
from the five cohort studies are considered. 56% of the un-vaccinated pregnant women experience
composite adverse maternal outcomes (RR: 3.97; 95% CI:0.73,21.49; p-value: ˂0.11). There was a
reduced risk of occurrence of the meconium-stained amniotic fluid who are vaccinated (RR: 0.89;
95% CI:0.71, 1.12; p value=0.33). The unvaccinated group is 3.16 times more likely to take infertility treatment (RR: 3.54; 95% CI:2.04, 6.12; p-value: ˂0.00001). There was no significant difference concerning neonatal outcomes between both groups. The pregnant women who were not vaccinated against SARS-CoV had an increased risk for a composite adverse maternal outcome and
meconium-stained amniotic fluid. The vaccine has effectively prevented the disease in the first six
months. Additional studies are needed to understand the safety of the SARS-CoV vaccine.","Anand Vijayakumar Palur Ramakrishnan, R. Basutkar, S. Murugan, Shonitha Sagadevan, Oorvashree Sri Hari, Shanmuga Priyan",10.2174/0126667975269133231117062158,https://doi.org/10.2174/0126667975269133231117062158,Coronaviruses,0,2023,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women and neonatal outcomes.,yes,"The study reports on maternal and neonatal outcomes, including effectively preventing disease.",yes,The study is a systematic review and meta-analysis of cohort studies.,yes,The study involves human participants (pregnant women and neonates).,yes,The study includes pregnant women and neonates.,yes,The study examines outcomes for pregnant women who received the vaccine during pregnancy.,no,The study specifically evaluates SARS-CoV-2 vaccines rather than vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This study is a systematic review focusing on the maternal and neonatal outcomes of SARS-CoV-2 vaccination in pregnant women. However, it does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines, which is a requirement for this review.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,,,"  - ""The vaccine has effectively prevented the disease""",,,,,,,,,,,
Effectiveness and Safety of the MVA–BN Vaccine against Mpox in At-Risk Individuals in the United States (USMVAc),"The mpox 2022 outbreak was declared a public health emergency in July 2022. In August 2022, the MVA–BN vaccine received emergency use authorization in the United States (US) to target at-risk groups. This study (EUPAS104386) used HealthVerity’s administrative US healthcare data to generate real-world evidence for MVA–BN vaccine effectiveness and safety to prevent mpox disease in men who have sex with men (MSM) and transgender women, the most affected population during the 2022 mpox outbreak. Fully vaccinated subjects (two doses ≥ 28 days apart) were initially matched with five unvaccinated subjects on calendar date, age, US region, and insurance type. Subjects were followed from index date (14 days after the second dose) until death or data end to ascertain mpox occurrence. After propensity score adjustment, the MVA–BN vaccine effectiveness against mpox disease was 89% (95% CI: 12%, 99%) among those fully vaccinated; attenuated to 64% (95% CI: 40%, 78%) among those with any dose and 70% (95% CI: 44%, 84%) for those with only a single dose. One pericarditis adverse event of special interest was observed when the risk window was extended to 28 days. These results contribute to the totality of evidence supporting the favorable benefit/risk profile of the MVA–BN vaccine.","Soowoo Back, Bethany Knox, Ciara Coakley, N. Deltour, Emmanuelle Jacquot, Hanaya Raad, E. Garry",10.3390/vaccines12060651,https://doi.org/10.3390/vaccines12060651,Vaccines,11,2024,no,"The study is focused on at-risk groups during the 2022 outbreak, specifically men who have sex with men and transgender women. It appears to be limited search to these adult groups and does not mention non-adults or pregnant women.",yes,The study reports on both vaccine effectiveness and safety outcomes such as pericarditis.,yes,This is a primary research study using a historical cohort design based on administrative healthcare data.,yes,The study involves human subjects (MSM and transgender women).,no,"The paper defines its population as men who have sex with men and transgender women. It does not include or mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Because the study did not include the target populations (pregnant women or children), it does not meet the criteria for vaccination during those specific life stages.",yes,The study involves the MVA–BN vaccine.,Exclude,"This study evaluates the MVA-BN vaccine's effectiveness and safety in a cohort of MSM and transgender women. However, it does not include the target populations of pregnant women, children, or adolescents required by the inclusion criteria.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""men who have sex with men (MSM) and transgender women, the most affected population during the 2022 mpox outbreak.""",,"  - ""MVA–BN vaccine effectiveness against mpox disease was 89%""
  - ""One pericarditis adverse event of special interest was observed""",,"  - ""men who have sex with men (MSM) and transgender women, the most affected population during the 2022 mpox outbreak.""",,"  - ""men who have sex with men (MSM) and transgender women, the most affected population during the 2022 mpox outbreak.""",,"  - ""men who have sex with men (MSM) and transgender women, the most affected population during the 2022 mpox outbreak.""",,"  - ""men who have sex with men (MSM) and transgender women, the most affected population during the 2022 mpox outbreak.""",,"  - ""MVA–BN vaccine""",
Both Smallpox and Mpox Vaccine Reduce Mpox Disease Severity and Symptoms,"Abstract Background Data on the impact of completing 2 doses of the modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine and/or smallpox vaccination in childhood on Mpox disease severity remain limited. During the last months of 2024, Germany faced a second Mpox wave, providing the opportunity to study the impact of different vaccine regimens including smallpox vaccination. Methods Retrospective analysis of all cases diagnosed at 17 participating German centers 2022–2024. Cases were grouped according to smallpox vaccine history: “unvaccinated,” “incomplete” (vaccinations only during childhood and/or only one MVA BN), “complete” (2 MVA BN > 1 month prior to Mpox diagnosis). Disease severity was assessed by a 7-item Mpox Severity Scoring System (MPOX-SSS). Results Of 273 Mpox infections, 42% occurred in men who have sex with men (MSM) living with HIV (median CD4 724 cells/µl, 91% <50 HIV-RNA copies/ml), 58% occurred in MSM without HIV, most of them using HIV PrEP. Median age was 38 years (IQR 30–44). Vaccinated individuals demonstrated lower MPOX-SSS scores compared to unvaccinated individuals (mean score 5.46 vs 7.15, P < .001). Vaccinated individuals also had less fever (23% vs 59%, P < .001), headaches/chills (29% vs 54%, P < .001), and swollen lymph nodes (38% vs 56%, P = .02). Conclusions Both incomplete and complete vaccine courses including smallpox vaccination during childhood appear to reduce Mpox disease severity and symptoms. In settings of limited vaccine resources, unvaccinated risk groups should be prioritized.","Christian Hoffmann, S. Breitschwerdt, Markus Bickel, Christina Appelhans, Heiko Jessen, S. Franz, Henning Kolarikal, I. Krznaric, Johannes Lenz, C. Schuler, Axel Baumgarten, Timo Schultheiß, S. Esser, Marc Biller, Luca D Stein, Max Bender, M. Rausch, R. Baumann, U. Haars, Irmela Dialer, P. Khaykin, J. Roider, Christoph Wyen, Christoph Spinner, L. Wagner, Guido Schäfer, Christoph Boesecke",10.1093/ofid/ofag201,https://doi.org/10.1093/ofid/ofag201,Open Forum Infectious Diseases,0,2026,yes,"The study includes adults, but specifically focuses on men who have sex with men (MSM) and individuals living with HIV. While most are adults, the focus is on these specific populations rather than exclusively 'healthy' adults.",yes,The study reports on disease severity and symptoms using the MPOX-SSS score.,yes,This is a retrospective analysis (cohort study) of cases diagnosed at multiple centers.,yes,The study evaluates human patients diagnosed at clinical centers.,no,"The study population consists of adults with a median age of 38. There is no mention of current pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as current study participants, despite childhood vaccination history being a factor.",yes,The study specifically evaluates participants who received smallpox vaccination during childhood.,yes,The study involves both the MVA-BN vaccine and historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This retrospective study examines the effect of childhood smallpox vaccination and MVA-BN on Mpox severity in an adult population. Although it involves childhood vaccination history, the current study participants are adults (median age 38) and the study fails the requirement to include pregnant women, neonates, or children as participants.",Target Population,Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Of 273 Mpox infections, 42% occurred in men who have sex with men (MSM) living with HIV""
  - ""58% occurred in MSM without HIV,""",,"  - ""Vaccinated individuals demonstrated lower MPOX-SSS scores compared to unvaccinated individuals""",,"  - ""Retrospective analysis of all cases diagnosed at 17 participating German centers 2022–2024.""",,"  - ""Retrospective analysis of all cases diagnosed at 17 participating German centers 2022–2024.""",,"  - ""Median age was 38 years (IQR 30–44).""
  - ""Data on the impact of completing 2 doses of the modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine and/or smallpox vaccination in childhood on Mpox disease severity remain limited.""
  - ""incomplete” (vaccinations only during childhood and/or only one MVA BN),""",,"  - ""Data on the impact of completing 2 doses of the modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine and/or smallpox vaccination in childhood on Mpox disease severity remain limited.""
