A protein-based smallpox vaccine protects non-human primates from a lethal monkeypox virus challenge,"Concerns about infections caused by orthopoxviruses, such as variola and monkeypox viruses, drive ongoing efforts to develop novel smallpox vaccines that are both effective and safe to use in diverse populations. A subunit smallpox vaccine comprising vaccinia virus membrane proteins A33, B5, L1, A27 and aluminum hydroxide (alum) ± CpG was administered to non-human primates, which were subsequently challenged with a lethal intravenous dose of monkeypox virus. Alum adjuvanted vaccines provided only partial protection but the addition of CpG provided full protection that was associated with a more homogeneous antibody response and stronger IgG1 responses. These results indicate that it is feasible to develop a highly effective subunit vaccine against orthopoxvirus infections as a safer alternative to live vaccinia virus vaccination.","G. Buchman, Matthew E. Cohen, Yuhong Xiao, N. Richardson-Harman, P. Silvera, L. Detolla, H. Davis, R. Eisenberg, G. Cohen, S. Isaacs",10.1016/j.vaccine.2010.07.030,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2010.07.030,Vaccine,87,2010,no,The population described consists of mon-human primates rather than any human population group.,yes,The study reports on the effectiveness of the vaccine in preventing death from monkeypox virus challenge.,yes,This is a primary research study involving an experimental challenge in animal models.,no,The study is an animal experiment (non-human primates) rather than a human study.,no,"The study does not include pregnant women, neonates, or children, but rather non-human primates.",no,"Vaccination occurred in animal models, not during human pregnancy, infancy, or childhood.",yes,The study evaluates a subunit smallpox vaccine using vaccinia virus membrane proteins.,Exclude,"This study is excluded because it is an animal study conducted on non-human primates. It does not involve human clinical participants or the required target populations of pregnant women, children, or neonates.","Population Focus, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""non-human primates,""",,"  - ""administered to non-human primates, which were subsequently challenged with a lethal intravenous dose of monkeypox virus.""",,"  - ""administered to non-human primates, which were subsequently challenged with a lethal intravenous dose of monkeypox virus.""",,"  - ""non-human primates,""
