Mpox and Chickenpox Co-infection: Case Series from Southern Nigeria.,"INTRODUCTION
We describe clinico-epidemiologic characteristics of mpox∕chickenpox co-infection in Nigeria.
METHODOLOGY
A retrospective cohort analysis of confirmed mpox cases in Nigeria from January 2022 to March 2023. Mpox and chickenpox were confirmed by RT-PCR.
RESULTS
56/94 (60.0%) suspected cases had confirmed mpox of whom 16 (28.6%) had chickenpox co-infection. Their median age was 29 (20-37) years, males were 24 (60.7%), 6 (10.7%) were bisexual, and 5 (8.9%) died. Mpox/chickenpox co-infected patients had more complications than mpox mono-infected cases (56.3% vs 22.5%, p = 0.015).
CONCLUSION
The high frequency of mpox/chickenpox co-infection argues for accelerated access to mpox and chickenpox vaccines in Africa.","Juliet I Mmerem, C. Umenzekwe, Mafuka S Johnson, A. Onukak, N. Chika-Igwenyi, Kyrian S Chukwu, C. Onyeaghala, J. J. Ozougwu, D. Alasia, Odianosen Ehiakhamen, Henry M Nwankwo, O. Ezejiofor, Uche S Unigwe, M. Iroezindu",10.1093/infdis/jiad556,https://doi.org/10.1093/infdis/jiad556,Journal of Infectious Diseases,10,2023,yes,"The study focus is on patients with confirmed mpox and chickenpox co-infections, meaning they are not 'healthy' individuals.",no,The study reports on clinical complications and death related to infection rather than vaccine safety or vaccine-mediated prevention (effectiveness).,yes,"The study is a retrospective cohort analysis and case series, which are primary research designs.",yes,The study involves human clinical cases and epidemiological data.,no,"The study participants are adults with a median age of 29 and a range of 20-37 years, which excludes children and adolescents (<18 years). There is no mention of pregnant women or neonates.",no,There is no mention of participants receiving a vaccine during the study period; the authors actually recommend future 'accelerated access' to vaccines.,no,The study does not involve the administration or evaluation of vaccines.,Exclude,This study is a retrospective cohort analysis of mpox and chickenpox co-infection in adults. It fails several inclusion criteria because it does not involve the administration or evaluation of vaccines and its study population (ages 20-37) does not include the target pediatric or adolescent groups.,"Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""A retrospective cohort analysis of confirmed mpox cases in Nigeria from January 2022 to March 2023.""
