"Interim Clinical Treatment Considerations for Severe Manifestations of Mpox — United States, February 2023","Monkeypox (mpox) is a disease caused by infection with Monkeypox virus (MPXV), an Orthopoxvirus (OPXV) in the same genus as Variola virus, which causes smallpox. During 2022, a global outbreak involving mpox clade IIb was recognized, primarily among gay, bisexual, and other men who have sex with men.* Most affected patients have been immunocompetent and experienced ≤10 rash lesions (1). CDC has recommended supportive care including pain control.† However, some patients have experienced severe mpox manifestations, including ocular lesions, neurologic complications, myopericarditis, complications associated with mucosal (oral, rectal, genital, and urethral) lesions, and uncontrolled viral spread due to moderate or severe immunocompromise, particularly advanced HIV infection (2). Therapeutic medical countermeasures (MCMs) are Food and Drug Administration (FDA)-regulated drugs and biologics that are predominantly stockpiled by the U.S. government; MCMs developed for smallpox preparedness or shown to be effective against other OPXVs (i.e., tecovirimat, brincidofovir, cidofovir, trifluridine ophthalmic solution, and vaccinia immune globulin intravenous [VIGIV]) have been used to treat severe mpox. During May 2022-January 2023, CDC provided more than 250 U.S. mpox consultations. This report synthesizes data from animal models, MCM use for human cases of related OPXV, unpublished data, input from clinician experts, and experience during consultations (including follow-up) to provide interim clinical treatment considerations. Randomized controlled trials and other carefully controlled research studies are needed to evaluate the effectiveness of MCMs for treating human mpox. Until data gaps are filled, the information presented in this report represents the best available information concerning the effective use of MCMs and should be used to guide decisions about MCM use for mpox patients.","A. Rao, Caroline A. Schrodt, F. Minhaj, M. Waltenburg, Shama Cash-Goldwasser, Yon C. Yu, B. Petersen, C. Hutson, I. Damon",10.15585/mmwr.mm7209a4,https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm7209a4,MMWR. Morbidity and mortality weekly report,94,2023,yes,"The study focuses on patients with severe manifestations of Mpox, including those with advanced HIV infection, and is not limited to healthy adults.",yes,The report discusses the effectiveness of medical countermeasures (MCMs) for treating severe mpox manifestations.,no,The paper is a synthesis of expert opinion and experience to provide clinical treatment considerations rather than a primary research study or a formal systematic review/meta-analysis.,yes,The report synthesizes data from animal models but also human cases and clinician consultations.,no,"The study primarily focuses on gay, bisexual, and other men who have sex with men and does not mention Inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents in the provided text.",no,"The study does not describe vaccination during pregnancy, childhood, or the neonatal period, but rather focuses on MCMs for treatment.",no,"The study discusses therapeutic medical countermeasures (tecovirimat, VIGIV, etc.) rather than vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This report provides clinical treatment considerations for severe mpox using therapeutic countermeasures like tecovirimat and VIGIV. It fails several inclusion criteria as it does not focus on pregnant women or children, does not involve mpox/smallpox vaccines, and is not a primary research study or evidence synthesis study.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Target Population,0.1,,,"  - ""This report synthesizes data from animal models, MCM use for human cases of related OPXV, unpublished data, input from clinician experts, and experience during consultations (including follow-up) to provide interim clinical treatment considerations.""",,"  - ""This report synthesizes data from animal models, MCM use for human cases of related OPXV, unpublished data, input from clinician experts, and experience during consultations (including follow-up) to provide interim clinical treatment considerations.""",,"  - ""This report synthesizes data from animal models, MCM use for human cases of related OPXV, unpublished data, input from clinician experts, and experience during consultations (including follow-up) to provide interim clinical treatment considerations.""",,"  - ""During 2022, a global outbreak involving mpox clade IIb was recognized, primarily among gay, bisexual, and other men who have sex with men.*""",,"  - ""This report synthesizes data from animal models, MCM use for human cases of related OPXV, unpublished data, input from clinician experts, and experience during consultations (including follow-up) to provide interim clinical treatment considerations.""",,,
Questions on Immunization and Vaccination and Short Answers,"Following the emergence of COVID-19 in December 2019, which dramatically transformed our lives, concerns arose regarding the risk of a new infectious agent that may impact all of our lives, particularly after the emergence of monkeypox cases in 2022 in geographical locations where it is not supposed to be seen. We have noticed that pediatricians and all healthcare professionals working on children’s health, in particular, need to refresh their knowledge of this zoonotic disease in greater depth due to information received that it may severely impact children and the fetus throughout the intrauterine period. Essentially a zoonotic infection, monkeypox is a disease caused by the pox virus and transferred from animals to humans. It can also spread from person to person through close contact. Typically, it has symptoms similar to a milder form of smallpox, and spontaneous recovery can be seen in two to four weeks (1). Vesicular rash, fever, weakness, severe fatigue, muscle pain, and generalized lymphadenopathy are the hallmark symptoms. As previously stated, children, fetuses, and people with immunodeficiency or immunosuppression may experience a more severe course of the disease (2). Monkeypox is endemic in 10 countries in Central Africa (especially the Democratic Republic of the Congo) and West Africa (especially Cameroon and Nigeria) (3). Its mortality rate is 3% in Central Africa and 1% in West Africa, based on the reports after the 1980s. It was first identified in monkeys in 1958 at the Statens Serum Institute in Copenhagen, Denmark, which is why it is also referred to as monkeypox. The first human case was reported in 1970 in the Democratic Republic of Congo. By 2022, cases were identified in all continents except Antarctica, and as of August 2022, a total of 28.219 cases were reported. According to the Ministry of Health, five cases have been reported in our country.","Pervin Özelçi, Aslıhan Coşkun, A. Kara",10.5578/ced.20229612,https://doi.org/10.5578/ced.20229612,Journal of Pediatric Infection,0,2022,yes,The paper specifically highlights knowledge needs for pediatricians and risks to children and fetuses.,no,"While the study mentions general disease mortality, it does not report safety or effectiveness outcomes for mpox vaccination.",no,"The paper appears to be an educational Q&A or narrative review for clinicians rather than a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or a systematic review.",yes,The article focuses on human disease transmission and risks.,yes,The text discusses risks associated with children and fetuses.,no,The study does not describe participants receiving a vaccine; it is a general information overview.,maybe,"The title mentions immunization and vaccination, but the provided text does not specify vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,The paper is a narrative overview or Q&A specifically intended to refresh the knowledge of clinicians regarding monkeypox history and risks in children. It does not constitute primary research or a systematic review and provides no data on vaccination outcomes.,"Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Vaccination Timing",Vaccination Timing,0.1,"  - ""pediatricians and all healthcare professionals working on children’s health, in particular, need to refresh their knowledge of this zoonotic disease""
  - ""children, fetuses, and people with immunodeficiency or immunosuppression may experience a more severe course of the disease""",,"  - ""Its mortality rate is 3% in Central Africa and 1% in West Africa, based on the reports after the 1980s.""",,"  - ""pediatricians and all healthcare professionals working on children’s health, in particular, need to refresh their knowledge of this zoonotic disease""",,"  - ""pediatricians and all healthcare professionals working on children’s health, in particular, need to refresh their knowledge of this zoonotic disease""",,"  - ""children, fetuses, and people with immunodeficiency or immunosuppression may experience a more severe course of the disease""",,,,,
"Ending the neglect of paediatric, maternal, and congenital mpox.","In The Lancet Global Health, Nuria Sanchez Clemente and colleagues present their global systematic review and meta-analysis on the epidemiology, prevention, and treatment of paediatric, maternal, and congenital mpox.1Sanchez Clemente N Coles C Paixao E et al.Paediatric, maternal, and congenital mpox: a systematic review and meta-analysis.Lancet Glob Health. 2024; (published online Feb 21.)https://doi.org/10.1016/S2214-109X(23)00607-1Summary Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (1) Google Scholar Human mpox has been reported from west and central Africa since 1970 and, in these endemic settings, children younger than 18 years remain an important affected demographic. From 1970 to 2022, 39–80% of endemic mpox cases reported to WHO were 0–17-year-old children.2Sam-Agudu NA Martyn-Dickens C Ewa AU A global update of mpox (monkeypox) in children.Curr Opin Pediatr. 2023; 35: 193-200Crossref PubMed Scopus (5) Google Scholar During the 2022–23 global mpox outbreak, children and pregnant women constituted only 1·3% (1150 of 88 051) and 0·06% (54 of 87 036), respectively, of cumulative reported cases as of November, 2023.3WHO2022–23 mpox (monkeypox) outbreak: global trends.https://worldhealthorg.shinyapps.io/mpx_global/#2_Global_situation_updateDate: 2024Date accessed: January 27, 2024Google Scholar In the same timeframe, African mpox cases constituted 2% of global but at least 30% of all paediatric cases.3WHO2022–23 mpox (monkeypox) outbreak: global trends.https://worldhealthorg.shinyapps.io/mpx_global/#2_Global_situation_updateDate: 2024Date accessed: January 27, 2024Google Scholar The global outbreak triggered a surge in mpox research and vaccine development, especially in highly resourced, non-endemic countries. Unfortunately, the availability of rigorous data on paediatric mpox epidemiology, prevention, treatment, and case fatality continues to be limited, particularly in endemic African countries.2Sam-Agudu NA Martyn-Dickens C Ewa AU A global update of mpox (monkeypox) in children.Curr Opin Pediatr. 2023; 35: 193-200Crossref PubMed Scopus (5) Google Scholar, 3WHO2022–23 mpox (monkeypox) outbreak: global trends.https://worldhealthorg.shinyapps.io/mpx_global/#2_Global_situation_updateDate: 2024Date accessed: January 27, 2024Google Scholar, 4Africa Centres for Disease Control and PreventionDisease outbreaks: mpox.https://africacdc.org/disease-outbreak/Date: 2023Date accessed: January 22, 2024Google Scholar To help narrow this data gap, Sanchez Clemente and colleagues systematically identified and analysed 61 mpox studies conducted in ten endemic African and six non-endemic countries between 1972 and 2023.1Sanchez Clemente N Coles C Paixao E et al.Paediatric, maternal, and congenital mpox: a systematic review and meta-analysis.Lancet Glob Health. 2024; (published online Feb 21.)https://doi.org/10.1016/S2214-109X(23)00607-1Summary Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (1) Google Scholar Included studies comprised outbreak and case reports, case series, and surveillance and observational studies. 53 studies reported on 2123 children aged 18 years and younger, out of which 39 studies reported on individual outcomes for only 101 children; viral clade was available for just six (7%) of 89 endemic and four (33%) of 12 non-endemic cases. Seven studies described maternal or congenital outcomes from 32 cases (75% from the USA, no deaths reported). Two studies reported on the use of live, non-replicating vaccine for 28 children during mpox outbreaks in the UK and USA with no reported side-effects or mpox development, and six observational studies described well tolerated tecovirimat treatment among 21 children and 12 pregnant women. The authors noted the absence of published randomised controlled trial findings on safety or efficacy for any mpox vaccine or therapeutic agent in these populations. Despite the absence of randomised trials and the low number of paediatric and pregnant cases or study participants, this analysis generated some key findings: the top three clinical features of paediatric mpox (74 [86%] of 86 analysed cases from endemic countries) were: rash (100%), fever (73%), and lymphadenopathy (47%); the pooled case fatality ratio (CFR) estimate for paediatric mpox was 11% (95% CI 4–20); the most common cause of complications in paediatric mpox was secondary bacterial infection; and available data showed a 50% fetal loss rate in 12 mpox-affected pregnancies. Data from endemic countries have established that clade 1 (previously Congo Basin or central African clade) mpox infections have a higher CFR (around 11%) than clade 2 (previously west African clade; <1% to around 4%).5Sklenovská N Van Ranst M Emergence of monkeypox as the most important orthopoxvirus infection in humans.Front Public Health. 2018; 6: 241Crossref PubMed Google Scholar, 6Bunge EM Hoet B Chen L et al.The changing epidemiology of human monkeypox—a potential threat? A systematic review.PLoS Negl Trop Dis. 2022; 16e0010141Crossref PubMed Scopus (909) Google Scholar The CFR for the 2022 clade 2b global outbreak is approximately 0·2%.3WHO2022–23 mpox (monkeypox) outbreak: global trends.https://worldhealthorg.shinyapps.io/mpx_global/#2_Global_situation_updateDate: 2024Date accessed: January 27, 2024Google Scholar Beyond mpox virus subtype, Sanchez Clemente and colleagues note the lack of evidence on the contribution of concurrent and sequential infections (eg measles, chickenpox,7Mmerem JI Umenzekwe CC Johnson MS et al.Mpox and chickenpox co-infection: case series from southern Nigeria.J Infect Dis. 2023; (published online Dec 7.)https://doi.org/10.1093/infdis/jiad556Crossref PubMed Google Scholar HIV,8Yinka-Ogunleye A Dalhat M Akinpelu A et al.Mpox (monkeypox) risk and mortality associated with HIV infection: a national case-control study in Nigeria.BMJ Glob Health. 2023; 8e013126Crossref PubMed Google Scholar and malaria) to poor paediatric and maternal mpox outcomes.1Sanchez Clemente N Coles C Paixao E et al.Paediatric, maternal, and congenital mpox: a systematic review and meta-analysis.Lancet Glob Health. 2024; (published online Feb 21.)https://doi.org/10.1016/S2214-109X(23)00607-1Summary Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (1) Google Scholar Other knowledge gaps remain, including: paediatric and maternal mpox CFR in endemic versus non-endemic countries; the epidemiology and clinical outcomes of clade 1 versus clade 2 subtype infections in paediatric and pregnant populations; rates and risk factors for hospitalisation, long hospital stay, severe disease, and death; maternal and fetal risk factors for congenital mpox; and safe and effective therapeutics for maternal and pediatric mpox prevention and treatment. WHO declared the mpox pandemic over on May 11, 2023. Reported cases have continued to decline substantially, from a peak of more than 30 000 monthly cases in August, 2022, to an average of fewer than 850 monthly cases as of November, 2023.3WHO2022–23 mpox (monkeypox) outbreak: global trends.https://worldhealthorg.shinyapps.io/mpx_global/#2_Global_situation_updateDate: 2024Date accessed: January 27, 2024Google Scholar Nonetheless, endemic African countries continue to report cases, particularly from DR Congo, which experienced a large clade 1 outbreak starting in March, 2023, that was linked, for the first time, to sexual contact.9Kibungu EM Vakaniaki EH Kinganda-Lusamaki E et al.Clade I-associated mpox cases associated with sexual contact, the Democratic Republic of the Congo.Emerg Infect Dis. 2024; 30: 172-176Crossref PubMed Scopus (8) Google Scholar As of November, 2023, WHO reports nearly 14 000 suspected cases and 607 deaths in 2023 for DR Congo, with children younger than 15 years comprising 70% of suspected cases and deaths.3WHO2022–23 mpox (monkeypox) outbreak: global trends.https://worldhealthorg.shinyapps.io/mpx_global/#2_Global_situation_updateDate: 2024Date accessed: January 27, 2024Google Scholar Given the downward trend of the global outbreak, we find it prudent for large studies on mpox to focus on endemic countries and prioritise paediatric and pregnant populations. The analysis by Sanchez Clemente and colleagues adds much-needed evidence to an mpox data vacuum. However, as the authors rightly stated, “(t)he scope of the results is limited by the availability and quality of the datasets that have been published.”1Sanchez Clemente N Coles C Paixao E et al.Paediatric, maternal, and congenital mpox: a systematic review and meta-analysis.Lancet Glob Health. 2024; (published online Feb 21.)https://doi.org/10.1016/S2214-109X(23)00607-1Summary Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (1) Google Scholar The mpox pandemic might be declared over, but mpox remains an important infectious disease of public health concern, especially in west and central Africa. We call for sustained investments in multi-country clinical trials and rigorous observational studies prioritising paediatric and maternal mpox to further address this longstanding data vacuum. NAS-A reports funding from the European Commission to her institution to conduct mpox studies in Nigeria. DO declares no competing interests. Paediatric, maternal, and congenital mpox: a systematic review and meta-analysisOur review highlights critical knowledge gaps in the epidemiology, prevention, and treatment of mpox in children and pregnant individuals, especially those residing in endemic countries. Increased funding, international collaboration, and equitable research is needed to inform mpox control strategies tailored for at-risk communities in endemic countries. Full-Text PDF Open Access","N. A. Sam-Agudu, D. Ogoina",10.1016/S2214-109X(24)00054-8,https://doi.org/10.1016/S2214-109X(24)00054-8,Lancet Global Health,4,2024,yes,"The focus of the paper is on paediatric and maternal mpox, specifically children under 18 and pregnant women, not healthy adults.",yes,"The paper mentions safety (no side-effects) and effectiveness (prevention of mpox), as well as case fatality ratio (CFR).",no,This paper is a commentary/editorial rather than a primary research study or a formal evidence synthesis study (systematic review/meta-analysis).,yes,The paper discusses human epidemiological data and clinical case outcomes.,yes,The study discusses children under 18 and cases of maternal and congenital mpox.,yes,The paper reports on outcomes for 28 children who received vaccines during mpox outbreaks.,yes,"The paper identifies the use of a 'live, non-replicating vaccine', which refers to current vaccinia-based mpox vaccines.",Exclude,"The paper is a commentary that summarizes a systematic review on paediatric and maternal mpox populations. Although it mentions vaccine safety and effectiveness findings in children, it is excluded because it is an editorial commentary rather than a primary study or the systematic review itself.",Study Design,Study Design,0.1,"  - ""children younger than 18 years remain an important affected demographic.""
