Effectiveness of the SARS-CoV-2 mRNA Vaccines in Pregnant Women,"Pregnancy may increase the risk of adverse maternal and neonatal outcomes in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Effectiveness of SARS-CoV-2 vaccines in pregnant women is not known. Using a test-negative case-control study, we determined the vaccine effectiveness of mRNA vaccines in preventing confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection in pregnant women at a national referral hospital, which handles > 75% of the deliveries in Qatar. Among 2,020 pregnant women who met the study criteria, 397 had a positive SARS-CoV-2 RT-PCR and 1,623 had a negative test. Vaccine effectiveness ≥ 14 days after the second dose was 67.7% (95%CI 30.5–86.9), while vaccine effectiveness ≥ 14 days after the first dose but before the second dose was 40.3% (95%CI 0.0-80.4). There were nine severe/critical disease cases, and no deaths in the PCR-positive pregnant women, all among unvaccinated. The mRNA vaccines provide high level of protection against documented SARS-CoV-2 infection, which supports including pregnant women in vaccination campaigns.","A. Butt, A. Abou-Samra, H. Chemaitelly, A. A. Khal, P. Coyle, H. Saleh, A. Kaleeckal, A. Latif, R. Bertollini, L. Abu-Raddad",10.21203/rs.3.rs-622782/v1,https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-622782/v1,,2,2021,yes,"The study specifically focuses on pregnant women, not healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study reports on the prevention of SARS-CoV-2 infection, not mpox infection or outcomes related to mpox.",yes,"The study is a test-negative case-control study, which is a type of primary research.",yes,"The study involves human participants (2,020 pregnant women).",yes,The study participants are pregnant women.,yes,"The study evaluates vaccine effectiveness in pregnant women, implying they received the vaccine during or shortly before pregnancy to measure its effectiveness during that period.",no,"The study evaluates mRNA vaccines for SARS-CoV-2, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,This study focuses on the effectiveness of mRNA vaccines against SARS-CoV-2 in a population of pregnant women. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not involve mpox vaccines or mpox-related outcomes.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Among 2,020 pregnant women who met the study criteria,""",,"  - ""Effectiveness of SARS-CoV-2 vaccines in pregnant women is not known.""
