Safety of BNT162b2 and CoronaVac during pregnancy on birth outcomes and neonatal mortality: a cohort study from Brazil,"Abstract Background COVID-19 vaccines have been shown to protect pregnant individuals against mild and severe COVID-19 outcomes. However, limited safety data are available for inactivated (CoronaVac) and mRNA (BNT162b2) vaccines during pregnancy regarding their effect on birth outcomes and neonatal mortality, especially in low- and middle-income countries. Methods We conducted a retrospective population-based cohort study in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil, with 17 513 singleton live births conceived between 15 May 2021 and 23 October 2021. The primary exposure was maternal vaccination with CoronaVac or mRNA BNT162b2 vaccines and sub-analyses were performed by the gestational trimester of the first dose and the number of doses given during pregnancy. The outcomes were pre-term birth (PTB), small for gestational age (SGA), low birthweight (LBW), low Apgar 5 and neonatal death. We used the Cox model to estimate the hazard ratio (HR) with a 95% CI and applied the inverse probability of treatment weights to generate adjusted HRs. Results We found no significant increase in the risk of PTB (HR: 0.98; 95% CI 0.88, 1.10), SGA (HR: 1.09; 95% CI 0.96, 1.27), LBW (HR: 1.00; 95% CI 0.88, 1.14), low Apgar 5 (HR: 0.81; 95% CI 0.55, 1.22) or neonatal death (HR: 0.88; 95% CI 0.56, 1.48) in women vaccinated with CoronaVac or BNT162b2 vaccines. These findings were consistent across sub-analyses stratified by the gestational trimester of the first dose and the number of doses given during pregnancy. We found mild yet consistent protection against PTB in women who received different vaccine platforms during the third trimester of pregnancy (any vaccines, HR: 0.78; 95% CI 0.63, 0.98; BNT162b2, HR: 0.75; 95% CI 0.59, 0.99). Conclusions This study provides evidence that COVID-19 vaccination in all trimesters of pregnancy, irrespective of the vaccine type, is safe and does not increase the risk of adverse birth outcomes or neonatal deaths.","Pilar Tavares Veras Florentino, T. Cerqueira-Silva, Luciana Freire De Carvalho, Flávia Jôse Oliveira Alves, V. de Araujo Oliveira, Gislani Mateus Oliveira Aguilar, Rodrigo De Sousa Prado, Daniel Soranz, N. Pearce, V. Boaventura, Guilherme Loreiro Werneck, Gerson Oliveira Penna, Mauricio Lima Barreto, Márcio Henrique De Oliveira Garcia, Manoel Barral-Netto, Enny Santos da Paixão",10.1093/ije/dyad120,https://doi.org/10.1093/ije/dyad120,International Journal of Epidemiology,9,2023,yes,The study specifically focuses on pregnant individuals.,no,"The study reports safety outcomes (birth outcomes and neonatal mortality), but these are for COVID-19 vaccines, not for mpox prevention or vaccinia-based vaccines.",yes,The study is a retrospective population-based cohort study.,yes,"The study is a human study involving a cohort of 17,513 live births.",yes,The study includes pregnant women and neonates.,yes,The participants received the vaccines during pregnancy.,no,"The study investigates BNT162b2 (mRNA) and CoronaVac (inactivated) COVID-19 vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study investigates the safety of COVID-19 vaccines (BNT162b2 and CoronaVac) in a cohort of pregnant women. It does not involve vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines, thus failing the required vaccine type criterion.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""limited safety data are available for inactivated (CoronaVac) and mRNA (BNT162b2) vaccines during pregnancy""",,"  - ""outcomes were pre-term birth (PTB), small for gestational age (SGA), low birthweight (LBW), low Apgar 5 and neonatal death.""",,"  - ""We conducted a retrospective population-based cohort study in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil, with 17 513 singleton live births""",,"  - ""We conducted a retrospective population-based cohort study in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil, with 17 513 singleton live births""",,"  - ""We conducted a retrospective population-based cohort study in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil, with 17 513 singleton live births""",,"  - ""limited safety data are available for inactivated (CoronaVac) and mRNA (BNT162b2) vaccines during pregnancy""",,"  - ""primary exposure was maternal vaccination with CoronaVac or mRNA BNT162b2 vaccines""",
"Safety, immunogenicity, and effectiveness of COVID-19 vaccines for pregnant persons: A protocol for systematic review and meta analysis","Introduction: Numerous vaccines have been evaluated and approved for coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19). Since pregnant persons have been excluded from most clinical trials of COVID-19 vaccines, sufficient data regarding the safety of these vaccines for the pregnant person and their fetus have rarely been available at the time of product licensure. However, as COVID-19 vaccines have been deployed, data on the safety, reactogenicity, immunogenicity, and efficacy of COVID-19 vaccines for pregnant persons and neonates are becoming increasingly available. A living systematic review and meta-analysis of the safety and effectiveness of COVID-19 vaccines for pregnant persons and newborns could provide the information necessary to help guide vaccine policy decisions. Methods and analysis: We aim to conduct a living systematic review and meta-analysis based on biweekly searches of medical databases (e.g., MEDLINE, EMBASE, CENTRAL) and clinical trial registries to systematically identify relevant studies of COVID-19 vaccines for pregnant persons. Pairs of reviewers will independently select, extract data, and conduct risk of bias assessments. We will include randomized clinical trials, quasi-experimental studies, cohort, case-control, cross-sectional studies, and case reports. Primary outcomes will be the safety, efficacy, and effectiveness of COVID-19 vaccines in pregnant persons, including neonatal outcomes. Secondary outcomes will be immunogenicity and reactogenicity. We will conduct paired meta-analyses, including prespecified subgroup and sensitivity analyses. We will use the grading of recommendations assessment, development, and evaluation approach to evaluate the certainty of evidence.","A. Ciapponi, M. Berrueta, J. Ballivian, Ariel Esteban Bardach, A. Mazzoni, S. Anderson, F. Argento, K. Bok, D. Comandé, Erin Goucher, B. Kampmann, E. Parker, F. Rodriguez-Cairoli, V. Santa María, Andy Stergachis, Gerald Voss, X. Xiong, S. Zaraa, F. Muñoz, R. Karron, S. Gottlieb, P. Buekens",10.1097/MD.0000000000032954,https://doi.org/10.1097/MD.0000000000032954,Medicine,5,2023,yes,The study focuses specifically on pregnant persons.,yes,"The study aims to evaluate safety, efficacy, and effectiveness outcomes.",no,"The document is a protocol for a systematic review and meta-analysis, not an completed evidence synthesis study or primary research study.",yes,The protocol describes a review of human studies.,yes,The target populations are pregnant persons and neonates.,yes,The study investigates vaccines deployed for pregnant persons.,no,"The study is exclusively focused on COVID-19 vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This paper is a protocol for a systematic review focused on COVID-19 vaccines rather than mpox or smallpox vaccines. Because the intervention (COVID-19 vaccines) and the study document type (protocol) do not meet the required criteria, it is excluded.","Study Design, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""COVID-19 vaccines for pregnant persons""",,"  - ""safety, efficacy, and effectiveness""",,,,"  - ""COVID-19 vaccines for pregnant persons""",,"  - ""pregnant persons and neonates""",,"  - ""COVID-19 vaccines for pregnant persons""",,"  - ""COVID-19 vaccines for pregnant persons""
  - ""coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19).""",
Modified Vaccinia Virus Ankara Protects Macaques against Respiratory Challenge with Monkeypox Virus,"ABSTRACT The use of classical smallpox vaccines based on vaccinia virus (VV) is associated with severe complications in both naïve and immune individuals. Modified vaccinia virus Ankara (MVA), a highly attenuated replication-deficient strain of VV, has been proven to be safe in humans and immunocompromised animals, and its efficacy against smallpox is currently being addressed. Here we directly compare the efficacies of MVA alone and in combination with classical VV-based vaccines in a cynomolgus macaque monkeypox model. The MVA-based smallpox vaccine protected macaques against a lethal respiratory challenge with monkeypox virus and is therefore an important candidate for the protection of humans against smallpox.","K. Stittelaar, G. van Amerongen, I. Kondova, T. Kuiken, Rob van Lavieren, F. Pistoor, H. Niesters, G. V. van Doornum, B. van der Zeijst, L. Mateo, P. Chaplin, A. Osterhaus",10.1128/JVI.79.12.7845-7851.2005,https://doi.org/10.1128/JVI.79.12.7845-7851.2005,Journal of Virology,210,2005,maybe,The study focuses on cynomolgus macaques rather than human adults.,yes,The study reports on protection against lethal respiratory challenge and vaccine efficacy in a animal model.,yes,The study is a primary research investigation using an animal model.,no,"This is an animal study conducted in cynomolgus macaques, not a human study.",no,"The study does not include human participants and therefore does not include pregnant women, neonates, or children.",no,"Because the study uses macaques rather than humans, it does not evaluate vaccination during human pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,The study evaluates Modified vaccinia virus Ankara (MVA) and VV-based vaccines.,Exclude,"This study is an animal research paper evaluating the efficacy of MVA smallpox vaccines in a cynomolgus macaque monkeypox model. It fails the mandatory criteria for human study types and inclusion of the target human populations (pregnant women, children, or neonates).","Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""efficacies of MVA alone and in combination with classical VV-based vaccines in a cynomolgus macaque monkeypox model.""",,"  - ""MVA-based smallpox vaccine protected macaques against a lethal respiratory challenge with monkeypox virus""",,"  - ""efficacies of MVA alone and in combination with classical VV-based vaccines in a cynomolgus macaque monkeypox model.""",,"  - ""efficacies of MVA alone and in combination with classical VV-based vaccines in a cynomolgus macaque monkeypox model.""",,"  - ""efficacies of MVA alone and in combination with classical VV-based vaccines in a cynomolgus macaque monkeypox model.""",,"  - ""efficacies of MVA alone and in combination with classical VV-based vaccines in a cynomolgus macaque monkeypox model.""",,"  - ""efficacies of MVA alone and in combination with classical VV-based vaccines in a cynomolgus macaque monkeypox model.""
