Association of Inadvertent 9-Valent Human Papillomavirus Vaccine in Pregnancy With Spontaneous Abortion and Adverse Birth Outcomes,"This cohort study examines the risks for spontaneous abortion, preterm birth, small-for-gestational age birth, and major structural birth defects among female teenagers and adults who received the human papillomavirus vaccine.","E. Kharbanda, G. Vazquez-Benitez, M. DeSilva, A. Naleway, N. Klein, R. Hechter, J. Glanz, J. Donahue, L. Jackson, Sangini S. Sheth, Victoria R. Greenberg, L. Panagiotakopoulos, A. Mba-Jonas, H. Lipkind",10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2021.4340,https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2021.4340,JAMA Network Open,25,2021,yes,The study includes pregnant women and teenagers.,yes,The study reports on adverse birth outcomes including spontaneous abortion and birth defects.,yes,The study is described as a cohort study.,yes,The participants are human female teenagers and adults.,yes,The study specifically focuses on female teenagers and inadvertent vaccination during pregnancy.,yes,The study examines vaccination during pregnancy and among teenagers.,no,"The vaccine studied is the 'human papillomavirus vaccine' (HPV), not a vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This cohort study examines adverse birth outcomes in pregnant women and teenagers; however, it evaluates the human papillomavirus vaccine rather than the required mpox or smallpox vaccines. Consequently, it fails the intervention criterion.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""This cohort study examines the risks for spontaneous abortion, preterm birth, small-for-gestational age birth, and major structural birth defects""
