MINIMAL‐PRESSURE OXYGEN RECOMPRESSION TREATMENT OF DECOMPRESSION SICKNESS,"In the reported cases of fretal vaccinia, the stage of pregnancy at which the mother was vaccinate.d varied from three to 24 weeks. In only one case did the fretus survive the infection; in this instance vaccination was undertaken at the sixth month. In most cases primary vaccination had been performed, but in two instances in which revaccination was carried out, it resulted in fretal death. The interval between vaccination and delivery was four to 12 weeks, with a mean of eight weeks and a median of nine weeks. In six cases the tcetus was born alive; however, all but one of these infants succumbed. In a single instance twins were involved; these died before delivery. The case reported by Wielenga et alii (1961) from Leiden is of special interest, since the mother was not vaccinated, but gave birth to an affected infant. Her young son had been vaccinated when she was 18 weeks pregnant, and it was postulated that he had infected his mother, who then suffered an infection without eruption, which subsequently involved the fretus.",Ronald P. Palmer,10.5694/j.1326-5377.1968.tb29385.x,https://doi.org/10.5694/j.1326-5377.1968.tb29385.x,Medical Journal of Australia,0,1968,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women and fetuses rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.,yes,The study reports on fetal death and infant survival outcomes following vaccination.,yes,"The text describes a summary of reported cases and a specific historical case study, which fits the definition of a case series or evidence synthesis.",yes,The study describes clinical cases in human mothers and infants.,yes,The participants/subjects are pregnant women and their infants/fetuses.,yes,"Vaccination occurred during pregnancy, specifically between 3 and 24 weeks of gestation.",yes,The study explicitly mentions cases of 'fretal vaccinia' resulting from vaccination.,Include,"This paper provides a summary of clinical cases involving vaccinia vaccination in pregnant women. It reports safety outcomes such as fetal death and infant mortality, satisfying all specified inclusion criteria.",,,5,"  - ""In the reported cases of fretal vaccinia, the stage of pregnancy at which the mother was vaccinate.d varied from three to 24 weeks.""",,"  - ""In most cases primary vaccination had been performed, but in two instances in which revaccination was carried out, it resulted in fretal death.""
