"Genomic, virologic, and epidemiologic surveillance to track intrafamilial Mpox, Brazil","ABSTRACT Here, we report a fatal case of Orthopoxvirus monkeypox (MPV) infection in an immunocompromised patient with advanced HIV/AIDS, syphilis, and methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus co-infection during the 2022 mpox epidemic in Brazil. Clinical, serologic, molecular, viral isolation, and genomic investigations revealed intrafamilial transmission within a multigenerational household, including older women and children. Notably, a 3-year-old child without direct contact with the index case developed clinical signs of mpox and tested positive for MPV by PCR and viral isolation. Genomic sequencing and phylogenetic analyses confirmed the B.1.9 lineage and showed that all viral sequences clustered within the same transmission chain. Serologic assays detected OPV-neutralizing antibodies in an older woman. These findings underscore the potential for prolonged MPV replication and household spread beyond traditionally recognized risk groups, emphasizing the need for strengthened genomic and epidemiologic surveillance, particularly for immunocompromised individuals and their families and caregivers. IMPORTANCE Mpox expanded globally in 2022, but pediatric and household transmission remains poorly defined. We report a fatal infection in an immunocompromised patient with advanced HIV/AIDS during the epidemic in Brazil. Integrated clinical and genomic investigations revealed intrafamilial spread within a multigenerational household, including a 3-year-old child who had no direct contact with the index case. Viral genomes clustered in lineage B.1.9, and neutralizing antibodies were detected in an older woman and domestic animals. These findings highlight the potential for household transmission beyond recognized risk groups and underscore the need for strengthened surveillance. Mpox expanded globally in 2022, but pediatric and household transmission remains poorly defined. We report a fatal infection in an immunocompromised patient with advanced HIV/AIDS during the epidemic in Brazil. Integrated clinical and genomic investigations revealed intrafamilial spread within a multigenerational household, including a 3-year-old child who had no direct contact with the index case. Viral genomes clustered in lineage B.1.9, and neutralizing antibodies were detected in an older woman and domestic animals. These findings highlight the potential for household transmission beyond recognized risk groups and underscore the need for strengthened surveillance.","A. Andrade, Mariella Sousa Coêlho Maciel, A. G. Stoffella-Dutra, Sílvia Carvalho, A. Bentes, Paulo Emilio Tonaco Costa, Iago J S Domingos, P. H. Bastos e Silva, T. Adelino, F. Iani, L. Alcantara, G. Trindade, E. G. Kroon, M. Lima, M. Campos",10.1128/spectrum.03701-25,https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.03701-25,Microbiology spectrum,0,2026,yes,"The study includes an immunocompromised patient and a child, so it is not limited to healthy adults.",no,The study reports on the clinical outcomes of natural infection (death and clinical signs) rather than reporting on the safety or effectiveness of a vaccine in preventing infection or severe disease.,yes,This is a primary research study characterizing an outbreak and a specific case series/report.,yes,The study focuses on human cases of mpox within a household.,yes,The study specifically includes a 3-year-old child.,no,"The study does not involve the administration of vaccines or evaluate participants who received vaccines during the specified target periods (pregnancy, childhood, etc.); it focuses on natural transmission.",no,There is no mention of vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or specific evaluation of historical smallpox vaccines being used as an intervention.,Exclude,"This study describes the intrafamilial transmission of mpox in Brazil, including a case involving a 3-year-old child. While it includes the target pediatric population, it is an epidemiologic and genomic surveillance study of natural infection and does not involve or evaluate any mpox or smallpox vaccines.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccination Timing,0.1,"  - ""intrafamilial transmission within a multigenerational household, including older women and children.""
