"Estimated Effectiveness of JYNNEOS Vaccine in Preventing Mpox: A Multijurisdictional Case-Control Study — United States, August 19, 2022–March 31, 2023","As of March 31, 2023, more than 30,000 monkeypox (mpox) cases had been reported in the United States in an outbreak that has disproportionately affected gay, bisexual, and other men who have sex with men (MSM) and transgender persons (1). JYNNEOS vaccine (Modified Vaccinia Ankara vaccine, Bavarian Nordic) was approved by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) in 2019 for the prevention of smallpox and mpox via subcutaneous injection as a 2-dose series (0.5 mL per dose, administered 4 weeks apart) (2). To expand vaccine access, an Emergency Use Authorization was issued by FDA on August 9, 2022, for dose-sparing intradermal injection of JYNNEOS as a 2-dose series (0.1 mL per dose, administered 4 weeks apart) (3). Vaccination was available to persons with known or presumed exposure to a person with mpox (postexposure prophylaxis [PEP]), as well as persons at increased risk for mpox or who might benefit from vaccination (preexposure mpox prophylaxis [PrEP]) (4). Because information on JYNNEOS vaccine effectiveness (VE) is limited, a matched case-control study was conducted in 12 U.S. jurisdictions,† including nine Emerging Infections Program sites and three Epidemiology and Laboratory Capacity sites,§ to evaluate VE against mpox among MSM and transgender adults aged 18-49 years. During August 19, 2022-March 31, 2023, a total of 309 case-patients were matched to 608 control patients. Adjusted VE was 75.2% (95% CI = 61.2% to 84.2%) for partial vaccination (1 dose) and 85.9% (95% CI = 73.8% to 92.4%) for full vaccination (2 doses). Adjusted VE for full vaccination by subcutaneous, intradermal, and heterologous routes of administration was 88.9% (95% CI = 56.0% to 97.2%), 80.3% (95% CI = 22.9% to 95.0%), and 86.9% (95% CI = 69.1% to 94.5%), respectively. Adjusted VE for full vaccination among immunocompromised participants was 70.2% (95% CI = -37.9% to 93.6%) and among immunocompetent participants was 87.8% (95% CI = 57.5% to 96.5%). JYNNEOS is effective at reducing the risk for mpox. Because duration of protection of 1 versus 2 doses remains unknown, persons at increased risk for mpox exposure should receive the 2-dose series as recommended by the Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices (ACIP),¶ regardless of administration route or immunocompromise status.","Alexandra F Dalton, A. Diallo, A. Chard, Danielle L. Moulia, Nicholas P. Deputy, A. Fothergill, Ian Kracalik, C. Wegner, Tiffanie M. Markus, P. Pathela, W. Still, Sam Hawkins, A. Mangla, N. Ravi, Erin Licherdell, Amber Britton, R. Lynfield, M. Sutton, Amberjean P. Hansen, Gabriela S. Betancourt, Jemma V. Rowlands, S. Chai, R. Fisher, Phoebe Danza, M. Farley, J. Zipprich, Gregory Prahl, K. Wendel, L. Niccolai, J. Castilho, D. Payne, A. Cohn, Leora R. Feldstein, Kayla Saadeh, Robert E Snyder, Madeline Anderson, Vanessa Aryana Anguiano, J. Nadle, G. Rothrock, Sydney A. Jones, Lauren Duval, R. Herlihy, Ginger Stringer, Robyn Weber, Quyen Phan, L. Sosa, J. Meek, Michelle Lee, Allison S. Morrow, Christina Willut, J. Carlson, Kevin F. Kamis, Masayo Nishiyama, Gena Simien, J. Colasanti, Tamsin M. van der Woude, Roxanne Archer, L. Finn, J. Lam, Bret D. Moulton, Erin Peterson, R. Bolan, Gabriel Garcia-Lopez, K. Como‐Sabetti, Anna Ruff, Dakota Schneider, T. Robinson, Bridget J. Anderson, Kerianne Engesser, S. McGuire, Adam Rowe, Christopher Pride, Jaxon Mitchell, Yelena Tourkina, P. Cieslak, M. Fill, Caleb Wiedeman, G. Dumyati, C. Felsen, J. Lewnard, Bentley Akoko, Kristyne Mansilla-Dubon, D. Ndi, H. Talbot, Sweta Tiwari, Dayna Wyatt",10.15585/mmwr.mm7220a3,https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm7220a3,MMWR. Morbidity and mortality weekly report,146,2023,yes,"The study focuses on adult MSM and transgender persons, including those who are immunocompromised. While it includes adults, it is not limited to healthy adults as it includes immunocompromised individuals.",yes,The study measures Vaccine Effectiveness (VE) against mpox infection.,yes,"The study is a matched case-control study, which is a primary research design.",yes,The study involves human case-patients and control patients.,no,"The study specifically states the population was adults aged 18-49 years. No pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents are included.",no,"Participants received vaccinations as adults (age 18-49), not during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,"The study evaluates the JYNNEOS vaccine, which is a Modified Vaccinia Ankara (vaccinia-based) vaccine.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the effectiveness of the JYNNEOS vaccine against mpox in a case-control study. However, it is excluded because the study population is exclusively adults aged 18-49 and does not include the required target populations of children, pregnant women, or neonates.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""evaluate VE against mpox among MSM and transgender adults aged 18-49 years.""
