Perinatal Outcomes After RSV Vaccination During Pregnancy-Addressing Emerging Concerns.,"Moeun Son, MD, MSCI; Laura E. Riley, MD; Anna P. Staniczenko, MD, MSc; Julia Cron, MD; Steven Yen, MS; Charlene Thomas, MS; Evan Sholle, MS; Lauren M. Osborne, MD; Heather S. Lipkind, MD, MS",Annette K. Regan,10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2024.19229,https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2024.19229,JAMA Network Open,7,2024,yes,"The study focuses on pregnant women undergoing vaccination, therefore it is not limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study evaluates perinatal outcomes following vaccination. However, since these outcomes are not related to mpox or smallpox vaccines, it does not meet the thematic requirements for relevant mpox-related outcomes.",maybe,"The title implies an evaluation of perinatal outcomes, which typically involves a primary research design like a cohort study, though the specific design is not explicitly stated in the provided text.",yes,"The study involves perinatal outcomes and pregnancy, which are human medical contexts.",yes,The study specifically includes pregnant women.,yes,The vaccination occurred during pregnancy.,no,"The study explicitly focuses on RSV (Respiratory Syncytial Virus) vaccination, not vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,This study evaluates RSV vaccination during pregnancy. It fails the inclusion criteria because it focuses on RSV vaccines rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
COMPLICATIONS OF SMALLPOX VACCINATION UNITED STATES 1963,"Four thousand nine hundred physicians in four states, representing 4.8% of the population of the United States, were surveyed in order to assess the frequency and types of complications that occurred during 1963 in association with smallpox vaccination. Four hundred ninety-one physicians initially reported observing 810 complications during this year. On follow-up of these cases, many were not actually complications or were complications that occurred in a year other than 1963.
Accidental infection was the most common complication. Generalized vaccinia and eczema vaccinatum occurred at a frequency of 238 and 80, respectively, per million primary vaccinations. Severe complications were infrequent. No deaths and only one case each of post-vaccinal encephalitis and vaccinia necrosum were detected. The high frequency of many preventable complications and the disproportionate frequency of complications in infants suggest that morbidity and mortality now associated with smallpox vaccination could be significantly reduced if primary vaccination could be deferred until after the first year of life and if more care were taken to detect individuals in whom complications can be expected to occur.","J. Neff, R. Levine, J. Lane, E. Ager, Helen Moore, B. Rosenstein, J. Millar, D. A. Henderson",10.1542/peds.39.6.916,https://doi.org/10.1542/peds.39.6.916,Pediatrics,1,1967,yes,The study is a survey of complications across a portion of the US population and specifically highlights findings regarding infants.,yes,"The study reports on various safety outcomes and complications such as accidental infection, generalized vaccinia, eczema vaccinatum, encephalitis, and death.",yes,The study is a survey-based observational study assessing incidence rates of complications.,yes,The study involves human physicians reporting on human patients.,yes,The study explicitly discusses results and frequencies for infants.,yes,"The study focuses on complications from primary vaccination, particularly highlighting those occurring in the first year of life (infancy).",yes,The study involves smallpox vaccination occurring in 1963.,Include,"This study is a primary research survey of smallpox vaccination complications in the United States, including a focus on infants. It reports specific safety outcomes such as generalized vaccinia and encephalitis among vaccinated individuals.",,,5,"  - ""Four thousand nine hundred physicians in four states, representing 4.8% of the population of the United States, were surveyed in order to assess the frequency and types of complications that occurred during 1963 in association with smallpox vaccination.""
  - ""the disproportionate frequency of complications in infants suggest that morbidity and mortality now associated with smallpox vaccination could be significantly reduced if primary vaccination could be deferred until after the first year of life""",,"  - ""Accidental infection was the most common complication. Generalized vaccinia and eczema vaccinatum occurred at a frequency of 238 and 80, respectively, per million primary vaccinations.""
