"Smallpox vaccine adverse events among civilians--United States, February 25-March 3, 2003.","During the civilian smallpox vaccination program, CDC, the Food and Drug Administration, and state health departments are conducting surveillance for vaccine-associated adverse events. In the first stage of the program, active surveillance is being conducted for potentially life-threatening, moderate-to-severe, and other serious adverse events and for vaccinia transmission to contacts of vaccinees (Table). Nonserious events are reported through passive surveillance and are expected to be underreported. This report summarizes smallpox vaccine adverse events reported among civilians vaccinated as of February 28, 2003, and among contacts of vaccinees, received by CDC from the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS) as of March 3.",,10.1001/archderm.139.5.683,https://doi.org/10.1001/archderm.139.5.683,MMWR. Morbidity and mortality weekly report,5,2003,maybe,The study reports on civilians in the United States receiving smallpox vaccines. The abstract does not specify if the population is limited to healthy non-pregnant adults; it refers broadly to civilians.,yes,The study specifically conducts surveillance for and reports on vaccine-associated adverse events.,yes,"The paper is a surveillance report, which functions as a primary research study/case series summarizing events from VAERS.",yes,The study involves humans (civilians) vaccinated in a national program.,maybe,"The abstract mentions only 'civilians' and does not explicitly state the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",maybe,"The timing is during the civilian program in 2003, but it is not specified if participants were children or pregnant at the time of vaccination.",yes,The study involves the smallpox vaccine (vaccinia-based).,Include,"This surveillance report describes adverse events following smallpox vaccination among a civilian population. While it covers relevant safety outcomes 