Low levels of monkeypox virus neutralizing antibodies after MVA-BN vaccination in healthy individuals,"In July 2022, the ongoing monkeypox (MPX) outbreak was declared a public health emergency of international concern by the World Health Organization. Modified vaccinia virus Ankara (MVA-BN, also known as Imvamune, Jynneos, or Imvanex) is a 3rd generation smallpox vaccine that was generated by serial passaging of the more pathogenic parental vaccinia virus (VACV), and is authorized as a vaccine against MPX in humans in a two-shot regimen. Up to now, there is a lack of data that demonstrate MPX virus (MPXV)-neutralizing antibodies in vaccinated individuals and vaccine efficacy data against MPXV infection. Here, we measure MVA-, VACV-, and MPXV-reactive binding and neutralizing antibodies with validated in-house assays in cohorts of historically smallpox-vaccinated, MPXV PCR-positive, and recently MVA-BN-vaccinated individuals. We show that MPXV neutralizing antibodies were detected across all cohorts in individuals with MPXV exposure as well as those who received historic (VACV) vaccination. However, a primary MVA-BN immunization series in non-primed individuals yields relatively low levels of MPXV neutralizing antibodies. As the role of MPXV neutralizing antibodies for protection against disease and transmissibility is currently unclear and no correlate of protection against MPXV infection has been identified yet, this raises the question how well vaccinated individuals are protected. Dose-sparing leads to lower antibody levels, whereas a third MVA vaccination further boosts the antibody response. Cohort studies following vaccinated individuals are necessary to further assess vaccine efficacy in risk populations and determine correlates of protection for this emerging pathogen.","L. Zaeck, M. Lamers, B. Verstrepen, M. Bestebroer, Martine J E Van, Royen, Hannelore M Götz, M. Shamier, Leanne P. M. van Leeuwen, Kimberley Alblas, Suzanne van Efferen, S. Bogers, S. Scherbeijn, G. Rimmelzwaan, E. V. Gorp, Marion M. P. Koopmans, B. Haagmans, C. Geurtsvankessel, R. D. Vries",10.1101/2022.08.31.22279414,https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.08.31.22279414,medRxiv,0,2022,no,The study title and content indicate it was conducted in 'healthy individuals' without mentioning any inclusion of vulnerable populations like children or pregnant women.,no,The study focuses on immunogenicity (neutralizing antibodies) rather than clinical effectiveness (prevention of infection) or safety outcomes like adverse events.,yes,This is a primary research study involvingcohorts of vaccinated and infected individuals.,yes,The study involved human participants who were vaccinated or PCR-positive.,no,"The study describes participants only as 'healthy individuals' and does not mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"There is no evidence that vaccinations occurred during the specified periods (pregnancy, childhood, etc.); it appears to focus on the general adult population during the 2022 outbreak.",yes,The study specifically evaluates the MVA-BN vaccine and historical vaccinia virus (VACV) vaccination.,Exclude,This study evaluates neutralizing antibody responses to the MVA-BN vaccine in healthy adults. It fails multiple inclusion criteria as it does not target the specific populations (pregnant women or children) required for this review.,"Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,,,"  - ""measure MVA-, VACV-, and MPXV-reactive binding and neutralizing antibodies""",,"  - ""recently MVA-BN-vaccinated individuals.""",,"  - ""recently MVA-BN-vaccinated individuals.""",,,,"  - ""recently MVA-BN-vaccinated individuals.""",,"  - ""Modified vaccinia virus Ankara (MVA-BN, also known as Imvamune, Jynneos, or Imvanex) is a 3rd generation smallpox vaccine""
