Single Dose of Recombinant Chimeric Horsepox Virus (TNX-801) Vaccination Protects Macaques from Lethal Monkeypox Challenge,"The ongoing global Monkeypox outbreak that started in the spring of 2022 has reinforced the importance of protecting the population using live virus vaccines based on the vaccinia virus (VACV). Smallpox also remains a biothreat and although some U.S. military personnel are immunized with VACV, safety concerns limit its use in other vulnerable groups. Consequently, there is a need for an effective and safer, single dose, live replicating vaccine against both viruses. One potential approach is to use the horsepox virus (HPXV) as a vaccine. Contemporary VACV shares a common ancestor with HPXV, which from the time of Edward Jenner and through the 19th century, was extensively used to vaccinate against smallpox. However, it is unknown if early HPXV-based vaccines exhibited different safety and efficacy profiles compared to modern VACV. A deeper understanding of HPXV as a vaccine platform may allow the construction of safer and more effective vaccines against the poxvirus family. In a proof-of-concept study, we vaccinated cynomolgus macaques with TNX-801, a recombinant chimeric horsepox virus (rcHPXV), and showed that the vaccine elicited protective immune responses against a lethal challenge with monkeypox virus (MPXV), strain Zaire. The vaccine was well tolerated and protected animals from the development of lesions and severe disease. These encouraging data support the further development of TNX-801.","R. Noyce, Landon W Westfall, Siobhan Fogarty, Karen S. Gilbert, O. Mpanju, Helen Stillwell, J. Esparza, B. Daugherty, F. Koide, D. Evans, Seth Lederman",10.3390/v15020356,https://doi.org/10.3390/v15020356,Viruses,11,2023,no,The study does not involve human subjects; it utilizes cynomolgus macaques.,yes,The study reports on protective immune responses (effectiveness) and tolerability (safety).,yes,The study is a primary research experiment using a non-human primate model.,no,The study is not a human study; it is an animal study conducted on macaques.,no,"The study does not include human pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it focuses on macaques.",no,"Participants are macaques, not human children, neonates, or pregnant women, meaning the vaccination timing in humans is not applicable.",yes,"The study uses TNX-801, which is a recombinant chimeric horsepox virus related to the vaccinia-based vaccine platform.",Exclude,"This study is an animal experiment conducted on cynomolgus macaques to evaluate the TNX-801 vaccine. Because it is not a human study and does not include the target human populations (pregnant women, children, etc.), it fails several mandatory inclusion criteria.","Population Focus, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""vaccinated cynomolgus macaques with TNX-801,""",,"  - ""the vaccine elicited protective immune responses against a lethal challenge with monkeypox virus (MPXV),""
