Human Mpox Virus Infection After Receipt of Modified Vaccinia Ankara Vaccine-Reply.,"In Reply In response to our Research Letter1 on mpox infection in the immediate period following vaccination with MVA-BN, Dr Zhou raises 2 important concerns about how to better assess vaccine effectiveness of MVA-BN against mpox.","A. Hazra, J. Schneider",10.1001/jama.2023.0450,https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2023.0450,Journal of the American Medical Association (JAMA),0,2023,maybe,"The text does not specify the population demographics beyond mentioning vaccine recipients, which does not confirm inclusion of populations other than healthy adults.",yes,The study discusses assessing vaccine effectiveness of MVA-BN against mpox infection.,no,"The document identifies itself as a 'Reply' to concerns regarding a 'Research Letter', which characterizes it as a commentary or correspondence piece rather than a primary research study or evidence synthesis.",yes,The study involves human vaccination with MVA-BN and mpox infection.,no,"The provided text does not mention the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"There is no mention of participants receiving the vaccine during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,"The study involves the MVA-BN vaccine, which is a modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine.",Exclude,This article is a reply letter discussing concerns about the assessment of vaccine effectiveness for the MVA-BN vaccine against mpox. It is excluded as it does not include target populations such as children or pregnant women and does not represent a required primary research or synthesis design.,"Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""In response to our Research Letter1 on mpox infection in the immediate period following vaccination with MVA-BN, Dr Zhou raises 2 important concerns about how to better assess vaccine effectiveness of MVA-BN against mpox.""",,"  - ""In response to our Research Letter1