Extended surveillance to assess safety of 9-valent human papillomavirus vaccine,"ABSTRACT The safety of 9-valent HPV vaccine (9vHPV) has been established with regard to common and uncommon adverse events. However, investigation of rare and severe adverse events requires extended study periods to capture rare outcomes. This observational cohort study investigated the occurrence of three rare and serious adverse events following 9-valent human papillomavirus (9vHPV) vaccination compared to other vaccinations, in US individuals 9–26 years old, using electronic health record data from the Vaccine Safety Datalink (VSD). We searched for occurrences of Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS), chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP), and stroke following 9vHPV vaccination from October 4, 2015, through January 2, 2021. We compared the risks of GBS, CIDP, and stroke following 9vHPV vaccination to risks of those outcomes following comparator vaccines commonly given to this age group (Td, Tdap, MenACWY, hepatitis A, and varicella vaccines) from January 1, 2007, through January 2, 2021. We observed 1.2 cases of stroke, 0.3 cases of GBS, and 0.1 cases of CIDP per 100,000 doses of 9vHPV vaccine. After observing more than 1.8 million doses of 9vHPV, we identified no statistically significant increase in risks associated with 9vHPV vaccination for any of these adverse events, either combined or stratified by age (9–17 years of age vs. 18–26 years of age) and sex (males vs. females). Our findings provide additional evidence supporting 9vHPV vaccine safety, over longer time frames and for more serious and rare adverse events.","M. Sundaram, B. Kieke, K. Hanson, E. Belongia, Eric S. Weintraub, M. Daley, R. Hechter, N. Klein, E. Lewis, A. Naleway, Jennifer C. Nelson, J. Donahue",10.1080/21645515.2022.2159215,https://doi.org/10.1080/21645515.2022.2159215,Human Vaccines & Immunotherapeutics,10,2022,yes,"The study includes individuals aged 9–17 years, meaning it is not exclusively limited to healthy adults.",yes,"The study reports safety outcomes including GBS, CIDP, and stroke.",yes,The study is an observational cohort study.,yes,The study uses electronic health record data from human individuals.,yes,The study includes children and adolescents aged 9–17 years.,yes,Participants received the vaccine during childhood or adolescence (ages 9–26).,no,"The study evaluates the 9-valent human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccine, not a vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccine. The comparators are also unrelated to mpox.",Exclude,"This observational cohort study focused on the safety of the 9-valent HPV vaccine in individuals aged 9–26 years. While it includes an eligible pediatric population, it fails the inclusion criteria because it does not investigate vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""US individuals 9–26 years old,""
