Adverse events following human papillomavirus vaccine in adolescents: systematic review of the literature,"Objective: The objective of this study was to investigate the occurrence of Events Supposedly Attributable to Vaccination and/or Immunization associated with the Human Papillomavirus vaccine among adolescents of both sexes. Methods: This is a systematic review carried out according to the principles of the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA). Results: Initially, 11,016 articles were identified, of which 6,824 remained after removing duplicates, and 59 of these were selected for full reading. The sample comprised nine studies. The results indicate that most Events Supposedly Attributable to Vaccination and/or Immunization were mild and moderate, and events at the injection site, such as pain and edema, prevailed. The most common systemic Events Supposedly Attributable to Vaccination and/or Immunization were fever, headache, fatigue and dizziness. Conclusion: The Human Papillomavirus vaccine for adolescents is safe, reinforcing its importance as a strategy to reduce the incidence rates of Human Papillomavirus associated cancers.","Bianca Maria Oliveira Luvisaro, Thales Philipe Rodrigues Silva, A. Paula, Vieira Faria, C.K. Duarte, Tércia Moreira, Ribeiro da Silva, Adalton Elérito, Satil Neto, Natatia Santana Carvalho, F. Matozinhos",,,,0,,yes,"The study focuses on adolescents, who are not healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports on events supposedly attributable to vaccination (adverse events).,yes,The study is a systematic review.,yes,The study is a systematic review of clinical literature involving humans.,yes,The study specifically includes adolescents.,yes,Participants received the vaccine during adolescence.,no,"The vaccine investigated is the Human Papillomavirus vaccine, not a vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"While the study focuses on the target population of adolescents, it evaluates the Human Papillomavirus vaccine rather than the required vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines. As it does not cover the relevant intervention, it is excluded.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Human Papillomavirus vaccine among adolescents of both sexes.""",,"  - ""investigate the occurrence of Events Supposedly Attributable to Vaccination and/or Immunization""",,"  - ""systematic review carried out according to the principles of the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA).""",,"  - ""systematic review carried out according to the principles of the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA).""",,"  - ""Human Papillomavirus vaccine among adolescents of both sexes.""",,"  - ""Human Papillomavirus vaccine among adolescents of both sexes.""",,"  - ""Human Papillomavirus vaccine among adolescents of both sexes.""",
Associations of COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy with adverse neonatal and maternal outcomes: A systematic review and meta-analysis,"Objectives The low COVID-19 vaccine uptake rate among pregnant women is mainly due to safety concerns about COVID-19 vaccines due to limited safety evidence. Our goal was to evaluate the safety of COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy with up-to-date evidence. Methods A comprehensive search of MEDLINE, EMBASE, the Cochrane Library, and clinicaltrials.gov was performed on April 5th, 2022, and updated on May 25th, 2022. Studies evaluating the association of COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy with adverse maternal and neonatal outcomes were included. Two reviewers independently performed the risk of bias assessment and data extraction. Inverse variance random effect meta-analyses were performed to pool outcome data. Results Forty-three observational studies were included. COVID-19 vaccination [96,384 (73.9%) BNT162b2, 30,889 (23.7%) mRNA-1273, and 3,172 (2.4%) other types] during pregnancy [23,721 (18.3%) in the first trimester, 52,778 (40.5%) in the second trimester, and 53,886 (41.2%) in the third trimester].was associated with reduced risks of stillbirth or neonatal death (OR, 0.74; 95% CI, 0.60–0.92). Sensitivity analysis restricted to studies in participants without COVID-19 showed that the pooled effect was not robust. COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy was not associated with congenital anomalies (OR, 0.83; 95% CI, 0.63–1.08), preterm birth (OR, 0.98; 95% CI, 0.90–1.06), NICU admission or hospitalization (OR, 0.94; 95% CI, 0.84–1.04), an Apgar score at 5 min <7 (OR, 0.93; 95% CI, 0.86–1.01), low birth weight (OR, 1.00; 95% CI, 0.88–1.14), miscarriage (OR, 0.99; 95% CI, 0.88–1.11), cesarean delivery (OR, 1.07; 95% CI, 0.96–1.19), or postpartum hemorrhage (OR, 0.91; 95% CI, 0.81–1.01). Conclusions COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy was not associated with any of the adverse neonatal or maternal outcomes studied. Interpretation of study findings is limited by the types and timing of vaccination. The vaccinations in our study received during pregnancy were primarily mRNA vaccines administered in the second and third trimester. Future RCTs and meta-analysis are warranted to evaluate the efficacy and long-term effects of the COVID-19 vaccines. Systematic review registration https://www.crd.york.ac.uk/prospero/display_record.php?ID=CRD42022322525, identifier: PROSPERO, CRD42022322525.","Cailin Ding, Yakun Liu, W. Pang, Dan Zhang, Kai Wang, Yajun Chen",10.3389/fpubh.2023.1044031,https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2023.1044031,Frontiers in Public Health,23,2023,yes,"The study focuses on pregnant women, which is a specific (not healthy non-pregnant) population.",yes,The study specifically evaluates safety outcomes such as adverse maternal and neonatal outcomes.,yes,The study is a systematic review and meta-analysis.,yes,The study includes data from human participants.,yes,The participants are explicitly pregnant women and their neonates.,yes,The study evaluates vaccination received during pregnancy.,no,The study evaluates COVID-19 vaccines (mRNA-1273 and BNT162b2) rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This paper is a systematic review and meta-analysis assessing the safety of COVID-19 vaccines during pregnancy. Despite covering the target population (pregnant women), it focuses exclusively on COVID-19 vaccines rather than mpox or smallpox vaccines, failing to meet the required intervention criteria.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""low COVID-19 vaccine uptake rate among pregnant women""",,"  - ""adverse maternal and neonatal outcomes""",,,,"  - ""COVID-19 vaccination [96,384 (73.9%) BNT162b2, 30,889 (23.7%) mRNA-1273, and 3,172 (2.4%) other types]""",,"  - ""low COVID-19 vaccine uptake rate among pregnant women""
  - ""adverse maternal and neonatal outcomes""",,"  - ""vaccination during pregnancy""",,"  - ""COVID-19 vaccination [96,384 (73.9%) BNT162b2, 30,889 (23.7%) mRNA-1273, and 3,172 (2.4%) other types]""",
Monkeypox: A New Threat?,"The global vaccination programme against smallpox led to its successful eradication and averted millions of deaths. Monkeypox virus (MPXV) is a close relative of the Variola (smallpox) virus. Due to antigenic similarity, smallpox vaccines cross-protect against MPXV. However, over 70% of people living today were never vaccinated against smallpox. Symptoms of monkeypox (MPX) include fever, head- and muscle ache, lymphadenopathy and a characteristic rash that develops into papules, vesicles and pustules which eventually scab over and heal. MPX is less often fatal (case fatality rates range from <1% to up to 11%) than smallpox (up to 30%). MPXV is endemic in sub-Saharan Africa, infecting wild animals and causing zoonotic outbreaks. Exotic animal trade and international travel, combined with the increasing susceptibility of the human population due to halted vaccination, facilitated the spread of MPXV to new areas. The ongoing outbreak, with >10,000 cases in >50 countries between May and July 2022, shows that MPXV can significantly spread between people and may thus become a serious threat to public health with global consequences. Here, we summarize the current knowledge about this re-emerging virus, discuss available strategies to limit its spread and pathogenicity and evaluate its risk to the human population.","Dorota Kmieć, F. Kirchhoff",10.3390/ijms23147866,https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms23147866,International Journal of Molecular Sciences,178,2022,maybe,The paper does not report on a specific study population but rather summarizes general knowledge about the virus. No specific age groups or pregnant statuses are described for a primary study.,no,The paper discusses case fatality rates and pathogenicity in a general summary context rather than reporting outcomes from a primary clinical study.,no,"The paper is a summary/review of current knowledge rather than a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",no,"While the review evaluates risk to the human population, it is not a primary study involving human participants.",no,"The text contains no mention of specific inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, or children as participants of a study; it discusses the general population.",no,There is no primary data regarding participants receiving vaccines during specific life stages like pregnancy or childhood; it only discusses population-level historical vaccination.,maybe,"The paper mentions smallpox vaccines and vaccination programs, but not in the context of a primary study.",Exclude,"This article is a narrative review summarizing existing knowledge about Monkeypox and public health risks. It fails inclusion because it is not a primary research study or systematic review, and it does not report on a study population involving children or pregnant individuals receiving vaccinations.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""Here, we summarize the current knowledge about this re-emerging virus, discuss available strategies to limit its spread and pathogenicity and evaluate its risk to the human population.""",,"  - ""Here, we summarize the current knowledge about this re-emerging virus, discuss available strategies to limit its spread and pathogenicity and evaluate its risk to the human population.""",,"  - ""Here, we summarize the current knowledge about this re-emerging virus, discuss available strategies to limit its spread and pathogenicity and evaluate its risk to the human population.""",,"  - ""Here, we summarize the current knowledge about this re-emerging virus, discuss available strategies to limit its spread and pathogenicity and evaluate its risk to the human population.""",,"  - ""Here, we summarize the current knowledge about this re-emerging virus, discuss available strategies to limit its spread and pathogenicity and evaluate its risk to the human population.""",,"  - ""The ongoing outbreak, with >10,000 cases in >50 countries between May and July 2022, shows that MPXV can significantly spread between people and may thus become a serious threat to public health with global consequences.""",,"  - ""Here, we summarize the current knowledge about this re-emerging virus, discuss available strategies to limit its spread and pathogenicity and evaluate its risk to the human population.""",
Estimating the Size of the U.S. Population at Risk of Severe Adverse Events from Replicating Smallpox Vaccine,"Objective: To quantify the population at risk of serious adverse reactions to replicating smallpox vaccine. Design and Sample: Conditions known or suspected to carry risk were identified via Centers for Disease Control and Prevention planning documents, other federal publications, and peer‐reviewed literature. Conditions identified were categorized as historically recognized risks or more recently recognized immunocompromised states that may pose risk. Major historical risk factors were as follows: eczema/atopic dermatitis, pregnancy, HIV, and primary immunodeficiency. More recently identified states were as follows: rheumatoid arthritis, inflammatory bowel disease, dialysis, bone marrow transplant recipients within 24 months post‐transplant, solid‐organ transplant recipients within 3 months post‐transplant, age under 1 year, and systemic lupus erythematosus. Measures: The estimated prevalence or absolute number of affected individuals for each condition was ascertained from peer‐reviewed studies, vital statistics, and registry databases. Results: An estimated 48,121,280 to 50,028,045 individuals (15.2–15.8% of the U.S. population) are potentially contraindicated to replicating smallpox vaccine. This rises to 119,244,531 to 123,669,327 (37.4–38.8%) if household contacts are included. Conclusions: These figures are significant and larger than the only previously published study. Understanding this number allows for improved clinical utilization, equitable attention to the health needs of a vulnerable population, and strategic vaccine stockpiling.","E. Carlin, Nichole Giller, R. Katz",10.1111/phn.12291,https://doi.org/10.1111/phn.12291,Public Health Nursing,8,2017,yes,"The study is specifically designed to estimate populations with contraindications, including pregnant women and infants, meaning it is not limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study does not report safety or effectiveness outcomes from vaccine recipients; instead, it estimates how many people might be at risk based on the prevalence of contraindications in databases.",maybe,"This is a population estimation/modelling study using secondary data from statistics and registries, which does not fit the typical primary research designs such as RCTs or cohorts required for clinical evidence reviews.",yes,The study estimates figures for the U.S. human population.,yes,The analysis explicitly includes pregnant women and infants under 1 year of age as part of the at-risk population being quantified.,no,No participants in this study actually received the vaccine; the researchers only estimated the size of population groups who have conditions that might carry risk if they were vaccinated.,yes,"The study focuses on the 'replicating smallpox vaccine', which is a vaccinia-based vaccine.",Exclude,"This study is an epidemiological estimation of the size of U.S. populations with contraindications to the smallpox vaccine, such as pregnancy and infancy. It is excluded because it does not involve the administration of vaccines to study participants and does not report clinical safety or effectiveness outcomes from vaccinated individuals.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing",Vaccination Timing,0.1,"  - ""pregnancy, HIV, and primary immunodeficiency.""
