Impact of vaccination with third generation modified vaccinia Ankara and sexual behaviour on mpox incidence in men who have sex with men: analysis among participants of the ANRS-174 DOXYVAC trial,"Summary Background Mpox was first reported in France on May 19 and third-generation live Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA-BN) vaccination of multiple-partner men who have sex with men (MSM) was recommended as of July 11, 2022. We assessed the impact of vaccination and of sexual behavior adopted during the epidemic period on mpox incidence in the ANRS-174-DOXYVAC trial enrolling MSM on HIV pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) with history of sexually-transmitted infections (STI) in the previous year. Methods We compared pre-epidemic socio-behavioral characteristics and change in sexual behaviors after the onset of the epidemic of participants with mpox and mpox-free. Then we compared incidence rates of mpox per 1000 person-months (p-m) between May 9-July 10 (before vaccination of MSM, period-1) and July 11-September 20 2022 (after vaccination launch, period-2) and explored factors explaining the period effect using Poisson regression model. Findings 472 MSM had data before and after May 9, 2022. Twenty percent had received smallpox vaccine during childhood. Mpox occurred in 77/472 participants (incidence 49.3 per 1000 p-m (95% CI 38.9–61.6)). MVA-BN vaccination roll-out was rapid, with 86% (341/398) of eligible participants having received at least one dose by September 20, 2022. Sexual behavior significantly changed before and after May 9, with a decrease in the proportion of mpox-free participants with >10 partners during last 3 months (45% vs 38%, p = 0.0035). Mpox incidence was 67.4 per 1000 p-m (95% CI 51.6–86.6) in period-1, and 24.4 per 1000 p-m (95% CI 13.9–39.6) in period-2, with an incidence rate ratio of 0.36 (95% CI 0.21–0.63). In multivariable Poisson regression model, only MVA-BN vaccination in 2022 remained significantly associated with mpox incidence, with a 99% risk reduction (95% CI 96.6–99.7). Interpretation In MSM on PrEP enrolled in the ANRS-174-DOXYVAC trial, rapid roll-out of MVA-BN vaccination was associated with a strong reduction in mpox incidence. Funding ANRS Maladies Infectieuses Emergentes (ANRS/MIE).","J. Ghosn, L. Assoumou, Moussa Ouattara, Emma Rubenstein, Gilles Pialoux, C. Katlama, Laure Surgers, C. Duvivier, J. Pavie, Jean-Paul Viard, M. Algarte-Genin, S. Gibowski, Manon Ollivier, D. Costagliola, Jean-Michel Molina",10.1016/j.lanepe.2024.101020,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.lanepe.2024.101020,The Lancet Regional Health - Europe,10,2024,yes,"The study population consists of MSM on HIV PrEP with a history of STIs, which are not considered 'healthy' non-pregnant adults in the context of this criterion. Additionally, some participants received vaccines during childhood.",yes,"The study reports on mpox incidence, which is an effectiveness outcome.",yes,"The study is an analysis of participants from a clinical trial (ANRS-174-DOXYVAC), representing primary research.",yes,The study involves human participants (MSM on PrEP).,yes,"The paper mentions that 20% of the participants received the smallpox vaccine during childhood. While the current trial participants are likely adults (given they are on PrEP), the criterion asks if it includes children or participants who received it during childhood/adolescence.",yes,The study includes participants who received a smallpox vaccine during their childhood.,yes,The study investigates both historical smallpox vaccines (childhood) and the third-generation MVA-BN vaccine.,Include,"This study evaluates the impact of MVA-BN and historical smallpox vaccination on mpox incidence in a high-risk cohort. Importantly, it includes data on individuals who were vaccinated during childhood, meeting the criteria for specific target populations and vaccination timing.",,,4.9,"  - ""ANRS-174-DOXYVAC trial enrolling MSM on HIV pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) with history of sexually-transmitted infections (STI)""
