Short-term Outcome in Pregnant Women Vaccinated with COVID-19 Vaccine—Covishield (ChAdOx1 nCoV-19 Coronavirus Vaccine): A Prospective Observational Study,"Pregnant and lactating women have been excluded from clinical trials of existing COVID-19 vaccines. 1 Hence, there is an immediate need to follow-up these women and report the side effects, adverse effects, obstetric effects, and also maternal and fetal outcomes.In India, the COVID-19 vaccines were approved on 25 June 2021 by the Government of India. 4 The Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 1 the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, 5 and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists 6 recommend that COVID-19 vaccination can be given to pregnant women. IntroductIonCoronavirus is known as severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2).This virus causes coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19), which has caused significant morbidity and mortality globally. 1 COVID-19 infection in pregnancy is associated with more complications in pregnant women when compared to nonpregnant women.The chances of needing intensive care unit admission, mechanical ventilation, and death are increased in pregnant women compared to the nonpregnant population.Hence, pregnancy with COVID-19 infection is considered moderate risk.There is also an increased risk for preterm birth (2-3 times increased). 1 Covishield is ChAdOx1 nCoV-19 recombinant coronavirus vaccine.It is a viral vector vaccine.The viral vector is chimpanzee adenovirus, which is not capable of replication and encodes the SARS-CoV-2 spike (S) glycoprotein.This vaccine is developed in genetically modified human embryonic kidney (HEK) 293 cells. 2 The AstraZeneca vaccine is also a ChAdOx1 nCoV-19 recombinant coronavirus vaccine.Preliminary animal studies do not indicate harmful effects on the embryo, fetus, or postnatal development of the newborn.Covishield is found to be safe and well-tolerated in phase 2 and 3 trials in India.The AstraZeneca vaccine is found to be 70.4% effective. 3 The safety and efficacy of COVID-19 vaccines for use in pregnant women, fetuses, and infants are not clearly defined.","G. Shivamurthy, A. Rathod, Ramani Ramu",10.5005/jp-journals-10016-1371,https://doi.org/10.5005/jp-journals-10016-1371,International Journal of Infertility &amp; Fetal Medicine,0,2025,yes,The study is not limited to healthy non-pregnant adults as it specifically focuses on pregnant and lactating women.,no,"The study reports safety and side effects, but these are exclusively related to the COVID-19 vaccine and SARS-CoV-2 disease, rather than mpox prevention, infection, or safety outcomes.",yes,"The text describes an observational follow-up of women to report clinical outcomes, which constitutes primary research.",yes,"The study involves human participants, specifically pregnant women and their infants.",yes,The study target population includes pregnant women and potentially infants.,yes,The study focuses on women who received the vaccine during pregnancy.,no,"The vaccine used is Covishield (ChAdOx1 nCoV-19), which is a recombinant coronavirus vaccine, not a vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,The paper is a study of the COVID-19 vaccine (Covishield) in pregnant women. It fails the inclusion criteria because it assesses a recombinant coronavirus vaccine rather than a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine and does not report outcomes relevant to mpox infection.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Pregnant and lactating women have been excluded from clinical trials of existing COVID-19 vaccines.""
