Neonatal and maternal outcomes following SARS-CoV-2 infection and COVID-19 vaccination: a population-based matched cohort study,"Understanding the impact of SARS-CoV-2 infection and COVID-19 vaccination in pregnancy on neonatal and maternal outcomes informs clinical decision-making. Here we report a national, population-based, matched cohort study to investigate associations between SARS-CoV-2 infection and, separately, COVID-19 vaccination just before or during pregnancy and the risk of adverse neonatal and maternal outcomes among women in Scotland with a singleton pregnancy ending at ≥20 weeks gestation. Neonatal outcomes are stillbirth, neonatal death, extended perinatal mortality, preterm birth (overall, spontaneous, and provider-initiated), small-for-gestational age, and low Apgar score. Maternal outcomes are admission to critical care or death, venous thromboembolism, hypertensive disorders of pregnancy, and pregnancy-related bleeding. We use conditional logistic regression to derive odds ratios adjusted for socio-demographic and clinical characteristics (aORs). We find that infection is associated with an increased risk of preterm (aOR=1.36, 95% Confidence Interval [CI] = 1.16–1.59) and very preterm birth (aOR = 1.90, 95% CI 1.20–3.02), maternal admission to critical care or death (aOR=1.72, 95% CI = 1.39–2.12), and venous thromboembolism (aOR = 2.53, 95% CI = 1.47–4.35). We find no evidence of increased risk for any of our outcomes following vaccination. These data suggest SARS-CoV-2 infection during pregnancy is associated with adverse neonatal and maternal outcomes, and COVID-19 vaccination remains a safe way for pregnant women to protect themselves and their babies against infection.","Laura Lindsay, C. Calvert, T. Shi, J. Carruthers, C. Denny, J. Donaghy, L. Hopcroft, L. Hopkins, A. Goulding, T. Mclaughlin, E. Moore, B. Taylor, K. Bhaskaran, S. Katikireddi, R. McCabe, C. McCowan, Colin Simpson, C. Robertson, Aziz Sheikh, R. Wood, S. Stock",10.1038/s41467-023-40965-9,https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-40965-9,Nature Communications,28,2023,yes,"The study specifically focuses on pregnant women and their neonates, not healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports various safety outcomes including neonatal death, stillbirth, and maternal complications.",yes,"The study is a national population-based matched cohort study, which is a primary research design.",yes,The study involves human participants (women in Scotland).,yes,The study includes pregnant women and neonates as the primary participants.,yes,The study investigates outcomes following vaccination just before or during pregnancy.,no,"The study investigates COVID-19 vaccination, not vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study is excluded because it evaluates COVID-19 vaccination rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines. Although it focuses on the relevant target population of pregnant women and neonates and reports safety outcomes, it does not meet the mandatory vaccine type requirement.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""investigate associations between SARS-CoV-2 infection and, separately, COVID-19 vaccination just before or during pregnancy and the risk of adverse neonatal and maternal outcomes among women in Scotland""",,"  - ""Neonatal outcomes are stillbirth, neonatal death, extended perinatal mortality, preterm birth (overall, spontaneous, and provider-initiated), small-for-gestational age, and low Apgar score.""",,"  - ""national, population-based, matched cohort study""",,"  - ""investigate associations between SARS-CoV-2 infection and, separately, COVID-19 vaccination just before or during pregnancy and the risk of adverse neonatal and maternal outcomes among women in Scotland""",,"  - ""investigate associations between SARS-CoV-2 infection and, separately, COVID-19 vaccination just before or during pregnancy and the risk of adverse neonatal and maternal outcomes among women in Scotland""
  - ""Neonatal outcomes are stillbirth, neonatal death, extended perinatal mortality, preterm birth (overall, spontaneous, and provider-initiated), small-for-gestational age, and low Apgar score.""",,"  - ""investigate associations between SARS-CoV-2 infection and, separately, COVID-19 vaccination just before or during pregnancy and the risk of adverse neonatal and maternal outcomes among women in Scotland""",,"  - ""investigate associations between SARS-CoV-2 infection and, separately, COVID-19 vaccination just before or during pregnancy and the risk of adverse neonatal and maternal outcomes among women in Scotland""
  - ""COVID-19 vaccination remains a safe way for pregnant women to protect themselves and their babies against infection.""",
A single-dose nanoparticle vaccine protects against mpox in preclinical models.,"The ongoing outbreak and emergence of monkeypox virus (MPXV) clade Ib and IIb lineage have heightened its transmissibility, morbidity, and mortality, underscoring the urgent need for safe and effective vaccines against MPXV infection. In this study, we developed a ""two-in-one"" nanoparticle vaccine, DAM-NP, which incorporates bivalently fused M1 and A35 antigens conjugated to a self-assembled 24-meric ferritin nanoparticle. Preclinical evaluations demonstrated that DAM-NP elicits robust humoral and cellular immune responses, providing complete protection against lethal vaccinia virus challenge. Importantly, pre-existing antibodies resulting from prior infection with the VACV Tiantan strain utilized for smallpox prevention did not diminish vaccine efficacy. A single dose was sufficient to prevent both morbidity and mortality following a lethal MPXV challenge. These findings establish DAM-NP as a highly immunogenic and promising single-dose vaccine candidate for prevention of MPXV and other orthopoxvirus infections.","Run-Yu Yuan, Meng-Jun Li, Xiao-Bing Lin, Xin Zhang, Fei Yu, Xiao Zhang, Qi Qian, Yun Peng, De-Lin Li, Qing Zhao, Hui Zhang, Yang Yang, Chen-Guang Shen, Baisheng Li, Yin-Feng Kang",10.1016/j.celrep.2025.116358,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.celrep.2025.116358,Cell Reports,1,2025,no,"The study is conducted in preclinical models and does not involve human populations, thus failing the focus on human health groups.",yes,The study reports on the prevention of morbidity and mortality.,yes,This is a primary research study (preclinical trial).,no,"The paper describes 'preclinical models' and 'preclinical evaluations', which refers to non-human laboratory or animal research.",no,"The study does not include any human participants, such as pregnant women or children, as it uses preclinical models.",no,The study participants are non-human models and did not receive the vaccine during human pregnancy or childhood.,yes,The study involves an mpox vaccine candidate (DAM-NP).,Exclude,This is a preclinical study conducted in animal models rather than humans. It focuses on the efficacy of a nanoparticle vaccine candidate (DAM-NP) against lethal challenges but does not meet the population requirements for human subjects.,"Population Focus, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Population Focus,0.1,,,"  - ""prevent both morbidity and mortality following a lethal MPXV challenge.""",,,,"  - ""Preclinical evaluations""",,,,,,"  - ""DAM-NP, which incorporates bivalently fused M1 and A35 antigens conjugated to a self-assembled 24-meric ferritin nanoparticle.""",
Quadrivalent human papillomavirus vaccination and non-targeted infectious disease hospitalisation: Population-based self-controlled case series analysis,"Summary Background Claims of non-live vaccines having deleterious effects on non-targeted infectious disease and mortality among females persists. The majority of the available evidence is from West Africa and consists of observational studies and the interpretation and implications are controversial. Results from high-income countries have been conflicting. We evaluated the association between a human papillomavirus vaccine, a non-live vaccine primarily administered to pre-adolescent females, and non-targeted infectious disease in a high-income country. Methods We constructed a nationwide cohort of all Danish females 10 to 29 years of age during 2007 to 2016 with information on quadrivalent human papillomavirus vaccination status and infectious disease hospital contacts using national registers. Nested in this cohort, we conducted a self-controlled case series (SCCS) analysis comparing the rates of hospitalisation in a 90-day main risk period following the latest vaccination to reference period rates with adjustment for age and season. Findings We included 853,879 Danish-born females aged 10 to 29 years of age during the 2007 to 2016 study period in the study cohort. We identified a total of 65,293 infectious disease hospitalisations among 50,599 participants; 46,955 cases among 37,003 participants vaccinated during follow-up were included in the SCCS analysis. There was no statistically significantly increased risk of infectious disease hospitalisation in the 90-day main risk period (rate ratio 0.92, 95% CI 0.88 to 0.95). Interpretation Reassuringly, our large well-controlled study does not support that human papillomavirus vaccination increases the risk of non-targeted infectious disease in any clinically meaningful way. While our study does not provide evidence against adverse effects of other non-live vaccines, it does provide evidence against the claim that all non-live vaccines increase risk of heterologous infections in females. Funding The study was supported by the Novo Nordisk Foundation.","A. Hviid, A. Laksafoss",10.1016/j.lanepe.2021.100189,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.lanepe.2021.100189,The Lancet Regional Health - Europe,2,2021,yes,"The study includes females aged 10-29, which includes individuals under 18.",maybe,The study reports on infectious disease hospitalizations as a potential safety outcome (non-targeted effects) following vaccination.,yes,The study is a primary research study using a self-controlled case series analysis.,yes,The study is based on human participants (Danish females).,yes,The participants include children and adolescents starting at age 10.,yes,Vaccination occurred during childhood or adolescence for a portion of the cohort (ages 10-29).,no,"The vaccine studied is the quadrivalent human papillomavirus vaccine, not a vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,This study is a self-controlled case series evaluating the safety (non-targeted infectious disease hospitalisation) of the quadrivalent human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccine. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not investigate vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.,Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""We constructed a nationwide cohort of all Danish females 10 to 29 years of age""",,"  - ""We evaluated the association between a human papillomavirus vaccine, a non-live vaccine primarily administered to pre-adolescent females, and non-targeted infectious disease in a high-income country.""",,,,"  - ""We constructed a nationwide cohort of all Danish females 10 to 29 years of age""",,"  - ""We constructed a nationwide cohort of all Danish females 10 to 29 years of age""",,"  - ""We constructed a nationwide cohort of all Danish females 10 to 29 years of age""",,"  - ""We evaluated the association between a human papillomavirus vaccine, a non-live vaccine primarily administered to pre-adolescent females, and non-targeted infectious disease in a high-income country.""
