Mpox exposes fundamental obstacles for vaccinating children in outbreaks,"Summary box Children could not be included in mpox vaccination roll-out within the first 100 days of the latest Mpox outbreak in the Democratic Republic of Congo and are yet to be rolled out in many countries with rising cases, despite this age group accounting for a large proportion of suspected cases and deaths.","Neal Russell, R. Scourse, Sarah Galau, A. Abbara, Yuanqiong Hu, M. Amirtharajah, D. Laeken, D. Worku, Jennifer Stella, Alain Alsalhani, Natalie N Roberts",10.1136/bmjgh-2025-019009,https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjgh-2025-019009,BMJ Global Health,0,2025,yes,"The paper discusses children, who are not healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The paper identifies cases and deaths in the age group but does not report primary data on safety or effectiveness outcomes resulting from vaccination, as children were excluded from the rollout.",no,"This appears to be a commentary or editorial rather than a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,The discussion is focused on human populations (children) and public health policy.,yes,The paper's focus is on children.,no,"The text explicitly states that children 'could not be included' in the vaccination roll-out, meaning participants did not receive the vaccine.",maybe,"The paper mentions mpox vaccination, but it does not report data on specific vaccinia-based vaccines administered to the target group.",Exclude,"This paper is a commentary on why children were excluded from vaccination roll-outs during the mpox outbreak. It does not contain primary research or effectiveness/safety outcomes, and confirms that children were not actually vaccinated in the described period.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Vaccination Timing",Relevant Outcomes,0.1,"  - ""Children could not be included in mpox vaccination roll-out within the first 100 days of the latest Mpox outbreak in the Democratic Republic of Congo""",,"  - ""Chi