Immunogenicity and Safety of Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA) Vaccine—A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials,"Prevention of mpox has become an important public health interest. We aimed to evaluate the safety and immunogenicity of the Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA) vaccine. We conducted a systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized-controlled trials (RCTs) comparing MVA versus no intervention, placebo, or another vaccine. Outcomes included safety and immunogenicity outcomes. We also performed a systematic review of RCTs evaluating various MVA regimens. Fifteen publications were included in the quantitative meta-analysis. All but one (ACAM2000) compared MVA with placebo. We found that cardiovascular adverse events following two MVA doses were significantly more common compared to placebo (relative risk [RR] 4.07, 95% confidence interval [CI] 1.10–15.10), though serious adverse events (SAEs) were not significantly different. Following a single MVA dose, no difference was demonstrated in any adverse event outcomes. Seroconversion rates were significantly higher compared with placebo after a single or two doses. None of the RCTs evaluated clinical effectiveness in preventing mpox. This meta-analysis provides reassuring results concerning the immunogenicity and safety of MVA. Further studies are needed to confirm the immunogenicity of a single dose and its clinical effectiveness. A single vaccine dose may be considered according to vaccine availability, with preference for two doses.","Lior Nave, I. Margalit, Noam Tau, I. Cohen, D. Yelin, F. Lienert, D. Yahav",10.3390/vaccines11091410,https://doi.org/10.3390/vaccines11091410,Vaccines,20,2023,no,The study summarizes 15 RCTs and shows no evidence of including participants other than healthy adults.,yes,The study reports on immunogenicity and several safety outcomes including cardiovascular and serious adverse events.,yes,This is a systematic review and meta-analysis.,yes,The study is a systematic review of human randomized-controlled trials.,no,"There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents in the study.",no,"The study does not indicate participants were vaccinated during childhood, pregnancy, or the neonatal period.",yes,The study focuses on the Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA) vaccine.,Exclude,"This systematic review and meta-analysis evaluates the safety and immunogenicity of the MVA vaccine across 15 RCTs. However, the study does not include the required target populations of pregnant women or children and instead focuses on adult trials.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized-controlled trials (RCTs)""
