Comparing the maternal and neonatal outcomes in vaccinated and unvaccinated pregnant women against COVID-19: a retrospective cohort study,"Following the emergence of COVID-19 disease, and considering the limited number of studies regarding vaccination among pregnant women, as well as the differences between the vaccine administered in Iran and those used in other countries, this study aimed to compare maternal and neonatal outcomes in vaccinated and unvaccinated women against COVID-19 disease. This retrospective cohort study was conducted at the comprehensive healthcare centers of Rafsanjan city. Initially, the contact information of expectant mothers who were pregnant between June 22, 2021, and December 22, 2021, was obtained through Iran’s integrated healthcare system (Sib). Subsequently, the required information was collected via a checklist during phone interviews. Out of 969 pregnant women, after applying the inclusion and exclusion criteria, 610 subjects were included in the study. Among these, 330 were unvaccinated, while the remaining participants had received the inactivated COVID-19 vaccine prior to or during pregnancy. Maternal and neonatal outcomes were compared between vaccinated and unvaccinated women. Data analysis was performed using SPSS version 26, employing one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA), Tukey’s multiple comparison test, Fisher’s exact test, Chi-square test, and multiple logistic regression. The findings indicated that vaccination against COVID-19 in pregnant women significantly increased the risk of neonatal jaundice (P < 0.05). Conversely, the miscarriage rate among these women was significantly lower (P < 0.05). No adverse outcomes were observed including hypertensive disorders, gestational diabetes, maternal hospitalization, maternal COVID-19 infection, preterm labor, premature rupture of membranes, perinatal mortality, admission to the neonatal intensive care unit, and low birth weight. COVID-19 Vaccination is recommended for pregnant women to mitigate adverse neonatal and maternal outcomes.","Z. Gholami, Maryam Mohseni, Pouran Allahbakhshi Nasab",10.1186/s12884-025-07462-x,https://doi.org/10.1186/s12884-025-07462-x,BMC Pregnancy and Childbirth,0,2025,yes,"The study is specifically focused on pregnant women and their neonates, which means it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study reports outcomes such as neonatal jaundice and miscarriage related to COVID-19 vaccination, but it does not report outcomes related to mpox infection, hospitalization, or death.",yes,"The study is a retrospective cohort study, which is an eligible primary research design.",yes,The study involved human subjects (pregnant women and neonates).,yes,The participants include pregnant women and their neonates.,yes,Participants received the vaccine prior to or during pregnancy.,no,"The study investigated the inactivated COVID-19 vaccine, not a vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,This study focuses on outcomes following COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant women. It does not meet inclusion criteria because it does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccines or report on the prevention or safety of such vaccines.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""this study aimed to compare maternal and neonatal outcomes in vaccinated and unvaccinated women against COVID-19 disease.""",,"  - ""this study aimed to compare maternal and neonatal outcomes in vaccinated and unvaccinated women against COVID-19 disease.""",,"  - ""This retrospective cohort study was conducted at the comprehensive healthcare centers of Rafsanjan city.""",,"  - ""this study aimed to compare maternal and neonatal outcomes in vaccinated and unvaccinated women against COVID-19 disease.""",,"  - ""this study aimed to compare maternal and neonatal outcomes in vaccinated and unvaccinated women against COVID-19 disease.""",,"  - ""the remaining participants had received the inactivated COVID-19 vaccine prior to or during pregnancy.""",,"  - ""the remaining participants had received the inactivated COVID-19 vaccine prior to or during pregnancy.""",
IMVAMUNE®: modified vaccinia Ankara strain as an attenuated smallpox vaccine,"Smallpox vaccines based on replicating vaccinia virus are known to elicit rare yet serious adverse events, particularly in human populations with immune deficiency, atopic dermatitis and at the extremes of age. A vaccine that induces protective immune responses equivalent to first-generation smallpox vaccines while reducing the risk for severe adverse events is critical for a national stockpile of smallpox vaccines. Modified vaccinia Ankara (MVA) has been proposed as an immediate solution for vaccination of high-risk individuals. Bavarian Nordic’s vaccine MVA-BN® (IMVAMUNE®) is a MVA strain that is replication incompetent in mammalian cell lines. IMVAMUNE has been administered to more than 1900 human subjects to date, including high-risk populations (e.g., people diagnosed with atopic dermatitis or infected with HIV) in which standard replicating vaccines are contraindicated. We review the Phase I clinical trial safety profile and immune responses and compare them with other smallpox vaccines, including ACAM2000™ and Dryvax®.","J. Kennedy, R. Greenberg",10.1586/14760584.8.1.13,https://doi.org/10.1586/14760584.8.1.13,Expert Review of Vaccines,127,2009,yes,The study includes high-risk populations such as individuals with atopic dermatitis or HIV.,yes,The study reviews safety profiles and immune responses.,yes,The study is an evidence synthesis (review) of Phase I clinical trial data.,yes,The study reviews trials involving over 1900 human subjects.,no,"The paper does not explicitly report data on pregnant women, neonates, or children. While it mentions the 'extremes of age' as a risk factor for traditional vaccines, the specific high-risk populations included in the IMVAMUNE trials reviewed are limited to atopic dermatitis and HIV patients, which in early Phase I trials are typically adults.",no,"There is no evidence that participants received the vaccine during the specified periods (pregnancy, childhood, etc.) because the target population criteria were not met.",yes,"The study evaluates IMVAMUNE, a modified vaccinia Ankara strain vaccine.",Exclude,"The paper is a review of Phase I clinical trials for the IMVAMUNE vaccine. While it includes human safety data for high-risk groups like those with HIV or atopic dermatitis, it does not specifically include or report outcomes for pregnant women, children, or adolescents, failing the target population criteria.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""IMVAMUNE has been administered to more than 1900 human subjects to date, including high-risk populations (e.g., people diagnosed with atopic dermatitis or infected with HIV)""",,"  - ""We review the Phase I clinical trial safety profile and immune responses and compare them with other smallpox vaccines, including ACAM2000™ and Dryvax®.""",,"  - ""We review the Phase I clinical trial safety profile and immune responses and compare them with other smallpox vaccines, including ACAM2000™ and Dryvax®.""",,"  - ""IMVAMUNE has been administered to more than 1900 human subjects to date, including high-risk populations (e.g., people diagnosed with atopic dermatitis or infected with HIV)""",,"  - ""Smallpox vaccines based on replicating vaccinia virus are known to elicit rare yet serious adverse events, particularly in human populations with immune deficiency, atopic dermatitis and at the extremes of age.""
  - ""IMVAMUNE has been administered to more than 1900 human subjects to date, including high-risk populations (e.g., people diagnosed with atopic dermatitis or infected with HIV)""",,"  - ""IMVAMUNE has been administered to more than 1900 human subjects to date, including high-risk populations (e.g., people diagnosed with atopic dermatitis or infected with HIV)""
