Brincidofovir in the Era of Mpox,"ABSTRACT Introduction Mpox (formerly monkeypox), a systemic infection caused by the mpox virus (MPXV), has become a global problem of increasing concern. There are currently no antiviral treatments that have been shown to be safe and effective for the treatment of mpox. Areas covered Brincidofovir is a lipid-modified acyclic nucleotide with in vitro and in vivo activity against multiple DNA viruses, including MPXV. It is licensed in the US and Canada for the treatment of human smallpox disease in adults and children, including neonates. It has been used under FDA-authorized emergency use for patients in the US with severe mpox. The authors review the antiviral activity, clinical development, pharmacokinetics, safety and efficacy of brincidofovir and its potential as an mpox treatment. A comprehensive review was conducted in PubMed, Science Direct, Embase and Google Scholar. Expert opinion Research is needed to better understand mpox epidemiology, natural history, antiviral therapy and vaccines. Brincidofovir is being evaluated in a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial under the Mpox Study in Africa (MOSA) protocol. Additional studies will assist in clarification of single vs. combination therapy, safety and populations most likely to benefit, as well as the management of treatment complications such as immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS).","J. McCarty, David Cassie, S. Kodihalli, Efe Aigbedo, Jing Tian, Kevin Yeo, Vanja Komlenovic, Lovelyn Charles, Bojan Drobic, Bob Warnock",10.1080/14787210.2025.2532029,https://doi.org/10.1080/14787210.2025.2532029,Expert Review of Anti-Infective Therapy,8,2025,yes,"The study reviews brincidofovir which is licensed for adults and children, including neonates.",yes,The study reviews the safety and efficacy of brincidofovir.,yes,The paper is a comprehensive review based on searches in multiple databases.,yes,The study discusses human clinical development and use of the drug.,yes,The paper specifically mentions the drug's use and licensure in children and neonates.,no,"The paper focuses on the antiviral treatment Brincidofovir, not vaccination timing for mpox/smallpox vaccines.",no,"The study focuses on Brincidofovir, an antiviral treatment, rather than vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This paper is a comprehensive review of the antiviral drug brincidofovir. While it discusses pediatric populations, it does not evaluate mpox or smallpox vaccines, failing the mandatory vaccine intervention criteria.","Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,,,"  - ""The authors review the antiviral activity, clinical development, pharmacokinetics, safety and efficacy of brincidofovir and its potential as an mpox treatment.""",,"  - ""A comprehensive review was conducted in PubMed, Science Direct, Embase and Google Scholar.""",,"  - ""The authors review the antiviral activity, clinical development, pharmacokinetics, safety and efficacy of brincidofovir and its potential as an mpox treatment.""",,,,"  - ""The authors review the antiviral activity, clinical development, pharmacokinetics, safety and efficacy of brincidofovir and its potential as an mpox treatment.""
  - ""Research is needed to better understand mpox epidemiology, natural history, antiviral therapy and vaccines.""",,"  - ""The authors review the antiviral activity, clinical development, pharmacokinetics, safety and efficacy of brincidofovir and its potential as an mpox treatment.""
