"Comparison of the risk of hospital admission, need for ventilation, sepsis, pneumonitis and death among the recent monkeypox outbreak and historical outbreaks","Background The course of monkeypox can be severe. Our aim was to retrospectively compare the risk of hospital admission, the need for ventilation, sepsis, pneumonitis and death between the recent outbreak and historical outbreaks. Materials and Methods Cases of monkeypox were retrieved from the TriNetX database and assigned to either cohort I (recent outbreak between May 1st and September 16th, 2022) and cohort II (historical outbreaks before May 1st, 2022). After matching for age distribution, statistical analysis was performed. Results Of 640 patients with monkeypox 81 subjects per cohort remained after matching (mean age±standard deviation = 36.1±18.3 years). Within 56 days after diagnosis 10 patients per cohort were hospitalized (12.4%) and/or developed sepsis (12.4%). The risk of ventilation and pneumonitis were significantly lower among cohort I compared with cohort II (0 vs. 10 cases; risk difference = 12.4%; p = 0.001; Log-Rank test). No cases of death were recorded. Conclusion Even though monkeypox provides a risk of severe courses, the infection is self-limiting in most cases. Unlike past outbreaks, the risk of ventilation and pneumonitis may be relatively low among recent outbreaks.","Florian Gaertner, S. Preissner, William A. Petri, O. Atolani, M. Heiland, S. Nahles, R. Preissner, M. Hertel",10.1186/s12879-023-08599-w,https://doi.org/10.1186/s12879-023-08599-w,BMC Infectious Diseases,4,2023,yes,"The study population consists of individuals diagnosed with monkeypox, not healthy adults.",no,"While the study reports hospitalization and death, these are descriptive clinical outcomes of the disease across different time periods rather than effectiveness or safety outcomes tied to vaccination.",yes,This is a retrospective cohort study using database records.,yes,The study uses data from human patients retrieved from the TriNetX database.,maybe,"The study reports a mean age of 36.1 with a standard deviation of 18.3, which mathematically allows for the inclusion of adolescents or children, but the paper does not explicitly report or analyze these populations.",no,The study does not mention or investigate vaccination status or timing.,no,The study is entirely focused on disease outcomes of monkeypox outbreaks and does not involve vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,This study is a retrospective comparison of monkeypox clinical severity between historical and recent outbreaks using human database records. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not involve or report on the use of vaccines and does not explicitly confirm inclusion of the targeted pediatric or pregnant populations.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Cases of monkeypox were retrieved from the TriNetX database""
