A Case of Pityriasis Rosea Following Monkeypox Vaccination,"Introduction: Pityriasis rosea (PR) is a well-known but uncommon, benign papulosquamous skin condition that often occurs after viral infections or vaccinations. The most commonly implicated vaccine is smallpox. Monkeypox (MPX), a related virus to smallpox within the orthopox family, has recently grown in public health concern. We present here a case of PR following MPX vaccination in an African-American man.
Case Report: A 32-year old African-American man presented for evaluation of a one-month history of rash that first appeared as an isolated itching spot on his right mid-back, followed by papular eruptions on his trunk with extremity sparing. He denied recent illnesses, medication use including NSAIDs, or history of similar rash. He did report receiving his first MPX dose about one week prior to rash onset. Pityriasis rosea was diagnosed based on clinical presentation and suspected secondary to the patient’s MPX vaccine course.
Conclusion: There are currently two available vaccines for MPX protection in the United States, both of which are vaccinia-based and developmentally related to the smallpox vaccine. Given the known association of smallpox and PR, PR reactions following MPX vaccination may occur.","K. Beiter, Anna Swigert, B. Elewski",10.25251/skin.6.6.11,https://doi.org/10.25251/skin.6.6.11,SKIN The Journal of Cutaneous Medicine,0,2022,no,"The study describes a 32-year-old male. However, this criterion asks if the study is NOT exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults. Since the study only describes a single 32-year-old adult, it is limited to an adult population.",yes,"The study reports a safety outcome, specifically a diagnosis of Pityriasis rosea following vaccination.",yes,The paper is a case report.,yes,The study involves a human patient.,no,"The participant is a 32-year-old man, which is not a child, adolescent, neonate, or pregnant woman.",no,The participant received the vaccine as an adult (age 32).,yes,The study involves a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine.,Exclude,"This case report describes a 32-year-old man who developed Pityriasis rosea after receiving an mpox vaccine. The study fails to meet the target population criteria, as it focuses exclusively on a healthy adult rather than pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""A 32-year old African-American man presented for evaluation of a one-month history of rash""",,"  - ""Pityriasis rosea was diagnosed based on clinical presentation and suspected secondary to the patient’s MPX vaccine course.""",,"  - ""We present here a case of PR following MPX vaccination""",,"  - ""A 32-year old African-American man presented for evaluation of a one-month history of rash""",,"  - ""A 32-year old African-American man presented for evaluation of a one-month history of rash""",,"  - ""A 32-year old African-American man presented for evaluation of a one-month history of rash""
  - ""He did report receiving his first MPX dose about one week prior to rash onset.""",,"  - ""There are currently two available vaccines for MPX protection in the United States, both of which are vaccinia-based""",
International Conference on Pharmaceutical & Health Sciences 2024,"Background: Mpox (Monkeypox), a zoonotic Orthopoxvirus, produces a disease in humans similar to smallpox but with a significantly lower death rate.The largest outbreak in history began in May 2022 and has since spread rapidly worldwide, with over 70 countries reporting cases.Objective: The objective of this research work is to highlight the significant role of 3D-printed microneedles in the delivery of Mpox vaccines.Materials and methods: Vaccines can be administered before and after exposure to the virus, with optimal protection achieved through pre-exposure vaccination.Data indicate that vaccination within 4 days after first exposure to Mpox can reduce the risk of disease by up to 85%.Even individuals who have previously received smallpox vaccines remain at risk for Mpox.The MVA-BN (Modified Vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic) vaccine, approved in 2019 for the prevention of smallpox and monkeypox in the USA and Canada, is administered subcutaneously in the upper arm.Recent studies show that 3D-printed microneedles, measuring a few hundred micrometers in height, can penetrate the skin's outer layer without reaching deeper tissues, thereby delivering therapeutics effectively and minimally invasively.Results: Research demonstrates that 3D-printed microneedle delivery systems enhance skin cargo retention by up to 50% compared to traditional injection methods, activate immune cells more effectively, and elicit stronger immune responses.Microneedles minimize discomfort and are suitable for self-administration, which reduces reliance on healthcare providers and enhances vaccination accessibility.Conclusion: Health systems must continuously enhance awareness and update preparedness plans for Mpox outbreaks.This includes establishing clear protocols for surveillance, testing, vaccination, and treatment, along with strategies for communication and coordination among various stakeholders.",,10.17576/mh.2025.s2001,https://doi.org/10.17576/mh.2025.s2001,Medicine &amp; Health,0,2025,no,"The study does not include any description of human participants, thus failing to meet the requirement for focusing on non-adult or pregnant populations.",yes,"The paper reports outcomes related to the effectiveness of vaccine delivery systems, including cargo retention and immune response levels.",no,The work serves as a technology highlight or summary of laboratory findings rather than a primary clinical study (like an RCT or cohort study) or a systematic review.,no,"The abstract discusses microneedle penetration and immune activation which, along with the results provided, suggests laboratory or animal research rather than a clinical human study.",no,"The paper does not mention or include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as study participants.",no,"Since no target populations (children, pregnant women) were included, there is no evidence of vaccination timing during these periods.",yes,The study explicitly references the MVA-BN vaccinia-based mpox vaccine.,Exclude,"This research focuses on the development of 3D-printed microneedle delivery systems for Mpox vaccines rather than evaluating outcomes in human populations. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not include participants from the target populations, such as pregnant women or children.","Population Focus, Study Design, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""The objective of this research work is to highlight the significant role of 3D-printed microneedles in the delivery of Mpox vaccines.Materials""",,"  - ""Research demonstrates that 3D-printed microneedle delivery systems enhance skin cargo retention by up to 50% compared to traditional injection methods,""
  - ""activate immune cells more effectively, and elicit stronger immune responses.Microneedles""",,"  - ""The objective of this research work is to highlight the significant role of 3D-printed microneedles in the delivery of Mpox vaccines.Materials""",,"  - ""The objective of this research work is to highlight the significant role of 3D-printed microneedles in the delivery of Mpox vaccines.Materials""
