  - ""A further 100 vaccine-naive individuals, aged 18-30 years,""",,"  - ""We aimed to compare the safety and immunogenicity of the new cell-cultured smallpox vaccine (CCSV) to that of the calf-lymph derived vaccine (as a positive control) in 350 healthy, adult volunteers.""",
Efficacy and Safety of Anti-Respiratory Syncytial Virus Monoclonal Antibody Nirsevimab in Neonates: A Real-World Monocentric Study,"Background: RSV remains a leading cause of infant hospitalization worldwide, and the recently approved nirsevimab could represent an effective and safe prophylactic strategy to prevent severe infections in the general neonatal population. Objectives: We conducted a retrospective observational monocentric pilot study in a mixed preterm/term birth cohort to add real-world evidence of the efficacy and safety of nirsevimab in preventing severe RSV infection. Methods: We included a total of 2035 consecutive infants admitted to the Neonatal Unit, University Hospital “San Giovanni di Dio e Ruggi d’Aragona”, Salerno, Italy, from November 2024 to April 2025. We evaluated 30-day safety profiles and season-wide RSV infection rates, and the outcomes were also compared to newborns’ birth rate in the two previous seasons (2022–2023 and 2023–2024). Results: After the introduction of nirsevimab, a lower RSV infection rate was reported compared to previous seasons, and no adverse effects were observed. Compared to previous seasons, the clinical outcomes were more favorable, as only one unvaccinated neonate with RSV infection required invasive ventilation. Conclusions: In this real-world analysis, we demonstrated a good short-term safety profile of nirsevimab, as well as a potentially high efficacy in the general neonatal population with lower RSV infection incidence. However, future studies are needed to better assess its long-term safety and season-wide efficacy.","Maria Costantino, M. B. Marongiu, M. G. Corbo, A. M. Della Corte, Anna Rita Frascogna, A. Plantulli, Federica Campana, Luigi Fortino, E. Santoro, E. A. Vozzella, Walter Longanella, G. Boccia, Amelia Filippelli, Francesco De Caro",10.3390/vaccines13080838,https://doi.org/10.3390/vaccines13080838,Vaccines,0,2025,yes,The study focuses on infants in a Neonatal Unit.,no,"The study reports outcomes for Respiratory Syncytial Virus (RSV), not mpox.",yes,This is a primary research study described as a retrospective observational pilot study.,yes,The study is conducted in a human population (infants) in a hospital setting.,yes,The study specifically includes neonates.,no,"While participants received an injection during the neonatal period, it was not an mpox vaccine.",no,"The study evaluates Nirsevimab, which is a monoclonal antibody for RSV, not a vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,The study examines the efficacy and safety of Nirsevimab for preventing RSV infections in neonates. It is excluded because it does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccines and does not report outcomes relevant to mpox prevention.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""2035 consecutive infants admitted to the Neonatal Unit,""",,"  - ""evaluated 30-day safety profiles and season-wide RSV infection rates,""",,"  - ""retrospective observational monocentric pilot study""",,"  - ""2035 consecutive infants admitted to the Neonatal Unit,""",,"  - ""2035 consecutive infants admitted to the Neonatal Unit,""",,"  - ""2035 consecutive infants admitted to the Neonatal Unit,""",,,
Asymptomatic Mpox Virus Infection in Subjects Presenting for MVA-BN Vaccine,n.a.,"G. Matusali, V. Mazzotta, P. Piselli, Aurora Bettini, Francesca Colavita, S. Coen, F. Vaia, E. Girardi, A. Antinori, F. Maggi",10.1093/cid/ciad414,https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciad414,Clinical Infectious Diseases,11,2023,no,"The title specifies the study population as subjects presenting for MVA-BN vaccine, with no indication of pregnant participants or non-healthy adults.",yes,"The study investigates asymptomatic mpox virus infection, which is an effectiveness outcome related to vaccine performance.",yes,"The description of subjects presenting for vaccination suggests a primary research study, likely a cohort or cross-sectional study.",yes,The term 'Subjects' implies the study involves human participants.,no,"There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents in the study title or available content.",no,"There is no information confirming that vaccination occurred during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,"The study explicitly evaluates the MVA-BN vaccine, which is a vaccinia-based vaccine.",Exclude,"Although the study evaluates a relevant vaccine and infection outcome, it does not specifically include children, pregnant women, or neonates, failing the required target population criteria. The subjects are likely limited to healthy non-pregnant adults as is standard for MVA-BN vaccine implementation studies.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
Maternal and Neonatal Outcomes in Pregnant Women Vaccinated Against COVID-19 Compared to an Unvaccinated Group,"Introduction and Objectives Immunizing pregnant women against COVID-19 is crucial due to their heightened risk of severe outcomes. However, data on vaccination’s clinical effects during pregnancy are limited. This study evaluated maternal and neonatal outcomes in vaccinated pregnant women, stratified by vaccine doses, compared to unvaccinated controls. Patients and Methods This retrospective cohort study analyzed pregnant women without a history of COVID-19 infection who delivered at Amir-Al-Momenin Hospital in Semnan, Iran (2020–2022). Data on demographics, obstetric history, vaccination status, maternal complications, and neonatal outcomes were extracted from hospital records. Results The cohort included 300 vaccinated and 104 unvaccinated women with comparable baseline characteristics. Overall maternal complication rates were similar between groups (41.0% vs 44.2%). However, miscarriage was reported in 12/300 (4.0%) vaccinated women compared to 0/104 (0%) in the unvaccinated group (p=0.038). No other maternal outcomes differed significantly. Neonatal 5-minute Apgar scores were slightly lower in the vaccinated group (mean difference −0.25, 95% CI [−0.48 to −0.02]; p=0.031). Conversely, intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR) was significantly less frequent in neonates born to vaccinated mothers (2.0% vs 9.6%; Odds Ratio 0.19, 95% CI [0.07 to 0.54]; p=0.005). The number of vaccine doses received was not associated with maternal or neonatal outcomes. Conclusion In this single-center cohort, COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy was not associated with an overall increase in adverse maternal complications and was linked to a lower risk of neonatal IUGR. However, the findings also indicated a higher incidence of miscarriage and slightly lower 5-minute Apgar scores in the vaccinated group, which warrant further investigation. These results, while largely reassuring, highlight the need for continued surveillance and larger studies to fully characterize the safety profile of COVID-19 vaccines in pregnancy.","Tooba Nourian, S. Sarandili, Safa Mousavi, M. Mirmohammadkhani, M. Rahmanian",10.2147/IJWH.S548444,https://doi.org/10.2147/IJWH.S548444,International Journal of Women's Health,0,2025,yes,The study specifically focuses on pregnant women and their neonates.,yes,"The study reports on maternal complications, miscarriage, and neonatal outcomes like Apgar scores and IUGR.",yes,The study is a retrospective cohort study.,yes,This is a human study involving patients at a hospital.,yes,The study population consists of pregnant women and neonates.,yes,The participants received the vaccine during pregnancy.,no,"The study focuses exclusively on COVID-19 vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This retrospective cohort study evaluates the safety of COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant women and neonates. However, it does not evaluate vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines, which is a required intervention for inclusion.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""This retrospective cohort study analyzed pregnant women without a history of COVID-19 infection""
