"Two cases of MPXV infection during pregnancy in heterosexual cisgender women without classic cutaneous lesions, Northern California, 2022","As part of an epidemiologic survey, we screened remnant samples collected for STI testing for mpox virus. We identified two cases of presumed MPXV infection in pregnant, heterosexual cisgender women. Here, we describe their pregnancy and birth outcomes. Both patients required induction of labor and experienced labor complicated by chorioamnionitis.","Zachary T. Renfro, C. Contag, Jacky Lu, D. Solis, ChunHong Huang, M. Sahoo, F. Yamamoto, Jordan K. Mah, Morris S. Jones, Jennifer Lin, V. Levy, B. Pinsky",10.1016/j.idcr.2023.e01881,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.idcr.2023.e01881,IDCases,3,2023,yes,"The study specifically focuses on pregnant women, not healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"While the study reports outcomes like chorioamnionitis and labor induction, these are associated with mpox infection rather than vaccination safety or effectiveness. Since no vaccine was administered, the relevant vaccine outcomes are not reported.",yes,The study is a case report/series describing two specific patients.,yes,The study involves human subjects (pregnant women).,yes,The participants are pregnant women.,no,There is no mention of the participants receiving any vaccine; the study identifies cases of mpox infection only.,no,The study mentions infection with MPXV but does not involve or test any vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This paper describes two cases of mpox infection during pregnancy and the resulting birth outcomes, but it does not involve any vaccination. Because the participants were not vaccinated, it fails criteria related to vaccination timing and vaccine type.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccination Timing,0.1,"  - ""We identified two cases of presumed MPXV infection in pregnant, heterosexual cisgender women.""",,"  - ""describe their pregnancy and birth outcomes. Both patients required induction of labor and experienced labor complicated by chorioamnionitis.""",,"  - ""We identified two cases of presumed MPXV infection in pregnant, heterosexual cisgender women.""",,"  - ""We identified two cases of presumed MPXV infection in pregnant, heterosexual cisgender women.""",,"  - ""We identified two cases of presumed MPXV infection in pregnant, heterosexual cisgender women.""",,"  - ""We identified two cases of presumed MPXV infection in pregnant, heterosexual cisgender women.""",,"  - ""We identified two cases of presumed MPXV infection in pregnant, heterosexual cisgender women.""",
Breakthrough infections after post-exposure vaccination against Monkeypox,"Background A third-generation smallpox vaccine was recommended in France for individuals who had a high-risk contact with a PCR-confirmed Monkeypox patient. We aimed to describe the outcomes of high-risk contacts receiving third-generation smallpox vaccine as an early post-exposure ring vaccination (EPRV) especially tolerance and potential breakthrough infections after the first dose. Methods We performed an observational analysis of all consecutive individuals vaccinated with the IMVANEX smallpox vaccine after a high-risk contact defined as close skin-to-skin or mucosal contact and/or indirect contact on textile or surface and/or droplets exposure defined by a contact at less than 2 meters during at least 3 hours with a PCR-confirmed Monkeypox patient. Results Between May 27th and July 13th, 2022, 276 individuals received one dose of IMVANEX with a median delay of 11 days [IQR 8-14] after exposure with a confirmed Monkeypox patient. Mode of exposure was droplets for 240 patients (91%), indirect contact for 189 (71%) and unprotected sexual intercourse for 146 (54%). Most of the patients were men (91%, n=250) and men who have sex with men (88%, n=233). The vaccine was well tolerated with no severe adverse event. Among the 276 vaccinated individuals, 12 (4%) had a confirmed Monkeypox breakthrough infection with no severe infection. Ten out of 12 patients developed a Monkeypox infection in the five days following vaccination and two had a breakthrough infection at 22 and 25 days. Conclusion EPRV with a third-generation smallpox vaccine was well tolerated and effective against Monkeypox but did not completely prevent breakthrough infections.","Michael Thy, Nathan Peiffer-Smadja M.D., Morgane Mailhe PhD, Laura Kramer M.D., Valentine Marie Ferré M.D., Nadhira Houhou-Fidouh M.D., Hassan Tarhini M.D., Chloé Bertin M.D., Anne-Lise Beaumont M.D., Mathilde Garé M.D., M. D. L. Pluart, Ségolène Perrineau M.D., Mayda Rahi M.D., Laurène Deconinck M.D., Bao Phung M.D., Bastien Mollo M.D., Marie Cortier M.D., Mélanie Cresta M.D., M. De, L. Porte, Des Vaux, S. M.D., Cécile Somarriba M.D., F. M.D., Charlotte Charpentier PhD, Yazdan Yazdanpanah PhD, Jade Ghosn PhD",10.1101/2022.08.03.22278233,https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.08.03.22278233,medRxiv,14,2022,maybe,"The study focuses on high-risk contacts of mpox patients, primarily men who have sex with men. There is no explicit indication that it is exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults, though the demographics described suggest an adult population.",yes,The study reports on vaccine tolerance (adverse events) and breakthrough mpox infections (effectiveness).,yes,The study is an observational analysis (cohort study) of consecutive individuals.,yes,The study is an analysis of 276 human individuals.,maybe,"The paper does not state whether any participants were children, adolescents (<18 years), or pregnant women. It describes the population as majority men and MSM, but does not provide ages or pregnancy status for the remaining 9-12% of the cohort.",maybe,"As the inclusion of children, adolescents, or pregnant women is not confirmed, it is unknown if vaccination occurred during those specific periods.",yes,"The study uses IMVANEX, which is a third-generation smallpox vaccine.",Include,"This observational study of 276 individuals reports on the safety and effectiveness of the IMVANEX vaccine after mpox exposure. However, it does not explicitly confirm the inclusion of children, adolescents, or pregnant women within its cohort, leaving the target population criteria uncertain.",,,3.4,"  - ""Most of the patients were men (91%, n=250) and men who have sex with men (88%, n=233).""
