Safety of immunization during pregnancy: a review of the evidence of selected inactivated and live attenuated vaccines.,"Vaccine-preventable infectious diseases are responsible for significant maternal, neonatal, and young infant morbidity and mortality. While there is emerging scientific evidence, as well as theoretical considerations, indicating that certain vaccines are safe for pregnant women and fetuses, policy formulation is challenging because of perceived potential risks to the fetus. This report presents an overview of available evidence on pregnant women vaccination safety monitoring in pregnant women, from both published literature and ongoing surveillance programs. Safety data were reviewed for vaccines against diseases which increase morbidity in pregnant women, their fetus or infant as well as vaccines which are used in mass vaccination campaigns against diseases. They include inactivated seasonal and pandemic influenza, mono- and combined meningococcal polysaccharide and conjugated vaccines, tetanus toxoid and acellular pertussis combination vaccines, as well as monovalent or combined rubella, oral poliomyelitis virus and yellow fever vaccines. No evidence of adverse pregnancy outcomes has been identified from immunization of pregnant women with these vaccines.
Copyright © 2014 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.","B. Keller-Stanislawski, J. Englund, G. Kang, P. Mangtani, K. Neuzil, H. Nohynek, R. Pless, P. Lambach, P. Zuber",10.1016/j.vaccine.2014.09.052,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2014.09.052,Vaccine,216,2014,yes,The study specifically focuses on pregnant women and the safety of vaccination during pregnancy.,yes,The study reviews safety data and pregnancy outcomes associated with vaccination.,yes,The paper is a review article summarizing evidence on vaccine safety.,yes,The review focuses on human vaccination safety evidence.,yes,The study population consists of pregnant women.,yes,The participants received vaccines specifically during pregnancy.,no,"The review covers influenza, meningococcal, tetanus, pertussis, rubella, oral poliomyelitis, and yellow fever vaccines, but does not mention vaccinia, mpox, or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This review provides safety data for several vaccines administered during pregnancy, but it does not include vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines, thus failing the mandatory vaccine type criterion.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""indicating that certain vaccines are safe for pregnant women and fetuses,""",,"  - ""This report presents an overview of available evidence on pregnant women vaccination safety monitoring""
