Malaria in pregnancy: adverse pregnancy outcomes and the future of prevention,"Malaria in pregnancy (MiP) poses a dangerous health risk to both mothers and their fetuses, causing severe outcomes such as preterm delivery, intrauterine growth restriction, miscarriage, stillbirth, and neonatal and maternal death. Plasmodium falciparum infected erythrocytes sequester in placental intervillous spaces causing placental malaria (PM), eliciting inflammatory responses associated with severe sequelae. Current MiP prevention strategies have improved pregnancy outcomes, but serious morbidity and mortality persist. Vaccines to prevent MiP and PM are under development and are expected to improve pregnancy outcomes. To prepare for safety and efficacy trials of these vaccines, the incidence of adverse pregnancy outcomes including those caused by MiP should be documented at clinical sites. This review summarizes reported key adverse pregnancy outcomes attributable to MiP, providing important baseline context to define measurable safety and efficacy endpoints for malaria vaccine trials in pregnancy.","Anne D. Berhe, Justin Y. A. Doritchamou, Patrick E. Duffy",10.3389/fitd.2023.1229735,https://doi.org/10.3389/fitd.2023.1229735,Frontiers in Tropical Diseases,24,2023,yes,The study focuses on Malaria in pregnancy and its effects on mothers and fetuses.,no,"The study reports on adverse pregnancy outcomes such as preterm delivery and neonatal death, but these are outcomes of malaria (MiP), not mpox or vaccinia-based vaccination safety/effectiveness.",yes,The paper describes itself as a review summarizing reported outcomes.,yes,The study discusses outcomes in pregnant women and their fetuses.,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women and neonatal outcomes.,no,The study discusses vaccines that are under development and provides baseline context for future trials. It does not involve participants who have already received a vaccine during the relevant periods.,no,"The study is focused on malaria vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,This paper is a review focused on malaria in pregnancy and the development of malaria vaccines. It does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines and therefore does not meet the specified vaccine type or outcome requirements.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Malaria in pregnancy (MiP) poses a dangerous health risk to both mothers and their fetuses,""",,"  - ""Malaria in pregnancy (MiP) poses a dangerous health risk to both mothers and their fetuses,""
