Comparison of Maternal–Fetal Outcomes among Unvaccinated and Vaccinated Pregnant Women with COVID-19,"Abstract Background: This study sought to elucidate whether COVID-19 vaccination, during gestation or before conception, entails a decreased incidence of severe COVID-19 disease during pregnancy. Methods: This retrospective cohort study included all pregnant women that were followed up at a tertiary University Hospital with SARS-CoV-2 infection diagnosed between 1 March 2020 and 30 July 2022. The primary outcome of the study was to compare maternal and perinatal outcomes in unvaccinated and vaccinated pregnant patients with SARS-CoV-2 infection. Results: A total of 487 pregnant women with SARS-CoV-2 infection were included. SARS-CoV-2 infection during the third trimester of pregnancy was associated with an 89% lower probability of positive cord-blood SARS-CoV-2 IgG antibodies (OR 0.112; 95% CI 0.039–0.316), compared with infection during the first or the second trimester. Vaccinated pregnant women (201 (41.27%)) with COVID-19 had an 80% lower risk for developing pneumonia and requiring hospital admission due to COVID-19 than unvaccinated patients (aOR 0.209; 95% CI 0.044–0.985). Noticeably, pregnant patients with SARS-CoV-2 infection with at least two doses of the COVID-19 vaccine did not develop severe COVID-19. Conclusion: Vaccinated women with SARS-CoV-2 infection during pregnancy are associated with decreased hospital admission due to COVID-19 as well as reduced progression to severe COVID-19.","A. Martínez-Varea, E. Satorres, S. Flórez, J. Domenech, Julia Desco-Blay, Sagrario Monfort-Pitarch, M. Hueso, A. Perales-Marín, V. Diago-Almela",10.3390/jpm12122008,https://doi.org/10.3390/jpm12122008,Journal of Personalized Medicine,11,2022,yes,The study focus is exclusively on pregnant women.,yes,The study reports maternal and perinatal outcomes including pneumonia and hospital admission.,yes,The study is a retrospective cohort study.,yes,The study involves human participants (pregnant women) followed at a hospital.,yes,The study population consists of pregnant women.,yes,Participants received the vaccine during gestation or before conception.,no,"The study investigates COVID-19 vaccines (SARS-CoV-2), not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study focuses on the effects of COVID-19 vaccination on pregnant women and their outcomes. While it includes a relevant target population and reports clinical outcomes, it does not evaluate mpox or smallpox vaccines and is therefore irrelevant to the current systematic review.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""This study sought to elucidate whether COVID-19 vaccination, during gestation or before conception, entails a decreased incidence of severe COVID-19 disease during pregnancy.""
