"Household Transmission of Mpox to Children and Adolescents, California, 2022","Abstract Background In California, the 2022 mpox outbreak cumulated 5572 cases, 20% of US cases, as of November 28, 2022; 0.3% of cases were among children <16 years old. The secondary attack rate (SAR) for children sharing households with infected adults is unknown. Methods A line list of pediatric mpox household contacts aged <16 years reported through August 31, 2022 was created. It included demographic and clinical information on the contacts. Pediatric contact lists were crossmatched with the state vaccination database to identify those who received postexposure prophylaxis (PEP) with the JYNNEOS vaccine. Results We identified 129 pediatric household contacts with median age of 7 years (range, 0–15 years). Among 18 symptomatic contacts, 12 (66.7%) underwent mpox testing; 5 (41.2%) were confirmed cases, 6 (50%) were negative, and 1 (0.8%) had an indeterminate result. Six symptomatic children were not tested for mpox (33.3%). Overall, 6 infected contacts were identified, resulting in a SAR of 4.7% (6 of 129). The majority of pediatric household contacts and 4 of 6 infected children identified as Hispanic/Latino. Only 18 children (14%) reported receiving PEP. Conclusions The SAR was overall low among pediatric household contacts; none had severe disease. This may be underestimated given low testing rates.","K. Wendorf, Rilene Ng, C. Stainken, M. Haddix, Erin Peterson, Jessica Watson, Darpun D. Sachdev",10.1093/infdis/jiad448,https://doi.org/10.1093/infdis/jiad448,Journal of Infectious Diseases,13,2023,yes,"The study focuses on pediatric household contacts aged <16 years, not healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports on mpox infection rates (secondary attack rate) and mentions the clinical severity (none had severe disease).,yes,This is a primary research study (analysis of a line list/cohort of contacts).,yes,The study involves human pediatric contacts.,yes,The study specifically includes children and adolescents aged 0-15 years.,yes,Participants received postexposure prophylaxis (PEP) with the JYNNEOS vaccine during childhood or adolescence (ages 0-15).,yes,The study explicitly identifies the use of the JYNNEOS vaccine.,Include,This study evaluates mpox transmission and JYNNEOS PEP usage in a pediatric cohort aged 0-15 years. It provides effectiveness-related data by reporting the secondary attack rate and clinical outcomes in children who were household contacts.,,,5,"  - ""A line list of pediatric mpox household contacts aged <16 years reported through August 31, 2022 was created.""",,"  - ""overall low among pediatric household contacts; none had severe disease.""",,"  - ""A line list of pediatric mpox household contacts aged <16 years reported through August 31, 2022 was created.""",,"  - ""We identified 129 pediatric household contacts with median age of 7 years (range, 0–15 years).""",,"  - ""We identified 129 pediatric household contacts with median age of 7 years (range, 0–15 years).""",,"  - ""Pediatric contact lists were crossmatched with the state vaccination database to identify those who received postexposure prophylaxis (PEP) with the JYNNEOS vaccine.""
