Vaccines and Antiviral Therapies for Mpox Virus in Pregnant and Breastfeeding Women: Efficacy and Maternal–Child Outcomes,"Mpox (formerly known as monkeypox), the major public health concern of 2022, has elicited much attention globally. In addition to the usual symptoms observed in smallpox virus infections, infected mothers were found to hold a possible risk of transmission to newborns during delivery. This review aimed to summarize recent clinical trials that involved antiviral therapy, vaccines, immunoglobulin therapy, and other pharmacological interventions specifically for treating infected pregnant women. A comprehensive search was performed using databases such as PubMed, Google Scholar, and Medline to find appropriate disease management strategies. Amongst the vaccines and antivirals being used for treatment, vaccines such as Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA/MVA-BN) and Lister clone 16-medium pocket size-8 (LC16m8), while prophylactically effective, have been deemed unsafe for pregnant and lactating females. Antivirals like Tecovirimat, on the other hand, are considered to be a better alternative, but they are not without risks that may outweigh the potential benefits. Additionally, efforts to reduce maternal and fetal complications include administering the MVA-BN vaccine and awareness campaigns regarding herd immunity. Therefore, necessary precautions, prophylactic vaccinations in high-risk outbreak regions, and symptomatic treatment in pregnant and lactating females currently appear to be more feasible approaches against the mpox virus.","Maryum Imran, Myra Sohail, Javeria Kamran, S. Q. Abbas, Khadija Azeem, Emmanuel Korir",10.3390/v17040456,https://doi.org/10.3390/v17040456,Viruses,5,2025,yes,The study focuses on pregnant and breastfeeding women rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.,yes,The study reports on 'Efficacy and Maternal–Child Outcomes' as well as safety findings (deeming some vaccines unsafe).,yes,The study is an evidence synthesis (review) that utilized a comprehensive search of databases to summarize clinical trials.,yes,The study summarizes clinical trials involving pregnant women.,yes,The study specifically targets pregnant women.,yes,The study evaluates interventions specifically for 'infected pregnant women' and discusses the administration of vaccines during pregnancy.,yes,The study involves Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA-BN) and LC16m8.,Include,This paper is an evidence synthesis study reviewing the efficacy and safety outcomes of vaccinia-based vaccines (MVA-BN and LC16m8) in pregnant and breastfeeding women. It specifically addresses the use of these vaccines during pregnancy and the associated maternal–fetal complications.,,,4.9,"  - ""specifically for treating infected pregnant women.""",,"  - ""deemed unsafe for pregnant and lactating females.""",,"  - ""This review aimed to summarize recent clinical trials""
  - ""comprehensive search was performed using databases such as PubMed, Google Scholar, and Medline""",,"  - ""specifically for treating infected pregnant women.""
