Does Influenza Vaccination during Pregnancy Have Effects on Non-Influenza Infectious Morbidity? A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of Randomised Controlled Trials,"The recommendation to provide inactivated influenza vaccine (IIV) to pregnant women is based on observed protection against influenza-related morbidity in mother and infant. Non-live vaccines may have non-specific effects (NSEs), increasing the risk of non-targeted infections in females. We reviewed the evidence from available randomised controlled trials (RCTs) of IIV to pregnant women, to assess whether IIV may have NSEs. Four RCTs, all conducted in low- and middle-income settings, were identified. We extracted information on all-cause and infectious mortality and adverse events in women and their infants. We conducted meta-analyses providing risk ratios (RR). The meta-analysis for maternal all-cause mortality provided a RR of 1.48 (95% CI = 0.52–4.16). The estimates for miscarriage/stillbirth and infant all-cause mortality up to 6 months of age were 1.06 (0.78–1.44) and 1.11 (0.87–1.41), respectively. IIV was associated with a higher risk of non-influenza infectious adverse events, with meta-estimates of 2.01 (1.15–3.50) in women and 1.36 (1.12–1.67) in infants up to 6 months of age. Thus, following a pattern seen for other non-live vaccines, IIV was associated with a higher risk of non-influenza infectious adverse events. To ensure that scarce resources are used well, and no harm is inflicted, further RCTs are warranted.","K. Hansen, C. Benn, Thomas Aamand, Martin Buus, Isaquel da Silva, P. Aaby, A. Fisker, Sanne Marie Thysen",10.3390/vaccines9121452,https://doi.org/10.3390/vaccines9121452,Vaccines,7,2021,yes,The study focuses specifically on pregnant women and their infants rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.,yes,The study reports on mortality and adverse events.,yes,The study is a systematic review and meta-analysis.,yes,The study involves human participants (pregnant women and infants).,yes,The participants are pregnant women and their infants.,yes,The vaccination occurred during pregnancy.,no,"The study investigates the inactivated influenza vaccine (IIV), not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This is a systematic review of inactivated influenza vaccines (IIV) administered during pregnancy. While it involves the correct target population and outcomes, it does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""IIV) to pregnant women""
