Recomendación del Comité Asesor de Vacunas y Estrategias de Inmunización (CAVEI) sobre vacunación contra viruela símica en Chile,"Monkeypox was declared a public health emergency of international concern by the World Health Organization during 2022. In Chile, over 450 patients have been confirmed until September, mostly young men. This zoonotic poxvirus is transmitted from humans to humans through close contact; it is a self-limiting disease and can be fatal in people with immunodeficiency. Prevention by immunization is important. MVA-BN, one of the three vaccines available, it is a third generation vaccine, based on non-replicating modified vaccinia virus, therefore can be administered to immunocompromised patients and pregnant women and it has been approved for monkeypox in people over 18 years of age. The available information on efficacy and effectiveness is limited. The CAVEI recommends incorporating this vaccine to interrupt the chain of transmission and reduce the risk of severe disease, administered subcutaneously in two doses, 28 days apart, prioritizing post exposure use for close contacts of confirmed cases with risk of severe disease, ideally within 4 days of exposure and it can be used up to 14 days after exposure and in the absence of symptoms. When the vaccine supply allows it, its application is recommended as pre-exposure prevention for people with high-risk sexual practices or with occupational risk. This recommendation could be modified according to epidemiology, vaccines supply and new scientific information.","Vivian Luchsinger, Jeannette Dabanch, A. King, Jan Wilhelm, A. Saldaña, M. P. Bertoglia, C. García, M. L. Endeiza, Jaime Rodríguez",10.4067/s0716-10182022000600731,https://doi.org/10.4067/s0716-10182022000600731,Revista chilena de infectología,0,2022,no,"The recommendation focuses on individuals over 18 years of age, and notes the majority of cases in Chile are young men. There is no evidence of primary research on non-healthy adults or pediatric populations.",no,The paper mentions general efficacy and safety properties of the MVA-BN vaccine but does not report original data on outcomes from a study cohort.,no,"This is a recommendation statement from a vaccine advisory committee (CAVEI), not a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or systematic review.",no,This is a guidelines or recommendation document rather than a clinical study involving human human research participants.,no,"The document mentions that the vaccine can be administered to pregnant women but specifies approval for those over 18, and does not include pediatric or pregnant populations as study participants.",no,"The paper does not report on a cohort of participants who received the vaccine during the specified periods, as it is a policy recommendation.",yes,The paper discusses the MVA-BN (Modified Vaccinia Ankara) vaccine.,Exclude,"This document is a vaccine advisory committee recommendation from Chile, rather than a primary research study. It focuses on vaccination policy for adults and does not provide original data on outcomes or specific populations like children or pregnant women.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""it has been approved for monkeypox in people over 18 years of age.""
