Clinical review of human mpox.,"BACKGROUND
Historically, human mpox was predominantly a zoonotic disease occurring more frequently in rural children in Africa and characterized by a largely self-limiting febrile centrifugal monomorphic rash illness. However, the 2022 mpox global outbreak has shown that the disease is changing in many ways, including sustained human-to-human transmission via sexual contact, novel clinical presentations, and adverse associations between mpox and advanced HIV.
OBJECTIVES
The aim of this paper is to review the traditional and emerging clinical aspects of human mpox and provide updated information on the clinical course and outcome of the disease.
SOURCES
We searched electronic databases including PubMed and Google Scholar and identified relevant published literature on mpox.
CONTENT
The clinical presentation of human mpox is influenced by the route of infectious exposure, the strain and dose of the infecting virus, and the host immune system. Exposure to the virus can result in subclinical or clinical diseases of variable severity. Infections caused by Clade I viral strains are more severe than Class IIa and IIb strains, which are associated with a milder febrile rash illness, and with anogenital skin lesions in Clade IIb infections. Most cases of mpox recover entirely within two to four weeks after onset of illness and a few develop skin-related sequelae. Overall, people with advanced HIV infection, children < 5 years of age, and pregnant women may present with more severe disease and higher case fatalities.
IMPLICATIONS
The continued endemicity of the classical mpox in Africa, the emergence of a new clinical form of the disease during the 2022 global outbreak, and the adverse associations between advanced HIV and mpox have implications for the surveillance, clinical diagnosis, and management of human mpox.","D. Ogoina, Inger Damon, E. Nakoune",10.1016/j.cmi.2023.09.004,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cmi.2023.09.004,Clinical Microbiology and Infection,41,2023,yes,"The review covers mpox in various populations, including children and pregnant women, and is not limited to healthy adults.",maybe,"The review discusses clinical courses, outcomes, and case fatalities of mpox. However, it focuses on the disease itself rather than vaccine outcomes.",yes,"The paper describes itself as a review of published literature identified via electronic databases, which characterizes it as an evidence synthesis study.",yes,The review focuses on human mpox.,yes,The paper explicitly reviews clinical aspects relevant to children and pregnant women.,no,The abstract does not mention any data or reviews specifically regarding vaccination timing (during pregnancy or childhood); it focuses on the clinical description of the disease.,no,The abstract discusses the clinical disease of human mpox but does not mention vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This paper is a clinical review of human mpox disease course and outcomes across various populations, including children and pregnant women. However, it does not address vaccination or vaccine outcomes, failing the criteria for vaccine type and vaccination timing.","Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccination Timing,0.1,"  - ""Overall, people with advanced HIV infection, children < 5 years of age, and pregnant women may present with more severe disease and higher case fatalities.""",,"  - ""The aim of this paper is to review the traditional and emerging clinical aspects of human mpox and provide updated information on the clinical course and outcome of the disease.""
  - ""Overall, people with advanced HIV infection, children < 5 years of age, and pregnant women may present with more severe disease and higher case fatalities.""",,"  - ""The aim of this paper is to review the traditional and emerging clinical aspects of human mpox and provide updated information on the clinical course and outcome of the disease.""
