Pockenausbrüche nach dem zweiten Weltkrieg in Deutschland,"BACKGROUND
Even though smallpox was declared eradicated by WHO in 1980, it cannot be ruled out that the etiological variola virus could be used as a biological weapon. Undestroyed viruses from biowarfare programmes, virus strains left undetected in a freezer or dangerous recombinant poxvirus constructs could cause dangerous outbreaks in a relatively unprotected population.
OBJECTIVES
Despite an abundance of studies performed during the eradication of smallpox, epidemiological data for preparedness planning and outbreak control in modern, industrialized countries are scarce.
MATERIAL AND METHODS
Full-text hand search for the period from 1945 to 1975 in the main German public health journals.
RESULTS
After World War II 12 smallpox outbreaks occurred in Germany. They were studied with the focus on the period of contagiousness, the protective effect of vaccination, booster-effect of revaccination and the place of infection. A total of 95 individuals contracted smallpox, including 10 fatalities. Despite having been previously vaccinated, 81 vaccinated persons came down with smallpox, yet 91% of them developed only mild symptoms. These patients presented a high risk for spreading the infection to contact persons due to misinterpretation of symptoms and the continuing social contacts. Basically, the risk of transmission in the first 2 to 3 days after onset of symptoms was low, thus facilitating antiepidemic measures. The importance of hospital preparedness is emphasized by the fact that most infections occurred in hospitals.
CONCLUSION
The data analyzed provide valuable information for today's outbreak response planning and counter bioterrorism preparedness.","J. Sasse, H. Gelderblom",10.1007/s00103-015-2168-8,https://doi.org/10.1007/s00103-015-2168-8,Bundesgesundheitsblatt - Gesundheitsforschung - Gesundheitsschutz,5,2015,yes,"The study reviews historical outbreaks in the general German population, which is not limited to healthy adults.",yes,The study reports on fatalities and symptoms (severity) in vaccinated vs. unvaccinated individuals.,yes,The study is an evidence synthesis/archival review based on a hand search of public health journals.,yes,The study involves human clinical cases from historical outbreaks.,maybe,"The abstract mentions 95 individuals in general outbreaks but does not explicitly state whether children, adolescents, or pregnant women were included, though it is likely.",maybe,"The study mentions vaccinated individuals and revaccination. While primary vaccination in that period was typically in childhood, the timing for these specific participants is not explicitly detailed.",yes,The study specifically looks at smallpox vaccination and its protective effects.,Include,"The paper summarizes 12 smallpox outbreaks in post-WWII Germany involving 95 individuals and analyzes the effectiveness of prior smallpox vaccination. While the study likely includes relevant populations given the general nature of the outbreaks and mandatory childhood vaccination laws of the era, the abstract does not explicitly confirm the inclusion of children or pregnant women.",,,3.4,"  - ""After World War II 12 smallpox outbreaks occurred in Germany.""
