Secondary and Tertiary Transmission of Vaccinia Virus from US Military Service Member,"During February and March 2010, the New York State Department of Health investigated secondary and tertiary vaccinia contact transmission from a military vaccinee to 4 close contacts. Identification of these cases underscores the need for strict adherence to postvaccination infection control guidance to avoid transmission of the live virus.","Gregory Young, C. Hidalgo, Ann Sullivan-Frohm, Cynthia Schulte, S. Davis, C. Kelly-Cirino, C. Egan, Kimberly Wilkins, G. Emerson, Kimberly A Noyes, D. Blog",10.3201/eid1704.101316,https://doi.org/10.3201/eid1704.101316,Emerging Infectious Diseases,11,2011,maybe,"The study includes four close contacts of a military vaccinee. It is unclear if these contacts are healthy non-pregnant adults or if they include children or pregnant women, as their demographic details are not provided in the abstract.",yes,"The study reports on the transmission of the vaccinia virus to contacts, which is a safety outcome (adverse event related to the live virus vaccine).",yes,"The paper describes an investigation of a case series/transmission event, which constitutes primary research.",yes,The study involves a military service member and their human contacts.,maybe,"The abstract mentions 4 close contacts but does not specify if any were children or pregnant women. Such transmission investigations often involve family members (including children), but this cannot be confirmed from the text provided.",maybe,"The military vaccinee received the vaccine, and they were likely an adult; however, it is unclear if any contacts (who received the virus via transmission) were children or if their exposure counts as receiving the vaccine virus in those life stages.",yes,"The study involves the vaccinia virus from a military vaccinee, which refers to the historical smallpox vaccine.",Include,"The study investigates the secondary and tertiary transmission of the vaccinia virus from a military member to four contacts. While it clearly reports on safety outcomes and involves a relevant vaccine type, the abstract does not provide the ages or pregnancy status of the contacts, making it uncertain if it meets the target population criteria.",,,3.6,"  - ""New York State Department of Health investigated secondary and tertiary vaccinia contact transmission from a military vaccinee to 4 close contacts.""",,"  - ""New York State Department of Health investigated secondary and tertiary vaccinia contact transmission from a military vaccinee to 4 close contacts.""
