Evaluation of monkeypox‐ and vaccinia‐virus neutralizing antibodies before and after smallpox vaccination: A sero‐epidemiological study,"Since May 2022, several countries outside of Africa experienced multiple clusters of monkeypox virus (MPXV)‐associated disease. In the present study, anti‐MPXV and anti‐vaccinia virus (VACV) neutralizing antibody responses were evaluated in two cohorts of subjects from the general Italian population (one half born before the WHO‐recommended end of smallpox vaccination in 1980, the other half born after). Higher titers (either against MPXV or VACV) were observed in the cohort of individuals born before the interruption of VACV vaccination. An association between VACV and MPXV antibody levels was observed, suggesting that the smallpox vaccination may confer some degree of cross‐protection against MPXV infection. Results from this study highlight low levels of immunity toward the assessed Orthopoxviruses, especially in young adults, advocating the introduction of a VACV‐ or MPXV‐specific vaccine in case of resurgence of monkeypox disease outbreaks.","S. Marchi, Giulia Piccini, Paolo Cantaloni, Noemi Guerrini, Roberta Zannella, R. Coluccio, Linda Benincasa, Niccolò Solfanelli, Edmond J Remarque, Simonetta Viviani, Otfried Kistner, Nigel Temperton, Emanuele Montomoli, A. Manenti, C. Trombetta",10.1002/jmv.29728,https://doi.org/10.1002/jmv.29728,Journal of Medical Virology,11,2024,no,"The study focuses on the general Italian population, categorized into those born before and after 1980. While it mentions 'young adults', it does not explicitly state that it excludes healthy non-pregnant adults; rather, it appears to be a study of general adult cohorts.",no,The study reports on neutralizing antibody responses (immunogenicity) rather than clinical effectiveness (prevention of infection) or safety outcomes like adverse events.,yes,The study is a primary sero-epidemiological research study.,yes,The study involves human subjects from the general population.,no,"The study evaluates cohorts born before and after 1980, focusing on the general population and 'young adults'. There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as a specific target group for evaluating vaccination effects.",no,"The study compares individuals born after the end of routine vaccination (who are unvaccinated) with those born before 1980 who received historical vaccinations, but it does not specifically track or investigate vaccination administered during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence in a way that meets the focus on these specific life stages for current safety/efficacy monitoring.",yes,The study evaluates responses to vaccinia-based smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This sero-epidemiological study evaluates neutralizing antibodies in adult cohorts of the general Italian population. It fails the target population criteria as it does not focus on pregnant women, children, or adolescents, and it reports immunogenicity rather than clinical safety or effectiveness outcomes.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""anti‐MPXV and anti‐vaccinia virus (VACV) neutralizing antibody responses were evaluated in two cohorts of subjects from the general Italian population (one half born before the WHO‐recommended end of smallpox vaccination in 1980, the other half born after).""
