"Safety of 9-valent human papillomavirus vaccine administration among pregnant women: Adverse event reports in the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS), 2014-2017.","INTRODUCTION
9-valent human papillomavirus vaccine (9vHPV) was approved by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) in December 2014. 9vHPV is not recommended during pregnancy, but some women of childbearing age may be inadvertently exposed. This study aims to evaluate reports submitted to the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS) of pregnant women exposed to 9vHPV.
METHODS
We searched the VAERS database, a national post-licensure vaccine safety surveillance system, for reports of pregnant women vaccinated with 9vHPV in the United States between December 10, 2014 and December 31, 2017. Disproportionate reporting of adverse events (AEs) was assessed using proportional reporting ratios (PRRs).
RESULTS
A total of 82 pregnancy reports were identified. Sixty reports (73.2%) did not describe an AE and were submitted only to report the vaccine exposure during pregnancy. The most frequently reported pregnancy-specific AE was spontaneous abortion (n = 3; 3.7%), followed by vaginal bleeding (n = 2; 2.4%). Among non-pregnancy-specific AEs, injection site reaction (n = 3; 3.7%) was most common. No disproportionate reporting of any AE was found.
DISCUSSION
No unexpected AEs were observed among these pregnancy reports.","Claudia S Landazabal, P. Moro, Paige W. Lewis, S. Omer",10.1016/j.vaccine.2018.11.077,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2018.11.077,Vaccine,9,2019,yes,"The study population consists of pregnant women, which means it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports safety outcomes, specifically adverse events.",yes,"The study is an analysis of reports from a national post-licensure vaccine safety surveillance system (VAERS), which is a type of primary research (case series or cohort observational).",yes,The study uses human data from vaccine safety reports.,yes,The study explicitly includes pregnant women.,yes,Participants received the vaccine during pregnancy.,no,"The study focuses on the 9-valent human papillomavirus vaccine (9vHPV), not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the safety of the 9-valent human papillomavirus vaccine (9vHPV) in pregnant women. While it involves a target population (pregnant women) and relevant safety outcomes, it does not involve the required vaccine types (mpox or smallpox vaccines).",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""This study aims to evaluate reports submitted to the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS) of pregnant women exposed to 9vHPV.""",,"  - ""Disproportionate reporting of adverse events (AEs) was assessed""",,"  - ""This study aims to evaluate reports submitted to the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS) of pregnant women exposed to 9vHPV.""
