[Biohazards due to Orthopoxvirus: should we re-vaccinate against smallpox?].,"Although the WHO declared global smallpox eradication in 1980, the Orthopoxvirus remains a source of concern for several reasons. Firstly, stocks of the smallpox virus have been preserved for experimental use (at least officially in the USA and Russia) so that an escaped isolate could lead to reemergence and spread of the disease worldwide. Secondly discontinuation of smallpox vaccination programs has led to dwindling acquired immunity in the world population thus raising the risk of epidemic extension of several Orthopoxvirus zoonoses (e.g., monkeypox). Thirdly stocks of camelpox virus which is very similar to Smallpox virus and was intended for biological warfare were discovered during the Gulf War in 1991 and pose a potentially serious threat. Finally official stocks of Smallpox virus could be stolen and used by bioterrorists. Thus reemergence of Orthopoxvirus including smallpox, monkeypox, cowpox, and camelpox is a real danger and contingency planning is needed to define prophylactic and therapeutic strategies to prevent and/or stop an epidemics. Adverse effects from earlier smallpox vaccine (vaccinia) in healthy people or immunocompromised people (congenital or acquired as in HIV infected patients) are absolute contraindications to smallpox vaccination at this time. Further research is needed to develop new vaccines (e.g., attenuated isolates of vaccinia) and effective treatment. This is the only valid reasons for postponing planned destruction of remaining Smallpox virus stocks.","A. Georges, M. Georges-Courbot",,,Medecine tropicale : revue du Corps de sante colonial,1,1999,yes,"The paper discusses the general world population and specific mentions of immunocompromised or HIV infected patients, rather than being limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,The paper mentions historical adverse effects but does not report primary data or systematic results on safety or effectiveness outcomes for a specific study population.,no,"The text appears to be a review or commentary/opinion piece discussing the threat of Orthopoxvirus and the need for vaccines, rather than a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,"The paper is a discussion piece about human health risks and vaccination strategies, not an animal or laboratory-only study.",no,"There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as participants or as a specific sub-focus of the discussion.",no,"The study does not involve participants receiving vaccines during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence; it discusses general historical vaccination and future needs.",yes,The paper discusses vaccinia-based smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This paper is a general commentary or review discussing the risks of Orthopoxvirus reemergence and the need for new vaccination strategies. It fails several inclusion criteria as it is not a primary research study or systematic review, and it does not specifically include or report data on pregnant women, children, or outcomes in those populations.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Adverse effects from earlier smallpox vaccine (vaccinia) in healthy people or immunocompromised people (congenital or acquired as in HIV infected patients) are absolute contraindications to smallpox vaccination at this time.""",,"  - ""Adverse effects from earlier smallpox vaccine (vaccinia) in healthy people or immunocompromised people (congenital or acquired as in HIV infected patients) are absolute contraindications to smallpox vaccination at this time.""",,,,"  - ""Adverse effects from earlier smallpox vaccine (vaccinia) in healthy people or immunocompromised people (congenital or acquired as in HIV infected patients) are absolute contraindications to smallpox vaccination at this time.""",,,,"  - ""Adverse effects from earlier smallpox