OpenSAFELY: Effectiveness of COVID-19 Vaccination in Children and Adolescents,"Background Children and adolescents in England were offered BNT162b2 as part of the national COVID-19 vaccine roll out from September 2021. We assessed the safety and effectiveness of first and second dose BNT162b2 COVID-19 vaccination in children and adolescents in England. Methods With the approval of NHS England, we conducted an observational study in the OpenSAFELY-TPP database, including a) adolescents aged 12-15 years, and b) children aged 5-11 years and comparing individuals receiving i) first vaccination with unvaccinated controls and ii) second vaccination to single-vaccinated controls. We matched vaccinated individuals with controls on age, sex, region, and other important characteristics. Outcomes were positive SARS-CoV-2 test (adolescents only); COVID-19 A&E attendance; COVID-19 hospitalisation; COVID-19 critical care admission; COVID-19 death, with non-COVID-19 death and fractures as negative control outcomes and A&E attendance, unplanned hospitalisation, pericarditis, and myocarditis as safety outcomes. Results Amongst 820,926 previously unvaccinated adolescents, the incidence rate ratio (IRR) for positive SARS-CoV-2 test comparing vaccination with no vaccination was 0.74 (95% CI 0.72-0.75), although the 20-week risks were similar. The IRRs were 0.60 (0.37-0.97) for COVID-19 A&E attendance, 0.58 (0.38-0.89) for COVID-19 hospitalisation, 0.99 (0.93-1.06) for fractures, 0.89 (0.87-0.91) for A&E attendances and 0.88 (0.81-0.95) for unplanned hospitalisation. Amongst 441,858 adolescents who had received first vaccination IRRs comparing second dose with first dose only were 0.67 (0.65-0.69) for positive SARS-CoV-2 test, 1.00 (0.20-4.96) for COVID-19 A&E attendance, 0.60 (0.26-1.37) for COVID-19 hospitalisation, 0.94 (0.84-1.05) for fractures, 0.93 (0.89-0.98) for A&E attendance and 0.99 (0.86-1.13) for unplanned hospitalisation. Amongst 283,422 previously unvaccinated children and 132,462 children who had received a first vaccine dose, COVID-19-related outcomes were too rare to allow IRRs to be estimated precisely. A&E attendance and unplanned hospitalisation were slightly higher after first vaccination (IRRs versus no vaccination 1.05 (1.01-1.10) and 1.10 (0.95-1.26) respectively) but slightly lower after second vaccination (IRRs versus first dose 0.95 (0.86-1.05) and 0.78 (0.56-1.08) respectively). There were no COVID-19-related deaths in any group. Fewer than seven (exact number redacted) COVID-19-related critical care admissions occurred in the adolescent first dose vs unvaccinated cohort. Among both adolescents and children, myocarditis and pericarditis were documented only in the vaccinated groups, with rates of 27 and 10 cases/million after first and second doses respectively. Conclusion BNT162b2 vaccination in adolescents reduced COVID-19 A&E attendance and hospitalisation, although these outcomes were rare. Protection against positive SARS-CoV-2 tests was transient.","Colm D. Andrews, Edward P. K. Parker, Elsie M. F. Horne, Venexia M. Walker, T. Palmer, Andrea L Schaffer, A. Green, Helen J. Curtis, A. Walker, L. Bridges, Christopher Wood, V. Speed, Christopher Bates, J. Cockburn, John Parry, A. Mehrkar, B. Mackenna, Sebastian C J Bacon, B. Goldacre, Miguel A. Hernán, J. Sterne, William J. Hulme",10.1101/2024.05.20.24306810,https://doi.org/10.1101/2024.05.20.24306810,medRxiv,1,2024,yes,"The study focus is on children and adolescents, not exclusively healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports on both effectiveness and safety outcomes.,yes,This is an observational study.,yes,This is a human population study based on medical records.,yes,The participants are children and adolescents aged 5-15 years.,yes,Participants received the vaccine during childhood or adolescence.,no,"The study investigates the BNT162b2 COVID-19 vaccine, not a vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"While the study focuses on a relevant population (children and adolescents), it evaluates the BNT162b2 COVID-19 vaccine rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines, thus failing the required vaccine type criterion.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""adolescents aged 12-15 years, and b) children aged 5-11 years""",,"  - ""A&E attendance, unplanned hospitalisation, pericarditis, and myocarditis as safety outcomes.""",,"  - ""observational study in the OpenSAFELY-TPP database,""",,"  - ""observational study in the OpenSAFELY-TPP database,""",,"  - ""adolescents aged 12-15 years, and b) children aged 5-11 years""",,"  - ""offered BNT162b2 as part of the national COVID-19 vaccine roll out""",,"  - ""offered BNT162b2 as part of the national COVID-19 vaccine roll out""",
Vaccines and pregnancy,"Vaccines have been used for centuries to protect us from dangerous and deadly diseases. The first was for smallpox in 1796, and since then, many other vaccines have been created for infections such as rabies, cholera, and anthrax. By the end of the 20th century, there were 26 safe and effective vaccines available. When it comes to maternal health, vaccines have been used for three main purposes: to protect the wellbeing of expectant mothers, reduce the risk of fetal infection, and confer passive immunity to newborns. One example of a vaccine that protects maternal health is the one against influenza. Pregnant women are more susceptible to viral infections, particularly respiratory ones, due to the physiological changes that occur during pregnancy. Throughout history, various flu pandemics (Spanish flu, Asian flu, Hong Kong flu, swine flu, etc) have been a leading cause of maternal morbidity and mortality and pregnant women were overrepresented amongst the hospital/ intensive care unit (ICU) admissions and deaths. This has led to recommendations that pregnant women should receive the influenza inactivated vaccine; a view that was also endorsed by the Centers for Disease Control in the US in 1997. Following the 2009 HI1N1 pandemic, influenza vaccination has been strongly recommended in pregnancy; randomisedcontrolled trials confirmed that vaccination leads to a reduction in the rates of potentially lifethreatening maternal illness as well as rates of vertical transmission to the fetus/ infant. Of importance, the evidence indicates that influenza vaccination in any trimester is not associated with adverse pregnancy outcomes such as preterm birth, small for gestational age, congenital abnormality or fetal death (Juvet et al. Front Immunol. 2021;12:740048) More recently, coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID19), caused by the severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus (SRASCov2), spread worldwide to a pandemic level which, according to the most recent WHO estimates, have resulted in over 6.7 million deaths. Several studies have shown that pregnancy is a risk factor for severe COVID19 infection and can lead to severe maternal illness, hospitalisation, ICU admission requiring invasive ventilation or extracorporeal membrane oxygenation, and even death. There is also some evidence that maternal COVID19 infection can lead to pregnancy complications including preeclampsia and stillbirth. In the current issue Goldenberg et al (pages 366-376) conducted a prospective, populationbased observational study in seven LMICs and found no association between maternal COVID19 antibody positivity, detected at delivery, and adverse pregnancy outcomes. Their findings suggest that COVID19 in pregnancy may not result in a significant increase in adverse outcomes, but in symptomatic highrisk women major complications are possible. As it is impossible to know before contracting COVID19 whether symptomatic disease will result, this study, together with a large body of evidence, emphasizes that prevention is critical. COVID 19 vaccination has been successful in reducing the incidence and severity of disease and therefore has been widely recommended in pregnancy. The exclusion of pregnant women from the initial COVID19 vaccine trials and initial lack of information led to high rates of vaccine hesitancy among pregnant women. Studies have demonstrated that mRNA COVID19 vaccines, which cause both antibody and cellular immune response, are effective in pregnancy without increasing the risk of adverse pregnancy/fetal outcomes; risk of stillbirth and preterm delivery appear to be lower in vaccinated compared to unvaccinated women (Prasad et al. Nat Commun. 2022;13:2414). In the current issue, Tormen et al (pages 348357) focus mainly on maternal outcomes postCOVID19 vaccination. Their systematic review reports that fully vaccinated pregnant women have >50% less chance of suffering from severe disease or being hospitalised, although the effect on ICU admissions was not very clear. Their findings on neonatal outcomes were also reassuring. Although stratification according to the trimester of vaccination was not possible, the study adds to the current body of evidence on vaccines' safety. It is also well documented that the highest levels of maternal SARS CoV2 IgG antibodies are seen in pregnant women who have had three doses of vaccine compared to those who received two or no vaccines. Considering the transplacental transfer of the antibodies, maternal vaccination also translates into higher newborn protection. (ToussiaCohen et al. Vaccines (Basel). 2022;10:2019.) The second role of vaccination in maternal health is fetal protection, and Rubella vaccine is the best example of this. In 1941, Norman McAllister Gregg, an Australian ophthalmologist, first reported the association between maternal rubella infection and congenital cataract but it took another 20 years until the whole spectrum of rubella embryopathy syndrome was really appreciated. The liveattenuated rubella vaccine was licenced for use in 1970s and the populationbased MMR vaccination programs were successful in achieving herd immunity which led to DOI: 10.1111/1471-0528.17405",M. Savvidou,10.1111/1471-0528.17405,https://doi.org/10.1111/1471-0528.17405,BJOG: an International Journal of Obstetrics and Gynaecology,4,2023,yes,The study explicitly focuses on maternal health and pregnant women rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.,no,"While the paper discusses outcomes for influenza and COVID-19 vaccines in pregnancy, it does not report safety or effectiveness outcomes for mpox or smallpox vaccines.",no,The article is a commentary/editorial that summarizes other research papers in the journal's current issue rather than being a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis of the topic.,yes,The content discussion involves human health and vaccination.,yes,The study specifically discusses expectant mothers and newborns.,yes,The discussion is centered around vaccines administered during pregnancy.,no,"The paper focuses on influenza, COVID-19, and rubella vaccines; smallpox is mentioned only briefly as a historical fact and the paper does not actually study vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines in the target populations.",Exclude,"This article is a journal commentary summarizing other studies on influenza and COVID-19 vaccinations in pregnancy. It fails to meet inclusion criteria because it does not involve a primary study or systematic review of vaccinia-based vaccines for mpox, only mentioning smallpox in a historical context.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""When it comes to maternal health, vaccines have been used for three main purposes: to protect the wellbeing of expectant mothers,""
  - ""Following the 2009 HI1N1 pandemic, influenza vaccination has been strongly recommended in pregnancy;""",,"  - ""In the current issue Goldenberg et al (pages 366-376) conducted a prospective, populationbased observational study""
  - ""Following the 2009 HI1N1 pandemic, influenza vaccination has been strongly recommended in pregnancy;""",,"  - ""In the current issue Goldenberg et al (pages 366-376) conducted a prospective, populationbased observational study""",,"  - ""When it comes to maternal health, vaccines have been used for three main purposes: to protect the wellbeing of expectant mothers,""",,"  - ""When it comes to maternal health, vaccines have been used for three main purposes: to protect the wellbeing of expectant mothers,""",,"  - ""Following the 2009 HI1N1 pandemic, influenza vaccination has been strongly recommended in pregnancy;""",,"  - ""The first was for smallpox in 1796,""
