Self-reported adverse health events following smallpox vaccination in a large prospective study of US military service members,"In December 2002, the Department of Defense re-instituted smallpox vaccination for US military forces following growing concerns that smallpox might be employed as a bioterrorist weapon. More than one million service members have been given the smallpox vaccine since 2002, although there have been concerns about the safety of the vaccine. Using a large self-reported prospective database, this analysis investigated a wide variety of self-reported health outcomes and possible association with smallpox vaccination. After confirming self-reported vaccination history with electronic vaccine data, 40,472 individuals were included in the analyses, 8,793 of whom received the smallpox vaccine and 31,679 who did not. No significant adverse associations between smallpox vaccination and self-reported health outcomes, including mental and physical functioning, cardiovascular diseases, and autoimmune disorders, were found. These findings complement studies that utilize other data sources, such as electronic hospitalization records, and may be reassuring to health care providers and those who receive the smallpox vaccination.","T. Wells, C. LeardMann, T. Smith, Besa Smith, I. Jacobson, R. Reed, M. Ryan",10.4161/hv.4.2.5217,https://doi.org/10.4161/hv.4.2.5217,Human Vaccines,15,2008,no,"The study population is limited to US military service members, which represents a group of healthy adults. There is no indication that it includes pregnant women or children.",yes,"The study reports on various health outcomes such as cardiovascular and autoimmune disorders, which are safety outcomes.",yes,"The study is a large prospective analysis of self-reported data, making it a primary research study (cohort design).",yes,The research is conducted on human service members.,no,"The study participants are military service members, which means they are adults. There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents being included in the sample.",no,"Participants received the smallpox vaccine while serving in the military, which occurs during adulthood, not during childhood, the neonatal period, or pregnancy.",yes,The study explicitly focuses on smallpox vaccination.,Exclude,"This study examines smallpox vaccination safety in US military service members. It is excluded because the study population is limited to adults and does not include the required target populations of pregnant women, neonates, or children.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,,,"  - ""self-reported health outcomes, including mental and physical functioning, cardiovascular diseases, and autoimmune disorders,""",,,,"  - ""40,472 individuals were included in the analyses, 8,793 of whom received the smallpox vaccine""",,"  - ""40,472 individuals were included in the analyses, 8,793 of whom received the smallpox vaccine""",,"  - ""In December 2002, the Department of Defense re-instituted smallpox vaccination for US military forces""",,"  - ""In December 2002, the Department of Defense re-instituted smallpox vaccination for US military forces""",
"Mpox in children and adolescents and contact follow-up in school settings in greater Paris, France, May 2022 to July 2023","Background During the 2022 mpox outbreak in Europe, primarily affecting men who have sex with men, a limited number of cases among children and adolescents were identified. Paediatric cases from outbreaks in endemic countries have been associated with a higher likelihood of severe illness. Detailed clinical case descriptions and interventions in school settings before 2022 are limited. Aim To describe clinical characteristics of mpox cases among children (< 15 years) and adolescents (15–17 years) in the greater Paris area in France, and infection control measures in schools. Methods We describe all notified laboratory-confirmed and non-laboratory-confirmed cases among children and adolescents identified from May 2022 to July 2023, including demographic and clinical characterisation and infection control measures in school settings, i.e. contact tracing, contact vaccination, secondary attack rate and post-exposure vaccination uptake. Results Nineteen cases were notified (13 children, 6 adolescents). Four adolescent cases reported sexual contact before symptom onset. Ten child cases were secondary cases of adult patients; three cases were cryptic, with vesicles on hands, arms and/or legs and one case additionally presented with genitoanal lesions. Five cases attended school during their infectious period, with 160 at-risk contacts identified, and one secondary case. Five at-risk contacts were vaccinated following exposure. Conclusion Cases among children and adolescents are infrequent but require a careful approach to identify the source of infection and ensure infection control measures. We advocate a ‘contact warning’ strategy vs ‘contact tracing’ in order to prevent alarm and stigma. Low post-exposure vaccination rates are expected.","L. Reques, Lilas Mercuriali, Y. Silué, Emilie Chazelle, G. Spaccaferri, Annie Velter, A. Mailles, P. Frange, Arnaud Tarantola",10.2807/1560-7917.ES.2024.29.21.2300555,https://doi.org/10.2807/1560-7917.ES.2024.29.21.2300555,Euro surveillance : bulletin Europeen sur les maladies transmissibles = European communicable disease bulletin,3,2024,yes,"The study focus is exclusively on children and adolescents, not healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports on effectiveness outcomes such as the secondary attack rate following exposure and infection control measures like vaccination.,yes,This is a primary research study describing notified cases and infection control measures (case series/cohort type).,yes,The study involves human participants (children and adolescents) in school settings.,yes,The study participants are children (<15 years) and adolescents (15-17 years).,yes,Five at-risk contacts received post-exposure vaccination during their childhood/adolescence as part of school-setting infection control.,yes,"The study discusses mpox vaccines used during the 2022-2023 outbreak in France, which are vaccinia-based.",Include,"This study describes mpox cases and infection control measures among children and adolescents, specifically reporting on post-exposure vaccination uptake and secondary attack rates. It meets all inclusion criteria by focusing on pediatric populations and reporting outcomes related to vaccination effectiveness.",,,4.9,"  - ""describe clinical characteristics of mpox cases among children (< 15 years) and adolescents (15–17 years) in the greater Paris area in France, and infection control measures in schools.""
