Pregnancy Outcomes among Women Receiving rVSVΔ-ZEBOV-GP Ebola Vaccine during the Sierra Leone Trial to Introduce a Vaccine against Ebola,"Little information exists regarding Ebola vaccine rVSVΔG-ZEBOV-GP and pregnancy. The Sierra Leone Trial to Introduce a Vaccine against Ebola (STRIVE) randomized participants without blinding to immediate or deferred (18–24 weeks postenrollment) vaccination. Pregnancy was an exclusion criterion, but 84 women were inadvertently vaccinated in early pregnancy or became pregnant <60 days after vaccination or enrollment. Among immediate vaccinated women, 45% (14/31) reported pregnancy loss, compared with 33% (11/33) of unvaccinated women with contemporaneous pregnancies (relative risk 1.35, 95% CI 0.73–2.52). Pregnancy loss was similar among women with higher risk for vaccine viremia (conception before or <14 days after vaccination) (44% [4/9]) and women with lower risk (conception >15 days after vaccination) (45% [10/22]). No congenital anomalies were detected among 44 live-born infants examined. These data highlight the need for Ebola vaccination decisions to balance the possible risk for an adverse pregnancy outcome with the risk for Ebola exposure.","J. Legardy-Williams, R. Carter, S. Goldstein, O. Jarrett, E. Szefer, A. Fombah, Sarah C. Tinker, Mohamed Samai, B. Mahon",10.3201/eid2603.191018,https://doi.org/10.3201/eid2603.191018,Emerging Infectious Diseases,31,2020,yes,"The study population consists of pregnant women, meaning it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports safety outcomes including pregnancy loss and congenital anomalies.,yes,The study is primary research involving randomized participants from the STRIVE trial.,yes,The study involves human participants (women and live-born infants).,yes,The participants include pregnant women.,yes,Participants were vaccinated in early pregnancy.,no,"The study investigates the rVSVΔG-ZEBOV-GP Ebola vaccine, not a vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This study evaluates pregnancy outcomes following vaccination with the rVSVΔG-ZEBOV-GP Ebola vaccine. However, it does not meet the inclusion criteria because it focuses on an Ebola vaccine rather than an mpox or smallpox vaccine.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""84 women were inadvertently vaccinated in early pregnancy or became pregnant <60 days after vaccination or enrollment.""",,"  - ""No congenital anomalies were detected among 44 live-born infants examined.""
