Vaccine demands to achieve outbreak control for MPXV Clade I with one-time mass campaign in sub-Saharan Africa: scenario-based modelling study,"Background Declining cross-protection from historical smallpox vaccination campaigns and persistent zoonotic reservoirs leave many sub-Saharan countries susceptible to mpox outbreaks. With millions of vaccine doses made available to the region since late 2024, estimating the country specific impact of one-time mass vaccination strategies is crucial for ongoing outbreaks and for other countries at future risk. Methods We adapted a next generation matrix model to incorporate vaccination which was calibrated using mpox datasets from the Democratic Republic of Congo. We projected the effective reproduction number, Reff, for 47 sub-Saharan countries from 2025 to 2050 under four transmission scenarios with different contributions of community vs sexual contacts, informed by Clade Ia and Ib outbreaks. We then calculated country-specific minimum vaccine coverages required for outbreak control and evaluated one-time age-specific mass vaccination strategies for reducing Reff. Findings For all 47 countries, Reffs are projected to rise. At least 20 are estimated to require vaccination in 2025, and 36 in 2050, in the event of national outbreaks. For eleven clade I-affected countries, the minimum required coverage is estimated to range from 0%-4.8% to 14.5%-19.5% in 2025 with increasing sexual transmission, increasing to 0%-10.8% and 22.6%-26.0% in 2050 , respectively. The prioritised age group for vaccination shifts from 0-4 to 20-29 with additional sexual transmission. Interpretation With diminishing smallpox-vaccination-induced immunity, increasing Reffs and potential for persistent outbreaks are expected for mpox. Policymakers may face increasing vaccine demand, which will necessitate coordinated efforts to prioritise high-risk groups under supply constraints.","S. Jin, T. R. Asakura, H. Murayama, D. Niyukuri, D. Saila-Ngita, J. T. Lim, A. Endo, B. Dickens",10.1101/2025.01.17.25320753,https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.01.17.25320753,medRxiv,0,2025,yes,The study accounts for all age groups across 47 countries and identifies children aged 0-4 as a priority group in certain scenarios.,no,"The study focuses on modeled population parameters such as the effective reproduction number and required vaccine coverage rather than individual clinical outcomes like actual infection prevention, hospitalization, or death rates in study participants.",no,"The research is a 'scenario-based modelling study' and a 'next generation matrix model', which does not fit the inclusion criteria for primary human research or evidence synthesis.",no,This is a mathematical simulation study and does not involve direct experimentation or observation of human subjects.,yes,"The study includes modeling projections for children, specifically referencing the 0-4 age group.",yes,The study models vaccination strategies that target the childhood period (age 0-4).,yes,The study addresses mpox vaccination and references historical smallpox-vaccination-induced immunity.,Exclude,The paper is a mathematical simulation study focused on modeling vaccine coverage needs for outbreak control. It is excluded from the review because it is not a primary human study or evidence synthesis and does not report clinical outcomes observed in human participants.,"Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Study Type",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""prioritised age group for vaccination shifts from 0-4""",,"  - ""minimum vaccine coverages required for outbreak control""",,"  - ""adapted a next generation matrix model""",,"  - ""adapted a next generation matrix model""",,"  - ""prioritised age group for vaccination shifts from 0-4""",,"  - ""prioritised age group for vaccination shifts from 0-4""",,"  - ""historical smallpox vaccination""",
Is there any evidence for milder courses of monkeypox virus infections with childhood smallpox vaccination?,"Since May 2022, unusually high numbers of human monkeypox virus (MPXV) infections are being reported across Europe and other regions [1]. On July 23, 2022, WHO declared that the current outbreak represents a public health emergency of international concern and recommended targeted immunization for persons at high risk of exposure for MPXV [2]. Laboratory and animal studies, as well as epidemiological observations mainly from Africa suggest that smallpox vaccination may provide some protection against MPXV [3]. However, in a small outbreak in the US in 2003, no significant differences in serious clinical conditions or complications were seen between vaccinated and unvaccinated individuals [4]. In the absence of controlled clinical trials, we were interested if smallpox vaccination could alter the clinical picture of MPXV infection. In our ongoing nationwide observational study of 546 MPVX infections among men who have sex with men (MSM), the acute clinical presentation did not differ between MSM with and without HIV infection [5]. For the present analysis, 298 MPVX patients with at least 7 days of follow-up were grouped according to documented smallpox vaccine during childhood (33 vaccinated, 191 unvaccinated, and 74 with unknown status). Hospitalization rates due to MPXV infection were 9·1% in vaccinated persons and 6·8% in unvaccinated persons. However, acute systemic symptoms such as fever, headache or body aches were lower in vaccinated persons, while rates of limited cutaneous disease (defined as 0–3 lesions) were higher (Fig. 1). In a logistic regression analysis including HIV status, PrEP use and vaccination status, odds ratio of limited disease without systemic symptoms was 4·17 with vaccination (95% confidence interval 1·63–10·65, p = 0·003). Our results may provide some limited evidence for a milder course of disease with a history of a smallpox vaccination. However, hospitalization rates were not lower in vaccinated persons. As hospitalizations probably correlate not only with vaccination history but also with the nature and route of exposure in the current outbreak as well as age and comorbidities, it may be challenging to demonstrate any clinical benefit of vaccines even in large populations, based on hospitalization rates alone. We believe that an accurate, consensual index of MPXV infection severity and complications is imperative. Such an index, preferably considering systemic symptoms as well as lesion numbers, could not only help to demonstrate clinical benefits of the current smallpox vaccination campaign, but also to evaluate potential risk factors for disease severity and the efficacy of future MPXV therapies. * Christian Hoffmann hoffmann@ich-hamburg.de","C. Hoffmann, Eva Wolf, H. Jessen, M. Bickel, C. Boesecke",10.1007/s15010-022-01923-7,https://doi.org/10.1007/s15010-022-01923-7,Infection,7,2022,yes,"The study focuses on individuals with MPXV infections, not healthy adults.",yes,The study reports on hospitalization rates and clinical symptoms (severity) of MPXV.,yes,"The paper describes an ongoing nationwide observational study, which is a primary research design.",yes,The study is a human observational study involving patients with monkeypox.,no,"The study population consists of men who have sex with men (MSM) who received vaccinations during childhood; it does not currently involve participants who are pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",yes,The study specifically examines individuals who received the smallpox vaccine during childhood.,yes,The study evaluates the effects of the smallpox vaccination.,Exclude,"While the study evaluates the effectiveness of childhood smallpox vaccination on MPXV severity and reports relevant clinical outcomes, the current study population consists of adult men (MSM) rather than the required target populations of children or pregnant women. Therefore, it does not meet the target population inclusion criteria.",Target Population,Target Population,0.1,"  - ""298 MPVX patients with at least 7 days of follow-up were grouped according to documented smallpox vaccine during childhood""",,"  - ""Hospitalization rates due to MPXV infection were 9·1% in vaccinated persons and 6·8% in unvaccinated persons.""
  - ""acute systemic symptoms such as fever, headache or body aches were lower in vaccinated persons,""",,"  - ""ongoing nationwide observational study of 546 MPVX infections among men who have sex with men (MSM),""",,"  - ""298 MPVX patients with at least 7 days of follow-up were grouped according to documented smallpox vaccine during childhood""",,"  - ""298 MPVX patients with at least 7 days of follow-up were grouped according to documented smallpox vaccine during childhood""
