The Changing Global Epidemiology of Re-emerging Human Monkeypox Virus Infection: A Systematic Review,"Human monkeypox virus (MPVX) infection represents an emerging zoonotic disease caused by an orthopoxvirus, resulting in a condition reminiscent of smallpox. More recent developments have witnessed a notable surge in global MPVX outbreaks, eliciting significant concerns. We aimed to investigate the epidemiological factors of the emerging human monkeypox virus infection, including the number of suspected, confirmed, and fatal cases, as well as the risk factors for contracting monkeypox infection. We performed a systematic review of peer-reviewed literature by following the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) guidelines. An electronic database search (PubMed, Wiley Online Library, and Science Direct) was undertaken. For monkeypox-related studies, we included 25 peer-reviewed articles from 2018 and 2022, and data were extracted on the current evidence on the cases and the risk factors for MPVX infection, to develop public health advisories. Our reports show a rapid rise of MPVX cases in the highly endemic African regions after the 1970s, spread to other countries, and an increase in the median age from young children to young adults. The cessation of smallpox vaccination might have been one of the factors responsible for these findings. As of 2022, the genomic sequences of ten MPVX strains associated with the recent countrywide outbreak have been determined. While the West African Clade has been primarily implicated in the recent viral surge, data were insufficient to determine which mutation contributed to increased transmissibility. In the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC), sleeping on the floor was significantly associated with contracting MPVX, while eating or processing of animal foods was not a significant risk factor. In the United States, cleaning the cages and bedding of sick animals, touching infected animals, and daily exposure to sick animals were associated with an increased probability of contracting the MPVX infection. Recent global outbreaks and the rising incidence of MPVX infections among young adults in the endemic zones might be a result of the cessation of the smallpox vaccine. The increased risk associated with exposure to sick animals or sleeping on the floor suggests high infectivity from animal excretions. Increasing awareness, strict surveillance, and contact tracing can help contain global outbreaks. The ring vaccination approach for exposed individuals is another potential disease containment strategy. Future studies should investigate measures for rapid laboratory diagnosis, maintaining lab safety, and transmissibility.","S. Sham, F. Sapna, F. Anjali, S. Kumar, V. Podder, Soumya Jaladi, A. Bendari, R. Al-Refai, Manal M Baloch, Mohammed Abdelwahed, Nfn Kiran, S. Geetha, H. Laharwani",10.7759/cureus.45123,https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.45123,Cureus,3,2023,yes,"The study includes data on young children, thus it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study focuses on epidemiological data such as case counts and risk factors for infection rather than assessing the safety or effectiveness (e.g., prevention of infection or death) of administered vaccines.",yes,The study is a systematic review.,yes,The review focuses on human MPVX infection.,yes,The study mentions 'young children' as part of the demographic data investigated in the review.,no,The study discusses the 'cessation' of vaccination rather than reporting on participants who received the vaccine during the specified life stages.,yes,The study references the impact of smallpox vaccination.,Exclude,This systematic review investigates the global epidemiology and risk factors of monkeypox infection. It does not meet the inclusion criteria because it does not report on vaccine safety or effectiveness outcomes and does not evaluate individuals who were vaccinated during the required childhood or pregnancy periods.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing",Relevant Outcomes,0.1,"  - ""increase in the median age from young children to young adults.""",,"  - ""We aimed to investigate the epidemiological factors of the emerging human monkeypox virus infection, including the number of suspected, confirmed, and fatal cases, as well as the risk factors for contracting monkeypox infection.""",,"  - ""We performed a systematic review of peer-reviewed literature""",,"  - ""Human monkeypox virus (MPVX) infection represents an emerging zoonotic disease""",,"  - ""increase in the median age from young children to young adults.""",,"  - ""The cessation of smallpox vaccination might have been one of the factors responsible for these findings.""",,"  - ""The cessation of smallpox vaccination might have been one of the factors responsible for these findings.""",
Estimating baseline rates of adverse perinatal and neonatal outcomes using a facility-based surveillance approach: A prospective observational study from the WHO Global Vaccine Safety Multi-Country Collaboration on safety in pregnancy,"Summary Background Most perinatal and neonatal deaths occur in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs), yet, quality data on burden of adverse outcomes of pregnancy is limited in such countries. Methods A network of 21 maternity units, across seven countries, undertook surveillance for low birthweight, preterm birth, small for gestational age (SGA), stillbirths, congenital microcephaly, in-hospital neonatal deaths, and neonatal infections in a cohort of over 85,000 births from May 2019 - August 2020. For each outcome, site-specific rates per 1,000 livebirths (or per 1,000 total births for stillbirth) and 95% confidence intervals (CI) were calculated. Descriptive sensitivity analysis was conducted to gain insight regarding underreporting of four outcomes at 16 sites. Findings Estimated rates varied across countries and sites, ranging between 43·3-329·5 and 21·4-276·6/1000 livebirths for low birthweight and preterm birth respectively and 11·8-81/1,000 livebirths for SGA. No cases of congenital microcephaly were reported by three sites while the highest estimated rate was 13/1,000 livebirths. Neonatal infection and neonatal death rates varied between 1·8-73 and 0-59·9/1000 livebirths respectively while stillbirth rates ranged between 0-57·1/1000 total births across study sites. Results from the sensitivity analysis confirmed the underreporting of congenital microcephaly and SGA in our study. Interpretation Our study establishes site-specific baseline rates for important adverse perinatal and neonatal outcomes and addresses a critical evidence gap towards improved monitoring of benefits and risks of emerging pregnancy and neonatal interventions. Funding The study was sponsored by the World Health Organization with funding from the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation.","Apoorva Sharan, A. Stuurman, Shubhashri Jahagirdar, Varalakshmi Elango, M. Riera-Montes, Neeraj Kumar Kashyap, J. Biccler, Ramesh Poluru, N. Arora, M. Mathai, P. Mangtani, H. Devlieger, S. Anderson, B. Whitaker, H. Wong, A. Moran, C. Maure",10.1016/j.eclinm.2022.101506,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eclinm.2022.101506,EClinicalMedicine,3,2022,yes,The study focuses on neonates and pregnant women in the context of births.,no,"While the study reports neonatal outcomes, it does not report safety or effectiveness outcomes related to mpox infection or vaccines, only baseline event rates.",yes,The study is a prospective observational surveillance study.,yes,This is a human study involving a cohort of births.,yes,"The study participants include neonates and mothers (over 85,000 births).",no,Participants in this study did not receive a vaccine; the study's purpose was to establish baseline rates of adverse events before interventions are introduced.,no,The study does not involve vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines; it is explicitly a baseline study for future monitoring.,Exclude,This study is a prospective observational surveillance study that establishes baseline rates for adverse perinatal and neonatal outcomes. It does not involve any mpox or smallpox vaccination or clinical outcomes related to such vaccines.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Most perinatal and neonatal deaths occur in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs),""
  - ""cohort of over 85,000 births""",,"  - ""surveillance for low birthweight, preterm birth, small for gestational age (SGA), stillbirths, congenital microcephaly, in-hospital neonatal deaths, and neonatal infections""
  - ""Our study establishes site-specific baseline rates for important adverse perinatal and neonatal outcomes and addresses a critical evidence gap towards improved monitoring of benefits and risks of emerging pregnancy and neonatal interventions.""",,"  - ""A network of 21 maternity units, across seven countries, undertook surveillance""",,"  - ""cohort of over 85,000 births""",,"  - ""cohort of over 85,000 births""",,"  - ""Our study establishes site-specific baseline rates for important adverse perinatal and neonatal outcomes and addresses a critical evidence gap towards improved monitoring of benefits and risks of emerging pregnancy and neonatal interventions.""",,"  - ""Our study establishes site-specific baseline rates for important adverse perinatal and neonatal outcomes and addresses a critical evidence gap towards improved monitoring of benefits and risks of emerging pregnancy and neonatal interventions.""",
"P-2176. Descriptive Epidemiology of Mpox and Varicella-Zoster Virus (VZV) Co-Infection, January 2022 to March 2025 in Imo State, Southeastern Nigeria","Abstract Background Mpox is a zoonotic disease that is endemic to Nigeria and is caused by the Mpox virus, a member of the orthopoxvirus family. In humans, this virus can produce a severe illness that may resemble chickenpox or smallpox, and it frequently results in co-infections with varicella-zoster viruses (VZV). The first occurrence in Imo State was reported in 2018 and resulted in death. Beginning on January 6, 2022, a rising number of instances signaled the beginning of the outbreak. We looked into the severity of the co-infections of Mpox and varicella-zoster (VZV) outbreaks in the state. Methods Using the accepted standard case definition for Mpox, which is defined as a person with an acute illness who has a fever >38.3 °C, a severe headache, lymphadenopathy, back pain, and rashes that spread to every part of the body, including the soles of the feet and palms of the hands. In order to create a line list, we gathered sociodemographic and clinical information. For laboratory validation utilizing reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR), we collected swabs and blood samples. We computed ratios and rates. Results 357 suspected cases were found and investigated from January 2022 to March 2025, there were 56 laboratory-confirmed cases of Mpox-VZV coinfection with 2 deaths and the highest proportion of cases being reported from Owerri Municipal LGA (23.8%), males (69.0%), aged 0-5 years (21.4%), and family members as contacts (50.3%). With a case fatality rate of 3.6% and a positivity rate of 15.7%. Fever, palm, foot, and cheek rashes, as well as pustules, were present in all 56 patients. Conclusion During the response, we enhanced surveillance for active case search, which helped to identify more cases. In order to encourage early diagnosis and case treatment, there is a need to increase awareness of the condition and its risk factors for high levels of index suspicion. In Nigeria, the introduction of the Mpox and varicella-zoster viruses (VZV) infection vaccines is needed, especially for children under 5 years children because they are mostly infected. Disclosures All Authors: No reported disclosures","H. Egbuna, L. Ihedioha, E. Oparaocha, Ugonma Winnie Dozie, Chimezie Iwuala",10.1093/ofid/ofaf695.2339,https://doi.org/10.1093/ofid/ofaf695.2339,Open Forum Infectious Diseases,0,2026,yes,The study is not limited to healthy adults; it specifically identifies children aged 0-5 years as a significant portion of its cohort.,no,"The study reports on clinical outcomes of infection such as deaths, but it does not report safety or effectiveness outcomes for a vaccine, as no vaccine was administered.",yes,"The study is a descriptive epidemiological investigation (outbreak report), which is a form of primary research.",yes,"The study is a human study focusing on patients in Imo State, Nigeria.",yes,The study includes children (aged 0-5 years).,no,The study participants did not receive a vaccine; the authors explicitly state that the introduction of vaccines is needed for this population.,no,"The study does not involve the administration or evaluation of Mpox or smallpox vaccines, only recommending their future introduction.",Exclude,"This is an epidemiological study describing an outbreak of Mpox and VZV co-infection in Nigeria, which included young children. However, it is not a vaccine study, as no participants received vaccinations; instead, the authors used the results to advocate for the future introduction of vaccine programs.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccination Timing,0.1,"  - ""56 laboratory-confirmed cases of Mpox-VZV coinfection with 2 deaths and the highest proportion of cases being reported from Owerri Municipal LGA (23.8%), males (69.0%), aged 0-5 years (21.4%),""",,"  - ""56 laboratory-confirmed cases of Mpox-VZV coinfection with 2 deaths and the highest proportion of cases being reported from Owerri Municipal LGA (23.8%), males (69.0%), aged 0-5 years (21.4%),""",,"  - ""We looked into the severity of the co-infections of Mpox and varicella-zoster (VZV) outbreaks in the state.""",,"  - ""We looked into the severity of the co-infections of Mpox and varicella-zoster (VZV) outbreaks in the state.""",,"  - ""56 laboratory-confirmed cases of Mpox-VZV coinfection with 2 deaths and the highest proportion of cases being reported from Owerri Municipal LGA (23.8%), males (69.0%), aged 0-5 years (21.4%),""",,"  - ""In Nigeria, the introduction of the Mpox and varicella-zoster viruses (VZV) infection vaccines is needed, especially for children under 5 years children because they are mostly infected.""",,"  - ""In Nigeria, the introduction of the Mpox and varicella-zoster viruses (VZV) infection vaccines is needed, especially for children under 5 years children because they are mostly infected.""",
Secondary complications and management strategies in human monkeypox: A case series,"PURPOSE
This case series aims to characterize the demographics, complications, and spectrum of clinical manifestations of emerging human monkeypox (HMPX) cases.
METHODS
A descriptive case series of nine patients with confirmed HMPX infection via monkeypox virus (West African clade) DNA in real-time polymerase chain reaction (PCR) using swabs from human pustular or vesicular rash specimens is presented. Data was collected from May to August 2022. The clinical characterization of the types and location of the injury, sexual preferences, risk factors, history of sexually transmitted infections (STI), complications, and management strategies is reported.
RESULTS
9/9 (100%) of the participants were men who had sex with men (MSM) and engaged in unprotected sexual intercourse in the last thirty days prior to presentation. All participants reported significant impairment in daily activities. All reported cases had skin lesions, and 7/9 (77.7%) of the participants had anogenital and/or mucosal lesions. 6/9 (77.7%) participants had a history of sexually transmitted infection (STI) and 1/9 (11.1%) were positive for human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) on active antiretroviral therapy (ART). 2/9 (22.2%) cases had secondary complications, including cellulitis of the scrotum or arm, and were admitted to the hospital with antibiotics. 1/9 (11.1%) cases reported severe migraine-type headaches. Participants with severe anal pain and tenesmus were adequately treated with lidocaine jelly and polyethylene glycol. Tecovirimat was utilized in 2/9 (22.2%) cases, and only one patient was vaccinated with the smallpox vaccine Imvanex (JYNNEOSTM ) after infection. Fiduciary isolation was activated for all patients who were not hospitalized.
CONCLUSIONS
HMPX has significant morbidity; all reported cases in this series described significant impairment in daily living. The identification of high-risk populations and the initiation of prophylactic treatment should be considered. Severe complications such as agonizing migraines and secondary bacterial infections of skin lesions are possible. This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.","B. Srichawla, M. Garcia-Dominguez, Subtain Zia",10.1002/jmv.28449,https://doi.org/10.1002/jmv.28449,Journal of Medical Virology,5,2022,yes,The study focuses on patients with HMPX infection and includes individuals with HIV. It is not limited to healthy adults.,yes,The study reports complications like cellulitis and management outcomes.,yes,This is a descriptive case series.,yes,The study describes nine human patients.,no,"The paper describes a cohort of nine men who have sex with men (MSM). There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"One patient received the vaccine after infection, but the population consists of adult MSM. There is no evidence of vaccination during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The study mentions the smallpox vaccine Imvanex (JYNNEOS).,Exclude,"This is a case series of nine adult MSM with monkeypox. It fails the target population criteria as it does not include pregnant women, children, or adolescents, nor does it address vaccination during those periods.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""A descriptive case series of nine patients with confirmed HMPX infection""
