"Estimated Effectiveness of JYNNEOS Vaccine in Preventing Mpox: A Multijurisdictional Case-Control Study — United States, August 19, 2022–March 31, 2023","As of March 31, 2023, more than 30,000 monkeypox (mpox) cases had been reported in the United States in an outbreak that has disproportionately affected gay, bisexual, and other men who have sex with men (MSM) and transgender persons. 1 Mpox: 2022 outbreak cases and data. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. https://www.cdc.gov/poxvirus/mpox/response/2022/index.htmlDate accessed: March 31, 2023 Google Scholar JYNNEOS vaccine (Modified Vaccinia Ankara vaccine, Bavarian Nordic) was approved by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) in 2019 for the prevention of smallpox and mpox through subcutaneous injection as a 2-dose series (0.5 mL per dose, administered 4 weeks apart). 2 Food and Drug AdministrationJYNNEOS [package insert, revised June 2021]. US Department of Health and Human Services, Food and Drug Administration. https://www.fda.gov/media/131078/downloadDate: 2021 Google Scholar To expand vaccine access, an Emergency Use Authorization was issued by FDA on August 9, 2022, for dose-sparing intradermal injection of JYNNEOS as a 2-dose series (0.1 mL per dose, administered 4 weeks apart). 3 Food and Drug AdministrationEmergency use authorization for the emergency use of JYNNEOS. US Department of Health and Human Services, Food and Drug Administration. https://www.fda.gov/media/160774/downloadDate: 2022 Google Scholar Vaccination was available to persons with known or presumed exposure to a person with mpox (postexposure prophylaxis [PEP]) and persons at increased risk for mpox or who might benefit from vaccination (pre-exposure prophylaxis [PrEP]). 4 Mpox: vaccination basics. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. https://www.cdc.gov/poxvirus/mpox/vaccines/index.html Google Scholar Because information on JYNNEOS vaccine effectiveness (VE) is limited, a matched case-control study was conducted in 12 US jurisdictions, including 9 Emerging Infections Program sites and 3 Epidemiology and Laboratory Capacity sites, to evaluate VE against mpox among MSM and transgender adults aged 18 to 49 years. From August 19, 2022, to March 31, 2023, a total of 309 case patients were matched to 608 control patients. Adjusted VE was 75.2% (95% confidence interval [CI], 61.2 to 84.2) for partial vaccination (1 dose) and 85.9% (95% CI, 73.8 to 92.4) for full vaccination (2 doses). Adjusted VE for full vaccination by subcutaneous, intradermal, and heterologous routes of administration was 88.9% (95% CI, 56.0 to 97.2), 80.3% (95% CI, 22.9 to 95.0), and 86.9% (95% CI, 69.1 to 94.5), respectively. Adjusted VE for full vaccination among immunocompromised participants was 70.2% (95% CI, −37.9 to 93.6) and among immunocompetent participants was 87.8% (95% CI, 57.5 to 96.5). JYNNEOS is effective at reducing the risk for mpox. Because duration of protection of 1 versus 2 doses remains unknown, persons at increased risk for mpox exposure should receive the 2-dose series as recommended by the Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices regardless of administration route or immunocompromised status.","Michelle N. Huber, Stephen Lim",10.1016/j.annemergmed.2023.08.376,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.annemergmed.2023.08.376,Annals of Emergency Medicine,10,2023,yes,"The study includes immunocompromised participants, meaning it is not exclusively limited to healthy adults.",yes,The study reports on vaccine effectiveness (VE) against mpox infection.,yes,The study is a matched case-control study.,yes,The study involved human case patients and control patients.,no,"The study was explicitly limited to adults aged 18 to 49 years. No pregnant women, children, or adolescents were included.",no,Participants received the vaccine as adults (18-49 years).,yes,The study evaluates the JYNNEOS (Modified Vaccinia Ankara) vaccine.,Exclude,"This study is a matched case-control evaluation of JYNNEOS vaccine effectiveness in human adults. However, it is excluded because it focused exclusively on adults aged 18 to 49 years, failing the requirement to include or focus on pregnant women, children, or adolescents.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Adjusted VE for full vaccination among immunocompromised participants was 70.2%""",,"  - ""evaluate VE against mpox among MSM and transgender adults aged 18 to 49 years.""",,"  - ""matched case-control study""",,"  - ""total of 309 case patients were matched to 608 control patients.""",,"  - ""evaluate VE against mpox among MSM and transgender adults aged 18 to 49 years.""",,"  - ""evaluate VE against mpox among MSM and transgender adults aged 18 to 49 years.""",,"  - ""JYNNEOS vaccine (Modified Vaccinia Ankara vaccine, Bavarian Nordic)""",
Major Advances in Monkeypox Vaccine Research and Development – An Update,"Monkeypox (MPX) is a zoonotic disease that is endemic to the western and central regions of Africa and it is caused by monkeypox virus (MPXV), which is classified as a member of the Poxviridae family, specifically the Chordopoxvirinae subfamily, and the Orthopoxvirus genus. The current multiregional outbreak of MPX, which started in May of 2022, has since swiftly spread across the globe and thus has been declared a global public health emergency by the World Health Organization (WHO). Protective immunity against MPXV can be achieved by administering a smallpox vaccination, as the two viruses share antigenic properties. Although smallpox was declared eradicated in 1980, the vaccine campaign was halted the following year, leaving the population with significantly less immunity than it had before. The potential for human-to-human transmission of MPXV has grown as a result. Due to the lack of a particular treatment for MPX infection, anti-viral medications initially designed for the smallpox virus are being employed. However, the prognosis for MPX may vary depending on factors like immunization history, pre-existing illnesses, and comorbidities, even though the majority of persons who develop MPX have a mild, self-limiting illness. Vaccines and antiviral drugs are being researched as potential responses to the latest 2022 MPX epidemic. The first-generation smallpox vaccinations maintained in national stockpiles of several countries are not recommended due to not meeting the current safety and manufacturing criteria, as stated by the WHO. Newer, safer (second- and third-generation) smallpox vaccines, such as JYNNEOSTM, which has been licensed for the prevention of MPX, are indicated as potentially useful in the interim guideline. Studies on vaccines and antiviral drugs are still being investigated as possible remedies to the recent MPX outbreak. This mini-review article serves as a retrospective look at the evolution of smallpox vaccines from their inception in the 1700s to the current trends up to the end of year 2022, specifically for developing monkeypox vaccines.","Deepak Chandran, V.G. Nandanagopal, M. Gopan, K. Megha, C.R. Hari Sankar, M. M. Aslam, V. Savanth, M. Pran, F. Nainu, M. I. Yatoo, Mohammad Ebad ur Rehman, Hitesh Chopra, T. Emran, A. Dey, A. Sharma, AbdulRahman A. Saied, K. Dhama",10.22207/jpam.16.spl1.08,https://doi.org/10.22207/jpam.16.spl1.08,Journal of Pure and Applied Microbiology,3,2022,maybe,The paper is a mini-review of vaccine evolution and does not specify a study population of healthy or non-pregnant adults.,no,The article discusses the efficacy and development of vaccines generally but does not provide primary data on safety or effectiveness outcomes for specific participants.,no,The paper explicitly identifies itself as a 'mini-review article'. It is not a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis.,maybe,"As a review article, it discusses human health and vaccines but is not a clinical study involving human subjects.",no,"The paper does not indicate the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as participants in a study.",no,The paper is a review of historical trends and current research rather than a study where participants received vaccines during specific life stages.,yes,The study discusses vaccinia-based and historical smallpox vaccines like JYNNEOS.,Exclude,"This is a mini-review article regarding the development of smallpox and mpox vaccines, rather than a primary research study or systematic review. It does not report outcomes from a specific study population, including children or pregnant women.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""This mini-review article serves as a retrospective look at the evolution of smallpox vaccines from their inception in the 1700s to the current trends up to the end of year 2022,""",,"  - ""This mini-review article serves as a retrospective look at the evolution of smallpox vaccines from their inception in the 1700s to the current trends up to the end of year 2022,""",,"  - ""This mini-review article serves as a retrospective look at the evolution of smallpox vaccines from their inception in the 1700s to the current trends up to the end of year 2022,""",,"  - ""This mini-review article serves as a retrospective look at the evolution of smallpox vaccines from their inception in the 1700s to the current trends up to the end of year 2022,""",,"  - ""This mini-review article serves as a retrospective look at the evolution of smallpox vaccines from their inception in the 1700s to the current trends up to the end of year 2022,""",,"  - ""This mini-review article serves as a retrospective look at the evolution of smallpox vaccines from their inception in the 1700s to the current trends up to the end of year 2022,""",,"  - ""This mini-review article serves as a retrospective look at the evolution of smallpox vaccines from their inception in the 1700s to the current trends up to the end of year 2022,""",
"Monkeypox: Single dose of smallpox vaccine offers 78% protection, UKHSA reports","A single dose of the MVA-BN (modified vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic) smallpox vaccine provides around 78% protection against monkeypox 14 days after vaccination, the UK Health Security Agency (UKHSA) has said.1 The agency’s analysis, released as a preprint, looked atmonkeypox cases in England and vaccinationuptake data from 4 July to 3 November 2022 in the eligible gay, bisexual, and men who have sex with men (GBMSM) cohort. Of 363 cases in this period, eight people had been vaccinated at least 14 days before, and 32 had been vaccinated 0-13 days before. The remaining 323 had not been vaccinated during this outbreak, giving an estimated vaccine effectiveness of 78%. The agency said that this was the “strongest UK evidence yet for the effectiveness of the vaccine,” which is sold as Imvanex in the UK and EU and as Jynneos in the US. Currently, the UK is providing a single dose of the vaccine to the GBMSM cohort, as well as to healthcare workers caring for patients with monkeypox and people who have already had close contact with someone with monkeypox.2 Steve Russell, NHS national director of vaccinations and screening, said, “More than 55 000 doses of the monkeypox vaccine have been delivered so far. While the risk of monkeypox remains low, we urge anyone eligible to come forward to get their vaccination.” Since the outbreakbegan inMay 2022 theUKhas reported 3720monkeypox cases up to 21November.3 Vaccine uptake among eligible GBMSM increased steadily from July, reaching 47% by 9 October. Jamie Lopez-Bernal, consultant epidemiologist at the UKHSA, said, “While monkeypox cases are low it remains vital to stay alert to the risks. We now know that a single vaccine dose provides strong protection against monkeypox, which shows just how important vaccination is to protect yourself and others. A second dose is expected to offer even greater and longer lasting protection.” The UKHSA has said that it will continue to investigate the duration of protection from a single dose and two doses of the vaccine.",Elisabeth Mahase,10.1136/bmj.o2829,https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.o2829,British medical journal,6,2022,maybe,"The study focuses on the GBMSM cohort in England. The text doesn't specify if this is limited exclusively to healthy non-pregnant adults, though it is the standard eligibility for this cohort.",yes,The study reports on vaccine effectiveness in preventing monkeypox infection.,maybe,"This is a news report in the BMJ about an analysis (preprint) of uptake and cases. While it describes a study (cohort analysis), the document itself is a secondary news summary.",yes,The analysis focuses on human cases and vaccination data in England.,no,"The study cohort is described as GBMSM (gay, bisexual, and men who have sex with men). There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents specifically being included or analyzed as a target group.",no,"The study looks at vaccination timing relative to the outbreak, but does not specify that doses were administered during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood/adolescence.",yes,The study involves the MVA-BN (modified vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic) smallpox vaccine.,Exclude,"This is a news article reporting on an UKHSA effectiveness analysis of the MVA-BN vaccine in the GBMSM population. It fails the target population criteria as it focuses on adult men who have sex with men rather than pregnant women, children, or neonates.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""analysis, released as a preprint, looked atmonkeypox cases in England and vaccinationuptake data""
