Safety and Efficacy of Post-Eradication Smallpox Vaccine as an Mpox Vaccine: A Systematic Review with Meta-Analysis,"According to the World Health Organization, 83,339 laboratory-confirmed cases, including 72 deaths, of mpox (formerly known as monkeypox), have been reported from 110 locations globally as of 20 December 2022, making the disease a public health concern. Most of the cases (56,171, 67.4%) were reported from countries in North America. Limited data on vaccine effectiveness in the current mpox outbreak are available. However, the modified vaccinia virus (smallpox vaccine) has been predicted to prevent or reduce the severity of the mpox infection. The present study of systematic review and meta-analysis aimed to evaluate the modified vaccinia vaccine’s safety and efficacy on mpox by using reported randomized clinical trials. Following guidelines from the Cochrane Collaboration and PRISMA, multiple databases including PubMed, PLOS ONE, Google Scholar, British Medical Journal, and the U. S. National Library of Medicine were searched. Out of 13,294 research articles initially identified, 187 were screened after removing duplicates. Following the inclusion and exclusion criteria, the meta-analysis included ten studies with 7430 patients. Three researchers independently assessed the risk of bias in the included study. The pooled results suggest that the vaccinia-exposed group had fewer side effects when compared to the vaccinia naïve group (odds ratio: 1.66; 95% CI: 1.07–2.57; p = 0.03). Overall, the modified vaccinia has proven safe and effective in both vaccinia naïve and previously exposed groups, with higher efficacy in the previously exposed groups.","Shelia Malone, A. Mitra, Nwanne Onumah, A. Brown, L. M. Jones, Da’Chirion Tresvant, C. Susan Brown, Austine U Onyia, Faith Iseguede",10.3390/ijerph20042963,https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph20042963,International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health,23,2023,maybe,The abstract mentions 7430 patients but does not specify if they are healthy adults or if the study excludes other groups; the demographics are not explicitly characterized.,yes,The study reports on safety (side effects) and effectiveness (proven safe and effective) for mpox.,yes,The paper is a systematic review and meta-analysis.,yes,The study involves human patients.,maybe,"The study includes '7430 patients', but the abstract does not state whether pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents were included.",maybe,"While the study evaluates the vaccine's efficacy/safety, it does not specify the timing of vaccination relative to pregnancy, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The study specifically evaluates the modified vaccinia (smallpox) vaccine.,Include,"The study is a systematic review of modified vaccinia vaccines for mpox that evaluates safety and efficacy in a large cohort of 7430 patients. However, the abstract does not specify the age or pregnancy status of the participants, making it unclear if it includes the required target populations.",,,3.4,"  - ""meta-analysis included ten studies with 7430 patients.""",,"  - ""The present study of systematic review and meta-analysis aimed to evaluate the modified vaccinia vaccine’s safety and efficacy on mpox""",,"  - ""The present study of systematic review and meta-analysis aimed to evaluate the modified vaccinia vaccine’s safety and efficacy on mpox""",,"  - ""meta-analysis included ten studies with 7430 patients.""",,"  - ""meta-analysis included ten studies with 7430 patients.""",,"  - ""The present study of systematic review and meta-analysis aimed to evaluate the modified vaccinia vaccine’s safety and efficacy on mpox""",,"  - ""The present study of systematic review and meta-analysis aimed to evaluate the modified vaccinia vaccine’s safety and efficacy on mpox""",
Safety of COVID-19 Vaccination in US Children Ages 5-11 Years.,"BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVES
Limited post-authorization safety data for BNT-162b2 COVID-19 vaccination among children ages 5-11 years are available, particularly for the adverse event myocarditis, which has been detected in adolescents and young adults. We describe adverse events observed during the first 4 months of the US COVID-19 vaccination program in this age group.
METHODS
We analyzed data from 3 US safety monitoring systems: v-safe, a voluntary smartphone-based system that monitors reactions and health effects; the Vaccine Adverse Events Reporting System (VAERS), the national spontaneous reporting system co-managed by CDC and FDA; and the Vaccine Safety Datalink (VSD), an active surveillance system that monitors electronic health records for prespecified events, including myocarditis.
RESULTS
Among 48,795 children ages 5-11 years enrolled in v-safe, most reported reactions were mild-to-moderate, most frequently reported the day after vaccination, and were more common after dose 2. VAERS received 7,578 adverse event reports; 97% were non-serious. On review of 194 serious VAERS reports, 15 myocarditis cases were verified; 8 occurred in males after dose 2 (reporting rate 2.2 per million doses). In VSD, no safety signals were detected in weekly sequential monitoring after administration of 726,820 doses.
CONCLUSIONS
Safety findings for BNT-162b2 vaccine from 3 US monitoring systems in children ages 5-11 years show that most reported adverse events were mild and no safety signals were observed in active surveillance. VAERS reporting rates of myocarditis after dose 2 in this age group were substantially lower than those observed among adolescents ages 12-15 years.","Anne M. Hause, D. Shay, N. Klein, W. Abara, J. Baggs, M. Cortese, B. Fireman, J. Gee, J. Glanz, Kristin Goddard, K. Hanson, Brandon Hugueley, T. Kenigsberg, E. Kharbanda, Bruno J Lewin, N. Lewis, P. Marquez, T. Myers, A. Naleway, Jennifer C. Nelson, John R. Su, Deborah Thompson, B. Olubajo, M. Oster, Eric S. Weintraub, Joshua T. B. Williams, A. Yousaf, O. Zerbo, Bicheng Zhang, T. Shimabukuro",10.1542/peds.2022-057313,https://doi.org/10.1542/peds.2022-057313,Pediatrics,44,2022,yes,"The study focuses specifically on children ages 5-11 years, not healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on safety outcomes, specifically adverse events including myocarditis.",yes,The study is a primary research project (analysis of surveillance data from three monitoring systems).,yes,The study involves human children monitored through safety systems.,yes,The participants are children ages 5-11 years.,yes,Study participants received the vaccine during childhood (ages 5-11).,no,"The study evaluates the BNT-162b2 COVID-19 vaccine, not a vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the safety of the BNT-162b2 COVID-19 vaccine in children. While it focuses on a relevant pediatric population and reports safety outcomes, it does not involve the required vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""BNT-162b2 COVID-19 vaccination among children ages 5-11 years""",,"  - ""adverse events observed during the first 4 months of the US COVID-19 vaccination program in this age group.""",,"  - ""We analyzed data from 3 US safety monitoring systems:""",,"  - ""We analyzed data from 3 US safety monitoring systems:""
