Vaxxing to elimination: smallpox vaccines as tools to fight mpox,"Introduction An outbreak of monkeypox (mpox) virus emerged on the global landscape in May 2022 and rapidly spread to several nonendemic countries in every WHO health region. To date, over 80,000 cases have been reported, mostly concentrated in North America, Brazil, and Western Europe (1). After an initial period of relatively rapid spread, which earned the outbreak the designation of a public health emergency of international concern (PHEIC), case numbers have steadily declined, especially in high-income countries. This decline in new cases can be attributed to rapid rollout of vaccines, growing herd immunity built by the rapid spread of infection in individuals considered at highest risk, and behavior modification. There has also likely been a herd effect, with nonvaccinated individuals benefiting from the interruption of transmission among vaccinated individuals. Much of the response to the mpox outbreak has relied heavily on repurposing smallpox vaccines as tools for pre-and postexposure prevention to contain the outbreak. However, many unanswered questions remain about the role vaccines could play in eliminating mpox as a global threat to human health. Elimination of disease will require a reduction in the number of cases of mpox to zero in defined geographic areas, a goal that may only be feasible in newly affected, nonendemic countries. Eradication is a much more challenging proposition and probably impossible to attain […]","Boghuma K. Titanji, V. Marconi",10.1172/JCI167632,https://doi.org/10.1172/JCI167632,Journal of Clinical Investigation,7,2023,no,The paper does not define a specific study population and mentions individuals at highest risk in a general context.,no,The paper discusses global trends and vaccine rollout but does not provide primary data or specific outcome results for effectiveness or safety.,no,"The article is a narrative 'Introduction' or commentary, rather than a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or systematic review.",yes,The paper discusses the human mpox outbreak.,no,"The text does not mention the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"As the paper is not a clinical study collecting participant data, it does not record vaccination timing for these target groups.",yes,The paper explicitly mentions the use of smallpox vaccines in the mpox response.,Exclude,"This paper is a narrative introduction or commentary about the 2022 mpox outbreak and the role of smallpox vaccines. It is not a primary study and does not include data on the target populations of interest, such as children or pregnant women.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""individuals considered at highest risk,""",,"  - ""Much of the response to the mpox outbreak has relied heavily on repurposing smallpox vaccines as tools for pre-and postexposure prevention""",,"  - ""Introduction""",,"  - ""Much of the response to the mpox outbreak has relied heavily on repurposing smallpox vaccines as tools for pre-and postexposure prevention""",,"  - ""Much of the response to the mpox outbreak has relied heavily on repurposing smallpox vaccines as tools for pre-and postexposure prevention""
