Low levels of monkeypox virus-neutralizing antibodies after MVA-BN vaccination in healthy individuals,"In July 2022, the ongoing monkeypox (MPX) outbreak was declared a public health emergency of international concern. Modified vaccinia Ankara—Bavarian Nordic (MVA-BN, also known as Imvamune, JYNNEOS or Imvanex) is a third-generation smallpox vaccine that is authorized and in use as a vaccine against MPX. To date, there are no data showing MPX virus (MPXV)-neutralizing antibodies in vaccinated individuals nor vaccine efficacy against MPX. Here we show that MPXV-neutralizing antibodies can be detected after MPXV infection and after historic smallpox vaccination. However, a two-shot MVA-BN immunization series in non-primed individuals yields relatively low levels of MPXV-neutralizing antibodies. Dose-sparing of an MVA-based influenza vaccine leads to lower MPXV-neutralizing antibody levels, whereas a third vaccination with the same MVA-based vaccine significantly boosts the antibody response. As the role of MPXV-neutralizing antibodies as a correlate of protection against disease and transmissibility is currently unclear, we conclude that cohort studies following vaccinated individuals are necessary to assess vaccine efficacy in at-risk populations. Historic smallpox vaccination and monkeypox virus (MPXV) infection elicit MPXV-neutralizing antibodies, but MPXV-neutralizing antibodies are less frequent and of lower magnitude after vaccination with MVA-BN—the vaccine approved and in use for protection against MPXV and smallpox.","Luca M. Zaeck, M. Lamers, B. Verstrepen, T. Bestebroer, M. V. van Royen, H. Götz, M. Shamier, L. V. van Leeuwen, K. Schmitz, Kimberley Alblas, Suzanne van Efferen, S. Bogers, S. Scherbeijn, G. Rimmelzwaan, E. V. van Gorp, M. Koopmans, B. Haagmans, C. Geurtsvankessel, R. D. de Vries",10.1038/s41591-022-02090-w,https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-022-02090-w,Nature Medicine,218,2022,no,"The study focuses on healthy individuals and non-primed individuals, which typically refers to healthy non-pregnant adults in this context.",no,The study only reports immunogenicity findings (neutralizing antibodies). It explicitly states that clinical vaccine efficacy (protection against disease) and and data on the role of antibodies in protection are lacking or unclear.,yes,The study is a primary research study evaluating antibody levels in vaccinated individuals.,yes,The study involves human participants who were infected or vaccinated.,no,"The study focuses on 'healthy individuals' and those with 'historic smallpox vaccination'. There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",maybe,"While participants with 'historic smallpox vaccination' were likely vaccinated in childhood, the study's primary investigation of the MVA-BN vaccine is in non-primed healthy adults. It does not focus on pregnancy or pediatric vaccination timing.",yes,The study involves MVA-BN (Imvanex/JYNNEOS) and historic smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This study evaluates immunogenicity (MPXV-neutralizing antibodies) rather than clinical effectiveness or safety. Furthermore, there is no evidence that the study included the target populations of interest, such as children or pregnant women, focusing instead on healthy individuals and those with historic vaccinations.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Target Population",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Here we show that MPXV-neutralizing antibodies can be detected after MPXV infection and after historic smallpox vaccination. However, a two-shot MVA-BN immunization series in non-primed individuals yields relatively low levels of MPXV-neutralizing antibodies.""",,"  - ""Here we show that MPXV-neutralizing antibodies can be detected after MPXV infection and after historic smallpox vaccination. However, a two-shot MVA-BN immunization series in non-primed individuals yields relatively low levels of MPXV-neutralizing antibodies.""
  - ""To date, there are no data showing MPX virus (MPXV)-neutralizing antibodies in vaccinated individuals nor vaccine efficacy against MPX.""
