Adverse events following smallpox vaccination with ACAM2000 in a military population.,"BACKGROUND
Generalized vaccinia and benign exanthems are 2 adverse events that have been associated with the smallpox vaccination. Accurate incidence and prevalence rates of each are not readily available, but these events are thought to be uncommon. To our knowledge, this is the first case series to provide clinical as well as pathologic descriptions of multiple papulovesicular eruptions occurring after receiving the second-generation smallpox vaccine, ACAM2000 (Acambis, Canton, Massachusetts), among a vaccinia-naïve military population. In addition, we report the first confirmed case, to our knowledge, of generalized vaccinia following administration of the ACAM2000 vaccine.
OBSERVATIONS
All patients received primary smallpox immunization as well as 1 to 3 concurrent or near-concurrent (within the preceding 21 days) immunizations for typhoid, anthrax, hepatitis B, and/or seasonal influenza. One patient presented with a flulike prodrome and diffuse vesiclopustules covering the face, neck, chest, back, and upper and lower extremities. Vaccinia polymerase chain reaction confirmed generalized vaccinia. The remaining 7 patients presented with unusual, painful, and pruritic papulovesicular eruptions occurring on the extensor surfaces of their upper and lower extremities without systemic symptoms. Histologic findings revealed 2 general patterns, including a dermal hypersensitivity reaction with lymphocytic vasculitis and a vesicular spongiotic dermatitis with eosinophils.
CONCLUSIONS
We present the first confirmed case of generalized vaccinia following immunization with the second-generation smallpox vaccine ACAM2000. In addition, we describe 7 cases of benign, acral, papulovesicular eruptions thought to be associated with ACAM2000 administration. Further research is needed to discern the pathogenesis of these benign eruptions as well as their incidence and prevalence and that of generalized vaccinia with ACAM2000.","Thomas M Beachkofsky, Scott C Carrizales, Jeffrey J Bidinger, D. Hrncir, D. Whittemore, C. Hivnor",10.1001/archdermatol.2010.46,https://doi.org/10.1001/archdermatol.2010.46,Archives of Dermatology,28,2010,no,"The study is exclusively limited to a 'military population', which consists of adults who are typically healthy and not pregnant at the time of active duty vaccinations. Therefore, it does not meet the requirement of NOT being exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports safety outcomes, specifically 'generalized vaccinia' and 'papulovesicular eruptions'.",yes,The study is a 'case series'.,yes,The study involves human 'patients' from a military population.,no,"The study only includes a 'military population' and does not mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Vaccination occurred during adulthood while serving in the military, not during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,"The study involves the 'second-generation smallpox vaccine, ACAM2000', which is a vaccinia-based vaccine.",Exclude,"This study is a case series describing adverse events like generalized vaccinia in a military population vaccinated with ACAM2000. It fails the inclusion criteria because it is limited to healthy adults and does not include pregnant women, children, or vaccination during those life stages.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,,,"  - ""describe 7 cases of benign, acral, papulovesicular eruptions thought to be associated with ACAM2000 administration.""",,"  - ""first case series to provide clinical as well as pathologic descriptions of multiple papulovesicular eruptions occurring after receiving the second-generation smallpox vaccine, ACAM2000 (Acambis, Canton, Massachusetts), among a vaccinia-naïve military population.""",,"  - ""All patients received primary smallpox immunization""",,"  - ""first case series to provide clinical as well as pathologic descriptions of multiple papulovesicular eruptions occurring after receiving the second-generation smallpox vaccine, ACAM2000 (Acambis, Canton, Massachusetts), among a vaccinia-naïve military population.""",,,,"  - ""first case series to provide clinical as well as pathologic descriptions of multiple papulovesicular eruptions occurring after receiving the second-generation smallpox vaccine, ACAM2000 (Acambis, Canton, Massachusetts), among a vaccinia-naïve military population.""",
Evaluation of Cross-Immunity to the Mpox Virus Due to Historic Smallpox Vaccination,"When the Mpox virus (MPXV) began spreading globally in 2022, it became critical to evaluate whether residual immunity from smallpox vaccination provided cross-protection. To assess the cross-immune response to MPXV, we collected serum samples (n = 97) and PBMCs (n = 30) from healthy-donors, either born before 1974 and reporting smallpox vaccination during childhood or born after 1975 and not vaccinated with Vaccinia virus (VACV)-based vaccines. We evaluated the levels of anti-MPXV IgG and neutralizing antibodies (Nabs) and the presence of a T cell response against MPXV. We found anti-MPXV IgG and Nabs in 60 (89.6%) and 40 (70.1%) vaccinated individuals, respectively. We observed a T cell response to Orthopoxviruses and MPXV peptide pools in 30% of vaccinated individuals. We thus show that a high proportion of subjects who received the smallpox vaccine 40 to 60 years ago have humoral cross-immunity, while the T-cell-specific response against MPXV was observed in a smaller group (30%) of vaccinated individuals. This study, combined with information on immunity developed during natural infection or the administration of current vaccines, will contribute to a better understanding of humoral and cellular responses against MPXV.","G. Matusali, E. Petruccioli, E. Cimini, Francesca Colavita, Aurora Bettini, Eleonora Tartaglia, Settimia Sbarra, S. Meschi, D. Lapa, Massimo Francalancia, L. Bordi, V. Mazzotta, Sabrina Coen, K. Mizzoni, A. Beccacece, E. Nicastri, Luca Pierelli, A. Antinori, Enrico Girardi, F. Vaia, Alessandro Sette, A. Grifoni, D. Goletti, V. Puro, F. Maggi",10.3390/vaccines11101541,https://doi.org/10.3390/vaccines11101541,Vaccines,29,2023,no,"The study is exclusively limited to adults, described as healthy donors born before 1974 or after 1975.",no,"The study reports immunogenicity (IgG, Nabs, T cell responses) rather than clinical effectiveness (prevention of infection) or safety outcomes.",yes,This is a primary research study involving the collection and evaluation of human samples.,yes,The study is a human study using samples from healthy donors.,no,"The study participants were healthy adult donors at the time of sample collection, having received their vaccinations 40 to 60 years prior. No pregnant women, children, or adolescents were participants in the study.",yes,Participants reported receiving the smallpox vaccination during childhood.,yes,The study investigates historic smallpox vaccinations and Vaccinia virus (VACV)-based vaccines.,Exclude,"This study is excluded because it focuses exclusively on healthy adult donors and fails to report relevant clinical effectiveness or safety outcomes, focusing instead on long-term immunogenicity. Although participants received the vaccine during childhood, they were older adults at the time of study participation, failing the population criteria.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Target Population",Population Focus,0.1,"  - ""serum samples (n = 97) and PBMCs (n = 30) from healthy-donors, either born before 1974 and reporting smallpox vaccination during childhood or born after 1975 and not vaccinated""",,"  - ""evaluated the levels of anti-MPXV IgG and neutralizing antibodies (Nabs)""",,"  - ""serum samples (n = 97) and PBMCs (n = 30) from healthy-donors, either born before 1974 and reporting smallpox vaccination during childhood or born after 1975 and not vaccinated""",,"  - ""serum samples (n = 97) and PBMCs (n = 30) from healthy-donors, either born before 1974 and reporting smallpox vaccination during childhood or born after 1975 and not vaccinated""",,"  - ""serum samples (n = 97) and PBMCs (n = 30) from healthy-donors, either born before 1974 and reporting smallpox vaccination during childhood or born after 1975 and not vaccinated""
  - ""received the smallpox vaccine 40 to 60 years ago""",,"  - ""serum samples (n = 97) and PBMCs (n = 30) from healthy-donors, either born before 1974 and reporting smallpox vaccination during childhood or born after 1975 and not vaccinated""",,"  - ""serum samples (n = 97) and PBMCs (n = 30) from healthy-donors, either born before 1974 and reporting smallpox vaccination during childhood or born after 1975 and not vaccinated""",
Effectiveness of BNT162b2 and CoronaVac against COVID-19-related severe outcomes among children and adolescents: A Brazilian nationwide cohort study.,"INTRODUCTION
Nationwide databases from large countries may provide real-world evidence about COVID-19 vaccine effectiveness (VE). This study sought to assess the VE of BNT162b2 and CoronaVac against COVID-19-related severe outcomes in school-aged children and adolescents during the Omicron wave of the COVID-19 pandemic in Brazil.
METHODS
A nationwide population-based cohort study compared the incidence risk ratios (IRRs) of hospitalization due to COVID-19-associated severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS), need for invasive ventilatory support, and death among school-aged children (age 5 to 11 years) and adolescents (age 12 to 17 years), stratified by vaccination status (none, one, or two doses), in 2022. The period included epidemiological weeks (EW) 10 to 34 for school-aged children and EW 1 to EW 22 for adolescents. Data from all individuals hospitalized due to laboratory-confirmed COVID-19-related SARS were extracted from OpenDATASUS, where individual data including clinical outcomes and vaccination status are available. Vaccine coverage was estimated using data from the Brazilian Ministry of Health ""Vacinômetro COVID-19"" dashboard.
RESULTS
An eligible population of 19,219,424 school-aged children and 22,580,918 adolescents was assessed. For school-aged children, the VE against hospitalization for SARS, invasive ventilatory support, and death after one and two doses was 61 % and 58 %, 62 % and 74 %, and 81 % and 88 %, respectively (all p < 0.01). Among adolescents, the VE against the same outcomes after one and two doses was 55 % and 72 %, 60 % and 78 %, and 83 % and 80 %, respectively (all p < 0.05). CoronaVac was noninferior to BNT162b2 considering all outcomes among fully vaccinated school-aged children, a group that could have received either of the two vaccines.
CONCLUSIONS
COVID-19 vaccines are effective against severe outcomes in school-aged children and adolescents and are protective against mortality even after a single dose. CoronaVac was not inferior to BNT162b2 in school-aged children.","Eliandra da Silveira de Lima, Marcos Otávio Brum Antunes, Jesuély Spieckert de Souza, M. Jones, Renato T. Stein, L. Pinto, F. Friedrich, M. Scotta",10.1016/j.vaccine.2024.126550,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2024.126550,Vaccine,0,2024,yes,"The study focuses on school-aged children (5 to 11 years) and adolescents (12 to 17 years), not adults.",no,"The study focuses on COVID-19-related outcomes (hospitalization, death) rather than mpox infection or associated outcomes.",yes,This is a nationwide population-based cohort study.,yes,The study is a cohort analysis of a human population in Brazil.,yes,The participants are children and adolescents aged 5 to 17 years.,yes,The study evaluates vaccines administered during childhood and adolescence.,no,The study investigates BNT162b2 and CoronaVac (COVID-19 vaccines) rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This study is excluded because it evaluates the effectiveness of COVID-19 vaccines (BNT162b2 and CoronaVac) rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines. Additionally, the outcomes measured relate to COVID-19 infection rather than mpox.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""school-aged children (age 5 to 11 years) and adolescents (age 12 to 17 years),""",,"  - ""assess the VE of BNT162b2 and CoronaVac against COVID-19-related severe outcomes in school-aged children and adolescents""
