"A Single Vaccination of Nonhuman Primates with Highly Attenuated Smallpox Vaccine, LC16m8, Provides Long-term Protection against Monkeypox.","Monkeypox virus (MPXV) causes human monkeypox (human MPX), which is a similar disease to smallpox in humans. A previous study showed that a single vaccination of monkeys with LC16m8, a highly attenuated smallpox vaccine, protected them from MPX from 4-5 weeks post-vaccination. In this study, we evaluated the long-term efficacy of a single vaccination with LC16m8 in a nonhuman primate model of MPXV infection. The monkeys were inoculated with either LC16m8, Lister (parental strain of LC16m8), or a mock-up vaccine, and then challenged with MPXV via a subcutaneous route, at 6 and 12 months after vaccination, which we compared with either Lister or the mock-up vaccination. The LC16m8 monkeys exhibited almost no MPX-associated symptoms, whereas most of the naïve monkeys died. LC16m8 generated the protective memory immune response against MPXV, as suggested by the immediate viremia reduction and the response of the IgG antibody. The results demonstrated that the vaccination of monkeys with a single dose of LC16m8 provided durable protection against MPXV for longer than one year after immunization. The results suggest that the vaccination of humans with LC16m8 could induce long-term protection against MPXV infection.","Itoe Iizuka, Y. Ami, Y. Suzaki, N. Nagata, S. Fukushi, M. Ogata, S. Morikawa, H. Hasegawa, M. Mizuguchi, I. Kurane, M. Saijo",10.7883/yoken.JJID.2016.417,https://doi.org/10.7883/yoken.JJID.2016.417,Japanese journal of infectious diseases (Print),44,2017,yes,The study is conducted in a nonhuman primate model and is not limited to healthy human adults.,yes,The study reports on vaccine effectiveness in preventing monkeypox virus symptoms and death.,yes,This is a primary experimental research study in primates.,no,The study is conducted using a 'nonhuman primate model' involving 'monkeys' and is therefore an animal study rather than a human study.,no,"The participants are monkeys and the study does not include human pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Because the study is not conducted in the target human population, it does not meet the vaccination timing requirements for humans.",yes,"The study uses LC16m8, which is an attenuated smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This is an animal model study conducted in nonhuman primates (monkeys) rather than human subjects. While it evaluates the LC16m8 vaccine and its effectiveness against mpox, it fails the required criteria for human studies and specific target populations like children or pregnant women.","Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""evaluated the long-term efficacy of a single vaccination with LC16m8 in a nonhuman primate model of MPXV infection.""",,"  - ""LC16m8 generated the protective memory immune response against MPXV, as suggested by the immediate viremia reduction and the response of the IgG antibody.""
  - ""vaccination of monkeys with a single dose of LC16m8 provided durable protection against MPXV for longer than one year after immunization.""",,"  - ""evaluated the long-term efficacy of a single vaccination with LC16m8 in a nonhuman primate model of MPXV infection.""",,"  - ""evaluated the long-term efficacy of a single vaccination with LC16m8 in a nonhuman primate model of MPXV infection.""
  - ""The monkeys were inoculated with either LC16m8, Lister (parental strain of LC16m8), or a mock-up vaccine,""",,"  - ""The monkeys were inoculated with either LC16m8, Lister (parental strain of LC16m8), or a mock-up vaccine,""",,,,"  - ""evaluated the long-term efficacy of a single vaccination with LC16m8 in a nonhuman primate model of MPXV infection.""",
Vaccines Safety in Children and in General Population: A Pharmacovigilance Study on Adverse Events Following Anti-Infective Vaccination in Italy,"Background: The concern for adverse events following immunization (AEFI) and anti-vaccination movements that lacked scientific evidence-based supports may reduce vaccine uptake in the general population. Thus, the aims of the present study were to characterize AEFI in general population (all age groups), in terms of frequency, preventability, and seriousness and to define predictors of their seriousness in children. Methods: A retrospective study was performed on suspected AEFI reports for children and adults who received any form of vaccinations, collected in Tuscany, Italy, between 1 January and 31 December 2017. Patients’ characteristics, suspected vaccines, and AEFI description were collected. Causality and preventability were assessed using WHO and Schumock and Thornton algorithms, respectively. Logistic regression was used to estimate the reporting odds ratios of potential predictors of AEFI seriousness in children. Results: A total of 223 suspected AEFI reports were collected, and the majority of them were defined as non-serious (76.7%). Reports were mostly related to one vaccine, and to a median of two to five strains/toxoids. The total number of simultaneously administered strains/toxoids and the presence of allergens did not correlate with AEFI seriousness. Considering vaccines with a high number of administered doses (≥60,000 doses), the rates estimated for serious AEFI reports were always very low, ranging between 0.01 and 0.2/1,000 doses. Twenty-four vaccines (8,993 doses) were not related to any AEFI. Conclusion: Results of present study showed that AEFI were very rare; the vast majority of them was non-serious and, despite the claims of anti-vaccination movements, the simultaneous administration of vaccines was safe and did not influence the risk of reporting a serious AEFI, particularly in children.","N. Lombardi, G. Crescioli, A. Bettiol, M. Tuccori, M. Rossi, R. Bonaiuti, C. Ravaldi, M. Levi, A. Mugelli, S. Ricci, F. Lippi, C. Azzari, P. Bonanni, A. Vannacci",10.3389/fphar.2019.00948,https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2019.00948,Frontiers in Pharmacology,35,2019,yes,The study includes children and adults of all age groups.,yes,The study reports on the frequency and seriousness of adverse events following immunization (AEFI).,yes,The study is a retrospective pharmacovigilance study.,yes,The study is a human study analyzing clinical AEFI reports.,yes,The study specifically highlights results for children.,yes,The study evaluates vaccines administered during childhood.,no,"The study evaluates any form of vaccinations administered in Italy in 2017. Smallpox vaccine was discontinued in Italy in the early 1980s, and mpox vaccines were not in routine use in 2017. No mention of vaccinia-based vaccines is made.",Exclude,"This pharmacovigilance study evaluates adverse events following any vaccination in Italy in 2017, including children. However, it does not include vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines, as these were not part of the Italian routine vaccination schedule in 2017.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""A retrospective study was performed on suspected AEFI reports for children and adults who received any form of vaccinations, collected in Tuscany, Italy, between 1 January and 31 December 2017.""
