Pregnancy outcome after covid-19 vaccination in Dakahlia Governorate,"Background: COVID-19 vaccines have been demon-strated to provoke potent protective responses in highly susceptible older adults and subjects with weak immunity. Generally, vaccination is safe throughout pregnancy with the exception of live attenuated vaccines. On the other hand, the efﬁciency and safety of COVID-19 vaccination in pregnancy remain uncertain. Most of COVID-19 vac-cine trials failed to comprise pregnant females due to eth-ical issues and the generalizability of the resultant data. Aim: To detect maternal, and neonatal, outcomes of preg-nant women receiving COVID-19 vaccination (AstraZen-eca or Sinopharm vaccine and others available vaccines in Egypt) three months prior to pregnancy or accidently during 1st trimester of unplanned pregnancy. Methods: This prospective case control observation-al study included 100 pregnant females who received COVID-19 vaccine, and age matched 100 pregnant fe-males who didn’t received vaccine as control group.Lab-oratory investigation included complete blood count and ultrasound (US) examination was done for the pregnant females at 1st, 2nd, and 3rd trimesters. Primary outcomes included the rate of pregnancy loss in the ﬁrst half of preg-nancy, while secondary outcomes included assessment of fetal congenital anomalies, IUGR, preterm labor (PTL), postpartum APGAR score, IUFD, maternal morbidity and maternal mortality. Results: There was NO statistically signiﬁcant difference between vaccinated and non- vaccinated women as regard hemoglobin level (HB) p value (0,055). There was sta-tistically insigniﬁcant difference between vaccinated and non-vaccinated women as regard mode of delivery, total complications, outcome of pregnancy (living fetus, dead fetus or abortion) and mean APGAR score. There was insigniﬁcant relationship between receiving second dose of vaccination and outcome among studied cases (pregnancy complications, ultrasound ﬁndings, outcome of pregnancy and NICU admission). Conclusion: The pregnant women who were immunized against COVID-19 had compara-ble pregnancy outcomes to those who were not immunized.","E. Ibrahim, E. A. Fyala, Mostafa M. Alkhiary",10.21608/egyfs.2025.469349,https://doi.org/10.21608/egyfs.2025.469349,The Egyptian Journal of Fertility and Sterility,0,2025,yes,"The study specifically focuses on pregnant women, not healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"While the study reports safety outcomes like pregnancy loss and congenital anomalies, these are reported for COVID-19 vaccines and not in the context of mpox infection or prevention.",yes,"The study is a prospective case-control observational study, which is a primary research design.",yes,The study involved human participants (pregnant females).,yes,The study's participants are 100 pregnant females.,yes,Participants received the vaccine three months prior to or during the first trimester of pregnancy.,no,"The study evaluates AstraZeneca and Sinopharm COVID-19 vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,This study evaluates the safety and maternal outcomes of COVID-19 vaccinations in pregnant women. It is excluded because it does not investigate vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines and does not report on mpox-related effectiveness or safety outcomes.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""prospective case control observation-al study included 100 pregnant females""",,"  - ""To detect maternal, and neonatal, outcomes of preg-nant women receiving COVID-19 vaccination (AstraZen-eca or Sinopharm vaccine and others available vaccines in Egypt)""",,"  - ""prospective case control observation-al study included 100 pregnant females""",,"  - ""prospective case control observation-al study included 100 pregnant females""",,"  - ""prospective case control observation-al study included 100 pregnant females""",,"  - ""three months prior to pregnancy or accidently during 1st trimester of unplanned pregnancy.""",,"  - ""To detect maternal, and neonatal, outcomes of preg-nant women receiving COVID-19 vaccination (AstraZen-eca or Sinopharm vaccine and others available vaccines in Egypt)""",
"Monkeypox, pregnancy, and evidence of vertical transmission: reply","We appreciate the correspondence by Schwartz et al on our publication on monkeypox and pregnancy.1Mungmunpuntipantip R. Wiwanitkit V. Monkeypox and pregnancy: correspondence.Am J Obstet Gynecol. 2023; 228: 48-49Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (2) Google Scholar The report on Congo’s pregnancy outcomes is mentioned.2Mbala P.K. Huggins J.W. Riu-Rovira T. et al.Maternal and fetal outcomes among pregnant women with human monkeypox infection in the Democratic Republic of 74 Congo.J Infect Dis. 2017; 216: 824-828Crossref PubMed Scopus (135) Google Scholar Mbala et al2Mbala P.K. Huggins J.W. Riu-Rovira T. et al.Maternal and fetal outcomes among pregnant women with human monkeypox infection in the Democratic Republic of 74 Congo.J Infect Dis. 2017; 216: 824-828Crossref PubMed Scopus (135) Google Scholar conclusively showed that monkeypox infections in pregnant women can have catastrophic repercussions, including fetal death. In the study by Mbala et al,2Mbala P.K. Huggins J.W. Riu-Rovira T. et al.Maternal and fetal outcomes among pregnant women with human monkeypox infection in the Democratic Republic of 74 Congo.J Infect Dis. 2017; 216: 824-828Crossref PubMed Scopus (135) Google Scholar a pan-orthopoxvirus minor groove binder–hemagglutinin reverse transcriptase–polymerase chain reaction (PCR) assay was used, and it is not the gold standard for the diagnosis of monkeypox. In addition, the PCR test was conducted according to Kulesh’s protocol, which is for rodent orthopox virus diagnosis.3Kulesh D.A. Loveless B.M. Norwood D. et al.Monkeypox virus detection in rodents using real-time 3’-minor groove binder TaqMan assays on the Roche LightCycler.Lab Invest. 2004; 84: 1200-1208Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (89) Google Scholar Mbala and et al2Mbala P.K. Huggins J.W. Riu-Rovira T. et al.Maternal and fetal outcomes among pregnant women with human monkeypox infection in the Democratic Republic of 74 Congo.J Infect Dis. 2017; 216: 824-828Crossref PubMed Scopus (135) Google Scholar also mentioned that they observed a vertical transmission situation. The fetal death following a monkeypox infection reported by Mbala et al2Mbala P.K. Huggins J.W. Riu-Rovira T. et al.Maternal and fetal outcomes among pregnant women with human monkeypox infection in the Democratic Republic of 74 Congo.J Infect Dis. 2017; 216: 824-828Crossref PubMed Scopus (135) Google Scholar is not a unique incident according to Kisalu and Mokili’s comments on this case.4Kisalu N.K. Mokili J.L. Toward understanding the outcomes of monkeypox infection in human pregnancy.J Infect Dis. 2017; 216: 795-797Crossref PubMed Scopus (21) Google Scholar Orthopoxvirus, according to Kisalu and Mokili,4Kisalu N.K. Mokili J.L. Toward understanding the outcomes of monkeypox infection in human pregnancy.J Infect Dis. 2017; 216: 795-797Crossref PubMed Scopus (21) Google Scholar causes more complications, including higher rates of preterm birth, stillbirth, and spontaneous abortion in pregnant women than in nonpregnant ones. This is because it increases the risk for morbidity and mortality in pregnant patients when compared with nonpregnant ones.4Kisalu N.K. Mokili J.L. Toward understanding the outcomes of monkeypox infection in human pregnancy.J Infect Dis. 2017; 216: 795-797Crossref PubMed Scopus (21) Google Scholar The indexed case reported by Mbala et al2Mbala P.K. Huggins J.W. Riu-Rovira T. et al.Maternal and fetal outcomes among pregnant women with human monkeypox infection in the Democratic Republic of 74 Congo.J Infect Dis. 2017; 216: 824-828Crossref PubMed Scopus (135) Google Scholar also had a concurrent malaria infection.4Kisalu N.K. Mokili J.L. Toward understanding the outcomes of monkeypox infection in human pregnancy.J Infect Dis. 2017; 216: 795-797Crossref PubMed Scopus (21) Google Scholar Monkeypox and pregnancy: correspondenceAmerican Journal of Obstetrics & GynecologyVol. 228Issue 3PreviewWe read with great interest the correspondence of Mungmunpuntipantip and Wiwanitkit1 written in response to the article on monkeypox and pregnancy by Dashraath et al2 published in a recent issue of this journal. In their correspondence, Mungmunpuntipantip and Wiwanitkit state that there is no evidence for vertical transmission of the monkeypox virus (MPXV). However, these authors overlooked the article by Mbala et al3 describing pregnancy outcomes following maternal monkeypox infections in the Democratic Republic of Congo that included 2 miscarriages and a fetal death. Full-Text PDF","R. Mungmunpuntipantip, V. Wiwanitkit",10.1016/j.ajog.2022.10.035,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajog.2022.10.035,American Journal of Obstetrics and Gynecology,0,2022,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women and their outcomes rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.,no,"The study discusses outcomes related to the infection itself, such as fetal death, but does not report on vaccine-related safety or effectiveness.",no,"The text is a correspondence reply, not a primary research study or evidence synthesis.",yes,The study discusses human cases of monkeypox infection during pregnancy.,yes,The paper's population of interest is pregnant women.,no,There is no mention of participants receiving a vaccine at any point in the provided text.,no,The study discusses the monkeypox virus and vertical transmission but does not involve vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This paper is a correspondence reply discussing monkeypox infection outcomes and vertical transmission. It does not address vaccination or report vaccine-related outcomes, failing all intervention-related inclusion criteria.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""We appreciate the correspondence by Schwartz et al on our publication on monkeypox and pregnancy.1Mungmunpuntipantip""",,,,"  - ""We appreciate the correspondence by Schwartz et al on our publication on monkeypox and pregnancy.1Mungmunpuntipantip""",,,,"  - ""We appreciate the correspondence by Schwartz et al on our publication on monkeypox and pregnancy.1Mungmunpuntipantip""",,,,,
Maternal Vaccination Safety in Infants,"Source: Sukumaran L, McCarthy NL, Kharbanda EO, et al. Infant hospitalizations and mortality after maternal vaccination. Pediatrics. 2018; 141(3): e20173310; doi: 10.1542/peds.2017-3310Investigators from multiple institutions conducted a case-control study to determine whether maternal receipt of influenza and/or Tdap vaccines increases the risk of infant hospitalization or death within the first 6 months of life. Pregnant women were eligible if they had at least one prenatal visit at 1 of 5 integrated health care systems that participate in the Vaccine Safety Datalink (VSD), and had a singleton, live-born infant born at ≥34 weeks’ gestation. Cases included infants who had any hospitalization or who died within the first 6 months of life, identified using state electronic medical records, administrative sources, and state death records. Controls were infants without death or hospitalizations in the first 6 months of life. Cases were matched 1:1 to controls by VSD site, birth month and year, and gestational age.The primary exposure was maternal vaccination as determined by medical record review. Exposure status was stratified by influenza and Tdap vaccine. Investigators used multivariable logistic regression to estimate the odds of maternal vaccination in matched cases and controls, after controlling for potential confounders.Among 413,034 eligible infants, 25,222 had at least one hospitalization and 157 died within the first 6 months. The most common causes of death were unknown (32%) and SIDS (21%). There was no significant association between infant hospitalization and maternal influenza (adjusted odds ratio [aOR]: 1.00; 95% CI: 0.96, 1.04) or Tdap (aOR: 0.94; 95% CI: 0.88, 1.01) vaccines. There was also no significant association between infant death and maternal influenza (aOR: 0.96; 95% CI: 0.54, 1.69) or Tdap (aOR: 0.44; 95% CI: 0.17, 1.13) vaccines.The authors conclude that there is no association between maternal vaccination and infant hospitalization or death within the first 6 months of life.Dr Dubik has disclosed no financial relationship relevant to this commentary. This commentary does not contain a discussion of an unapproved/investigative use of a commercial product/device.It might seem to be common sense that the more robust a mother’s immune response to an infectious pathogen is, the better it is for her baby. After all, infants are much more susceptible to influenza and pertussis but can receive passive immunity from the mother. However, is augmenting the mother’s immunity through immunization safe for the unborn child and infant?Pursuing the answer to this question, the current researchers looked for a possible temporal association between maternal immunization with Tdap and inactivated influenza vaccines, and hospitalization rates and mortality among infants. In a case-controlled study of >400,000 children, they found no association in the first 6 months of life. These findings are in accord with those of other studies that show no serious adverse events among infants whose mothers had been vaccinated with Tdap and inactivated influenza vaccines while pregnant.1–6Combined with previous studies, the results of the current study provide strong evidence that maternal Tdap and influenza vaccines are safe. They firmly support the Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices guidelines that recommend administration of influenza and Tdap vaccines during each pregnancy.7Prevention of influenza and pertussis in newborns through maternal immunization is safe and effective for mothers and infants. (See related article AAP Grand Rounds, 2017;38[2]:15.8)The results of the current study should diminish the concerns of health care providers and patients who remain hesitant to vaccinate during pregnancy.",,10.1542/gr.40-1-8,https://doi.org/10.1542/gr.40-1-8,AAP Grand Rounds,0,2018,yes,"The study focus is on pregnant women and their infants, which are not healthy non-pregnant adults."