1278: SMALLPOX VACCINATION AND MULTIPLE ORGAN DYSFUNCTION IN THE ICU,"Introduction: The terrorist attacks of September 11th, 2001 and ensuing anthrax attacks led to the inoculation of more than a million members of the military and thousands of their civilian counterparts against the eradicated variola virus for fear that terrorist organizations may have obtained weaponized smallpox virus. Nevertheless, incoulation against smallpox is not without risk and has been associated with a myriad of well-documented adverse reactions. Case: A previously healthy 18 year old soldier was admitted to the hospital for presumed heat stroke after collapsing during a 5-mile run. He was initially alert and oriented, febrile and tachycardic. Laboratory data failed to reveal any abnormalities on a toxicology screen, but was notable for an elevated serum creatine phosphokinase (CPK) and lactate, 204 U/L and 9.2 mmol/L, respectively. A review of his medical record revealed vaccination for smallpox 8 days prior to presentation. Supportive therapy failed to prevent the patient from developing disseminated intravascular coagulopathy with rapidly upward trending liver enzymes. CPK level and urinalysis demonstrated myoglobunirua. He progressed to multiple organ dysfunction syndrome with hepatic encephalopathy and acute kidney injury requiring dialysis. On hospital day five he became hypotensive and developed a fatal arrhythmia. Autopsy revealed a dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM) with histologic evidence of myocarditis, a necrotic liver, an infarcted right adrenal gland and an edematous brain with patchy areas of acute neuronal necrosis directly attributed to immunologic consequences of the smallpox vaccine. Discussion: To our knowledge, this is the first case of death from the smallpox vaccine in a young, healthy, active duty soldier. Myocarditis is well-reported; however, in this soldier myocarditis silently progressed to DCM as a result of a systemic immunologic response to the vaccine. The same immunologic response coupled with exertion which occurred post-vaccine day 8, the peak of expected response to vaccine, likely led to his multi-organ system failure and death. Continuous vigilance for adverse reactions to smallpox vaccine is warranted.","Patrick Smith, Anita A. Shah, C. Mount, C. Schofield",10.1097/01.CCM.0000425489.28614.56,https://doi.org/10.1097/01.CCM.0000425489.28614.56,Critical Care Medicine,0,2012,maybe,"The study describes an 18-year-old soldier, which is a healthy non-pregnant adult. However, since the criterion asks if it is NOT exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults and the paper focuses on a single 18-year-old adult case who was healthy prior to vaccination, it likely fails this exclusion criteria, but I will check later criteria first.",yes,The study reports severe adverse events and death following vaccination.,yes,The study is a case report.,yes,The study is a case report of a human patient.,no,"The participant is described as an 18-year-old soldier. The target population requires participants to be <18 years, pregnant, or neonates. 18 years is categorized as an adult in this protocol.",no,"The patient received the vaccine at age 18, which is adulthood according to the criteria defined in this protocol.",yes,The study involves the smallpox vaccine.,Exclude,"This is a case report of an 18-year-old soldier who died following smallpox vaccination. It fails the target population and vaccination timing criteria because the participant was an 18-year-old adult, not a child, adolescent (<18), or pregnant woman.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""A previously healthy 18 year old soldier""
