Vaccine Effectiveness of JYNNEOS against Mpox Disease in the United States.,"BACKGROUND
In the United States, more than 30,000 cases of mpox (formerly known as monkeypox) had occurred as of March 1, 2023, in an outbreak disproportionately affecting transgender persons and gay, bisexual, and other men who have sex with men. In 2019, the JYNNEOS vaccine was approved for subcutaneous administration (0.5 ml per dose) to prevent mpox infection. On August 9, 2022, an emergency use authorization was issued for intradermal administration (0.1 ml per dose); however, real-world effectiveness data are limited for either route.
METHODS
We conducted a case-control study based on data from Cosmos, a nationwide Epic electronic health record (EHR) database, to assess the effectiveness of JYNNEOS vaccination in preventing medically attended mpox disease among adults. Case patients had an mpox diagnosis code or positive orthopoxvirus or mpox virus laboratory result, and control patients had an incident diagnosis of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection or a new or refill order for preexposure prophylaxis against HIV infection between August 15, 2022, and November 19, 2022. Odds ratios and 95% confidence intervals were estimated from conditional logistic-regression models, adjusted for confounders; vaccine effectiveness was calculated as (1 - odds ratio for vaccination in case patients vs. controls) × 100.
RESULTS
Among 2193 case patients and 8319 control patients, 25 case patients and 335 control patients received two doses (full vaccination), among whom the estimated adjusted vaccine effectiveness was 66.0% (95% confidence interval [CI], 47.4 to 78.1), and 146 case patients and 1000 control patients received one dose (partial vaccination), among whom the estimated adjusted vaccine effectiveness was 35.8% (95% CI, 22.1 to 47.1).
CONCLUSIONS
In this study using nationwide EHR data, patients with mpox were less likely to have received one or two doses of JYNNEOS vaccine than control patients. The findings suggest that JYNNEOS vaccine was effective in preventing mpox disease, and a two-dose series appeared to provide better protection. (Funded by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention and Epic Research.).","Nicholas P. Deputy, Joseph Deckert, A. Chard, Neil C. Sandberg, Danielle L. Moulia, Eric Barkley, Alexandra F Dalton, Cory Sweet, A. Cohn, D. Little, A. Cohen, Danessa Sandmann, D. Payne, Jacqueline L. Gerhart, Leora R. Feldstein",10.1056/NEJMoa2215201,https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2215201,New England Journal of Medicine,212,2023,yes,"The study includes participants with HIV infection, meaning it is not exclusively limited to healthy individuals, though it focuses on adults.",yes,The study reports on the effectiveness of the vaccine in preventing mpox disease.,yes,The study is a primary case-control research study.,yes,The study is a human study using electronic health record data.,no,"The study explicitly limits its population to adults, thereby excluding children and adolescents. It does not mention the inclusion of pregnant women.",no,"Participants were adults receiving the vaccine during the 2022 outbreak, which does not occur during childhood, adolescence, or pregnancy as required by the criteria.",yes,"The study evaluates the JYNNEOS vaccine, which is a vaccinia-based vaccine approved for mpox prevention.",Exclude,"This case-control study evaluates the effectiveness of the JYNNEOS vaccine in preventing mpox disease. However, it is excluded because it focuses exclusively on adults and does not include children, adolescents, or pregnant women, nor does it evaluate vaccination during those periods.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""control patients had an incident diagnosis of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection""
  - ""assess the effectiveness of JYNNEOS vaccination in preventing medically attended mpox disease among adults.""",,"  - ""estimated adjusted vaccine effectiveness was 66.0% (95% confidence interval [CI], 47.4 to 78.1),""",,"  - ""conducted a case-control study based on data from Cosmos,""",,"  - ""conducted a case-control study based on data from Cosmos,""",,"  - ""assess the effectiveness of JYNNEOS vaccination in preventing medically attended mpox disease among adults.""",,"  - ""In 2019, the JYNNEOS vaccine was approved for subcutaneous administration (0.5 ml per dose) to prevent mpox infection.""
