Systematic review and meta-analysis of neonatal outcomes of COVID-19 vaccination in pregnancy,"Background The safety of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) vaccines during pregnancy is a particular concern. Here, we addressed the neonatal outcomes after maternal vaccination of COVID-19 during pregnancy. Methods We systematically searched PubMed, EMBASE, and the WHO COVID-19 Database for studies on neonatal outcomes after maternal COVID-19 vaccination from inception to 3 July 2022. Main neonatal outcomes were related to preterm, small for gestation (SGA), NICU admission, low Apgar score at 5 min (<7), and additional neonatal outcomes such as gestation <34 weeks, low birth weight and some neonatal morbidity were all also analyzed. Results A total of 15 studies were included. We found that maternal vaccination during pregnancy was related to the reduction rates of Preterm, SGA, Low Apgar score at 5 min (<7). In addition, there was no evidence of a higher risk of adverse neonatal outcomes after maternal vaccination of COVID-19 during pregnancy, including NICU admission, preterm birth with gestation <34 weeks, low birth weight, very low birth weight, congenital anomalies, and so on. Conclusions COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant women does not raise significant adverse effects on neonatal outcomes and is related to a protective effect on some neonatal outcomes. Impact Present study has addressed the neonatal outcomes after maternal vaccination of COVID-19 during pregnancy. COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant women does not raise significant adverse effects on neonatal outcomes and is related to a protective effect on some neonatal outcomes. The present study could encourage pregnant women to be vaccinated against COVID-19.","Dingning Zhang, Tingting Huang, Zhihui Chen, Lulu Zhang, Qi Gao, Ge Liu, Jun Zheng, Fangrui Ding",10.1038/s41390-022-02421-0,https://doi.org/10.1038/s41390-022-02421-0,Pediatric Research,25,2023,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women and their neonates.,yes,"The paper reports safety and effectiveness outcomes such as preterm birth, NICU admission, and overall adverse effects.",yes,The study is a systematic review and meta-analysis.,yes,The study evaluates human subjects (pregnant women and neonates).,yes,The study population includes pregnant women and neonates.,yes,Participants received the vaccination during pregnancy.,no,"The study evaluates COVID-19 vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This systematic review and meta-analysis focuses on the safety and outcomes of COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy for neonates. While it targets a relevant population (pregnant women and neonates), it does not investigate vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines, which is a required criterion.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""maternal vaccination of COVID-19 during pregnancy.""
