"Epidemiology, clinical features, and outcomes of Mpox in children and adolescents in Uganda, August 2024–January 2025","Highlights • Children younger than 5 years had the highest Mpox attack rate in Uganda (3.7/100,000).• Of pediatric cases, 91% were classified as severe, with delayed care-seeking (>7 days after symptom onset) significantly increasing severity.• Households and schools were the primary settings for Mpox transmission among children and adolescents.• Mpox case fatality in children was 1.9%, higher than in the general population.","Charity Mutesi, R. Migisha, Emmanuel Okello, Emmanuel Mfitundinda, J. K. Lubega, Patrick Kwizera, Joanita Nalwanga, Hannington Katumba, Joyce Owens Kobusingye, Gertrude Abbo, Daniel Wenani, Bridget Ainembabazi, Olive Namakula Loryndah, A. Namusisi, Lilian Bulage, Ivan Lukabwe, Dansan Atim, A. Ario",10.1016/j.ijregi.2026.100838,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijregi.2026.100838,IJID Regions,0,2026,yes,"The study focuses specifically on children and adolescents, rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"While the study reports outcomes like severity and fatality, these are clinical outcomes of the disease itself, not safety or effectiveness outcomes related to a vaccine (e.g., vaccine-induced adverse events or vaccine effectiveness).",yes,This is an epidemiological study providing primary research data on mpox cases in Uganda.,yes,The study involves human participants (children and adolescents).,yes,The study specifically targets children and adolescents.,no,"There is no information suggesting the participants received a vaccine during childhood or any other period; the study describes infection outcomes, not vaccination.",no,The study does not involve or investigate any vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This study focus on the epidemiology and clinical outcomes of Mpox in children and adolescents. It provides data on disease severity and case fatality, but it is excluded because it does not involve the use or evaluation of any mpox or smallpox vaccines.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Children younger than 5 years had the highest Mpox attack rate in Uganda (3.7/100,000).•""
