A new attenuated and highly immunogenic orthopoxvirus vaccine protects against mpox in mice and macaques,"Shortly after 2022, mpox again becomes a public health emergency of international concern as declared by the World Health Organization on August 14, 2024. Smallpox vaccines ACAM2000 and MVA-BN were approved for mpox prevention in several countries. However, the side effects of ACAM2000 and the limited supply of MVA call for the development of new mpox vaccines to prevent mpox and other orthopoxvirus infections. In this study, we engineered a new generation of attenuated and highly immunogenic vaccinia virus named dBTF based on the vaccinia Tiantan strain which is a replication-competent smallpox vaccine widely used in China. The dBTF vaccinia virus is impaired in replication and low in virulence, induces strong vaccinia virus-specific humoral and cellular immune responses. Importantly, single dose of dBTF vaccination effectively protects mice and cynomolgus macaques from mpox virus challenge. These results support the development of dBTF into a new generation of mpox and orthopoxvirus vaccines.","Fengwen Xu, Yu Huang, Yongzhi Hou, Yu Xie, Baoying Huang, Fei Zhao, Zhao Gao, Chen Chen, Jiaxun Wang, S. Mei, Yamei Hu, Liming Wang, Liang Wei, Jingjing Zhang, Na Li, Zhe Cong, Jianrong Ma, Lin Zhu, Ting Chen, Jiahan Lu, Qiang Wei, Wenjie Tan, Jing Xue, Fei Guo",10.1038/s41541-025-01193-y,https://doi.org/10.1038/s41541-025-01193-y,npj Vaccines,6,2025,no,"The study is conducted in mice and macaques, not humans, therefore it does not meet the population criteria.",yes,The study reports effectiveness outcomes (protection against mpox challenge) in animal models.,yes,The study is primary laboratory research involving animal models.,no,"The study is an animal study using mice and macaque models, not a human study.",no,"The participants are mice and macaques rather than pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"The vaccination timing refers to animal models, not the human stages required (pregnancy, childhood, etc.).",yes,The study involves a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine derived from the Tiantan strain.,Exclude,This is an animal study evaluating the effectiveness of a new vaccinia-based vaccine in mice and macaques. It does not involve human participants and therefore fails multiple required criteria including study type and target population.,"Population Focus, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""single dose of dBTF vaccination effectively protects mice and cynomolgus macaques from mpox virus challenge.""",,"  - ""single dose of dBTF vaccination effectively protects mice and cynomolgus macaques from mpox virus challenge.""",,"  - ""single dose of dBTF vaccination effectively protects mice and cynomolgus macaques from mpox virus challenge.""",,"  - ""single dose of dBTF vaccination effectively protects mice and cynomolgus macaques from mpox virus challenge.""",,"  - ""single dose of dBTF vaccination effectively protects mice and cynomolgus macaques from mpox virus challenge.""",,"  - ""single dose of dBTF vaccination effectively protects mice and cynomolgus macaques from mpox virus challenge.""",,"  - ""In this study, we engineered a new generation of attenuated and highly immunogenic vaccinia virus named dBTF based on the vaccinia Tiantan strain""",
"Mpox: emergence following smallpox eradication, ongoing outbreaks and strategies for prevention","Purpose of review This review focuses on the temporal relationship between the discontinuation of the global smallpox eradication effort with the rise of mpox in Africa and worldwide. It also discusses the global 2022 clade II mpox epidemic and the current 2024 clade I mpox outbreak. Newer findings on viral evolution and pathogenesis, plus current and future strategies for disease prevention, are reviewed. Recent findings The temporal association between the incidence of mpox and the World Health Organization's Global Smallpox Eradication Program (GSEP) is presented. The 2022 global mpox epidemic is discussed. Recent data show that clade IIb monkeypox virus (MPXV)-2022 has novel genetic features supporting a greater propensity for mutations that may be responsible for enhanced human-to-human transmissibility, increased disease severity and accelerated viral evolution. In 2023, another outbreak of mpox began in Africa, this time due to the potentially more virulent MPXV clade Ib strains. This outbreak remains ongoing in Africa, and clade Ib mpox cases have recently been reported elsewhere including the United States and Great Britain. The World Health Organization has deemed mpox to be a global public health emergency. Two smallpox vaccines are approved for mpox prevention in the United States; a third smallpox vaccine and an improved diagnostic test have recently received WHO Emergency Use authorization. Newer mRNA-based vaccines for evolving orthopoxvirus infections are discussed. Summary Vaccination to prevent smallpox provides immunologic cross-protection against infection with other members of genus Orthopoxvirus, including mpox. Discontinuation of the global smallpox eradication program in the 1980s and the subsequent waning of herd immunity contributed to the 2022 multinational epidemic of human clade IIb mpox infections. A second multinational outbreak with clade Ib MPXV is ongoing. Vaccination against smallpox remains the gold standard for mpox prevention, however newer multiepitope mRNA-based vaccines are in development and hold promise for prevention of mpox and other orthopoxvirus outbreaks.","A. Bryant, Stanford T. Shulman",10.1097/QCO.0000000000001100,https://doi.org/10.1097/QCO.0000000000001100,Current Opinion in Infectious Diseases,5,2025,yes,"The study is a broad review of global outbreaks and prevention strategies across Africa and worldwide, which encompasses various populations and is not exclusively limited to healthy adults.",yes,The paper reviews strategies for disease prevention and mentions the use of vaccines for mpox prevention.,no,"The paper identifies itself as a review and appears to be a narrative Current Opinion piece rather than a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,The paper discusses human outbreaks (clade I and II) and public health emergencies.,no,"The provided abstract does not mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as specific participants or the focus of reviewed data.",no,"The text does not specify vaccination during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence for the target groups.",yes,The paper specifically discusses smallpox vaccines approved for mpox prevention.,Exclude,"This paper is a narrative review discussing global mpox outbreaks and general prevention strategies, rather than primary research or a systematic review. It further fails the inclusion criteria as it does not explicitly discuss vaccination outcomes in the required target populations such as pregnant women or children.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""This review focuses on the temporal relationship between the discontinuation of the global smallpox eradication effort with the rise of mpox in Africa and worldwide.""",,"  - ""Newer findings on viral evolution and pathogenesis, plus current and future strategies for disease prevention, are reviewed.""
