"From Pandemic to Epidemic: Lessons Learned From COVID-19 Applied to Mpox Outbreak Response, Westchester County, Metropolitan New York.","The COVID-19 pandemic vaccination infrastructure was redeployed to address the Mpox epidemic. The Westchester County Department of Health coordinated an effective vaccine distribution, tracking, and data collection process with community partners with real-time feedback of operational challenges and updated public health directives. Westchester County, which comprises 9% of the New York State population, administered 24% (6770 doses) of JYNNEOS (smallpox and monkeypox vaccine) across the state. Among first-dose recipients, 13% were Black and 25% were Hispanic, approaching countywide US Census race and ethnicity breakdowns. The operational template designed during COVID-19 can be readily redeployed for subsequent epidemics of even seemingly dissimilar infections like Mpox.","Marina Keller, Melissa Hellman, Dial Hewlett, Vishnu Chaturvedi, Donald S Chen, J. Watson, A. Huang, R. Recchia, S. Amler, Renee Garrick",10.1097/PHH.0000000000001814,https://doi.org/10.1097/PHH.0000000000001814,Journal of Public Health Management and Practice,2,2024,maybe,"The study focuses on the general population recipients of the JYNNEOS vaccine in Westchester County, broken down by race and ethnicity, but does not specify if children or pregnant women were included.",no,"The study reports operational data on vaccine distribution and doses administered, but does not report clinical safety outcomes (adverse events) or effectiveness outcomes (mpox prevention, hospitalization, or death).",yes,The study is a descriptive report or case series of a public health operational response.,yes,This is a study of a human public health intervention in a New York county.,no,"The paper does not mention the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it focuses on general distribution metrics and racial/ethnic demographics.",no,"There is no documentation of vaccination occurring during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood/adolescence; participants are identified generally as 'first-dose recipients'.",yes,"The study utilizes JYNNEOS, which is specifically identified as a smallpox and monkeypox vaccine.",Exclude,This study reports on the public health operational response to the Mpox outbreak and vaccine distribution in Westchester County. It fails several required criteria as it does not report clinical safety or effectiveness outcomes and does not specify the inclusion of pregnant women or pediatric populations.,"Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Westchester County, which comprises 9% of the New York State population, administered 24% (6770 doses) of JYNNEOS (smallpox and monkeypox vaccine) across the state.""
