Reactogenicity and Immunogenicity Against MPXV of the Intradermal Administration of Modified Vaccinia Ankara Compared to the Standard Subcutaneous Route,"The recent resurgence of Mpox in central Africa has been declared again a Public Health Emergency of International Concern (PHEIC) requiring coordinated international responses. Vaccination is a priority to expand protection and enhance control strategies, but the vaccine's need exceeds the currently available doses. Intradermal administration of one-fifth of the standard Modified-Vaccinia-Ankara (MVA-BN) dose was temporarily authorized during the 2022 PHEIC. Studies conducted before 2022 provided evidence about the humoral response against the Vaccinia virus (VACV) after vaccination but not against the Mpox virus (MPXV). Moreover, no data are available on the T-cell response elicited by MVA-BN administered subcutaneously or intradermally. Here, we compare the two vaccine administration routes according to reactogenicity and immunogenicity based on data from 943 vaccine recipients during the 2022 vaccination campaign in Rome, Italy. We found that the intradermal route elicited slightly higher titers of MPXV-specific IgG and nAbs than the subcutaneous one. At the same time, no differences in cellular response were detected. MVA-BN was globally well tolerated despite higher reactogenicity for the intradermal than the subcutaneous route, especially for the reactions at the local injection site. The intradermal dose-sparing strategy was proven safe and immunogenic and would make vaccination available to more people.","V. Mazzotta, P. Piselli, Alessandro Cozzi Lepri, G. Matusali, E. Cimini, Rozenn Esvan, Francesca Colavita, R. Gagliardini, S. Notari, Alessandra Oliva, S. Meschi, R. Casetti, Giulia Micheli, L. Bordi, Alessandro Giacinta, G. Grassi, Saba Gebremeskel Teklè, C. Cimaglia, J. Paulicelli, Alessandro Caioli, Paola Gallì, Giulia Del Duca, M. Lichtner, L. Sarmati, E. Tamburrini, C. Mastroianni, A. Latini, Paolo Faccendini, Carla Fontana, E. Nicastri, A. Siddu, Alessandra Barca, F. Vaia, E. Girardi, F. Maggi, A. Antinori",10.3390/vaccines13010032,https://doi.org/10.3390/vaccines13010032,medRxiv,4,2024,maybe,"The abstract does not explicitly state that the 943 participants were exclusively healthy non-pregnant adults. However, there is no mention of pregnant women or children.",yes,"The study reports on reactogenicity, safety, and immunogenicity of the vaccine.",yes,This is a primary research study comparing two administration routes during a vaccination campaign.,yes,"The study involves 943 vaccine recipients, which are human subjects.",no,"The study description does not mention the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents. It refers to ""vaccine recipients"" generally, and typical vaccination campaigns during the 2022 mpox outbreak targeted adult high-risk populations.",no,"Because the study does not clearly include the target populations (children, pregnant women), it's not confirmed they were vaccinated during these specific periods.",yes,The study evaluates the Modified-Vaccinia-Ankara (MVA-BN) vaccine.,Exclude,"This study evaluates the MVA-BN vaccine in 943 recipients, but it focuses on the general adult population from the 2022 campaign and does not mention pregnant women, children, or adolescents. Therefore, it fails the mandatory target population criteria despite reporting relevant safety and immunogenicity outcomes.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,,,"  - ""MVA-BN was globally well tolerated despite higher reactogenicity""
  - ""intradermal dose-sparing strategy was proven safe and immunogenic""",,,,,,,,,,"  - ""Modified-Vaccinia-Ankara (MVA-BN)""",
Monkeypox virus: past and present,"Background The objective of this paper is to analyze the current status of monkeypox worldwide. In the face of this public health threat, our purpose is to elucidate the clinical characteristics and epidemiology of monkeypox, the developmental progress of monkeypox-related drugs and the vaccines available. Data sources The literature review was performed in databases including PubMed, Science Direct and Google Scholar up to July 2022. Results Since May 2022, the World Health Organization has reported more than 45,000 confirmed cases from 92 nonendemic countries, including nine deaths. Although some women and children have been infected so far, most cases have occurred among men who have sex with other men, especially those with multiple sexual partners or anonymous sex. Conclusions Pediatric monkeypox infection has been associated with a higher likelihood of severe illness and mortality than in adults. Severe monkeypox illness in pediatrics often requires adjunctive antiviral therapy. It is crucial for all countries to establish sound monitoring and testing systems and be prepared with emergency preparedness.","Yansu Dou, H. Yuan, H. Tian",10.1007/s12519-022-00618-1,https://doi.org/10.1007/s12519-022-00618-1,World Journal of Pediatrics,31,2022,yes,"The study explicitly focuses on populations including children and women, and is not limited to healthy adults.",no,"While the paper mentions vaccines generally in its objectives, it does not report specific safety or effectiveness outcomes for those vaccines.",no,"The paper describes itself as a ""literature review"", which is a narrative overview rather than a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,The study discusses human epidemiology and clinical characteristics.,yes,The study includes discussion of children and women as part of the infected population.,no,The paper does not report on a study group that received vaccinations during the specified periods; it is a general review of status.,yes,"The paper mentions the status of ""vaccines available"" for monkeypox generally.",Exclude,"This paper is a narrative literature review describing the global status and epidemiology of monkeypox, which mentions the presence of pediatric cases. It fails the inclusion criteria because it is not a primary research study or systematic review and does not report specific effectiveness or safety outcomes for vaccines.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Vaccination Timing",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""some women and children have been infected so far,""
