SMS-Based Active Surveillance of Adverse Events following Immunization in Children: The VigiVax Study,"Underreporting is the main limitation of spontaneous reporting systems. This cohort-event monitoring study aims to examine the potential of short message service (SMS)-based surveillance compared to traditional surveillance systems. Using VigiVax software, parents of vaccinated children aged two years or younger, in the period March 2021–May 2022, received a single SMS inquiry about adverse events following immunization (AEFI). Responses were collected, validated by health operators and integrated with the information on electronic immunization registries. AEFI reports were automatically submitted to the Italian Pharmacovigilance system. Among 254,160 SMS messages sent, corresponding to 451,656 administered doses (AD), 71,643 responses were collected (28.2% response rate), and 21,231 of them (8.3%) reported AEFI. After a seriousness assessment based on clinical criteria, 50 reports (0.24%) were classified as serious. Among these, a causality assessment identified 31 reports at least potentially related to the vaccination (RR: 6.86/100,000 AD). Febrile seizures following MMRV (measles, mumps, rubella, varicella) vaccination accounted for 11 of these 31 cases, with an incidence of 32 per 100,000 AD. No fatal outcomes were reported. Our findings support the highly favorable risk profile of pediatric vaccinations and the possibility to improve spontaneous reporting through the integration of digital technologies.","L. Gonella, Francesca Moretti, Annalisa Capuano, Caterina De Sarro, L. Ferrara, Elisabetta Geninatti, Greta Guarnieri, Xhikjana Hysolakoj, Margherita Lalli, Olivia Leoni, A. Mangano, Patrizia Marani Toro, Viviana Mecchia, M. Merlano, Caterina Palleria, Anna Maria Potenza, Paola Rossi, M. Rossi, Francesca Sanità, Ester Sapigni, C. Scavone, Claudia Sommaro, M. Tuccori, Giovanna Zanoni, U. Moretti",10.3390/vaccines12091076,https://doi.org/10.3390/vaccines12091076,Vaccines,1,2024,yes,"The study focuses on children aged two years or younger, which is not a population of healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on adverse events following immunization (AEFI), including serious reports and febrile seizures.",yes,The paper explicitly states it is a cohort-event monitoring study.,yes,The study involves human participants (vaccinated children).,yes,The participants are children aged two years or younger.,yes,"The vaccinations occurred during childhood, specifically in children aged two years or younger.",no,"The study evaluates routine pediatric vaccinations, such as MMRV (measles, mumps, rubella, varicella), rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,This study examines adverse events in children aged two years or younger following routine pediatric vaccinations like MMRV. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not investigate vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.,Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""parents of vaccinated children aged two years or younger,""",,"  - ""received a single SMS inquiry about adverse events following immunization (AEFI).""
  - ""Febrile seizures following MMRV (measles, mumps, rubella, varicella) vaccination""",,"  - ""This cohort-event monitoring study""",,"  - ""parents of vaccinated children aged two years or younger,""",,"  - ""parents of vaccinated children aged two years or younger,""",,"  - ""parents of vaccinated children aged two years or younger,""",,"  - ""Febrile seizures following MMRV (measles, mumps, rubella, varicella) vaccination""
