Effectiveness of COVID-19 mRNA primary and booster vaccination against infection and hospitalisation during pregnancy: a target trial emulation and meta-analysis of data from 4 European countries,"Objective To estimate vaccine effectiveness (VE) of COVID-19 mRNA vaccines administered during pregnancy. Primary (2-dose) vaccination was compared with unvaccinated women; and 1st booster (3rd dose) with primary (2-dose) vaccination. Design Target trial emulation cohort study. Setting Routinely collected health data from UK, Sweden, Spain, and Norway. All had complete vaccine exposure data and were mapped to the OMOP common data model. Participants Pregnant women (aged 12-55) with <=34 weeks of gestation, no history of COVID-19 in the 90 days prior and >=1 year of data visibility. Women were matched 1:1 to those with no dose and 2-dose vaccination, respectively, using weekly sequential matching from vaccination roll-out to 2023 (Swedish data: 2024). Main outcome measures The main outcomes were COVID-19 infection, hospitalisation and Intensive Care Unit (ICU) admission (COVID-related, excluding delivery-related care). Hazard ratios (HR) were estimated with Cox regression from 15 days post-vaccination, and VE was reported as 100 x (1-HR). Analyses were stratified by pregnancy trimester and vaccine brand. Database-specific results were pooled using a random-effects meta-analysis. Results We included 62,061 and 41,096 pregnant women at date of vaccination with a first and third dose, respectively. Median follow-up across databases ranged from 42 to 55 days for primary vaccination and 73 to 116 days for boosters. For primary vaccination, the meta-analysis showed a VE of 26.2% (16.2 to 35.1; I2 =0.7) against infection and 55.6% (44.1 to 64.8; I2 =0) against hospitalisation. For boosters, corresponding figures were 33.6% (-1.6 to 56.6; I2=1) and 34.1% (0.6 to 56.4; I2 =0.5). Event counts were insufficient to analyse VE against ICU admission. Results were consistent across pregnancy trimesters and vaccine brands. Conclusions Vaccination with a primary (2-dose) or a 1st booster (3rd dose) during pregnancy was effective in preventing COVID-19 hospitalisation, with modest and heterogeneous VE against infection across 4 European countries.","N. Mercadé-Besora, B. Raventós, N. T. Trinh, H. Li, S. Hayati, A. Giuliodori, T. Burkard, E. Burn, M. Catala, D. Prieto-Alhambra, D. R. Morales, F. Nyberg, H. Nordeng, T. Duarte-Salles",10.1101/2025.05.16.25327593,https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.05.16.25327593,medRxiv,1,2025,yes,The study focus is exclusively on pregnant women and includes individuals as young as 12.,no,"The study reports on COVID-19 outcomes (infection, hospitalization, ICU admission), not mpox infection or outcomes.",yes,This is a primary research study using a target trial emulation cohort design.,yes,This is a human study based on routinely collected health data.,yes,The study specifically includes pregnant women and adolescents aged 12-17.,yes,The vaccination was administered during pregnancy.,no,"The study investigates COVID-19 mRNA vaccines, rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,The paper focuses on COVID-19 mRNA vaccines and their effectiveness against COVID-19 infection and hospitalization in pregnant women. It does not meet the criteria for vaccine type (mpox/smallpox vaccines) or relevant outcomes related to mpox.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Participants Pregnant women (aged 12-55)""",,"  - ""The main outcomes were COVID-19 infection, hospitalisation and Intensive Care Unit (ICU) admission""",,"  - ""Target trial emulation cohort study.""",,"  - ""Target trial emulation cohort study.""",,"  - ""Participants Pregnant women (aged 12-55)""",,"  - ""estimate vaccine effectiveness (VE) of COVID-19 mRNA vaccines administered during pregnancy.""",,"  - ""estimate vaccine effectiveness (VE) of COVID-19 mRNA vaccines administered during pregnancy.""",
Neonatal smallpox vaccination--a sound proposition for smallpox endemic areas.,"The World Health Organization has recommended smallpox vaccination of newborns in areas carrying out smallpox eradication campaigns which in practice means simultaneous BCG and smallpox vaccination. Some health personnel have expressed skepticism about the safety of neonatal smallpox immunization. This article examines questions that have been raised about this strategy. It is noted that although infants are assumed to inherit passive immunity some cases of smallpox have been reported among infants under 4 months of age whose mothers had been immunized. There have been no cases of smallpox in a successfully vaccinated infant. If neonates are not vaccinated they could constitute a reservoir of smallpox in an area with an eradication program. There does not appear to be any evidence that vaccination produces any more complications when carried out before as opposed to after 6 months of age. It has been demonstrated moreover that simultaneous vaccination with BCG and smallpox vaccines can be done without compromising the effectiveness or safety of either vaccine. Such a policy seems particularly advisable in smallpox endemic areas. The question of introducing a higher potency vaccine for primary vaccination should be considered as well since inherited relative immunity makes ""takes"" more difficult to obtain. Further research is recommended in 3 areas: 1) the antibody response after vaccination of neonates of immune versus nonimmune mothers 2) the duration of immunity after neonatal smallpox vaccination and 3) postvaccination complications in neonates of nonimmune mothers.",M. A. Ukeje,,,The Journal of tropical medicine and hygiene,0,1972,yes,The study focuses specifically on newborns rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.,yes,"The article discusses safety and effectiveness outcomes, including the occurrence of smallpox and vaccine-related complications.",no,"The paper is a narrative commentary or perspective article that 'examines questions' and 'recommends research'. It does not represent primary research (e.g., RCT, cohort) or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,"The discussion pertains to human subjects, specifically infants and their mothers.",yes,The participants discussed are neonates and infants under 4 months of age.,yes,The study specifically discusses vaccination during the neonatal period.,yes,The study explicitly concerns smallpox vaccination.,Exclude,"Although the paper discusses smallpox vaccination in neonates and addresses relevant safety and effectiveness outcomes, it fails the study design criterion. It is a narrative commentary or editorial piece rather than a primary research study or systematic review.",Study Design,Study Design,0.1,"  - ""smallpox vaccination of newborns""",,"  - ""no cases of smallpox in a successfully vaccinated infant.""
