"Pregnancy, birth, and infant health outcomes from the National Smallpox Vaccine in Pregnancy Registry, 2003-2006.","When the United States implemented civilian and military smallpox vaccination programs in 2003, the National Smallpox Vaccine in Pregnancy Registry was established to better evaluate outcomes after the inadvertent vaccination of pregnant women. Women were referred to the registry by vaccine administrators, health care providers, or state health departments or through self-referral. Registry professionals actively follow up with all enrolled women and collect data on pregnancy, birth, and infant health outcomes. As of September 2006, pregnancy outcome data were available from 376 women. Most (77%) were vaccinated near the time of conception, before results of a standard pregnancy test would have been positive. To date, outcome evaluations have not revealed higher-than-expected rates of pregnancy loss (11.9%), preterm birth (10.7%), or birth defects (2.8%), compared with those in healthy referent populations. No cases of fetal vaccinia have been identified. The Smallpox Vaccine in Pregnancy Registry continues to actively enroll women and follow infant and early-childhood health outcomes.","M. Ryan, J. Seward",10.1086/524744,https://doi.org/10.1086/524744,Clinical Infectious Diseases,39,2008,yes,"The study specifically focuses on pregnant women enrolled in a registry, which means it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports safety outcomes including pregnancy loss, preterm birth, birth defects, and fetal vaccinia.",yes,The study is a registry-based cohort study where participants are actively followed up.,yes,The study involves human participants (pregnant women and their infants).,yes,The study population consists of pregnant women and their infants.,yes,Participants were vaccinated during pregnancy or near the time of conception.,yes,The study investigates the outcomes of smallpox vaccination.,Include,"This study monitors safety outcomes in pregnant women who were inadvertently vaccinated with the smallpox vaccine. It reports on specific adverse outcomes such as pregnancy loss and fetal vaccinia, directly meeting all inclusion criteria for the target population and vaccine type.",,,5,"  - ""National Smallpox Vaccine in Pregnancy Registry was established to better evaluate outcomes after the inadvertent vaccination of pregnant women.""",,"  - ""outcome evaluations have not revealed higher-than-expected rates of pregnancy loss (11.9%), preterm birth (10.7%), or birth defects (2.8%),""
