"Safety of MVA-BN in Healthcare Personnel, Democratic Republic of the Congo","Abstract Background Modified vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic (MVA-BN) is a third generation, replication- deficient, smallpox and mpox vaccine prepared in liquid and lyophilized formulations. The Democratic Republic of the Congo reports the highest number of cases of clade I mpox annually. Methods During the summer of 2017 and 2019, 1,600 healthcare personnel were enrolled in an investigational prospective cohort study to evaluate safety of the 2-dose MVA-BN vaccine series; 1000 were administered liquid and 600 were administered reconstituted lyophilized MVA-BN. Vaccine doses were given on study days 0 and 28 and presence or absence of adverse events were evaluated at routine follow-up appointments for up to two years. Results There were 49% of liquid and 54% of lyophilized formulation participants who experienced at least one adverse event within 7 days. Eighteen serious adverse events, including seventeen deaths and one stillbirth, occurred within the 2-year study period; safety monitors deemed none were causally associated with vaccine administration. Fourteen pregnancies occurred within one month of vaccination, 13 gave birth to healthy infants. Conclusions This study adds to the growing body of literature on the safety of MVA-BN in different populations. The data from this Congolese cohort demonstrate good safety outcomes for up to two years with both liquid and lyophilized vaccine formulations. As MVA-BN vaccination campaigns are launched in endemic and surrounding countries in late 2024 as part of clade I mpox outbreak response efforts, the data from this study supporting safety of MVA-BN in an African population are crucial to build vaccine confidence. Trial registry number: NCT02977715","F. S. Minhaj, A. Mandra, B. Nguete, S. Tran, J. Kennedy, B. Monroe, C. Hughes, T. Joseph, M. Person, M. B. Townsend, P. Satheshkumar, T. Likafi, G. Kokola, J. Kabamba, M. Reynolds, A. Rao, D. Kasongo, P. A. Yu, Y. Yu, R. Shongo, D. Kaba, B. Petersen, A. McCollum",10.1101/2025.06.11.25328898,https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.06.11.25328898,medRxiv,0,2025,yes,"The study includes data on pregnant women, as fourteen pregnancies occurred within one month of vaccination and their outcomes were recorded.",yes,"The study reports adverse events, serious adverse events including deaths, and pregnancy outcomes (stillbirth and healthy births).",yes,The study is a primary research prospective cohort study.,yes,"The study involves 1,600 healthcare personnel, which are human subjects.",yes,"The study followed 14 participants who were pregnant around the time of vaccination, reporting on their birth outcomes.",yes,"The participants were vaccinated twice (Day 0 and 28), and 14 pregnancies occurred within one month of vaccination, indicating overlap between the vaccination period and pregnancy.",yes,"The study investigates the MVA-BN vaccine, which is a modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine.",Include,"This prospective cohort study evaluates the safety of the MVA-BN vaccine in healthcare personnel. It specifically reports on safety outcomes for 14 pregnancies that occurred within a month of vaccination, including the occurrence of a stillbirth and healthy births.",,,4.9,"  - ""Fourteen pregnancies occurred within one month of vaccination,""
