The protective effect of COVID-19 vaccines on developing multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C): a systematic literature review and meta-analysis,"Objective To review whether the current COVID-19 vaccines can prevent the occurrence of multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C) and adolescents. Methods A systematic literature review and meta-analysis were performed. The data were abstracted following the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) guidelines. Primary outcome was the efficacy of COVID-19 vaccination in preventing MIS-C development. The search was performed in PubMed and Embase. Results The review yielded 13 studies, which were included for critical appraisal and data extraction. The available studies showed a reduced incidence of MIS-C after mRNA COVID-19 vaccination in children aged 12–18 years. Four studies were eligible for meta-analysis and the pooled odds ratio for MIS-C in vaccinated children compared to unvaccinated children was 0.04 (95% confidence interval: 0.03–0.06). Additionally, the risk of MIS-C as an adverse effect of vaccination was much lower compared to the risk of MIS-C post-infection. Conclusions Our systematic review highlights the current available evidence on the efficacy of COVID-19 vaccination in preventing MIS-C. The published studies so far – mainly conducted during the Delta wave – indicate that (original strain) COVID-19 mRNA vaccines in children are safe and associated with significantly less development of MIS-C. These findings further reinforce the recommendation for COVID-19 vaccination in children, which should be promoted and largely supported.","M. Hamad Saied, Laura van der Griend, Joeri W. van Straalen, N. Wulffraat, S. Vastert, M. Jansen",10.1186/s12969-023-00848-1,https://doi.org/10.1186/s12969-023-00848-1,Pediatric Rheumatology Online Journal,30,2023,yes,The study concentrates on children and adolescents rather than exclusively healthy non-pregnant adults.,no,"The study reports on the prevention of MIS-C and general COVID-19 vaccine safety rather than mpox infection, hospitalization, or death.",yes,The study is a systematic review and meta-analysis.,yes,The study involves human participants (children and adolescents).,yes,The study includes children and adolescents aged 12–18 years.,yes,Participants received the vaccines during childhood or adolescence.,no,The study evaluates mRNA COVID-19 vaccines rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,This systematic review evaluates the efficacy of mRNA COVID-19 vaccines in preventing MIS-C among children and adolescents. It is excluded because it focuses on COVID-19 vaccines rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines and does not address mpox-related outcomes.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""whether the current COVID-19 vaccines can prevent the occurrence of multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C) and adolescents.""
