COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy and adverse perinatal outcomes: a systematic review and meta-analysis.,"We aimed to estimate the associations between coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) vaccination during pregnancy and the risks of adverse perinatal outcomes. We performed a literature search in PubMed, Web of Science and Embase to identify eligible studies published up to 24 September 2023, yielding 39 included studies. Pooled relative risks (RRs) with 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were calculated with a random effects model. The pooled results showed that COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy (any type or dose of COVID-19 vaccination during any trimester) was not associated with an increased risk of adverse perinatal outcomes. In particular, COVID-19 vaccination in the third trimester was associated with a decreased risk of preterm birth (<37 weeks) (RR 0.85 [95% CI 0.74 to 0.98]), 5-min Apgar <7 (RR 0.87 [95% CI 0.78 to 0.97]) and neonatal intensive care unit (NICU) admission (RR 0.90 [95% CI 0.86 to 0.95]). The inverse associations were also found in analysis of one-dose vaccination during pregnancy and the risk of miscarriage (RR 0.83 [95% CI 0.72 to 0.96]) and preterm birth (<37 weeks) (RR 0.90 [95% CI 0.80 to 1.00]) and two-dose vaccination during pregnancy and the risk of NICU admission (RR 0.86 [95% CI 0.76 to 0.96]). COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy does not increase the risk of negative outcomes for the mother or baby.","Jia Wang, Yuzhi Deng, Weijing Wang",10.1093/trstmh/trad093,https://doi.org/10.1093/trstmh/trad093,Transactions of the Royal Society of Tropical Medicine and Hygiene,4,2024,yes,"The study focuses on pregnant women and neonatal outcomes, not healthy non-pregnant adults.",maybe,"The study reports safety outcomes including preterm birth, miscarriage, and NICU admission. However, these are in relation to COVID-19 vaccines, not mpox vaccines.",yes,The paper is a systematic review and meta-analysis.,yes,The study is a human systematic review.,yes,The study specifically includes pregnant women and neonates.,yes,Study participants received vaccination during pregnancy.,no,"The study exclusively investigates COVID-19 vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This is a systematic review and meta-analysis evaluating the safety of COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy. While it focuses on a relevant population (pregnant women), it is excluded because it does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""We aimed to estimate the associations between coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) vaccination during pregnancy and the risks of adverse perinatal outcomes.""",,"  - ""COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy (any type or dose of COVID-19 vaccination during any trimester) was not associated with an increased risk of adverse perinatal outcomes.""",,,,"  - ""COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy (any type or dose of COVID-19 vaccination during any trimester) was not associated with an increased risk of adverse perinatal outcomes.""",,"  - ""COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy (any type or dose of COVID-19 vaccination during any trimester) was not associated with an increased risk of adverse perinatal outcomes.""",,"  - ""We aimed to estimate the associations between coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) vaccination during pregnancy and the risks of adverse perinatal outcomes.""",,"  - ""We aimed to estimate the associations between coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) vaccination during pregnancy and the risks of adverse perinatal outcomes.""
  - ""COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy (any type or dose of COVID-19 vaccination during any trimester) was not associated with an increased risk of adverse perinatal outcomes.""",
"Prevention Treatment and Management Strategies for Monkeypox Virus Infection in Pregnant and Breastfeeding Women: 
A Systematic Review","Background: Monkeypox (Mpox) is an infectious disease caused by the virus, which has become a global concern in recent years. As a result of its outbreak, the World Health Organization (WHO) has twice declared monkeypox a global emergency, in June 2022 and on 14 August 2024. In cases of such diseases, pregnant and breastfeeding women are typically considered a sensitive or high-risk group; however, very few studies have been published on that group. Objective: This review aims to evaluate the risks of monkeypox virus infection in pregnant and breastfeeding women, assessing prevention strategies, safety measures, treatment options, and management protocols. Methods: we systematically searched for desired data in PubMed, ScienceDirect, Wiley Online, Scilit, and Google Scholar from 2009 to 2024. After removing duplicates, the search yielded 2031 results, of which 49 full-text papers were assessed for eligibility. From these, 32 studies were included in the final analysis. Results: It has been established that Mpox infection contributes to adverse maternal outcomes and complications such as miscarriage, fetal death, congenital Mpox, etc. This review noted that although there are some accessible guidelines for the treatment of Mpox in general, there is a severe lack of comprehensive protocols for pregnant and breastfeeding mothers. This article covers precautions such as vaccination and isolation, available therapies like tecovirimat, and guidelines on fetal monitoring. Conclusion: Finally, this paper proposes an integrated defense and treatment system for monkeypox prevention in pregnant and breastfeeding women, potentially aiding in determining prevalence and contributing to the third number goal of the Sustainable Development Goal (SDG).","Mahafuza Akter Eva, Md. Arafat Rahman Ovi, Mst. Sanjida Akhter, Rehnuma Tanjin, Rupa Akter, Mst Mabiya Sultana Samapti, Runa Masuma, Md. Ataur Rahman",10.4236/aid.2025.151009,https://doi.org/10.4236/aid.2025.151009,Advances in Infectious Diseases,0,2025,yes,"The study specifically focuses on pregnant and breastfeeding women, rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on adverse outcomes such as miscarriage and fetal death, and addresses prevention via vaccination.",yes,The paper is explicitly described as a systematic review.,yes,The study involves human clinical outcomes in pregnant women.,yes,The study population includes pregnant and breastfeeding women.,yes,The review covers vaccination as a precaution for the target group of pregnant and breastfeeding women.,yes,The article discusses vaccination specifically as a strategy for preventing Mpox infection.,Include,This is a systematic review focused on the prevention and treatment of Mpox in pregnant and breastfeeding women. It evaluates safety and health outcomes such as miscarriage and fetal death and discusses management strategies including vaccination.,,,5,"  - ""This review aims to evaluate the risks of monkeypox virus infection in pregnant and breastfeeding women,""",,"  - ""established that Mpox infection contributes to adverse maternal outcomes and complications such as miscarriage, fetal death, congenital Mpox, etc.""
