HIV Status Not Linked to Treatment Outcomes for People With Mpox.,"Although a disproportionate number of mpox cases—between 35% and 47%, according to some studies—occur in people who also have HIV infection, research comparing the safety and outcomes of mpox treatments in patients with and without HIV is lacking.",Emily Harris,10.1001/jama.2023.7767,https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2023.7767,Journal of the American Medical Association (JAMA),2,2023,yes,"The article discusses individuals with HIV infection, which is a population other than healthy adults.",no,The study explicitly states that safety and effectiveness outcomes (specifically for mpox treatments) are lacking rather than reporting them.,no,"This is a brief news report or editorial statement in JAMA highlighting a gap in research, not a primary research study or a systematic review.",yes,"The text refers to mpox cases and patients, implying human subjects.",no,"The article does not mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,The text mentions treatments but does not mention vaccination timing for any of the target groups.,no,The article discusses mpox treatments rather than vaccines.,Exclude,This article is a news brief stating that research comparing mpox treatment outcomes between patients with and without HIV is lacking. It does not include the target populations (pregnant women or children) and focuses on treatments rather than vaccinia-based vaccines.,"Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Target Population,0.1,,,"  - ""research comparing the safety and outcomes of mpox treatments in patients with and without HIV is lacking.""",,"  - ""research comparing the safety and outcomes of mpox treatments in patients with and without HIV is lacking.""",,"  - ""research comparing the safety and outcomes of mpox treatments in patients with and without HIV is lacking.""",,"  - ""research comparing the safety and outcomes of mpox treatments in patient