Monkeypox disease and pregnancy. Where are we today? A review of literature,"Monkeypox has emerged as a significant human pathogen, posing severe risks in vulnerable populations. At present, there is not enough data available as to whether pregnant women are more vulnerable to monkeypox infection, or they suffer more severe symptoms, and studies on this issue as well as to the possible adverse effects on the developing fetus are limited. The aim of this review, was to bring together what is known so far about monkeypox virus transmission, the clinical course of the disease, and associated maternal-fetal outcomes. Furthermore, to summarize the current available recommendations on the prevention and management of monkeypox infection during pregnancy, in order to help obstetricians and neonatologists navigate through this new challenging area and provide the best available care to their patients.
© 2023. The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature America, Inc.","Paschalia Kopanou Taliaka, A. Tsantes, Aikaterini Konstantinidi, P. Liakou, E. Tavoulari, D. Piovani, S. Bonovas, N. Iacovidou, A. Tsantes, R. Sokou",10.1038/s41372-023-01629-0,https://doi.org/10.1038/s41372-023-01629-0,Journal of Perinatology,3,2023,yes,The study focus includes pregnant women and the developing fetus.,yes,The review looks at maternal-fetal outcomes and prevention/management.,no,"The paper describes itself as a 'review of literature'. It does not state it is a systematic review or meta-analysis, nor is it a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) as defined by the criteria.",yes,The study focuses on human pregnancy and maternal-fetal outcomes.,yes,The study explicitly mentions pregnant women and the developing fetus.,maybe,"The abstract mentions summarizing recommendations for prevention during pregnancy, but it does not specify if the review includes studies or data on actual participants receiving the vaccine in those periods.",maybe,The abstract mentions 'prevention' but does not specify the type of vaccine used (vaccinia-based or historical smallpox).,Exclude,"This article is a narrative 'review of literature' rather than a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis, failing the study design criterion. While it addresses relevant populations like pregnant women and fetal outcomes, its methodology does not meet the specified inclusion requirements.",Study Design,Study Design,0.1,"  - ""The aim of this review, was to bring together what is known so far about monkeypox virus transmission, the clinical course of the disease, and associated maternal-fetal outcomes.""
