[Biohazards due to Orthopoxvirus: should we re-vaccinate against smallpox?].,"Although the WHO declared global smallpox eradication in 1980, the Orthopoxvirus remains a source of concern for several reasons. Firstly, stocks of the smallpox virus have been preserved for experimental use (at least officially in the USA and Russia) so that an escaped isolate could lead to reemergence and spread of the disease worldwide. Secondly discontinuation of smallpox vaccination programs has led to dwindling acquired immunity in the world population thus raising the risk of epidemic extension of several Orthopoxvirus zoonoses (e.g., monkeypox). Thirdly stocks of camelpox virus which is very similar to Smallpox virus and was intended for biological warfare were discovered during the Gulf War in 1991 and pose a potentially serious threat. Finally official stocks of Smallpox virus could be stolen and used by bioterrorists. Thus reemergence of Orthopoxvirus including smallpox, monkeypox, cowpox, and camelpox is a real danger and contingency planning is needed to define prophylactic and therapeutic strategies to prevent and/or stop an epidemics. Adverse effects from earlier smallpox vaccine (vaccinia) in healthy people or immunocompromised people (congenital or acquired as in HIV infected patients) are absolute contraindications to smallpox vaccination at this time. Further research is needed to develop new vaccines (e.g., attenuated isolates of vaccinia) and effective treatment. This is the only valid reasons for postponing planned destruction of remaining Smallpox virus stocks.","Georges Aj, Georges-Courbot Mc",,,Médecine tropicale : revue du Corps de santé colonial,6,1999,maybe,"The text discusses vaccine contraindications for 'healthy people or immunocompromised people' and general population immunity, but does not provide data on a specific study population or primary research findings related to non-healthy adults.",no,"The paper mentions adverse effects as a general historical concept and the need for new treatment/prevention strategies, but does not report outcomes from a specific study or actual patient data.",no,"This is a commentary or review article discussing the threat of Orthopoxviruses and the need for planning, not a primary research study (RCT, cohort, case series) or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",no,"The paper is a discussion piece about human health risks and vaccine strategy, but it is not a study with human participants.",no,"The paper does not present data on or include a study population of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it speaks in generalities about the world population and immunocompromised patients.",no,"There is no study population described, so there is no timing of vaccination during the specified periods.",yes,The paper discusses vaccinia and smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This is a commentary or discussion paper regarding the threat of Orthopoxvirus reemergence and the general need for new vaccines. It lacks a primary research design, does not report clinical outcomes from a specific cohort, and does not specifically include or study pregnant women, children, or neonates.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""Adverse effects from earlier smallpox vaccine (vaccinia) in healthy people or immunocompromised people (congenital or acquired as in HIV infected patients) are absolute contraindications to smallpox vaccination at this time.""",,"  - ""Adverse effects from earlier smallpox vaccine (vaccinia) in healthy people or immunocompromised people (congenital or acquired as in HIV infected patients) are absolute contraindications to smallpox vaccination at this time.""
  - ""contingency planning is needed to define prophylactic and therapeutic strategies to prevent and/or stop an epidemics.""",,"  - ""Further research is needed to develop new vaccines (e.g., attenuated isolates of vaccinia) and effective treatment.""
