"Cardiac Safety of Modified Vaccinia Ankara for Vaccination against Smallpox in a Young, Healthy Study Population","Background Conventional smallpox vaccines based on replicating vaccinia virus (VV) strains (e.g. Lister Elstree, NYCBOH) are associated with a high incidence of myo-/pericarditis, a severe inflammatory cardiac complication. A new smallpox vaccine candidate based on a non-replicating Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA) poxvirus has been assessed for cardiac safety in a large placebo-controlled clinical trial. Methods Cardiac safety of one and two doses of MVA compared to placebo was assessed in 745 healthy subjects. Vaccinia-naïve subjects received either one dose of MVA and one dose of placebo, two doses of MVA, or two doses of placebo by subcutaneous injection four weeks apart; vaccinia-experienced subjects received a single dose of MVA. Solicited and unsolicited adverse events (AE) and cardiac safety parameters (recorded as Adverse Events of Special Interest, AESI) were monitored after each injection. Results A total of 5 possibly related AESI (3 cases of palpitations, 2 of tachycardia) were reported during the study. No case of myo- or pericarditis occurred. One possibly related serious AE (SAE) was reported during the 6-month follow-up period (sarcoidosis). The most frequently observed AEs were injection site reactions. Conclusions Vaccination with MVA was safe and well tolerated and did not increase the risk for development of myo-/pericarditis. Trial Registration ClinicalTrials.gov NCT00316524","E. Zitzmann-Roth, F. von Sonnenburg, S. de la Motte, Nathaly Arndtz-Wiedemann, A. von Krempelhuber, N. Uebler, J. Vollmar, G. Virgin, P. Chaplin",10.1371/journal.pone.0122653,https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0122653,PLoS ONE,39,2015,no,"The study is exclusively limited to a healthy population, specifically 745 healthy subjects.",yes,"The study reports on safety outcomes, specifically adverse events of special interest (AESIs) such as palpitations and tachycardia.",yes,The study is a placebo-controlled clinical trial.,yes,The study is a human clinical trial involving 745 subjects.,no,"While the title mentions a 'young' population, the abstract characterizes them as 'subjects' and 'healthy study population' without confirming the inclusion of children, adolescents (<18), or pregnant women; furthermore, clinical trials for MVA safety in this era typically focused on adults (18-55) unless specified otherwise. No mention of neonates or pregnant women exists.",no,"There is no evidence that vaccination occurred during childhood, adolescence, or pregnancy, as the participants are described generally as a 'healthy study population'.",yes,The study evaluates Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA).,Exclude,"This study is a clinical trial assessing the cardiac safety of Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA). However, it is explicitly focused on a healthy adult population and does not include the target populations of pregnant women, children, or adolescents required by the inclusion criteria.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Population Focus,0.1,"  - ""745 healthy subjects.""",,"  - ""A total of 5 possibly related AESI (3 cases of palpitations, 2 of tachycardia) were reported during the study.""",,"  - ""assessed for cardiac safety in a large placebo-controlled clinical trial.""",,"  - ""assessed for cardiac safety in a large placebo-controlled clinical trial.""
