The resurgence of a neglected orthopoxvirus: Immunologic and clinical aspects of monkeypox virus infections over the past six decades,"Monkeypox is a zoonotic Orthopoxvirus which has predominantly affected humans living in western and central Africa since the 1970s. Type I and II interferon signaling, NK cell function, and serologic immunity are critical for host immunity against monkeypox. Monkeypox can evade host viral recognition and block interferon signaling, leading to overall case fatality rates of up to 11%. The incidence of monkeypox has increased since cessation of smallpox vaccination. In 2022, a global outbreak emerged, predominantly affecting males, with exclusive human-to-human transmission and more phenotypic variability than earlier outbreaks. Available vaccines are safe and effective tools for prevention of severe disease, but supply is limited. Now considered a public health emergency, more studies are needed to better characterize at-risk populations and to develop new anti-viral therapies.","A. Al-Musa, J. Chou, B. LaBere",10.1016/j.clim.2022.109108,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.clim.2022.109108,Clinical Immunology,41,2022,maybe,"The abstract discusses monkeypox generally in humans and mentions outbreaks predominantly affecting males, but does not provide specific participant data to determine if it is NOT exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The paper mentions that vaccines are safe and effective tools, which qualifies as reporting on relevant outcomes.",no,"Based on the title and abstract, this appears to be a review or summary of clinical/immunologic aspects rather than a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,The paper discusses human infections and outbreaks.,no,"The abstract does not mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it notes that recent outbreaks predominantly affect males.",no,"There is no information indicating study participants received vaccines during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,"The paper mentions available vaccines and cessation of smallpox vaccination, which refers to vaccinia-based vaccines.",Exclude,"This paper is a general narrative review of the clinical and immunologic aspects of monkeypox rather than a primary research or evidence synthesis study. It does not specifically address the target populations of interest such as children or pregnant women, nor does it provide primary data on vaccine timing in these groups.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,,,"  - ""Available vaccines are safe and effective tools for prevention of severe disease,""",,,,,,,,"  - ""Available vaccines are safe and effective tools for prevention of severe disease,""",,"  - ""Available vaccines are safe and effective tools for prevention of severe disease,""",
Tecovirimat for the treatment of monkeypox: A review of clinical trials,"Monkeypox (mpox) is a disease caused by infection with the monkeypox virus, which was previously endemic in Africa and has been reported in numerous countries on other continents since 2003. Clinically, patients initially present with lymphadenopathy and flu-like prodromal symptoms, followed by a generalized rash. Mpox infection is also associated with several health complications, including pneumonia, sepsis, encephalitis, and ocular damage. Treatment is primarily symptomatic and involves relieving pain and fever, managing skin lesions, and administering antibiotics for secondary infections. Potential antiviral treatments for mpox include virostatic drugs effective against smallpox viruses. The aim of this study was to identify clinical trials from the last 10 years evaluating the potential efficacy of tecovirimat, brincidofovir, and cidofovir in treating mpox infection, using the PubMed and ClinicalTrials.gov databases. The review revealed that currently ongoing or recruiting studies are exclusively assessing the efficacy and safety of tecovirimat. This drug has demonstrated activity against the mpox virus in preclinical studies using experimental models of the disease induced in nonhuman primates. Tecovirimat's efficacy has also been clinically confirmed in case reports of patients treated with this virostatic agent. However, the results of randomized clinical trials identified during the review are inconsistent. The STOMP and PALM007 trials did not demonstrate a benefit of tecovirimat in patients with mpox, while studies conducted in Italy and Japan indicate clinical efficacy of the drug. Given the inconclusive clinical evaluation of tecovirimat so far, the results of ongoing studies are awaited and should provide further evidence to better assess the role of tecovirimat in the pharmacotherapy of mpox.",Łukasz Dobrek,10.32394/pe/214793,https://doi.org/10.32394/pe/214793,Przeglad Epidemiologiczny,0,2025,yes,The study focuses on patients with symptomatic mpox infection and associated complications rather than healthy adults.,yes,The study specifically looks at the efficacy and safety of treatments for mpox.,yes,This is a review of clinical trials identifying studies from PubMed and ClinicalTrials.gov.,yes,The review discusses clinical trials and case reports involving human patients.,no,"The abstract does not mention the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it refers generally to 'patients'.",no,"The participants received antiviral treatment (tecovirimat) for active infection, not a vaccine during the specified periods.",no,The study investigates antiviral therapy (virostatic drugs) like tecovirimat rather than vaccinia-based vaccines.,Exclude,"This study is a clinical review focused on the efficacy of antiviral treatments (tecovirimat, brincidofovir, cidofovir) for mpox infection. It fails several inclusion criteria because it does not involve vaccination and does not specify target populations such as children or pregnant women.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""evaluating the potential efficacy of tecovirimat, brincidofovir, and cidofovir in treating mpox infection,""
