Association of periconceptional or pregnancy exposure of HPV vaccination and adverse pregnancy outcomes: a systematic review and meta-analysis with trial sequential analysis,"Objective: To evaluate whether periconceptional or pregnancy exposure of human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccination would increase the risk of adverse pregnancy outcomes. Methods: The PubMed, Web of Science, Embase, the Cochrane Library of clinical trials were searched from inception to March 2023. We computed relative risk (RR) and 95% confidence intervals (CIs) and prediction intervals (PIs) regarding the association between HPV vaccination in periconceptional period or during pregnancy and the risks of adverse pregnancy outcomes by using R software Version 4.1.2 and STATA Version 12.0. A trial sequential analysis (TSA) was performed with TSA v0.9.5.10 Beta software. Results: Four randomized controlled trials (RCTs) and eight cohort studies were included in this meta-analysis. Analysis of RCTs showed that HPV vaccination in periconceptional period or during pregnancy did not increase the risks of spontaneous abortion (RR = 1.152, 95% CI: 0.909–1.460, 95% PI: 0.442–3.000), birth defects (RR = 1.171, 95% CI: 0.802–1.709, 95% PI: 0.320–4.342), stillbirth (RR = 1.053, 95% CI: 0.616–1.800, 95% PI: 0.318–3.540), preterm birth (RR = 0.940, 95% CI: 0.670–1.318) and ectopic pregnancy (RR = 0.807, 95% CI: 0.353–1.842, 95% PI: 0.128–5.335). In cohort studies, periconceptional or pregnancy exposures of HPV vaccine were not associated with the increased risk of spontaneous abortion (RR = 0.987, 95% CI: 0.854–1.140, 95% PI: 0.652–1.493), birth defects (RR = 0.960, 95% CI: 0.697–1.322, 95% PI: 0.371–2.480), stillbirth (RR = 1.033, 95% CI: 0.651–1.639, 95% PI: 0.052–21.064), small size for gestational age (SGA) (RR = 0.971, 95% CI: 0.873–1.081, 95% PI: 0.657–1.462) and preterm birth (RR = 0.977, 95% CI: 0.874–1.092, 95% PI: 0.651–1.444). Conclusion: HPV vaccine exposures in periconceptional period or during pregnancy did not increase the risks of adverse pregnancy outcomes, including spontaneous abortion, birth defects, stillbirth, SGA, preterm birth and ectopic pregnancy. Systematic Review Registration: https://www.crd.york.ac.uk/prospero/, identifier CRD42023399777.","Xiao-Qing Yan, Hongyu Li, B. Song, Ge Huang, Qing Chang, Dan Wang, Ping Yan",10.3389/fphar.2023.1181919,https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2023.1181919,Frontiers in Pharmacology,10,2023,yes,The study explicitly focuses on pregnant women and those in the periconceptional period.,yes,"The study reports safety outcomes including spontaneous abortion, birth defects, stillbirth, and preterm birth.",yes,The study is a systematic review and meta-analysis.,yes,The study evaluates clinical outcomes in humans following vaccination.,yes,The study includes participants exposed during pregnancy.,yes,Participants received the vaccine in the periconceptional period or during pregnancy.,no,"The study examines only the human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccine, not vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This systematic review evaluates the safety of HPV vaccination during pregnancy. While it addresses a target population (pregnant women) and relevant safety outcomes, it is focused exclusively on the HPV vaccine and does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""To evaluate whether periconceptional or pregnancy exposure of human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccination would increase the risk of adverse pregnancy outcomes.""",,"  - ""did not increase the risks of spontaneous abortion (RR = 1.152, 95% CI: 0.909–1.460, 95% PI: 0.442–3.000), birth defects (RR = 1.171, 95% CI: 0.802–1.709, 95% PI: 0.320–4.342), stillbirth (RR = 1.053, 95% CI: 0.616–1.800, 95% PI: 0.318–3.540), preterm birth (RR = 0.940, 95% CI: 0.670–1.318) and ectopic pregnancy (RR = 0.807, 95% CI: 0.353–1.842, 95% PI: 0.128–5.335).""",,"  - ""To evaluate whether periconceptional or pregnancy exposure of human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccination would increase the risk of adverse pregnancy outcomes.""
  - ""Four randomized controlled trials (RCTs) and eight cohort studies were included in this meta-analysis.""",,"  - ""To evaluate whether periconceptional or pregnancy exposure of human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccination would increase the risk of adverse pregnancy outcomes.""
