EU's Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme and GlaxoSmithKline Biologicals SA.","Milagritos D. Tapia, S. Sow, K. Mbaye, A. Thiongane, B. Ndiaye, C. Ndour, S. Mboup, Babajide Keshinro, T. Kinge, G. Vernet, J. Bigna, S. Oguche, K. Koram, Kwaku Poku Asante, P. Gobert, W. Hogrefe, I. De Ryck, M. Debois, P. Bourguignon, E. Jongert, W. Ballou, M. Koutsoukos, F. Roman",10.1016/S1473-3099(20)30019-0,https://doi.org/10.1016/S1473-3099(20)30019-0,Lancet. Infectious Diseases (Print),43,2020,yes,"The study is focused on children and adolescents (1-17 years), not adults.",yes,The study reports safety outcomes including serious adverse events and local/general adverse events.,yes,This is a phase 2 randomised controlled trial.,yes,The study was conducted on humans (children in Mali and Senegal).,yes,The study includes children and adolescents aged 1-17 years.,yes,Participants received the vaccine during childhood or adolescence (ages 1-17).,no,"The vaccine studied is an adenovirus-vectored Ebola vaccine (ChAd3-EBO-Z), which is not a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine or a historical smallpox vaccine. The mention of Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA) is only as a possible future direction, not as part of this trial.",Exclude,"This is a phase 2 RCT evaluating an Ebola vaccine (ChAd3-EBO-Z) in a paediatric population aged 1-17. While it focuses on a relevant target population and reports safety outcomes, it does not involve a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine or any historical smallpox vaccine.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""600 were randomly assigned (200 [33%] in each age strata: 1-5, 6-12, 13-17 years),""",,"  - ""Primary outcomes assessed were serious adverse events (up to study end, month 12), solicited local or general adverse events""",,"  - ""phase 2, randomised, observer-blind, controlled trial""",,"  - ""evaluate the safety, reactogenicity, and immunogenicity of a monovalent, recombinant, chimpanzee adenovirus type-3 vectored Zaire Ebola glycoprotein vaccine (ChAd3-EBO-Z) in