Antiviral treatment is more effective than smallpox vaccination upon lethal monkeypox virus infection,"There is concern that variola virus, the aetiological agent of smallpox, may be used as a biological weapon. For this reason several countries are now stockpiling (vaccinia virus-based) smallpox vaccine. Although the preventive use of smallpox vaccination has been well documented, little is known about its efficacy when used after exposure to the virus. Here we compare the effectiveness of (1) post-exposure smallpox vaccination and (2) antiviral treatment with either cidofovir (also called HPMPC or Vistide) or with a related acyclic nucleoside phosphonate analogue (HPMPO-DAPy) after lethal intratracheal infection of cynomolgus monkeys (Macaca fascicularis) with monkeypox virus (MPXV). MPXV causes a disease similar to human smallpox and this animal model can be used to measure differences in the protective efficacies of classical and new-generation candidate smallpox vaccines. We show that initiation of antiviral treatment 24 h after lethal intratracheal MPXV infection, using either of the antiviral agents and applying various systemic treatment regimens, resulted in significantly reduced mortality and reduced numbers of cutaneous monkeypox lesions. In contrast, when monkeys were vaccinated 24 h after MPXV infection, using a standard human dose of a currently recommended smallpox vaccine (Elstree-RIVM), no significant reduction in mortality was observed. When antiviral therapy was terminated 13 days after infection, all surviving animals had virus-specific serum antibodies and antiviral T lymphocytes. These data show that adequate preparedness for a biological threat involving smallpox should include the possibility of treating exposed individuals with antiviral compounds such as cidofovir or other selective anti-poxvirus drugs.","K. Stittelaar, J. Neyts, L. Naesens, G. Amerongen, R. F. V. Lavieren, A. Holý, E. Clercq, H. Niesters, E. Fries, Chantal Maas, P. Mulder, B. V. D. Zeijst, A. Osterhaus",10.1038/nature04295,https://doi.org/10.1038/nature04295,Nature,215,2006,no,The study does not involve human populations; it is conducted in monkeys.,yes,The study evaluates efficacy outcomes such as mortality and cutaneous lesions.,yes,This is a primary experimental research study looking at vaccine and antiviral effectiveness.,no,"The study is an animal study (laboratory/veterinary study), not a human study, as indicated by the use of cynomolgus monkeys.",no,"The subjects are cynomolgus monkeys, and the study clearly does not include human pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"The vaccination was administered to monkeys 24 hours post-infection, not humans during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The study uses the Elstree-RIVM smallpox vaccine.,Exclude,"This study is excluded because it is an animal study conducted in cynomolgus monkeys rather than human participants. It fails to meet the target population criteria requiring pregnant women, neonates, or children.","Population Focus, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""lethal intratracheal infection of cynomolgus monkeys (Macaca fascicularis)""",,"  - ""effectiveness of (1) post-exposure smallpox vaccination and (2) antiviral treatment""",,"  - ""effectiveness of (1) post-exposure smallpox vaccination and (2) antiviral treatment""",,"  - ""lethal intratracheal infection of cynomolgus monkeys (Macaca fascicularis)""",,"  - ""lethal intratracheal infection of cynomolgus monkeys (Macaca fascicularis)""",,"  - ""using a standard human dose of a currently recommended smallpox vaccine (Elstree-RIVM),""",,"  - ""using a standard human dose of a currently recommended smallpox vaccine (Elstree-RIVM),""",
Protective Murine and Human Monoclonal Antibodies against Eczema Vaccinatum,"Background Eczema vaccinatum is the most common severe pathology associated with smallpox vaccination (vaccinia virus), occurring at high rates among individuals with a previous history of atopic dermatitis (atopic eczema). Methods Monoclonal antibodies capable of neutralizing vaccinia virus, anti-H3 and anti-B5, were developed as a potential therapy for treatment of human eczema vaccinatum. Results Using a small animal model of eczema vaccinatum, we demonstrated that both murine and fully human monoclonal antibodies effectively limited eczema vaccinatum disease, foreshortening both the disease kinetics and the severity of the erosive viral skin lesions. Conclusions These neutralizing antibodies would likely be effective at reducing or eliminating clinical disease in people with eczema vaccinatum or other severe side effects of the smallpox vaccine.","Y. Tomimori, Y. Kawakami, M. Mccausland, T. Ando, L. Koriazova, Shinichiro Kato, T. Kawakami, Shane Crotty",10.3851/IMP1717,https://doi.org/10.3851/IMP1717,Antiviral Therapy,11,2011,no,"The study does focus on a specific population of human interest (eczema vaccinatum patients), but the actual research is conducted in a small animal model, not on human subjects.",yes,The study reports on the effectiveness of monoclonal antibodies in limiting disease kinetics and severity of skin lesions.,yes,This is a primary research study involving the development and testing of antibodies.,no,"The paper explicitly states that the research was conducted using a ""small animal model"" and does not involve human clinical subjects.",no,"The study does not include human participants of any age or status, as it is a laboratory animal study.",no,"Since there are no human participants, this criterion regarding timing of vaccination in humans is not met.",yes,"The study investigates complications and treatments related to the ""smallpox vaccination (vaccinia virus)"".",Exclude,"This is an animal model study investigating monoclonal antibody treatments for eczema vaccinatum rather than a human study. It fails several required criteria, most notably the requirement for human participants and target populations like children or pregnant women.","Population Focus, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""Using a small animal model of eczema vaccinatum,""",,"  - ""demonstrated that both murine and fully human monoclonal antibodies effectively limited eczema vaccinatum disease,""",,"  - ""Using a small animal model of eczema vaccinatum,""",,"  - ""Using a small animal model of eczema vaccinatum,""",,"  - ""Using a small animal model of eczema vaccinatum,""",,"  - ""Using a small animal model of eczema vaccinatum,""",,"  - ""Eczema vaccinatum is the most common severe pathology associated with smallpox vaccination (vaccinia virus),""",
mRNA-based monkeypox virus vaccine prevents disease in non-human primates.,"The mpox outbreak in 2022 launched a vaccination campaign employing an existing vaccine with moderate protection, highlighting the lack of scalable Orthopoxvirus vaccines with optimal protection. In this issue of Cell, Zuiani et al. report pre-clinical findings of an mRNA-based mpox vaccine, paving the way for Phase I/II clinical trials.","Adam N. Nitido, Alejandro B. Balazs",10.1016/j.cell.2024.02.011,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2024.02.011,Cell,5,2024,no,"The study is a pre-clinical investigation in non-human primates, not humans.",no,"While the study reports on disease prevention, these are pre-clinical findings in primates rather than human effectiveness outcomes.",no,"This is a commentary or preview article summarizing results from another paper in the issue, not a primary research study or systematic review.",no,The study explicitly focuses on non-human primates and pre-clinical research.,no,The study does not involve human participants such as pregnant women or children.,no,"As a non-human and pre-clinical study, it does not involve human vaccination timing.",no,"The study evaluates an mRNA-based vaccine, which is distinct from traditional vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This is a commentary on a pre-clinical study conducted in non-human primates. It evaluates an mRNA-based vaccine rather than a vaccinia-based vaccine, failing multiple mandatory criteria related to human population and study type.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""Zuiani et al. report pre-clinical findings of an mRNA-based mpox vaccine, paving the way for Phase I/II clinical trials.""",,"  - ""Zuiani et al. report pre-clinical findings of an mRNA-based mpox vaccine, paving the way for Phase I/II clinical trials.""",,"  - ""Zuiani et al. report pre-clinical findings of an mRNA-based mpox vaccine, paving the way for Phase I/II clinical trials.""",,"  - ""Zuiani et al. report pre-clinical findings of an mRNA-based mpox vaccine, paving the way for Phase I/II clinical trials.""",,"  - ""Zuiani et al. report pre-clinical findings of an mRNA-based mpox vaccine, paving the way for Phase I/II clinical trials.""",,"  - ""Zuiani et al. report pre-clinical findings of an mRNA-based mpox vaccine, paving the way for Phase I/II clinical trials.""",,"  - ""Zuiani et al. report pre-clinical findings of an mRNA-based mpox vaccine, paving the way for Phase I/II clinical trials.""",
Effectiveness of Maternal Influenza Vaccination During Pregnancy Against Influenza-Associated Emergency Department Visits and Hospitalizations in Infants <6 Months of Age,"Using a test-negative design, we assessed the effectiveness (VE) of influenza vaccination during pregnancy against laboratory-confirmed influenza-associated ED visits or hospitalizations in infants <6 months enrolled from 7 sites in the 2016-2017 through 2019-2020 influenza seasons. We assessed maternal influenza vaccination and timing of vaccine receipt during pregnancy using documentation from state immunization information systems, providers, or self-report. We estimated VE by comparing the odds of maternal influenza vaccination ≥14 days prior to delivery in influenza-positive case-infants versus influenza-negative control-infants (stratified by infant age, trimester of vaccination, and severity of infant disease).","Olson S, Sahni L, Halasa N, Stewart L, Michaels M, Williams J, Englund J, Klein E, Staat M, Schlaudecker E, Selvarangan R, Schuster J, Weinberg G, Szilagyi P, Boom J, Patel M, Munoz F",10.1016/j.ajog.2023.09.036,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajog.2023.09.036,American Journal of Obstetrics and Gynecology,0,2024,yes,"The study focuses on infants under 6 months old and pregnant women, therefore it is not limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study reports on effectiveness against influenza-associated visits, but does not report outcomes related to mpox infection, hospitalization, or death.",yes,"The study uses a test-negative design, which is a form of primary research study.",yes,The study is a clinical investigation of human participants (infants and mothers).,yes,The study includes infants under 6 months and pregnant women.,yes,Participants received the vaccination during the maternal pregnancy period.,no,"The study evaluates the influenza vaccine, not vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,This study evaluates the effectiveness of maternal influenza vaccination in protecting infants against influenza. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccines and does not report mpox-related outcomes.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""infants <6 months""
