Impact of Mpox on Children's Health: A Comprehensive Overview,"Mpox (formerly monkey pox) is an emerging zoonotic disease caused by infection with the Mpox virus, which affects both humans and animals. The disease is usually self-limiting, but severe illness can occur in certain groups especially children, and people who are immunocompromised or pregnant. In recent years, rapid globalization, population movement and deepening trade networks have contributed to the international spread of Mpox, resulting in outbreaks in various countries around the world. Based on information from previous outbreaks, children may be more susceptible to severe manifestations of the disease, leading to sepsis, encephalitis, and even death or long-term disability. In addition, the reports recommends that babies born to mothers who had Mpox virus while pregnant should be properly monitored in specialized care facilities. This overview presents a thorough review of Mpox, including its transmission patterns, epidemiology and pathogenesis. This review aims to provide updated information about Mpox virus for designing better treatment.",Ni Made Gita Andariska,10.54543/kesans.v3i12.313,https://doi.org/10.54543/kesans.v3i12.313,KESANS International Journal of Health and Science,0,2024,yes,The study explicitly focuses on children and pregnant women.,no,"The study discusses clinical outcomes of the disease such as death, but does not report safety or effectiveness outcomes for any vaccine.",no,The paper is a narrative overview/review rather than primary research or a systematic review/meta-analysis.,yes,The paper is a human-focused epidemiological and clinical overview.,yes,"The study includes children, pregnant women, and neonates as the primary subjects of discussion.",no,There is no mention of participants receiving vaccines during pregnancy or childhood.,no,The study does not involve or mention vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This paper is a narrative overview of Mpox epidemiological patterns and clinical manifestations in children. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not involve the study of vaccines, nor is it a primary research or systematic review study.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""severe illness can occur in certain groups especially children, and people who are immunocompromised or pregnant.""
