Monkeypox - exotic infection outbreak or a new global challenge to global Health system?,"Introduction. Monkeypox (MPX), a zoonotic disease caused by an orthopoxvirus, results in a smallpox-like or a chickenpox-like disease. MPX in human was first diagnosed in 1970 in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC) and subsequently has spread to other regions of Africa (primarily West and Central). In 2003 the first MPX outbreak occurred outside of Africa in USA. The latest outbreak of MPX in May 2022 is characterized by the simultaneous involvement of several hundred people. The appearance of cases outside of Africa highlights the risk for geographical spread and the global relevance of the disease requiring the epidemiologic studies and the search for effective methods of prevention and therapy.
Aim. To summarize and systematize the results of studies on the epidemiology, diagnosis, clinical picture, the role of vaccination and treatment of monkeypox.
Materials and methods. A systematic literature search was conducted in PubMed and Google Scholar database published up to May 31, 2022, using the keywords monkeypox or monkeypox virus or smallpox human monkeys."" We primary selected 112 abstracts. After removing duplicates and irrelevant articles, 48 full-text articles have been selected. After reviewing the full-text article versions, 15 articles were excluded. Ultimately, this review included 33 articles.
Results. Monkeypox cases among humans have been on the rise since the 1970s, primarily in endemic areas. The median age at the time of treatment increased from 4 (1970s) to 21 (20102019). Since 2003, the spread of MPX outside Africa has been noted, associated with the importation of infected animals, migration and tourism. Transmission to humans occurs through close contact with an infected animal (70%), a person or material contaminated with the virus. MPX presents with fever, vesicular rash, and lymphadenopathy lasting 2 to 4 weeks. Mortality is associated with the development of complications and is 10.6% for the Central African clade compared to 3.6% for the West African clade. There have been no deaths outside of Africa. Unfortunately, smallpox vaccination is contraindicated in immunocompromised individuals, despite the availability of recommended vaccines. The effectiveness of existing antiviral drugs (tecovirimate and brincidofovir) has been little studied in humans.
Conclusion. The weakening of population immunity, associated with the cessation of vaccination against smallpox, created the prerequisites for the outbreaks of MPX, including in non-endemic areas. In light of the emergence of cases outside of Africa, the potential for human-to-human transmission of the virus, the public health significance of MPX should not be underestimated.","I. Dolgopolov, M. Rykov, Zhanna Valer'evna Hamtsova",10.17816/eid108550,https://doi.org/10.17816/eid108550,Epidemiology and Infectious Diseases,2,2022,yes,"The study population is not limited to healthy non-pregnant adults, as it reports on the median age of infection being as low as 4 years old.",no,"The study reports on epidemiological and clinical outcomes like mortality and complications, but it does not report specific safety or effectiveness outcomes for the mpox or smallpox vaccines beyond general statements about counterindications and population-level immunity decline.",yes,The study is a systematic review of 33 articles.,yes,The study analyzes human monkeypox cases and history.,yes,"The study includes data on children, specifically noting a median age of 4 in the 1970s.",no,"While the study mentions the cessation of smallpox vaccination, it does not specifically report on outcomes for participants who received the vaccine during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The study discusses smallpox vaccination in the context of preventing monkeypox.,Exclude,"This systematic review provides an epidemiological overview of monkeypox and mentions that cases included children. However, it fails the screening criteria because it does not report specific safety or effectiveness outcomes for vaccination or provide data on participants who received the vaccine during the required target periods like childhood or pregnancy.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing",Vaccination Timing,0.1,"  - ""The median age at the time of treatment increased from 4 (1970s) to 21 (20102019).""",,"  - ""The median age at the time of treatment increased from 4 (1970s) to 21 (20102019).""
