Risk factors for mortality and clinical presentation of monkeypox,"Objectives: To describe risk factors for mortality and clinical characteristics in patients with mpox infection at a reference hospital in Mexico. Design: A prospective cohort study was conducted from September to December 2022 at Hospital de Infectología La Raza National Medical Center. Methods: Study participants were patients that met operational definition of confirmed case of mpox according to WHO criteria. Information was obtained through a case report form that included epidemiological, clinical, and biochemical information. The follow-up period was from initial evaluation for hospitalization until discharge due to clinical improvement or death. Written informed consent was obtained from all participants. Results: Seventy-two patients were included in the analysis, 64 of 72 (88.9%) were people with HIV (PWH). Of the total of patients 71 of 72 (98.6%) were male, with a median age of 32 years old [95% confidence interval (CI), interquartile range (IQR) 27–37]. Coinfection with sexually transmitted infections was reported in 30 of 72 (41.7%). The overall mortality was five of 72 (6.9%). The incidence of mortality rate in PWH was 6.3%. Median days from onset of symptoms to death from any cause during hospitalization was 50 days (95% CI, IQR 38–62). Risk factors for mpox mortality in the bivariate analysis were CD4+ cells count ≤100 cells/μl at the time of assessment RR 20 (95% CI, IQR 6.6–60.2) (P < 0.001), absence of antiretroviral therapy RR 6.6 (95% CI, IQR 3.6–12.1) (P = 0.001) and ≥50 skin lesions at presentation RR 6.4 (95% CI, IQR 2.6–15.7) (P = 0.011). Conclusions: The clinical presentation between PWH and non-HIV patients was similar in this study, however, reported mortality was associated with advanced-HIV disease.","Salma Triana-González, Cristina Román-López, S. Mauss, Ana Luz Cano-Díaz, J. A. Mata-Marín, E. Pérez-Barragán, E. Pompa-Mera, J. Gaytán-Martínez",10.1097/QAD.0000000000003623,https://doi.org/10.1097/QAD.0000000000003623,AIDS (London),16,2023,yes,"The study focuses on adults with mpox, specifically people with HIV, and is not limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports on mortality (death) which is a relevant effectiveness/severity outcome for mpox.,yes,"The study is a prospective cohort study, which is a primary research design.",yes,The study involves human patients at a hospital.,no,"The study population consists of adults (median age 32) and is 98.6% male. There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Vaccination timing is not discussed as the study is focused on the clinical presentation and mortality risk factors of infected individuals, and the population is adult.",no,The study does not evaluate or involve vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines; it is a descriptive study of clinical risk factors for mortality in infected patients.,Exclude,"This is a prospective cohort study describing risk factors for mortality in adult patients with mpox. It fails the target population criteria as it focuses on an adult male population (median age 32) and does not include pregnant women or children, nor does it evaluate vaccination.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""To describe risk factors for mortality and clinical characteristics in patients with mpox infection""
