"Epidemiology, clinical presentation and outcome of human mpox in Rivers State, Nigeria during the 2022-23 outbreak: an observational retrospective study","Introduction limited studies describe the epidemiology, clinical presentation, and outcome of mpox in Rivers State, Nigeria. Methods an observational retrospective study of suspected mpox cases seen between October 21, 2021, and April 30, 2023, at the University of Port Harcourt Teaching Hospital, Rivers State, Nigeria. Laboratory confirmation was by qPCR testing of skin swabs, or crust samples. A data extraction form was used to obtain sociodemographic and clinical data. The primary outcome was the rate of hospitalization and death. Data was analyzed using IBM SPSS version 25. A p-value of <0.05 was considered statistically significant. Results of 35 participants, 24 (68.6%) were males with a mean age [standard deviation] of 33.7 (± 9.9 years), and 5 (14.3%) had HIV infection. The majority (n=33, 94.3%) reported being in a heterosexual relationship with a household identified as the probable place of contact (n=13, 72.2%). None of the patients reported contact with animals or a travel history. The most common symptoms were skin rash (35, 100%), followed by fever (26, 74.3%), and fatigue (26, 74.3%). Concomitant varicella zoster virus infection (n=6, 17.1%), hospital admission (n=28, 80%), and death (n=3, 8.6%) were observed. There was a statistically significant association between mortality and patients with HIV (p = 0.002). Conclusion the 2022-23 mpox outbreak in Rivers State predominantly affected young adults and was characterized by human-to-human transmission and typical febrile rash syndrome with an association between mpox mortality and HIV infection. Targeted public health interventions are needed to control the spread of mpox in Nigeria.","C. Onyeaghala, A. Somiari, Ujile Ichechiek, J. Ogan, H. Avundaa, Odianosen Ehiakhamen, B. Otike-odibi, O. Maduka, D. Alasia",10.11604/pamj.supp.2025.50.1.46067,https://doi.org/10.11604/pamj.supp.2025.50.1.46067,The Pan African Medical Journal,6,2025,yes,"The study focuses on suspected mpox cases, which are symptomatic individuals rather than a healthy population. However, the participants are adults with a mean age of 33.7.",yes,The study reports on hospitalization and death rates.,yes,The study is an observational retrospective study.,yes,The study involves human cases of mpox in Nigeria.,no,"The mean age of participants is 33.7 years (SD ± 9.9). There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; the study states it predominantly affected 'young adults'.",no,"The study does not describe vaccination of participants, nor does it include the target pediatric/pregnant population.",no,"The study does not mention vaccines, vaccination status, or vaccine types; it focuses on the outcome of mpox infection in a clinical series.",Exclude,"This retrospective observational study focuses on clinical outcomes of mpox in a cohort of adults in Nigeria with a mean age of 33.7 years. It fails to meet the inclusion criteria because it does not include the target population of children, adolescents, or pregnant women and does not evaluate vaccination.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""observational retrospective study of suspected mpox cases""
