Fatal Case of Progressive Mpox in a Patient with AIDS—Viral Enteropathy and Malabsorption Demanding the Use of Full Parenteral ARV and Endovenous Cidofovir,"We report a fatal case of disseminated mpox infection that progressed over more than three months in an HIV-infected patient with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS). Mucocutaneous, pleuropulmonary, central nervous system, and gastrointestinal involvement was documented. This course of disease resembles progressive vaccinia, a formerly reported disease caused by uncontrolled replication of smallpox vaccination orthopoxviruses in immunosuppressed patients. Severe small bowel involvement jeopardized normal oral tecovirimat and antiretroviral therapy absorption. This problem prompted the use of full parenteral antiretrovirals and endovenous cidofovir. Although a remarkable decrease in HIV viral load occurred in six days, mpox infection continued to progress, and the patient died of septic shock. This case offers new clinical insights on the presentation of severe disease in AIDS patients. Moreover, this case alerts for the need for prompt therapy initiation in patients at risk of ominous clinical progression.","João Caria, F. Vara-Luíz, I. Maia, A. Joosten, Luis Val-Flores, H. Pinheiro, D. Póvoas, N. Germano, F. Maltez",10.3390/idr15020018,https://doi.org/10.3390/idr15020018,Infectious Disease Reports,8,2023,yes,"The study focuses on an HIV-infected patient with AIDS, and therefore is not limited to healthy adults.",yes,The study reports on clinical outcomes including disseminated infection and death (fatal case).,yes,The study is a case report.,yes,The study is a clinical report of a human patient.,no,The study describes a single patient with AIDS and does not indicate the participant was under 18 years old or pregnant.,no,The patient in the study was treated for mpox infection with antivirals; there is no evidence the participant received a vaccine during the specified periods.,no,"The study focuses on the treatment of an active mpox infection with tecovirimat and cidofovir, not on the evaluation of mpox or smallpox vaccines. Reference to vaccines is made only for clinical comparison.",Exclude,This is a case report of a fatal mpox infection in an adult patient with AIDS. It does not include the target populations of children or pregnant women and evaluates therapeutic interventions (cidofovir and tecovirimat) rather than vaccines.,"Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""We report a fatal case of disseminated mpox infection that progressed over more than three months in an HIV-infected patient with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS).""",,"  - ""We report a fatal case of disseminated mpox infection that progressed over more than three months in an HIV-infected patient with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS).""",,"  - ""We report a fatal case of disseminated mpox infection that progressed over more than three months in an HIV-infected patient with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS).""",,"  - ""We report a fatal case of disseminated mpox infection that progressed over more than three months in an HIV-infected patient with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS).""",,"  - ""We report a fatal case of disseminated mpox infection that progressed over more than three months in an HIV-infected patient with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS).""",,"  - ""This problem prompted the use of full parenteral antiretrovirals and endovenous cidofovir.""",,"  - ""This problem prompted the use of full parenteral antiretrovirals and endovenous cidofovir.""
