"Dual Maternal Immunization During Pregnancy (Tetanus, Diphtheria, and Acellular Pertussis and Influenza): Maternal–Fetal Clinical Effectiveness, Timing Effects, and Safety – A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis","Maternal vaccination during pregnancy is a key strategy to prevent severe influenza and pertussis in mothers and young infants. We conducted a Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-analyses-2020 systematic review and meta-analysis of dual maternal immunization with inactivated/recombinant influenza vaccines and tetanus, diphtheria, and acellular pertussis (Tdap). Searches of PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science, and guideline repositories (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists, World Health Organization, Perhimpunan Obstetri dan Ginekologi Indonesia) through September 2025 had no language restrictions. Eligible designs included randomized trials, cohort, case–control, test-negative studies, and meta-analyses assessing maternal or fetal/neonatal outcomes. Two reviewers independently screened, extracted, and assessed risk of bias (ROB-2, ROBINS-I, AMSTAR-2); certainty was rated with Grading of Recommendations, Assessment, Development and Evaluations. From 2,220 records, 46 studies met inclusion; 25 were core studies for detailed appraisal. Maternal influenza vaccination in any trimester reduced laboratory-confirmed infection and hospitalization, and lowered infant influenza within the first 6 months of life. Tdap administered at 27–36 weeks’ gestation reduced pertussis in infants <6 months, with earlier vaccination within that window enhancing transplacental antibody transfer. Across large datasets, there was no increase in adverse obstetric outcomes or major congenital anomalies; co-administration was tolerated. Some studies noted mild blunting of infant responses to routine vaccinations after maternal Tdap, but this did not translate into worse clinical outcomes. Limitations include the absence of formal protocol registration (e.g., PROSPERO), heterogeneity of study designs that may affect synthesis, and limited long-term follow-up beyond infancy. The evidence supports dual maternal immunization as safe, effective, and time-sensitive. Public health implementation should emphasize equitable access, timing optimization, and integration within antenatal care to strengthen global maternal–infant protection.","W. Andonotopo, Efendi Lukas, M. B. A. Pramono, J. Dewantiningrum, Wisnu Prabowo, M. A. Bachnas, I. N. H. Sanjaya, A. P. Wiradnyana, Anak Agung Ngurah Jaya Kusuma, K. E. Gumilar, Ernawati Darmawan, M. I. Akbar, Dudy Aldiansyah, Aloysius Suryawan, Ridwan Abdullah Putra, Anita Deborah Anwar, Laksmana Adi Krista Nugraha, W. E. K. Andanaputra, Wibisana Andika Krista Dharma, Milan Stanojević",10.4103/sbvj.sbvj_56_25,https://doi.org/10.4103/sbvj.sbvj_56_25,"SBV Journal of Basic, Clinical and Applied Health Science",0,2025,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women and their infants.,yes,The study reports on clinical effectiveness (infection reduction) and safety outcomes (obstetric outcomes and congenital anomalies).,yes,The study is a systematic review and meta-analysis.,yes,The study evaluates outcomes in human mothers and infants.,yes,The study specifically includes pregnant women and infants <6 months of life.,yes,Vaccination occurred during pregnancy (any trimester for influenza; 27-36 weeks for Tdap).,no,"The study exclusively examines influenza and Tdap (tetanus, diphtheria, and acellular pertussis) vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This systematic review evaluations the safety and effectiveness of dual maternal immunization with influenza and Tdap vaccines. While it focuses on the correct target populations of pregnant women and infants, it does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccines, failing the intervention criterion.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""systematic review and meta-analysis of dual maternal immunization with inactivated/recombinant influenza vaccines and tetanus, diphtheria, and acellular pertussis (Tdap).""",,"  - ""Maternal influenza vaccination in any trimester reduced laboratory-confirmed infection and hospitalization, and lowered infant influenza within the first 6 months of life.""
  - ""Across large datasets, there was no increase in adverse obstetric outcomes or major congenital anomalies; co-administration was tolerated.""",,"  - ""systematic review and meta-analysis of dual maternal immunization with inactivated/recombinant influenza vaccines and tetanus, diphtheria, and acellular pertussis (Tdap).""",,"  - ""systematic review and meta-analysis of dual maternal immunization with inactivated/recombinant influenza vaccines and tetanus, diphtheria, and acellular pertussis (Tdap).""
