Smallpox vaccination‐elicited antibodies cross‐neutralize 2022‐Monkeypox virus Clade II,"Since early May 2022, some monkeypox virus (MPXV) infections have been reported from countries where the disease is not endemic. Within 2 months, the number of patients has increased extensively, becoming the most considerable MPXV outbreak described. Smallpox vaccines demonstrated high efficacy against MPXVs in the past and are considered a crucial outbreak control measure. However, viruses isolated during the current outbreak carry distinct genetic variations, and the cross‐neutralizing capability of antibodies remains to be assessed. Here we report that serum antibodies elicited by first‐generation smallpox vaccines can neutralize the current MPXV more than 40 years after vaccine administration.","E. Criscuolo, Benedetta Giuliani, R. Ferrarese, Davide Ferrari, M. Locatelli, M. Clementi, N. Mancini, N. Clementi",10.1002/jmv.28643,https://doi.org/10.1002/jmv.28643,Journal of Medical Virology,20,2023,no,"The study is limited to individuals who were vaccinated more than 40 years ago, implying the study population is composed exclusively of adults and does not focus on the required non-adult or pregnant populations.",no,"The study reports on antibody neutralization levels, which is an immunogenicity outcome, rather than clinical safety or effectiveness outcomes such as prevention of mpox infection, hospitalization, or death.",yes,The study appears to be a primary research laboratory study evaluating human serum samples.,yes,The study uses human serum antibodies.,no,"The participants are adults assessed decades after their vaccination and do not include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as active study subjects.",maybe,"While participants likely received the vaccines as children over 40 years ago, the study describes their status as adults during the assessment period rather than focusing on childhood vaccination outcomes specifically within a pediatric cohort.",yes,The study evaluates first-generation smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This study investigates the long-term neutralizing capability of antibodies in adults vaccinated with first-generation smallpox vaccines more than 40 years ago. It fails several inclusion criteria, most notably lacking the target population (children or pregnant women at the time of study) and clinical effectiveness outcomes.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Target Population",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""serum antibodies elicited by first‐generation smallpox vaccines can neutralize the current MPXV more than 40 years after vaccine administration.""
