Association Between Influenza Vaccination During Pregnancy and Infant Influenza Vaccination,"(Obstet Gynecol. 2023;141:563–569)
Vaccines are safe and effective in preventing illness, including in mildly immunosuppressed individuals such as pregnant women. Influenza vaccines have been in use for decades and have been shown to be safe and effective at preventing illness in both mother and child, providing protection through the transplacental transfer of antibodies to infants too young to receive their own vaccination. Influenza presents increased risks to pregnant individuals as well as adverse outcomes for fetuses and infants. Annual influenza vaccinations are recommended for everyone 6 months and older, but rates of annual vaccinations are chronically low. This study was designed to evaluate the association between influenza vaccination during pregnancy and infant influenza vaccination.","F. Zhou, M. C. Lindley, J.T. Lee, T. C. Jatlaoui",10.1097/01.aoa.0001005436.35108.8c,https://doi.org/10.1097/01.aoa.0001005436.35108.8c,Obstetric Anesthesia Digest,0,2024,yes,"The study focus includes pregnant women and infants, not exclusively healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study reports on the association of vaccination uptake across generations, not on mpox-related safety or effectiveness outcomes such as prevention of mpox infection or hospitalization.",yes,The abstract describes a primary research study designed to evaluate associations between vaccinations.,yes,The study involves human participants (pregnant women and infants).,yes,The study includes pregnant women and infants.,yes,The study examines vaccination during pregnancy and infancy.,no,"The study evaluates influenza vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"The study evaluates influenza vaccination during pregnancy and its association with infant influenza vaccination uptake. It does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccines, and it does not report outcomes related to mpox prevention or safety, thus failing critical inclusion criteria.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""pregnant women.""
