"Comparative humoral profiles in mpox cases, survivors, and vaccinated individuals reveal correlates of protection against MPXV","Summary Mpox is regarded as the most important orthopoxvirus infection since the eradication of smallpox, yet the determinants of protective antibody responses remain poorly defined. Here, we investigate factors shaping humoral protection against MPXV and observe a moderate correlation between orthopoxvirus antibody levels and age among vaccinia virus (VACV)-vaccinated individuals. No correlation is found between orthopoxvirus-binding antibodies and gender. Pre-existing humoral immunity does not impair the induction of MPXV-specific antibody responses in mpox cases. Despite a high seropositivity rate for neutralizing antibodies in both mpox cases and vaccinated individuals, the overall titers remain modest. Quantitative analyses identify MPXV antigens A29, E8, and M1 on intracellular mature virion (IMV), together with A35 and B6 on extracellular enveloped virion (EEV), as the principal targets of neutralizing antibodies elicited during both MPXV infection and vaccination. Passive transfer of plasma from mpox patients and convalescents significantly reduces viral replication in vivo. These findings provide critical insights into the correlates of protection against MPXV.","Yanqun Wang, Lu Zhang, Lijuan Zhou, Jiantao Chen, Zhaoyong Zhang, Tiantian Wu, Peilan Wei, Airu Zhu, Ruoxi Cai, Jingjun Zhang, Zhiwei Lin, Canjie Chen, Yuanyuan Zhang, Qier Zhong, Jing Sun, Yongxia Shi, Jingxian Zhao, Jun Dai, Pengzhe Qin, Jincun Zhao",10.1016/j.xcrm.2025.102483,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xcrm.2025.102483,Cell Reports Medicine,1,2025,yes,"The study includes mpox cases and survivors, meaning the population is not exclusively limited to healthy individuals.",yes,"The study investigates humoral protection and neutralizing antibody targets, which are relevant correlates of effectiveness.",yes,This is a primary research study involving humoral profiling and in vivo passive transfer experiments.,yes,"The study utilizes samples from human participants, including mpox cases and vaccinated individuals.",no,"There is no evidence in the abstract that the study includes pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; the participants are described as cases, survivors, and individuals with VACV-vaccination history, who are typically adults in the context of recent outbreaks and historical vaccination.",no,"While participants received the VACV vaccine, which may have been during childhood, the study does not specifically focus on or identify the inclusion of participants currently in the pediatric or adolescent age ranges.",yes,The study specifically examines individuals vaccinated with the vaccinia virus (VACV).,Exclude,"The paper provides an immunological analysis of mpox cases and individuals previously vaccinated with the vaccinia virus. However, it does not include the required target populations of children, adolescents, or pregnant women, and thus does not meet the inclusion criteria for this specific review.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,,,"  - ""investigate factors shaping humoral protection against MPXV""
  - ""Quantitative analyses identify MPXV antigens A29, E8, and M1 on intracellular mature virion (IMV), together with A35 and B6 on extracellular enveloped virion (EEV), as the principal targets of neutralizing antibodies elicited during both MPXV infection and vaccination.""",,"  - ""Passive transfer of plasma from mpox patients and convalescents significantly reduces viral replication in vivo.""",,,,"  - ""moderate correlation between orthopoxvirus antibody levels and age among vaccinia virus (VACV)-vaccinated individuals.""",,"  - ""moderate correlation between orthopoxvirus antibody levels and age among vaccinia virus (VACV)-vaccinated individuals.""",,"  - ""moderate correlation between orthopoxvirus antibody levels and age among vaccinia virus (VACV)-vaccinated individuals.""",
Residual legacy immunity from prior smallpox vaccination and serological evidence of asymptomatic mpox transmission in a West African population,"Abstract The 2022 multi-country mpox outbreak, driven by MPXV Clade IIb has spread globally to over 100 countries, posing a new threat to global public health. Smallpox vaccination was discontinued in 1980 following global elimination with recent reports demonstrating evidence of asymptomatic infections particularly in individuals unlikely to have received a smallpox vaccine. To determine whether residual vaccination-derived immunity still shapes infection risk and to characterise population level burden of covert and asymptomatic transmission in a West African population, we combined serological analysis with phylogenetic analysis. We first quantified IgG binding to six MPXV antigens (A29L, A35R, B6R, D6L, H3L and M1R) in 176 Nigerian adults comprising of 75 healthcare workers sampled in 2021 and 101 community volunteers sampled in 2023 using a six-plex Luminex assay. Using a stringent ≥4-antigen reactivity threshold, at baseline, 24/176 (13.6%) participants met definition of MPXV seropositivity likely due to previous vaccination. Most participants with MPXV seropositivity were born before 1980, suggestive of residual smallpox immunity. Magnitude-breadth analysis scores were 2-fold higher in pre-1980 cohort relative to post-1980 cohort. In a subset of individuals (n=153) with follow-up samples over a median of 9 months, 5/153 (3%) showed evidence of silent asymptomatic exposure determined by antibody boosting and characterised by ≥2-fold increases in both total magnitude and breadth scores antibodies against ≥ 4/6 tested antigens all of which were MPXV seronegative individuals at baseline and mostly males. Participant level kinetics showed B6-specific titres boosted most strongly (median 11-folds), followed by M1R (6.2-folds) and A35R (5.2-folds) highlighting their potential utility as sero-surveillance markers. Complementary phylogenetic reconstruction of 105 Nigerian MPXV genomes showed two epidemic phases with over-dispersion (k≈0.3), indicating transmission sustained by sporadic superspreading despite high degree of dead-end infections. Serological and genomic evidence points to sustained asymptomatic transmission of MPXV in healthy individuals in the African setting, thereby informing public health interventions.","A. Abdullahi, I. Omah, R. Kassanjee, S. Osawe, M. Edun, F. A. Ige, E. Parker, H. Wisso, O. Folarin, U. Saulawa, L. Ceron-Gutierrez, A. Happi, O. Sokolova, R. Audu, S. Aliyu, C. Happi, B. Salako, R. Doffinger, A. Abimiku, R. Gupta",10.1101/2025.07.08.25330960,https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.08.25330960,medRxiv,0,2025,no,The study is exclusively limited to healthy adults.,yes,The study examines how historical vaccination shapes infection risk (effectiveness) and utilizes serological outcomes.,yes,The study uses a cohort design with follow-up samples over 9 months.,yes,The study involves a human population in Nigeria.,no,"The study population consists entirely of adults. It does not include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",yes,"Participants born before 1980 would have received the smallpox vaccine during childhood, as routine vaccination ceased globally in 1980.",yes,The study focuses on residual immunity from historical smallpox vaccination.,Exclude,"The study investigates residual immunity from historical smallpox vaccination in a cohort of 176 Nigerian adults. However, it is excluded because it focuses exclusively on healthy adults and does not include children, adolescents, or pregnant women as required by the population criteria.","Population Focus, Target Population",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""176 Nigerian adults comprising of 75 healthcare workers sampled in 2021 and 101 community volunteers sampled in 2023""
