"Correlations between Humoral Immunity, Vaccination Period and Demographic Characteristics of First Immunized Smallpox Vaccine Recipients","Relevance. After vaccination against smallpox is completed, more than 50% of the population are not immune to pathogens of the genus Orthopoxvirus, which creates the risk of mass infection if the smallpox virus is used as a biological weapon. Aim. To assess the correlations between humoral immunity, clinical signs during a vaccination period and, sex and age characteristics of vaccinia virus Practical Aspects of Epidemiology and Vaccine Prevention (VCV) recipients. Materials and methods . We studied a vaccination clinical data of 82 subjects first vaccinated with a smallpox vaccine. Vaccinia neutralizing antibody titers were determined for 27 subjects using plaque reduction neutralization tests. According to antibody titers they were divided into groups of mild (25%), moderate (50%), and heavy reaction (25%). Results. A total of 34% and 66% vaccine recipients experienced mild or moderate vaccination period, respectively. Among them 83% vaccine recipients experienced or local and systemic adverse events. Compared to mild group, moderate group had larger scab, hyperemia (p &lt; 0,01) and pustules (p &lt; 0,05). It was a tendency that men had smaller scabs, more rapid scab shedding and less intense hyperemia compared to women. In contrast to low and high neutralizing antibody titers, medium ones were associated with smaller local inflammation (induration zones, hyperemia, pustules). Conclusion. The same vaccine and vaccination schedule may produce different protective neutralizing antibody levels and different humoral immunity in similar demographic groups. The underlying reasons need further research.","O. S. Ermilova, Z. I. Ghinko, V. Belyavskaya",10.31631/2073-3046-2020-19-1-77-82,https://doi.org/10.31631/2073-3046-2020-19-1-77-82,Epidemiology and Vaccinal Prevention,0,2020,maybe,"The study describes 82 subjects but does not explicitly state their age or pregnancy status. However, it does not state it is exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports on local and systemic adverse events following vaccination.,yes,This is a primary research study analyzing clinical data from a cohort of vaccine recipients.,yes,The study involves human subjects who received the vaccine.,maybe,"The abstract does not specify the age of the 82 subjects or if any were pregnant, neonates, children, or adolescents.",maybe,The timing of vaccination relative to age (childhood/adolescence) or pregnancy is not specified for the 82 subjects.,yes,The study involves first-time recipients of a smallpox vaccine (vaccinia virus).,Include,"The study reports on safety and immunological outcomes in 82 first-time smallpox vaccine recipients, but it fails to specify the age or pregnancy status of these participants, making it unclear if it meets the population-specific inclusion criteria.",,,3.4,"  - ""vaccination clinical data of 82 subjects first vaccinated with a smallpox vaccine.""",,"  - ""83% vaccine recipients experienced or local and systemic adverse events.""",,"  - ""vaccination clinical data of 82 subjects first vaccinated with a smallpox vaccine.""",,"  - ""vaccination clinical data of 82 subjects first vaccinated with a smallpox vaccine.""",,"  - ""82 subjects first vaccinated with a smallpox vaccine.""",,"  - ""82 subjects first vaccinated with a smallpox vaccine.""",,"  - ""82 subjects first vaccinated with a smallpox vaccine.""",
Preparedness and strategies for addressing monkeypox infection in pregnant women in India,"The Lancet Regional Health Southeast Asia 2022;5: 100066 https://doi.org/10.1016/j. lansea.2022.100066 As the world is just recovering from the impact of COVID-19, another threat is looming large. More than 26,864 cases of monkeypox (MPX) are reported from 88 countries; including 6 cases from India. Currently Europe is the epicenter of the outbreak contributing to nearly 50% of the global cases without any recent travel links to MPX endemic areas. Orthopoxvirus, the causative agent of MPX, results in a smallpox-like disease in humans. MPX in humans was initially reported in 1970 in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC) and subsequently in other regions, especially in West and Central Africa. Currently, there is no approved treatment for MPX. Smallpox vaccination was reported to provide approximately 85% protection against human MPX. After the eradication of smallpox, vaccination was discontinued in India and other countries in 1980. Pregnant or breastfeeding women and younger children are at increased risk of developing severe disease and adverse health outcomes associated with MPX. Children are more prone for MPX associated complications such as bacterial superinfection, cellulitis, sepsis, abscess, encephalitis, respiratory complications, pneumonia, keratitis, and corneal scarring leading to higher mortality and morbidity. Similarly, women of childbearing age constituting 22.2 % of the total Indian population are vulnerable to MPX and need special attention. The Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Government of India issued the guidelines for the management of MPX disease. However, these guidelines do not provide guidance on the management of pregnant women infected with the MPX virus. Based on experience with the PregCovid registry (https://pregco vid.com/), we suggest developing guidelines for the diagnosis and management of MPX in pregnant women and newborns in India. The obstetricians and","R. Gajbhiye, N. Mahajan, G. Sachdeva",10.1016/j.lansea.2022.100066,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.lansea.2022.100066,The Lancet Regional Health - Southeast Asia,3,2022,maybe,"The article discusses the vulnerability of pregnant women and children, but does not study a specific group of these individuals; it is a commentary suggesting policy.",no,"The study mentions historical vaccine effectiveness and complications in children, but it does not report primary outcomes from a study population.",no,"This is a commentary or perspective piece suggesting the development of guidelines, which is not a primary research study or evidence synthesis (systematic review).",no,"The paper discusses humans, but is not a study.",no,"The paper identifies pregnant women and children as the group needing guidance, but does not include them as research participants.",no,"The paper mentions historical vaccination cessation, but does not study participants receiving vaccines during these periods.",no,"Smallpox vaccination is mentioned historically, but no actual vaccine study is conducted.",Exclude,This article is a commentary recommending the development of clinical guidelines for pregnant women in India. It is not a primary research study or systematic review and does not include a study population or report new effectiveness/safety data.,"Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""The Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Government of India issued the guidelines for the management of MPX disease. However, these guidelines do not provide guidance on the management of pregnant women infected with the MPX virus.""
