The Monkeypox Outbreak 2022: Women and Children Patients.,"Between January 1, 2022 and July 4, 2022, the World Health Organization (WHO) received 6,027 reports of laboratory-confirmed patients of monkeypox, predominantly men, that had occurred during a multi-country outbreak of the virus. During this period, 21 women and six children were reportedly infected with the monkeypox virus. Although the infected number of women and children was small, these data are worrisome, because young children and pregnant women are at increased risk of severe outcomes from monkeypox. One infant already has been hospitalized in intensive care in London, England. The WHO data regarding the 2022 monkeypox outbreak also indicate that 25 healthcare workers have been diagnosed with monkeypox, although it is still being investigated whether their infection was due to occupational exposure. Three deaths from monkeypox have been reported to WHO during this monkeypox outbreak. A special focus of this presentation is on the monkeypox infection involving women and children. (SKINmed. 2022;20:334-335).",L. Hoenig,,,SKINmed Dermatology for the Clinician,0,2022,yes,The study focuses on 21 women and six children infected during the 2022 outbreak and focuses specifically on monkeypox infection in women and children.,no,"While the paper reports on outcomes of infection such as hospitalization and death, it does not report on vaccine-related safety or effectiveness as no vaccines are discussed.",no,The paper is a descriptive clinical commentary or news report summarizing WHO surveillance data rather than a primary research study or systematic review.,yes,The report describes laboratory-confirmed human patients.,yes,"The study explicitly includes information on children, an infant, and mentions pregnant women as a high-risk group.",no,There is no mention of vaccination status or timing for the individuals described.,no,The study describes the monkeypox outbreak and infection outcomes but does not mention any vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This paper is a clinical commentary summarizing WHO surveillance data regarding monkeypox infections in women and children. It fails several mandatory criteria as it contains no information regarding vaccines, vaccination timing, or vaccine-related safety and effectiveness outcomes.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""21 women and six children were reportedly infected""
