Assessment of safety in newborns of mothers participating in clinical trials of vaccines administered during pregnancy.,"A panel of experts convened by the Division of Microbiology and Infectious Diseases, National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, National Institutes of Health, developed proposed guidelines for the evaluation of adverse events in newborns of women participating in clinical trials of maternal immunization in the United States.","F. Muñoz, L. Weisman, J. Read, G. Siberry, K. Kotloff, Jennifer Friedman, R. Higgins, H. Hill, Harry Seifert, M. Nesin",10.1093/cid/ciu727,https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciu727,Clinical Infectious Diseases,16,2014,yes,"The guidelines discuss evaluation in newborns and women, which are populations other than healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"While the paper discusses the 'evaluation of adverse events', it does not report safety or effectiveness data for a specific study, but rather proposes methodology.",no,"The study was conducted to develop 'proposed guidelines' based on expert consensus, which does not constitute primary research (RCT, cohort, etc.) or a systematic review.",maybe,"The topic concerns maternal immunization in humans, though it is a guideline paper rather than a subject study.",yes,The paper focuses on the evaluation of 'newborns of women participating in clinical trials'.,maybe,"The focus is on 'maternal immunization', referring to vaccines administered during pregnancy, but no specific mpox data is present.",no,The paper discusses 'maternal immunization' in general and does not mention vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This document describes the development of expert guidelines for evaluating newborns in maternal immunization clinical trials. It is excluded because it is not a primary research study or systematic review, it does not report specific clinical outcomes, and it does not focus on mpox or vaccinia-based vaccines.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Va