The Impact of Monkeypox Virus Infection on Pregnancy Outcome: Clinical Features and Potential Pathogenic Mechanisms.,"Monkeypox has garnered significant attention following its emergence in multiple countries and its designation as a global health emergency in 2022. There is a notable deficiency in clinical and experimental data regarding pregnancy outcomes following monkeypox infection. The complications associated with monkeypox virus (MPXV) infection, including fetal miscarriage, preterm delivery, and congenital infections, have not been adequately addressed, and there is a lack of effective clinical interventions. Two clades of MPXV have been identified: clades I and II, with clade I being the predominant strain previously associated with adverse pregnancy outcomes. Clade IIb has also been implicated in potential vertical transmission from mother to child. Infection of a pregnant individual with MPXV can lead to fetal conditions, such as generalized diffuse herpes hydatidis and extensive hemorrhage within the placenta. MPXV is thought to evade the immune response primarily by inhibiting interferon (IFN)‐mediated antiviral effects, suppressing host immune recognition, dampening host inflammatory responses, and modulating cellular apoptosis. This review aims to provide a comprehensive overview of the clinical manifestations, potential routes of vertical transmission, and possible pathogenic mechanisms of MPXV infection in pregnant women, thereby serving as a valuable reference for future diagnostic, therapeutic, and pharmacological strategies in managing MPXV‐related pregnancy complications.","Jingyi Wu, Fangbin Huang, Qingliang Zheng",10.1155/jimr/5158686,https://doi.org/10.1155/jimr/5158686,Journal of Immunological Research,0,2026,yes,The study focuses specifically on pregnant individuals and the impact of infection on pregnancy outcomes.,no,"The paper discusses the outcomes of infection (e.g., miscarriage, fetal conditions) rather than the safety or effectiveness of vaccines.",no,"The paper identifies itself as a review aiming to provide a 'comprehensive overview' and notes a 'deficiency in clinical and experimental data', indicating it is a narrative review rather than primary research or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,"The study focuses on humans, specifically pregnant individuals and potential vertical transmission to children.",yes,The study is focused on pregnant women and potential outcomes for fetuses/infants.,no,There is no mention of vaccine administration during pregnancy or any other period; the focus is on infection.,no,The study does not involve or evaluate any vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,This paper is a narrative review that examines the clinical manifestations and pathogenic mechanisms of mpox infection in pregnant women. It does not meet the inclusion criteria because it does not investigate or report data on mpox or smallpox vaccines.,"Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""This review aims to provide a comprehensive overview of the clinical manifestations, potential routes of vertical transmission, and possible pathogenic mechanisms of MPXV infection in pregnant women,""
