Smallpox vaccination and all-cause infectious disease hospitalization: a Danish register-based cohort study.,"BACKGROUND
There is growing evidence from observational studies and randomized trials in low-income countries that vaccinations have non-specific effects. Administration of live vaccines reduces overall child morbidity and mortality, presumably due to protection against non-targeted infections. In Denmark, the live vaccine against smallpox was phased out in the 1970s due to the eradication of smallpox. We used the phasing-out period to investigate the effect of smallpox vaccination on the risk of hospitalization for infections.
METHODS
From the Copenhagen School Health Records Register, a cohort of 4048 individuals was sampled, of whom 3559 had information about receiving or not receiving smallpox vaccination. Infectious disease hospitalizations were identified in the Danish National Patient Register.
RESULTS
During 87,228 person-years of follow-up, 1440 infectious disease hospitalizations occurred. Smallpox-vaccinated individuals had a reduced risk of all-cause infectious disease hospitalization compared with smallpox-unvaccinated individuals [hazard ratio (HR) 0.84; 95% confidence interval (CI) 0.72-0.98]. The reduced risk of hospitalizations was seen for most subgroups of infectious diseases. The effect may have been most pronounced after early smallpox vaccination (vaccination age <3.5 years: HR 0.81; 95% CI 0.69-0.95; vaccination age ≥ 3.5 years: HR 0.91 95% CI 0.76-1.10). Among the smallpox-vaccinated, the risk of infectious disease hospitalization increased 6% with each 1-year increase in vaccination age (HR 1.06; 95% CI 1.02-1.10).
CONCLUSION
Smallpox vaccination is associated with a reduced risk of infectious disease hospitalization in a high-income setting.","S. Sørup, M. Villumsen, H. Ravn, C. Benn, T. Sørensen, P. Aaby, Tine Jess, A. Roth",10.1093/ije/dyr063,https://doi.org/10.1093/ije/dyr063,International Journal of Epidemiology,56,2011,yes,"The study is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults, as it includes individuals vaccinated as children.",no,"The study reports all-cause infectious disease hospitalizations but does not report mpox-specific outcomes (prevention of mpox infection, hospitalization, or death) nor safety outcomes like adverse events.",yes,The study is a register-based cohort study.,yes,The study is a human study using Danish registry data.,yes,"The study includes participants vaccinated during childhood, specifically mentioning ages less than 3.5 years.",yes,"Participants received the vaccine during childhood, with data stratified by vaccination ages such as <3.5 years.",yes,The study evaluates the historical live smallpox vaccine.,Exclude,"This cohort study investigates the non-specific protective effects of historical smallpox vaccination against all-cause infectious disease hospitalizations in a population vaccinated during childhood. Despite using the relevant vaccine and population, it is excluded because it does not report mpox-specific effectiveness outcomes or safety data.",Relevant Outcomes,Relevant Outcomes,0.1,"  - ""The effect may have been most pronounced after early smallpox vaccination (vaccination age <3.5 years: HR 0.81; 95% CI 0.69-0.95; vaccination age ≥ 3.5 years: HR 0.91 95% CI 0.76-1.10).""",,"  - ""Smallpox-vaccinated individuals had a reduced risk of all-cause infectious disease hospitalization compared with smallpox-unvaccinated individuals""",,"  - ""In Denmark, the live vaccine against smallpox was phased out in the 1970s due to the eradication of smallpox. We used the phasing-out period to investigate the effect of smallpox vaccination on the risk of hospitalization for infections.""",,"  - ""In Denmark, the live vaccine against smallpox was phased out in the 1970s due to the eradication of smallpox. We used the phasing-out period to investigate the effect of smallpox vaccination on the risk of hospitalization for infections.""
