RSV Prefusion F Protein-Based Maternal Vaccine - Preterm Birth and Other Outcomes.,"BACKGROUND
Vaccination against respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) during pregnancy may protect infants from RSV disease. Efficacy and safety data on a candidate RSV prefusion F protein-based maternal vaccine (RSVPreF3-Mat) are needed.
METHODS
We conducted a phase 3 trial involving pregnant women 18 to 49 years of age to assess the efficacy and safety of RSVPreF3-Mat. The women were randomly assigned in a 2:1 ratio to receive RSVPreF3-Mat or placebo between 24 weeks 0 days and 34 weeks 0 days of gestation. The primary outcomes were any or severe medically assessed RSV-associated lower respiratory tract disease in infants from birth to 6 months of age and safety in infants from birth to 12 months of age. After the observation of a higher risk of preterm birth in the vaccine group than in the placebo group, enrollment and vaccination were stopped early, and exploratory analyses of the safety signal of preterm birth were performed.
RESULTS
The analyses included 5328 pregnant women and 5233 infants; the target enrollment of approximately 10,000 pregnant women and their infants was not reached because enrollment was stopped early. A total of 3426 infants in the vaccine group and 1711 infants in the placebo group were followed from birth to 6 months of age; 16 and 24 infants, respectively, had any medically assessed RSV-associated lower respiratory tract disease (vaccine efficacy, 65.5%; 95% credible interval, 37.5 to 82.0), and 8 and 14, respectively, had severe medically assessed RSV-associated lower respiratory tract disease (vaccine efficacy, 69.0%; 95% credible interval, 33.0 to 87.6). Preterm birth occurred in 6.8% of the infants (237 of 3494) in the vaccine group and in 4.9% of those (86 of 1739) in the placebo group (relative risk, 1.37; 95% confidence interval [CI], 1.08 to 1.74; P = 0.01); neonatal death occurred in 0.4% (13 of 3494) and 0.2% (3 of 1739), respectively (relative risk, 2.16; 95% CI, 0.62 to 7.56; P = 0.23), an imbalance probably attributable to the greater percentage of preterm births in the vaccine group. No other safety signal was observed.
CONCLUSIONS
The results of this trial, in which enrollment was stopped early because of safety concerns, suggest that the risks of any and severe medically assessed RSV-associated lower respiratory tract disease among infants were lower with the candidate maternal RSV vaccine than with placebo but that the risk of preterm birth was higher with the candidate vaccine. (Funded by GlaxoSmithKline Biologicals; ClinicalTrials.gov number, NCT04605159.).","I. Dieussaert, Joon Hyung Kim, S. Luik, C. Seidl, W. Pu, Jens-Ulrich Stegmann, Geeta K Swamy, P. Webster, P. Dormitzer",10.1056/NEJMoa2305478,https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2305478,New England Journal of Medicine,96,2024,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women.,yes,The study reports on both effectiveness (prevention of RSV disease) and safety (preterm birth).,yes,The study is a phase 3 trial.,yes,The study involves a clinical trial with human participants (pregnant women and infants).,yes,The study includes pregnant women and infants.,yes,"Participants received the vaccine during pregnancy, specifically between 24 and 34 weeks of gestation.",no,"The study evaluates a Respiratory Syncytial Virus (RSV) prefusion F protein-based vaccine, not a vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This study is a phase 3 clinical trial evaluating the safety and efficacy of an RSV maternal vaccine in pregnant women. While it involves the correct target population and reports relevant safety outcomes like preterm birth, it does not evaluate mpox or smallpox vaccines, as required by the inclusion criteria.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""We conducted a phase 3 trial involving pregnant women 18 to 49 years of age""",,"  - ""primary outcomes were any or severe medically assessed RSV-associated lower respiratory tract disease in infants""
  - ""Preterm birth occurred in 6.8% of the infants (237 of 3494) in the vaccine group""",,"  - ""We conducted a phase 3 trial involving pregnant women 18 to 49 years of age""",,"  - ""We conducted a phase 3 trial involving pregnant women 18 to 49 years of age""",,"  - ""We conducted a phase 3 trial involving pregnant women 18 to 49 years of age""
  - ""primary outcomes were any or severe medically assessed RSV-associated lower respiratory tract disease in infants""",,"  - ""We conducted a phase 3 trial involving pregnant women 18 to 49 years of age""",,"  - ""Efficacy and safety data on a candidate RSV prefusion F protein-based maternal vaccine (RSVPreF3-Mat) are needed.""",
Safety and Efficacy of the Modified Vaccinia Ankara–Bavaria Nordic Vaccine Against Mpox in the Real World: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis,"In May 2022, mpox began to spread worldwide, posing a serious threat to human public health. Modified Vaccinia Ankara–Bavaria Nordic (MVA–BN) is a live attenuated orthopoxvirus vaccine that has been authorized by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration as the vaccine of choice for the prevention of mpox. In this study, we conducted a meta-analysis of all currently published literature on the efficacy and safety of the MVA–BN vaccine in the real world, showing that the MVA–BN vaccine is effective and safe, with efficacy of up to 75% with a single dose and up to 80% with a two-dose vaccine. Meanwhile, we found that subcutaneous injection has lower local and systemic adverse events than intradermal injection, regardless of single- or two-dose vaccination, and subcutaneous injection is better tolerated in children, the elderly, or people with underlying medical conditions. These results have important reference value for clinical practice.","Yi Pang, Demin Cao, Xiaoying Zhu, Qinqin Long, Fengqin Tian, Xidai Long, Yulei Li",10.1089/vim.2023.0147,https://doi.org/10.1089/vim.2023.0147,Viral immunology,8,2024,yes,"The study explicitly mentions results for children and people with underlying medical conditions, indicating it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study evaluates efficacy and safety (adverse events) of the vaccine.,yes,The study is a systematic review and meta-analysis.,yes,The study is a meta-analysis of literature regarding real-world human use in clinical practice.,yes,The study included data and provided results specifically mentioning children.,yes,"The study reports on the tolerability of the vaccine specifically in children, implying they received the vaccine during childhood.",yes,The study focuses on the MVA-BN (Modified Vaccinia Ankara-Bavaria Nordic) vaccine.,Include,"This systematic review and meta-analysis evaluates the safety and efficacy of the MVA-BN vaccine. It explicitly includes summary results for children, making it relevant to the target population and timing criteria.",,,5,"  - ""subcutaneous injection is better tolerated in children, the elderly, or people with underlying medical conditions.""",,"  - ""meta-analysis of all currently published literature on the efficacy and safety of the MVA–BN vaccine""",,,,"  - ""subcutaneous injection is better tolerated in children, the elderly, or people with underlying medical conditions.""
