"Epidemiologic and Clinical Features of Children and Adolescents Aged <18 Years with Monkeypox — United States, May 17–September 24, 2022","Data on monkeypox in children and adolescents aged <18 years are limited (1,2). During May 17–September 24, 2022, a total of 25,038 monkeypox cases were reported in the United States,<sup>†</sup> primarily among adult gay, bisexual, and other men who have sex with men (3). During this period, CDC and U.S. jurisdictional health departments identified Monkeypox virus (MPXV) infections in 83 persons aged <18 years, accounting for 0.3% of reported cases. Among 28 children aged 0–12 years with monkeypox, 64% were boys, and most had direct skin-to-skin contact with an adult with monkeypox who was caring for the child in a household setting. Among 55 adolescents aged 13–17 years, most were male (89%), and male-to-male sexual contact was the most common presumed exposure route (66%). Most children and adolescents with monkeypox were non-Hispanic Black or African American (Black) (47%) or Hispanic or Latino (Hispanic) (35%). Most (89%) were not hospitalized, none received intensive care unit (ICU)–level care, and none died. Monkeypox in children and adolescents remains rare in the United States. Ensuring equitable access to monkeypox vaccination, testing, and treatment is a critical public health priority. Vaccination for adolescents with risk factors and provision of prevention information for persons with monkeypox caring for children might prevent additional infections.","Ian P. Hennessee, Victoria S Shelus, Cristin E McArdle, Maren Wolf, Sabrina S. Schatzman, Ann Carpenter, F. Minhaj, Julia K Petras, Shama Cash-Goldwasser, Meghan Maloney, L. Sosa, Sydney A. Jones, A. Mangla, Rachel Harold, Jason Beverley, Katharine E. Saunders, Jeremy N. Adams, D. Stanek, Amanda L Feldpausch, Jessica Pavlick, M. Cahill, V. O’Dell, Moon-Jung Kim, J. Alarcón, L. Finn, Maura Goss, M. Duwell, David A. Crum, Thelonious W. Williams, K. Hansen, Megan Heddy, Krystle Mallory, Darby McDermott, M. K. Q. Cuadera, E. Adler, Ellen H. Lee, Amanda Shinall, C. Thomas, Erin K. Ricketts, Tammy Koonce, Dana B Rynk, Kelly Cogswell, Meagan McLafferty, Dana Perella, Catherine Stockdale, B. Dell, Mellisa Roskosky, S. White, K. Davis, Rania S. Milleron, Skyler Mackey, L. Barringer, H. Bruce, Debra Barrett, Marisa A. D'Angeli, A. Kocharian, R. Klos, P. Dawson, S. Ellington, Oren Mayer, Shana Godfred-Cato, Sarah M. Labuda, David W. Mccormick, A. McCollum, A. Rao, J. Salzer, Anne A. Kimball, Jeremy A. W. Gold, R. Berumen, G. Cosentino, Shiffen Getabecha, Carol A. Glaser, K. Grosgebauer, K. H. Harriman, Monica Haw, Amanda Kamali, Chantha Kath, Elissa Kim, Linda S. Lewis, Darpun D. Sachdev, Maria Salas, C. Stainken, D. Wadford, Philip Peters, Akanksha Vaidya, Susan Hocevar Adkins, ;. N. Baird, Lisa C. Barrios, A. Beeson, Dawn Blackburn, Brian F Borah, Eleanor Click, W. Davidson, R. Galang, Kaitlin Hufstetler, Helena J Hutchins, Athena P. Kourtis, Maureen J Miller, Sapna Bamrah Morris, Emily O Olsen, Nicole M Roth, Emily Sims, K. Chatham-Stephens",10.15585/mmwr.mm7144a4,https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm7144a4,MMWR. Morbidity and mortality weekly report,49,2022,yes,"The study focuses specifically on individuals aged <18 years, not healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports on hospitalization and death (effectiveness/severity outcomes) among the pediatric cases.,yes,The study is a descriptive epidemiological report/case series based on surveillance data.,yes,This is a study of human cases reported to the CDC.,yes,The study includes children aged 0-12 and adolescents aged 13-17.,maybe,"The paper discusses the importance of vaccination for these groups and mentions vaccination for adolescents, but it primarily describes infected cases. It does not explicitly state that these participants were vaccinated during the period of interest in a way that allows evaluation of vaccine safety or effectiveness based on the abstract alone.",maybe,"The study mentions 'monkeypox vaccination' and 'vaccination for adolescents', which in the 2022 US context likely refers to JYNNEOS (vaccinia-based), but specific vaccine names or historical smallpox status are not detailed in the abstract.",Include,"This study describes the epidemiology and outcomes of 83 children and adolescents with mpox in the United States. While it focuses on the correct target population and discusses the potential role of vaccination, the abstract does not provide specific data on outcomes following vaccination for these participants.",,,3.6,"  - ""CDC and U.S. jurisdictional health departments identified Monkeypox virus (MPXV) infections in 83 persons aged <18 years,""",,"  - ""Most (89%) were not hospitalized, none received intensive care unit (ICU)–level care, and none died.""",,"  - ""CDC and U.S. jurisdictional health departments identified Monkeypox virus (MPXV) infections in 83 persons aged <18 years,""",,"  - ""CDC and U.S. jurisdictional health departments identified Monkeypox virus (MPXV) infections in 83 persons aged <18 years,""",,"  - ""Among 28 children aged 0–12 years with monkeypox,""
