Coadministration of Cidofovir and Smallpox Vaccine Reduced Vaccination Side Effects but Interfered with Vaccine-Elicited Immune Responses and Immunity to Monkeypox,"ABSTRACT While the smallpox vaccine, Dryvax or Dryvax-derived ACAM2000, holds potential for public immunization against the spread of smallpox by bioterror, there is serious concern about Dryvax-mediated side effects. Here, we report that a single-dose vaccination regimen comprised of Dryvax and an antiviral agent, cidofovir, could reduce vaccinia viral loads after vaccination and significantly control Dryvax vaccination side effects. However, coadministration of cidofovir and Dryvax also reduced vaccine-elicited immune responses of antibody and T effector cells despite the fact that the reduced priming could be boosted as a recall response after monkeypox virus challenge. Evaluations of four different aspects of vaccine efficacy showed that coadministration of cidofovir and Dryvax compromised the Dryvax-induced immunity against monkeypox, although the covaccinated monkeys exhibited measurable protection against monkeypox compared to that of naïve controls. Thus, the single-dose coadministration of cidofovir and Dryvax effectively controlled vaccination side effects but significantly compromised vaccine-elicited immune responses and vaccine-induced immunity to monkeypox.","Huiyong Wei, Dan Huang, J. Fortman, Richard C. Wang, Linyun Shao, Zheng W. Chen",10.1128/JVI.00984-08,https://doi.org/10.1128/JVI.00984-08,Journal of Virology,38,2008,no,The study is an animal model involving monkeys rather than human subjects.,yes,The study reports side effects and vaccine-elicited immune responses against monkeypox.,yes,This is a primary research study involving an experimental vaccination regimen.,no,"The study is an animal laboratory study, explicitly stating the use of monkeys.",no,"The study does not include any human participants, thus missing the required target populations of pregnant women, neonates, or children.",no,"No human participants were involved, and thus vaccination did not occur during human pregnancy, infancy, or childhood.",yes,The study involves the vaccinia-based smallpox vaccine Dryvax.,Exclude,This is an animal study conducted on monkeys to evaluate the effects of cidofovir on Dryvax vaccination outcomes and immunity to monkeypox. It does not meet the eligibility criteria because it is not a human study and does not include the target populations of interest.,"Population Focus, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""covaccinated monkeys exhibited measurable protection against monkeypox""",,"  - ""single-dose coadministration of cidofovir and Dryvax effectively controlled vaccination side effects but significantly compromised vaccine-elicited immune responses""",,"  - ""single-dose coadministration of cidofovir and Dryvax effectively controlled vaccination side effects but significantly compromised vaccine-elicited immune responses""",,"  - ""covaccinated monkeys exhibited measurable protection against monkeypox""",,"  - ""covaccinated monkeys exhibited measurable protection against monkeypox""",,"  - ""covaccinated monkeys exhibited measurable protection against monkeypox""",,"  - ""While the smallpox vaccine, Dryvax or Dryvax-derived ACAM2000,""",
"Modeling a safer smallpox vaccination regimen, for human immunodeficiency virus type 1-infected patients, in immunocompromised macaques.","We have modeled smallpox vaccination with Dryvax (Wyeth) in rhesus macaques that had depletion of CD4(+) T cells induced by infection with simian immunodeficiency virus or simian/human immunodeficiency virus. Smallpox vaccination induced significantly larger skin lesions in immunocompromised macaques than in healthy macaques. Unexpectedly, ""progressive vaccinia"" was infrequent. Vaccination of immunocompromised macaques with the genetically-engineered, replication-deficient poxvirus NYVAC, before or after retrovirus infection, was safe and lessened the severity of Dryvax-induced skin lesions. Neutralizing antibodies to vaccinia were induced by NYVAC, even in macaques with severe CD4(+) T cell depletion, and their titers inversely correlated with the time to complete resolution of the skin lesions. Together, these results provide the proof of concept, in macaque models that mirror human immunodeficiency virus type 1 infection, that a prime-boost approach with a highly attenuated poxvirus followed by Dryvax increases the safety of smallpox vaccination, and they highlight the importance of neutralizing antibodies in protection against virulent poxvirus.","Y. Edghill-Smith, D. Venzon, T. Karpova, James G. McNally, J. Nacsa, W. Tsai, E. Tryniszewska, M. Moniuszko, J. Manischewitz, L. King, Steven J Snodgrass, J. Parrish, P. Markham, Marsha Sowers, D. Martin, M. Lewis, J. Berzofsky, I. Belyakov, B. Moss, J. Tartaglia, M. Bray, V. Hirsch, H. Golding, G. Franchini",10.1086/378518,https://doi.org/10.1086/378518,Journal of Infectious Diseases,42,2003,no,"The study population consists entirely of rhesus macaques, not humans.",yes,The study reports on safety outcomes such as skin lesions and immune responses.,yes,This is a primary research study involving experimental modeling of vaccination.,no,The study is an animal model using rhesus macaques and is not a study involving human participants.,no,"The study does not involve human participants, and therefore does not include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Vaccination was administered to macaques, not humans during the specified life stages.",yes,"The study uses Dryvax and NYVAC, which are historical or vaccinia-based smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study is an animal model using rhesus macaques rather than human participants. Because it is not a human study and does not include the target populations (e.g., pregnant women, children), it fails several mandatory criteria.","Population Focus, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""We have modeled smallpox vaccination with Dryvax (Wyeth) in rhesus macaques""",,"  - ""Smallpox vaccination induced significantly larger skin lesions in immunocompromised macaques than in healthy macaques.""",,"  - ""We have modeled smallpox vaccination with Dryvax (Wyeth) in rhesus macaques""",,"  - ""We have modeled smallpox vaccination with Dryvax (Wyeth) in rhesus macaques""
