Monkeypox in pregnancy: update on current outbreak,"The monkeypox case count in the current global outbreak surpassed 52 000 on Sept 1, 2022. Community transmission is affecting people considered to be at high risk of severe disease, including pregnant women and neonates, albeit in small numbers so far. As of Sept 2, 2022, ten cases of monkeypox in pregnant women have been reported worldwide, mostly via local news media rather than medical or public health publications, with the first case reported in the USA on July 23, 2022. Based on available information, vertical transmission did not occur; the neonate received prophylactic vaccinia immunoglobulin and did not develop monkeypox disease. On Aug 4, 2022, the Government of São Paulo, Brazil, announced that two pregnant women had been diagnosed with monkeypox and Fondazione Centro Studi Allineare Sanità e Salute, 20131 Milan, Italy","A. Khalil, A. Samara, P. O’brien, C. Coutinho, G. Duarte, S. M. Quintana, S. Ladhani",10.1016/S1473-3099(22)00612-0,https://doi.org/10.1016/S1473-3099(22)00612-0,Lancet. Infectious Diseases (Print),24,2022,yes,The study specifically focuses on pregnant women and neonates.,no,"The study reports on the prevention of mpox in a neonate using vaccinia immunoglobulin, but does not report outcomes related to vaccination.",yes,The study is a brief update or case series report on monkeypox cases in pregnancy.,yes,The study reports on human clinical cases.,yes,The participants are explicitly pregnant women and neonates.,no,The study does not describe individuals who were vaccinated during pregnancy or childhood; it only discusses the administration of vaccinia immunoglobulin.,no,"The study mentions 'vaccinia immunoglobulin', which is an antibody treatment for passive immunization, not a vaccinia-based vaccine or historical smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"While the study focuses on the target population of pregnant women and neonates, it evaluates the use of vaccinia immunoglobulin rather than a vaccine. Since no vaccinia-based vaccines were administered to the population, the study fails the intervention-related criteria.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccination Timing,0.1,"  - ""including pregnant women and neonates,""",,"  - ""the neonate received prophylactic vaccinia immunoglobulin and did not develop monkeypox disease.""",,"  - ""ten cases of monkeypox in pregnant women have been reported worldwide,""",,"  - ""ten cases of monkeypox in pregnant women have been reported worldwide,""",,"  - ""including pregnant women and neonates,""",,"  - ""the neonate received prophylactic vaccinia immunoglobulin and did not develop monkeypox disease.""",,"  - ""the neonate received prophylactic vaccinia immunoglobulin and did not develop monkeypox disease.""",
"Monitoring and Characteristics of Mpox Contacts, Virginia, USA, May–November 2022","During 2022, a global outbreak of mpox resulted primarily from human-to-human contact. The Virginia Department of Health (Richmond, VA, USA) implemented a contact tracing and symptom monitoring system for residents exposed to monkeypox virus, assessed their risk for infection, and offered interventions as needed. Among 991 contacts identified during May 1–November 1, 2022, import records were complete for 943 (95.2%), but 99 (10.0%) were not available for follow-up during symptom monitoring. Mpox developed in 28 (2.8%) persons; none were healthcare workers exposed at work (n = 275). Exposure risk category and likelihood of developing mpox were strongly associated. A total of 333 persons received >1 dose of JYENNOS (Bavarian Nordic, https://www.bavarian-nordic.com) vaccine, most (n = 295) administered after virus exposure. Median time from exposure to vaccination was 8 days. Those data tools provided crucial real-time information for public health responses and can be used as a framework for other emerging diseases.","Eleanor N. Field, E. McCarty, Dawn Saady, Brandy Darby",10.3201/eid3003.230609,https://doi.org/10.3201/eid3003.230609,Emerging Infectious Diseases,0,2024,maybe,"The study describes 'residents' and 'contacts', but does not explicitly state the exclusion or inclusion of healthy versus non-healthy individuals or whether it includes non-adults.",yes,"The study reports on the development of mpox after exposure and vaccination, which is an effectiveness outcome.",yes,The study is a primary research report describing a contact tracing and monitoring system.,yes,The study involves human participants identified through contact tracing in Virginia.,no,"The paper does not mention children, adolescents, or pregnant women; it only refers broadly to 'residents' and '991 contacts'.",no,"Although 333 people were vaccinated, there is no evidence that vaccination occurred during pregnancy, childhood, or adolescence as these groups are not mentioned.",yes,The study explicitly mentions the use of the JYNNEOS vaccine.,Exclude,"This study monitors 991 human contacts for mpox infection following exposure, but it fails to meet inclusion criteria because it does not explicitly include or analyze the required target populations of children, adolescents, or pregnant women. While effectiveness outcomes and vaccination with JYNNEOS are discussed, the lack of a specified focus on the required vulnerable populations results in exclusion.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""implemented a contact tracing and symptom monitoring system for residents exposed to monkeypox virus,""
