Use of tecovirimat for mpox infection followed by JYNNEOS vaccination postinfection in a liver transplant recipient,"In May 2022, an mpox (monkeypox) outbreak was recognized outside the regions of established endemicity of Central and West Africa.1 By July 2022, this outbreak was deemed a public health emergency of international concern by the World Health Organization.2 As of November 2022, there have been over 29 000 confirmed cases and 14 deaths related to monkeypox virus (MPXV) infection,3 with risk of severe disease and mortality described in those with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS).4 To date, there has been one published case ofmpox in a kidney transplant recipient treatedwith tecovirimat.5 Herein, we describe the clinical course and management of a liver transplant recipient with mpox treated with tecovirimat followed by intradermal JYNNEOS vaccination after resolution of symptoms.","Jae Hyung Jung, Eric Bhaimia, N. Reau",10.1111/tid.14030,https://doi.org/10.1111/tid.14030,Transplant Infectious Disease,2,2023,yes,"The study focuses on a liver transplant recipient. While the age is not explicitly stated in the provided abstract snippet, there is no indication that the study is 'exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults' as the participant is a transplant patient with mpox infection.",yes,"The study describes the clinical course and management, which usually includes safety/effectiveness outcomes in a clinical case report scenario.",yes,"The text states 'we describe the clinical course', which classifies this as a case report, a primary research study design.",yes,The study involves a human liver transplant recipient.,no,"The paper does not mention that the liver transplant recipient is a pregnant woman, neonate, child, or adolescent. There is no evidence of these specific populations being included.",no,"There is no information suggested that the participant received the vaccine during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence, as the vaccine was given post-infection to a transplant recipient.",yes,"The study involves the JYNNEOS vaccine, which is a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This case report describes the clinical course of a liver transplant recipient who received the JYNNEOS vaccine. However, it does not involve the required target populations of pregnant women, neonates, or children/adolescents, failing the primary population inclusion criteria.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""we describe the clinical course and management of a liver transplant recipient""",,"  - ""we describe the clinical course and management of a liver transplant recipient""",,"  - ""we describe the clinical course and management of a liver transplant recipient""",,"  - ""we describe the clinical course and management of a liver transplant recipient""",,"  - ""we describe the clinical course and management of a liver transplant recipient""",,"  - ""we describe the clinical course and management of a liver transplant recipient""
