Personalized immunization against Mpox Clades I and Ib: Strategies to combat the emerging epidemic,"Highlight • MVA-BN (JYNNEOS) vaccine reduced infection by 65% in high-risk patients within 7 days and 79% by day 14.• The WHO recommends LC16-KMB for high-risk groups in Mpox-affected areas, suitable for pre- and post-exposure use.• Comparative studies show MVA-BN as the leading U.S. vaccine with strong efficacy and minimal side effects.• The OrthopoxVac, a fourth-generation vaccine, offers expanded protection against orthopoxvirus infections and it is used in Russia.• The SMART clinical trial in Africa aims to assess the efficacy of MVA-BN for post-exposure prophylaxis, particularly in household exposures.","Deepak Kumar, Rishabha Malviya, Shriyansh Srivastava, S. Sridhar, S. Talath, J. Shareef, Bhupendra G. Prajapati",10.1016/j.imj.2025.100166,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.imj.2025.100166,Infectious Medicine,4,2025,maybe,"The paper discusses 'high-risk patients' and 'high-risk groups', which generally excludes healthy populations, but it does not specify if these include only adults.",yes,The paper reports on vaccine efficacy (65-79% reduction in infection) and side effects.,no,The structure and content (discussing strategies and external clinical trials like SMART) indicate this is a narrative review or commentary rather than primary research or a systematic review.,yes,The study discusses vaccination in human populations and clinical trial outcomes.,no,"The paper does not mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as study participants or specific focus groups.",no,"There is no information provided regarding vaccination during pregnancy, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,"The paper focuses on MVA-BN (JYNNEOS) and LC16-KMB, which are vaccinia-based vaccines.",Exclude,"This paper is a review of Mpox vaccination strategies and does not constitute a primary research study or a systematic review. It fails to meet the include population criteria as it does not