Safety of Administering Live Vaccines during Pregnancy: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of Pregnancy Outcomes,"Live-attenuated vaccines (LAV) are currently contraindicated during pregnancy, given uncertain safety records for the mother–infant pair. LAV might, however, play an important role to protect them against serious emerging diseases, such as Ebola and Lassa fever. For this systematic review we searched relevant databases to identify studies published up to November 2019. Controlled observational studies reporting pregnancy outcomes after maternal immunization with LAV were included. The ROBINS-I tool was used to assess risk of bias. Pooled odds ratios (OR) were obtained under a random-effects model. Of 2831 studies identified, fifteen fulfilled inclusion criteria. Smallpox, rubella, poliovirus, yellow fever and dengue vaccines were assessed in these studies. No association was found between vaccination and miscarriage (OR 0.98, 95% CI 0.87–1.10), stillbirth (OR 1.04, 95% CI 0.74–1.48), malformations (OR 1.09, 95% CI 0.98–1.21), prematurity (OR 0.99, 95% CI 0.90–1.08) or neonatal death (OR 1.06, 95% CI 0.68–1.65) overall. However, increased odds of malformations (OR 1.24; 95% CI 1.03–1.49) and miscarriage after first trimester immunization (OR 4.82; 95% CI 2.38–9.77) was found for smallpox vaccine. Thus, we did not find evidence of harm related to LAV other than smallpox with regards to pregnancy outcomes, but quality of evidence was very low. Overall risks appear to be small and have to be balanced against potential benefits for the mother-infant pair.","Almudena Laris-González, Daniel Bernal-Serrano, A. Jarde, B. Kampmann",10.3390/vaccines8010124,https://doi.org/10.3390/vaccines8010124,Vaccines,30,2020,yes,"The study focuses specifically on pregnant women and their infants, not just healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study evaluates safety outcomes including miscarriage, stillbirth, malformations, and neonatal death.",yes,The title and content identify this study as a systematic review and meta-analysis.,yes,The review investigates human maternal immunization and pregnancy outcomes.,yes,The study methodology and results focus on maternal immunization (pregnant women) and outcomes for the neonate/infant.,yes,The inclusion criteria specifically look for studies where maternal immunization occurred during pregnancy.,yes,Smallpox vaccines were specifically assessed within the review.,Include,This is a systematic review and meta-analysis evaluating the safety of vaccinia-based smallpox vaccines when administered during pregnancy. It provides data on critical safety outcomes such as miscarriage and congenital malformations for the maternal-infant population.,,,5,"  - ""Controlled observational studies reporting pregnancy outcomes after maternal immunization with LAV were included.""",,"  - ""No association was found between vaccination and miscarriage (OR 0.98, 95% CI 0.87–1.10), stillbirth (OR 1.04, 95% CI 0.74–1.48), malformations (OR 1.09, 95% CI 0.98–1.21), prematurity (OR 0.99, 95% CI 0.90–1.08) or neonatal death (OR 1.06, 95% CI 0.68–1.65) overall.""
  - ""increased odds of malformations (OR 1.24; 95% CI 1.03–1.49) and miscarriage after first trimester immunization (OR 4.82; 95% CI 2.38–9.77) was found for smallpox vaccine.""",,,,"  - ""Controlled observational studies reporting pregnancy outcomes after maternal immunization with LAV were included.""",,"  - ""Controlled observational studies reporting pregnancy outcomes after maternal immunization with LAV were included.""
