COVID-19 INFECTION AND VACCINATION DURING PREGNANCY AND RISK OF ADVERSE NEURODEVELOPMENT IN EARLY CHILDHOOD: RESULTS FROM THE ASPIRE STUDY,"Inflammation during pregnancy is associated with adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes in offspring. It is unknown whether COVID-19 infection, a disease characterized by a prominent inflammatory response, or vaccination against COVID-19, during pregnancy, are risk factors for neurodevelopmental impairment in early childhood. 7,880 pregnant women at <10 weeks gestation enrolled in a prospective cohort, Assessing the Safety of Pregnancy In the coronaviRus pandEmic (ASPIRE), collected frequent at-home blood spots for serology, and completed questionnaires throughout pregnancy and postpartum, following their infant(s) as they grew. Two primary exposures were examined: COVID-19 infection during pregnancy and COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy. The primary outcome was neurodevelopmental delay, indicated by a score <2 standard deviations below the mean in at least one of five key domains on the validated Ages and Stages Questionnaire (ASQ-3), completed by parents at 6, 12, and 18 months of age. Mixed effects logistic regression models were used, adjusted for covariates determined a priori including maternal age, race, education, income, anxiety and depression. 1,914 participants had complete data to ascertain key exposure and outcome status. The incidence of neurodevelopmental delay at 18 months was 11.9% among offspring exposed to COVID-19 infection in utero (N=150) versus 12.9% among those not exposed (N=1648) (aOR 0.88, 95% CI 0.43-1.83). With regards to vaccination, the incidence of neurodevelopmental delay at 18 months was 12.5% among offspring exposed to COVID-19 vaccination in utero (N=1316) versus 14.2% among those not exposed (N=454) (aOR 0.99, 95% CI 0.65-1.51). In this prospective cohort study, neither maternal COVID-19 infection nor COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy were associated with increased risk of offspring developmental delay up to 18 months of life. Follow-up studies examining the potential existence of critical windows during pregnancy and possible interactions between vaccination and infections are being conducted.","E. Jaswa, H. Huddleston, Karla J Lindquist, S. Gaw, Jamie Corley, Elena Hoskin, Marcelle I. Cedars",10.1016/j.fertnstert.2023.08.492,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fertnstert.2023.08.492,Fertility and Sterility,0,2023,yes,The study focuses specifically on pregnant women and their offspring.,yes,The study reports on the safety outcome of neurodevelopmental delay.,yes,This is a prospective cohort study.,yes,The study involved human participants (pregnant women and infants).,yes,The participants were pregnant women followed through pregnancy and postpartum.,yes,The vaccination exposure occurred during pregnancy.,no,"The study evaluates COVID-19 vaccination, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"The study is a prospective cohort investigation focusing on the safety of COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy. While it includes the target population of pregnant women, it fails the eligibility criteria because it examines COVID-19 vaccines rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""7,880 pregnant women at <10 weeks gestation enrolled in a prospective cohort,""",,"  - ""primary outcome was neurodevelopmental delay,""",,"  - ""7,880 pregnant women at <10 weeks gestation enrolled in a prospective cohort,""",,"  - ""7,880 pregnant women at <10 weeks gestation enrolled in a prospective cohort,""",,"  - ""7,880 pregnant women at <10 weeks gestation enrolled in a prospective cohort,""",,"  - ""Two primary exposures were examined: COVID-19 infection during pregnancy and COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy.""",,"  - ""Two primary exposures were examined: COVID-19 infection during pregnancy and COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy.""
  - ""In this prospective cohort study, neither maternal COVID-19 infection nor COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy""",
"For today, tomorrow, and the days to come","Around 89,000 Monkeypox (Mpox) cases have been registered worldwide since the beginning of the current outbreak. FDA and EMA approved a live attenuated, non-replicating vaccine (MVA-BN) for groups at risk, like men who have sex with men (MSM), people living with HIV (PLWH), sex workers, laboratory, and healthcare workers. We provide an overview of the uptake of mpox vaccination among PLWH in a single center in Italy and explore the nuances of its tolerability compared to individuals without HIV. We retrospectively collected routine data of people vaccinated with intradermal MVA-BN in a tertiary-level hospital in Florence, Italy, from September 15th to December 31st, 2022. Data were collected from standard pre-vaccination screening questionnaires. The data on adverse events were collected just before the second dose through a dedicated questionnaire","A. Biagio, Clinica Malattie Infettive, Giuseppe Formica1, Samuele Gaggioli1, Valentina Petrini1, Paola Corsi2, Alessandro Di Felice1, Costanza Fiorelli2, Costanza Malcontenti2, Emanuela Francalanci1, Giulia Modi1, Michele Trotta2, Seble Tekle Kiros1, Elena Gazzarri3, Irene Macrì3, Nicoletta Zocco3, L. Zammarchi, A. Bartoloni, Filippo Lagi2, F. Lagi, E. Lattuada, Andrea Delama, D. Fait, Giovanni Mori, Martina Gottardi, L. Guella, M. Danzi, M. Lanzafame, Petrocelli Paola, Cunsolo Alessandra1, Leonildi Veronica1, Moneta Alessandro2, Sara3 Nardone, Rapi Maria1, Stefano1, Veronica Cunsolo",,,,0,2023,yes,"The study includes people living with HIV (PLWH), who are not considered healthy adults.",yes,The study reports on tolerability and adverse events.,yes,"This is a retrospectively collected routine data study, falling under observational primary research.",yes,The study accounts for human individuals vaccinated in a hospital setting.,no,"The study population is exclusively focused on adult risk groups such as MSM, PLWH, and healthcare workers. There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,The study describes risk-based vaccination of adults in late 2022 rather than vaccination during pregnancy or childhood.,yes,"The vaccine used was MVA-BN, which is a live attenuated vaccinia-based vaccine.",Exclude,"This study evaluated the tolerability of the MVA-BN vaccine in people living with HIV and those without HIV in Italy. Although it assesses a relevant vaccine type and outcomes, it does not include the target populations of children, adolescents, or pregnant women.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""EMA approved a live attenuated, non-replicating vaccine (MVA-BN) for groups at risk, like men who have sex with men (MSM), people living with HIV (PLWH), sex workers, laboratory, and healthcare workers.""",,"  - ""explore the nuances of its tolerability compared to individuals without HIV.""
  - ""The data on adverse events were collected just before the second dose through a dedicated questionnaire""",,"  - ""retrospectively collected routine data of people vaccinated with intradermal MVA-BN in a tertiary-level hospital in Florence, Italy, from September 15th to December 31st, 2022.""",,"  - ""retrospectively collected routine data of people vaccinated with intradermal MVA-BN in a tertiary-level hospital in Florence, Italy, from September 15th to December 31st, 2022.""",,"  - ""EMA approved a live attenuated, non-replicating vaccine (MVA-BN) for groups at risk, like men who have sex with men (MSM), people living with HIV (PLWH), sex workers, laboratory, and healthcare workers.""",,"  - ""EMA approved a live attenuated, non-replicating vaccine (MVA-BN) for groups at risk, like men who have sex with men (MSM), people living with HIV (PLWH), sex workers, laboratory, and healthcare workers.""
