Adverse Reactions After Intradermal Vaccination With JYNNEOS for Mpox in Korea,"In response to the Mpox domestic epidemic, South Korea initiated a nationwide vaccination program in May 2023, administering a 0.1 mL intradermal dose of JYNNEOS (Modified Vaccinia Ankara vaccine, Bavarian Nordic) to a high-risk group. To investigate the adverse reactions after intradermal JYNNEOS vaccination, an anonymous online survey was conducted at the National Medical Center from May 22 to July 31, 2023. Overall, 142 individuals responded. Over 80% of the respondents reported local reactions of predominantly mild severity. The predominant local reactions were pruritus, redness, and swelling; their incidence rates after the first dose were 66.2%, 48.1%, and 49.4%, respectively; the corresponding rates after the second dose were 69.2%, 60.6%, and 53.8%. Fewer respondents reported systemic symptoms. The most common systemic symptom was fatigue, the incidence rates of which after the first and second doses were 37.7% and 24.6%, respectively. Overall, the intradermally administered JYNNEOS vaccine appeared well tolerated.","So Yun Lim, Y. Jung, Yeonjae Kim, Gayeon Kim, Jaehyun Jeon, BumSik Chin, Min-Kyung Kim",10.3346/jkms.2024.39.e100,https://doi.org/10.3346/jkms.2024.39.e100,Journal of Korean medical science,5,2024,maybe,"The study focuses on a 'high-risk group' and does not explicitly exclude or limit the study to healthy non-pregnant adults, though it does not describe the presence of special populations like pregnant women or children in the abstract.",yes,The study specifically investigates 'adverse reactions' including local reactions like pruritus and swelling and systemic symptoms like fatigue.,yes,"The study is a primary research study, specifically a survey-based cohort study/case series of vaccine recipients.",yes,The study involves 142 human respondents.,no,"The paper does not state that it includes pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; the 'high-risk group' in the context of the mpox epidemic in Korea usually refers to MSM (men who have sex with men) adults. There is no evidence of the inclusion of the required populations.",no,"There is no evidence that vaccination occurred during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The study uses JYNNEOS (Modified Vaccinia Ankara vaccine).,Exclude,"The study investigates adverse reactions to JYNNEOS intradermal vaccination in a high-risk group in South Korea. However, it does not confirm the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, or children, which are the required target populations. Therefore, it fails the population and timing criteria.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""administering a 0.1 mL intradermal dose of JYNNEOS (Modified Vaccinia Ankara vaccine, Bavarian Nordic) to a high-risk group.""",,"  - ""To investigate the adverse reactions after intradermal JYNNEOS vaccination, an anonymous online survey was conducted""
  - ""predominant local reactions were pruritus, redness, and swelling;""
  - ""most common systemic symptom was fatigue,""",,"  - ""To investigate the adverse reactions after intradermal JYNNEOS vaccination, an anonymous online survey was conducted""
  - ""Overall, 142 individuals responded.""",,"  - ""Overall, 142 individuals responded.""",,"  - ""administering a 0.1 mL intradermal dose of JYNNEOS (Modified Vaccinia Ankara vaccine, Bavarian Nordic) to a high-risk group.""",,"  - ""administering a 0.1 mL intradermal dose of JYNNEOS (Modified Vaccinia Ankara vaccine, Bavarian Nordic) to a high-risk group.""",,"  - ""administering a 0.1 mL intradermal dose of JYNNEOS (Modified Vaccinia Ankara vaccine, Bavarian Nordic) to a high-risk group.""",
Old Enemy with a New Face: Re-emerging Monkeypox Disease – An Update,"Human monkeypox (MPX), a multi-country re-emerging disease, is rapidly spreading around the world. The etiological agent of this disease, Monkeypox virus (MPXV), is a DNA virus classified into three genetic types (West Africa, Congo Basin clade, and one new clade-3). Atypical or unusual symptoms as well as asymptomatic infection of MPXV has also been reported. Transmission among humans is possible by droplets, contact, sexual intercourse, and fomites. Secondary transmission of this disease has been reported to occur in less than 10% of cases where it was found 35%–88% of smallpox. Mother-to-fetus transmission by vertical route is also possible for this disease. Modern equipment, biosafety level-3 laboratory facilities, and trained expert persons are needed to diagnose this disease. Previous data support that ~85% clinical protection is provided by smallpox vaccines for monkeypox, although initially non-human primates models were used for various experiments, and also side-effects of this vaccine have been notably mentioned in various studies. Limited research findings of JYNNEOS vaccine has supported the comparatively lower prevalence of MPX cases with vaccination. Few drugs, including cidofovir, tecovirimat, brincidofovir, and vaccinia immune globulin intravenous are preferable against this disease, although clinical trial data is limited and FDA-approval is also pending. This review-based study presents an overall scenario of Monkeypox disease (MPXD) based on previously published studies. Recommended clinical treatment and vaccination, appropriate infection prevention and control strategies, adopting one health approach, and quick identification of hotspots using a wastewater-based surveillance system need to be followed to check the further spread of MPX outbreaks.","Md. Aminul Islam, M. K. Hemo, Hitesh Chopra, M. R. Amin, Prosun Bhattacharya, K. Dhama",10.22207/jpam.16.spl1.18,https://doi.org/10.22207/jpam.16.spl1.18,Journal of Pure and Applied Microbiology,14,2022,yes,"The paper discusses general human monkeypox and mentions vertical transmission from mother to fetus, indicating it is not exclusively focused on healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study mentions clinical protection (~85%) and side effects of vaccines, as well as lower prevalence of cases with the JYNNEOS vaccine.",no,"The article explicitly defines itself as a 'review-based study' based on 'previously published studies'. It is a narrative review, not a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,The paper provides an update on human monkeypox disease.,no,"While the paper mentions vertical transmission, it does not include pregnant women, neonates, or children as participants in a clinical study; it merely reviews existing literature.",no,"The paper summarizes general vaccine protection data and does not track a cohort of individuals vaccinated during pregnancy, childhood, or the neonatal period.",yes,The study discusses historical smallpox vaccines and the JYNNEOS vaccine.,Exclude,"This is a narrative review-based study providing a general update on monkeypox based on previously published research. It fails the inclusion criteria as it is not a primary research study nor a systematic review, and it does not include a specific participant cohort of pregnant women, neonates, or children.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""Human monkeypox (MPX), a multi-country re-emerging disease, is rapidly spreading around the world.""
  - ""Mother-to-fetus transmission by vertical route is also possible for this disease.""",,"  - ""Previous data support that ~85% clinical protection is provided by smallpox vaccines for monkeypox, although initially non-human primates models were used for various experiments, and also side-effects of this vaccine have been notably mentioned in various studies.""
  - ""Limited research findings of JYNNEOS vaccine has supported the comparatively lower prevalence of MPX cases with vaccination.""",,"  - ""This review-based study presents an overall scenario of Monkeypox disease (MPXD) based on previously published studies.""",,"  - ""Human monkeypox (MPX), a multi-country re-emerging disease, is rapidly spreading around the world.""",,"  - ""Mother-to-fetus transmission by vertical route is also possible for this disease.""
