OC05.09: Effects of Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus‐2 (SARS‐CoV‐2) infection on fetal cortical development,"this control group. The primary endpoint was the proportion of positive CMV PCR on amniotic ﬂuid. Results: Between October 2019 and March 2022, 122 patients received VACV. MPI occurred during the ﬁrst trimester (T1) and the preconceptional period in 62% (76/122) and 38% (46/122) of the cases respectively. The mean terms at VACV introduction and AC were 12.0 + /-2.9. and 17.7 + /-1.0 WG respectively. In the control group (n = 205), the proportion of T1 and preconceptional MPI were 74% (151/205) and 26% (54/205) respectively. The mean term at AC was 19.9 + /- 1.3 WG. For T1 MPI, the proportions of CMV-positive PCR in the VACV and control groups were 11.8% and 32.8% (p = 0.0076) respectively and for preconceptional MPI these were 4.3 % and 9.3% (p = 0.4555). An odds ratio of 0.36 (95%CI = 0.17 to 0.78) was demonstrated for vertical transmission after T1 MPI. Conclusions: In case of MPI in T1, VACV reduced the rate of fetal infection by 64%. VACV must be started close to the date of MPI to be signiﬁcantly effective, which calls for and universal early screening of cCMV infection.","P. Maqina, M. Pietrolucci, M. Narcisi, J. Lu, I. Mappa, G. Rizzo",10.1002/uog.25026,https://doi.org/10.1002/uog.25026,Ultrasound in Obstetrics and Gynecology,0,2022,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women and is not limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.,no,The study reports on CMV PCR results and vertical transmission of CMV rather than safety or effectiveness outcomes for mpox.,yes,The study is primary research comparing a treatment group (VACV) to a control group.,yes,The study involves human clinical participants (pregnant women).,yes,The participants are pregnant women identified by gestational age (WG).,no,"Although the patients received 'VACV' during pregnancy, in this context it refers to Valacyclovir as an antiviral treatment, not a vaccine.",no,The study evaluates Valacyclovir (VACV) for reducing CMV vertical transmission. It does not involve vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This study evaluates the effectiveness of Valacyclovir (abbreviated in the text as VACV) in preventing the vertical transmission of Cytomegalovirus (CMV) in pregnant women. It is excluded because it does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccines, nor does it report on relevant mpox outcomes.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""122 patients received VACV. MPI occurred during the ﬁrst trimester (T1) and the preconceptional period""",,"  - ""The primary endpoint was the proportion of positive CMV PCR on amniotic ﬂuid.""
