"Mpox Awareness, Risk Reduction, and Vaccine Acceptance among People with HIV in Washington, DC","People with HIV (PWH) are disproportionally affected by mpox and at risk of severe complications. We assessed mpox knowledge, adoption of preventive behaviors, and vaccination attitudes among PWH enrolled in a longitudinal HIV cohort in Washington, DC, the DC Cohort. We conducted uni- and multivariable analyses comparing participants by vaccination status and HIV risk group, and multinomial regression to identify factors associated with vaccine acceptance. Among 430 PWH, 378 (87.9%) were aware of mpox. Among 373 participants with vaccination status data, 101 (27.1%) were vaccinated, 129 (34.6%) planned to vaccinate, and 143 (38.3%) did not plan to vaccinate. The three vaccination groups differed significantly by age, race, education, HIV risk group, recent STI status, and level of mpox worry (all p < 0.05). A higher proportion of men who have sex with men (MSM) reported limiting their number of sexual partners compared to non-MSM (p < 0.0001). Multinomial regression models comparing vaccinated to unvaccinated PWH found age, education, mode of HIV transmission/gender, and survey period were significantly associated with vaccination status (all p < 0.05). High levels of mpox awareness were observed among this cohort of PWH with more MSM employing risk reduction behaviors and being vaccinated. Ensuring that PWH, regardless of gender, sexual orientation, or age, understand the risks of mpox may improve vaccination uptake.","Elisabeth W Andersen, Paige E. Kulie, A. Castel, Jose Lucar, Debra A Benator, Alan E. Greenberg, Anne K. Monroe",10.3390/pathogens13020124,https://doi.org/10.3390/pathogens13020124,Pathogens,6,2024,yes,"The study focuses on people living with HIV (PWH), who are not considered healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study focuses on vaccination attitudes, knowledge, and uptake status, but does not report on safety (adverse events) or effectiveness (prevention of infection, hospitalization, or death) outcomes.",yes,The study is a primary research study based on a longitudinal cohort.,yes,The study involves human participants within the DC Cohort.,no,"The participant group consists of PWH and specifically discusses MSM; there is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as participants.",no,"The vaccination status described relates to current status in an adult HIV cohort, not vaccination during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,"The study evaluates mpox vaccination status, which refers to the current vaccinia-based vaccines.",Exclude,"The study investigates vaccine attitudes and uptake among adults with HIV, primarily focused on men who have sex with men. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not include the target populations (pregnant women, children, or adolescents) and does not report on safety or clinical effectiveness outcomes.","Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""We assessed mpox knowledge, adoption of preventive behaviors, and vaccination attitudes among PWH enrolled in a longitudinal HIV cohort in Washington, DC, the DC Cohort.""",,"  - ""We assessed mpox knowledge, adoption of preventive behaviors, and vaccination attitudes among PWH enrolled in a longitudinal HIV cohort in Washington, DC, the DC Cohort.""
