Monkeypox outbreak 2022: disparities and prevention,"We read with interest the letter by Gagneux-Brunon et al. who emphasized a need for better communication regarding potential exposure and subsequent consequences to increase acceptance of monkeypox virus (MPV) vaccine [[1]Gagneux-Brunon A. Dauby N. Launay O. Botelho-Nevers E. Attitudes towards monkeypox vaccination among healthcare workers in France and Belgium: an element of complacency?.J Hosp Infect. 2022; 130: 144-145Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (3) Google Scholar]. We commend the authors on their important contribution, and further expand on their conclusions by drawing a parallel with the coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic and focusing on disparities and prevention strategies for monkeypox. There has been a massive surge in cases of monkeypox recently throughout the world. More than 80,000 cases have been reported in over 100 countries that have previously been non-endemic for MPV [[2]Centers for Disease Control and PreventionMonkeypox in the U.S. CDC, Atlanta, GA2022https://www.cdc.gov/poxvirus/monkeypox/response/2022/us-map.htmlGoogle Scholar]. MPV is an orthopoxvirus that can cause disease progression like that of smallpox, albeit with a much lower mortality rate [[3]Hirani R. Rashid D. Lewis J. Hosein-Woodley R. Issani A. Monkeypox outbreak in the age of COVID-19: a new global health emergency.Milit Med Res. 2022; 9: 55Crossref PubMed Scopus (2) Google Scholar]. This rapidly developing outbreak is of grave concern for a multitude of reasons. Partial immunity for monkeypox was achieved through vaccinia vaccination; however, with the eradication of smallpox, there has been a decline in vaccination efforts [[4]Okwo-Bele J.M. Cherian T. The expanded programme on immunization: a lasting legacy of smallpox eradication.Vaccine. 2011; 29: D74-D79Crossref PubMed Scopus (92) Google Scholar]. Furthermore, the world is still recovering from the devastation of the COVID-19 pandemic, in which both healthcare systems and infrastructure were ravaged and left debilitated. In dealing with the ongoing effects of the COVID-19 pandemic, we have developed substantial infrastructure systems coupled with newly improved legislation, such as the COVID-19 Vaccine Distribution and Production Act, that could be leveraged to combat the new threat of MPV [[5]Crist C.H.R. 8508 – 116th Congress (2019–2020): COVID-19 Vaccine Distribution and production Act.2020http://www.congress.gov/Google Scholar]. However, we must still work towards dismantling the health disparities that exist when examining health prevention between groups of differing socio-economic statuses. Perhaps drawing a parallel with the ongoing COVID-19 pandemic could help to identify several problems. For example, in understanding the disparity among marginalized groups due to COVID-19, we see that African Americans are over-represented in the number of deaths in certain states. For instance, in Louisiana, where African Americans represent 32% of the population, they account for >70% of COVID-19-related deaths [[6]Ferdinand K.C. Nasser S.A. African-American COVID-19 mortality.J Am Coll Cardiol. 2020; 75: 2746-2748Crossref PubMed Scopus (52) Google Scholar]. Furthermore, these outcomes are exacerbated due to the fact that African Americans seek treatment at minority-serving institutions [[6]Ferdinand K.C. Nasser S.A. African-American COVID-19 mortality.J Am Coll Cardiol. 2020; 75: 2746-2748Crossref PubMed Scopus (52) Google Scholar]; these institutions have a limited budget, a shortage of critical care physicians, and inadequate medical supplies and equipment, which results in a lower quality of care [[3]Hirani R. Rashid D. Lewis J. Hosein-Woodley R. Issani A. Monkeypox outbreak in the age of COVID-19: a new global health emergency.Milit Med Res. 2022; 9: 55Crossref PubMed Scopus (2) Google Scholar]. Early distribution of the vaccine and containment of disease through equitable efforts for contact tracing, isolation and prevention of monkeypox may decrease the need for mass isolation that we saw with COVID-19, which tends to create distrust in the government and opposition to containment efforts. Also, MPV is conducive to a ring vaccination strategy because it spreads more slowly than most human viruses and has a long incubation period. Increasing availability of testing through commercial laboratories which are less fragile after the recent influx of testing due to the COVID-19 pandemic can help to accelerate tracing efforts and track data regarding progression of the disease. Contact tracing infrastructure used for the COVID-19 pandemic can also be pivoted and repurposed for MPV. We can also take measures to make the contact tracing infrastructure most robust and oriented towards the future with the implementation of artificial intelligence. Algorithmic frameworks based on machine learning can be integrated into electronic health record systems to enhance the speed and efficacy of contact tracing [[7]Wani S.U.D. Khan N.A. Thakur G. Gautam S.P. Ali M. Alam P. et al.Utilization of artificial intelligence in disease prevention: diagnosis, treatment, and implications for the healthcare workforce.Healthcare. 2022; 10: 608Crossref Scopus (10) Google Scholar]. Decreasing any barriers to prescription of these vaccines through policy changes can increase the rate of adoption and administration of the vaccine. Making these vaccines more accessible in the form of grassroots movements that engage and educate the community regarding the vaccines may also help. It is imperative as clinicians and future leaders that we take this threat of MPV seriously. The gravity of both monkeypox and COVID-19 are witness to the fact that healthcare disparities exist and are more prevalent than ever. Minority groups are being cast as fungible in the grasp for a cash nexus and squall for a limited number of resources – it is crucial to lay foundations to treat this ravaging disease systematically. To engage in a transformative pedagogy, we must engage in active discourse through recognition of the massive inequities that exist, and devise plans to combat them thoroughly. None declared. None.","Rahim Hirani, Rasheed Hosein-Woodley, Dawood Rashid, Elizabeth D. Drugge, Mill Etienne",10.1016/j.jhin.2022.12.005,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhin.2022.12.005,Journal of Hospital Infection,3,2022,no,"The article is a commentary/letter discussing general prevention strategies and healthcare disparities, not a study of a specific clinical population.",no,The paper discusses prevention strategies broadly but does not report primary safety or effectiveness data.,no,"This is a commentary or letter to the editor, not a primary research study or evidence synthesis.",no,"This is a theoretical/perspective piece discussing human healthcare systems, but it is not a study involving human participants.",no,"The paper does not include participants from any of the specified target populations (pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents).",no,The paper does not evaluate specific vaccination events for the required age or physiological groups.,yes,"The paper mentions vaccinia and smallpox vaccines generally in the context of history and prevention, but does not present a study of them.",Exclude,This article is a commentary/letter to the editor discussing disparities and prevention strategies for monkeypox rather than a primary research study. It does not include the target populations (pregnant women or children) or report primary clinical outcomes.,"Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""We read with interest the letter by Gagneux-Brunon et al.""
  - ""focusing on disparities and prevention strategies for monkeypox.""",,"  - ""focusing on disparities and prevention strategies for monkeypox.""",,"  - ""We read with interest the letter by Gagneux-Brunon et al.""",,"  - ""We read with interest the letter by Gagneux-Brunon et al.""
  - ""focusing on disparities and prevention strategies for monkeypox.""",,"  - ""focusing on disparities and prevention strategies for monkeypox.""",,"  - ""focusing on disparities and prevention strategies for monkeypox.""",,"  - ""Partial immunity for monkeypox was achieved through vaccinia vaccination; however, with the eradication of smallpox, there has been a decline in vaccination efforts""",
Sero-genomic evidence for occult mpox exposure in healthy Nigerian adults,"The 2022 multi-country mpox (formerly monkeypox) outbreak, driven by mpox virus (MPXV) Clade IIb poses renewed threat to global public health. The cessation of smallpox vaccination has created large immunologically naïve cohorts, with uncertain implications for contemporary MPXV susceptibility. To assess whether residual vaccination-derived immunity influences exposure risk, we combine serological and phylodynamic analyses. Using a six-plex Luminex assay, we measure immunoglobulin G (IgG) binding to six MPXV antigens in 176 Nigerian adults comprising of 75 healthcare workers sampled in 2021 and 101 community volunteers sampled in 2023. At baseline, 24/176 (13.6%) were MPXV seropositive, predominantly born before 1980. Magnitude-breadth analysis scores were two-fold higher in pre-1980 cohort relative to post-1980 cohort. In 153 participants with follow-up samples (median 9 months), 5/153 (3%) showed evidence of exposure, with ≥2-fold increases in magnitude-breadth scores and antigen-specific responses against ≥4/6 antigens without reported clinical illness. Antigen-specific responses were strongest to B6R (11-fold), followed by M1R and A35R, with marked individual-level heterogeneity. Complementary phylodynamic reconstruction of 105 Nigerian MPXV genomes identified sporadic transmission against frequent dead-end infections. Together, these data show that residual smallpox immunity continues to shape mpox transmission and asymptomatic exposure contributes to under-detected spread, informing surveillance and targeted vaccination strategies. There are limited data on mpox immunity in West Africa. In this study, authors present serological and genomic evidence of residual smallpox vaccination immunity and possibly unrecognized mpox exposure among ostensibly healthy Nigerian adults.","A. Abdullahi, I. Omah, R. Kassanjee, F. Ige, M. Edun, S. Osawe, Catherine K. Koofhethile, H. Wisso, E. Onyemata, E. Parker, Onikepe Folarin, Aniekwe M. Isaac, U. Saulawa, L. Ceron-Gutierrez, E. Okpokoro, A. Happi, O. Sokolova, R. Audu, Sani H. Aliyu, Manhattan E Charurat, C. Happi, B. Salako, R. Doffinger, A. Abimiku, Ravindra K. Gupta",10.1038/s41467-026-68335-1,https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-68335-1,Nature Communications,0,2026,no,"The study was exclusively limited to healthy adults, including healthcare workers and community volunteers.",maybe,The study reports on serological evidence of immunity and exposure (IgG binding) but does not report clinical effectiveness outcomes or safety outcomes of active vaccination programs.,yes,The study is a primary research study utilizing serological and genomic analyses.,yes,The study was conducted in 176 human participants.,no,"The study population consisted of adults and did not include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",maybe,"While participants born before 1980 likely received smallpox vaccination as children, the study's current evaluation focus is on them as adults.",yes,The study investigates residual immunity from historical smallpox vaccination.,Exclude,"This study is a primary research investigation using serological and genomic data from healthy Nigerian adults to assess mpox immunity. It is excluded because it focuses exclusively on healthy non-pregnant adults and does not include the target populations of pregnant women, children, or neonates.","Population Focus, Target Population",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""176 Nigerian adults comprising of 75 healthcare workers sampled in 2021 and 101 community volunteers sampled in 2023.""
  - ""ostensibly healthy Nigerian adults.""",,"  - ""To assess whether residual vaccination-derived immunity influences exposure risk, we combine serological and phylodynamic analyses.""
  - ""Using a six-plex Luminex assay, we measure immunoglobulin G (IgG) binding to six MPXV antigens""",,"  - ""To assess whether residual vaccination-derived immunity influences exposure risk, we combine serological and phylodynamic analyses.""",,"  - ""176 Nigerian adults comprising of 75 healthcare workers sampled in 2021 and 101 community volunteers sampled in 2023.""",,"  - ""176 Nigerian adults comprising of 75 healthcare workers sampled in 2021 and 101 community volunteers sampled in 2023.""",,"  - ""predominantly born before 1980.""",,"  - ""To assess whether residual vaccination-derived immunity influences exposure risk, we combine serological and phylodynamic analyses.""",
Atypical Mpox Cutaneous Presentation After Full Vaccination.,"ABSTRACT
In 2022-2023 mpox outbreak led to declaration of a public health emergency. Smallpox vaccination has led to increased immunity and a decrease in clinical disease. The role of vaccination in immunosuppressed patients is not well understood. We present an atypical case of cutaneous mpox, presenting in a fully vaccinated, immunosuppressed, HIV-negative patient as an initial isolated, subcutaneous neck mass. We review the diagnostic dilemma and workup for these atypical cases.","Celine M. Schreidah, Blair Jenkins, M. Trager, Sameera Husain, A. Coromilas, Joyeeta G. Dastidar, Benjamin A Miko, J. Zucker, S. Gallitano",10.1097/DAD.0000000000002945,https://doi.org/10.1097/DAD.0000000000002945,American journal of dermatopathology,1,2025,maybe,"The study describes a case of an adult, and it is not explicitly stated whether they are healthy or a 'non-pregnant adult', though they are noted as immunosuppressed. However, without confirmation of age <18 or pregnancy status, this is unclear.",yes,"The study reports on clinical presentation (breakthrough mpox) in a vaccinated individual, which relates to effectiveness outcomes.",yes,The study is a case report.,yes,The study involves a human patient.,no,"The abstract does not indicate that the patient is a pregnant woman, neonate, child, or adolescent. The context suggests an adult patient.",no,"There is no evidence that the vaccine was received during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence; the patient's age and timing of vaccination are not specified as meeting these criteria.",yes,The paper mentions smallpox vaccination and a fully vaccinated status in the context of the mpox outbreak.,Exclude,"This is a case report of an atypical mpox presentation in a fully vaccinated, immunosuppressed adult. It fails the target population criteria because it does not include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""We present an atypical case of cutaneous mpox, presenting in a fully vaccinated, immunosuppressed, HIV-negative patient as an initial isolated, subcutaneous neck mass.""",,"  - ""We present an atypical case of cutaneous mpox, presenting in a fully vaccinated, immunosuppressed, HIV-negative patient as an initial isolated, subcutaneous neck mass.""",,"  - ""We present an atypical case of cutaneous mpox, presenting in a fully vaccinated, immunosuppressed, HIV-negative patient as an initial isolated, subcutaneous neck mass.""",,"  - ""We present an atypical case of cutaneous mpox, presenting in a fully vaccinated, immunosuppressed, HIV-negative patient as an initial isolated, subcutaneous neck mass.""",,"  - ""We present an atypical case of cutaneous mpox, presenting in a fully vaccinated, immunosuppressed, HIV-negative patient as an initial isolated, subcutaneous neck mass.""",,"  - ""We present an atypical case of cutaneous mpox, presenting in a fully vaccinated, immunosuppressed, HIV-negative patient as an initial isolated, subcutaneous neck mass.""",,"  - ""Smallpox vaccination has led to increased immunity and a decrease in clinical disease.""
