Clinical Outcomes of Mpox Disease in Patients Treated with Tecovirimat.,"BACKGROUND
There is emerging literature describing clinical characteristics and outcomes in patients with mpox treated with tecovirimat.
METHODS
We analyzed retrospective deidentified data from healthcare systems in New York City with the highest number of tecovirimat prescriptions. Individuals with probable or confirmed mpox initiating tecovirimat from May-December 2022 were included. A chart abstraction instrument was used to extract demographic and clinical data from medical records. We examined factors associated with delay in treatment and hospitalization.
RESULTS
A total of 708 individuals prescribed tecovirimat for mpox were included in the analysis; median age was 36 years (IQR 31-43); 566 (80%) were cisgender men who have sex with men; and 399 (56%) were living with HIV. Side effects (100, 14%) and severe adverse events (7, 1%) were rare. The most common long-term sequela was scarring (69, 10%). One hundred and one (14%) were hospitalized. Median time from symptom onset to treatment initiation was 8 days (IQR 6-11). Non-Hispanic Black patients had higher risk of initiating tecovirimat ≥8 days after symptom onset (relative risk [RR]=1.3, 95%CI: 1.2-1.7) and being hospitalized (adjusted relative risk [aRR]=2.2, 95%CI: 1.4-3.5) compared with Hispanic patients, after adjusting for HIV and insurance status.
CONCLUSIONS
We described common reasons for tecovirimat initiation and hospitalization. There were racial inequities in hospitalization and treatment delays. More research and focused efforts to mitigate these inequities are needed. These findings may better inform decisions for treatment initiation and hospitalization for mpox.","Ciarra Leocadio, Elizabeth A Garcia, Maura Lash, Jonathan Berardi, Justin Chan, R. Chasan, Blanca Esquivel, Mary M K Foote, Marshall J Glesby, Amiyah Guerra, Alexander B Harris, Dorothy Knutsen, Sharon Mannheimer, Dana Mazo, Jacob McLean, T. McPherson, Eric A. Meyerowitz, Ofole Mgbako, Gopi Patel, Patricia Pagán Pirallo, A. Radix, Paul Riska, M. Sayegh, Leah Seifu, Raphael Shaw, Barry S Zingman, Rustin A Zomorodi, Jason Zucker, Marcia Wong",10.1093/cid/ciaf520,https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciaf520,Clinical Infectious Diseases,0,2025,yes,The study population is not exclusively limited to healthy adults as 56% (399/708) are living with HIV.,yes,The study reports safety outcomes (side effects and severe adverse events) and effectiveness-related outcomes such as hospitalization.,yes,The study is a retrospective study analyzing clinical data.,yes,The study is a human study involving 708 individuals.,no,"The study describes an adult population with a median age of 36. There is no evidence that pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents were included, as the population focus is on MSM (80%) and individuals with HIV (56%).",no,"The study focus is on tecovirimat initiation post-symptom onset, not on vaccination timing in childhood, adolescence, or pregnancy.",no,"The study evaluates outcomes for tecovirimat, which is an antiviral treatment, not a vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This study is a retrospective analysis of clinical outcomes in mpox patients treated with the antiviral tecovirimat. It fails the inclusion criteria because it focuses on a treatment intervention rather than vaccination and does not include children, adolescents, or pregnant women in its reported population.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""399 (56%) were living with HIV.""",,"  - ""Side effects (100, 14%) and severe adverse events (7, 1%) were rare.""
