"Mpox in Children and Adolescents: Epidemiology, Clinical Features, Diagnosis, and Management.","While mpox is rare among children in the United States, pediatric cases are being reported during the 2022 multinational mpox outbreak. Vaccines and antiviral medications developed for other orthopoxviruses have recently become widely used to prevent and treat mpox in both children and adults in the United States. Although scientific literature regarding mpox in children and adolescents is scant, prior case reports can provide valuable information about the clinical features and potential complications of untreated clade II mpox in these age groups. In this review, we summarize the epidemiology and clinical features of mpox in children and adolescents and provide recommendations for clinicians regarding its diagnosis, management, and prevention. Robust, dedicated surveillance of pediatric exposures and cases in the current outbreak, including the use of vaccines and therapeutics, are needed to guide clinical management and public health strategies.","Amy Beeson, Julia C Haston, D. McCormick, M. Reynolds, K. Chatham-Stephens, A. McCollum, Shana Godfred-Cato",10.1542/peds.2022-060179,https://doi.org/10.1542/peds.2022-060179,Pediatrics,43,2022,yes,"The study focuses specifically on children and adolescents, and pediatric cases in the 2022 outbreak.",yes,"The study discusses clinical features, complications, and prevention of mpox.",no,The paper describes itself as a review that summarizes and provides clinical recommendations. It is not a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis as required by the criteria.,yes,The study discusses human pediatric cases.,yes,The study explicitly includes children and adolescents.,yes,The paper mentions that vaccines have become widely used to prevent and treat mpox in children.,yes,"The study involves vaccines developed for orthopoxviruses, which include vaccinia-based vaccines.",Exclude,"This article is a clinical review that summarizes the epidemiology and features of mpox in children and adolescents. Although it addresses the target population and vaccine use, it fails the study design criterion because it is not a primary study or systematic review.",Study Design,Study Design,0.1,"  - ""In this review, we summarize the epidemiology and clinical features of mpox in children and adolescents and provide recommendations for clinicians regarding its diagnosis, management, and prevention.""
