How Can We Best Use COVID-19 Vaccines in Adolescents? An International Perspective,"Mass vaccination of the world population is our ticket out of the coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic. We are fortunate that many efficacious vaccines against COVID-19 have been produced in less than 1 year since the first case was reported in China, and countries have launched successful population vaccination campaigns resulting in >50% vaccination rates in Israel, the United States (US), the United Kingdom (UK), Germany, Italy, Singapore, and so on by the time of writing this article in July 2021. Since the publication of the phase III trial for BNT162b2 mRNA vaccine (produced by Pfizer-BioNTech) in 12- to 15-year-olds demonstrating 100% efficacy at preventing symptomatic COVID-19 infection [[1]Frenck R.W. Klein N.P. Kitchin N. et al.Safety, immunogenicity, and efficacy of the BNT162b2 Covid-19 vaccine in adolescents.N Engl J Med. 2021; 385: 239-250Crossref PubMed Scopus (610) Google Scholar], this vaccine has also been rolled out among adolescents in Israel, US, Italy, Singapore, and so on. Countries such as UK and Germany, however, have opted to limit vaccination in adolescents to those at high risk of severe COVID-19. Here, we discuss the risk-benefit ratio of vaccinating healthy adolescents against COVID-19 at the present time and some of the ethical considerations surrounding this practice. Among the high Human Development Index (HDI) countries, the risk of severe COVID-19 in children and adolescents is low, with a mortality of 1–2 per million population [[2]Bhopal S.S. Bagaria J. Olabi B. Bhopal R. Children and young people remain at low risk of COVID-19 mortality.Lancet Child Adolesc Health. 2021; 5: e12-e13Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (177) Google Scholar]; this is similar to seasonal influenza, for which mortality in persons younger than 21 years is 2 per million. The incidence rate of symptomatic COVID-19 in adolescents was 23,000 per million in the US at the peak of the pandemic between 1 January 2021 and 31 March 2021 [[3]CDCDemographic Trends of COVID-19 cases and deaths in the US reported to CDC.https://covid.cdc.gov/covid-data-tracker/#demographicsDate accessed: June 25, 2021Google Scholar]; of these, 64 required treatment in the intensive care unit [[4]Havers F.P. Whitaker M. Self J.L. et al.Hospitalization of adolescents aged 12–17 Years with Laboratory-Confirmed COVID-19 — COVID-NET, 14 States, March 1, 2020–April 24, 2021.MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep. 2021; 70: 851-857Crossref PubMed Scopus (75) Google Scholar] (Table 1) [1Frenck R.W. Klein N.P. Kitchin N. et al.Safety, immunogenicity, and efficacy of the BNT162b2 Covid-19 vaccine in adolescents.N Engl J Med. 2021; 385: 239-250Crossref PubMed Scopus (610) Google Scholar, 2Bhopal S.S. Bagaria J. Olabi B. Bhopal R. Children and young people remain at low risk of COVID-19 mortality.Lancet Child Adolesc Health. 2021; 5: e12-e13Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (177) Google Scholar, 3CDCDemographic Trends of COVID-19 cases and deaths in the US reported to CDC.https://covid.cdc.gov/covid-data-tracker/#demographicsDate accessed: June 25, 2021Google Scholar, 4Havers F.P. Whitaker M. Self J.L. et al.Hospitalization of adolescents aged 12–17 Years with Laboratory-Confirmed COVID-19 — COVID-NET, 14 States, March 1, 2020–April 24, 2021.MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep. 2021; 70: 851-857Crossref PubMed Scopus (75) Google Scholar, 5Gargano J.W. Wallace M. Hadler S.C. et al.Use of mRNA COVID-19 vaccine after reports of myocarditis among vaccine Recipients: Update from the Advisory Committee on Immunization practices — United States, June 2021.MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep. 2021; 70: 977-982Crossref PubMed Scopus (0) Google Scholar, 6Marshall M. Ferguson I.D. Lewis P. et al.Symptomatic acute myocarditis in seven adolescents following Pfizer-BioNTech COVID-19 vaccination.Pediatrics. 2021; 148e2021052478Crossref PubMed Scopus (255) Google Scholar, 7Snapiri O. Rosenberg Danziger C. Shirman N. et al.Transient cardiac Injury in adolescents receiving the BNT162b2 mRNA COVID-19 vaccine.Pediatr Infect Dis J. 2021; 14: e360-e363Crossref Scopus (53) Google Scholar, 8Belay E.D. Abrams J. Oster M.E. et al.Trends in Geographic and Temporal distribution of US children with Multisystem inflammatory Syndrome during the COVID-19 pandemic.JAMA Pediatr. 2021; 175: 837-845Crossref PubMed Scopus (187) Google Scholar]. Risk of severe disease depends on individual risk factors. Within the pediatric age group, apart from infants, the greatest risk factor for severe COVID-19 or mortality is underlying medical conditions including chronic lung disease, obesity, neurologic diseases, and cardiovascular diseases. In addition, psychiatric disorders have also been associated with COVID-19-related morbidity and mortality [[9]Mazereel V. Van Assche K. Detraux J. De Hert M. COVID-19 vaccination for people with severe mental illness: Why, what, and how?.Lancet Psychiatry. 2021; 8: 444-450Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (110) Google Scholar]. Specifically, among adolescents hospitalised for COVID-19 in the US, 70.6% had at least one underlying medical condition [[4]Havers F.P. Whitaker M. Self J.L. et al.Hospitalization of adolescents aged 12–17 Years with Laboratory-Confirmed COVID-19 — COVID-NET, 14 States, March 1, 2020–April 24, 2021.MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep. 2021; 70: 851-857Crossref PubMed Scopus (75) Google Scholar]. Another major concern for COVID-19-associated morbidity in children and adolescents is the Multisystem Inflammatory Syndrome in Children (MISC); the incidence of which among 12- to 15-year-olds is 15–22 per million persons [[8]Belay E.D. Abrams J. Oster M.E. et al.Trends in Geographic and Temporal distribution of US children with Multisystem inflammatory Syndrome during the COVID-19 pandemic.JAMA Pediatr. 2021; 175: 837-845Crossref PubMed Scopus (187) Google Scholar] (Table 1). Furthermore, there are indirect health effects of the pandemic on youth and adolescents, such as interrupted education and, concerningly, mental health issues associated with social isolation [[10]Lee C.M. Cadigan J.M. Rhew I.C. Increases in Loneliness among young adults during the COVID-19 pandemic and association with increases in mental health Problems.J Adolesc Health. 2020; 67: 714-717Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (288) Google Scholar,[11]Hu Y. Qian Y. COVID-19 and adolescent mental health in the United Kingdom.J Adolesc Health. 2021; 69: 26-32Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (60) Google Scholar].Table 1Incidence rates of symptomatic and severe COVID-19 in 12- to 15-year-olds in the US, between 1 January 2021 and 31 March 2021 as reported by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention [[3]CDCDemographic Trends of COVID-19 cases and deaths in the US reported to CDC.https://covid.cdc.gov/covid-data-tracker/#demographicsDate accessed: June 25, 2021Google Scholar,[4]Havers F.P. Whitaker M. Self J.L. et al.Hospitalization of adolescents aged 12–17 Years with Laboratory-Confirmed COVID-19 — COVID-NET, 14 States, March 1, 2020–April 24, 2021.MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep. 2021; 70: 851-857Crossref PubMed Scopus (75) Google Scholar], with mortality rate taken from longitudinal data as there were no deaths in this particular period [[2]Bhopal S.S. Bagaria J. Olabi B. Bhopal R. Children and young people remain at low risk of COVID-19 mortality.Lancet Child Adolesc Health. 2021; 5: e12-e13Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (177) Google Scholar,[3]CDCDemographic Trends of COVID-19 cases and deaths in the US reported to CDC.https://covid.cdc.gov/covid-data-tracker/#demographicsDate accessed: June 25, 2021Google Scholar], vaccine efficacy for various clinical endpoints [[1]Frenck R.W. Klein N.P. Kitchin N. et al.Safety, immunogenicity, and efficacy of the BNT162b2 Covid-19 vaccine in adolescents.N Engl J Med. 2021; 385: 239-250Crossref PubMed Scopus (610) Google Scholar], and the risk of myocarditis associated with mRNA COVID vaccination for 12- to 15-year-olds as reported in the US Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS) up to 23 June 2021 [[5]Gargano J.W. Wallace M. Hadler S.C. et al.Use of mRNA COVID-19 vaccine after reports of myocarditis among vaccine Recipients: Update from the Advisory Committee on Immunization practices — United States, June 2021.MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep. 2021; 70: 977-982Crossref PubMed Scopus (0) Google Scholar], with accompanying risk for severe disease [[6]Marshall M. Ferguson I.D. Lewis P. et al.Symptomatic acute myocarditis in seven adolescents following Pfizer-BioNTech COVID-19 vaccination.Pediatrics. 2021; 148e2021052478Crossref PubMed Scopus (255) Google Scholar,[7]Snapiri O. Rosenberg Danziger C. Shirman N. et al.Transient cardiac Injury in adolescents receiving the BNT162b2 mRNA COVID-19 vaccine.Pediatr Infect Dis J. 2021; 14: e360-e363Crossref Scopus (53) Google Scholar]Clinical outcomeIncidence rate of COVID-19 in 12- to 15-year-olds in the US, per millionVaccine efficacy in 12- to 15-year-oldsRisk associated with mRNA COVID vaccination in 12- to 15-year-olds, per million second dosesSymptomatic disease23,000aThese numbers were taken from the peak of the pandemic 1 January 2021 to 31 March 2021. [[3]CDCDemographic Trends of COVID-19 cases and deaths in the US reported to CDC.https://covid.cdc.gov/covid-data-tracker/#demographicsDate accessed: June 25, 2021Google Scholar]95% [[1]Frenck R.W. Klein N.P. Kitchin N. et al.Safety, immunogenicity, and efficacy of the BNT162b2 Covid-19 vaccine in adolescents.N Engl J Med. 2021; 385: 239-250Crossref PubMed Scopus (610) Google Scholar]Perimyocarditis [[5]Gargano J.W. Wallace M. Hadler S.C. et al.Use of mRNA COVID-19 vaccine after reports of myocarditis among vaccine Recipients: Update from the Advisory Committee on Immunization practices — United States, June 2021.MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep. 2021; 70: 977-982Crossref PubMed Scopus (0) Google Scholar]:In girls: 8-10In boys: 56-69Severe disease requiring intensive care unit admission64aThe","Youjia Zhong, L. Lee, P. Tambyah, W. Liew, B. Lee",10.1016/j.jadohealth.2021.08.014,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jadohealth.2021.08.014,Journal of Adolescent Health,6,2021,yes,"The study focuses on adolescents, so it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports on vaccine efficacy against infection and safety outcomes like myocarditis.,no,This is an 'International Perspective' piece that discusses existing data rather than a primary research study or a formal systematic review/meta-analysis.,yes,The focus is on human adolescents.,yes,The study specifically addresses 12- to 15-year-old adolescents.,yes,The vaccination occurs during adolescence.,no,The study evaluates COVID-19 mRNA vaccines (BNT162b2) rather than vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,This paper is a perspective piece discussing the risks and benefits of COVID-19 mRNA vaccines in adolescents. It fails the inclusion criteria because it focuses on COVID-19 rather than mpox or historical smallpox vaccines and does not follow a required primary or synthesis study design.,"Study Design, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""12- to 15-year-olds""",,"  - ""risk of myocarditis associated with mRNA COVID vaccination""",,"  - ""discuss the risk-benefit ratio of vaccinating healthy adolescents against COVID-19""",,"  - ""12- to 15-year-olds""",,"  - ""12- to 15-year-olds""",,"  - ""12- to 15-year-olds""",,"  - ""BNT162b2 mRNA vaccine (produced by Pfizer-BioNTech)""",
Monkeypox Virus and Pregnancy,"Human monkeypox (Mpox) is a zoonotic disease caused by monkeypox virus (MPXV) present in western Africa and exported sporadically worldwide. MPXV causes illness in individuals and pregnant women which constitute a population at risk with obstetrical and fetal complications including miscarriage, stillbirth and premature delivery. There are accumulated data suggesting a vertical transmission of MPXV from mother to fetus. This review provides an overview of the literature on MPXV infection in pregnant women with a specific focus on vertical transmission.","Jonatane Andrieu, J. Mège, S. Mezouar",10.1002/jmv.70337,https://doi.org/10.1002/jmv.70337,Journal of Medical Virology,6,2025,yes,"The study focus is specifically on pregnant women and vertical transmission to the fetus, meaning it is not limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study discusses complications like miscarriage and stillbirth, but these are outcomes of MPXV infection, not safety or effectiveness outcomes (such as prevention of infection) related to vaccination.",no,"The paper is described as an 'overview of the literature', which identifies it as a narrative review rather than a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,"The study focuses on illness and transmission in humans, specifically pregnant women and fetuses.",yes,The study explicitly includes pregnant women and fetuses as participants/subjects of interest.,no,"The study focuses on 'MPXV infection' and 'vertical transmission' and does not mention or include participants receiving any vaccine during the neonatal period, childhood, adolescence, or pregnancy.",no,The paper provides an overview of 'MPXV infection' and its complications but does not involve the study or evaluation of vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"The paper is a narrative literature review focused on MPXV infection and vertical transmission in pregnant women. It does not involve any vaccination 