Pathogenesis and potential antiviral therapy of complications of smallpox vaccination.,"Vaccination against smallpox may result in a variety of complications, ranging in severity from benign to lethal. Universal vaccination was halted in the US in 1972, so almost half the present population has never been vaccinated. Because side effects occur most often in first-time vaccinees, current plans for rapid large-scale vaccination in the event of bioterrorist attack raise concerns about the occurrence of a large number of adverse events. Most complications result from the excessive replication of vaccinia virus, making them potential targets for antiviral therapy. Effective treatment is especially needed for persons with atopic dermatitis or eczema, who are unusually susceptible to the initiation and spread of vaccinia infection because of defects of innate immunity in the skin, and for individuals with defective cell-mediated immunity, who are unable to eliminate vaccinia infection once it has begun. In the past, many complications were treated with vaccinia immune globulin (VIG) and/or the antiviral drug methisazone, but neither was tested in placebo-controlled trials. New antiviral drugs are now available, but have not yet been evaluated for treating vaccinia infections in humans. Both laboratory research and clinical studies are needed to help prevent serious complications in any major vaccination campaign.",M. Bray,10.1016/S0166-3542(03)00008-1,https://doi.org/10.1016/S0166-3542(03)00008-1,Antiviral Research,101,2003,yes,"The paper discusses patients with atopic dermatitis, eczema, and defective cell-mediated immunity.",yes,The paper focuses on complications and adverse events following smallpox vaccination.,no,"The paper is a narrative review or commentary, not a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or a systematic review. It explicitly states that clinical studies are needed.",yes,The paper discusses human complications and historical human treatments like VIG and methisazone.,no,"The paper does not report on a study population including pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,The paper does not provide data on a specific study population vaccinated during the specified periods.,yes,The study involves vaccinia-based smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This paper is a narrative review or commentary discussing the pathogenesis and treatment of smallpox vaccine complications. It fails to meet the inclusion criteria because it is not a primary research study or systematic review, and it does not specifically include pregnant women, children, or adolescents in its scope.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""Effective treatment is especially needed for persons with atopic dermatitis or eczema,""
