Monkeypox virus infection in pregnancy: description of two cases reported to the Colombian National Institute of Health,"Monkeypox (mpox), caused by the MPOXV (monkeypox virus), has been endemic in Africa since its first identification in 1958. However, in May 2022, the world witnessed the first global outbreak associated with the West African clade. Even though thousands of cases have been recorded, our understanding of vertical transmission during pregnancy remains restricted due to an absence of reported cases in pregnant women and a lack of adequate clinical descriptions. The cases of two pregnant women, ages 33 and 24, who tested positive for MPOXV at 31 and 13.4 weeks of gestation, respectively, are presented. In all cases, extensive clinical, histological, and molecular examinations of the mothers and neonates revealed no indication of vertical transmission.","Mercado-Reyes Marcela, Daza Marcela, Rodríguez Peña Yazmín, Araque Muñoz Paula, Franco-Muñoz Carlos, Ruíz-Moreno Hector, Corchuelo Chavarro Sheryll, Portilla Velandia Claudia, Osorio Usaquén Johana, Ávila Mellizo Greace, Narvaez Díaz Nubia, Walteros Diana, Cortes Palacio Katherinne, Piñeros Daza José Ángel",10.1111/apm.13488,https://doi.org/10.1111/apm.13488,"Acta Pathologica, Microbiologica et Immunologica Scandinavica (APMIS)",3,2024,yes,The study focus is on pregnant women and neonates.,no,The study reports on clinical outcomes of infection and vertical transmission rather than safety or effectiveness of a vaccine.,yes,The study is a case report/series.,yes,The study involves human participants (mothers and neonates).,yes,The study specifically includes pregnant women and neonates.,no,"There is no mention of the participants receiving any vaccine during pregnancy; rather, they were naturally infected with MPOXV.",no,The study does not involve or mention vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This paper describes case reports of pregnant women and neonates affected by MPOXV infection, but it does not involve any vaccination, which is a mandatory element of the screening criteria. Consequently, it fails criteria related to vaccination timing, vaccine type, and vaccine-related outcomes.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccination Timing,0.1,"  - ""cases of two pregnant women, ages 33 and 24, who tested positive for MPOXV at 31 and 13.4 weeks of gestation, respectively, are presented.""
