Low Specific T‐Cell Immunity Against Mpox Elicited in People With HIV‐1 and PrEP Users After Subcutaneous Vaccination Compared to Natural Infection,"In August 2024, the World Health Organization declared mpox infection a Public Health Emergency of International Concern for second time since 2022 and recommended vaccinating all people at‐risk, such as people with HIV‐1 (PWH) and prophylaxis pre‐exposition (PrEP) users. We recruited PWH and PrEP users who received one or two doses of subcutaneous Imvanex® or Jynneos® and compared specific T‐cell immunity with participants who passed mpox natural infection or were Nonexposed to mpox. CD4 + T cells from people who had mpox showed the highest capacity to produce IL‐2 (1.8‐fold, p = 0.0328), as well as IFNγ (2.5‐fold, p = 0.0247), and IL‐4 (1.8‐fold, p = 0.0373) from naïve CD4 + T cells, in response to MHC‐II‐restricted mpox‐related peptides, compared to vaccinated participants. CD8 + T cells from individuals who had mpox also showed the highest capacity to produce IFN γ $\gamma $ (1.6‐fold, p = 0.0321) and TNF α $\alpha $ (2.1‐fold, p = 0.0084) against MHC‐I‐restricted peptides. Therefore, the most potent and robust T‐cell responses were developed after mpox infection, while they were barely detectable after vaccination. These results support the need to explore booster doses or improved vaccines to enhance cellular immunity in at‐risk populations. More studies are needed to evaluate the capacity of mpox vaccines to confer long‐term protection.","Olivia de la Calle-Jiménez, Guiomar Casado-Fernández, Inés Armenteros-Yeguas, Luis Lemus-Aguilar, B. Baza, J. Pérez-García, Javier Rodríguez-Añover, E. Mateos, Reynaldo Homen, Eva Orviz-García, Noemí Cabello, A. Negredo, M. P. S. Seco, J. del Romero, Vicente Estrada, Montserrat Torres, M. Coiras",10.1002/jmv.70498,https://doi.org/10.1002/jmv.70498,Journal of Medical Virology,2,2025,yes,"The study includes people with HIV-1 (PWH), meaning it is not exclusively limited to healthy adults.",no,"The study measures T-cell immunity markers rather than clinical effectiveness outcomes like prevention of infection, hospitalization, or death.",yes,This is a primary research study comparing vaccinated and infected individuals.,yes,The study involves human participants recruited from clinical populations.,no,"The study specifies recruitment of PWH and PrEP users, which in this context corresponds to adult populations, with no mention of pregnant women, neonates, or children.",no,"There is no evidence that vaccination occurred during pregnancy, childhood, or the neonatal period.",yes,"The study uses Imvanex and Jynneos, which are vaccinia-based mpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study focuses on immunological responses in PWH and PrEP users, but it does not include the required target populations of children or pregnant women. Additionally, it reports on T-cell immunity rather than clinical effectiveness outcomes such as the prevention of infection or death.","Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""We recruited PWH and PrEP users who received one or two doses of subcutaneous Imvanex® or Jynneos®""",,"  - ""compared specific T‐cell immunity with participants who passed mpox natural infection""
