Monkeypox (Mpox) vaccines and their side effects: the other side of the coin,"On the verge of smallpox eradication, human monkeypox (Mpox) – as recommended by the WHO (https://www.who.int/ news/item/28-11-2022-who-recommends-new-name-for-mon keypox-disease) – was discovered in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC), in the year 1970, when sporadic cases were reported in rural areas of DRC [1] . It is a zoonosis without any identi ﬁ ed de ﬁ nitive reservoir host. Other than DRC, it has been reported from adjacent West and Central African countries, including Nigeria, Cameroon, Sierra Leone, the Republic of Congo, and Liberia [2] . For decades the virus was restricted to this part of the world only, unless outbreaks started getting reported from different nonendemic countries since early May 2022. The number of cases increased so much globally that WHO declared this multicountry outbreak as a Public Health Emergency of International Concern on 23 July 2022 [3] . As of 23 November 2022, the total number of con ﬁ rmed Mpox cases is 80,850, with 55 deaths involving 110 countries, out of which 103 are nonendemic [3] . Mpox","R. Sah, Diptanu Paul, A. Mohanty, Abhishek Shah, A. MohanaSundaram, B. Padhi",10.1097/JS9.0000000000000142,https://doi.org/10.1097/JS9.0000000000000142,International Journal of Surgery,20,2023,maybe,"The paper discusses patients with confirmed Mpox infection rather than healthy individuals. However, it does not specify the age or pregnancy status of the infected group beyond general human cases.",maybe,"The title indicates a focus on 'side effects', which are safety outcomes, but the provided text does not yet report results or specific safety data.",maybe,"The title and introduction suggest this may be a review or commentary on the global outbreak and vaccine side effects, which could qualify as an evidence synthesis, but the text is insufficient to confirm the exact methodology.",yes,The text explicitly refers to 'human monkeypox' and global human case counts.,no,"The provided text does not mention the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it only provides an aggregate count of 80,850 global cases.",no,"There is no evidence in the text that vaccination occurred during pregnancy, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,"The title identifies the subject as 'Monkeypox (Mpox) vaccines', which typically refer to vaccinia-based vaccines.",Exclude,"While the paper addresses human Mpox cases and the side effects of vaccines, it fails to mention or include the specific target populations of pregnant women or children required by the criteria. Because it does not meet the mandatory population focus, it is excluded.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""total number of con ﬁ rmed Mpox cases is 80,850, with 55 deaths involving 110 countries,""
