"Metanalysis of Vaccine Efficacy for Monkeypox According to Clinical Criteria of History Taking, Clinical Features and Laboratory Investigation","Monkeypox virus is DNA virus of Orthopoxvirus. The Monkeypox is zoonosis disease first diagnosed in the Democratic Republic of the Congo in 1970 and originally transmitted from Vertebrate Reservoir. In 1796, Edward Jenner was the first physician achieved vaccine and prove its efficacy through exposing James Phipps to smallpox after immunization. The current study revises the vaccine efficacy and mortality rate of monkeypox. It is mainly transmitted from Squirrels to human and cause mortality rate ranging from 2.7 to 10.1%. Globally, it estimated 4.4% the mortality rate of Monkeypox cases. Based on current review study, the effectiveness of smallpox vaccine in preventing the monkeypox is quite variable due to several selective clinical cases criteria such as history taking and clinical features and laboratory investigation, which modify the statistical result of the vaccine efficacy. The current study clarifies the faults of calculation by eliminating several reasons and estimating the effectiveness of vaccine in the household and find it to be 16.3% whereas total Vaccine efficacy is 0.6%. Using preventive measures are the first line to avoid infection therefore healthcare workers must adhere to infection control precautions. A current study warns scientists to create monkeypox vaccine rather than depending on smallpox vaccine effectiveness due to lacking of current vaccine effectiveness evidence and treatment.","W. A. Talalwah, Shorok Aldorazi",10.9734/arrb/2022/v37i1130551,https://doi.org/10.9734/arrb/2022/v37i1130551,Annual Research &amp; Review in Biology,2,2022,maybe,The abstract does not specify the demographic composition of its household or global effectiveness calculations beyond referring to them as human cases.,yes,The study explicitly calculates vaccine efficacy and mortality rates for monkeypox.,yes,The title and text identify the study as a metanalysis.,yes,The study focuses on the transmission and prevention of monkeypox in humans.,no,"The study does not state that pregnant women, neonates, or children were included as participants in its meta-analysis of monkeypox efficacy. The reference to James Phipps is a historical anecdote regarding smallpox, not a participant in the current analysis of monkeypox vaccine effectiveness.",no,"There is no evidence that the vaccinations analyzed in the meta-analysis occurred during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,The study specifically evaluates the effectiveness of the smallpox vaccine against monkeypox.,Exclude,"This study is a meta-analysis of smallpox vaccine effectiveness for monkeypox. While it reports general efficacy and mortality outcomes, it fails to include or analyze the specific target populations (pregnant women, neonates, or children) required for this review, mentioning a pediatric case only in a historical context.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""transmitted from Squirrels to human""
  - ""effectiveness of vaccine in the household and find it to be 16.3% whereas total Vaccine efficacy is 0.6%.""",,"  - ""revises the vaccine efficacy and mortality rate of monkeypox.""",,,,"  - ""transmitted from Squirrels to human""",,"  - ""effectiveness of vaccine in the household and find it to be 16.3% whereas total Vaccine efficacy is 0.6%.""
