lla (chickenpox) vaccine, not a vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"The paper describes a pregnancy registry for the VARIVAX vaccine. Because VARIVAX is a varicella vaccine and not a vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccine, it fails the inclusion criteria for vaccine type.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
Efficacy of three key antiviral drugs used to treat orthopoxvirus infections: a systematic review,"Background: In a global political climate increasingly concerned about terrorism, bioterrorism agents such as smallpox would undoubtedly be catastrophic. Since WHO announced the eradication of smallpox in 1980, consequently discontinuing the worldwide vaccination campaign, today’s population is either immunologically naive or has waning levels of protection. Further, up to 25% of today’s population are contraindicated for smallpox vaccination due to various immunodeficiency conditions. The aim of this study was to evaluate the efficacy of the anti-DNA antivirals cidofovir (CDV), brincidofovir (BCV), and tecovirimat. As of July 2018, FDA approved tecovirimat as the first treatment for smallpox. Methods: A systematic review was conducted to identify relevant literature describing the efficacy and safety of CDV, BCV and tecovirimat in vitro, in vivo animal studies, human safety trials and human case reports of orthopoxvirus infection. Results: 158 studies met the inclusion criteria. CDV, BCV and tecovirimat are highly effective in vitro and in vivo animal studies when used therapeutically and prophylactically. They are partially protective in moderate, but not severe, immunodeficiency models. Clinical trials consistently report BCV and tecovirimat to be safe and well tolerated in humans. In human case reports, CDV, BCV and tecovirimat contributed to recovery from orthopoxvirus infection. BCV and tecovirimat demonstrate strong synergistic effect, and may reduce risk of antiviral-resistant strains. Conclusion: BCV and tecovirimat are particularly promising as anti-smallpox agents. Gaps in the literature indicate that further research should focus on developing more robust immunodeficiency and antiviral-resistance models.","Joyce Yu, S. M. Raj",10.31646/GBIO.12,https://doi.org/10.31646/GBIO.12,Global Biosecurity,29,2019,yes,"The study includes human case reports of infection and animal studies, meaning it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study evaluates the efficacy and safety of the drugs in question.,yes,The study is a systematic review.,yes,The review includes human safety trials and human case reports.,maybe,"The abstract mentions human safety trials and case reports but does not explicitly state whether children, neonates, or pregnant women were included in those studies.",no,"The study participants were evaluated for the efficacy of antiviral drugs, not vaccines. No information is provided regarding vaccination during pregnancy or childhood.",no,"The study is focused on the efficacy of antiviral drugs (cidofovir, brincidofovir, tecovirimat) rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This systematic review evaluates the efficacy and safety of antiviral drugs (cidofovir, brincidofovir, and tecovirimat) for treating orthopoxvirus infections. Because it focuses on therapeutic drugs rather than vaccines and does not specifically address the target populations of children or pregnant women, it fails to meet the required intervention and population criteria.","Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""human safety trials and human case reports of orthopoxvirus infection.""
  - ""CDV, BCV and tecovirimat are highly effective in vitro and in vivo animal studies""",,"  - ""The aim of this study was to evaluate the efficacy of the anti-DNA antivirals cidofovir (CDV), brincidofovir (BCV), and tecovirimat.""
  - ""A systematic review was conducted to identify relevant literature describing the efficacy and safety of CDV, BCV and tecovirimat""",,"  - ""A systematic review was conducted to identify relevant literature describing the efficacy and safety of CDV, BCV and tecovirimat""",,"  - ""human safety trials and human case reports of orthopoxvirus infection.""",,"  - ""human safety trials and human case reports of orthopoxvirus infection.""
