Newborn and Early Infant Outcomes Following Maternal COVID-19 Vaccination During Pregnancy,This study attempts to determine if maternal mRNA COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy is associated with adverse newborn and early infant outcomes.,"Sarah C J Jorgensen, Samantha S M Drover, D.B. Fell, Peter C Austin, R. D’Souza, Astrid Guttmann, S. Buchan, Sarah E Wilson, S. Nasreen, K. L. Schwartz, M. Tadrous, Kumanan Wilson, Jeffrey C. Kwong",10.1001/jamapediatrics.2023.4499,https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapediatrics.2023.4499,JAMA pediatrics,46,2023,yes,"The study focuses on pregnant women and their infants, not exclusively healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports on adverse newborn and early infant outcomes.,yes,The study is a primary research effort evaluating associations between vaccination and outcomes.,yes,The study involves human participants (mothers and infants).,yes,The study includes pregnant women and infants.,yes,Participants received the vaccination during pregnancy.,no,The study evaluates mRNA COVID-19 vaccines rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"While the study focuses on pregnancy and infant outcomes, it investigates mRNA COVID-19 vaccination rather than the required mpox or smallpox vaccines. Consequently, it does not meet the intervention criteria for this review.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""maternal mRNA COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy""",,"  - ""adverse newborn and early infant outcomes.""",,"  - ""maternal mRNA COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy""",,"  - ""maternal mRNA COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy""
