"JYNNEOS Vaccine Safety Surveillance During the 2022 Mpox Outbreak Using the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System and V-safe, United States, 2022 to 2023","Postlicensure and postauthorization vaccine safety monitoring of JYNNEOS during the 2022 US mpox outbreak did not identify any unexpected safety concerns. Background In response to the 2022 mpox outbreak in the United States, people with higher potential for exposure to mpox were recommended to receive 2 doses of the JYNNEOS vaccine. Vaccine safety was monitored using 2 complementary systems. Methods The Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS) is a passive surveillance system that accepts reports of adverse events after vaccination. VAERS is capable of rapidly identifying rare adverse events and unusual reporting patterns. Medical records were requested and reviewed for adverse events of special interest, including myocarditis. Adverse event reporting rates were calculated as the number of verified adverse event cases divided by the number of JYNNEOS doses administered. V-safe for mpox was a voluntary smartphone-based vaccine safety surveillance system that sent enrolled persons text messages linked to health surveys asking about reactions and health impact events occurring after vaccination. Results There were 1,207,056 JYNNEOS doses administered in the United States. VAERS received 1927 reports for JYNNEOS. The myocarditis reporting rate per million doses was 2.69 after dose 1 and 8.64 after dose 2. V-safe had 213 participants complete at least one health survey. Rates of injection site and systemic reactions were similar in the first week after dose 1 and dose 2. Conclusions JYNNEOS vaccine safety surveillance findings from VAERS and v-safe did not identify any unexpected safety concerns. The VAERS reporting rate for myocarditis was similar to previously published population background rates.","J. Duffy, T. Myers, P. Marquez, Douglas Rouse, Hannah Brown, Bicheng Zhang, D. Shay, P. Moro",10.1097/OLQ.0000000000001978,https://doi.org/10.1097/OLQ.0000000000001978,Sexually Transmitted Diseases,7,2024,maybe,"The study analyzes national surveillance data (VAERS and V-safe) during the US mpox outbreak. These surveillance systems capture data from any vaccine recipient, not exclusively healthy non-pregnant adults, although specific subgroups are not detailed in the abstract.",yes,"The study explicitly reports on safety outcomes, including verified myocarditis cases and injection site/systemic reactions.",yes,This is a primary research study using surveillance data (passive and active surveillance) which constitutes a cohort-style observation of national vaccine recipients.,yes,The study involves human participants reported through VAERS and V-safe systems.,maybe,"While VAERS and V-safe generally include all age groups, the abstract does not mention the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents specifically. It describes the general population vaccinated during the outbreak.",maybe,"The abstract does not specify vaccination occurred during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood, as the age/status profile is not provided.",yes,"The study focuses on the JYNNEOS vaccine, which is a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine.",Include,"This study reports on JYNNEOS vaccine safety using national surveillance systems (VAERS and V-safe) in the US. While it provides critical safety outcomes such as myocarditis rates, it is unclear if the data include the specific target subpopulations like children or pregnant women without reviewing the full-text age and status demographics.",,,3.4,"  - ""Vaccine safety was monitored using 2 complementary systems.""
  - ""The Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS) is a passive surveillance system""
  - ""V-safe for mpox was a voluntary smartphone-based vaccine safety surveillance system""",,"  - ""The myocarditis reporting rate per million doses was 2.69 after dose 1 and 8.64 after dose 2.""
