Does HPV vaccination in pregnancy lead to adverse pregnancy outcomes?,"Department of Family Medicine and Community Health, New Brunswick, NJ The authors declare no conflicts of interest.","K. Oo, B. J. McGarry, Jennifer Amico",10.1097/ebp.0000000000000096,https://doi.org/10.1097/ebp.0000000000000096,Evidence-Based Practice,0,2018,yes,"The title indicates the study focuses on pregnant women, who are not part of the healthy non-pregnant adult population.",no,"The study reports adverse pregnancy outcomes for HPV vaccination, which are safety outcomes, but they are not related to mpox infection or historical smallpox/vaccinia vaccine efficacy/safety.",maybe,"The venue 'Evidence-Based Practice' typically includes clinical evidence syntheses, but the specific design isn't clarified in the provided text, though it appears to be a study or review focused on specific intervention outcomes.",yes,The study focuses on pregnancy outcomes in humans.,yes,The study explicitly includes pregnant women.,yes,The study evaluates vaccination specifically 'in pregnancy'.,no,"The study specifies 'HPV vaccination', which is not a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine or a historical smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This study is excluded because it evaluates the HPV vaccine rather than vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines. Additionally, it does not report safety or effectiveness outcomes relevant to mpox prevention or treatment.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
"Elderly Individuals Exhibit Elevated Levels of Anti‐Monkeypox Virus Antibodies Compared to Adults, Youth, and Children","The variola virus, the causative agent of smallpox, has claimed more human lives than all other infectious diseases combined. Consequently, any epidemiological event involving related viruses (orthopoxviruses) triggers significant concern, as was the case with the pandemic rise of monkeypox virus (MPXV), the causative agent of monkeypox in 2022−2023. Smallpox vaccines, based on vaccinia virus antigens, are expected to provide cross‐protection against MPXV. Studying the general seroprevalence of orthopoxviruses in populations worldwide is crucial for estimating susceptibility, risks, and the need for vaccination campaigns. In this study, we aimed to evaluate the seroprevalence of antibodies capable of recognizing MPXV in a Brazilian population and assess the potential for cross‐protection from previous smallpox vaccinations. We analyzed demographic data, vaccination records, and serum levels of anti‐MPXV IgG measured by enzyme‐linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) in 319 volunteers from Barreiras, Bahia, Brazil. Our main findings show that the numbers of volunteers with serum samples recognizing MPXV in ELISA, as well as their serum levels of anti‐MPXV IgG, did not differ significantly according to gender or address type. However, both male and female elderly volunteers exhibited significantly higher concentrations of anti‐MPXV IgG than adults, youth, and children. These results suggest that the elderly may benefit from a cross‐reactive immune response due to previous smallpox vaccinations, whereas adults, youth, and children appear susceptible to MPXV. Further investigations involving other populations are necessary to assess the necessity of vaccinating potentially vulnerable groups.","Izabelle Caires Moreira Dos Santos, Milena Silva Souza, M. M. C. Fogaça, Ruth Dálety da Silva Brito, Amanda da Rocha Santos, J. H. Amorim",10.1002/jmv.70083,https://doi.org/10.1002/jmv.70083,Journal of Medical Virology,0,2024,yes,The study population is not limited to healthy non-pregnant adults; it explicitly includes children and youth.,no,"The study only reports seroprevalence (IgG levels). It does not report safety outcomes or clinical effectiveness outcomes such as the prevention of mpox infection, hospitalization, or death.",yes,This is a primary research study analyzing seroprevalence in a group of human volunteers.,yes,The study involves human volunteers (319 individuals).,yes,The study includes youth and children as part of its demographic comparison.,maybe,"While the elderly participants likely received the smallpox vaccine during childhood, the study notes that the youth and children appear susceptible, implying they have not been vaccinated. The study does not evaluate outcomes for individuals vaccinated during childhood/adolescence as its primary outcome is current seroprevalence across ages.",yes,The study investigates cross-protection stemming from previous smallpox vaccinations.,Exclude,"The study aims to evaluate the seroprevalence of anti-MPXV IgG antibodies across different age groups, including children and youth. However, it does not meet the outcome criteria as it reports laboratory serological data (IgG levels) rather than clinical effectiveness or safety outcomes like the prevention of infection or hospitalization.",Relevant Outcomes,Relevant Outcomes,0.1,"  - ""both male and female elderly volunteers exhibited significantly higher concentrations of anti‐MPXV IgG than adults, youth, and children.""",,"  - ""We analyzed demographic data, vaccination records, and serum levels of anti‐MPXV IgG measured by enzyme‐linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) in 319 volunteers from Barreiras, Bahia, Brazil.""",,"  - ""We analyzed demographic data, vaccination records, and serum levels of anti‐MPXV IgG measured by enzyme‐linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) in 319 volunteers from Barreiras, Bahia, Brazil.""",,"  - ""We analyzed demographic data, vaccination records, and serum levels of anti‐MPXV IgG measured by enzyme‐linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) in 319 volunteers from Barreiras, Bahia, Brazil.""",,"  - ""both male and female elderly volunteers exhibited significantly higher concentrations of anti‐MPXV IgG than adults, youth, and children.""",,"  - ""These results suggest that the elderly may benefit from a cross‐reactive immune response due to previous smallpox vaccinations, whereas adults, youth, and children appear susceptible to MPXV.""",,"  - ""In this study, we aimed to evaluate the seroprevalence of antibodies capable of recognizing MPXV in a Brazilian population and assess the potential for cross‐protection from previous smallpox vaccinations.""",
"Residual Immunity from Smallpox Vaccination and Possible Protection from Mpox, China","Among persons born in China before 1980 and tested for vaccinia virus Tiantan strain (VVT), 28.7% (137/478) had neutralizing antibodies, 71.4% (25/35) had memory B-cell responses, and 65.7% (23/35) had memory T-cell responses to VVT. Because of cross-immunity between the viruses, these findings can help guide mpox vaccination strategies in China.","Yu Huang, Li Guo, Yanan Li, Lili Ren, Jiqin Nie, Fengwen Xu, Tingxuan Huang, J. Zhong, Zhangling Fan, Yin Zhang, Yu Xie, Qiao Zhang, S. Mei, Yan Xiao, Xinming Wang, Liuhui Xu, Fei Guo, Jianwei Wang",10.3201/eid3002.230542,https://doi.org/10.3201/eid3002.230542,Emerging Infectious Diseases,9,2024,no,"The study focuses on adults born before 1980, who would be approximately 43 years or older at the time of study. There is no indication of study inclusion for pregnant women or non-adults.",no,"The study reports immunological outcomes (antibodies and cell responses), but does not report clinical effectiveness (prevention of infection, hospitalization, or death) or safety outcomes based on the provided text.",yes,The study appears to be a cross-sectional primary research study evaluating immunity in a specific cohort.,yes,The study involves testing human subjects who were born before 1980.,no,"The study population is defined as persons born before 1980, which explicitly excludes children and adolescents (<18 years) and does not mention pregnant women or neonates.",maybe,"While participants likely received the vaccinia vaccine during childhood (given the 1980 cutoff for compulsory smallpox vaccination in China), the study is measuring residual immunity in adults. However, because it fails the target population criteria, this is moot.",yes,The study specifically evaluates immunity to the vaccinia virus Tiantan strain (VVT).,Exclude,"This study focuses on residual immunity in adults born before 1980. It fails the target population criteria as it does not include children, adolescents, or pregnant women, and it focuses on laboratory immunological outcomes rather than clinical safety or effectiveness.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Target Population",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""persons born in China before 1980""",,"  - ""28.7% (137/478) had neutralizing antibodies, 71.4% (25/35) had memory B-cell responses, and 65.7% (23/35) had memory T-cell responses to VVT.""",,"  - ""persons born in China before 1980""
