"""MATERNAL AND FETAL OUTCOMES FOLLOWING COVID-19 VACCINATION DURING PREGNANCY”: A RETROSPECTIVE COHORT STUDY","Introduction: The safety and efcacy of COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy are still being studied. The purpose of this retrospective cohort
study was to look into the incidence of adverse outcomes in pregnant women who received COVID-19 vaccination during the second and third
trimesters, the prevalence of adverse outcomes in neonates born to vaccinated mothers, and the overall safety prole of COVID-19 vaccination in
terms of maternal and fetal outcomes. This retrospective cohort study was ca Methods: rried in patients who were admitted in Lalla Ded hospital,
Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology Government Medical College Srinagar during the study period from September 2021 to March 2022.
The study included 565 pregnant women who had received COVID-19 vaccination. The ndings reve Results: aled that the incidence of adverse
outcomes was low in pregnant women who received COVID-19 vaccination, with 3 cases of stillbirths, 5 neonatal death and 4 fetal abnormalities.
124 neonates, on the other hand, were admitted to the NICU, primarily due to Meconium Aspiration Syndrome (74) and low birth weight (36).
Maternal outcomes were also positive, with no reports of PPH or APH. COVID-19 vaccination during preg Conclusion: nancy had a generally
positive safety prole, with no serious adverse events reported. The lack of a comparison group and the retrospective nature of the data are two of
the study's limitations. Overall, the ndings of this study provide important information about the safety and efcacy of COVID-19 vaccination
during pregnancy, which can be used to inform clinical care and public health policies","Bilal Ahmad Wani, Syed Masuma razvi, Nasir Rashid, Asif Iqbal, Waafia Muzafar",10.36106/ijsr/9501264,https://doi.org/10.36106/ijsr/9501264,International journal of scientific research,0,2023,yes,The study focuses specifically on 565 pregnant women.,yes,"The study reports safety outcomes including stillbirths, neonatal deaths, and fetal abnormalities.",yes,The study is a retrospective cohort study.,yes,The study was carried out in human patients in a hospital setting.,yes,The study participants are pregnant women.,yes,Vaccination occurred during the second and third trimesters of pregnancy.,no,"The study investigates COVID-19 vaccination, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the safety of COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant women. While it meets population and design criteria, it fails the intervention requirement as it does not involve vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""The study included 565 pregnant women who had received COVID-19 vaccination.""",,"  - ""incidence of adverse

