"Effectiveness of Maternal Vaccination with mRNA COVID-19 Vaccine During Pregnancy Against COVID-19–Associated Hospitalization in Infants Aged <6 Months — 17 States, July 2021–January 2022","COVID-19 vaccination is recommended for persons who are pregnant, breastfeeding, trying to get pregnant now, or who might become pregnant in the future, to protect them from COVID-19.§ Infants are at risk for life-threatening complications from COVID-19, including acute respiratory failure (1). Evidence from other vaccine-preventable diseases suggests that maternal immunization can provide protection to infants, especially during the high-risk first 6 months of life, through passive transplacental antibody transfer (2). Recent studies of COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy suggest the possibility of transplacental transfer of SARS-CoV-2-specific antibodies that might provide protection to infants (3-5); however, no epidemiologic evidence currently exists for the protective benefits of maternal immunization during pregnancy against COVID-19 in infants. The Overcoming COVID-19 network conducted a test-negative, case-control study at 20 pediatric hospitals in 17 states during July 1, 2021-January 17, 2022, to assess effectiveness of maternal completion of a 2-dose primary mRNA COVID-19 vaccination series during pregnancy against COVID-19 hospitalization in infants. Among 379 hospitalized infants aged <6 months (176 with COVID-19 [case-infants] and 203 without COVID-19 [control-infants]), the median age was 2 months, 21% had at least one underlying medical condition, and 22% of case- and control-infants were born premature (<37 weeks gestation). Effectiveness of maternal vaccination during pregnancy against COVID-19 hospitalization in infants aged <6 months was 61% (95% CI = 31%-78%). Completion of a 2-dose mRNA COVID-19 vaccination series during pregnancy might help prevent COVID-19 hospitalization among infants aged <6 months.","N. Halasa, Samantha M. Olson, M. Staat, M. Newhams, Ashley M. Price, J. Boom, Leila C. Sahni, M. Cameron, P. Pannaraj, Katherine E. Bline, Samina S. Bhumbra, T. Bradford, K. Chiotos, B. Coates, M. Cullimore, N. Cvijanovich, H. Flori, S. Gertz, S. Heidemann, C. Hobbs, J. Hume, K. Irby, S. Kamidani, M. Kong, Emily R. Levy, E. Mack, A. Maddux, K. Michelson, Ryan A. Nofziger, J. Schuster, S. Schwartz, L. Smallcomb, Keiko M. Tarquinio, Tracie C. Walker, M. Zinter, S. Gilboa, Kara N. Polen, A. Campbell, A. Randolph, M. Patel, Laura D. Zambrano, M. Murdock, Mary G Gaspers, Connor P. Kelley, Katri V. Typpo, Peter M. Mourani, R. Sanders, Charanne P. Smith, M. Yates, Katheryn Crane, G. Lionetti, Juliana Murcia-Montoya, Denise Villarreal-Chico, Daniel Hakimi, Adam Skura, Imogene A. Carson, Justin Lockwood, Emily Port, Brandon Chatani, N. Baida, L. Hussaini, Hassan A. Khan, Simone T. Rhodes, C. Rowan, M. Stumpf, Marla S. Johnston, L. Berbert, Benjamin J. Boutselis, Sabrina Chen, Jie He, S. Kucukak, Timothy P. McCadden, Amber O. Orzel, Edie Weller, P. Moran, Ellen R. Bruno, Lexie A. Goertzen, Supriya Behl, Noelle M. Drapeau, Lacy Malloch, Lora M Martin, A. Palmer, R. Santos, Abigail P. Kietzman, melissa. sullivan, Lauren A. Hoody, Valerie H. Rinehart, P. Bennett, Merry L. Tomcany, Nicole A. Twinem, Chelsea C. Rohlfs, Amber Wolfe, R. Douglas, Kathlyn Phengchomphet, Megan M. Bickford, Lauren E. Wakefield, Meenakshi Golchha, Laura S. Stewart, Jennifer N. Oates, Cindy Bowens, Mia Maamari",10.15585/mmwr.mm7107e3,https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm7107e3,MMWR. Morbidity and mortality weekly report,184,2022,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women and their infants.,yes,The study reports on the effectiveness of vaccination against COVID-19 hospitalization in infants.,yes,"The study is a test-negative, case-control study.",yes,The study involves human participants (hospitalized infants and their mothers).,yes,The study includes pregnant women and infants aged <6 months.,yes,The vaccination occurred during pregnancy.,no,"The study evaluates mRNA COVID-19 vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the effectiveness of maternal mRNA COVID-19 vaccination against COVID-19 hospitalizations in infants. While it includes relevant populations like pregnant women and infants, it does not assess vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines, which is a requirement for this review.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""assess effectiveness of maternal completion of a 2-dose primary mRNA COVID-19 vaccination series during pregnancy against COVID-19 hospitalization in infants.""
