JYNNEOS™ effectiveness as post-exposure prophylaxis against mpox: Challenges using real-world outbreak data.,"BACKGROUND
JYNNEOSTM vaccine has been used as post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) during a mpox outbreak in New York City (NYC). Data on effectiveness are limited.
METHODS
Effectiveness of a single dose of JYNNEOSTM vaccine administered subcutaneously ≤ 14 days as PEP for preventing mpox disease was assessed among individuals exposed to case-patients from May 22, 2022-August 24, 2022. Individuals were evaluated for mpox through 21 days post-exposure. An observational study was conducted emulating a sequence of nested ""target"" randomized trials starting each day after exposure. Results were adjusted for exposure risk and race/ethnicity. Analyses were conducted separately based on last (PEPL) and first (PEPF) exposure date. We evaluated the potential to overestimate PEP effectiveness when using conventional analytic methods due to exposed individuals developing illness before they can obtain PEP (immortal time bias) compared to the target trial.
RESULTS
Median time from last exposure to symptom onset (incubation period) among cases that did not receive PEPL was 7 days (range 1-16). Time to PEPL receipt was 7 days (range 0-14). Among 549 individuals, adjusted PEPL and PEPF effectiveness was 19 % (95 % Confidence Interval [CI], -54 % to 57 %) and -7% (95 % CI, -144 % to 53 %) using the target trial emulation, respectively, and 78 % (95 % CI, 50 % to 91 %) and 73 % (95 % CI, 31 % to 91 %) using conventional analysis.
CONCLUSIONS
Determining PEP effectiveness using real-world data during an outbreak is challenging. Time to PEP in NYC coupled with the observed incubation period resulted in overestimated PEP effectiveness using a conventional method. The target trial emulation, while yielding wide confidence intervals due to small sample size, avoided immortal time bias. While results from these evaluations cannot be used as reliable estimates of PEP effectiveness, we present important methodologic considerations for future evaluations.","Jennifer B. Rosen, Robert J. Arciuolo, P. Pathela, Christopher B Boyer, Jennifer Baumgartner, Julia Latash, L. Malec, Ellen H. Lee, V. Reddy, R. King, Joseph Edward Real, Marc Lipsitch, Jane R. Zucker",10.1016/j.vaccine.2023.12.066,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2023.12.066,Vaccine,27,2024,maybe,"The study investigates individuals exposed to mpox in New York City. However, the abstract does not specify the age range or whether the population was limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports on the effectiveness of preventing mpox disease.,yes,"The study is an observational target trial emulation, which is a primary research design.",yes,The study evaluates human individuals exposed to cases in NYC.,maybe,"The abstract does not mention the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents. It describes 'individuals' without demographic breakdowns that would confirm the presence of these priority groups.",maybe,"The study focuses on post-exposure prophylaxis timing, but it does not specify if the participants were children or adolescents at the time of vaccination.",yes,"The study evaluates JYNNEOS, which is a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine.",Include,"This observational study evaluates the effectiveness of the JYNNEOS vaccine as post-exposure prophylaxis in New York City. While it addresses the correct intervention and outcomes, the abstract provides no information regarding the inclusion of the required target populations (pregnant women or minors).",,,3.6,"  - ""Effectiveness of a single dose of JYNNEOSTM vaccine administered subcutaneously ≤ 14 days as PEP for preventing mpox disease was assessed among individuals exposed to case-patients from May 22, 2022-August 24, 2022.""",,"  - ""Effectiveness of a single dose of JYNNEOSTM vaccine administered subcutaneously ≤ 14 days as PEP for preventing mpox disease was assessed among individuals exposed to case-patients from May 22, 2022-August 24, 2022.""",,"  - ""An observational study was conducted emulating a sequence of nested ""target"" randomized trials""",,"  - ""Effectiveness of a single dose of JYNNEOSTM vaccine administered subcutaneously ≤ 14 days as PEP for preventing mpox disease was assessed among individuals exposed to case-patients from May 22, 2022-August 24, 2022.""",,"  - ""Effectiveness of a single dose of JYNNEOSTM vaccine administered subcutaneously ≤ 14 days as PEP for preventing mpox disease was assessed among individuals exposed to case-patients from May 22, 2022-August 24, 2022.""",,"  - ""Effectiveness of a single dose of JYNNEOSTM vaccine administered subcutaneously ≤ 14 days as PEP for preventing mpox disease was assessed among individuals exposed to case-patients from May 22, 2022-August 24, 2022.""",,"  - ""Effectiveness of a single dose of JYNNEOSTM vaccine administered subcutaneously ≤ 14 days as PEP for preventing mpox disease was assessed among individuals exposed to case-patients from May 22, 2022-August 24, 2022.""",
"Mpox Epidemiology and Vaccine Effectiveness, England, 2023","Reported mpox cases in England continued at a low but steady frequency during 2023. Of 137 cases reported in 2023, approximately half were acquired overseas and half were in vaccinated persons. Estimated effectiveness of 2-dose vaccine was 80%, and no vaccinated mpox patient was hospitalized.","H. Charles, K. Thorley, Charlie Turner, Kirsty F Bennet, N. Andrews, M. Bertran, Sema Mandal, G. Amirthalingam, M. E. Ramsay, H. Mohammed, K. Sinka",10.3201/eid3010.240292,https://doi.org/10.3201/eid3010.240292,Emerging Infectious Diseases,18,2024,yes,"The study focus is on 137 reported mpox cases, which constitutes a clinical population rather than exclusively healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports vaccine effectiveness and hospitalization status.,yes,This is an epidemiological study reporting on 137 cases and vaccine effectiveness.,yes,The study involves human mpox patients in England.,no,"There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents in the study population of 137 cases.",no,"There is no information indicating that participants were vaccinated during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,"The study assesses a 2-dose vaccine regimen for mpox in England in 2023, which utilized vaccinia-based vaccines (MVA-BN).",Exclude,"The paper evaluates the effectiveness of the mpox vaccine among 137 cases in England in 2023, but it does not specify the inclusion of children, adolescents, or pregnant women. Although it reports relevant clinical outcomes, it fails to meet the specific target population and vaccination timing requirements.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""137 cases reported in 2023,""",,"  - ""Estimated effectiveness of 2-dose vaccine was 80%,""
  - ""no vaccinated mpox patient was hospitalized.""",,"  - ""137 cases reported in 2023,""
