Human monkeypox virus in the shadow of the COVID-19 pandemic,"Background The end of smallpox in 1980 and the subsequent stopping of vaccination against smallpox was followed by the emergence of monkeypox (mpox), a viral disease of animal origin, meaning that it is transmitted from animal to human. The symptoms of mpox are similar to smallpox, except that they are less severe in terms of clinical features. In the case of public health, the mpox virus is one of the most important orthopoxviruses (such as variola, cowpox, and vaccinia) that come from the family Poxviridae. Mpox occurs mostly in central Africa and sometimes in tropical rainforests or some urban areas. Also, there are threats other than COVID-19, that must be addressed and prevented from spreading, as there has been an outbreak of mpox cases since May 7, 2022, throughout the USA, Europe, Australia, and part of Africa. Objectives In this review, we will discuss mpox between the past, the present and during the COVID-19 pandemic. Also, it offers an updated summary of the taxonomy, etiology, transmission, and epidemiology of mpox illness. In addition, the current review aims to highlight the importance of emerging pandemics in the same era such as mpox and COVID-19. Methods A literature search was done for the study using online sources like PubMed and Google Scholar. Publications in English were included. Data for study variables were extracted. After the duplicate articles were eliminated, full-text screening was performed on the papers' titles and abstracts. Results The evaluation included a series documenting mpox virus outbreaks, and both prospective and retrospectiveinvestigations. Conclusions monkeypox is a viral disease caused by the monkeypox virus (MPXV), which is primarily found in central and western Africa. The disease is transmitted from animals to humans and presents symptoms similar to those of smallpox, including fever, headache, muscle aches, and a rash. Monkeypox can lead to complications such as secondary integument infection, bronchopneumonia, sepsis, and encephalitis, as well as corneal infection that can result in blindness. There is no specific clinically proven treatment for monkeypox, and treatment is primarily supportive. However, antiviral drugs and vaccines are available for cross-protection against the virus, and strict infection control measures and vaccination of close contacts of affected individuals can help prevent and control outbreaks.","Almu’atasim Khamees, Sajeda Awadi, K. Al-Shami, Hayat Abu Alkhoun, Sharaf F. Al-Eitan, A. Alsheikh, Ahmad Saeed, Raed M. Al‐Zoubi, M. A. Zoubi",10.1016/j.jiph.2023.05.013,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jiph.2023.05.013,Journal of Infection and Public Health,22,2023,maybe,"The study is a general review of human monkeypox virus and is not explicitly restricted to a specific health-state subset of adults, though it does not focus on the target populations either.",no,"While the review mentions that vaccines are available, it does not report specific safety or effectiveness outcomes (e.g., prevention of infection or death) for the vaccine in the required participant groups.",no,"This is described as an 'updated summary' and a 'review'. Although a literature search was conducted, it is a narrative review rather than a systematic review or meta-analysis.",yes,The study focuses on human monkeypox virus and epidemiology.,no,"The abstract does not mention including pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as specific study participants or focal points.",no,"The study does not describe vaccination occurring during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood/adolescence.",yes,The study mentions smallpox vaccination and vaccines for cross-protection against mpox.,Exclude,"This paper is a general narrative review of monkeypox etiology and epidemiology rather than a systematic review or primary research study. It fails several mandatory criteria as it does not report data for the target populations (pregnant women, children, or neonates) or specific vaccine outcomes.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""In this review, we will discuss mpox between the past, the present and during the COVID-19 pandemic. Also, it offers an updated summary of the taxonomy, etiology, transmission, and epidemiology of mpox illness.""",,"  - ""antiviral drugs and vaccines are available for cross-protection against the virus,""",,"  - ""In this review, we will discuss mpox between the past, the present and during the COVID-19 pandemic. Also, it offers an updated summary of the taxonomy, etiology, transmission, and epidemiology of mpox illness.""
  - ""A literature search was done for the study using online sources like PubMed and Google Scholar.""",,"  - ""In this review, we will discuss mpox between the past, the present and during the COVID-19 pandemic. Also, it offers an updated summary of the taxonomy, etiology, transmission, and epidemiology of mpox illness.""",,"  - ""In this review, we will discuss mpox between the past, the present and during the COVID-19 pandemic. Also, it offers an updated summary of the taxonomy, etiology, transmission, and epidemiology of mpox illness.""
  - ""The evaluation included a series documenting mpox virus outbreaks, and both prospective and retrospectiveinvestigations.""",,,,"  - ""the subsequent stopping of vaccination against smallpox was followed by the emergence of monkeypox""
  - ""antiviral drugs and vaccines are available for cross-protection against the virus,""",
Safety and Efficacy of Vaccines During Pregnancy: A Systematic Review,"Maternal immunization is a safe and effective strategy for protecting mothers and infants from vaccine-preventable diseases. This systematic review evaluated the safety and efficacy of various vaccines administered during pregnancy, focusing on maternal and infant outcomes. A comprehensive literature search was conducted in PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science to identify relevant studies. The search used terms and combinations such as (“maternal vaccination” OR “vaccination during pregnancy”) AND (“safety” OR “efficacy” OR “immunogenicity”) AND (“influenza” OR “DTaP” OR “respiratory syncytial virus” OR “group B streptococcus” OR “COVID-19”). Boolean operators “AND” and “OR” enhanced precision and filtered the limited results to studies published from 2018 to 2024. Eight studies were included in the review after applying inclusion and exclusion criteria. Influenza, diphtheria-tetanus-pertussis, respiratory syncytial virus, group B streptococcus, and COVID-19 vaccines are safe and effective when administered during pregnancy. These vaccines elicit robust immune responses in pregnant women, with efficient transplacental antibody transfer providing passive immunity to newborns. Adverse effects were mostly mild to moderate and similar to those observed in nonpregnant individuals. No significant increase in adverse pregnancy or neonatal outcomes was associated with maternal vaccination. Most of the included randomized controlled trials (had a low risk of bias, thus supporting the reliability of the findings. However, vaccine hesitancy remains a challenge, highlighting the need for transparent communication between healthcare providers and pregnant women. Future research should focus on long-term infant health outcomes, vaccine safety, immunogenicity in diverse populations, and strategies to optimize maternal immunization timing and enhance neonatal antibody persistence. This review supports the implementation of routine maternal vaccination programs and emphasizes the importance of addressing knowledge gaps and ensuring equitable access to immunization during pregnancy.","Lakshmi Venkata Sharmista Chittajallu, Rohini Kaku, Poshitha Kondadasula, Jun Yi Lim, A. Zhumabekova",10.7759/cureus.77176,https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.77176,Cureus,5,2025,yes,"The study focuses specifically on pregnant women and infants, not exclusively healthy non-pregnant adults.",maybe,"The study reports on safety and efficacy (effectiveness), though not for mpox. However, it does report on adverse pregnancy and neonatal outcomes.",yes,The study is explicitly identified as a systematic review.,yes,The review evaluates outcomes in human mothers and infants.,yes,The participants include pregnant women and their infants.,yes,The study specifically evaluates vaccines administered during pregnancy.,no,"The study evaluates influenza, DTaP, RSV, GBS, and COVID-19 vaccines; it does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This systematic review evaluates the safety and efficacy of maternal vaccination for several conditions such as influenza and COVID-19 in pregnant women and neonates. However, it fails the inclusion criteria because it does not investigate vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""This systematic review evaluated the safety and efficacy of various vaccines administered during pregnancy, focusing on maternal and infant outcomes.""",,"  - ""This systematic review evaluated the safety and efficacy of various vaccines administered during pregnancy, focusing on maternal and infant outcomes.""
  - ""No significant increase in adverse pregnancy or neonatal outcomes was associated with maternal vaccination.""",,"  - ""This systematic review evaluated the safety and efficacy of various vaccines administered during pregnancy, focusing on maternal and infant outcomes.""
  - ""A comprehensive literature search was conducted in PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science to identify relevant studies.""",,"  - ""This systematic review evaluated the safety and efficacy of various vaccines administered during pregnancy, focusing on maternal and infant outcomes.""",,"  - ""This systematic review evaluated the safety and efficacy of various vaccines administered during pregnancy, focusing on maternal and infant outcomes.""
  - ""No significant increase in adverse pregnancy or neonatal outcomes was associated with maternal vaccination.""",,"  - ""This systematic review evaluated the safety and efficacy of various vaccines administered during pregnancy, focusing on maternal and infant outcomes.""",,"  - ""The search used terms and combinations such as (“maternal vaccination” OR “vaccination during pregnancy”) AND (“safety” OR “efficacy” OR “immunogenicity”) AND (“influenza” OR “DTaP” OR “respiratory syncytial virus” OR “group B streptococcus” OR “COVID-19”).""",
"COVID-19 mRNA vaccine in pregnancy: Results of the Swiss COVI-PREG registry, an observational prospective cohort study","Summary Background Pregnant individuals with coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) are at increased risk of severe disease, prematurity, and stillbirth. In March 2021, vaccination for at risk pregnant women was recommended in Switzerland, expanding this to all pregnant women in May 2021. Our aim was to assess the safety of mRNA COVID-19 vaccines in pregnancy. Methods This multicentre prospective cohort study describes early adverse events and perinatal outcomes in pregnant women who received at least one dose of mRNA vaccine between March 1st and December 27th, 2021 in Switzerland, using the COVI-PREG registry. Early adverse events were collected at least one month following vaccine administration. Pregnancy and neonatal outcomes were extracted from medical records using the maternity discharge letters providing follow-up information up to 5 days after birth. Findings Of 1012 vaccinated women, 894 (88·3%) received both injections during pregnancy, with BNT162b2 (n = 271) or mRNA-1273 (n = 623) vaccines. Local events (mainly local pain) were reported in 81·3% and 80·5% after the first and second doses. Rates of systemic reactions (mainly fatigue and headache) were similar after the first dose and most frequent after the second dose of mRNA-1273. Of the 1012 women, four (0·4%; 95%CI [0·1-1·0]) severe early adverse events occurred: pulmonary embolism, preterm premature rupture of membranes, isolated fever with hospitalisation, and herpes zoster. Of 107 patients vaccinated before 14 weeks, one (0·9%; 95%CI [0·0-5·1]) early spontaneous abortions was reported (8 weeks). Of 228 vaccinated before 20 weeks one (0·4%; 95%CI [0·0-2·4]) late spontaneous abortion was reported (16 weeks). Of 513 women exposed before 37 weeks, 33 (6·4%; 95%CI [4·5-8·9]) delivered preterm. Among 530 patients exposed in pregnancy, no stillbirth was reported and 25 (4·7%; 95%CI [3·0-6·8]) neonates were admitted to intensive care unit. Interpretation Frequent local and systemic effects were described after exposure to mRNA COVID-19 vaccines during pregnancy but severe events were rare. Women vaccinated during pregnancy did not experience higher adverse pregnancy or neonatal outcomes when compared to historical data on background risks in the obstetric population. Funding This research was funded by a grant from the Swiss Federal Office of Public Health and the CHUV Foundation.","G. Favre, E. Maisonneuve, L. Pomar, U. Winterfeld, Charlotte Daire, B. Martinez de Tejada, Dominique Delecraz, Sonia Campelo, M. Moser, Monya Todesco-Bernasconi, Stefanie Sturm, I. Hösli, C. Monod, Brigitte Frey Tirri, S. Kalimeris, C. Blume, J. Mathis, R. Zimmerman, Anda Radan, D. Surbek, D. Baud, A. Panchaud",10.1016/j.lanepe.2022.100410,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.lanepe.2022.100410,The Lancet Regional Health - Europe,33,2022,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women.,yes,The study reports adverse events and perinatal outcomes such as spontaneous abortion and intensive care unit admission.,yes,This is a multicentre prospective cohort study.,yes,This is a study involving human pregnant women.,yes,The study specifically includes pregnant women.,yes,Participants received the vaccine injections during pregnancy.,no,The study investigates mRNA COVID-19 vaccines (BNT162b2 or mRNA-1273) rather than vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,This study is a prospective cohort study assessing safety outcomes in pregnant women who received mRNA COVID-19 vaccines. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.,Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""This multicentre prospective cohort study describes early adverse events and perinatal outcomes in pregnant women""",,"  - ""This multicentre prospective cohort study describes early adverse events and perinatal outcomes in pregnant women""",,"  - ""This multicentre prospective cohort study describes early adverse events and perinatal outcomes in pregnant women""",,"  - ""This multicentre prospective cohort study describes early adverse events and perinatal outcomes in pregnant women""",,"  - ""This multicentre prospective cohort study describes early adverse events and perinatal outcomes in pregnant women""",,"  - ""received both injections during pregnancy,""",,"  - ""Our aim was to assess the safety of mRNA COVID-19 vaccines in pregnancy.""
  - ""BNT162b2 (n = 271) or mRNA-1273 (n = 623) vaccines.""",
Transmission of mpox in educational settings and risk of severe disease in children and adolescents—Let's not make the same mistakes as COVID‐19 again,"Dear Editor, We read with interest the recent systematic review by Núñez‐ Cortés et al. on risk factors, modes of transmission and outcomes of mpox (formerly monkeypox). We were, however, concerned about the authors' conclusions regarding the risks and outcomes of mpox in children, based on the very limited published data. In particular, the authors' conclusions advocating the prevention of mpox outbreaks “in specific settings, especially in settings densely populated by children, such as day care centres and schools” is unwarranted and not supported by the findings of their systematic review, or indeed from any current evidence from the 2022 outbreak. In their systematic review that included 1480 cases with reported mode of transmission, no data were presented on mpox acquisition in children in educational settings in endemic or non‐ endemic countries. On the contrary, during the 2022 global outbreak due to the Clade IIb (West African) mpox strain, 84.4% of confirmed cases with known data on sexual orientation have been identified among gay, bisexual and men who have sex with men. In England, outbreak investigations in educational settings, including a nursery where close contact between the case and the toddlers was unavoidable, found no secondary mpox cases among 340 children and 118 staff members exposed to a confirmed mpox case. Similarly, despite 3582 cases confirmed in England between 6 May and 20 December 2022, including multiple household and other similar close‐contact exposures to children, only one case was confirmed in a child, a neonate with mpox and adenovirus co‐infection. Globally, reports of mpox disease in children and adolescents are also reassuring. As of 20 December 2022, across the six World Health Organization regions, only 1.3% (631/49,664) of cases with available detailed data were reported in 0–17 year‐olds, including 83 in the United States, and 77 in the European Union and European Economic Area countries. Of these, none had reported exposure in a school setting, highlighting a very low risk of mpox infection in children, especially when compared to adults. Additionally, even with the authors' acknowledgement that their mortality “risk estimates should be interpreted with uncertainty”, we are concerned about their conclusions of higher mortality risk in children, based on only 24 subjects reported by a small proportion of publications over a period of 50 years, and mainly from mpox‐ endemic countries. In the 2022 outbreak affecting 83,339 individuals worldwide, there have been no reported fatalities in children so far. All the available literature indicates a mild clinical course in children and adolescents irrespective of mode of transmission with the exception of one neonate with severe mpox and adenovirus co‐infection and one adolescent who had surgical drainage of secondary bacterial abscesses. At the same time, in endemic countries where mpox virus constantly circulates, children may appear to have more severe disease following their first exposure to the virus compared to adults who may be protected from severe symptoms because of prior immunity and/or smallpox vaccination. Interestingly, another recent systematic review found that 100% of reported mpox deaths (47/47) in 1970–1989 were in <10 year‐olds compared to only 37.5% (6/16) of deaths in 2000–2019. Similarly, the median age of mpox cases increased from 4 years of age in 1970–1989 to 21 years in 2010–2019. The cessation of the smallpox immunisation in the 1980s could have contributed to these changes in the epidemiology of the disease in endemic countries, since increasingly fewer people are now vaccinated against smallpox that would have partially protected them from mpox. Additionally, publications that do not systematically identify cases prior to assessing outcomes should be interpreted with caution. Asymptomatic and mild infections, for example, may be missed due to the lack of systematic testing and there may be publication biases associated with the reporting of more severe cases in the literature. Finally, this systematic review makes no distinction between mpox strains which are associated with different case fatality rates (1%– 12% for the Central African Clade I and <0.1% for the West African Clade II strain). Early in the 2022 global mpox outbreak, in the absence of any evidence, strict isolation of contacts along with hospitalisation and aggressive management of cases may have been justifiable. With emerging data, it is becoming increasingly clear that cases in children and adolescents are infrequent, the disease is generally mild and the risk of transmission in education settings is very small. In the UK, this has led to a stratified risk assessment based on the degree of exposure to the index case and the presence or absence of symptoms. Consequently, only a small proportion of children and adolescents would be classified as high‐risk, following close physical (e.g. kissing or cuddling from a household member with blisters or scabs) or sexual contact with a confirmed case. Importantly, isolation and exclusion","Konstantinos Karampatsas, A. Khalil, P. Heath, K. L. Doare, S. Ladhani",10.1002/rmv.2467,https://doi.org/10.1002/rmv.2467,Reviews in Medical Virology,0,2023,yes,"The study focuses specifically on children, adolescents, and neonates.",no,The paper discusses mpox transmission and severity but does not report safety or effectiveness outcomes for mpox or smallpox vaccines. It only discusses epidemiological trends and disease clinical course.,no,"The paper is a Letter to the Editor and does not constitute a primary research study (e.g., RCT, cohort) or an evidence synthesis (systematic review) by the authors.",yes,The paper discusses human epidemiological data and clinical cases.,yes,The study explicitly includes neonates and children/adolescents up to 17 years old.,no,"The study mentions the historical cessation of smallpox immunization but does not include participants who received a vaccine during the specified periods (pregnancy, childhood, etc.) in a prospective or retrospective study context.",yes,The paper mentions historical smallpox immunization.,Exclude,"This is a Letter to the Editor that comments on epidemiological trends and transmission risks of mpox in children, rather than being a primary research study or systematic review. It fails multiple required criteria, including study design and the reporting of vaccine safety or effectiveness outcomes during the specified target periods.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Vaccination Timing",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""only 1.3% (631/49,664) of cases with available detailed data were reported in 0–17 year‐olds,""
  - ""only one case was confirmed in a child, a neonate with mpox and adenovirus co‐infection.""",,"  - ""We were, however, concerned about the authors' conclusions regarding the risks and outcomes of mpox in children,""
  - ""only one case was confirmed in a child, a neonate with mpox and adenovirus co‐infection.""",,"  - ""Dear Editor, We read with interest the recent systematic review by Núñez‐ Cortés et al.""
  - ""We were, however, concerned about the authors' conclusions regarding the risks and outcomes of mpox in children,""",,"  - ""only one case was confirmed in a child, a neonate with mpox and adenovirus co‐infection.""
