An evaluation of measles and smallpox vaccines simultaneously administered.,"An evaluation of measles and smallpox vaccines simultaneously administered. M A Budd, R G Scholtens, R F McGehee, Jr, and P GardnerCopyRight https://doi.org/10.2105/AJPH.57.1.80 Published Online: August 29, 2011","M. A. Budd, R. Scholtens, R. F. Mcgehee, P. Gardner",10.2105/AJPH.57.1.80,https://doi.org/10.2105/AJPH.57.1.80,American Journal of Public Health and the Nations Health,15,1967,maybe,"The title suggests the use of measles and smallpox vaccines, which are typically administered to non-adult populations, but the provided text does not explicitly confirm the age or status of the study participants.",maybe,"The title mentions an evaluation of vaccines, which likely includes safety or effectiveness outcomes, but these are not specifically detailed in the limited content.",maybe,"The term evaluation suggests a primary research study, but the specific design (e.g., trial or cohort) is not stated.",maybe,"While typically an evaluation of measles and smallpox vaccines would be a human study, the text does not explicitly rule out laboratory or animal evaluation of administration techniques.",maybe,"The mention of measles vaccines strongly suggests the inclusion of children or infants, though the text does not explicitly define the cohort ages.",maybe,"The title points to an evaluation of vaccine administration, which likely occurred during the childhood period associated with measles vaccination, but specific timing is not mentioned.",yes,The title explicitly states the study involves smallpox vaccines.,Include,"The paper provides evidence that it evaluates smallpox vaccines. However, due to the absence of an abstract, specific details regarding the study population (e.g., age), outcomes, and design cannot be definitively confirmed, though the mention of measles vaccines makes pediatric involvement highly probable.",,,4.2,"  - ""An evaluation of measles and smallpox vaccines simultaneously administered.""",,"  - ""An evaluation of measles and smallpox vaccines simultaneously administered.""",,"  - ""An evaluation of measles and smallpox vaccines simultaneously administered.""",,"  - ""An evaluation of measles and smallpox vaccines simultaneously administered.""",,"  - ""An evaluation of measles and smallpox vaccines simultaneously administered.""",,"  - ""An evaluation of measles and smallpox vaccines simultaneously administered.""",,"  - ""An evaluation of measles and smallpox vaccines simultaneously administered.""",
Prospective monitoring of adverse events following vaccination with Modified vaccinia Ankara - Bavarian Nordic (MVA-BN) administered to a Canadian population at risk of Mpox: A Canadian Immunization Research Network study.,"MVA-BN is an orthopoxvirus vaccine that provides protection against both smallpox and mpox. In June 2022, Canada launched a publicly-funded vaccination campaign to offer MVA-BN to at-risk populations including men who have sex with men (MSM) and sex workers. The safety of MVA-BN has not been assessed in this context. To address this, the Canadian National Vaccine Safety Network (CANVAS) conducted prospective safety surveillance during public health vaccination campaigns in Toronto, Ontario and in Vancouver, British Columbia. Vaccinated participants received a survey 7 and 30 days after each MVA-BN dose to elicit adverse health events. Unvaccinated individuals from a concurrent vaccine safety project evaluating COVID-19 vaccine safety were used as controls. Vaccinated and unvaccinated participants that reported a medically attended visit on their 7-day survey were interviewed. Vaccinated participants and unvaccinated controls were matched 1:1 based on age group, gender, sex and provincial study site. Overall, 1,173 vaccinated participants completed a 7-day survey, of whom 75 % (n = 878) also completed a 30-day survey. Mild to moderate injection site pain was reported by 60 % of vaccinated participants. Among vaccinated participants 8.4 % were HIV positive and when compared to HIV negative vaccinated individuals, local injection sites were less frequent in those with HIV (48 % vs 61 %, p = 0.021), but health events preventing work/school or requiring medical assessment were more frequent (7.1 % vs 3.1 %, p = 0.040). Health events interfering with work/school, or requiring medical assessment were less common in the vaccinated group than controls (3.3 % vs. 7.1 %, p < 0.010). No participants were hospitalized within 7 or 30 days of vaccination. No cases of severe neurological disease, skin disease, or myocarditis were identified. Our results demonstrate that the MVA-BN vaccine appears safe when used for mpox prevention, with a low frequency of severe adverse events and no hospitalizations observed.","M.P. Muller, C. Navarro, S. Wilson, H. Shulha, M. Naus, G. Lim, S. Padhi, A. McGeer, M. Finkelstein, A. Liddy, J. Bettinger",10.1016/j.vaccine.2023.12.068,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2023.12.068,Vaccine,8,2024,maybe,"The study focus is on high-risk populations like MSM and sex workers, and includes HIV-positive individuals. However, there is no specific mention that the study excludes healthy adults or focuses on the target groups of interest (pediatrics/pregnant).",yes,"The study reports on adverse events, health events interfering with work, and hospitalizations.",yes,This is a prospective safety surveillance study (cohort study).,yes,The study involves human participants receiving vaccinations.,no,"The study does not explicitly mention inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents. It describes matching for 'age group' and targeting MSM/sex workers, which usually implies an adult population.",no,"There is no evidence that vaccination occurred during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,"The study evaluates MVA-BN, which is a vaccinia-based vaccine.",Exclude,"The study evaluates the safety of the MVA-BN vaccine in a Canadian population at risk for Mpox, specifically targeting MSM and sex workers. However, it does not include the target populations of interest (pregnant women, children, etc.), and thus fails the inclusion criteria.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""at-risk populations including men who have sex with men (MSM) and sex workers.""",,"  - ""No participants were hospitalized within 7 or 30 days of vaccination. No cases of severe neurological disease, skin disease, or myocarditis were identified.""",,"  - ""prospective safety surveillance during public health vaccination campaigns in Toronto, Ontario and in Vancouver, British Columbia.""",,"  - ""prospective safety surveillance during public health vaccination campaigns in Toronto, Ontario and in Vancouver, British Columbia.""",,"  - ""at-risk populations including men who have sex with men (MSM) and sex workers.""",,"  - ""at-risk populations including men who have sex with men (MSM) and sex workers.""",,"  - ""MVA-BN is an orthopoxvirus vaccine that provides protection against both smallpox and mpox.""",
Comprehensive literature review of monkeypox,"ABSTRACT The current outbreak of monkeypox (MPX) infection has emerged as a global matter of concern in the last few months. MPX is a zoonosis caused by the MPX virus (MPXV), which is one of the Orthopoxvirus species. Thus, it is similar to smallpox caused by the variola virus, and smallpox vaccines and drugs have been shown to be protective against MPX. Although MPX is not a new disease and is rarely fatal, the current multi-country MPX outbreak is unusual because it is occurring in countries that are not endemic for MPXV. In this work, we reviewed the extensive literature available on MPXV to summarize the available data on the major biological, clinical and epidemiological aspects of the virus and the important scientific findings. This review may be helpful in raising awareness of MPXV transmission, symptoms and signs, prevention and protective measures. It may also be of interest as a basis for performance of studies to further understand MPXV, with the goal of combating the current outbreak and boosting healthcare services and hygiene practices. Trial registration: ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT02977715.. Trial registration: ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT03745131.. Trial registration: ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT00728689.. Trial registration: ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT02080767..","Ma’mon M. Hatmal, Mohammad A. I. Al-Hatamleh, Amin N. Olaimat, Suhana Ahmad, Hanan Hasan, Nurfatihah Azlyna Ahmad Suhaimi, Khaled A. Albakri, Anas Abedalbaset Alzyoud, Ramlah Kadir, Rohimah Mohamud",10.1080/22221751.2022.2132882,https://doi.org/10.1080/22221751.2022.2132882,Emerging Microbes and Infections,84,2022,maybe,"This is a general literature review of MPXV biology and epidemiology, likely covering various populations, but doesn't specify exclusions.",maybe,"The review covers prevention and protective measures, which may include vaccine effectiveness.",maybe,The authors state they 'reviewed the extensive literature available' but do not explicitly describe it as a systematic review or meta-analysis.,maybe,"The review summarizes biological, clinical, and epidemiological aspects of the virus, which usually involves human data.",maybe,"The abstract does not specify whether the literature reviewed includes pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",maybe,The abstract does not provide details on the timing of vaccination for specific sub-populations.,yes,The paper mentions 'smallpox vaccines and drugs have been shown to be protective against MPX'.,Include,"This is a comprehensive literature review summarizing biological and clinical aspects of MPXV. While it discusses prevention and smallpox vaccines, it is unclear if it follows a systematic methodology or specifically focuses on the required pediatric or maternal populations.",,,3.4,"  - ""In this work, we reviewed the extensive literature available on MPXV to summarize the available data on the major biological, clinical and epidemiological aspects of the virus and the important scientific findings.""",,"  - ""This review may be helpful in raising awareness of MPXV transmission, symptoms and signs, prevention and protective measures.""",,"  - ""In this work, we reviewed the extensive literature available on MPXV to summarize the available data on the major biological, clinical and epidemiological aspects of the virus and the important scientific findings.""",,"  - ""In this work, we reviewed the extensive literature available on MPXV to summarize the available data on the major biological, clinical and epidemiological aspects of the virus and the important scientific findings.""",,"  - ""In this work, we reviewed the extensive literature available on MPXV to summarize the available data on the major biological, clinical and epidemiological aspects of the virus and the important scientific findings.""",,"  - ""In this work, we reviewed the extensive literature available on MPXV to summarize the available data on the major biological, clinical and epidemiological aspects of the virus and the important scientific findings.""",,"  - ""In this work, we reviewed the extensive literature available on MPXV to summarize the available data on the major biological, clinical and epidemiological aspects of the virus and the important scientific findings.""",
Mpox: Clinical Outcomes and Impact of Vaccination in People with and without HIV: A Population-Wide Study,"We investigated differences in mpox clinical outcomes in people with HIV (PWH) and without HIV (PWoH) and the impact of vaccination in Catalonia, Spain. We used surveillance data and the PISCIS HIV cohort. We included all confirmed mpox cases (May–December 2022). Of 2122 mpox cases, the majority had mild disease, 56% were Spanish, and 24% were from Latin America. A total of 40% were PWH, with a median CD4+T-cell of 715 cells/μL; 83% had HIV-RNA < 50 copies/mL; and 1.8% CD4+T-cell < 200 cells/μL. PWH had no increased risk for complications, except those with CD4+T-cell < 200 cells/μL. PWH with CD4+T-cell < 200 cells/μL were more likely to be from Latin America, had more generalized exanthema, and required hospitalization more frequently (p = 0.001). Diagnosis of other sexually transmitted infections (STIs) was common, both at mpox diagnosis (17%) and two years before (43%). Dose-sparing smallpox intradermal vaccination was accompanied by a sharp decrease in mpox incidence in both populations (p < 0.0001). In conclusion, unless immunosuppressed, PWH were not at increased risk of severe disease or hospitalization. Mpox is a marker of high-risk sexual behavior and was associated with high HIV and STI rates, supporting the need for screening in all mpox cases. Ethnicity disparities demonstrate the need for interventions to ensure equitable healthcare access. Dose-sparing smallpox vaccination retained effectiveness.","R. Martín-Iguacel, C. Pericas, A. Bruguera, Gemma Rosell, Erica Martínez, Yesika Díaz, Lucía Alonso, D. Nomah, J. L. Blanco, P. Domingo, Patricia Álvarez-López, M. S. Linares, Consuelo Vilades Laborda, Arantxa Mera, Sonia Calzado Isbert, I. Johansen, J. M. Miró, J. Casabona, J. Llibre",10.3390/microorganisms11112701,https://doi.org/10.3390/microorganisms11112701,Microorganisms,6,2023,yes,"The study includes people with HIV (PWH), so it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on mpox incidence (prevention), complications, and hospitalization.",yes,This is a primary population-wide research study using surveillance and cohort data.,yes,The study is a human study involving over 2000 mpox cases.,no,"The abstract does not mention the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; the context of high-risk sexual behavior and STIs suggests an adult population.",no,"There is no indication that participants were vaccinated during childhood, pregnancy, or adolescence; the vaccination described occurs during the 2022 outbreak.",yes,The study evaluates a smallpox vaccine.,Exclude,"This population-wide study evaluates mpox outcomes and vaccination impact in adults, specifically comparing people with and without HIV. However, it does not include the target populations of pregnant women, children, or adolescents, nor does it address vaccination during those life stages.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""40% were PWH, with a median CD4+T-cell of 715 cells/μL;""
  - ""Mpox is a marker of high-risk sexual behavior and was associated with high HIV and STI rates,""",,"  - ""required hospitalization more frequently""
  - ""Dose-sparing smallpox intradermal vaccination was accompanied by a sharp decrease in mpox incidence""",,"  - ""all confirmed mpox cases (May–December 2022).""",,"  - ""Of 2122 mpox cases, the majority had mild disease,""",,"  - ""Mpox is a marker of high-risk sexual behavior and was associated with high HIV and STI rates,""",,"  - ""all confirmed mpox cases (May–December 2022).""
