The impact of HPV vaccination beyond cancer prevention: effect on pregnancy outcomes,"ABSTRACT While the benefits of human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccination relating to cervical cancer prevention have been widely documented, recent published evidence is suggestive of an impact on adverse pregnancy outcomes (APOs) in vaccinated mothers and their infants, including a reduction in rates of preterm births and small for gestational age infants. In this review, we examine this evidence and the possible mechanisms by which HPV vaccination may prevent these APOs. Large-scale studies linking HPV vaccination status with birth registries are needed to confirm these results. Potential confounding factors to consider in future analyses include other risk factors for APOs, and historical changes in both the management of cervical precancerous lesions and prevention of APOs. If confirmed, these additional benefits of HPV vaccination in reducing APO rates will be of global significance, due to the substantial health, social and economic costs associated with APOs, strengthening the case for worldwide HPV immunization.","Susan Yuill, L. Velentzis, Megan A. Smith, S. Egger, C. D. Wrede, D. Bateson, M. Arbyn, K. Canfell",10.1080/21645515.2021.1936860,https://doi.org/10.1080/21645515.2021.1936860,Human Vaccines & Immunotherapeutics,16,2021,yes,The study focus includes pregnant mothers and their infants.,no,"The study reports on adverse pregnancy outcomes related to HPV vaccination, rather than mpox infection, hospitalization, or death.",yes,The paper is a review of existing evidence.,yes,The study examines effects in mothers and infants.,yes,The participants mentioned are pregnant women (mothers) and infants.,yes,The study examines outcomes in vaccinated mothers.,no,"The study is explicitly about human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccination, not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,This paper evaluates the impact of HPV vaccination on pregnancy outcomes. It is excluded because it does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccines and does not report on mpox-related outcomes.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""impact on adverse pregnancy outcomes (APOs) in vaccinated mothers and their infants,""",,"  - ""impact on adverse pregnancy outcomes (APOs) in vaccinated mothers and their infants,""",,"  - ""In this review, we examine this evidence""",,"  - ""impact on adverse pregnancy outcomes (APOs) in vaccinated mothers and their infants,""",,"  - ""impact on adverse pregnancy outcomes (APOs) in vaccinated mothers and their infants,""",,"  - ""impact on adverse pregnancy outcomes (APOs) in vaccinated mothers and their infants,""",,"  - ""human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccination""",
Mpox emergence and new vaccines: a story of susceptibility.,"Mpox is a smallpox-like disease caused by the monkeypox virus. In 2022, a global outbreak of mpox resulted in more than 113 000 cases. Just a year later, a different strain of mpox caused the largest ever outbreak in Africa, with over 46 000 cases. Previous mpox epidemics were small, primarily occurred in Africa, had a case-fatality rate of 1-10%, and spread readily through contact with lesions and fomites. The new outbreaks had features never seen before with mpox - milder presentations and sexual transmission - that, together, fueled much wider dissemination of the disease. In this review, we discuss the potential drivers of increased mpox outbreaks, the efficacy of currently licensed vaccines, and the development of new vaccines. With a more susceptible human population than ever before, we are preparing for future mpox outbreaks with a better understanding of its transmission and more preventive tools.","Samuel D. Stampfer, A. Dretler, R. Amara",10.1016/j.coi.2026.102755,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.coi.2026.102755,Current Opinion in Immunology,0,2026,maybe,"The study discusses the 'human population' and global outbreaks, which usually includes non-healthy or diverse adult groups, but does not explicitly specify a focus outside healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The paper discusses the 'efficacy of currently licensed vaccines'.,no,"The authors state 'In this review, we discuss...'. Narrative reviews are generally not considered 'systematic reviews' or 'meta-analyses' as required by the inclusion criteria for evidence synthesis studies.",yes,The study focuses on the 'human population' and epidemiological data from outbreaks.,no,"There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents in the provided text.",no,"The study does not describe vaccination specifically during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,"The study evaluates 'currently licensed vaccines' for mpox, which are 