Prenatal Infection with Vaccinia Virus: Report of a Case,"been vaccinated before, and had a mild but typical primary reaction. She was ill for two days one week after the vaccination, but made an eventful recovery with no further illness. She was admitted to the City of London Maternity Hospital as an emergency, threatening to abort at what was, according to her dates, approximately 22 weeks. Examination showed the uterus to be the size of a 34 weeks' pregnancy; vaginally the cervix was soft and three fingers dilated with a bulging bag of membranes. A provisional diagnosis of twins with inevitable abortion was made. Two hours after admission she delivered the first twin followed by two placentae. and then the second twin. The twins, both males, showed evidence of generalized vaccinia, as illustrated on the Special Plate. The larger twin weighed 1 lb. 2oz. (530 g.) and was the more severely affected. There were discrete vaccinial lesions on the neck, and most of the body was covered with confluent lesions. At birth the purple-brown colour suggested maceration (but movements of the limbs were observed, though no respiratory movements occurred). The smaller twin weighed only 11 oz. (312 g.) and appeared to have fewer though larger lesions, as shown by the one round the eye (see Special Plate). There was another large lesion on the top of the head. This twin was of normal colour and had a definite but weak heart-beat, though again no respiratory movements occurred. The infants did not survive. No post-mortem examination was performed. Scrapings were taken from the lesions of both twins, and were reported on as follows from the Virus Reference Laboratory at Colindale: "" Gel-diffusion test positive for variola-vaccinia group; egg culture positive for vaccinia."" Blood taken from the mother showed antivaccinial haemagglutination inhibition positive to a titre of 1: 64, consistent with recent successful vaccination.","D. Entwistle, P. Bray, K. Laurence",10.1136/bmj.2.5299.238,https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.2.5299.238,British medical journal,24,1962,yes,The study involves a pregnant woman and twins.,yes,The study reports on fetal generalized vaccinia and neonatal death as outcomes.,yes,The paper is a case report of a prenatal infection following vaccination.,yes,The study is a report on human subjects (a mother and her twin infants).,yes,The study participants include a pregnant woman and her twin neonates.,yes,"The mother was vaccinated during her pregnancy, as evidenced by the infants born with vaccinia at 22 weeks gestation shortly after maternal vaccination.",yes,The study reports on reactions to vaccinia vaccination and laboratory confirmation of the vaccinia virus.,Include,"This case report describes a mother vaccinated during pregnancy who subsequently delivered twins with generalized vaccinia. Both infants died, and the diagnosis was confirmed through viral culture. The paper perfectly aligns with all screening criteria focusing on vaccinia vaccine safety in pregnant populations.",,,5,"  - ""approximately 22 weeks.""
