Effect of COVID-19 Vaccination on Pregnancy Outcomes - A Cross-Sectional Comparative Study,"Abstract
Background: Pregnant women are at increased risk of complications from COVID-19. Vaccination during pregnancy offers protection for mothers and newborns. This study aimed to assess the impact of COVID-19 vaccination on birth outcomes.
Methods: A cross-sectional comparative study was conducted among 200 pregnant females (100 vaccinated, 100 unvaccinated) who delivered at a hospital in Karachi, Pakistan. Vaccination status, trimester of vaccination, and birth outcomes (preterm delivery, stillbirth) were compared.
Results: Most vaccinated mothers received Sinopharm (47%) or Sinovac (39%). No significant differences were found in preterm delivery (10% vs 10%) or stillbirths (3% vs 2%) between vaccinated and unvaccinated groups. Additionally, no significant associations were observed between vaccination status and other maternal or fetal outcomes, including mode of delivery and pregnancy complications.
Conclusions: COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy appears safe and does not adversely affect birth outcomes. Routine vaccination should be recommended for pregnant women to prevent COVID-19 complications and protect both mothers and newborns.
Keywords: COVID-19, vaccination, pregnancy, birth outcomes, Pakistan","Rubia Sami, M. Yasir",10.62807/jowach.v1i1.2023.31-35,https://doi.org/10.62807/jowach.v1i1.2023.31-35,Journal of Women and Child Health,0,2024,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women.,yes,The study reports on birth outcomes including preterm delivery and stillbirth.,yes,The study is a cross-sectional comparative study.,yes,The study involves human participants (200 pregnant females).,yes,The study specifically includes pregnant women.,yes,Participants received the vaccination during pregnancy.,no,The study investigates COVID-19 vaccines (Sinopharm and Sinovac) rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,This study evaluates the impact of COVID-19 vaccination (Sinopharm/Sinovac) on pregnancy outcomes. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.,Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""cross-sectional comparative study was conducted among 200 pregnant females""",,"  - ""birth outcomes (preterm delivery, stillbirth) were compared.""",,"  - ""cross-sectional comparative study was conducted among 200 pregnant females""",,"  - ""cross-sectional comparative study was conducted among 200 pregnant females""",,"  - ""cross-sectional comparative study was conducted among 200 pregnant females""",,"  - ""cross-sectional comparative study was conducted among 200 pregnant females""
