Development of a Broad-Spectrum Pan-Mpox Vaccine via Immunoinformatic Approaches,"Monkeypox virus (MPXV) has caused 148,892 confirmed cases and 341 deaths from 137 countries worldwide, as reported by the World Health Organization (WHO), highlighting the urgent need for effective vaccines to prevent the spread of MPXV. Traditional vaccine development is low-throughput, expensive, time consuming, and susceptible to reversion to virulence. Alternatively, a reverse vaccinology approach offers a rapid, efficient, and safer alternative for MPXV vaccine design. Here, MPXV proteins associated with viral infection were analyzed for immunogenic epitopes to design multi-epitope vaccines based on B-cell, CD4+, and CD8+ epitopes. Epitopes were selected based on allergenicity, antigenicity, and toxicity parameters. The prioritized epitopes were then combined via peptide linkers and N-terminally fused to various protein adjuvants, including PADRE, beta-defensin 3, 50S ribosomal protein L7/12, RS-09, and the cholera toxin B subunit (CTB). All vaccine constructs were computationally validated for physicochemical properties, antigenicity, allergenicity, safety, solubility, and structural stability. The three-dimensional structure of the selected construct was also predicted. Moreover, molecular docking and molecular dynamics (MD) simulations between the vaccine and the TLR-4 immune receptor demonstrated a strong and stable interaction. The vaccine construct was codon-optimized for high expression in the E. coli and was finally cloned in silico into the pET21a (+) vector. Collectively, these results could represent innovative tools for vaccine formulation against MPXV and be transformative for other infectious diseases.","Japigorn Puagsopa, Panuwid Jumpalee, Sittichoke Dechanun, Sukanya Choengchalad, Pana Lohasupthawee, T. Sutjaritvorakul, Bunyarit Meksiriporn",10.3390/ijms26157210,https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms26157210,International Journal of Molecular Sciences,3,2025,no,"The study does not involve a human population of any kind, as it is a computational vaccine design study.",no,"The study reports computational predictions of safety and stability, not clinical safety or effectiveness outcomes in humans.",no,"The study is primary laboratory research (in silico), but not a clinical trial, cohort study, or systematic review of clinical data.",no,"The study is a laboratory-only/computational study, not a human study.",no,"The study does not include human participants, and therefore does not include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,No vaccinations were administered to human subjects in this study.,no,"The study involves the in silico design of a novel multi-epitope vaccine, not the evaluation of existing vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This is a computational (in silico) study focusing on the design of a novel multi-epitope vaccine using reverse vaccinology. It does not involve any human participants, clinical outcomes, or evaluation of established vaccinia-based vaccines, failing all primary inclusion criteria.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""All vaccine constructs were computationally validated for physicochemical properties, antigenicity, allergenicity, safety, solubility, and structural stability.""
