A Multicenter Cohort Study on the Adverse Effects Evaluation After Messenger RNA COVID-19 Vaccination Among Pregnant Healthcare Employees in Penang General Hospitals,"Introduction The year 2020 saw the emergence of novel severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2), which became a great threat to public health worldwide. The exponential spread of the disease with millions of lives lost worldwide saw the emergence of an accelerated vaccine development with emergency approval from well-known regulatory bodies such as the US Food and Drug Administration, followed by widespread vaccine deployment despite a paucity in safety profile data. This issue becomes even more pronounced when it involves expectant mothers considering the possible undesirable effect toward the unborn child. Method This was a retrospective cohort study which was conducted at six general hospitals in the state of Penang, Malaysia. All the pregnant employees who have consented to take the mRNA COVID-19 vaccine and participate in this study were monitored from the time of their first vaccination and up to 28 days after they delivered their babies. Results All the participants had adequate maximum vertical pocket (MVP) and no obvious anomalies or detection of intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR) were detected during the second trimester. However, one subject was reported to have miscarried during the second trimester. The reported mean neonate birth weight was 3.0 kg with the mean Apgar score of 8.8 and 9.8 at 1 and 5 min, respectively. Approximately seven (5.8%) neonates were reported to be small for their gestational age. Another three (2.5%) neonates were reported to have anomalies. Conclusion As a whole, the inference that can be made from this study is that mRNA COVID-19 vaccine appears to be safe in pregnant women regardless of the trimester as the findings did not show obvious safety warning signs.","Ann L Arulappen, M. Danial, G. Shanmugam, Joo Thye Cheng, Mairin Dulasi, T. S. Chow",10.3389/fpubh.2022.876966,https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2022.876966,Frontiers in Public Health,8,2022,yes,The study focuses on pregnant healthcare employees.,yes,The study evaluates adverse effects.,yes,The study is a retrospective cohort study.,yes,The study involved human participants (healthcare employees).,yes,The participants were pregnant women.,yes,Participants received the vaccine during pregnancy and were monitored until after delivery.,no,"The study evaluates the mRNA COVID-19 vaccine, not a vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the safety of mRNA COVID-19 vaccines in pregnant women. However, it does not involve vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines, thus failing a mandatory inclusion criterion.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,,,,,"  - ""This was a retrospective cohort study""",,,,,,"  - ""monitored from the time of their first vaccination and up to 28 days after they delivered their babies.""",,"  - ""mRNA COVID-19 vaccine""",
"Safety of Tetanus, Diphtheria, and Pertussis Vaccination During Pregnancy: A Systematic Review.","OBJECTIVE
To assess antenatal, birth, and infant outcomes for pregnant women, fetuses, and infants after antenatal vaccination with any antigen present in combination pertussis vaccines.
DATA SOURCES
PubMed, EMBASE, Literature in the Health Sciences in Latin America and the Caribbean, ClinicalTrials.gov, Cochrane Library, and World Health Organization (inception to May 5, 2016).
METHODS OF STUDY SELECTION
Studies reporting outcomes for pregnant women, their fetus, or infant after antenatal exposure to either monovalent or combined tetanus toxoid, reduced diphtheria toxoid, and acellular pertussis (Tdap) or inactivated polio vaccines were considered for inclusion.
RESULTS
A total of 21 studies were included in this review. Point estimates ranged from 0.47 to 1.50 for preterm birth (less than 37 weeks of gestation), 0.65-1.00 for small for gestational age (birth weight less than the 10th percentile), 0.36-0.85 for stillbirth, 0.16-1.00 for neonatal death, 0.76-1.20 for low birth weight (less than 2,500 g), and 0.20-0.91 for congenital anomalies. All lower 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were less than 1.0. Of three retrospective studies assessing chorioamnionitis after vaccination, one showed a small but statistically significant increase. Point estimates for all anomalies after antenatal tetanus toxoid vaccination ranged from 1.20 to 1.60 and had 95% CIs that crossed 1.0. There was substantial clinical and methodologic heterogeneity from mainly retrospective observational studies with an overall high risk of bias. Objective rates of fever were low, 3% or below, and more common systemic events observed included headache, malaise, and myalgia.
CONCLUSION
Evidence suggests that antenatal combined Tdap administered during the second or third trimester of pregnancy is not associated with clinically significant harms for the fetus or neonate. Medically attended events in pregnant women are similar between vaccinated and unvaccinated groups.","M. McMillan, M. Clarke, A. Parrella, D. Fell, G. Amirthalingam, H. Marshall",10.1097/AOG.0000000000001888,https://doi.org/10.1097/AOG.0000000000001888,Obstetrics and Gynecology,82,2017,yes,The study focuses specifically on pregnant women and their infants.,no,"The study investigates safety outcomes for Tdap and polio vaccines, but does not report outcomes related to mpox infection, prevention, or complications.",yes,The study is a systematic review.,yes,The study involves human participants (pregnant women and neonates).,yes,"The study population includes pregnant women, fetuses, and infants.",yes,The participants received vaccination during the antenatal (pregnancy) period.,no,"The study investigates Tdap (tetanus, diphtheria, acellular pertussis) and inactivated polio vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,This systematic review focuses on the safety of Tdap and polio vaccines during pregnancy. It is excluded because it does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccines and does not address outcomes related to mpox infection.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""assess antenatal, birth, and infant outcomes for pregnant women, fetuses, and infants after antenatal vaccination with any antigen present in combination pertussis vaccines.""",,"  - ""assess antenatal, birth, and infant outcomes for pregnant women, fetuses, and infants after antenatal vaccination with any antigen present in combination pertussis vaccines.""
