"A cross sectional study on COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy and its safety, perinatal and neonatal outcomes in a tertiary care hospital","Background: Pregnant women are more likely to suffer severe COVID-19 illness, during the second wave of the COVID-19 pandemic in India, there were increased pregnant cases of severe COVID-19. Following first wave due to the wild alpha variant, the vaccine efficacy was questioned during second wave caused by beta and omicron. When compared to receiving only two doses, a third dose (booster dose) that was given at least five months following the second dose significantly decreased the rate of COVID-19 symptoms and severe COVID-19 caused by all variants.Methods: A prospective hospital based cross-sectional study was undertaken at SSIMS AND RC, Davangere. This study was conducted on pregnant women visiting antenatal clinics and delivering at the department of OBG at S. S. hospital, Davangere.Results: The data was collected from Feb 2022 to April 2022 at our institute in women undergoing delivery with history of COVID 19 vaccination during pregnancy. A total of 188 cases were studied in the following duration, majority of the women belonged to the age group of 26 to 35 years. 54.8% of the cases were multiparous followed by 45.2% of the cases who were primigravida’s. The mean gestational age during admission and delivery of the patients involved in the study was 37.33 weeks, ranging from as early as 27 weeks of gestation to 40 weeks of gestation. There was no significant correlation between the neonatal and maternal complications seen in the study to the number of doses, gestational age and type of vaccine.Conclusions: Vaccinating everyone is the most effective method of battling the current COVID-19 outbreak. Despite the lack of sufficient clinical data on long-term side effects, the decline in the number of new cases, critically sick patients, and mortality in many nations suggests that vaccines will put an end to the pandemic.","Anita Sitimani, Achala K. P., B. S.",10.18203/2320-1770.ijrcog20223489,https://doi.org/10.18203/2320-1770.ijrcog20223489,International Journal of Reproduction Contraception Obstetrics and Gynecology,0,2022,yes,The study explicitly focuses on pregnant women.,yes,The study reports on safety and perinatal/neonatal outcomes (complications).,yes,This is a prospective hospital-based cross-sectional study.,yes,The study involved 188 human cases (women undergoing delivery).,yes,The participants were pregnant women.,yes,The vaccine was administered during pregnancy.,no,"The study focuses exclusively on COVID-19 vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This cross-sectional study focuses on the safety and outcomes of COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant women. While it meets the population and timing criteria, it does not investigate mpox or smallpox vaccines, which is a mandatory requirement for inclusion.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""The data was collected from Feb 2022 to April 2022 at our institute in women undergoing delivery with history of COVID 19 vaccination during pregnancy.""",,"  - ""There was no significant correlation between the neonatal and maternal complications seen in the study""",,"  - ""A prospective hospital based cross-sectional study""",,"  - ""A total of 188 cases were studied""",,"  - ""The data was collected from Feb 2022 to April 2022 at our institute in women undergoing delivery with history of COVID 19 vaccination during pregnancy.""",,"  - ""The data was collected from Feb 2022 to April 2022 at our institute in women undergoing delivery with history of COVID 19 vaccination during pregnancy.""",,"  - ""The data was collected from Feb 2022 to April 2022 at our institute in women undergoing delivery with history of COVID 19 vaccination during pregnancy.""",
"Human mpox (monkeypox): Epidemiologic, pathogenetic and clinical characteristics, and prevention","Mpox (monkeypox) is a zoonotic disease caused by monkeypox virus, which belongs to the orthopoxvirus genus. Concern has recently been expressed over the appearance of the human monkeypox virus and its severe clinical presentation that resembles smallpox. Currently, due to the decrescence of immunity among smallpox-vaccinated residents, and the accumulation of unvaccinated cohorts, people are generally susceptible to mpox. A cumulative number of 79411 laboratory-confirmed cases of mpox and 50 fatalities have been reported to the World Health Organization from 110 countries between 1 January and 13 November 2022. This paper provides an overview of the epidemiology and clinical features of mpox, and control and prevention approaches for mpox to fully understand and prevent human infections.","Husheng Xiong, Jinlin Tan, Dingmei Zhang",10.4103/2773-0344.369071,https://doi.org/10.4103/2773-0344.369071,One Health Bulletin,2,2023,yes,"The paper discusses a wide susceptibility of people generally and mentions global WHO cases, which would include populations beyond healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The paper discusses prevention approaches and mentions fatalities, but it does not report primary effectiveness or safety data outcomes from a specific study or trial.",no,"The authors explicitly state the paper is an 'overview' of characteristics and prevention approaches, which identifies it as a narrative review rather than primary research or a systematic review.",yes,The paper focuses on 'human mpox' and 'human infections'.,no,"The paper does not explicitly mention or analyze data specifically for pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as participants.",no,"There is no description of participants receiving vaccines during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence in the context of a primary research study.",yes,"The paper mentions immunity in smallpox-vaccinated residents and prevention approaches, which encompasses historical smallpox and vaccinia-based vaccines.",Exclude,"This paper is a narrative overview of mpox epidemiology and prevention rather than a primary research study or systematic review, thus failing the required study design criteria. It also does not explicitly include or analyze the target populations of pregnant women, children, or neonates.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""people are generally susceptible to mpox.""
