Smallpox Vaccine and Adverse Reproductive Health Outcomes in Military Service Members,"Abstract : In response to threats of bioterrorism, US military personnel began receiving smallpox vaccinations in 2003. Although some adverse health effects associated with smallpox vaccination are well described, much less has been documented about reproductive health outcomes. Smallpox vaccine, as a live-virus product, has caused fetal vaccinia in rare cases when given in pregnancy. The potential for the product to cause pregnancy loss, birth defects, or other birth problems has not been as well documented, especially in the recent era. The objective of this study is to describe the incidence and prevalence of adverse reproductive health outcomes among military members who received smallpox vaccine, Methods are based on evaluating electronic birth records accessed directly from the Standard Inpatient Data Record (SIDR), Standard Ambulatory Data Record (SADR), Health Care Service Record (HCSR); and immunization data obtained from the Defense Eligibility and Enrollment System (DEERS). All data are accessed through formal agreements established by the DoD Birth and Infant Health Registry. Using these data, adverse reproductive health outcomes are assessed, including birth defects, pregnancy loss, and infertility among military families exposed to smallpox vaccine. Analyses are ongoing.",M. Ryan,10.21236/ada442817,https://doi.org/10.21236/ada442817,,0,2005,yes,"The study explicitly focuses on reproductive health outcomes, including birth defects and pregnancy loss, and refers to effects when the vaccine is given in pregnancy, meaning it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",maybe,"The study assesses safety outcomes including fetal vaccinia, birth defects, and pregnancy loss, although it notes that specific incidence analyses were ongoing at the time of the abstract.",yes,The study is a primary research effort using electronic birth and immunization records to evaluate outcomes in a specific cohort.,yes,"The study uses data from military personnel and families, confirming it is a human study.",yes,"The population includes pregnant women and their infants, evidenced by the assessment of birth defects, fetal vaccinia, and the use of the DoD Birth and Infant Health Registry.",yes,The study specifically addresses the consequences when the vaccine is given during pregnancy and assesses subsequent birth defects and pregnancy loss among those exposed.,yes,The study focuses on the smallpox vaccine administered to military personnel.,Include,"The study investigates the safety of the smallpox vaccine in pregnant women and infants by assessing reproductive outcomes such as birth defects, pregnancy loss, and fetal vaccinia. It utilizes comprehensive military birth and immunization records for its analysis. While the abstract indicates that analyses were ongoing at the time of publication, the study's scope fully aligns with the screening criteria for population, intervention, and relevant outcomes.",,,4.6,"  - ""adverse reproductive health outcomes are assessed, including birth defects, pregnancy loss, and infertility among military families exposed to smallpox vaccine.""
