"Safety and Immunogenicity of Modi fi ed Vaccinia Ankara in Hematopoietic Stem Cell Transplant Recipients : A Randomized , Controlled Trial","Stephen R. Walsh, Marissa B. Wilck, David J. Dominguez, Elise Zablowsky, Shringkhala Bajimaya, Lisa S. Gagne, Kelly A. Verrill, Jane A. Kleinjan, Alka Patel, Ying Zhang, Heather Hill, Aruna Acharyya, David C. Fisher, Joseph H. Antin, Michael S. Seaman, Raphael Dolin, and Lindsey R. Baden Division of Infectious Diseases, Brigham and Women’s Hospital, Center for Virology and Vaccine Research, Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center, Harvard Medical School, and Division of Hematologic Oncology, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, Massachusetts; and EMMES Corporation, Rockville, Maryland","S. Walsh, M. Wilck, David Dominguez, Elise Zablowsky, Shringkhala Bajimaya, Lisa S. Gagne, K. Verrill, J. Kleinjan, Alka Patel, Ying Zhang, H. Hill, A. Acharyya, D. Fisher, J. Antin, M. Seaman, R. Dolin, L. Baden",,,,1,2013,yes,"The study focuses on hematopoietic stem cell transplant recipients, who are a clinical population rather than healthy adults.",yes,The study title explicitly mentions safety and immunogenicity.,yes,"The title identifies the study as a randomized, controlled trial.",yes,"The study is a clinical trial conducted in hospitals, indicating human subjects.",no,"The study participants are hematopoietic stem cell transplant recipients; there is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"There is no evidence that vaccinations were administered during pregnancy or childhood, as the study population is not defined by those life stages.",yes,"The study uses Modified Vaccinia Ankara, which is a vaccinia-based vaccine.",Exclude,"This study is a randomized, controlled trial assessing the safety of Modified Vaccinia Ankara in hematopoietic stem cell transplant recipients. While it meets the criteria for vaccine type and study design, it fails the population criteria because it does not study pregnant women or children.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,,,,,,,"  - ""Division of Infectious Diseases, Brigham and Women’s Hospital, Center for Virology and Vaccine Research, Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center, Harvard Medical School, and Division of Hematologic Oncology, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute,""",,,,,,,
Studies on Smallpox and Complications of Smallpox Vaccination. E. Mead Johnson Award Address.,"Introductory remarks the presentation of the E. Mead Johnson Award affords the recipient the honor of publicly acknowledging his debts to his teachers. I pay such grateful tribute to Dr. Grover Powers and Dr. Edward B. Shaw, my preceptors in academic pediatrics and to Dr. Joseph Smadel who introduced me to the pox viruses and urged me to take another look at the well-trodden field of smallpox. Helpful in his own way was the senior virologist who urged me instead to get involved in another, more fruitful field because ""smallpox leaves no room for further work, having been well studied in the 19209s"" and who, thereby, strengthened my resolve to re-examine current concepts in the field of smallpox and smallpox vaccination, a subject which has occupied us for the past 14 years. I also acknowledge my lasting debt to my associate, Dr. Henry Silver, who has been a critical colleague, supporter and friend over many years. The WELL KNOWN clinical observation that it is sometimes difficult to vaccinate the very young chi9d against smallpox led to our early assessment of passive immunity as a modifying factor1 and this, in turn, resulted in the development of hyper-immune vaccinal gamma-globulin and its use in the prevention of smallpox after known exposure.2 The principal subject of this report will be our therapeutic experience with hyperimmune vaccinal gamma-globulin in over 300 patients suffering from serious complications of smallpox vaccination. Finally, we hope to show how basic immunologic studies can be of great value in the management of some of the unfortunate patients whose experience with the vaccinia virus has been a tragic one.",C. Kempe,,,Pediatrics,194,1960,yes,"The study focuses on complications in children and diverse patients, not just healthy adults.",yes,The study reports on serious complications (safety outcomes) of smallpox vaccination.,yes,This is a report of therapeutic experience in a case series of over 300 patients.,yes,The study involves human patients suffering from vaccination complications.,yes,The text explicitly mentions 'very young' children.,yes,The intervention discussed is the vaccination of 'the very young' children.,yes,The study specifically addresses the smallpox vaccination and vaccinia virus.,Include,"This study reports on the safety outcomes and complications associated with smallpox (vaccinia) vaccination in a population that includes very young children. It provides data on over 300 patients, meeting all clinical and demographic requirements for this review.",,,5,"  - ""difficult to vaccinate the very young chi9d against smallpox""
