Clade I mpox vaccination: strategies for deployment and evaluation,"Clade I mpox continues to spread in Central Africa with no sign of abating,1 however very few doses of vaccine have been deployed. Mpox vaccination strategies using either of the licenced vaccines, MVA-BN and LC16m8, are being drawn up across 17 African countries and vaccination has begun in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC), Rwanda and Nigeria.2 Clinical studies during the rollout could deliver much-needed data about the effectiveness of the vaccine against clade I mpox. Choosing the right study design is fundamental.","Caitlin Pley, J. Panovska-Griffiths, R. Hinch, C. Wymant, L. Abeler-Dörner, D. Ssemwanga, R. Galiwango, M. K. Grabowski, F. Di Lauro, Luca Ferretti, James M. Hay, Marc Lipsitch, Jake Dunning, J. Kagaayi, P. Kaleebu, C. Fraser",10.1016/j.ebiom.2025.105890,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ebiom.2025.105890,EBioMedicine,0,2025,maybe,"The paper mentions general vaccine rollouts across several African countries but does not specify its own study population, making it unclear whether it meets the population focus requirements.",no,The text discusses that clinical studies during the rollout 'could deliver' data on effectiveness but it does not report any actual safety or effectiveness results.,no,The article is a perspective or strategy piece discussing 'Mpox vaccination strategies' and study design selection rather than a primary research study or a systematic review.,yes,The vaccination rollout and strategies described refer to human populations in Africa.,maybe,"The provided paper content does not specify if the rollouts or strategies include pregnant women, neonates, or children.",maybe,"Because the paper does not report results from specific participants, the timing of vaccination relative to pregnancy or childhood is not documented.",yes,"The paper focuses on MVA-BN and LC16m8 vaccines, which are vaccinia-based.",Exclude,"This article discusses 'Mpox vaccination strategies' and evaluation methods rather than reporting results from a primary research study or systematic review. It does not provide data on vaccine effectiveness or safety, noting only that future studies 'could deliver' such data.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design",Relevant Outcomes,0.1,"  - ""vaccination has begun in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC), Rwanda and Nigeria.2""",,"  - ""Clinical studies during the rollout could deliver much-needed data about the effectiveness""",,"  - ""Mpox vaccination strategies using either of the licenced vaccines, MVA-BN and LC16m8,""
  - ""Choosing the right study design is fundamental.""",,"  - ""vaccination has begun in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC), Rwanda and Nigeria.2""",,"  - ""vaccination has begun in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC), Rwanda and Nigeria.2""",,"  - ""Mpox vaccination strategies using either of the licenced vaccines, MVA-BN and LC16m8,""
  - ""Clinical studies during the rollout could deliver much-needed data about the effectiveness""",,"  - ""Mpox vaccination strategies using either of the licenced vaccines, MVA-BN and LC16m8,""",
Mpox in Neonates and Children: A Review Article,"Mpox, caused by the monkeypox virus, has expanded beyond its historical African boundaries, with significant global outbreaks, including a 2022 surge. While traditionally affecting adults, recent cases in neonates and children show more severe complications, including respiratory failure, encephalitis, and sepsis. Children are more vulnerable due to an immature immune system and at higher risk for multi-organ involvement. Prevention focuses on vaccination, personal hygiene, and safe sexual practices, with JYNNEOS® and ACAM2000® vaccines recommended for high-risk groups. Early diagnosis, supportive care, and antiviral treatment are crucial in managing severe cases. This review highlights differences in disease severity between adults and children and outlines key prevention and treatment strategies","Thiraporn Kanjanaphan, M. Gunst",10.62691/jmuh.2026.7425,https://doi.org/10.62691/jmuh.2026.7425,Journal of Medicine and Urban Health,0,2026,yes,The paper focuses on neonates and children rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.,maybe,"The paper discusses severe complications and prevention strategies, but does not explicitly report data on vaccine safety or effectiveness outcomes from a primary research context.",no,"The paper identifies itself as a review article, which does not meet the specified inclusion criteria for primary research or systematic reviews/meta-analyses.",yes,The study involves human clinical cases in neonates and children.,yes,The study specifically addresses neonates and children.,maybe,The paper mentions vaccination recommendations for high-risk groups but does not report on a cohort of participants who were vaccinated during childhood or the neonatal period.,yes,The study refers to the JYNNEOS and ACAM2000 vaccines.,Exclude,The paper is a narrative review focusing on mpox complications and management in neonates and children. It fails the inclusion criteria because it is not a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis.,Study Design,Study Design,0.1,"  - ""recent cases in neonates and children show more severe complications,""",,"  - ""recent cases in neonates and children show more severe complications,""
  - ""This review highlights differences in disease severity between adults and children and outlines key prevention and treatment strategies""",,"  - ""This review highlights differences in disease severity between adults and children and outlines key prevention and treatment strategies""",,"  - ""recent cases in neonates and children show more severe complications,""",,"  - ""recent cases in neonates and children show more severe complications,""",,"  - ""Prevention focuses on vaccination, personal hygiene, and safe sexual practices, with JYNNEOS® and ACAM2000® vaccines recommended for high-risk groups.""",,"  - ""Prevention focuses on vaccination, personal hygiene, and safe sexual practices, with JYNNEOS® and ACAM2000® vaccines recommended for high-risk groups.""",
"Vaccine Effectiveness Against Hospitalization Among Adolescent and Pediatric SARS-CoV-2 Cases in Ontario, Canada","Background: Vaccines to prevent SARS-CoV-2 infection and severe outcomes were approved in 2021 for adolescent and subsequently pediatric populations. With the emergence of variants of concern, it is important to continue to assess vaccine effectiveness against both infection and severe disease manifestations. Methods: Using an age and time-matched nested case-control design, we estimated the vaccine effectiveness of SARS-CoV-2 vaccination at preventing hospitalization among adolescent and pediatric patients infected with SARS-CoV-2. We linked SARS-CoV-2 cases ages 4 to 17 years in Ontarios reportable diseases database to SARS-CoV-2 vaccination records. We included 1,441 incident SARS-CoV-2 cases occurring between May 28, 2021 and January 10, 2022. We used multivariable logistic regression to estimate the effectiveness of one and two mRNA vaccine doses against hospitalization among adolescent and pediatric SARS-CoV-2 cases. We quantified and mitigated the impact of unmeasured confounding by calculating an E-value and by applying instrumental variable methods. Results: We included n=131 hospitalized SARS-CoV-2 cases and n=1,310 non-hospitalized SARS-CoV-2 cases. One vaccine dose was shown to be 37% effective against hospitalization among SARS-CoV-2 cases (adjusted odds ratio [aOR] = 0.63 [95% CI: 0.33, 1.13]). In contrast, two doses were 59% (aOR = 0.41 [95% CI: 0.21, 0.77]) effective at preventing hospitalization among SARS-CoV-2 cases. Conclusions: Even with immune evasion by SARS-CoV-2 variants, two vaccine doses continue to provide protection against hospitalization among adolescent and pediatric patients, even when the vaccines do not prevent infection. SARS-CoV-2 vaccines remain a safe and effective intervention to prevent severe outcomes.","A. E. Simmons, A. Amoako, A. Grima, K. Murison, A. Tuite, D. Fisman",10.1101/2022.03.24.22272919,https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.03.24.22272919,medRxiv,3,2022,yes,The study focuses on adolescent and pediatric populations aged 4 to 17 years.,no,"The study reports on hospitalization for SARS-CoV-2, not mpox infection, prevention, or related outcomes.",yes,The study is an age and time-matched nested case-control design.,yes,The study uses data from human patients in Ontario's reportable diseases database.,yes,The study population includes children and adolescents aged 4 to 17 years.,yes,The study population (ages 4-17) received the vaccines during childhood or adolescence.,no,"The study evaluates mRNA SARS-CoV-2 vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,This study evaluates the effectiveness of mRNA SARS-CoV-2 vaccines against hospitalization in pediatric and adolescent populations in Canada. It does not address mpox or smallpox vaccines nor does it report on mpox-related clinical outcomes.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""linked SARS-CoV-2 cases ages 4 to 17 years""",,"  - ""estimated the vaccine effectiveness of SARS-CoV-2 vaccination at preventing hospitalization among adolescent and pediatric patients""",,"  - ""estimated the vaccine effectiveness of SARS-CoV-2 vaccination at preventing hospitalization among adolescent and pediatric patients""",,"  - ""linked SARS-CoV-2 cases ages 4 to 17 years""",,"  - ""linked SARS-CoV-2 cases ages 4 to 17 years""",,"  - ""estimated the vaccine effectiveness of SARS-CoV-2 vaccination at preventing hospitalization among adolescent and pediatric patients""
