Waking Up to Monkeypox in the Midst of COVID-19,"The first incidence of the monkeypox virus (MPXV) was reported in a Danish research facility. Even though first discovered in monkeys, rodents account for the largest reservoir of the disease. It is an encapsulated, brick-shaped double-stranded DNA virus strongly related to the smallpox virus. The risk of acquiring MPXV has been found to be inversely related to smallpox vaccination. Although the cases were initially restricted to African countries, they were first reported outside Africa in the early 2000s. MPXV is transmitted through close personal contact, most commonly through direct skin-skin contact. The fatality rates associated with the MPXV tend to vary in different regions, with Congo clad basin having the highest mortality rate. The majority of the cases of MPXV have been reported in men who have sex with men. Although optimal infection control and treatment strategies are under investigation, the current management focus is on immunization and the isolation of patients. Effective control strategies are based on implementing a method of contact tracing, quarantining exposed and infected individuals, and using vaccines. There is no proven cure for MPXV, and most infected patients recover without medical intervention. Extensive studies are being conducted to determine the efficacy of antivirals in managing MPXV, with tecovirimat being the first antiviral medication approved by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) to manage MPXV. The smallpox vaccine has traditionally been thought of as the most effective method of controlling the infection, possibly due to the similarities between the two viruses. However, numerous obstacles prevent the effective control of MPXV, including social isolation and stigma, poor understanding of the disease dynamics, lack of adequate patient education, and public health strategies.","Maleesha Jayasinghe, Dilushini Caldera, Omesh Prathiraja, A. Kayani, O. Siddiqui, James Anwar Coffie-Pierre, Dilka Sewwandi Abeysundara, Avinash Sewsurn, Imesha Hewavitharana, Rahul Jena",10.7759/cureus.30920,https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.30920,Cureus,6,2022,no,"The paper discusses monkeypox in a general context and specifically mentions men who have sex with men, who are typically adults. It does not focus on or include data for non-adult or pregnant populations.",no,The article provides a general discussion of vaccine effectiveness but does not report specific outcome data (safety or effectiveness) from a primary study or systematic review.,no,"The paper is a narrative review or editorial as indicated by its summary of disease dynamics and current management, rather than primary research or a systematic review.",yes,The paper discusses the human monkeypox epidemic.,no,"The study population discussed consists primarily of men who have sex with men, with no mention of pregnant women, neonates, or children.",no,"There is no mention of vaccine administration during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood/adolescence.",yes,The paper specifically mentions the use of smallpox vaccines for controlling the infection.,Exclude,"This paper is a narrative review that summarizes the current state of monkeypox infection and management, specifically noting its prevalence in men who have sex with men. It fails to meet the inclusion criteria because it does not include the target populations of pregnant women or children, nor does it provide primary research data on vaccine outcomes.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""majority of the cases of MPXV have been reported in men who have sex with men.""",,"  - ""smallpox vaccine has traditionally been thought of as the most effective method of controlling the infection,""",,"  - ""current management focus is on immunization""",,"  - ""majority of the cases of MPXV have been reported in men who have sex with men.""",,"  - ""majority of the cases of MPXV have been reported in men who have sex with men.""",,,,"  - ""smallpox vaccine has traditionally been thought of as the most effective method of controlling the infection,""",
Smallpox vaccine acceptability among French men having sex with men living with HIV in settings of monkeypox outbreak.,"Von Tokarski, Florent; Fourn, Erwan; Faucheron, Antoine; Chan Hew Wai, Aurélie; Farfour, Eric; Vallée, Alexandre; Zucman, David Author Information","Florent Von Tokarski, E. Fourn, Antoine Faucheron, A. Chan Hew Wai, E. Farfour, A. Vallée, D. Zucman",10.1097/QAD.0000000000003497,https://doi.org/10.1097/QAD.0000000000003497,AIDS (London),2,2023,yes,"The study focuses on individuals living with HIV, who are not considered 'healthy' adults.",no,The study reports on 'acceptability' rather than clinical safety (adverse events) or effectiveness (prevention of infection) outcomes.,yes,The study appears to be an observational/survey-based primary research study investigating acceptability.,yes,The study involves human participants ('French men').,no,"The study identifies the participants as 'men', which implies adult males, and contains no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"The focus is on the current acceptability of the vaccine during an outbreak among men, not vaccination during childhood, pregnancy, or the neonatal period.",yes,The study explicitly mentions the 'Smallpox vaccine'.,Exclude,"This study examines smallpox vaccine acceptability among adult men having sex with men (MSM) living with HIV. It fails to meet the inclusion criteria as it does not target the required populations (pregnant women, children, etc.) and focuses on perception/acceptability rather than clinical effectiveness or safety outcomes.","Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
Monkeypox in Patient Immunized with ACAM2000 Smallpox Vaccine During 2022 Outbreak,"We report a case of monkeypox in the United States in a patient who had been vaccinated with ACAM2000 smallpox vaccine 8 years earlier. Despite his vaccination status, he still contracted disease. He showed prodromal symptoms preceding development of painless penile lesions that later coalesced.","M. Turner, J. Mandia, Case H Keltner, R. Haynes, Paul Faestel, L. Mease",10.3201/eid2811.221215,https://doi.org/10.3201/eid2811.221215,Emerging Infectious Diseases,26,2022,no,"The patient described is an adult male, which is inferred from 'painless penile lesions'. Therefore, the study is exclusively limited to a healthy non-pregnant adult.",yes,"The study reports effectiveness outcomes, specifically a breakthrough mpox infection ('contracted disease') despite vaccination.",yes,The study is a case report of a single patient.,yes,The study is a case report involving a human patient.,no,"The patient is not a pregnant woman, neonate, or child, which is indicated by 'painless penile lesions'.",no,"The patient was vaccinated 8 years earlier. Given the context of ACAM2000 use and the presence of adult symptoms, this vaccination likely occurred during adulthood.",yes,The study involves the 'ACAM2000 smallpox vaccine'.,Exclude,This case report describes a breakthrough monkeypox infection in an adult male who received the ACAM2000 vaccine eight years prior. The study does not include any individuals from the required target populations such as children or pregnant women.,"Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""painless penile lesions""",,"  - ""Despite his vaccination status, he still contracted disease.""",,"  - ""We report a case of monkeypox in the United States in a patient who had been vaccinated with ACAM2000 smallpox vaccine 8 years earlier.""",,"  - ""We report a case of monkeypox in the United States in a patient who had been vaccinated with ACAM2000 smallpox vaccine 8 years earlier.""",,"  - ""painless penile lesions""",,"  - ""We report a case of monkeypox in the United States in a patient who had been vaccinated with ACAM2000 smallpox vaccine 8 years earlier.""
