Optimal deployment of limited vaccine supplies to control mpox outbreaks,"Mpox outbreaks in central and East Africa have been declared a public health emergency of international concern by the World Health Organization. Fortunately, real-world effectiveness studies of the MVA-BN vaccine indicate that it has an effectiveness of 74% after one dose, and 82% after two doses against mpox. However, given the very limited availability of vaccines, there remain questions around the optimal deployment of limited MVA-BN doses. In this study, we consider whether more mpox cases might be averted by following the traditional two-dose vaccine regimen (4 week dosing interval), or by giving a single dose of MVA-BN to as many individuals as possible. We find that the optimal strategy depends on (i) the degree to which a subpopulation might be at higher risk of mpox, or severe mpox, and (ii) how long ago the first dose was administered to the most at-risk subpopulation.","Matthew T. Berry, C. R. Macintyre, D. Cromer, Adam Hacker, Miles P. Davenport, D. Khoury",10.1038/s41541-025-01289-5,https://doi.org/10.1038/s41541-025-01289-5,npj Vaccines,1,2025,maybe,The study mentions 'at-risk' subpopulations but does not specify if children or pregnant women are included in the modeling parameters.,yes,"The study evaluates if 'mpox cases might be averted', which is an effectiveness outcome.",no,This is a mathematical modeling study ('we consider whether... cases might be averted') rather than one of the specified primary research types or a systematic review.,no,The study is a modeling exercise rather than a study involving human participants or direct clinical data collection.,no,"The abstract does not mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as participants or target populations for the modeling analysis.",no,"As this is a modeling study, no real participants received the vaccine as part of the research.",yes,The study evaluates strategies for the MVA-BN vaccine.,Exclude,This is a mathematical modeling study looking at vaccine deployment strategies for the MVA-BN vaccine. It does not specifically include or mention data for the target populations of children or pregnant women and fails the design criteria as it is not a primary clinical study or systematic review.,"Study Design, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""We find that the optimal strategy depends on (i) the degree to which a subpopulation might be at higher risk of mpox, or severe mpox,""",,"  - ""In this study, we consider whether more mpox cases might be averted by following the traditional two-dose vaccine regimen (4 week dosing interval), or by giving a single dose of MVA-BN to as many individuals as possible.""",,"  - ""In this study, we consider whether more mpox cases might be averted by following the traditional two-dose vaccine regimen (4 week dosing interval), or by giving a single dose of MVA-BN to as many individuals as possible.""",,"  - ""In this study, we consider whether more mpox cases might be averted by following the traditional two-dose vaccine regimen (4 week dosing interval), or by giving a single dose of MVA-BN to as many individuals as possible.""",,"  - ""We find that the optimal strategy depends on (i) the degree to which a subpopulation might be at higher risk of mpox, or severe mpox,""",,"  - ""In this study, we consider whether more mpox cases might be averted by following the traditional two-dose vaccine regimen (4 week dosing interval), or by giving a single dose of MVA-BN to as many individuals as possible.""",,"  - ""In this study, we consider whether more mpox cases might be averted by following the traditional two-dose vaccine regimen (4 week dosing interval), or by giving a single dose of MVA-BN to as many individuals as possible.""
