Smallpox vaccination campaigns resulted in age-associated population cross-immunity against monkeypox virus,"Abstract Increased human-to-human transmission of monkeypox virus (MPXV) is cause for concern, and antibodies directed against vaccinia virus (VACV) are known to confer cross-protection against Mpox. We used 430 serum samples derived from the Scottish patient population to investigate antibody-mediated cross-neutralization against MPXV. By combining electrochemiluminescence immunoassays with live-virus neutralization assays, we show that people born when smallpox vaccination was routinely offered in the United Kingdom have increased levels of antibodies that cross-neutralize MPXV. Our results suggest that age is a risk factor of Mpox infection, and people born after 1971 are at higher risk of infection upon exposure.","K. Dee, Maria Manali, Laura A. Bissett, Jordan Bone, Callum Magill, Chris Davis, Brian J. Willett, P. R. Murcia",10.1099/jgv.0.001999,https://doi.org/10.1099/jgv.0.001999,Journal of General Virology,5,2024,yes,The study uses a 'patient population' which implies individuals seeking medical care rather than exclusively healthy individuals.,no,"The study reports laboratory neutralization assays ('antibody-mediated cross-neutralization') rather than the clinical effectiveness outcomes specified (prevention of infection, hospitalization, or death).",yes,This is a primary research study using a cross-sectional serological survey design.,no,The study is a laboratory-only study focusing on in vitro neutralization assays from human serum samples.,no,"The study does not explicitly state that it includes pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it describes the cohort based on birth years (pre- and post-1971) without specifying if current minors are included in the 430 samples.",yes,"Participants born before 1971 received routine smallpox vaccination, which was historically administered during childhood in the United Kingdom.",yes,The study specifically investigates populations who received historical 'smallpox vaccination'.,Exclude,"The study is a laboratory-based investigation of human serum that measures antibody-mediated neutralization rather than clinical effectiveness or safety outcomes. It fails to specify the inclusion of required target populations such as children or pregnant women, and is classified as a laboratory-only study.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Type, Target Population",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""430 serum samples derived from the Scottish patient population""",,"  - ""investigate antibody-mediated cross-neutralization against MPXV. By combining electrochemiluminescence immunoassays with live-virus neutralization assays, we show that people born when smallpox vaccination was routinely offered in the United Kingdom have increased levels of antibodies that cross-neutralize MPXV.""
