Effectiveness and safety of coronavirus disease 2019 vaccines,"Purpose of review To review and summarise recent evidence on the effectiveness of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) vaccines against severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) infection and COVID-19 hospitalisation and death in adults as well as in specific population groups, namely pregnant women, and children and adolescents. We also sought to summarise evidence on vaccine safety in relation to cardiovascular and neurological complications. In order to do so, we drew primarily on evidence from two our own data platforms and supplement these with insights from related large population-based studies and systematic reviews. Recent findings All studies showed high vaccine effectiveness against confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection and in particular against COVID-19 hospitalisation and death. However, vaccine effectiveness against symptomatic COVID-19 infection waned over time. These studies also found that booster vaccines would be needed to maintain high vaccine effectiveness against severe COVID-19 outcomes. Rare cardiovascular and neurological complications have been reported in association with COVID-19 vaccines. Summary The findings from this paper support current recommendations that vaccination remains the safest way for adults, pregnant women, children and adolescents to be protected against COVID-19. There is a need to continue to monitor the effectiveness and safety of COVID-19 vaccines as these continue to be deployed in the evolving pandemic.","T. Shi, C. Robertson, Aziz Sheikh",10.1097/MCP.0000000000000948,https://doi.org/10.1097/MCP.0000000000000948,Current opinion in pulmonary medicine,3,2023,yes,"The study specifically includes pregnant women, children, and adolescents.",no,"The study reports on effectiveness and safety outcomes, but they are specific to SARS-CoV-2 and COVID-19, not mpox.",yes,This is a review summarising evidence from population-based studies and systematic reviews.,yes,The review summarizes data from human population-based studies.,yes,"The study explicitly mentions pregnant women, children, and adolescents.",yes,The study reviews evidence of vaccination outcomes in pregnant women and children.,no,"The study focuses exclusively on COVID-19 vaccines (targeting SARS-CoV-2), not vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This paper is ineligible as it focuses exclusively on COVID-19 vaccines and SARS-CoV-2 outcomes rather than the required vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines. Although it involves target populations such as pregnant women and children, it does not address the required disease or intervention.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""population groups, namely pregnant women, and children and adolescents.""",,"  - ""summarise recent evidence on the effectiveness of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) vaccines against severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) infection""
  - ""summarise evidence on vaccine safety in relation to cardiovascular and neurological complications.""",,,,"  - ""summarise recent evidence on the effectiveness of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) vaccines against severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) infection""",,"  - ""population groups, namely pregnant women, and children and adolescents.""",,"  - ""population groups, namely pregnant women, and children and adolescents.""",,"  - ""summarise recent evidence on the effectiveness of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) vaccines against severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) infection""",
"Mpox in pregnancy: Management, risks and challenges in Africa and lessons from the COVID‐19 pandemic","Data on mpox in pregnancy are currently limited. Historically, only 65 cases in pregnancy have been reported globally since mpox was discovered in 1958. This includes 59 cases in the current outbreak. Vertical transmission was confirmed in one patient. Pregnant women are at high risk of severe disease owing to immunological and hormonal changes that increase susceptibility to infections in pregnancy. African women appear to be at higher risk of mpox infection and adverse outcomes in pregnancy for epidemiological and immunologic reasons, in addition to the background high rates of adverse feto‐maternal outcomes in the region. This risk is potentially heightened during the COVID‐19 pandemic due to the possibility of mpox virus exportation/importation as a result of the lifting of movement restrictions and trans‐border travels between countries affected by the current outbreak. Furthermore, coinfection with mpox and COVID‐19 in pregnancy is possible, and the clinical features of both conditions may overlap. Challenges of diagnosis and management of mpox in pregnancy in Africa include patients concealing their travel history from healthcare providers and absconding from/evading isolation after diagnosis, shortage of personal protective equipment and polymerase chain reaction testing facilities for diagnosis, vaccine hesitancy/resistance, and poor disease notification systems. There is a need for local, regional and global support to strengthen the capacity of African countries to address these challenges and potentially reduce the disease burden among pregnant women in the continent.","A. Ubom, S. Oiwoh, A. Ajiboye, S. Nyeche, A. Appiah‐Kubi, A. E. Sokunbi, C. Mbiiza, F. Olanrewaju, Edesiri E. Ighorodje, A. Akinkugbe, J. Okoeguale, Oluwaseun Dorcas Ojo, E. Unwaha, P. Oriji, T. O. Adebawojo, O. P. Ekwebalam, Treasure Inyeneobong Okwaraoha, O. Ijarotimi, R. Eifediyi, S. Okogbenin, T. Okwor, J. Ikimalo, O. Kuti, O. Fasubaa, D. Ogoina",10.1002/ijgo.14810,https://doi.org/10.1002/ijgo.14810,International journal of gynaecology and obstetrics: the official organ of the International Federation of Gynaecology and Obstetrics,6,2023,yes,The paper specifically addresses mpox infections in pregnant women.,no,The paper reports on outcomes of mpox infection such as vertical transmission and fetal risk but does not provide safety or effectiveness data regarding vaccination.,no,The work is a narrative review or commentary discussing management and clinical challenges and is not a primary study or systematic review.,yes,The content is based on human clinical data and case histories.,yes,The study population consists of pregnant women.,no,The study does not involve individuals receiving vaccines during pregnancy; it only mentions vaccine hesitancy as a challenge to care.,no,The study does not evaluate or record the use of specific vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,The paper is a narrative clinical review discussing the risks and management of mpox in pregnancy. It is excluded because it does not involve vaccinated participants or report on vaccine safety or effectiveness outcomes.,"Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccination Timing,0.1,"  - ""Historically, only 65 cases in pregnancy have been reported globally since mpox was discovered in 1958.""
  - ""Pregnant women are at high risk of severe disease""",,"  - ""Vertical transmission was confirmed in one patient.""
