Pan‐pox‐specific T‐cell responses in HIV‐1‐infected individuals after JYNNEOS vaccination,"People living with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) are the individuals most affected by the current Monkeypox virus outbreak that was first announced in May 2022. Here we report Pan‐pox‐specific T‐cell responses in a cohort of HIV‐1‐infected individuals after receiving the nonreplicative, attenuated smallpox vaccine JYNNEOS from Bavarian Nordic. Intradermal (i.d.) and subcutaneous (s.c.) vaccination was safe without major side effects. Dose‐sparing i.d. vaccination was superior to s.c. vaccination and promoted T‐cell polyfunctionality, and the expression of the gut‐homing marker α4β7 integrin on lymphocytes. HIV‐1‐infected individuals with CD4 T‐cell counts ≤500/mm3 blood required at least a booster vaccination to exhibit efficient virus‐specific T‐cell responses. The magnitude of the Th1 response after this booster directly correlated with the CD4 T‐cell count of the vaccinees. Further studies with a larger number of participants are warranted to confirm and expand our observations.","Marta Sisteré-Oró, Juan Du, D. Wortmann, M. Filippi, E. Cañas-Ruano, I. Arrieta-Aldea, Agustín Marcos-Blanco, Xavier Castells, S. Grau, N. Garcia-Giralt, D. Perez-Zsolt, Rytis Boreika, N. Izquierdo-Useros, R. Güerri-Fernández, Andreas Meyerhans",10.1002/jmv.29317,https://doi.org/10.1002/jmv.29317,Journal of Medical Virology,9,2023,yes,"The study focuses on individuals living with HIV, which means it is not exclusively limited to healthy adults.",yes,"The study reports on safety outcomes, stating the vaccination was safe without major side effects.",yes,The study is a primary research study involving a cohort of individuals.,yes,The study involves human participants with HIV-1 infection.,no,"There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents in the study; it appears to be an adult HIV cohort.",no,"The vaccination occurred in response to the 2022 outbreak, and there is no evidence participants were vaccinated during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,"The study uses the JYNNEOS vaccine, which is a vaccinia-based attenuated smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This study focuses on T-cell responses in a cohort of HIV-1-infected adults vaccinated with JYNNEOS during the 2022 outbreak. While it provides safety data, it does not include participants from the target populations of pregnant women, children, or adolescents, thus failing the mandatory population inclusion criteria.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""People living with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) are the individuals most affected by the current Monkeypox virus outbreak that was first announced in May 2022.""
