"Exposures to Mpox Among Cases in Children Aged ≤ 12 Years- United States, September 25-December 31, 2022.","The Pediatric Infectious Disease Journal 42(9):p 748-749, September 2023. | DOI: 10.1097/INF.0000000000004020",,10.1097/INF.0000000000004020,https://doi.org/10.1097/INF.0000000000004020,The Pediatric Infectious Disease Journal,0,2023,yes,"The study explicitly focuses on children aged ≤ 12 years, which means the population is not limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,The title focuses on 'Exposures' and 'Cases' rather than reporting on vaccine-related effectiveness (prevention of infection) or safety outcomes.,yes,The paper appears to be an observational study (case series or descriptive research) report on cases in the United States.,yes,The study involves human cases (children) in the United States.,yes,The study explicitly includes children aged ≤ 12 years.,no,There is no mention of vaccination occurring during childhood or any other period; the focus is on exposures among those who already contracted mpox.,no,"The study does not mention vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines, focusing instead on exposures contributing to pediatric cases.",Exclude,"The paper is a study of mpox exposure sources among pediatric cases aged 12 and under. However, it does not involve or evaluate any vaccines, failing the intervention and outcome requirements.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
Effectiveness and safety of prenatal valacyclovir for congenital cytomegalovirus infection,"Objective . The main aim was to investigate the safety and effectiveness of prenatal valacyclovir therapy in pregnancies with maternal or congenital CMV infection. Materials and Methods . The inclusion criteria were pregnancies with confirmed maternal CMV infection. The primary outcome was the incidence of congenital CMV infection. The secondary outcomes were symptomatic and asymptomatic infection, perinatal death, termination of pregnancy, anomalies detected at follow-up ultrasound, fetal MRI imaging or at birth, severe and mild to moderate symptoms due to congenital CMV infection, neurologic, visual, hearing symptoms and adverse events related to valacyclovir. Results . When stratifying the analysis according to the gestational age at maternal infection, the risk of vertical transmission was significantly lower in pregnancies receiving va-laciclovir following first trimester infection (pooled OR 0.30, 95%CI 0.16-0.59, I2 = 0%, p = 0.001). Pregnancies treated with valaciclovir therapy had an increased likelihood of asymptomatic congenital CMV infection, when compared to those not receiving valacyclovir (pooled OR 2.98, 95%CI 1.18-7.55, I2 = 0%, p = 0.021), while there was no significant difference between the two groups in the risk of perinatal death (p = 0.923), termination of pregnancy (p = 0.089), anomalies detected at follow-up imaging assessment during pregnancy or at birth (p = 0","Nicole Meogrossi, Giovanna Salvani, Francesca Di, Danilo Italo, Pio Buca, M. Liberati, F. D'antonio",10.36129/jog.2022.s62,https://doi.org/10.36129/jog.2022.s62,Italian journal of gynaecology & obstetrics,0,2023,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women with CMV infection.,yes,The study reports on the incidence of congenital CMV infection and adverse events.,yes,The presence of pooled odds ratios and I2 statistics suggests this is an evidence synthesis study (meta-analysis).,yes,The study involves human pregnancies.,yes,The study includes pregnant women.,no,The study does not involve vaccination; it evaluates an antiviral drug (valacyclovir).,no,"The study investigates valacyclovir for CMV infection, not vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines for mpox.",Exclude,This study is a meta-analysis focused on prenatal valacyclovir therapy for congenital cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection. It is excluded because it does not investigate mpox or smallpox vaccines.,"Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""prenatal valacyclovir therapy in pregnancies with maternal or congenital CMV infection.""",,"  - ""prenatal valacyclovir therapy in pregnancies with maternal or congenital CMV infection.""
  - ""incidence of congenital CMV infection.""",,"  - ""pooled OR 0.30, 95%CI 0.16-0.59, I2 = 0%,""",,"  - ""prenatal valacyclovir therapy in pregnancies with maternal or congenital CMV infection.""",,"  - ""prenatal valacyclovir therapy in pregnancies with maternal or congenital CMV infection.""",,"  - ""prenatal valacyclovir therapy in pregnancies with maternal or congenital CMV infection.""",,"  - ""prenatal valacyclovir therapy in pregnancies with maternal or congenital CMV infection.""",
Neonatal mpox in Nigeria: a case of transplacental or postnatal transmission,"Little information exists in the literature regarding the burden and impact of mpox infection in the newborn population since the detection of the first case of human mpox over five decades ago. We report a case of laboratory-confirmed mpox in a full-term male neonate with HIV-exposure who presented with a febrile vesiculo-pustular rash syndrome and a centrifugal pattern of distribution (marked on the scalp, face, neck and limbs and sparse on the thorax and abdomen) on day 16 of life at the University of Port Harcourt Teaching Hospital, Rivers State, Nigeria. He had his first skin eruption on the fourth day of life. There was a history of similar lesions in both parents, who are living with human immunodeficiency virus. Vesicular fluid polymerase chain reaction testing confirmed the presence of monkeypox virus (MPXV) DNA and was negative for varicella-zoster virus. Despite the diagnosis of mpox in the early neonatal period with the high likelihood of a transplacental route of transmission, the possibility of a postnatal transmission could not be entirely ruled out. He was optimized on supportive care including nutrition, skin care, fluid management, blood transfusion, and the use of targeted antibiotics. While on admission, he developed bacterial skin co-infection with methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, prolonged illness, and eventually recovered following a delayed time to resolution of skin lesion of 48 days. Neonatal mpox infection is rare and could lead to prolonged morbidity and mortality. Clinicians should always maintain a high index of suspicion and consider mpox in the differential diagnosis of a neonatal vesiculo-pustular rash, particularly if there is a history of similar rash in the family, as early disease recognition and early treatment are associated with improved outcomes. Further studies are needed to better understand the transmission routes of MPXV in newborns.","C. Onyeaghala, E. Alinnor, Mary Irabor, Jennifer Ngufan Nongo, I. Diamond, Chioma Ada Nnah, U. S. Azubogu, Paul Ichendu Nsirimobu, O. Maduka, P. Opara, R. O. Ugwu, D. Alasia",10.1186/s12879-025-11362-y,https://doi.org/10.1186/s12879-025-11362-y,BMC Infectious Diseases,4,2025,yes,"The study focus is a neonate, which is not a healthy non-pregnant adult.",no,The study reports on clinical outcomes of infection and recovery rather than vaccine safety or effectiveness.,yes,"The study is a case report, which is a primary research design.",yes,The study involves a human neonate.,yes,The study specifically includes a neonate as the participant.,no,There is no mention of participants receiving a vaccine at any stage; management was limited to supportive care.,no,The study does not involve the administration or evaluation of vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines; treatment was supportive.,Exclude,"This paper is a clinical case report of mpox infection in a neonate. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not involve any vaccines or vaccination-related outcomes, focusing instead on clinical management and recovery.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""report a case of laboratory-confirmed mpox in a full-term male neonate""",,"  - ""optimized on supportive care including nutrition, skin care, fluid management, blood transfusion, and the use of targeted antibiotics.""
  - ""recovered following a delayed time to resolution of skin lesion of 48 days.""",,"  - ""report a case of laboratory-confirmed mpox in a full-term male neonate""",,"  - ""report a case of laboratory-confirmed mpox in a full-term male neonate""",,"  - ""report a case of laboratory-confirmed mpox in a full-term male neonate""",,"  - ""optimized on supportive care including nutrition, skin care, fluid management, blood transfusion, and the use of targeted antibiotics.""",,"  - ""optimized on supportive care including nutrition, skin care, fluid management, blood transfusion, and the use of targeted antibiotics.""",
"Reports of cell-based influenza vaccine administered during pregnancy in the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS), 2013-2020.","BACKGROUND
In November 2012, the first cell cultured influenza vaccine, a trivalent subunit inactivated influenza vaccine (Flucelvax(®), ccIIV3), was approved in the United States for adults aged ≥18 years. A quadrivalent version (ccIIV4) was later approved in 2016 and replaced ccIIV3. The safety of ccIIV3 or ccIIV4 (ccIIV) was not assessed for pregnant women or their infants during pre-licensure studies.
OBJECTIVE
To assess the safety of ccIIV administered during pregnancy in pregnant women and their infants whose reports were submitted to VAERS during 2013-2020.
MATERIAL AND METHODS
We searched VAERS for United States reports of adverse events (AEs) in pregnant women who received ccIIV from 1 July 2013 through 31 May 2020. Clinicians reviewed reports and available medical records and assigned a primary clinical category for each report. Reports were coded as serious based on the Code of Federal Regulations definition.
RESULTS
VAERS received 391 reports following ccIIV administered to pregnant women. Twenty-four (6.1%) were serious. Two neonatal deaths were reported. No maternal deaths occurred. Among reports with trimester information (n = 340), ccIIV was administered during the second trimester in 170 (50%). The most frequent pregnancy-specific AE was premature delivery in 85 (21.7%) reports, followed by dysmature placenta in 13 (3.3%) and pre-eclampsia/eclampsia in ten (2.3%). The most common non-pregnancy specific conditions were infectious conditions in 32 (8.2%). Among infant conditions, low birth weight was reported in 62 (15.9%) reports. Fifteen birth defects were reported; in 12 with gestational age information, administration of the vaccine occurred late in the second trimester or later.
CONCLUSIONS
Review of maternal ccIIV reports in VAERS was not unexpectedly different from other maternal influenza vaccine safety VAERS reviews.","P. Moro, P. Marquez",10.1016/j.vaccine.2020.12.045,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2020.12.045,Vaccine,11,2020,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women and infants rather than non-pregnant adults.,no,"The study reports safety outcomes such as adverse events and deaths, but these are relative to influenza vaccination and not the prevention or safety associated with mpox or smallpox treatments.",yes,This is a primary research study (a review of VAERS surveillance reports).,yes,The study involves human participants (pregnant women and infants).,yes,The study specifically includes pregnant women and their infants.,yes,The participants received the vaccine during pregnancy.,no,The study evaluates influenza vaccines (ccIIV3 and ccIIV4) and does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,This study is a surveillance review of adverse events following influenza vaccination during pregnancy. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccines and does not report outcomes relevant to mpox infection.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""assess the safety of ccIIV administered during pregnancy in pregnant women and their infants""",,"  - ""VAERS received 391 reports following ccIIV administered to pregnant women. Twenty-four (6.1%) were serious.""
  - ""Two neonatal deaths were reported.""",,"  - ""VAERS received 391 reports following ccIIV administered to pregnant women. Twenty-four (6.1%) were serious.""",,"  - ""assess the safety of ccIIV administered during pregnancy in pregnant women and their infants""",,"  - ""assess the safety of ccIIV administered during pregnancy in pregnant women and their infants""",,"  - ""assess the safety of ccIIV administered during pregnancy in pregnant women and their infants""",,"  - ""first cell cultured influenza vaccine, a trivalent subunit inactivated influenza vaccine (Flucelvax(®), ccIIV3),""",
Historic smallpox vaccination and Mpox cross-reactive immunity: Evidence from healthcare workers with childhood and adulthood exposures.,"In recent years, human mpox has made multiple resurges, prompting public health professionals to consider factors that lead to the increased risk for the reemergence of other orthopoxviruses. Due to the genetic similarity between orthopoxviruses, vaccinia vaccines used to prevent smallpox transmission are also indicated and have been used for mpox infection prevention and control. In this study, cross-reactive immunity for mpox was observed among individuals with self-reported history of smallpox vaccination. Differences in mean antigen response among individuals vaccinated in childhood and adulthood versus individuals vaccinated in childhood only were also observed, supporting the hypothesis that childhood smallpox vaccination may not be sufficient in providing long-lasting protection against multiple orthopoxviruses. These results provide insight on the durability of mpox immunogenic proteins and can be used to inform future studies to assess the benefits of reestablishing vaccinia vaccines as standard recommended immunizations, particularly where orthopoxviruses, such as mpox, are endemic.","Sonja C. Galetti, A. Gadoth, M. Halbrook, Nicole H. Tobin, Kathie G Ferbas, A. Rimoin, Grace M. Aldrovandi",10.1016/j.vaccine.2024.126661,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2024.126661,Vaccine,6,2025,no,"The study is exclusively limited to healthcare workers, who are healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,The study reports on immunogenicity via cross-reactive immunity rather than safety or clinical effectiveness outcomes such as prevention of infection or death.,yes,This is a primary research study examining vaccine response in a cohort of healthcare workers.,yes,The study involves human healthcare workers.,no,"The participants are healthcare workers, which implies they are adults; the study does not include pregnant women or children as current participants, even though it assesses their past childhood vaccinations.",yes,The study includes participants who received their smallpox vaccination during childhood.,yes,The study evaluates historical smallpox vaccines and vaccinia vaccines.,Exclude,"The study evaluates adult healthcare workers to assess long-term immunity from childhood vaccination, but it does not include the target populations of children or pregnant women or report clinical effectiveness outcomes.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Target Population",Target Population,0.1,,,"  - ""cross-reactive immunity for mpox was observed among individuals with self-reported history of smallpox vaccination.""",,,,,,"  - ""individuals vaccinated in childhood and adulthood versus individuals vaccinated in childhood only""",,"  - ""individuals vaccinated in childhood and adulthood versus individuals vaccinated in childhood only""
