Short-term evolution of Mpox-specific IgG and neutralizing antibodies among individuals undergoing MVA-BN vaccination.,"OBJECTIVES
The data on immune responses of individuals undergoing MVA-BN vaccination are scarce. We aimed to compare Mpox virus-specific antibody and neutralizing antibody responses among people with and without HIV receiving MVA-BN vaccines.
METHODS
This prospective study enrolled participants undergoing two-dose MVA-BN vaccination to investigate seroresponses after vaccination. Blood samples were collected before and after each dose of vaccination for determinations of anti-A29 and anti-H3 IgG. Neutralization tests were conducted for samples tested positive for both anti-A29 and anti-H3 IgG.
RESULTS
Overall, 441 participants undergoing two-dose MVA-BN vaccination were enrolled. Seroconversion for anti-A29 and anti-H3 IgG, respectively, after the second dose of vaccination was 18.2% and 61.2%, 10.9% and 65.0%, and 51.6% and 90.6% among PWH, PWoH, and those who had had smallpox vaccination previously, respectively. About 20% of the participants with seroconversion lost seroresponses after a 7-month period of observation. None of the serum samples from vaccinated participants demonstrated neutralizing ability.
CONCLUSIONS
Participants with previous smallpox vaccination had higher and more sustained antibody responses after receiving two doses of MVA-BN vaccines than those who had not undergone smallpox vaccination. More studies are warranted to assess the seroresponses to booster MVA-BN vaccination for vaccine non-responders or those having lost seroresponses.","Wang-Da Liu, Tai-Ling Chao, Kai-Hsiang Chen, Hsin-Yun Sun, Kuan-Yin Lin, Y. Chuang, Yu-Shan Huang, Chi-Ying Lin, Wei-Ting Hsu, Chun-Fu Huang, Guei-Chi Li, Wen-Chun Liu, Cheng‐Hsin Wu, Yi-Ching Su, Lan-hsin Chang, Chia-Yi Lin, Pei-Ying Wu, Ling-Ya Chen, Yi-Ting Chen, Yu-Zhen Luo, Hsi-Yen Chang, Yi-Chun Chen, Yi Yao, Jann-Tay Wang, Wang-Huei Sheng, S. Hsieh, Shih-Chung Chang, Sui-Yuan Chang, Chien-Ching Hung",10.1016/j.ijid.2025.107830,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijid.2025.107830,International Journal of Infectious Diseases,6,2025,yes,"The study includes people living with HIV (PWH), and therefore is not exclusively limited to healthy adults.",maybe,"The study reports on antibody responses and neutralizing ability, but does not explicitly report clinical effectiveness outcomes (prevention of infection) or safety outcomes (adverse events) in the abstract.",yes,This is a prospective primary research study.,yes,The study involves human participants undergoing vaccination.,no,"The study participants are described as people with or without HIV, including those with prior smallpox vaccination (indicating an adult population). There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"As the participants appear to be adults (including those old enough to have received historical smallpox vaccines), they were not vaccinated during the specified periods of interest.",yes,The study involves the MVA-BN vaccine and historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This study focuses on antibody responses to the MVA-BN vaccine in people living with HIV (PWH) and individuals with prior smallpox vaccination. It does not include the target populations of interest such as children, adolescents, or pregnant women, failing the mandatory population criteria.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""compare Mpox virus-specific antibody and neutralizing antibody responses among people with and without HIV receiving MVA-BN vaccines.""",,"  - ""compare Mpox virus-specific antibody and neutralizing antibody responses among people with and without HIV receiving MVA-BN vaccines.""",,"  - ""This prospective study enrolled participants undergoing two-dose MVA-BN vaccination to investigate seroresponses after vaccination.""",,"  - ""Overall, 441 participants undergoing two-dose MVA-BN vaccination were enrolled.""",,"  - ""compare Mpox virus-specific antibody and neutralizing antibody responses among people with and without HIV receiving MVA-BN vaccines.""
