"Myopericarditis Associated With Smallpox Vaccination Among US Army Personnel – Fort Hood, Texas, 2018","Abstract Objective: In March 2018, the US Department of Defense (DOD) added the smallpox vaccination, using ACAM2000, to its routine immunizations, increasing the number of persons receiving the vaccine. The following month, Fort Hood reported a cluster of 5 myopericarditis cases. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention and the DOD launched an investigation. Methods: The investigation consisted of a review of medical records, establishment of case definitions, causality assessment, patient interviews, and active surveillance. A 2-sided exact rate ratio test was used to compare myopericarditis incidence rates. Results: This investigation identified 4 cases of probable myopericarditis and 1 case of suspected myopericarditis. No alternative etiology was identified as a cause. No additional cases were identified. There was no statistically significant difference in incidence rates between the observed cluster (5.23 per 1000 vaccinated individuals, 95% CI: 1.7–12.2) and the ACAM2000 clinical trial outcomes for symptomatic persons, which was 2.29 per 1000 vaccinated individuals (95% CI: 0.3–8.3). Conclusions: Vaccination with ACAM2000 is the presumptive cause of this cluster. Caution should be exercised before considering vaccination campaigns for smallpox given the clinical morbidity and costs incurred by a case of myopericarditis. Risk of myopericarditis should be carefully weighed with risk of exposure to smallpox.","A. Mandra, Michael J Superior, S. Guagliardo, Elisabeth M Hesse, L. Pacha, Ralph A Stidham, D. Colbeck, D. Hrncir, N. Hall, B. Petersen, A. Rao",10.1017/dmp.2020.478,https://doi.org/10.1017/dmp.2020.478,Disaster Medicine and Public Health Preparedness,6,2021,no,"The study population consists of US Department of Defense personnel receiving routine immunizations at Fort Hood, which are populations of healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on cases of myopericarditis, which is a safety outcome.",yes,"The study is an active surveillance investigation involving medical record reviews and patient interviews, which constitutes primary research.",yes,The study involves human military personnel.,no,"The participants are US Department of Defense personnel, and the study does not include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Vaccination occurred as part of 'routine immunizations' for the DOD during active military service, implying participants were adults at the time of vaccination.",yes,The study explicitly evaluates the ACAM2000 smallpox vaccine.,Exclude,"The investigation identifies safety outcomes related to smallpox vaccination but focuses exclusively on US Army personnel. It fails to include target populations such as children, infants, or pregnant women, and the vaccination occurred during adulthood rather than the specified periods of interest.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""US Department of Defense (DOD) added the smallpox vaccination, using ACAM2000, to its routine immunizations,""
  - ""Fort Hood reported a cluster of 5 myopericarditis cases.""",,"  - ""investigation identified 4 cases of probable myopericarditis and 1 case of suspected myopericarditis.""",,"  - ""review of medical records, establishment of case definitions, causality assessment, patient interviews, and active surveillance.""",,"  - ""US Department of Defense (DOD) added the smallpox vaccination, using ACAM2000, to its routine immunizations,""",,"  - ""US Department of Defense (DOD) added the smallpox vaccination, using ACAM2000, to its routine immunizations,""",,"  - ""US Department of Defense (DOD) added the smallpox vaccination, using ACAM2000, to its routine immunizations,""",,"  - ""US Department of Defense (DOD) added the smallpox vaccination, using ACAM2000, to its routine immunizations,""",
Erythema and Induration after Mpox (JYNNEOS) Vaccination Revisited.,"ents of the bivalent vaccine as compared with recipients of the monovalent vaccine.The evaluated events included ischemic stroke (hazard ratio, 0.86; 95% confidence interval [CI], 0.58 to 1.27), hemorrhagic stroke (hazard ratio, 0.86; 95% CI, 0.46 to 1.61), myocardial infarction (hazard ratio, 0.92; 95% CI, 0.62 to 1.36), pulmonary embolism (hazard ratio, 0.83; 95% CI, 0.49 to 1.40), and all four events combined (hazard ratio, 0.87; 95% CI, 0.69 to 1.09) (Table 1).Thus, our results provide reassurance regarding the continued use of this bivalent vaccine.","S. Frey, Johannes B. Goll, J. Beigel",10.1056/NEJMc2215846,https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMc2215846,New England Journal of Medicine,17,2023,maybe,"The study abstract does not specify the demographic details of the recipients, making it unclear whether it is limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on several safety outcomes, including ischemic stroke, hemorrhagic stroke, myocardial infarction, and pulmonary embolism.",yes,The presence of hazard ratios and confidence intervals suggests this is a primary research cohort study.,yes,The study involves human vaccine recipients and tracks medical events like myocardial infarction and stroke.,no,"The provided paper content contains no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"There is no information indicating that participants were vaccinated during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,"The title explicitly names the JYNNEOS vaccine, which is a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine, even though the content references bivalent vaccines.",Exclude,"Although the paper title mentions the JYNNEOS vaccine, the content focuses on safety outcomes such as strokes and myocardial infarction in a general population. There is no evidence of inclusion for the required target populations of pregnant women or children.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,,,"  - ""ischemic stroke (hazard ratio, 0.86; 95% confidence interval [CI], 0.58 to 1.27),""",,"  - ""ischemic stroke (hazard ratio, 0.86; 95% confidence interval [CI], 0.58 to 1.27),""",,,,"  - ""Thus, our results provide reassurance regarding the continued use of this bivalent vaccine.""",,,,,
"Complications of smallpox vaccination, 1968.","Abstract In 1968, 572 persons in the United States had confirmed vaccination complications. Of these, 82.5 per cent had received Vaccinia Immune Globulin. Sixty-eight per cent of the patients were primary vaccinees, 7 per cent were revaccinees, 20 per cent acquired vaccinia not by vaccination but by contact, and 5 per cent had unknown histories of vaccination. There were nine deaths: four caused by postvaccinial encephalitis, four associated with vaccinia necrosum, and one caused by eczema vaccinatum. There were 74 complications and one death per 1,000,000 primary vaccinations. Morbidity and mortality rates were highest for infants, with 112 complications and five deaths per 1,000,000 primary vaccinations. Eczema vaccinatum was more severe for contacts than for vaccinees. Although the risk with revaccination is less than a tenth that with primary vaccination, vaccinia necrosum develops in patients with immunologic disorders whether or not they have been previously vaccinated. These estimates, based on sur...","Lane Jm, F. Ruben, J. Neff, Millar Jd",10.1056/NEJM196911272812201,https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM196911272812201,New England Journal of Medicine,425,1969,yes,"The study includes infants, who are not healthy adults.",yes,"The study specifically reports safety outcomes, including confirmed complications and deaths.",yes,This is a primary epidemiological study using survey data from 1968.,yes,The study participants are human persons in the United States.,yes,The study explicitly includes infants.,yes,The study discusses primary vaccinations administered to infants.,yes,The study evaluates complications arising from smallpox vaccination.,Include,"This is a primary research study regarding smallpox vaccination complications in the United States, which includes specific safety data for infants. It meets all criteria, documenting serious adverse events such as encephalitis and death across differentage groups.",,,5,"  - ""Morbidity and mortality rates were highest for infants, with 112 complications and five deaths per 1,000,000 primary vaccinations.""",,"  - ""In 1968, 572 persons in the United States had confirmed vaccination complications.""
