Reactive Arthritis After mpox Vaccination,"Autoimmune inflammatory reaction after vaccination is a rare clinical entity. Reactive arthritis has been described after various vaccinations, but not after mpox vaccination. Here we present a case of recently diagnosed reactive arthritis after mpox vaccination that presented in the context of unrelenting fever and diarrhea complicated by migratory arthritis and anterior uveitis. We have reported this case to the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS).","Indira Acharya, L. W. Smith, Chandralekha Banerjee, L. Camire, R. Vij",10.55729/2000-9666.1287,https://doi.org/10.55729/2000-9666.1287,Journal of Community Hospital Internal Medicine Perspectives,3,2024,no,"The paper describes a case report that does not specify includes for non-adults or pregnant women, and as a case report in an internal medicine journal, it is presumably limited to adults.",yes,"The study reports the occurrence of reactive arthritis, which is a safety outcome (adverse event).",yes,The study is described as a case report.,yes,The study describes a clinical case in a human patient.,no,"The abstract gives no indication that the case involved a pregnant woman, neonate, child, or adolescent.",no,"There is no evidence that the participant received the vaccine during pregnancy, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The study involves mpox vaccination.,Exclude,"The paper is a case report of reactive arthritis following mpox vaccination. It is excluded because it does not include the target populations of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Here we present a case of recently diagnosed reactive arthritis after mpox vaccination""",,"  - ""Here we present a case of recently diagnosed reactive arthritis after mpox vaccination""",,"  - ""Here we present a case of recently diagnosed reactive arthritis after mpox vaccination""",,"  - ""Here we present a case of recently diagnosed reactive arthritis after mpox vaccination""",,"  - ""Here we present a case of recently diagnosed reactive arthritis after mpox vaccination""",,"  - ""Here we present a case of recently diagnosed reactive arthritis after mpox vaccination""",,"  - ""Here we present a case of recently diagnosed reactive arthritis after mpox vaccination""",
SARS-CoV-2 vaccination in pregnancy,"A 2021–2022 observational study found that a COVID-19 diagnosis during pregnancy increased risk of morbidity and death for the pregnant patient (relative risk [RR] 1.16, 95% confidence interval [CI] 1.03–1.31), and that SARS-CoV-2 vaccination was protective from severe disease and death.","Daniel Dalcin, Scott A. Halperin",10.1503/cmaj.221556,https://doi.org/10.1503/cmaj.221556,Canadian Medical Association Journal,32,2023,yes,The study focuses on pregnant patients.,yes,The study reports that vaccination was protective against severe disease and death.,yes,The text describes an observational study.,yes,The study involves human pregnant patients.,yes,The study explicitly includes pregnant women.,yes,The study evaluates vaccination administered during pregnancy.,no,"The study focuses exclusively on SARS-CoV-2 (COVID-19) vaccination, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study focuses on the effectiveness of SARS-CoV-2 vaccination in pregnant women. However, it does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines, thus failing the mandatory vaccine type criterion.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""A 2021–2022 observational study found that a COVID-19 diagnosis during pregnancy increased risk of morbidity and death for the pregnant patient""",,"  - ""SARS-CoV-2 vaccination was protective from severe disease and death.""",,"  - ""A 2021–2022 observational study found that a COVID-19 diagnosis during pregnancy increased risk of morbidity and death for the pregnant patient""",,"  - ""A 2021–2022 observational study found that a COVID-19 diagnosis during pregnancy increased risk of morbidity and death for the pregnant patient""",,"  - ""A 2021–2022 observational study found that a COVID-19 diagnosis during pregnancy increased risk of morbidity and death for the pregnant patient""",,,,"  - ""SARS-CoV-2 vaccination was protective from severe disease and death.""",
Expected adverse events in a mass smallpox vaccination campaign.,"CONTEXT
Recent anthrax attacks in the United States have raised concern about the nation's vulnerability to a smallpox attack. Many strategies have been suggested to minimize the impact of such an attack, ranging from quarantine and vaccination of case contacts to resumption of routine vaccination. Before the latter strategy is adopted, an understanding of the likely consequences of mass vaccination is essential.
COUNT
Number of adverse events resulting from two vaccination campaigns: vaccinating persons 1 to 29 years of age and vaccinating those 1 to 65 years of age.
CALCULATION
Number of adverse events = incidence rate of adverse events x number vaccinated. We assumed 75% vaccine uptake in the target group (i.e., we estimated that about 25% of potential vaccine recipients would be excluded because they are, or have close contact with, individuals who have eczema or are immunocompromised).
DATA SOURCE
Historical data on the incidence of adverse events from smallpox vaccination were identified by a literature search. Number vaccinated was drawn from the January 2000 U.S. Census estimate.
RESULTS
Fever (<1 case per 5 vaccine recipients) and rash (<1 case per 100 vaccine recipients) would be the most common adverse events. Serious adverse events, including encephalitis (<3 cases per million) and death (<2 cases per million), although rare, would be more common than with other currently recommended vaccines. After excluding high-risk individuals and their contacts, we estimate that a vaccination strategy directed at people aged 1 to 29 years would result in approximately 1600 serious adverse events and 190 deaths. Vaccinating people aged 1 to 65 years would result in approximately 4600 serious adverse events and 285 deaths.
LIMITATIONS
While advances in health care over the past three decades could mitigate vaccine complications, the increased number of unimmunized high-risk individuals (e.g., those with eczema or immune suppression) could increase complication rates.
CONCLUSIONS
The decision to resume smallpox vaccination depends on weighing the likelihood of a smallpox attack and its anticipated mortality against expected harm from a mass immunization program. Smallpox vaccine has a higher complication rate than any other vaccine currently being used. Careful prevaccination exclusion of high-risk individuals and their close contacts would be essential to minimize complications of a mass vaccination campaign, although such exclusions necessarily mean that some proportion of the population will remain susceptible to smallpox.","A. Kemper, Matthew M Davis, G. Freed",,,Effective clinical practice : ECP,145,2002,yes,"The study includes calculations for individuals aged 1 to 29 years, meaning it is not exclusively limited to adults over 18.",yes,"The study reports on adverse events, specifically fever, rash, encephalitis, and death.",no,"The study is a mathematical modeling or calculation effort based on historical literature and census data. It is not a primary clinical research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,The study uses human historical and census data to project outcomes in human populations.,yes,The study models a population that includes children and adolescents aged 1 to 17 within the 1-29 age bracket.,yes,"The study models the impact of vaccinating individuals starting from age 1, including the childhood and adolescent periods.",yes,The study explicitly investigates outcomes related to smallpox vaccination.,Exclude,"This study is a mathematical projection of adverse events related to smallpox vaccination in populations aged 1-29 and 1-65 years. Although it includes children in its projections and addresses safety outcomes, its methodology—calculating expected harms by applying historical incidence rates to census data —does not meet the requirement for primary research or evidence synthesis study designs.",Study Design,Study Design,0.1,"  - ""Number of adverse events resulting from two vaccination campaigns: vaccinating persons 1 to 29 years of age and vaccinating those 1 to 65 years of age.""",,"  - ""Serious adverse events, including encephalitis (<3 cases per million) and death (<2 cases per million),""",,"  - ""Historical data on the incidence of adverse events from smallpox vaccination were identified by a literature search.""
