"Demographic and Clinical Characteristics of Mpox in Persons Who Had Previously Received 1 Dose of JYNNEOS Vaccine and in Unvaccinated Persons — 29 U.S. Jurisdictions, May 22–September 3, 2022","As of November 14, 2022, monkeypox (mpox) cases had been reported from more than 110 countries, including 29,133 cases in the United States.* Among U.S. cases to date, 95% have occurred among males (1). After the first confirmed U.S. mpox case on May 17, 2022, limited supplies of JYNNEOS vaccine (Modified Vaccinia Ankara vaccine, Bavarian Nordic) were made available to jurisdictions for persons exposed to mpox. JYNNEOS vaccine was approved by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) in 2019 as a 2-dose series (0.5 mL per dose, administered subcutaneously) to prevent smallpox and mpox disease.† On August 9, 2022, FDA issued an emergency use authorization to allow administration of JYNNEOS vaccine by intradermal injection (0.1 mL per dose) (2). A previous report on U.S. mpox cases during July 31-September 3, 2022, suggested that 1 dose of vaccine offers some protection against mpox (3). This report describes demographic and clinical characteristics of cases occurring ≥14 days after receipt of 1 dose of JYNNEOS vaccine and compares them with characteristics of cases among unvaccinated persons with mpox and with the vaccine-eligible vaccinated population in participating jurisdictions. During May 22-September 3, 2022, among 14,504 mpox cases reported from 29 participating U.S. jurisdictions,§ 6,605 (45.5%) had available vaccination information and were included in the analysis. Among included cases, 276 (4.2%) were among persons who had received 1 dose of vaccine ≥14 days before illness onset. Mpox cases that occurred in these vaccinated persons were associated with lower percentage of hospitalization (2.1% versus 7.5%), fever, headache, malaise, myalgia, and chills, compared with cases in unvaccinated persons. Although 1 dose of JYNNEOS vaccine offers some protection from disease, mpox infection can occur after receipt of 1 dose, and the duration of protection conferred by 1 dose is unknown. Providers and public health officials should therefore encourage persons at risk for acquiring mpox to complete the 2-dose vaccination series and provide guidance and education regarding nonvaccine-related prevention strategies (4).","Jennifer L. Farrar, N. Lewis, Kennedy Houck, Michelle Canning, A. Fothergill, Amanda B. Payne, A. Cohen, Joshua Vance, Bridget Brassil, Erin M. Youngkin, Bailey Glenn, Anil Mangla, Nikki M. Kupferman, Katharine E. Saunders, Cristina Meza, D. Nims, S. Soliva, Brandon Blouse, T. Henderson, E. Banerjee, Brooklynn R. White, Rachael Birn, Anna M. Stadelman, M. Abrego, Meagan McLafferty, M. Eberhart, M. Pietrowski, S. D. de León, Emma Creegan, A. Diédhiou, Caleb Wiedeman, Jade Murray-Thompson, E. McCarty, J. Marcinkevage, A. Kocharian, Elizabeth A Torrone, L. Ray, D. Payne, Matthew M. Cole, Lauren Roper, Hazel J. Shah, L. McNitt, Stéphanie Gretsch, M. Pike, Patricia Firmender, W. Still, Jamie Ahlers, Aman Punwani, K. Patel, Nam-Kyu Cho, Marcia Pearlowitz, P. Schubert, Ryan E. Malosh, Sydney Kuramoto, M. Donahue, M. Durham, Charlotte DelBarba, Kelly Cogswell, Julie A. Miedlar, Dana Perella, Julian D. Cordero Calderon, Taidy Perez, Jacqueline Logan, Abigail Collingwood, N. Smith, R. Klos",10.15585/mmwr.mm715152a2,https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm715152a2,MMWR. Morbidity and mortality weekly report,31,2022,maybe,"The study focuses on the general population of mpox cases in the US without explicitly stating age or pregnancy status, though it notes 95% were males. As it covers broad demographic characteristics, it is not shown to be 'exclusively limited' to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on clinical outcomes including hospitalization and symptoms (fever, headache, etc.).",yes,This is a descriptive report/analysis of surveillance data comparing vaccinated and unvaccinated cases.,yes,This study involves human cases of mpox in U.S. jurisdictions.,no,"The abstract does not report enrollment of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it notes that 95% of cases were among males but does not specify age ranges for the remaining cases or include pediatriac/obstetric focus.",maybe,"The study looks at vaccination timing relative to infection (>= 14 days) but does not specify if the vaccine was administered during pregnancy, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The study involves the JYNNEOS (Modified Vaccinia Ankara) vaccine.,Exclude,"The study provides clinical comparisons between vaccinated and unvaccinated individuals during the 2022 US mpox outbreak. However, it does not explicitly include or analyze the target populations of pregnant women or children, failing a required demographic inclusion criterion.",Target Population,Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Among U.S. cases to date, 95% have occurred among males""",,"  - ""lower percentage of hospitalization (2.1% versus 7.5%), fever, headache, malaise, myalgia, and chills, compared with cases in unvaccinated persons.""",,"  - ""This report describes demographic and clinical characteristics of cases occurring ≥14 days after receipt of 1 dose of JYNNEOS vaccine and compares them with characteristics of cases among unvaccinated persons with mpox""",,"  - ""This report describes demographic and clinical characteristics of cases occurring ≥14 days after receipt of 1 dose of JYNNEOS vaccine and compares them with characteristics of cases among unvaccinated persons with mpox""",,"  - ""Among U.S. cases to date, 95% have occurred among males""",,"  - ""This report describes demographic and clinical characteristics of cases occurring ≥14 days after receipt of 1 dose of JYNNEOS vaccine and compares them with characteristics of cases among unvaccinated persons with mpox""
