Preemptive Tecovirimat Use in an Active Duty Member Presenting with Acute Myeloid Leukemia after Smallpox Vaccination.,"Smallpox vaccine is contraindicated in immunosuppression due to increased risk for adverse reactions (e.g., progressive vaccinia). We describe the first-ever use of tecovirimat as a preemptive vaccinia virus treatment strategy during induction chemotherapy in an active duty member who presented with acute leukemia and inadvertent auto-inoculation after smallpox vaccination.","D. Lindholm, Raymond D Fisher, Jay R. Montgomery, W. Davidson, Patricia A. Yu, Yon C. Yu, Jillybeth Burgado, Kimberly Wilkins, B. Petersen, J. Okulicz",10.1093/cid/ciz286,https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciz286,Clinical Infectious Diseases,22,2019,yes,The patient is not a healthy adult as they have acute myeloid leukemia.,yes,The study describes the use of tecovirimat as a preemptive treatment strategy for potential adverse reactions after vaccination.,yes,The study is a case report describing a single patient's treatment.,yes,This is a report of a human patient.,no,"The study involves an 'active duty member', which refers to an adult population and not pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"The participant received the vaccine while serving as an active duty member, which occurs during adulthood, not childhood, adolescence, or pregnancy.",yes,The study involves the smallpox vaccination.,Exclude,"This paper describes a case report of an active duty adult member with acute myeloid leukemia who received a smallpox vaccination. Because the study focuses on an adult and does not include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents, it fails the mandatory target population criteria.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""active duty member who presented with acute leukemia and inadvertent auto-inoculation after smallpox vaccination.""",,"  - ""active duty member who presented with acute leukemia and inadvertent auto-inoculation after smallpox vaccination.""
