Non-specific effects of childhood vaccinations - A case control study nested into a Health and Demographic Surveillance System in rural Burkina Faso.,"INTRODUCTION
Previous studies in African countries have been suggestive of non-specific effects (NSE) of vaccination on child survival. Live vaccines (e.g. measles, MV) have been found to reduce child mortality while inactivated vaccines (e.g. diphtheria-tetanus-pertussis, DTP) have been associated with increased mortality; NSE were often found to be sex-specific.
METHODS
A case-control study nested into the Health and Demographic Surveillance System (HDSS) cohort of the Centre de Recherche en Santé de Nouna (CRSN) was conducted in northwestern Burkina Faso. A total of 3,010 children born in 2009-11, were included in the study, 375 cases and 2635 age and village matched controls. The main outcome measures were the mortality odds ratios for vaccinated versus unvaccinated children by antigen. The main outcome measures were the mortality odds ratios for vaccinated versus unvaccinated children by antigen.
RESULTS
Most deaths occurred in late infancy, and there were significantly more deaths in males as compared to females (OR 1.29, CI 1.04-1.60). Overall, there was no statistically significant association between vaccine status and mortality. However, among children in the age group 2-8 months, there was a consistent sex-differential pattern for all doses of oral polio vaccine combined with pentavalent vaccine (OPV + Penta), with the vaccines being associated with lower mortality in boys, but not in girls. Routine MV + yellow fever vaccine was associated with reduced mortality, but only before mass vaccination campaigns with meningitis and measles vaccines took place.
CONCLUSIONS
The findings of this study provide further support on the existence of NSE of childhood vaccinations in a large population of rural Burkina Faso. More randomized controlled trials are needed to confirm these observations.
Copyright © 2017 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.","G. Pfeiffer, A. Fisker, E. Nébié, J. Hengelbrock, Ali Sié, Heiko Becher, Heiko Becher, Olaf Müller",10.1016/j.vaccine.2017.10.089,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2017.10.089,Vaccine,6,2017,yes,"The study is focused on children born between 2009-11, which meets the requirement of not being exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"While the study reports mortality outcomes, these are regarding general non-specific effects of routine childhood vaccinations on survival, not mortality related to mpox or prevention of mpox infection.",yes,The study uses a northern Burkina Faso case-control study design.,yes,The study involves a human population of children in a health and demographic surveillance system.,yes,The study includes children born during 2009-11.,yes,Study participants received vaccinations during their infancy/childhood (ages 2-8 months mentioned).,no,"The vaccines studied are routine childhood vaccines such as MV, DTP, OPV, pentavalent, yellow fever, and meningitis vaccines. There is no mention of vaccinia-based mpox vaccines or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This is a case-control study investigating the non-specific survival effects of routine childhood vaccinations (like measles, DTP, and polio) in children in Burkina Faso. It does not evaluate vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines, nor does it report outcomes related to mpox infection, thus failing two mandatory inclusion criteria.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,,,"  - ""The main outcome measures were the mortality odds ratios for vaccinated versus unvaccinated children by antigen.""",,,,,,,,,,"  - ""Live vaccines (e.g. measles, MV) have been found to reduce child mortality while inactivated vaccines (e.g. diphtheria-tetanus-pertussis, DTP) have been associated with increased mortality;""",
The effect of COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy on rate of COVID-19 infections,"OBJECTIVE: Maternal COVID-19 vaccination has been shown to produce a robust antibody response, but data regarding prevention of clinical disease during pregnancy is lacking. We sought to deter-mine the impact of COVID-19 vaccination on infection rates during pregnancy and other obstetric outcomes. STUDY DESIGN: We performed a prospective cohort study at two urban tertiary care centers enrolling COVID-19 vaccinated and unvaccinated pregnant patients. The primary outcome was the incidence of COVID-19 infection during pregnancy by positive PCR or antigen test, or by antibody testing at the beginning of pregnancy, every trimester, and delivery and was compared based on vaccination status. The secondary outcomes were gestational age at COVID-19 infection and delivery, hypertensive disorder of pregnancy, and NICU admission for the neonate. Multivariable logistic regression was used to adjust for confounders. RESULTS: 350 patients were enrolled, of which the majority (54.3%) were unvaccinated. The unvaccinated cohort was younger (28.9 vs 32.3 years, p < 0.01), included more patients self-identifying as Black (59.1% vs 25.3%, p < 0.01), with public insurance (53.8% vs 21.6%, p < 0.01), with a history of asthma (25.2% vs 12.4%, p < 0.01) and with prior preterm birth (5.3% vs 0.6%, p ¼ 0.01) than the vaccinated cohort","James D. Doss, I. Mysorekar, Fan Zhang, Grace Gerbi, A. Odibo, E. Carter, N. Raghuraman, K. Bligard, J. Kelly",10.1016/j.ajog.2022.11.1107,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajog.2022.11.1107,American Journal of Obstetrics and Gynecology,0,2023,yes,"The study focuses on pregnant patients, meaning it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study reports on COVID-19 infection rates and obstetric outcomes, not mpox-related infection, hospitalization, or death.",yes,"The study is a prospective cohort study, which is a primary research design.",yes,The study involved 350 human patients.,yes,The participants were pregnant patients.,yes,Participants received the vaccination during pregnancy.,no,"The study involves COVID-19 vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,This study evaluates COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant patients. It is excluded because it does not involve vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines and does not report outcomes related to mpox infection.,"Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""We performed a prospective cohort study at two urban tertiary care centers enrolling COVID-19 vaccinated and unvaccinated pregnant patients.""",,"  - ""The primary outcome was the incidence of COVID-19 infection during pregnancy""
