"Comparison of the safety and immunogenicity of ACAM1000, ACAM2000 and Dryvax in healthy vaccinia-naive adults.","Currently, more than half of the world's population has no immunity against smallpox variola major virus. This phase I double-blind, randomized trial was conducted to compare the safety and immunogenicity of two clonally derived, cell-culture manufactured vaccinia strains, ACAM1000 and ACAM2000, to the parent vaccine, Dryvax. Thirty vaccinia-naïve subjects were enrolled into each of three groups and vaccines were administered percutaneously using a bifurcated needle at a dose of 1.0x10(8)PFU/mL. All subjects had a primary skin reaction indicating a successful vaccination. The adverse events, 4-fold neutralizing antibody rise and T cell immune responses were similar between the groups.","S. Frey, F. Newman, J. Kennedy, F. Ennis, G. Abate, D. Hoft, T. Monath",10.1016/j.vaccine.2008.11.079,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2008.11.079,Vaccine,86,2009,no,"The study title explicitly states it was conducted in 'healthy vaccinia-naive adults'. As the criterion requires the study NOT to be exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults, this study fails the criterion.",yes,The study reports on safety (adverse events) and immunogenicity outcomes.,yes,"The study is a phase I double-blind, randomized trial.",yes,The study involves human subjects (30 per group).,no,"The study exclusively targeted healthy adults and does not mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Vaccination was administered to healthy adults, not during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,"The study evaluates vaccinia strains ACAM1000, ACAM2000, and Dryvax.",Exclude,"This study is a randomized trial comparing vaccinia-based vaccines in healthy adults. It is excluded because it does not include the target populations of interest (pregnant women, children, etc.) and is explicitly limited to healthy adults.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Population Focus,0.1,,,"  - ""This phase I double-blind, randomized trial was conducted to compare the safety and immunogenicity""
  - ""The adverse events, 4-fold neutralizing antibody rise and T cell immune responses were similar between the groups.""",,"  - ""This phase I double-blind, randomized trial was conducted to compare the safety and immunogenicity""",,"  - ""Thirty vaccinia-naïve subjects were enrolled into each of three groups""",,,,,,,
The Global Monkeypox (Mpox) Outbreak: A Comprehensive Review,"Monkeypox (Mpox) is a contagious illness that is caused by the monkeypox virus, which is part of the same family of viruses as variola, vaccinia, and cowpox. It was first detected in the Democratic Republic of the Congo in 1970 and has since caused sporadic cases and outbreaks in a few countries in West and Central Africa. In July 2022, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared a public-health emergency of international concern due to the unprecedented global spread of the disease. Despite breakthroughs in medical treatments, vaccines, and diagnostics, diseases like monkeypox still cause death and suffering around the world and have a heavy economic impact. The 85,189 reported cases of Mpox as of 29 January 2023 have raised alarm bells. Vaccines for the vaccinia virus can protect against monkeypox, but these immunizations were stopped after smallpox was eradicated. There are, however, treatments available once the illness has taken hold. During the 2022 outbreak, most cases occurred among men who had sex with men, and there was a range of 7–10 days between exposure and the onset of symptoms. Three vaccines are currently used against the Monkeypox virus. Two of these vaccines were initially developed for smallpox, and the third is specifically designed for biological-terrorism protection. The first vaccine is an attenuated, nonreplicating smallpox vaccine that can also be used for immunocompromised individuals, marketed under different names in different regions. The second vaccine, ACAM2000, is a recombinant second-generation vaccine initially developed for smallpox. It is recommended for use in preventing monkeypox infection but is not recommended for individuals with certain health conditions or during pregnancy. The third vaccine, LC16m8, is a licensed attenuated smallpox vaccine designed to lack the B5R envelope-protein gene to reduce neurotoxicity. It generates neutralizing antibodies to multiple poxviruses and broad T-cell responses. The immune response takes 14 days after the second dose of the first two vaccines and 4 weeks after the ACAM2000 dose for maximal immunity development. The efficacy of these vaccines in the current outbreak of monkeypox is uncertain. Adverse events have been reported, and a next generation of safer and specific vaccines is needed. Although some experts claim that developing vaccines with a large spectrum of specificity can be advantageous, epitope-focused immunogens are often more effective in enhancing neutralization.","Shriyansh Srivastava, S Kumar, Shagun Jain, A. Mohanty, Neeraj Thapa, Prabhat Poudel, Krishna Bhusal, Z. H. Al-qaim, J. J. Barboza, B. Padhi, R. Sah",10.3390/vaccines11061093,https://doi.org/10.3390/vaccines11061093,Vaccines,55,2023,maybe,The paper is a comprehensive review of the global outbreak and does not specify a primary research population. It discusses general epidemiology and vaccine recommendations rather than an empirical study of a specific population.,maybe,"The paper mentions that vaccines can protect against monkeypox and notes that adverse events have been reported, but as a review, it summarizes existing knowledge rather than reporting primary outcomes from a specific cohort.",no,"The title explicitly identifies this as a 'Comprehensive Review'. The content describes historical context, epidemiology, and vaccine descriptions, which aligns with a narrative review rather than a systematic review, meta-analysis, or primary research study.",yes,The paper discusses the human monkeypox outbreak.,no,"The paper mentions pregnancy in the context of vaccine recommendations (stating ACAM2000 is not recommended during pregnancy), but it does not include pregnant women, neonates, or children as participants in a study.",no,"There is no evidence in the text that the 'review' describes a cohort of participants vaccinated during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,The study discusses vaccinia-based vaccines such as ACAM2000 and LC16m8.,Exclude,"This paper is a comprehensive narrative review rather than a primary research study or systematic review. It fails to include a study population of pregnant women, neonates, or children, and does not report primary data on vaccinations during those specific periods.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""The second vaccine, ACAM2000, is a recombinant second-generation vaccine initially developed for smallpox. It is recommended for use in preventing monkeypox infection but is not recommended for individuals with certain health conditions or during pregnancy.""",,"  - ""Vaccines for the vaccinia virus can protect against monkeypox,""
  - ""The second vaccine, ACAM2000, is a recombinant second-generation vaccine initially developed for smallpox. It is recommended for use in preventing monkeypox infection but is not recommended for individuals with certain health conditions or during pregnancy.""",,,,"  - ""Monkeypox (Mpox) is a contagious illness that is caused by the monkeypox virus,""",,"  - ""The second vaccine, ACAM2000, is a recombinant second-generation vaccine initially developed for smallpox. It is recommended for use in preventing monkeypox infection but is not recommended for individuals with certain health conditions or during pregnancy.""",,,,"  - ""Vaccines for the vaccinia virus can protect against monkeypox,""
  - ""The second vaccine, ACAM2000, is a recombinant second-generation vaccine initially developed for smallpox. It is recommended for use in preventing monkeypox infection but is not recommended for individuals with certain health conditions or during pregnancy.""",
Maternal–Fetal Implications of Mpox Infection: Current Evidence,"Mpox is an emerging zoonotic infection caused by the Monkeypox virus, an Orthopoxvirus with increasing global relevance following the 2022 multinational outbreak. Historically endemic to Central and West Africa, the disease has evolved from sporadic zoonotic transmission to sustained human-to-human spread, particularly through close physical and intimate contact. Clinical manifestations typically include fever, lymphadenopathy, and progressive mucocutaneous lesions, although severity varies according to viral clade, immune status, and comorbidities. The 2022 outbreak, predominantly associated with the Clade IIb variant, was characterized by milder disease, localized lesions, and reduced mortality compared with the more virulent Clade I variant. Despite this, severe outcomes remain possible, particularly in vulnerable groups such as children, pregnant individuals, immunocompromised patients, and persons with extensive dermatological disorders. Diagnosis relies primarily on polymerase chain reaction testing from lesion-derived samples, with genomic sequencing serving as a complementary tool for epidemiological surveillance. Management is largely supportive, though antivirals such as tecovirimat may be considered in severe cases or in high-risk populations. Data regarding therapeutic safety in pregnancy are limited; however, tecovirimat appears to have the most favorable profile, whereas cidofovir and brincidofovir remain contraindicated. Prevention strategies include targeted vaccination with the non-replicating Modified Vaccinia Ankara–Bavarian Nordic vaccine, used for both pre- and post-exposure prophylaxis, particularly in individuals at elevated risk. Given the evolving epidemiological profile, the potential for vertical transmission, and the risk of adverse perinatal outcomes, Mpox infection during pregnancy poses unique clinical challenges. This review synthesizes current evidence on virology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, prevention, and management, with an emphasis on obstetric considerations and public health implications.","Stefany Silva Pereira, A. Braga, Beatriz Bussi Rosolen, Talita Almeida Durães, Marcela Fermoselle de Vita Silva, Giovanna Alves de Britto, Giuliana Augustinelli Sales, G. Y. Callado, Camilla Martins dos Santos Maia, Évelyn Traina, E. Araujo Júnior, G. Tonni, R. Granese",10.3390/jcm15010399,https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm15010399,Journal of Clinical Medicine,1,2026,yes,The focus of the paper is on pregnant individuals and children.,yes,The study discusses prevention and management outcomes.,no,"The paper identifies itself as a review that synthesizes current evidence. It does not specify a systematic methodology and is not a primary research study, which is required by this criterion.",yes,"The study mentions human-to-human spread and maternal-fetal implications, falling under human study context.",no,"Because the paper is a narrative review synthesising existing knowledge, it does not include its own study participants.",no,"As a narrative review with no study participants, there is no report of timing of vaccination for participants in a study format.",yes,The study discusses 'Modified Vaccinia Ankara–Bavarian Nordic vaccine'.,Exclude,"This paper is a narrative review focusing on the maternal-fetal implications and pediatric risks of Mpox. It fails the inclusion criteria because it is not a primary research study or a systematic review, and thus does not provide data on specific study participants or vaccination timing.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""severe outcomes remain possible, particularly in vulnerable groups such as children, pregnant individuals,""
  - ""This review synthesizes current evidence on virology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, prevention, and management, with an emphasis on obstetric considerations""",,"  - ""This review synthesizes current evidence on virology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, prevention, and management, with an emphasis on obstetric considerations""",,"  - ""This review synthesizes current evidence on virology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, prevention, and management, with an emphasis on obstetric considerations""",,"  - ""sustained human-to-human spread,""
  - ""This review synthesizes current evidence on virology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, prevention, and management, with an emphasis on obstetric considerations""",,"  - ""This review synthesizes current evidence on virology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, prevention, and management, with an emphasis on obstetric considerations""",,"  - ""This review synthesizes current evidence on virology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, prevention, and management, with an emphasis on obstetric considerations""",,"  - ""Prevention strategies include targeted vaccination with the non-replicating Modified Vaccinia Ankara–Bavarian Nordic vaccine,""",
Therapeutic agents for the treatment of human mpox.,"PURPOSE OF REVIEW
The aim of this study was to summarize the current knowledge of therapeutic options for mpox (formerly known as monkeypox) in the context of recent outbreaks and the ongoing evolution of the virus.
RECENT FINDINGS
Multiple therapeutic agents, including tecovirimat, cidofovir, brincidofovir, and vaccinia immune globulin, have been used during the multicountry outbreak of mpox caused by Clade 2b monkeypox virus that began in 2022. Tecovirimat has been most extensively used, based on efficacy against mpox lethal challenge in animal models, and human safety data. Real-world observational evidence has further supported safety with minimal adverse events in large cohorts and mixed reports of reductions in time to lesion resolution. Several prospective randomized controlled trials using tecovirimat are underway with headline results from a study in the Democratic Republic of the Congo showing no difference in lesion resolution compared to placebo. Other studies including in outpatient settings are underway in Europe and the Americas. Cidofovir and brincidofovir, limited by adverse event profiles, have been less extensively studied. Vaccinia immune globulin has been used predominantly in salvage therapy for severe mpox, with no large observational series available.
SUMMARY
The 2022 multicountry outbreak of mpox marked a public health emergency. Agents approved for smallpox management were widely used for mpox, supported by animal and in-vitro evidence, and human safety data. The large number of human cases has allowed retrospective observational study of these agents and facilitated recruitment in prospective trials. The ongoing evolution of the virus may pose challenges for therapeutic interventions, necessitating rigorous randomized controlled trials to guide clinical use.","Maxwell Braddick, Kasha P. Singh",10.1097/QCO.0000000000001069,https://doi.org/10.1097/QCO.0000000000001069,Current Opinion in Infectious Diseases,9,2024,maybe,"The study focuses on humans with mpox infection, who are not healthy individuals. However, it doesn't specify the age or pregnancy status of the general cohort beyond mentioning human cases.",yes,The study discusses safety (adverse events) and effectiveness (lesion resolution) outcomes.,no,The paper is a narrative review ('PURPOSE OF REVIEW') and not primary research or a systematic review/meta-analysis.,yes,The study summarizes data from human cases and trials.,no,"The abstract does not mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents [[paper_1-6]].",no,"The study focuses on therapeutic agents (treatments) rather than vaccination, and does not specify timing during pregnancy or childhood.",no,"The study evaluates therapeutic agents such as antivirals (tecovirimat, cidofovir) and immune globulin, rather than vaccinia-based vaccines.",Exclude,"This paper is a narrative review focused on therapeutic agents for mpox treatment rather than vaccination. It fails several required criteria, notably excluding the target populations (pregnant women and children) and the required intervention (vaccines).","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""The large number of human cases has allowed retrospective observational study of these agents""",,"  - ""Real-world observational evidence has further supported safety with minimal adverse events in large cohorts and mixed reports of reductions in time to lesion resolution.""",,"  - ""The aim of this study was to summarize the current knowledge of therapeutic options for mpox""",,"  - ""Real-world observational evidence has further supported safety with minimal adverse events in large cohorts and mixed reports of reductions in time to lesion resolution.""
