Monkeypox and the perinatal period: what does maternal–fetal medicine need to know?,"Background After the global elimination of smallpox, monkeypox has become the most threatening orthopoxvirus to human health. Very few studies have been reported on pregnant women and newborns. In the case of monkeypox infection, the virus can cause serious adverse pregnancy events in women, which can lead to fetal or neonatal death. Data sources We made a comprehensive review after an extensive literature search in the PubMed/Medline database and websites concerning smallpox and monkeypox. Results Two case reports reported a total of nine pregnant women, six of whom had fetal deaths. In the autopsy of a stillbirth, researchers found that the placenta was infected with monkeypox virus, but the mechanism of infection remains unclear. Smallpox vaccine should be administered to acutely exposed pregnant women and newborns. Several novel recombinant vaccinia immunogloblin (rVIG) and human-specific monoclonal antibodies are being developed for the prevention and treatment of monkeypox virus infection. After the fetus was delivered, the newborn should take a bath as soon as possible to remove the amniotic fluid and dirt from the body. The appropriate isolation protocol for the newborn should be selected according to the infection status of the mother. It is not known whether monkeypox virus is present in breast milk, and pasteurized breast milk can be given to newborns when breastfeeding is considered. Conclusion This review presents an overview of monkeypox in the perinatal period and guides the future research direction.","K. Yan, Lukun Tang, Feifan Xiao, Peng Zhang, Chunxue Lu, Li-Yuan Hu, Lai-shuan Wang, G. Cheng, Wen-Hao Zhou",10.1007/s12519-022-00630-5,https://doi.org/10.1007/s12519-022-00630-5,World Journal of Pediatrics,8,2022,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women and newborns.,yes,The paper discusses outcomes such as fetal death and neonatal health in the context of mpox and discusses the use of vaccines for prevention.,yes,The paper is a comprehensive review (evidence synthesis).,yes,The review focuses on human cases of pregnancy and neonatal outcomes.,yes,The study specifically addresses pregnant women and newborns.,maybe,"While the review mentions that the vaccine 'should be administered' to pregnant women and newborns, it is unclear if the specific cases reviewed in the literature search involved vaccination during these periods or if it is providing general clinical guidance.",yes,The study discusses the use of smallpox vaccines in these populations.,Include,"This paper is a comprehensive review focusing on pregnant women and newborns, providing clinical guidance and summarizing evidence on mpox outcomes. While it mentions the use of smallpox vaccines in these populations, it's borderline whether the cases described primarily focus on vaccine outcomes.",,,4.6,"  - ""Two case reports reported a total of nine pregnant women,""",,"  - ""Smallpox vaccine should be administered to acutely exposed pregnant women and newborns.""
