Safety Monitoring of Mpox Vaccines in Adults Aged 18-64 Years during the 2022 Mpox Outbreak in the U.S.,"Two vaccines (JYNNEOS; ACAM2000) were available in the United States to prevent mpox during the 2022 clade II mpox outbreak with the majority of people receiving JYNNEOS. As part of the Biologics Effectiveness and Safety (BEST) Initiative, FDA monitored the safety of JYNNEOS using three commercial health plan claims databases supplemented with local and state Immunization Information System data. We assessed vaccine uptake, described the vaccinated population, monitored rates of 11 potential adverse events (AEs) following vaccination, and compared the observed AE rates to expected background rates. There were 152,001 JYNNEOS doses administered. Of the 92,340 people receiving at least one JYNNEOS dose, most were males (93.1%), aged 25-44 years (63.3%), lived in urban areas (98.7%), and 64.2% received a second dose. AE rates following JYNNEOS doses were similar to expected background rates. Additional safety studies of larger vaccinated populations may be needed to evaluate rare AEs including myocarditis/pericarditis.","J. Gruber, K. Matuska, C. Zelaya, J. Wang, C. McMahill-Walraven, L. Peetluck, A. Secora, S. Krishnakumar, M. Hu, K. Amend, J. Song, D. Beachler, P. Lloyd, Y. Chillarige, J. Gwira, W. Tarazi, T. Clarke, D. Djibo, R. Forshee, A. Shoaibi, S. A. Anderson",10.1101/2024.12.17.24319168,https://doi.org/10.1101/2024.12.17.24319168,medRxiv,0,2024,no,The study title explicitly states it is limited to adults aged 18-64 years.,yes,The study monitored 11 potential adverse events following vaccination.,yes,This is a primary research study using commercial health plan claims databases.,yes,"The study includes data from 92,340 people.",no,"The study population is defined as adults aged 18-64 years and no mention is made of children, neonates, or pregnant women.",no,"The study participants were adults receiving the vaccine during adulthood, not during childhood, adolescence, or pregnancy.",yes,"The study evaluates JYNNEOS and ACAM2000, which are vaccinia-based vaccines.",Exclude,"This study evaluated the safety of JYNNEOS and ACAM2000 vaccines in a large adult cohort; however, it is explicitly restricted to adults aged 18-64 years. Because it does not include pregnant women, children, or adolescents, it fails several mandatory population criteria.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,,,"  - ""monitored rates of 11 potential adverse events (AEs)""",,"  - ""monitored the safety of JYNNEOS using three commercial health plan claims databases""",,"  - ""92,340 people receiving at least one JYNNEOS dose,""",,,,,,"  - ""Two vaccines (JYNNEOS; ACAM2000) were available""",
Mpox infection in neonates: what we know so far?,"Since May 2022, there have been more than 85,000 cases of Mpox worldwide, of which approximately 0.1% are in the paediatric age group (<18 years) [1]. There is minimal information on Mpox in the neonatal period. We systematically assessed the literature on Mpox in neonates and its transmission by breastfeeding. The latest World Health Organization (WHO) report included 782 children (491 males, 291 females) aged <18 years with Mpox [2]. With the increasing number of cases in pregnant women, there is concern regarding its transmission (vertical or horizontal) to newborns [3,4]. A few articles have been published on Mpox in the paediatric age group, but none has addressed the infection in neonates [5,6]. This letter focuses on current knowledge and the implications of Mpox infection during the neonatal period. PubMed, Embase and the websites of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), WHO and the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) were systematically searched for (i) the number of neonates with Mpox, route of transmission, clinical features and outcome, and (ii) evidence of transmission of the virus via breast-milk. Appropriate search terms (Mesh and Emtree) and free words were used (neonate, newborn and infant), infection (Mpox or Monkeypox), and breastfeeding (breastmilk and human milk) without any restrictions. There have been only two reports of Mpox in neonates [7,8]. The first was in the Mpox outbreak in Nigeria (2017/18). An infant girl of 28 days had generalised vesiculopustular lesions and later developed severe bronchopneumonia, generalised seizures and encephalitis. She died 8 days after the illness. The exact route of transmission was unknown. The second case was in the United Kingdom during the 2022 Mpox pandemic [7]. He developed a rash on Day 9 of life which evolved into generalised vesiculopustular lesions. Subsequently, he developed hypoxaemic respiratory failure which required intubation and invasive ventilation. The antiviral drugs tecovirimat and cidofovir are active against orthopoxviruses and are FDA-approved for smallpox, but not for Mpox [9]. Recently, tecovirimat was made available for clinical use under an expanded access protocol for Mpox treatment [10]. Although oral tecovirimat is not indicated for infants whose weight is <13 kg, the index neonate received a 2-week course of enteral tecovirimat (50 mg twice daily) in combination with intravenous cidofovir. The neonate gradually improved and was discharged after 4 weeks in the intensive care unit. Both parents had Mpox infection and were in close contact with the infant, and the transmission route was most probably perinatally acquired from caregivers (although vertical transmission cannot be ruled out as the placenta was not examined). These two neonatal cases and other cases in infants suggest that the disease is severe in early infancy and can be transmitted to neonates and young infants through close contact with caregivers [11]. There are reports of vertical transmission to the fetus [3,12], but confirmed vertical transmission to a neonate has not been reported. It is not known whether Mpox virus is secreted in breast-milk [1]. Owing to concerns about transmission, the CDC recommends avoiding breast-milk if the individual is symptomatic or isolated. We searched for reports on secretion of the Mpox virus into breast-milk but no studies support or refute viral secretion into breast-milk. As Mpox spreads through close contact and neonates might have a severe course, the AAP and CDC suggest isolating symptomatic mothers from their infants [1,11]. Both organizations advise against feeding the mother’s own milk until all maternal lesions have resolved, scabs have fallen off, and a fresh layer of intact skin has formed. These organizations also recommend against skin-to-skin contact (including Kangaroo mother care). WHO, however, leaves the decision on breastfeeding to parents and healthcare providers [13]. For evidence-based recommendations, more systematic data on Mpox transmission through breastmilk and the risk-benefit of withholding breast-milk feeds and skin-to-skin contact are needed.","Jogender Kumar, Jitendra Meena",10.1080/20469047.2023.2186077,https://doi.org/10.1080/20469047.2023.2186077,Paediatrics and International Child Health,2,2022,yes,"The study focuses specifically on the pediatric age group and neonates, not healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study reports on infection outcomes, clinical features, and treatment efficacy, but it does not report on vaccination outcomes such as safety or prevention of infection via vaccination.",yes,The paper is a systematic assessment/review of literature including case reports.,yes,The study discusses human infection and clinical cases.,yes,The study explicitly includes neonates and the pediatric age group (<18 years).,no,There is no information regarding study participants receiving a vaccine; the study focuses on infection and antiviral treatment.,no,The study does not involve or evaluate any vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines; it mentions antiviral drugs like tecovirimat and cidofovir.,Exclude,"This study focuses on Mpox infection, transmission, and antiviral treatment in neonates. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not involve or report on mpox or smallpox vaccination.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""approximately 0.1% are in the paediatric age group (<18 years)""
  - ""minimal information on Mpox in the neonatal period.""",,"  - ""There have been only two reports of Mpox in neonates""
  - ""index neonate received a 2-week course of enteral tecovirimat (50 mg twice daily) in combination with intravenous cidofovir.""",,"  - ""We systematically assessed the literature on Mpox in neonates and its transmission by breastfeeding.""",,"  - ""There have been only two reports of Mpox in neonates""",,"  - ""approximately 0.1% are in the paediatric age group (<18 years)""
