High Rates of Miscarriage and Stillbirth among Pregnant Women with Clade I Mpox (Monkeypox) Are Confirmed during 2023–2024 DR Congo Outbreak in South Kivu Province,"Mpox (monkeypox) is a neglected tropical disease that has received increased attention since the multi-nation outbreak that began in 2022. The virus is endemic in West and Central Africa, where the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC) is the most affected country. Clade I monkeypox virus (MPXV) infection is endemic in the DRC and has an overall case fatality rate of 10.6% among children and adults. A study conducted in Sankuru Province, DRC, from 2007 to 2011 demonstrated that 75% of pregnant women with mpox had miscarriages or stillbirth. Further analysis of a stillborn fetus showed that MPXV could infect both the placenta and fetus, causing congenital infection. No additional cases of Clade I MPXV in pregnant women were reported until a new outbreak occurred in South Kivu Province during 2023 and 2024. Eight pregnant women having Clade I MPXV infection were identified, of whom four had either miscarriages or stillbirth, representing a 50% fetal mortality rate. These reports confirm previous data from the DRC that indicate the capability of Clade I MPXV to affect the fetus, causing congenital infection and fetal loss in a high percentage of cases. In this article, we review both past and new data from the DRC on the effects of Clade I MPXV during pregnancy and discuss the association of mpox with fetal loss.",David A. Schwartz,10.3390/v16071123,https://doi.org/10.3390/v16071123,Viruses,32,2024,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women.,maybe,"The study reports on fetal mortality, including miscarriages and stillbirths. However, it does not explicitly discuss vaccine effectiveness or vaccine safety outcomes, as it focuses on the natural history of infection.",yes,The paper presents new data on a series of eight cases in South Kivu and reviews previous data; it functions as a case series or primary research report.,yes,"The study involves human participants, specifically pregnant women.",yes,The study population includes pregnant women.,no,The paper does not mention vaccination or the timing of vaccination for the pregnant women described; it focuses on the outcomes of Clade I MPXV infection during pregnancy.,no,The paper describes mpox infection but does not involve or evaluate vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"While the study focuses on a priority population (pregnant women) and relevant clinical outcomes of mpox infection, it contains no information regarding vaccination or vaccine-related outcomes. Because it does not involve any vaccines, it fails the mandatory criteria for vaccination timing and vaccine type.","Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Eight pregnant women having Clade I MPXV infection were identified, of whom four had either miscarriages or stillbirth,""",,"  - ""Eight pregnant women having Clade I MPXV infection were identified, of whom four had either miscarriages or stillbirth,""
