  - ""In this article, we review both past and new data from the DRC on the effects of Clade I MPXV during pregnancy and discuss the association of mpox with fetal loss.""",,"  - ""Eight pregnant women having Clade I MPXV infection were identified, of whom four had either miscarriages or stillbirth,""",,"  - ""Eight pregnant women having Clade I MPXV infection were identified, of whom four had either miscarriages or stillbirth,""",,"  - ""In this article, we review both past and new data from the DRC on the effects of Clade I MPXV during pregnancy and discuss the association of mpox with fetal loss.""",,"  - ""In this article, we review both past and new data from the DRC on the effects of Clade I MPXV during pregnancy and discuss the association of mpox with fetal loss.""",
Development of Eczema Vaccinatum in Atopic Mouse Models and Efficacy of MVA Vaccination against Lethal Poxviral Infection,"Smallpox vaccine based on live, replicating vaccinia virus (VACV) is associated with several potentially serious and deadly complications. Consequently, a new generation of vaccine based on non-replicating Modified vaccinia virus Ankara (MVA) has been under clinical development. MVA seems to induce good immune responses in blood tests, but it is impossible to test its efficacy in vivo in human. One of the serious complications of the replicating vaccine is eczema vaccinatum (EV) occurring in individuals with atopic dermatitis (AD), thus excluding them from all preventive vaccination schemes. In this study, we first characterized and compared development of eczema vaccinatum in different mouse strains. Nc/Nga, Balb/c and C57Bl/6J mice were epicutaneously sensitized with ovalbumin (OVA) or saline control to induce signs of atopic dermatitis and subsequently trans-dermally (t.d.) immunized with VACV strain Western Reserve (WR). Large primary lesions occurred in both mock- and OVA-sensitized Nc/Nga mice, while they remained small in Balb/c and C57Bl/6J mice. Satellite lesions developed in both mock- and OVA-sensitized Nc/Nga and in OVA-sensitized Balb/c mice with the rate 40–50%. Presence of mastocytes and eosinophils was the highest in Nc/Nga mice. Consequently, we have chosen Nc/Nga mice as a model of AD/EV and tested efficacy of MVA and Dryvax vaccinations against a lethal intra-nasal (i.n.) challenge with WR, the surrogate of smallpox. Inoculation of MVA intra-muscularly (i.m.) or t.d. resulted in no lesions, while inoculation of Dryvax t.d. yielded large primary and many satellite lesions similar to WR. Eighty three and 92% of mice vaccinated with a single dose of MVA i.m. or t.d., respectively, survived a lethal i.n. challenge with WR without any serious illness, while all Dryvax-vaccinated animals survived. This is the first formal prove of protective immunity against a lethal poxvirus challenge induced by vaccination with MVA in an atopic organism.","J. Knitlová, V. Hájková, L. Voska, Jana Elsterová, Barbora Obrova, Z. Melkova",10.1371/journal.pone.0114374,https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0114374,PLoS ONE,6,2014,maybe,The study does not focus on human adults as it uses mouse models.,yes,"The study reports on the efficacy and survival of subjects following vaccination and challenge, as well as complications like lesions, which are relevant outcomes, though in a non-human model.",yes,The study is a primary research laboratory study using mouse models.,no,"This is an animal study using mice, not a human study, and explicitly states the difficulty of testing in humans.",no,"The participants are mice, not human pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Vaccination occurred in mice, not during human pregnancy, neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,"The study evaluates MVA and Dryvax, which are vaccinia-based vaccines.",Exclude,"This study is excluded because it is an animal research study conducted using mouse models rather than human participants. Consequently, it does not involve the required human target populations, such as pregnant women or children.","Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""Nc/Nga, Balb/c and C57Bl/6J mice""",,"  - ""Dryvax vaccinations""",,,,"  - ""Nc/Nga, Balb/c and C57Bl/6J mice""
  - ""it is impossible to test its efficacy in vivo in human.""",,"  - ""Nc/Nga, Balb/c and C57Bl/6J mice""",,"  - ""Nc/Nga, Balb/c and C57Bl/6J mice""",,"  - ""Modified vaccinia virus Ankara (MVA)""
  - ""Dryvax vaccinations""",
Current and emerging Mpox vaccine strategies: A comprehensive review.,"Monkeypox virus (MPXV), a zoonotic orthopoxvirus closely related to variola virus, is a double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) virus in the Orthopoxvirus genus, which includes vaccinia and cowpox viruses. Identified in 1958 and first documented in humans in 1970, MPXV poses substantial public health challenges. Its origins remain uncertain, but African rodents like fire-footed rope squirrels and non-human primates are potential reservoirs. Recent global outbreaks have highlighted the urgent need for effective vaccination strategies to mitigate morbidity, mortality, and transmission risks. Approved vaccines such as ACAM2000 and MVA-BN demonstrate efficacy in preventing MPXV infection, with MVA-BN offering a safer profile, particularly for immunocompromised individuals. Emerging platforms, including mRNA and multivalent vaccines, show promise in enhancing immune responses and addressing limitations of traditional vaccines. Preclinical studies highlight significant advancements in rapid-deployment technologies, enabling robust humoral and cellular immunity against MPXV. Current vaccination strategies against MPXV provide foundational tools for outbreak control; however, gaps remain in accessibility, safety, and scalability. Equitable vaccine distribution and investment in research are essential to address the global mpox threat effectively.","Esteban Ortiz-Prado, N. Kyriakidis, Andrés López-Cortés, Jorge Vásconez-González, Isaac Suarez, J. Pazmiño-Almeida, M. Barriga-Collantes, María Paz Cadena, María Reascos-Arteaga, Esteban Acosta-Muñoz, Maria C. Acosta-Muñoz, Karen Villarreal, J. Izquierdo-Condoy",10.1016/j.vaccine.2025.127598,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2025.127598,Vaccine,8,2025,maybe,"The paper is a comprehensive review of strategies and does not specify a focus on healthy adults, but rather discusses global outbreaks and general strategies.",yes,The review discusses the efficacy of vaccines in preventing MPXV infection and mitigating morbidity and mortality.,maybe,"The title and content identify this as a 'comprehensive review'. It is not specified if this is a systematic review or meta-analysis, making it potentially an excluded narrative review or a non-primary study.",yes,"The paper reviews human vaccination strategies but also mentions preclinical studies. As a review, it synthesizes existing data which likely includes human data.",maybe,"The abstract does not mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it focuses on 'global outbreaks' and 'immunocompromised individuals' generally.",maybe,"There is no information regarding the timing of vaccination relative to pregnancy, childhood, or adolescence in the abstract.",yes,The paper explicitly mentions vaccinia-based vaccines such as ACAM2000 and MVA-BN.,Include,"This paper is a comprehensive review of mpox vaccine strategies including vaccinia-based vaccines. However, the abstract does not specify whether it focuses on the required target populations (pregnant women, children, etc.) or if it is a systematic review rather than a narrative review.",,,3.6,"  - ""Recent global outbreaks have highlighted the urgent need for effective vaccination strategies to mitigate morbidity, mortality, and transmission risks.""",,"  - ""Recent global outbreaks have highlighted the urgent need for effective vaccination strategies to mitigate morbidity, mortality, and transmission risks.""",,,,"  - ""Preclinical studies highlight significant advancements in rapid-deployment technologies,""",,,,,,"  - ""Recent global outbreaks have highlighted the urgent need for effective vaccination strategies to mitigate morbidity, mortality, and transmission risks.""",
