"Cutaneous Mpox: an epidemiological, clinical, diagnostic and therapeutic review of the dermatologic pandemic of the decade.","INTRODUCTION
Mpox (formerly monkeypox), caused by the monkeypox virus (MPXV), has historically been confined to parts of Central and West Africa. However, recent outbreaks in non-endemic regions (2022-2024) revealed efficient transmission within sexual networks and a broad range of cutaneous and mucosal presentations. The aim of this review is to summarize key aspects of Mpox, including its epidemiology, pathogenesis, clinical features, diagnosis, treatment, and public health strategies for outbreak control.
EVIDENCE ACQUISITION
A systematic literature search in PubMed until December 2024 was conducted using terms such as ""monkeypox,"", ""mpox"", ""orthopoxvirus,"" and ""skin lesions"". Selected articles pertinent to dermatological clinical features, diagnostic methods, management, and prevention strategies were reviewed.
EVIDENCE SYNTHESIS
While Mpox often presents with characteristic vesiculopustular lesions, the 2022 outbreak underscored frequent anogenital localization mimicking other sexually transmitted infections. Confirmatory testing via PCR remains essential, and supportive care suffices for most immunocompetent individuals. In severe or immunocompromised cases, antivirals like tecovirimat can shorten disease duration. Vaccination campaigns with Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA-BN) successfully reduced incidence in high-risk populations. Emerging evidence from Dermatology journals emphasizes atypical morphologies - hemorrhagic, necrotic, and bullous variants - in both immunocompetent and immunocompromised patients.
CONCLUSIONS
Cutaneous Mpox presents diagnostic and therapeutic challenges for dermatologists and venereologists, particularly when lesions deviate from classical patterns. Continued research on vaccine durability, combination antiviral therapies, and long-term cutaneous sequelae will refine future outbreak responses and patient care.","F. J. Rodríguez-Cuadrado, M. Fernández‐Parrado, E. L. Pinto-Pulido",10.23736/S2784-8671.25.08332-X,https://doi.org/10.23736/S2784-8671.25.08332-X,Italian journal of dermatology and venereology,0,2025,yes,"The study includes immunocompromised individuals, meaning it is not exclusively limited to healthy adults.",yes,"The study reports on prevention effectiveness, specifically that vaccination reduced incidence.",yes,This is described as a review based on a systematic literature search.,yes,The study discusses human epidemiology and clinical features observed in recent outbreaks.,no,"The abstract does not mention the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; instead, it focuses on high-risk populations and sexual networks, which typically imply adult populations in the context of recent Mpox outbreaks.",no,"There is no information indicating that participants received vaccination during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,"The study mentions MVA-BN (Modified Vaccinia Ankara), which is a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This study is a general dermatological review of Mpox that focuses on adult-centric contexts such as sexual networks and venereology. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not include or focus on the target populations of pregnant women, children, or adolescents.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""In severe or immunocompromised cases, antivirals like tecovirimat can shorten disease duration.""",,"  - ""Vaccination campaigns with Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA-BN) successfully reduced incidence in high-risk populations.""",,"  - ""A systematic literature search in PubMed until December 2024 was conducted""",,"  - ""efficient transmission within sexual networks""",,"  - ""efficient transmission within sexual networks""
