Adverse reactions following MPox (monkeypox) vaccination: An overview from the Dutch and global adverse event reporting systems,The spreading of MPox (monkeypox) virus led to a vaccination campaign in the Netherlands for individuals at high risk of infection. This overview elucidates the characteristics of the reported adverse events following immunization (AEFI) following active immunization against monkeypox and thereby provides insight on the safety profile in daily practice.,"M. van der Boom, Florence van Hunsel",10.1111/bcp.15830,https://doi.org/10.1111/bcp.15830,British Journal of Clinical Pharmacology,7,2023,maybe,"The abstract mentions individuals at high risk of infection, which in the Dutch mpox campaign context often referred to adults, but the text does not explicitly state that the study is limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study explicitly examines safety outcomes in the form of adverse events following immunization (AEFI).,yes,"This is an overview analyzing data from adverse event reporting systems, which constitutes a primary observational study of surveillance data.",yes,The study investigates results from a vaccination campaign in humans.,maybe,"The text discusses high-risk individuals and data from reporting systems, which might include pregnant women or children, but these groups are not specifically named in the provided abstract.",maybe,"The abstract does not specify the timing (e.g., pregnancy or childhood) for the vaccination of the high-risk individuals mentioned.",yes,"The study involves active immunization against monkeypox, which utilizes vaccinia-based vaccines.",Include,"This study analyze reports of adverse events following mpox vaccination in the Netherlands and globally. While it reports on safety outcomes and human subjects, it is unclear whether participants include pregnant women, children, or adolescents, as the population is described generally as high-risk individuals.",,,3.1,"  - ""vaccination campaign in the Netherlands for individuals at high risk of infection.""",,"  - ""reported adverse events following immunization (AEFI) following active immunization against monkeypox""",,"  - ""reported adverse events following immunization (AEFI) following active immunization against monkeypox""",,"  - ""vaccination campaign in the Netherlands for individuals at high risk of infection.""",,"  - ""vaccination campaign in the Netherlands for individuals at high risk of infection.""",,"  - ""vaccination campaign in the Netherlands for individuals at high risk of infection.""",,"  - ""reported adverse events following immunization (AEFI) following active immunization against monkeypox""",
Evaluating the Immunogenicity and Safety of a Smallpox Vaccine to Monkeypox in Healthy Japanese Adults: A Single-Arm Study,"Monkeypox (mpox) is an acute exanthematous disease caused by the monkeypox virus (MPXV). Since May 2022, patients with mpox have been reported worldwide, mainly in Europe and the Americas. In Japan, LC16”KMB,” which is a smallpox vaccine derived from a dried cell culture, against mpox, has been approved. Although inoculation with a smallpox vaccine has been recommended to prevent MPXV infection, the immunogenicity of the smallpox vaccine against the MPXV is unclear, and information regarding postvaccination safety is scarce. We present the protocol for a single-arm open-label study to investigate the immunogenicity and safety of LC16”KMB” against the MPXV in healthy Japanese adults. The primary endpoint is the seroconversion rate of neutralizing antibodies against the MPXV on postvaccination day 28. The secondary endpoints are the seroconversion rates against the MPXV on postvaccination days 14 and 168; the seroconversion rates against the vaccinia virus on postvaccination days 14, 28, and 168; the incidence of mpox until day 168; and adverse and serious adverse events until postvaccination days 28 and 168. These results will pave the way for larger comparative studies using other smallpox vaccines to evaluate the test vaccine’s safety and efficacy in preventing mpox.","N. Tomita, E. Morino, J. Terada-Hirashima, Y. Uemura, Yosuke Shimizu, S. Saito, Tetsuya Suzuki, N. Okumura, H. Iwasaki, H. Ebihara, M. Shimojima, W. Sugiura, N. Ohmagari, M. Ujiie",10.3390/life13030787,https://doi.org/10.3390/life13030787,Life,6,2023,no,The study is exclusively limited to healthy Japanese adults. It does not focus on pregnant women or other specified groups.,yes,The protocol lists the incidence of mpox and adverse events as secondary endpoints.,no,"While it describes a clinical trial, the document is a study protocol rather than a report of completed primary research with results.",yes,The study involves human participants (Japanese adults).,no,"The study only includes healthy adults and does not include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Participants are adults, and there is no indication vaccination occurs during pregnancy, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The study uses the LC16”KMB” smallpox vaccine.,Exclude,This paper is a study protocol for a trial conducted exclusively in healthy Japanese adults. It fails to include the mandatory target populations such as pregnant women or children and does not report primary research results.,"Population Focus, Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""healthy Japanese adults.""",,"  - ""incidence of mpox until day 168; and adverse and serious adverse events until postvaccination days 28 and 168.""",,"  - ""protocol for a single-arm open-label study""",,"  - ""healthy Japanese adults.""",,"  - ""healthy Japanese adults.""",,"  - ""healthy Japanese adults.""",,"  - ""LC16”KMB,” which is a smallpox vaccine derived from a dried cell culture,""",
Implementing a smallpox vaccination program aboard an aircraft carrier.,"OBJECTIVE
To determine the feasibility of implementing a smallpox vaccination program aboard an aircraft carrier in conjunction with anthrax vaccination.
METHODS
Retrospective review of smallpox vaccination program conducted from January 17, 2003 to February 19, 2003. Morbidity and loss of manpower were the major endpoints.
RESULTS
There were 5,204 sailors available for vaccination. There were 243 (4.7%) medical exemptions and 24 administrative exemptions. During the program, 4,931 sailors were vaccinated. There were five reportable complications. Three sailors had autoinoculation, one sailor had localized cellulitis, and one patient had a positive beta human chorionic gonadotropin during vaccination. None of the complications required medical evacuation. Only two sailors required time off from duty.
CONCLUSIONS
Smallpox vaccination can be accomplished rapidly and safely aboard an aircraft carrier. There was not an increase in adverse events compared to historical data despite the close-quarter conditions. Smallpox and anthrax vaccinations can be completed simultaneously with minimal morbidity.","Jeffrey Apple, P. Hare, Chris Crerar, Errika Walker, Joe Wilson, C. Brown, Kennett Moses, T. Hendrickson, D. Field, Francis Hoffman, Patrick Pedrus, John Miller, Brad Smith",10.7205/MILMED.169.6.455,https://doi.org/10.7205/MILMED.169.6.455,Military Medicine,0,2004,yes,"The study reports on one patient with a positive beta human chorionic gonadotropin during vaccination, which indicates the population was not exclusively limited to non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports safety outcomes including complications such as autoinoculation and localized cellulitis.,yes,The study is a retrospective review of a vaccination program.,yes,The study involves human sailors aboard an aircraft carrier.,yes,"The study identifies one participant with a positive beta human chorionic gonadotropin (pregnancy test) during vaccination, meeting the requirement for including pregnant women.",yes,"The vaccination occurred while the patient had a positive beta human chorionic gonadotropin test, indicating vaccination during pregnancy.",yes,The study explicitly involves a smallpox vaccination program.,Include,This study evaluated a smallpox vaccination program among sailors and included safety outcomes for a participant who tested positive for pregnancy (beta human chorionic gonadotropin) during the vaccination period. It is a primary research study that meets all inclusion criteria including target population and vaccine type.,,,5,"  - ""There were five reportable complications. Three sailors had autoinoculation, one sailor had localized cellulitis, and one patient had a positive beta human chorionic gonadotropin during vaccination.""
