Emulating target trials of postexposure vaccines using observational data.,"Postexposure vaccination has the potential to prevent or modify the course of clinical disease among those exposed to a pathogen. However, due to logistical constraints, postexposure vaccine trials have been difficult to implement in practice. In place of trials, investigators have used observational data to estimate the effectiveness or optimal timing window for postexposure vaccines, but the relationship between these analyses and those that would be conducted in a trial is often unclear. Here, we define several possible target trials for postexposure vaccination and show how, under certain conditions, they can be emulated using observational data. We emphasize the importance of the incubation period and the timing of vaccination in trial design and emulation. As an example, we specify a protocol for postexposure vaccination against mpox and provide a step-by-step description of how to emulate it using data from a healthcare database or contact tracing program. We further illustrate some of the benefits of the target trial approach through simulation.","Christopher B Boyer, Marc Lipsitch",10.1093/aje/kwae350,https://doi.org/10.1093/aje/kwae350,American Journal of Epidemiology,1,2024,maybe,The study defines a generic methodology and protocol for mpox vaccination without specifying the health or pregnancy status of participants beyond potential exposure.,no,"While the paper discusses estimating effectiveness, it does so as a methodological framework and does not report actual safety or effectiveness results from a clinical population.",no,"The study is a methodological paper involving trial emulation and simulation, rather than primary clinical research (RCT, cohort) or a systematic review of existing evidence.",no,This is a methodological paper illustrating a target trial approach through simulation rather than a study of human subjects.,no,"The abstract does not mention the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it specifies a general protocol for mpox exposure.",no,"There is no mention of participants receiving vaccines during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,The study explicitly discusses vaccination against mpox.,Exclude,"This paper is a methodological framework and simulation study focused on the emulation of target trials for postexposure mpox vaccination. It is excluded because it is not primary human research or a systematic review, and it does not include or focus on the required populations of pregnant women or children.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""define several possible target trials for postexposure vaccination and show how, under certain conditions, they can be emulated using observational data.""
  - ""specify a protocol for postexposure vaccination against mpox""",,"  - ""estimate the effectiveness or optimal timing window for postexposure vaccines,""",,"  - ""define several possible target trials for postexposure vaccination and show how, under certain conditions, they can be emulated using observational data.""
  - ""illustrate some of the benefits of the target trial approach through simulation.""",,"  - ""illustrate some of the benefits of the target trial approach through simulation.""",,"  - ""specify a protocol for postexposure vaccination against mpox""",,"  - ""define several possible target trials for postexposure vaccination and show how, under certain conditions, they can be emulated using observational data.""
