A randomized phase 3 trial to assess the immunogenicity and safety of 3 consecutively produced lots of freeze-dried MVA-BN® vaccine in healthy adults,"Since vaccination remains the only effective protection against orthopox virus-induced diseases such as smallpox or monkeypox, the strategic use and stockpiling of these vaccines remains of significant public health importance. The approved liquid-frozen formulation of Bavarian Nordic's Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA-BN) smallpox vaccine has specific cold-chain requirements, while the freeze-dried (FD) formulation of this vaccine provides more flexibility in terms of storage conditions and shelf life. In this randomized phase 3 trial, the immunogenicity and safety of 3 consecutively manufactured lots of the FD MVA-BN vaccine was evaluated. A total of 1129 healthy adults were randomized to 3 treatment groups (lots 1 to 3) and received 2 vaccinations 4 weeks apart. For both neutralizing and total antibodies, a robust increase of geometric mean titer (GMT) was observed across all lot groups 2 weeks following the second vaccination, comparable to published data. For the primary results, the ratios of the neutralizing antibody GMTs between the lot group pairs ranged from 0.936 to 1.115, with confidence ratios well within the pre-specified margin of equivalence. Results for total antibodies were similar. In addition, seroconversion rates were high across the 3 lots, ranging between 99.1 % and 99.7 %. No safety concerns were identified; particularly, no inflammatory cardiac disorders were detected. The most common local solicited adverse events (AEs) reported across lot groups were injection site pain (87.2%) and erythema (73.2%), while the most common general solicited adverse events were myalgia, fatigue, and headache in 40.6% to 45.5% of all participants, with no meaningful differences among the lot groups. No related serious AEs were reported. In conclusion, the data demonstrate consistent and robust immunogenicity and safety results with a freeze-dried formulation of MVA-BN. Clinical Trial Registry Number: NCT03699124.","Edgar Turner Overton, D. Schmidt, Sanja Vidojkovic, E. Menius, Katrin Nopora, J. Maclennan, H. Weidenthaler",10.1016/j.vaccine.2022.10.056,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2022.10.056,Vaccine,17,2022,no,"The study is exclusively limited to healthy adults, which violates the requirement that the study not be limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports safety outcomes including specific adverse events and the absence of serious adverse events.,yes,The study is a randomized phase 3 trial.,yes,The study is a human clinical trial involving 1129 adults.,no,"The study participants are healthy adults and do not include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Participants received the vaccine as adults, not during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,The study evaluates the MVA-BN smallpox vaccine.,Exclude,"This study is a phase 3 trial evaluating the safety and immunogenicity of the MVA-BN vaccine specifically in 1129 healthy adults. It fails multiple mandatory criteria because it does not include pregnant women, children, or other target demographic groups.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""A total of 1129 healthy adults were randomized to 3 treatment groups""",,"  - ""No safety concerns were identified; particularly, no inflammatory cardiac disorders were detected.""
  - ""The most common local solicited adverse events (AEs) reported across lot groups were injection site pain (87.2%) and erythema (73.2%),""
  - ""No related serious AEs were reported.""",,"  - ""In this randomized phase 3 trial,""",,"  - ""A total of 1129 healthy adults were randomized to 3 treatment groups""",,"  - ""A total of 1129 healthy adults were randomized to 3 treatment groups""",,"  - ""A total of 1129 healthy adults were randomized to 3 treatment groups""",,"  - ""The approved liquid-frozen formulation of Bavarian Nordic's Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA-BN) smallpox vaccine""",
Safety evaluation of COVID-19 vaccination during early pregnancy: A single-center prospective cohort study of Chinese pregnant women,"ABSTRACT This prospective cohort study aimed to evaluate the safety of an inactivated coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) vaccine in pregnant women at a tertiary hospital in Hubei, China. Pregnancy outcomes were compared between pregnant vaccinated and unvaccinated women. Composite adverse pregnancy outcomes were defined as one or more of maternal adverse outcomes (prenatal pyrexia, postpartum hemorrhage, maternal intensive care unit admission, and a prethrombotic state) and adverse neonatal outcomes (premature delivery, intrauterine fetal death or induction of labor, fetal macrosomia, fetal growth restriction, small-for-gestational age, fetal abnormalities, neonatal admission to the neonatal intensive care unit, and birth asphyxia). Of a total of 845 participants in the delivery cohort, 41.2% (348/845) received at least one dose of the COVID-19 vaccination, and 33.6% (284/845) received two doses. In total, 25.3% (88/348) of the vaccinated group were vaccinated at 0–4 weeks of pregnancy. No significant difference was found in the composite adverse outcomes between the vaccinated and unvaccinated participants, regardless of whether vaccination occurred before (44/243 [18.1%] vs. 71/497 [14.3%]; P = .17) or during early pregnancy (14/105 [13.3%] vs. 71/497[14.3%]; P = .79). These findings indicate that the pregnancy outcomes of women who received the COVID-19 vaccination, even if they were vaccinated early in pregnancy, were comparable to those of unvaccinated women. This study was registered with the Chinese Clinical Trial Center (ChiCTR2100051756).","Ling Lu, Lu Wang, T. Feng, Xin Du",10.1080/21645515.2023.2226995,https://doi.org/10.1080/21645515.2023.2226995,Human Vaccines & Immunotherapeutics,6,2023,yes,"The study focus is on pregnant women, not exclusively healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports safety outcomes including maternal adverse outcomes and neonatal outcomes.,yes,The study is a prospective cohort study.,yes,The study involves human participants (845 participants in the delivery cohort).,yes,The study includes pregnant women and reports neonatal outcomes.,yes,Participants received the vaccine during early pregnancy (0-4 weeks).,no,"The study evaluates an inactivated COVID-19 vaccine, not a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine or historical smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This is a prospective cohort study of 845 pregnant women evaluating the safety of an inactivated COVID-19 vaccine. While it focuses on a relevant population (pregnant women) and reports safety outcomes, it fails the inclusion criteria because the vaccine studied is for COVID-19 rather than mpox or smallpox.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""evaluate the safety of an inactivated coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) vaccine in pregnant women""",,"  - ""evaluate the safety of an inactivated coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) vaccine in pregnant women""",,"  - ""This prospective cohort study""",,"  - ""845 participants in the delivery cohort,""",,"  - ""evaluate the safety of an inactivated coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) vaccine in pregnant women""
  - ""845 participants in the delivery cohort,""",,"  - ""vaccinated at 0–4 weeks of pregnancy.""
  - ""88/348) of the vaccinated group were vaccinated at 0–4 weeks of pregnancy.""",,"  - ""evaluate the safety of an inactivated coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) vaccine in pregnant women""",
Results of safety monitoring of BNT162b2 (Pfizer-BioNTech) COVID-19 vaccine in U.S. children aged 5-17 years,"Importance: Active monitoring of health outcomes following COVID-19 vaccination offers early detection of rare outcomes that may not be identified in pre-licensure trials. Objective: To conduct near-real time monitoring of health outcomes following BNT162b2 COVID-19 vaccination in the U.S. pediatric population aged 5-17 years. Design: We conducted rapid cycle analysis of 20 pre-specified health outcomes, 13 of which underwent sequential testing and 7 of which were monitored descriptively within a cohort of vaccinated individuals. We tested for increased risk of each health outcome following vaccination compared to a historical baseline, while adjusting for repeated looks at the data as well as claims processing delay. Setting: This is a population-based study in three large commercial claims databases conducted under the U.S. FDA public health surveillance mandate. Participants: The study included over 3 million enrollees aged 5-17 years with BNT162b2 COVID-19 vaccination through mid-2022 in three commercial claims databases. We required continuous enrollment in a medical health insurance plan from the start of an outcome-specific clean window to the COVID-19 vaccination. Exposure: Exposure was defined as receipt of a BNT162b2 COVID-19 vaccine dose. The primary analysis assessed primary series doses together (Dose 1 + Dose 2), and dose-specific secondary analyses were conducted. Follow up time was censored for death, disenrollment, end of risk window, end of study period, or a subsequent vaccine dose. Main Outcome(s) and Measure(s): We monitored 20 pre-specified health outcomes. We performed descriptive monitoring for all outcomes and sequential testing for 13 outcomes. Results: Among 13 health outcomes evaluated by sequential testing, 12 did not meet the threshold for a statistical signal in any of the three databases. In our primary analysis, myocarditis/pericarditis signaled following primary series vaccination with BNT162b2 in ages 12-17 years across all three databases. Conclusions and Relevance: Consistent with published literature, our near-real time monitoring identified a signal for only myocarditis/pericarditis following BNT162b2 COVID-19 vaccination in children aged 12-17 years. This method is intended for early detection of safety signals. Our results are reassuring of the safety of the vaccine, and the potential benefits of vaccination outweigh the risks.","M. Hu, H. Wong, Y. Feng, P. Lloyd, E. R. Smith, K. Amend, A. Kline, D. Beachler, J. Gruber, M. Mitra, J. Seeger, C. Harris, A. Secora, J. Obidi, J. Wang, J. Song, C. McMahill-Walraven, C. Reich, R. McEvoy, R. Do, Y. Chillarige, R. Clifford, D. D. Cooper, A. Shoaibi, R. Forshee, S. Anderson",10.1101/2022.10.28.22281532,https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.10.28.22281532,medRxiv,2,2022,yes,"The study focus is pediatric populations (ages 5-17), not healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study monitors health outcomes including safety signals like myocarditis.,yes,The study is a rapid cycle analysis (cohort study/surveillance) within claims databases.,yes,The study uses data from over 3 million human enrollees.,yes,The study includes children and adolescents aged 5-17 years.,yes,Participants received the vaccine during childhood or adolescence (ages 5-17).,no,"The study specifically evaluates the BNT162b2 (Pfizer-BioNTech) COVID-19 vaccine, not a vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the safety of the BNT162b2 COVID-19 vaccine in a pediatric population aged 5-17 years. While it includes the target age group, it is excluded because it focuses on a COVID-19 vaccine rather than the required vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""To conduct near-real time monitoring of health outcomes following BNT162b2 COVID-19 vaccination in the U.S. pediatric population aged 5-17 years.""",,"  - ""To conduct near-real time monitoring of health outcomes following BNT162b2 COVID-19 vaccination in the U.S. pediatric population aged 5-17 years.""
