Early Experience With an Occupational JYNNEOS (Orthopoxvirus) Vaccination Program,"This study describes outcomes and lessons learned providing JYNNEOS vaccine for laboratory workers exposed to orthopoxviruses such as mpox. The findings are instructive for occupational health professionals seeking to implement an occupational vaccine program for a commercially unavailable vaccine. Objective The aim of the study is to identify lessons learned implementing JYNNEOS vaccination for laboratory workers exposed to orthopoxviruses such as mpox. Methods Workers at risk of laboratory exposure were offered vaccine in a carefully planned occupational health program. Vaccine was procured from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) Drug Service, which has special requirements. Reasons for accepting or declining vaccine and adverse effects were obtained by survey. Results Most workers accepted JYNNEOS, and occupational risk was the most commonly cited reason for acceptance. Most experienced mild local adverse effects. The administrative requirements of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention Drug Service are documented. Conclusions Occupational health programs caring for laboratory workers handling unusual biological agents require careful planning and coordination to facilitate access to vaccines that are not commercially available, anticipate and mitigate barriers to vaccination, and comply with special Centers for Disease Control and Prevention requirements.","M. Swift, Mary-Carmen McDermott, Caitlin Hainy, C. Arendt, Jennifer A. Anderson, Jacqueline J Mahoo, Laura E. Breeher",10.1097/JOM.0000000000002846,https://doi.org/10.1097/JOM.0000000000002846,Journal of Occupational and Environmental Medicine,1,2023,no,"The study focuses on laboratory workers. These are typically healthy non-pregnant adults, and there is no mention of participants who are pregnant, children, or adolescents.",yes,The study reports on adverse effects (safety) obtained by survey.,yes,This is a primary research study describing an occupational vaccination program and using survey data.,yes,The study involves human participants (laboratory workers).,no,"The study population is limited to laboratory workers, and there is no evidence that pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents were included.",no,"The vaccination occurred in an occupational setting for adult workers, not during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,"The study evaluates the JYNNEOS vaccine, which is a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine.",Exclude,"The study describes an occupational vaccination program for laboratory workers using the JYNNEOS vaccine. However, it does not include the target populations of pregnant women, children, or adolescents, and instead focuses on healthy adults in a workplace setting.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""JYNNEOS vaccine for laboratory workers exposed to orthopoxviruses""
