THE EFFECTS OF VACCINATION IN PREGNANCY ON THE FOETUS *,"IN recent years there has been great interest in the role that maternal virus infections in the early months of pregnancy play in abnormal foetal development. Vaccinia should be one of the easiest viruses to study in this connexion, as mass vaccination programmes during smallpox epidemics are frequently carried out, so that the exact time of vaccinia1 infection is known and its effects on the mother can be readily observed. Bellows et al. (1949) found that vaccination of about 700 pregnant women during a smallpox epidemic in 1947 did not affect the abortion, stillbirth, or congenital abnormality rate. During the same epidemic Greenberg et al. (1949) could find no increase in congenital abnormalities among babies born to about 2,000 pregnant women who were vaccinated during the first trimester. This was also the experience of Abramowitz (1957) in South Africa who followed up the babies of 1,121 vaccinated pregnant women. However the latter two studies were retrospective and did not include any women who might have aborted early in pregnancy. Set against these findings are the data of MacArthur (1952) who investigated a series of 67 women vaccinated in the first trimester, after an enquiry into 5,000 vaccinations and found a considerable increase in abortion and stillbirth rate among them. His technique differed from that of the other workers in that he included even women who had very early abortions. Because of these contradictory findings it seemed important to try to obtain further information. In the early months of 1962 there",H. J. Liebeschuetz,10.1111/j.1471-0528.1964.tb04256.x,https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1471-0528.1964.tb04256.x,British Journal of Obstetrics and Gynaecology,10,1964,yes,"The study focuses on pregnant women, which means it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports on clinical outcomes including abortion and stillbirth rates.,yes,"The study describes investigations and retrospective follow-ups of patient series, which constitutes primary research or synthesis of previous series.",yes,The study involves human participants (pregnant women and their babies).,yes,The study specifically includes pregnant women as participants.,yes,The study specifically examines the effects of vaccination administered during pregnancy.,yes,The study involves vaccinia-based vaccines administered during smallpox epidemics.,Include,"This study evaluates the effects of vaccinia (smallpox) vaccination during pregnancy on the fetus, investigating outcomes such as abortion and stillbirth rates. It specifically focuses on a target population (pregnant women) receiving historical smallpox vaccines, meeting all inclusion criteria.",,,5,"  - ""vaccination of about 700 pregnant women""",,"  - ""found a considerable increase in abortion and stillbirth rate among them.""",,"  - ""vaccination of about 700 pregnant women""
