"Long-term clinical, virological and immunological consequences of mpox virus infection or modified vaccinia virus Ankara vaccination: a 24-month prospective cohort study.","Background As mpox virus (MPXV) continues to circulate, a better understanding of the long-term clinical, virological, and immunological consequences of infection and MVA-BN vaccination is needed. Methods In thiscohort study, we prospectively followed MPXV-infected individuals and individuals vaccinated with MVA-BN up to 24 months. Mpox patients were assessed for physical and mental well-being. Saliva and anorectal swabs were tested by MPXV-PCR. Vaccinia virus (VACV) lysate and MPXV-E8 binding and MPXV-neutralising antibodies were analysed over time, taking into account the impact of childhood smallpox vaccination. Results We included 237 MPXV-infected individuals and 210 vaccinees; 93.9% (369/393) were gay or bisexual men who have sex with men, one-third (143/428, 33.4%) lived with HIV, and 31.8% (142/447) were born before or in 1976, (end of smallpox vaccination in Belgium). Scarring occurred in 31.7% (20/63) up to two years post-infection. MPXV was not detected [≥]8 months post-infection. Without childhood smallpox vaccination, MVA-BN vaccinees had significantly lower binding antibody levels than MPXV-infected individuals (0.39-fold, 95%CI 0.27-0.55, p<.001 for VACV-lysate antibodies, and 0.60-fold, 95%CI 0.46-0.79, p<.001 for MPXV-E8 antibodies). MPXV-E8 antibody levels decreased most rapidly over time in smallpox-naive MVA-BN vaccinees (-8%/month, 95%CI -9.09 to -7.42). MPXV-neutralising antibodies were rarely detected in smallpox-naive MVA-BN vaccinees (estimated seroprevalence 3%, 95%CI 1-11%, i.e. 0.04-fold, 95%CI 0.01-0.12, p<.001 lower compared to smallpox-naive MPXV-infected individuals). Discussion Lower antibody levels among MVA-BN vaccinees compared to MPXV-infected individuals suggest a less robust immunological priming induced by vaccination. Studies should assess if booster doses enhance the durability of the immunological memory.","C. V. Dijck, N. Berens-Riha, L. Zaeck, S. Bracke, Jacob Verschueren MSc, J. Coppens, Fien Vanroye MSc, Elisabeth Willems BSc, Evi Bosman BSc, Natalie De Cock BSc, Bart Smekens MSc, L. Vandenhove, O. Goovaerts, Anke Van Hul BSc, MSc Janne Wouters, Cécile Kremer, B. Jacobs, Matilde Hens, I. Brosius, Elise De Vos MSc, Eugene Bangwen Mph, S. Houben, P. H. Lopes, F. MSc, P. Soentjens, E. Bottieau, Chris M. Kenyon, J. Griensven, T. Reyniers, Niels Horst, K. Ariën, M. Esbroeck, A. Torneri, K. Vercauteren, W. Adriaensen, R. D. Vries, J. Mariën, L. Liesenborghs",10.1101/2025.05.21.25327977,https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.05.21.25327977,medRxiv,3,2025,yes,"The study includes individuals living with HIV and those infected with MPXV, meaning it is not limited to healthy individuals.",yes,The study reports on clinical consequences (scarring) and immunological outcomes like binding and neutralizing antibodies.,yes,The study is a prospective cohort study.,yes,The study is a human cohort study involving 447 participants.,no,"The study population consists of gay or bisexual men who have sex with men, many of whom were born before 1976; there is no indication that any participants were children, adolescents, or pregnant women at the time of the study.",no,"While some participants received childhood smallpox vaccination in the past (historical record), the study participants are currently adults, and the MVA-BN vaccination was administered in adulthood.",yes,The study involves the MVA-BN vaccine and historical smallpox vaccination.,Exclude,"This prospective cohort study focuses on adult gay and bisexual men, including those living with HIV and those infected with MPXV. Although it examines the effects of MVA-BN and historical smallpox vaccines, it does not include pregnant women, children, or adolescents as active participants.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""In thiscohort study, we prospectively followed MPXV-infected individuals and individuals vaccinated with MVA-BN up to 24 months.""
