Complement-dependent mpox-virus-neutralizing antibodies in infected and vaccinated individuals,"Mpox virus (MPXV) caused a multi-country outbreak in non-endemic areas in 2022. The Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA)-based vaccine was used as prophylaxis, but its effectiveness remains poorly characterized. Here, we developed two assays for quantification of neutralizing antibodies (NAbs), using MVA-GFP or a recently isolated MPXV. We measured NAb levels in 470 sera from control, MPXV-infected or MVA-vaccinated individuals. Various levels of MVA NAbs were detected after infection, historic smallpox or MVA vaccination. MPXV was barely sensitive to neutralization. Addition of complement enhanced detection of responsive individuals and NAb levels. Anti-MVA and -MPXV NAbs were observed in 94% and 82% of infected individuals, respectively, and 92% and 56% of MVA vaccinees, respectively. NAb titers were higher in individuals born before 1980, highlighting the impact of historic smallpox vaccination on humoral immunity. Altogether, our results indicate that MPXV neutralization is complement-dependent and help uncover the mechanisms underlying vaccine effectiveness.","Mathieu Hubert, F. Guivel-Benhassine, Timothée Bruel, F. Porrot, D. Planas, J. Vanhomwegen, A. Wiedemann, S. Burrel, S. Marot, Romain Palich, G. Monsel, Harouna Diombera, S. Gallien, José-Luis López-Zaragoza, W. Vindrios, F. Taieb, S. Fernandes-Pellerin, Maurine Delhaye, H. Laude, L. Arowas, M. Ungeheuer, L. Hocqueloux, V. Pourcher, T. Prazuck, A. Marcelin, J. Lelièvre, C. Batéjat, Yves Lévy, J. Manuguerra, O. Schwartz",10.1016/j.chom.2023.05.001,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chom.2023.05.001,medRxiv,58,2023,yes,"The study includes MPXV-infected individuals, meaning the population is not limited exclusively to healthy adults.",no,"The study reports immunogenicity (neutralizing antibody levels) rather than clinical effectiveness (prevention of infection, hospitalization, or death) or safety outcomes.",yes,"This is a primary laboratory-based assessment of human sera, which counts as a primary research study.",yes,The study uses human sera from 470 individuals.,no,"The study focuses on adults involved in the 2022 outbreak and individuals born before 1980; it does not include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as participants.",maybe,"While the individuals born before 1980 likely received the historic smallpox vaccine during childhood, the study's current population are adults.",yes,The study evaluates both the MVA-based vaccine and historic smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This study evaluates neutralizing antibody levels in human sera from adults infected with or vaccinated against mpox. It fails several mandatory criteria as it focuses exclusively on adults (e.g., those born before 1980) rather than children or pregnant women, and it reports immunogenicity outcomes rather than clinical safety or effectiveness.","Relevant Outcomes, Target Population",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""We measured NAb levels in 470 sera from control, MPXV-infected or MVA-vaccinated individuals.""",,"  - ""We measured NAb levels in 470 sera from control, MPXV-infected or MVA-vaccinated individuals.""",,"  - ""We measured NAb levels in 470 sera from control, MPXV-infected or MVA-vaccinated individuals.""",,"  - ""We measured NAb levels in 470 sera from control, MPXV-infected or MVA-vaccinated individuals.""",,"  - ""NAb titers were higher in individuals born before 1980, highlighting the impact of historic smallpox vaccination on humoral immunity.""",,"  - ""NAb titers were higher in individuals born before 1980, highlighting the impact of historic smallpox vaccination on humoral immunity.""",,"  - ""The Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA)-based vaccine was used as prophylaxis,""
  - ""NAb titers were higher in individuals born before 1980, highlighting the impact of historic smallpox vaccination on humoral immunity.""",
Cardiac Events Following JYNNEOS Vaccination for Prevention of Monkeypox,"Purpose: The JYNNEOS vaccine is an FDA-approved vaccine indicated for prevention of smallpox and monkeypox that is currently being distributed to high-risk individuals to combat the monkeypox public health emergency. The FDA safety data for JYNNEOS vaccine notes potential cardiac adverse events of special interest (AESI) following vaccination. The Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices (ACIP) stated that people with underlying heart disease or three or more major cardiac risk factors should be counseled about the theoretical risk for myopericarditis following vaccination with JYNNEOS. Our objective was to describe the incidence of cardiac AESIs in the Kaiser Permanente Northwest (KPNW) population who received a JYNNEOS vaccination for prevention of monkeypox. Methods: We conducted a retrospective cohort study of 2,126 KPNW patients aged 12 or older who were vaccinated with at least 1 dose of JYNNEOS vaccine between July 14th 2022 and October 10th 2022. We searched KPNW databases, including the electronic health record (EHR), for diagnoses of elevated troponins, chest pain, myocarditis, pericarditis, myopericarditis, acute myocardial infarction, cardiac dysrhythmia, cardiac arrest, or ventricular fibrillation that were documented during any encounter (visit) using ICD-10-CM diagnosis codes. Two KPNW physicians reviewed all possible cases in the EHR to adjudicate cases as cardiac AESIs in alignment with the case definition used in the FDA safety data. Results: There were 2,126 KPNW members who received a total of 3,235 JYNNEOS vaccine doses between July 14th and October 10th. Encounter ICD10 diagnosis codes and troponin values identified 24 possible cardiac AESI cases. After physician adjudication, there were 10 confirmed cardiac AESI for an incidence of 3.1 per 1000 doses given with (exact 95% CI, 1.5 to 5.7). Of these 10 events, none could be attributed directly to the vaccination. Conclusion: The incidence of cardiac adverse events following JYNNEOS vaccination for prevention of monkeypox is unknown and this is the first study to evaluate cardiac AESI following JYNNEOS vaccination in the 2022 monkeypox public health emergency. This retrospective cohort study of JYNNEOS vaccination for prevention of monkeypox identified 10 cardiac events that all had alternative explanations; and no hospitalizations or serious adverse outcomes were attributed to vaccination. This initial study provides timely information for the clinician counseling their patient with underlying cardiac risk factors on the low observed risk of cardiac events with JYNNEOS vaccination during the 2022 monkeypox public health emergency.","K. Sharff, T. K. Tandy, P. F. Lewis, E. S. Johnson",10.1101/2022.11.13.22282258,https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.11.13.22282258,medRxiv,2,2022,yes,"The study includes patients aged 12 or older, which means it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports on cardiac adverse events of special interest following vaccination.,yes,The study is a retrospective cohort study.,yes,The study involves a retrospective cohort of human patients.,yes,"The study includes individuals aged 12 or older, which encompasses adolescents (<18 years).",yes,"Since adolescents aged 12-17 were included and vaccinated during the study period, participants received the vaccine during adolescence.",yes,"The study evaluates the JYNNEOS vaccine, which is an FDA-approved vaccinia-based mpox vaccine.",Include,This retrospective cohort study evaluated cardiac safety outcomes in a population that included adolescents aged 12 or older who received the JYNNEOS vaccine. It meets all inclusion criteria by reporting safety data for a target population receiving a relevant vaccinia-based vaccine.,,,5,"  - ""We conducted a retrospective cohort study of 2,126 KPNW patients aged 12 or older who were vaccinated with at least 1 dose of JYNNEOS vaccine""",,"  - ""confirmed cardiac AESI for an incidence of 3.1 per 1000 doses given""",,"  - ""We conducted a retrospective cohort study of 2,126 KPNW patients aged 12 or older who were vaccinated with at least 1 dose of JYNNEOS vaccine""",,"  - ""We conducted a retrospective cohort study of 2,126 KPNW patients aged 12 or older who were vaccinated with at least 1 dose of JYNNEOS vaccine""",,"  - ""We conducted a retrospective cohort study of 2,126 KPNW patients aged 12 or older who were vaccinated with at least 1 dose of JYNNEOS vaccine""",,"  - ""We conducted a retrospective cohort study of 2,126 KPNW patients aged 12 or older who were vaccinated with at least 1 dose of JYNNEOS vaccine""
  - ""between July 14th 2022 and October 10th 2022.""",,"  - ""JYNNEOS vaccination for prevention of monkeypox.""",
"Monkey pox (Mpox): pathogenesis, genetic shifts, vaccination strategies and clinical insights","Monkeypox (mpox) is an emerging orthopoxviral disease that has re-emerged globally since 2022, with over 163,000 confirmed cases reported across multiple World Health Organization regions. Mpox has two large clades; Clade I which is mostly related to Central Africa and more severe disease and Clade II which has caused recent multinational outbreaks and is represented by the B.1 lineage. This review is an overview of the existing findings on the pathogenesis of mpox, viral evolution, immune evasion strategies, and the protective efficacy of smallpox and third-generation mpox vaccines based on epidemiological, clinical, immunological, and vaccine-related literature published in leading biomedical databases. Recent evidence suggests that mpox has several immune evasion mechanisms that interrupt innate and cellular immune responses, allowing prolonged human-to-human infection. High risks populations are disproportionately affected by the disease especially men who have sex with men and people living with HIV who become more susceptible, and severity of the disease worsens. Previous smallpox vaccination offers a significant level of cross-protective immunity, with aggregate evidence indicating that it offers protection of about 80 percent and a considerably lower risk of becoming infected. Third-generation vaccines such as MVA-BN/JYNNEOS, are showing good safety profiles, moderate to high efficacy in comparison to older vaccines like the ACAM2000, with other platforms being under clinical development. Knowledge of clade-specific viral evolution, immune resistance and vaccine efficacy is crucial to the optimization of vaccination response, the prioritization of susceptible population, and preparedness to future outbreaks of ortho poxvirus.
© 2026. The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature.","Faiz Ahmad, B. Sah, M. Zandi, L. Krishna, Ankit Kumar, Sachin Kumar Singh, Kamal Dua, Rajesh Kumar, Pritam Mondal",10.1007/s00203-025-04695-4,https://doi.org/10.1007/s00203-025-04695-4,Archives of Microbiology,1,2026,yes,"The study focuses on populations including men who have sex with men and people living with HIV, which are populations distinct from healthy adults.",yes,The paper discusses safety profiles and vaccine efficacy.,no,The paper identifies itself as an 'overview' of existing findings based on literature rather than a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis.,yes,The study review includes epidemiological and clinical literature related to human infection.,no,"The study does not mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; the identified high-risk populations are specifically men who have sex with men and people living with HIV.",no,"There is no information regarding vaccination during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood, as those populations are not addressed.",yes,The study evaluates third-generation vaccines like JYNNEOS and older vaccines like ACAM2000.,Exclude,"This study is a general narrative review ('overview') and does not meet the requirements for being a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis. Furthermore, it does not include the target populations of interest (pregnant women or children), focusing instead on MSM and HIV-positive populations.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""High risks populations are disproportionately affected by the disease especially men who have sex with men and people living with HIV""",,"  - ""Third-generation vaccines such as MVA-BN/JYNNEOS, are showing good safety profiles, moderate to high efficacy in comparison to older vaccines like the ACAM2000,""",,"  - ""This review is an overview of the existing findings on the pathogenesis of mpox, viral evolution, immune evasion strategies, and the protective efficacy of smallpox and third-generation mpox vaccines based on epidemiological, clinical, immunological, and vaccine-related literature""",,"  - ""This review is an overview of the existing findings on the pathogenesis of mpox, viral evolution, immune evasion strategies, and the protective efficacy of smallpox and third-generation mpox vaccines based on epidemiological, clinical, immunological, and vaccine-related literature""",,"  - ""High risks populations are disproportionately affected by the disease especially men who have sex with men and people living with HIV""",,"  - ""This review is an overview of the existing findings on the pathogenesis of mpox, viral evolution, immune evasion strategies, and the protective efficacy of smallpox and third-generation mpox vaccines based on epidemiological, clinical, immunological, and vaccine-related literature""
