Vaccines for Mpox - An Unmet Global Need.,"In 1970, the first case of mpox (formerly known as monkeypox) was documented in an infant in Equateur Province, Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC).1 Infections with clade I monkeypox virus (MPXV) are endemic in the rainforest regions of central Africa and result from both zoonotic and human-to-human transmission. The cessation of smallpox vaccination in 1980 because of the eradication of smallpox has led to an increase in the number of individuals who are orthopox immune naïve and is felt to be responsible for a recent increase in mpox cases in the DRC. Comparisons of active surveillance in Sankuru Province from 2005 through 2007 revealed a 20-fold increase in the incidence of mpox compared with the 1980s, with a 5-fold-lower incidence among those with a smallpox vaccination scar.2.","L. Dodd, H. Lane, J. Muyembe‐Tamfum",10.1056/EVIDe2300348,https://doi.org/10.1056/EVIDe2300348,NEJM Evidence,4,2024,yes,The text references cases in infants and surveillance in regions where vaccines were historically administered.,yes,The paper reports incidence rates and the protective effect of historical vaccination (evidenced by scars) on incidence.,no,"The content appears to be a perspective or editorial piece discussing surveillance data and global needs rather than presenting a primary research study or a systematic review. However, it references surveillance comparisons.",yes,The paper discusses human cases and human surveillance data.,maybe,"The paper mentions an infant case, but does not present new primary data or a systematic review focusing specifically on pregnant women, neonates, or children as participants.",maybe,"While it mentions vaccination scars, it does not specifically evaluate the timing of vaccination during childhood or pregnancy as a study primary focus.",yes,The study mentions smallpox vaccination scars.,Exclude,This paper is a commentary or editorial discussing the global need for vaccines and citing historical surveillance data. It lacks a primary research or systematic review design required for inclusion and does not provide specific data on outcomes for the target populations in a primary study format.,Study Design,Study Design,0.1,"  - ""the first case of mpox (formerly known as monkeypox) was documented in an infant in Equateur Province,""
