Pre-event smallpox vaccination for healthcare workers revisited—the need for a carefully screened multidisciplinary cadre,"Summary As healthcare institutions are a focus of smallpox transmission early in an epidemic, several mathematical models support pre-event smallpox vaccination of healthcare workers (HCWs). The deciding factor for HCW voluntary vaccination is the risk of disease exposure versus the risk of vaccine adverse events. In a United States military population, with careful screening to exclude atopic dermatitis/eczema and immunosuppression, over 1 million vaccinia (smallpox) vaccinations were delivered with one fatality attributed to vaccination. Among 37 901 United States civilian volunteer HCWs vaccinated, 100 serious adverse events were reported including 10 ischemic cardiac episodes and six myocardial infarctions – two were fatal. This older population had a higher rate of adverse events due to age-related coronary artery disease. T-cell mediated inflammatory processes induced by live vaccinia vaccination may have a role in the observed acute coronary artery events. With exclusion of individuals at risk for coronary artery disease, atopic dermatitis/eczema, and immunosuppression, HCWs can be smallpox vaccinated with minimal risk. A carefully screened multidisciplinary cadre (physician, nurse, infection control practitioner, technician), pre-event vaccinated for smallpox, will supply the necessary leadership to alleviate fear and uncertainty while limiting spread and initial mortality of smallpox.",J. D. Malone,10.1016/j.ijid.2006.11.005,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijid.2006.11.005,International Journal of Infectious Diseases,4,2007,no,"The study focuses on military personnel and civilian healthcare workers, which are populations typically comprised of healthy adults.",yes,"The study reports safety outcomes, specifically serious adverse events like myocardial infarctions and ischemic cardiac episodes.",yes,The study reports on findings from large-scale vaccination programs (cohort/surveillance data).,yes,The study describes outcomes in human populations.,no,"The study population is limited to military personnel and healthcare workers, with no mention of pregnant women, neonates, or children. In fact, it describes the group as an ""older population"".",no,The vaccinations occurred in adulthood as part of military or healthcare employment.,yes,The study evaluates smallpox (vaccinia) vaccinations.,Exclude,"This paper focuses exclusively on adult populations (military and healthcare workers) and discusses age-related adverse events such as coronary artery disease. It contains no data on the target populations of interest, such as children, pregnant women, or neonates.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""United States military population,""
