Examining the Patterns of Mpox Vaccine Uptake in a Vulnerable Population,"Background The current Mpox outbreak presents unique vaccination challenges in vulnerable populations. Understanding factors associated with vaccine uptake in vulnerable populations is required for a successful vaccination campaign. Methods This population-based cohort study was conducted in Clalit Health Services and included all individuals eligible for the Modified Vaccinia Ankara vaccine. Cox proportional hazards models were used to assess the characteristics associated with uptake of the first vaccine dose. Results Attendance to a primary healthcare clinic in the Tel Aviv district, repeated sexually transmitted infection screening, and the recent purchase of HIV-PrEP or PDE5 inhibitors were associated with higher vaccine adherence, whereas previous nonadherence with recommended vaccines, low sociodemographic status, and history of HIV were associated with lower adherence. Conclusions These findings highlight the need for proactive patient and healthcare provider–oriented educational campaigns to curb vaccine hesitancy, and may help direct resources toward underserved populations, hence increasing equality in vaccine enrollment.","R. Zucker, Y. Wolff-Sagy, Noga Ramot, Y. Raviv, G. Lavie, D. Netzer, A. Hammerman, R. Arbel",10.1097/OLQ.0000000000001839,https://doi.org/10.1097/OLQ.0000000000001839,Sexually Transmitted Diseases,5,2023,yes,"The study includes individuals with a history of HIV and those using HIV-PrEP, meaning it is not exclusively limited to healthy adults.",no,"The study only reports outcomes related to vaccine 'uptake' and 'adherence'; it does not report safety (adverse events) or effectiveness (prevention of infection, hospitalization, or death).",yes,The study is a population-based cohort study.,yes,This is a human study conducted within the Clalit Health Services system.,no,"The study focuses on vaccine uptake among individuals eligible for the MVA vaccine, with predictors such as PDE5 inhibitors and HIV-PrEP suggesting an adult focus. There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,There is no indication that vaccination occurred during pregnancy or the pediatric period; the study focuses on general uptake in a high-risk adult-oriented cohort.,yes,The study specifically examines the Modified Vaccinia Ankara vaccine.,Exclude,"This population-based cohort study examines factors associated with the uptake of the Modified Vaccinia Ankara vaccine, findings that adherence is related to STIs and HIV history. The paper is excluded because it does not report safety or effectiveness outcomes and does not include the target populations of children, adolescents, or pregnant women.","Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""recent purchase of HIV-PrEP or PDE5 inhibitors were associated with higher vaccine adherence,""
