Implementation of an Mpox Vaccination Program at a Large Sexual Health Clinic in the Bronx—Lessons in Vaccine Equity,"Abstract Mpox caused a global outbreak in 2022. Among 249 people who received mpox vaccination at a sexual health clinic in the Bronx, New York, those with private vs public insurance were more likely to complete the series. No mpox cases were seen during follow-up at a median 121 days (IQR, 97–139).","Usama Irshad, Derek Bishop, C. Mullis, Justin Toro, Barry S. Zingman, Eric A. Meyerowitz",10.1093/ofid/ofad544,https://doi.org/10.1093/ofid/ofad544,Open Forum Infectious Diseases,2,2023,maybe,The study describes a population of 249 people at a sexual health clinic. It does not specify whether this population is exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.,yes,"The study reports on the prevention of mpox infection, noting that no cases were seen during follow-up.",yes,The study is a primary research report (likely a cohort study or case series) describing the implementation and outcomes of a vaccination program.,yes,The study involves human participants vaccinated at a clinic.,maybe,"The abstract does not specify if any of the participants were pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents. It refers generally to '249 people'.",maybe,The abstract does not disclose the age or pregnancy status of participants at the time of vaccination.,yes,"The study involves mpox vaccination during the 2022 outbreak, which used vaccinia-based vaccines.",Include,"This study describes a cohort of 249 individuals receiving mpox vaccination at a clinic and reports on effectiveness outcomes. However, it is unclear if the population included the specific target groups (pregnant women or minors) required for the review.",,,3.6,"  - ""Among 249 people who received mpox vaccination at a sexual health clinic in the Bronx, New York,""",,"  - ""No mpox cases were seen during follow-up at a median 121 days (IQR, 97–139).""",,"  - ""Among 249 people who received mpox vaccination at a sexual health clinic in the Bronx, New York,""",,"  - ""Among 249 people who received mpox vaccination at a sexual health clinic in the Bronx, New York,""",,"  - ""Among 249 people who received mpox vaccination at a sexual health clinic in the Bronx, New York,""",,"  - ""Among 249 people who received mpox vaccination at a sexual health clinic in the Bronx, New York,""",,"  - ""Among 249 people who received mpox vaccination at a sexual health clinic in the Bronx, New York,""",
Induction of poxviral immunity by synthetic MVA-based dual-antigen COVID-19 vaccine COH04S1,"The recent outbreak of monkeypox outside its endemic boundaries has attracted global attention and prompted world leaders to reserve thousands of doses of the only approved third-generation smallpox/monkeypox vaccine Jynneos, which is based on the highly attenuated modified vaccinia Ankara (MVA) vector. Given the potential urgency to vaccinate individuals at risk in both endemic and non-endemic countries to curtail further spread of the outbreak, there is a legitimate need for substantial doses of smallpox/monkeypox vaccines. We previously developed COH04S1, a multiantigen SARS-CoV-2 vaccine candidate built on a synthetic MVA platform. COH04S1 was found to be safe and to induce SARS-CoV-2-specific immune responses in healthy adults participating in a phase 1, blinded, randomized, placebo-controlled clinical trial. Here we perform a retrospective analysis of vaccine-induced orthopoxvirus immunity in a subgroup of volunteers enrolled in the phase 1 clinical trial that were vaccinated with COH04S1 at different doses. At all dose levels, vaccination with COH04S1 resulted in robust MVA-specific binding and neutralizing antibody responses, which were sustained over six months post-vaccination. Similarly, both CD8+ and CD4+ T cells specific for MVA were induced by COH04S1 vaccination and remained durable for at least six months. Given that both arms of the immune response are thought to be involved in protection against orthopoxvirus infections, our results indicate that COH04S1 or other sMVA-based vaccines may represent valuable candidates for smallpox/monkeypox vaccination.","F. Chiuppesi, John A. Zaia, S. Francisco, Minh Ly, Felix Wussow, 3. DonJ., Diamond",10.1101/2022.07.26.22277958,https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.07.26.22277958,medRxiv,1,2022,no,"The study was exclusively limited to ""healthy adults"", failing the requirement to not be exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports that the vaccine was ""found to be safe"" and assesses ""MVA-specific binding and neutralizing antibody responses"", which are immunity markers relevant to orthopoxvirus immunity.",yes,"The study is a retrospective analysis of a ""phase 1, blinded, randomized, placebo-controlled clinical trial"".",yes,The study involves healthy human adults participating in a clinical trial.,no,"The study population consists of ""healthy adults"" and does not include pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Participants received the vaccine as ""healthy adults"" participating in a clinical trial, not during pregnancy, childhood, or the neonatal period.",yes,"The study uses COH04S1, which is ""built on a synthetic MVA platform"".",Exclude,"The study evaluates an MVA-based vaccine candidate in healthy adults, but fails the inclusion criteria as it does not include pregnant women, neonates, or children. Additionally, vaccination occurred during adulthood rather than during the required life stages such as pregnancy or childhood.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""COH04S1 was found to be safe and to induce SARS-CoV-2-specific immune responses in healthy adults participating in a phase 1, blinded, randomized, placebo-controlled clinical trial.""",,"  - ""COH04S1 was found to be safe and to induce SARS-CoV-2-specific immune responses in healthy adults participating in a phase 1, blinded, randomized, placebo-controlled clinical trial.""
  - ""vaccination with COH04S1 resulted in robust MVA-specific binding and neutralizing antibody responses,""",,"  - ""COH04S1 was found to be safe and to induce SARS-CoV-2-specific immune responses in healthy adults participating in a phase 1, blinded, randomized, placebo-controlled clinical trial.""
  - ""retrospective analysis of vaccine-induced orthopoxvirus immunity in a subgroup of volunteers enrolled in the phase 1 clinical trial""",,"  - ""COH04S1 was found to be safe and to induce SARS-CoV-2-specific immune responses in healthy adults participating in a phase 1, blinded, randomized, placebo-controlled clinical trial.""",,"  - ""COH04S1 was found to be safe and to induce SARS-CoV-2-specific immune responses in healthy adults participating in a phase 1, blinded, randomized, placebo-controlled clinical trial.""",,"  - ""COH04S1 was found to be safe and to induce SARS-CoV-2-specific immune responses in healthy adults participating in a phase 1, blinded, randomized, placebo-controlled clinical trial.""",,"  - ""COH04S1, a multiantigen SARS-CoV-2 vaccine candidate built on a synthetic MVA platform.""",
Uptake and safety of hepatitis A vaccination during pregnancy: A Vaccine Safety Datalink study.,"INTRODUCTION
Infection with hepatitis A virus (HAV) during pregnancy, although uncommon, is associated with gestational complications and pre-term labor. Hepatitis A vaccine (HepA) is recommended for anyone at increased risk for contracting hepatitis A, including women at risk who are also pregnant. Limited data are available on the safety of maternal HepA vaccination.
OBJECTIVES
Assess the frequency of maternal HepA receipt and evaluate the potential association between maternal vaccination and pre-specified maternal and infant safety outcomes.
METHODS
A retrospective cohort of pregnancies in the Vaccine Safety Datalink (VSD) resulting in live births from 2004 through 2015 was included. Pregnancies with HepA exposure were compared to those with other vaccine exposures, and to those with no vaccine exposures. Risk factors for contracting hepatitis A were identified up to one-year prior to or during the pregnancy using ICD-9 codes. Maternal and fetal adverse events were evaluated according to maternal HepA exposure status. Adjusted odds ratio (OR) were used to describe the association.
RESULTS
Among 666,233 pregnancies in the study period, HepA was administered at a rate of 1.7 per 1000 (n = 1140), most commonly within the first six weeks of pregnancy. Less than 3% of those exposed to HepA during pregnancy had an ICD-confirmed risk factor. There were no significant associations between HepA exposure during pregnancy and gestational hypertension, gestational diabetes, pre-eclampsia/eclampsia, cesarean delivery, pre-term delivery, and low birthweight. There was a statistically significant association between HepA exposure during pregnancy and small-for-gestational age (SGA) infants (aOR 1.32, [95% CI 1.09, 1.60], p = 0.004).
CONCLUSIONS
The rate of maternal HepA vaccination was low and rarely due to documented risk factors for vaccination. HepA vaccination during pregnancy was not associated with an increased risk for a range of adverse events examined among pregnancies resulting in live births, but an identified association between maternal HepA and SGA infant outcomes, while likely due to unmeasured confounding, warrants further exploration.","H. Groom, N. Smith, Stephanie A. Irving, P. Koppolu, Gabriela Vazquez Benitez, E. Kharbanda, M. Daley, J. Donahue, D. Getahun, L. Jackson, N. Klein, Natalie L. McCarthy, J. Nordin, L. Panagiotakopoulos, A. Naleway",10.1016/j.vaccine.2019.09.043,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2019.09.043,Vaccine,26,2019,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women.,yes,"The study reports on maternal and infant safety outcomes, specifically adverse events.",yes,The study is a retrospective cohort study.,yes,The study is a human study involving pregnant women and infants.,yes,The study includes pregnant women and infants as participants.,yes,Study participants received the vaccine during pregnancy.,no,"The study evaluates the Hepatitis A vaccine (HepA), not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study is a retrospective cohort of pregnant women focused on the safety of the Hepatitis A vaccine (HepA). While it includes target populations like pregnant women and infants, it does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines, failing the intervention criterion.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""A retrospective cohort of pregnancies in the Vaccine Safety Datalink (VSD) resulting in live births from 2004 through 2015 was included.""",,"  - ""Assess the frequency of maternal HepA receipt and evaluate the potential association between maternal vaccination and pre-specified maternal and infant safety outcomes.""
