Safety of COVID-19 Vaccination in Pregnancy: A Systematic Review,"The COVID-19 pandemic has posed significant risks to pregnant women and those recently pregnant, leading to heightened mortality and morbidity rates. Vaccination has emerged as a pivotal strategy in reducing COVID-19-related deaths and illnesses worldwide. However, the initial exclusion of pregnant individuals from most clinical trials raised concerns about vaccine safety in this population, contributing to vaccine hesitancy. This review aims to consolidate the existing literature to assess the safety and efficacy of COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant populations and neonatal outcomes. Diverse studies were included evaluating various aspects of safety for women and their newborns, encompassing mild to severe symptoms across different vaccines. The findings indicate the overall safety and efficacy of COVID-19 vaccination, with minimal adverse outcomes observed, including mild side effects like pain and fever. Although most studies reported the absence of severe adverse outcomes, isolated case reports have raised concerns about potential associations between maternal COVID-19 vaccination and conditions such as fetal supraventricular tachycardia and immune-mediated diseases. Our review underscores the importance of ongoing surveillance and monitoring to ensure vaccine safety in pregnant women. Overall, COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy remains a safe and effective strategy, emphasizing the need for continued research and vigilance to safeguard maternal and fetal health.","A. Gerede, G. Daskalakis, T. Mikos, C. Chatzakis, E. Vavoulidis, M. Eleftheriades, E. Domali, Konstantinos Nikolettos, E. Oikonomou, Panagiotis Antsaklis, Marianna Theodora, Alexandros Psarris, C. Margioula-Siarkou, S. Petousis, S. Stavros, Anastasios Potiris, A. Athanasiadis, K. Dinas, Panagiotis Tsikouras, N. Nikolettos, A. Sotiriadis",10.3390/diagnostics14161775,https://doi.org/10.3390/diagnostics14161775,Diagnostics,5,2024,yes,"The study focuses on pregnant populations and neonatal outcomes, rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study evaluates safety (adverse outcomes) and efficacy of the vaccine.,yes,The study is a systematic review.,yes,"The study evaluates pregnant women and neonates, which are human subjects.",yes,The study specifically includes pregnant women and neonates.,yes,The participants received the vaccination during pregnancy.,no,"The study focuses exclusively on COVID-19 vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This systematic review evaluates the safety and efficacy of COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant women and neonates. However, it does not meet the inclusion criteria because it focuses on COVID-19 vaccines rather than the required vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""assess the safety and efficacy of COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant populations and neonatal outcomes.""",,"  - ""assess the safety and efficacy of COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant populations and neonatal outcomes.""",,,,"  - ""assess the safety and efficacy of COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant populations and neonatal outcomes.""",,"  - ""assess the safety and efficacy of COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant populations and neonatal outcomes.""",,"  - ""COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy remains a safe and effective strategy,""",,"  - ""assess the safety and efficacy of COVID-19 vaccination in pregnant populations and neonatal outcomes.""",
Mpox (Monkeypox) in Pregnancy Updates,"Mpox is a zoonotic viral disease caused by the monkeypox virus, with potential for significant morbidity across all population groups, including pregnant individuals and neonates. Pregnancy represents an immunologically vulnerable state, rendering affected mothers more susceptible to severe disease progression.","Yeshey Dorjey, Deep Kiran Chhetri",10.1002/hsr2.72111,https://doi.org/10.1002/hsr2.72111,Health Science Reports,0,2026,yes,"The title and text address mpox in pregnancy and neonates, indicating the focus is not exclusively on healthy non-pregnant adults.",maybe,"The abstract mentions potential for significant morbidity and disease progression, but it is unclear if empirical safety or effectiveness outcomes are reported in this specific paper without further text.",maybe,"The title 'Updates' suggests a review article, which could be an evidence synthesis, but the text is too brief to confirm if it is primary research or a systematic review.",yes,"The text discusses human populations, specifically pregnant individuals and neonates.",yes,The paper explicitly focuses on pregnant individuals and neonates.,maybe,The provided text does not mention vaccination or the timing thereof.,maybe,The provided text does not mention specific vaccines used or studied.,Include,"This paper provides updates on mpox during pregnancy and for neonates. While it identifies the correct target populations, there is insufficient information in the provided abstract to determine if it reports on vaccination outcomes or meets specific study design criteria.",,,3.6,"  - ""potential for significant morbidity across all population groups, including pregnant individuals and neonates.""",,"  - ""potential for significant morbidity across all population groups, including pregnant individuals and neonates.""",,,,"  - ""potential for significant morbidity across all population groups, including pregnant individuals and neonates.""",,"  - ""potential for significant morbidity across all population groups, including pregnant individuals and neonates.""",,,,,
