Factors associated with vaccination against mpox in people under preexposure prophylaxis against HIV in the Community of Madrid.,"INTRODUCTION
In May 2022, a mpox outbreak was identified in Europe. The majority of those affected are young men with risky sexual relations. In Spain, to control the outbreak, vaccination is recommended, among others, for people on HIV pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP). The objective of the study is to determine the factors associated with vaccination against mpox in people included in PrEP in the Community of Madrid.
METHODS
Descriptive study. The sources of information used are: the PrEP Registry, Health Card, the Vaccine Registry and the Primary Care Clinical History. The 2011 deprivation index of the Spanish Society of Epidemiology is used as a socioeconomic indicator. Factors related to vaccination against mpox are analysed and a conditional logistic regression model is fitted.
RESULTS
6690 people were registered for PrEP. The average age was 37.7 years, 98.8% were men and 25.1% were foreigners. 39.9% had received vaccination against mpox, 47.6% had vaccination against hepatitis A, 32.8% vaccination against hepatitis B, 53.3% vaccination against COVID-19 and 24.3% vaccination against a complaint. The variables associated with mpox vaccination were age, country of birth, influenza vaccination, hepatitis A vaccination, COVID-19 vaccination, and deprivation index.
CONCLUSIONS
Men who had sex with men (MSM) on PrEP treatment, young adults, born in Spain, with a history of vaccination against influenza, hepatitis A and COVID-19 have a greater probability of vaccination against mpox. The proportion of vaccinated subjects decreases as the deprivation index increases (as socioeconomic level decreases).","M. Á. Gutiérrez Rodríguez, Cristina Rodríguez Luque, Amaya Sánchez Gómez, M. D. Lasheras Carbajo, S. Cañellas Llabrés, Beatriz López Centeno, Diego Morales Irala, M. V. Vázquez Torres, Marta Molina Olivas",10.1016/j.eimce.2025.02.003,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eimce.2025.02.003,Enfermedades Infecciosas y Microbiologia Clinica,0,2025,no,"The study population consists exclusively of adults on HIV PrEP with an average age of 37.7 years, which falls under the category of healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study only reports factors associated with the uptake of vaccination, not clinical safety or effectiveness outcomes such as side effects, infection prevention, or hospitalization.",yes,"The study is described as a descriptive study based on registry data, which counts as a primary research study.",yes,The study is a human study based on registry data from the Community of Madrid.,no,"The study focuses on adults on PrEP (98.8% men, average age 37.7) and does not include pregnant women, neonates, or children.",no,"The participants received the vaccine during the 2022 mpox outbreak as adults, not during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,The study involves mpox vaccination campaigns initiated in May 2022.,Exclude,"This study focuses on factors associated with mpox vaccine uptake among adults on HIV PrEP, with a population that is 98.8% male and has an average age of 37.7 years. It fails to include the target populations (pregnant women or children) and does not report relevant safety or effectiveness outcomes, focusing instead on sociodemographic factors of vaccination.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""The average age was 37.7 years, 98.8% were men""",,"  - ""The objective of the study is to determine the factors associated with vaccination against mpox in people included in PrEP""
