Monkeypox in children: what evidence do we have so far?,"Monkeypox virus (MPX) is a double-stranded DNA virus that belongs to the Orthopoxviral genera of the Poxviridae family, which is endemic in Central and West Africa. MPX appeared in 1970, since eradication of smallpox. Apparently, smallpox provides a significant cross-protection (>85%) with monkeypox, but probably most important in individuals with recent vaccination (<10 years). It has been suggested that older population (>60 years), vaccinated with smallpox would be partially protected. Nevertheless, this needs further careful assessment. Animal-to-human transmission (zoonotic infection) can occur through contact with blood, body fluids and skin lesions of infected animals such as arboreal and terrestrial rodents (which include squirrels). Long-term exposure to MPX results in human-to-human transmission through droplet infection, contact with an infected lesion or a contaminated object [1]. As per the recent World Health Organization (WHO) declaration, MPX is now treated as a public health emergency of international concern, affecting over 60 000 cases in more than 95 non-endemic countries worldwide, up to 15 September 2022 [2–10]. MPX has currently three clades [11], clade 1 (formerly ‘Congo Basin clade’ or Central Africa clade), clade 2 (formerly West African clade), and clade 3, the new one that contains most genomes from the 2017, 2018 and 2022 human outbreaks. At the clade 3 is included the lineage B.1, the most prevalent among the sequenced genomes of MPX virus in the 2022 outbreak [12]. The clade 1 has been classically related to be the most pathogenic and lethal, leading case fatalities rates up to 10% [13].","R. Sah, Alaa I. Abdelrahman, S. Sah, A. Abdelaal, A. Siddiq, A. Rodríguez-Morales",10.1093/tropej/fmac076,https://doi.org/10.1093/tropej/fmac076,Journal of Tropical Pediatrics,2,2022,yes,"The paper specifically focuses on children, which is a population other than exclusively healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The paper mentions effectiveness outcomes such as cross-protection against monkeypox.,no,The title 'what evidence do we have so far?' and the introductory content indicate that this is a narrative review or commentary rather than a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis.,yes,The paper discusses monkeypox in human populations.,yes,The study explicitly targets children as participants or subjects of the evidence review.,maybe,"The study mentions protection in individuals with recent vaccination (<10 years), but also highlights that historical smallpox vaccination primarily impacts the older population (>60 years). It is unclear from the provided text if child participants were vaccinated during childhood.",yes,The study discusses the smallpox vaccine and its cross-protection against monkeypox.,Exclude,"The paper is a narrative review or commentary regarding monkeypox in children rather than a primary research study or a systematic review. While it discusses the effectiveness of smallpox vaccination, it does not clearly identify primary data or systematic evidence for children who received the vaccine.",Study Design,Study Design,0.1,,,"  - ""smallpox provides a significant cross-protection (>85%) with monkeypox, but probably most important in individuals with recent vaccination (<10 years).""",,,,,,,,"  - ""smallpox provides a significant cross-protection (>85%) with monkeypox, but probably most important in individuals with recent vaccination (<10 years).""
  - ""It has been suggested that older population (>60 years), vaccinated with smallpox would be partially protected.""",,"  - ""smallpox provides a significant cross-protection (>85%) with monkeypox, but probably most important in individuals with recent vaccination (<10 years).""",
"Risk Factors, Preventive Measures, and Management of Monkeypox: A Systematic Review","Background: Mpox, formerly known as monkeypox, is an emerging viral disease caused by the mpox virus, related to the Orthopoxvirus genus. The 2022–2023 outbreak, driven by clade IIb, alongside ongoing outbreaks of clades Ia and Ib in Africa, highlighted mpox as a significant public health concern. Mpox spreads through close contact with infected individuals or contaminated surfaces, often causing a characteristic rash, fever, and swollen lymph nodes. Vaccination and supportive care are essential in managing mpox, but no specific antiviral treatments have been universally validated.
Aim: This systematic review aims to evaluate risk factors, preventive measures, and management strategies to improve understanding and patient outcomes for mpox.
Methods: Conducted according to PRISMA 2020 guidelines, this review included a comprehensive search across PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science for studies published until October 1, 2024. Eligible studies provided data on clinical features, risk factors, preventive strategies, and treatment outcomes related to mpox. Two independent reviewers conducted the study selection, and data extraction focused on key aspects such as population characteristics, preventive measures, and clinical outcomes.
Results: Ten studies encompassing 2666 participants were included. Findings highlighted a diverse range of mpox clinical presentations, from mild skin lesions to severe symptoms requiring hospitalization, particularly in patients with HIV. Vaccines like JYNNEOS and MVA-BN were found to be effective in reducing symptomatic mpox among high-risk groups, though efficacy varied with timing and analytical approach. Early administration of tecovirimat showed promise in reducing viral shedding, particularly in non-immunocompromised patients. The presence of co-infections, especially among MSM, emphasised the need for integrated healthcare approaches. Public health messaging that was culturally sensitive was crucial to avoid stigmatisation while effectively targeting at-risk groups.
Conclusion: Mpox management benefits from vaccination, early antiviral treatment, and supportive care. Targeted public health interventions, including culturally appropriate communication, are essential to control the spread and improve outcomes. Future research should focus on optimising vaccination strategies, treatment protocols for immunocompromised patients, and understanding long-term sequelae of mpox.","A. Agboola, Ayesha Taqi, M. Alsheikh, Tania M. Cobena Bravo, S. Alzaki, Oluwatobi Atiba, Macleans Anosike, Promise Osorochukwu Odumodu, George Paul Komolafe, Amarachi Valerie Duru, Olubusola Seun Oluwaseun, Omobolaji Samuel Ajayi, A. Y. Oyesile, Eman Alamin, O. Fagbenle",10.9734/jammr/2025/v37i105957,https://doi.org/10.9734/jammr/2025/v37i105957,Journal of Advances in Medicine and Medical Research,1,2025,yes,"The study includes patients with HIV, indicating it is not limited to healthy adults.",yes,The study reports on vaccine effectiveness in reducing symptomatic mpox and mentions outcomes like hospitalization.,yes,The paper is a systematic review conducted according to PRISMA guidelines.,yes,"The review includes human participants, specifically 2,666 individuals.",no,"The study focus is on high-risk groups such as Men who have Sex with Men (MSM) and patients with HIV. There is no mention of including pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"As the study does not include the target populations (pregnant women or children), it does not address vaccination during pregnancy or childhood.",yes,"The study evaluates JYNNEOS and MVA-BN, which are vaccinia-based mpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This systematic review evaluates mpox management and the effectiveness of vaccinia-based vaccines like JYNNEOS. However, it does not meet the population criteria as it focuses on MSM and HIV-positive adults rather than pregnant women, neonates, or children.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""particularly in patients with HIV.""",,"  -