Mpox virus (MPXV) vertical transmission and fetal demise in a pregnant rhesus macaque model,"Infection with clade I Mpox virus (MPXV) results in adverse pregnancy outcomes, yet the potential for vertical transmission resulting in fetal harm with clade IIb MPXV, the clade that is currently circulating in the Western Hemisphere, remains unknown. We established a rhesus macaque model of vertical MPXV transmission with early gestation inoculation. Three pregnant rhesus macaques were inoculated intradermally with 1.5 × 10^5 plaque forming units (PFU) of clade IIb MPXV near gestational day (GD) 30 and animals were monitored for viremia and maternal and fetal well-being. Animals were euthanized to collect tissues at 5, 14, or 25 days post-inoculation (dpi). Tissues were evaluated for viral DNA (vDNA) loads, infectious virus titers, histopathology, MPXV mRNA and protein localization, as well as MPXV protein co-localization with placental cells including, Hofbauer cells, mesenchymal stromal cells, endothelial cells, and trophoblasts. vDNA was detected in maternal blood and skin lesions by 5 dpi. Lack of fetal heartbeat was observed at 14 or 25 dpi for two dams indicating fetal demise; the third dam developed significant vaginal bleeding at 5 dpi and was deemed an impending miscarriage. vDNA was detected in placental and fetal tissue in both fetal demise cases. MPXV localized to placental villi by ISH and IHC. Clade IIb MPXV infection in pregnant rhesus macaques results in vertical transmission to the fetus and adverse pregnancy outcomes, like clade I MPXV. Further studies are needed to determine whether antiviral therapy with tecovirimat will prevent vertical transmission and improve pregnancy outcomes. One Sentence Summary Clade IIb Mpox virus infection of pregnant rhesus macaques results in vertical transmission from mother to fetus and adverse pregnancy outcomes.","Nicholas P. Krabbe, A. Mitzey, Saswati Bhattacharya, E. Razo, Xiankun Zeng, Nell Bekiares, Amy Moy, Amy Kamholz, J. Karl, Gregory Daggett, Grace VanSleet, Terry K. Morgan, S. Capuano, H. Simmons, Puja Basu, A. Weiler, David H. O’Connor, T. Friedrich, T. Golos, E. Mohr",10.1101/2024.05.29.596240,https://doi.org/10.1101/2024.05.29.596240,bioRxiv,6,2024,yes,"The study focuses on pregnancy and fetal demise, and is therefore not limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on adverse pregnancy outcomes, including fetal demise and miscarriage.",yes,This is a primary research study involving an experimental animal model.,no,"The study is conducted in a rhesus macaque model, not in humans.",no,"The participants are rhesus macaques, not pregnant women or human children.",no,The study does not involve vaccination; it involves viral inoculation to study infection and transmission.,no,"The study evaluates MPXV infection and suggests future research on the antiviral tecovirimat, but does not involve vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This is an animal model study using pregnant rhesus macaques to study vertical transmission of the Mpox virus. It fails the inclusion criteria because it is not a human study, does not include the human target population, and does not evaluate any vaccines.","Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Study Type,0.1,"  - ""established a rhesus macaque model of vertical MPXV transmission with early gestation inoculation.""",,"  - ""Three pregnant rhesus macaques were inoculated intradermally""
