EP32.02: Effectiveness and perinatal outcomes of COVID‐19 vaccination in pregnancy: systematic review and meta‐analysis,"Objectives: To synthesise evidence on vaccine effectiveness and perinatal outcomes after COVID-19 vaccination in pregnancy. Methods: The COVID-19 Research, MEDLINE and EMBASE were searched accordance PRISMA guidelines from to Additional hand searches of preprint servers and regulatory websites were performed. Studies reporting perinatal outcomes or vaccine effectiveness after COVID-19 vaccination in pregnancy were included. Pair-wise meta-analyses were undertaken. Results: There were 23 reports of 117,552 COVID-19 vaccinated women, almost exclusively with mRNA vaccines and all from high-income countries. The effectiveness of mRNA vaccination against RT-PCR conﬁrmed SARS-CoV-2 infection 7days after the second dose was 89.5% (95%CI 69.0–96.4%, 3 observational studies, 18,828 vaccinated pregnant women, I 2 = 73.9%). The odds of stillbirth were signiﬁcantly reduced in the vaccinated cohort by 15% (7 studies, pooled OR 0.85; 95%CI 0.73–0.99, 66,067 vaccinated versus 424,624 unvaccinated, p = 0.035, I 2 = 93.9%). The cumulative effect shows a non-statistically signiﬁcant 10% reduction in preterm birth before 37weeks of gestation following COVID-19 vaccination in studies accounting for time-varying confounding, (2 studies, pooled HR 0.90; 95%CI 0.81–1.00, 10,197 vaccinated versus 36,414 unvaccinated, I 2 = 11.1%, p = 0.051). Using pair-wise meta-analysis of outcomes among vaccinated (versus unvaccinated) pregnant women, there was no evidence of an increase in the risk of following outcomes: miscarriage, pre-eclampsia, placental abrup- tion, pulmonary embolism, postpartum hemorrhage, intensive care unit admission, maternal death, birthweight Z-score, or neonatal intensive care unit admission. Conclusions: COVID-19 mRNA vaccination in pregnancy appears to be effective in preventing proven SARS-CoV-2 infection in pregnancy. These mRNA vaccines are safe in pregnancy and associated with a signiﬁcant reduction in stillbirth. A nulliparous lady with good past health and uneventful antenatal course, was found to have fetal microcephaly, small cerebellum and unilateral right ventriculomegaly on morphology ultrasound at 20th gestational week. Fetal MRI brain performed at 21st gestational week revealed brain damage with microcephaly, abnormal hyperintense T2 signal at the right cerebral hemisphere, involving perisylvian, frontoparietal and superior temporal lobes. Corresponding high-signal on diffusion weighted imaging (DWI) and low-signal on apparent diffusion coefﬁcient (ADC) map suggested restricted diffusion. Associated hyperintense T1 signal indicated intracerebral hemorrhagic element. The abnormal signal intensity conformed to the right middle cerebral artery vascular territory. Unilateral right ventriculomegaly, porencephaly without intraventricular hemorrhage and hypoplastic cerebellum were observed. PCR of amniotic ﬂuid conﬁrmed fetal cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection. Maternal serology suggested non primary CMV infection. Patient opted for medical termination of pregnancy after counselling on diagnosis, imaging evidence of central nervous system anomalies and risk of neurodevelopmental compromise. To our knowledge, this is the ﬁrst reported case of congenital CMV infection with MRI DWI fetal brain demonstrating cerebral abnormality conforming cerebrovascular territory. Beyond direct neuronal damage, MRI ﬁndings support the hypothesis of CMV-induced vasculitis of the central nervous system, triggering thrombotic hemorrhagic insult. MRI helped elucidate other neurological malformation with similar sonographic appearance. USG and MRI have complementary roles in aiding diagnosis, comprehensive assessment of extent of CNS involvement and prognostication in fetal CMV infection. Objectives: The SARS-CoV2 virus spread rapidly worldwide, causing a pandemic with several implications in mental and physical health. Previous studies have reported adverse mental health outcomes in pregnant women such as anxiety, depression, insomnia, and posttraumatic stress disorder during the COVID-19 pandemic worldwide. However, few studies have reported exploring mental health outcomes of pregnant women in low and upper middle-income countries in Latin America. Therefore, the aim of this study was to evaluate the prevalence of mental health problems and their possible association with adverse pregnancy outcomes in pregnant women in Colombia.","S. Prasad, Erkan Kalafat, H. Blakeway, R. Townsend, P. O’Brien, E. Morris, T. Draycott, S. Thangaratinam, K. Le Doare, S. Ladhani, P. von Dadelszen, L. Magee, P. Heath, A. Khalil",10.1002/uog.25690,https://doi.org/10.1002/uog.25690,Ultrasound in Obstetrics and Gynecology,0,2022,yes,The study focuses specifically on pregnant women rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.,yes,The study reports both effectiveness against infection and safety outcomes like miscarriage and NICU admission.,yes,The study is a systematic review and meta-analysis.,yes,"The study involves a synthesis of human clinical data from over 100,000 women.",yes,The study participants are pregnant women.,yes,The participants received the vaccination during pregnancy.,no,"The study specifically evaluates COVID-19 mRNA vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This systematic review evaluates the effectiveness and safety of COVID-19 mRNA vaccines in pregnant women. While it meets population and design criteria, it is excluded because it does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""To synthesise evidence on vaccine effectiveness and perinatal outcomes after COVID-19 vaccination in pregnancy.""",,"  - ""The effectiveness of mRNA vaccination against RT-PCR conﬁrmed SARS-CoV-2 infection""
