"Comparison of the immunogenicity and protective efficacy of ACAM2000, MVA, and vectored subunit vaccines for Mpox in rhesus macaques","The 2022–2023 mpox outbreak triggered vaccination efforts using smallpox vaccines that were approved for mpox, including modified vaccinia Ankara (MVA; JYNNEOS), which is a safer alternative to live replicating vaccinia virus (ACAM2000). Here, we compare the immunogenicity and protective efficacy of JYNNEOS by the subcutaneous or intradermal routes, ACAM2000 by the percutaneous route, and subunit Ad35 vector–based L1R/B5R or L1R/B5R/A27L/A33R vaccines by the intramuscular route in rhesus macaques. All vaccines provided robust protection against high-dose intravenous mpox virus challenge with the current outbreak strain, with ACAM2000 providing near complete protection and JYNNEOS and Ad35 vaccines providing robust but incomplete protection. Protection correlated with neutralizing antibody responses as well as L1R/M1R- and B5R/B6R-specific binding antibody responses, although additional immune responses likely also contributed to protection. This study demonstrates the protective efficacy of multiple vaccine platforms against mpox virus challenge, including both current clinical vaccines and vectored subunit vaccines. MVA and multivalent Ad35-based vaccines induced robust protection against Mpox challenge in nonhuman primates, whereas ACAM200 induced near complete protection. Editor’s summary The global outbreak of mpox virus in 2022–2023 highlighted the need to have broadly protective poxvirus vaccines. Smallpox vaccines such as ACAM2000 and JYNNEOS offered protection to vaccinated individuals and helped to end the outbreak, but other platforms merit further investigation. Here, Jacob-Dolan et al. performed a head-to-head comparison of four different poxvirus vaccines: ACAM2000, JYNNEOS, and two adenovirus 35–vectored subunit vaccines encoding two or four vaccinia virus antigens. The authors vaccinated nonhuman primates, including with two different routes for the JYNNEOS vaccine, as used in human vaccination campaigns, and asked how well each vaccine protected against a subsequent intravenous challenge with mpox virus. All vaccines and routes elicited immune responses and conferred at least partial protection against infection. Differences between the vaccines allowed the authors to interrogate correlates of protection, which included neutralizing antibody titers and immune responses specific to the L1R and B5R proteins of mpox virus. Together, these data support further development of subunit mpox vaccines and offer a strategy to benchmark future vaccine candidates. —Courtney Malo","C. Jacob-Dolan, Darren M. Ty, David L. Hope, K. McMahan, Jinyan Liu, O. Powers, Catherine A. Cotter, Michela Sciacca, Cindy Wu, E. Borducchi, Emily Bouffard, Hannah Richter, Jason Velasco, Elyse Teow, Mona Boursiquot, Anthony Cook, Karen Feliciano, J. Yalley-Ogunro, Michael S. Seaman, Laurent Pessiant, Mark G. Lewis, H. Andersen, Bernard Moss, D. Barouch",10.1126/scitranslmed.adl4317,https://doi.org/10.1126/scitranslmed.adl4317,Science Translational Medicine,31,2024,no,"The study is conducted in rhesus macaques rather than humans. Therefore, it does not provide data concerning any human populations.",yes,The study reports on protective efficacy against mpox virus challenge.,yes,This is a primary research study involving an experimental animal model.,no,The study is an animal (laboratory) study using nonhuman primates.,no,The study does not include human participants; it is limited to rhesus macaques.,no,"The study involves vaccination of macaques, not humans during pregnancy or childhood.",yes,The study evaluates ACAM2000 and MVA (JYNNEOS) vaccines.,Exclude,"This is an animal study evaluating vaccine efficacy in rhesus macaques. It fails several required criteria, most notably the requirement to be a human study and the inclusion of target populations such as children or pregnant women.","Population Focus, Study 