Mpox: What Practicing Gastroenterologists Need to Know,"The World Health Organization declared human monkeypox virus (HMPXV) infection, or Mpox, a global health emergency on July 23, 2022.1World Health OrganizationMonkeypox.https://www.who.int/emergencies/situations/monkeypox-oubreak-2022Date accessed: November 21, 2022Google Scholar, 2Centers for Disease Control and PreventionMonkeypox in the U.S.https://www.cdc.gov/poxvirus/monkeypox/clinicians/infection-control-healthcare.html#anchor_1653508869481Date accessed: November 21, 2022Google Scholar, 3Centers for Disease Control and PreventionMonkeypox.https://www.cdc.gov/poxvirus/monkeypox/about.htmlDate accessed: November 21, 2022Google Scholar Although new case counts are declining, early diagnosis remains essential. This commentary provides a brief overview of Mpox infection and reviews the presentations of infection because gastroenterologists are uniquely positioned to detect Mpox during routine examination of the perianal region and during endoscopic evaluation. HMPXV is an enveloped double-stranded DNA virus. It is a member of the Orthopoxvirus genus of the Poxviridae family, the same family as Variola (smallpox) virus. Several rodents and small mammals are the suspected natural reservoirs of this zoonotic virus. HMPXV, first described in humans in 1970, is considered endemic in central and western Africa.4Ladnyj I.D. Ziegler P. Kima E. A human infection caused by monkeypox virus in Basankusu Territory, Democratic Republic of the Congo.Bull World Health Organ. 1972; 46: 593-597PubMed Google Scholar There are 2 circulating HMPXV clades (genetic groups). Clade 2 is associated with milder disease and fewer deaths. Before 2022, almost all nonendemic HMPXV cases were linked to international travel to endemic countries or through imported animals. In contrast, most HMPXV cases associated with the 2022 outbreak occurred by local transmission in the United States and other nonendemic regions. Transmission occurs through close or direct contact with skin lesions or bodily fluids, through contaminated fomites (porous surfaces like clothes and bedding, or surfaces that have been in contact with fluid from lesions), and possibly through large respiratory droplets. There is also evidence of vertical transmission.2Centers for Disease Control and PreventionMonkeypox in the U.S.https://www.cdc.gov/poxvirus/monkeypox/clinicians/infection-control-healthcare.html#anchor_1653508869481Date accessed: November 21, 2022Google Scholar During the 2022 outbreak, most new cases occurred in gay, bisexual, and other men who have sex with men. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) identified these groups as most likely to benefit from enhanced vaccination efforts.3Centers for Disease Control and PreventionMonkeypox.https://www.cdc.gov/poxvirus/monkeypox/about.htmlDate accessed: November 21, 2022Google Scholar Although vaccination against smallpox may be protective, segments of the population younger than ages 40–50 years may be more susceptible to Mpox because of the cessation of smallpox vaccination campaigns by the mid-1970s. Because of the risk of waning vaccination protection, the CDC recommends repeat vaccination for at-risk individuals whose last vaccine was more than 3 years ago.3Centers for Disease Control and PreventionMonkeypox.https://www.cdc.gov/poxvirus/monkeypox/about.htmlDate accessed: November 21, 2022Google Scholar Classically, infected persons present with prodromal symptoms (fever, headache, myalgia, lymphadenopathy, upper respiratory symptoms) followed by a characteristic rash that starts in the mouth and then spreads to the face and extremities, including the palms and soles. Lesions, ranging from tens to hundreds, begin as macules and progress to papules, vesicles, and pustules, eventually scabbing over and falling off with new skin underneath. During the current outbreak, the presentation of Mpox has differed from the classic presentation. For example, skin lesions may be limited to only the oral, genital, perigenital, and perianal areas and may present as a single lesion or ulcer; different stages of development may be present side by side; or individuals may present with only mild or absent prodromal symptoms that may begin after the onset of the skin lesion(s). Based on a systematic review of 46 articles, describing 1984 confirmed cases (48 from case reports and 1937 from observational cohort studies), anogenital lesions were the most observed skin manifestations in up to 66% of cases.5Pourriyahi H. Aryanian Z. Afshar Z.M. Goodarzi A. A systematic review and clinical atlas on mucocutaneous presentations of monkeypox: with a comprehensive approach to all aspects of the new and previous monkeypox outbreaks [published online ahead of print October 17, 2022].J Med Virol. 2022; https://doi.org/10.1002/jmv.28230Crossref PubMed Scopus (5) Google Scholar The lesions were vesiculopustular, vesicular-umbilicated, or pseudopustular lesions with less than 10 lesions per patient. Almost all patients reported systemic manifestations, including fever, lymphadenopathy, fatigue, myalgia, and headaches.5Pourriyahi H. Aryanian Z. Afshar Z.M. Goodarzi A. A systematic review and clinical atlas on mucocutaneous presentations of monkeypox: with a comprehensive approach to all aspects of the new and previous monkeypox outbreaks [published online ahead of print October 17, 2022].J Med Virol. 2022; https://doi.org/10.1002/jmv.28230Crossref PubMed Scopus (5) Google Scholar In ∼20% cases, pharyngitis or dysphagia was reported. The incidence of proctitis and/or proctalgia ranged from 22% to 37% in different case series.6Catala A. Clavo-Escribano P. Riera-Monroig J. et al.Monkeypox outbreak in Spain: clinical and epidemiological findings in a prospective cross-sectional study of 185 cases.Br J Dermatol. 2022; 187: 765-772Crossref PubMed Scopus (91) Google Scholar, 7Gedela K. Da Silva Fontoura D. Salam A. et al.Infectious proctitis due to human monkeypox [published online ahead of print September 2, 2022].Clin Infect Dis. 2022; (ciac713)Google Scholar, 8Tarin-Vicente E.J. Alemany A. Agud-Dios M. et al.Clinical presentation and virological assessment of confirmed human monkeypox virus cases in Spain: a prospective observational cohort study.Lancet. 2022; 400: 661-669Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (194) Google Scholar, 9Yakubovsky M. Shasha D. Reich S. et al.Monkeypox presenting as proctitis in men who have sex with men [published online ahead of print September 6, 2022].Clin Infect Dis. 2022; (ciac737)Google Scholar There is documentation of ulcers in the rectum and oropharynx, based on at least 1 publication.10Thornhill J.P. Barkati S. Walmsley S. et al.Monkeypox virus infection in humans across 16 countries—April-June 2022.N Engl J Med. 2022; 387: 679-691Crossref PubMed Scopus (629) Google Scholar In 1 study of 70 men who have sex with men, 26 (37.1%) had signs and symptoms of proctitis with anal pain as the most common presenting symptom.9Yakubovsky M. Shasha D. Reich S. et al.Monkeypox presenting as proctitis in men who have sex with men [published online ahead of print September 6, 2022].Clin Infect Dis. 2022; (ciac737)Google Scholar In 11 patients (42.3%), proctitis preceded the skin rash, appearing a median of 3 days (range, 1–7) before the rash. In 6 patients (23%) proctitis was the only clinical manifestation. Three patients with proctitis (11.5%) were found to have a protruding anal mass (positive polymerase chain reaction [PCR] test on rectal swab) accompanied by excruciating pain. Another 3 patients complained of having a feeling of a new anal mass, but no mass was found on inspection. One patient with proctitis had a pelvic computed tomography performed that demonstrated diffuse rectal wall thickening surrounded by infiltrated fat with associated lymphadenopathy. The cause of proctitis was believed to be direct inoculation of the virus into the rectal mucosa. All patients were sexually active men who have sex with men. More than one-fourth were HIV positive. The CDC currently recommends that individuals isolate at home for the duration of their illness, which can be up to 4 weeks.3Centers for Disease Control and PreventionMonkeypox.https://www.cdc.gov/poxvirus/monkeypox/about.htmlDate accessed: November 21, 2022Google Scholar Patients are infectious from the time prodromal symptoms start until the lesions scab and fall off, with a new layer of skin formation underneath. The extent to which asymptomatic infection may occur is unknown. To date, positive PCRs have been detected from rectal swabs (including in asymptomatic persons), stool, saliva, and semen in addition to the lesions themselves (including scabs). Detection of viral DNA is variably associated with infectious virus.11De Baetselier I. Van Dijck C. Kenyon C. et al.Retrospective detection of asymptomatic monkeypox virus infections among male sexual health clinic attendees in Belgium.Nat Med. 2022; 28: 2288-2292Crossref PubMed Scopus (83) Google Scholar,12Peiró-Mestres A. Fuertes I. Camprubí-Ferrer D. et al.Frequent detection of monkeypox virus DNA in saliva, semen, and other clinical samples from 12 patients, Barcelona, Spain, May to June 2022.Euro Surveill. 2022; 27 (2200503)Crossref PubMed Scopus (138) Google Scholar The US Food and Drug Administration has advised that HMPXV may be transmitted through fecal microbiota transplantation products (although the risk of such transmission is unknown) and that additional protections were needed for the investigational or clinical use of fecal microbiota transplantation (such as updated donor-screening processes and development of an efficacious and reliable test to detect HMPXV viral DNA in fecal matter).13Owais R. Iqbal M. Monkeypox and fecal microbiota for transplantation (FMT): an unprecedented risk?.Ann Med Surg (Lond). 2022; 82104779PubMed Google Scholar In a patient with suspected active Mpox infection, a diagnosis can be made by obtaining a specimen from a skin lesion. Certain laboratories can","S. Sultan, J. Zucker, D. Weinberg",10.1053/j.gastro.2022.12.008,https://doi.org/10.1053/j.gastro.2022.12.008,Gastroenterology,0,2022,maybe,"The article is a clinical commentary and does not report on a specific study population, although it mentions vertical transmission.",no,The paper does not report original data on safety or effectiveness outcomes of vaccination; it is a clinical overview.,no,The paper identifies itself as a 'commentary' rather than primary research or a systematic review. It refers to another systematic review rather than conducting its own protocol.,yes,The paper discusses human Mpox infection and clinical presentation.,no,"The paper does not include a study population of pregnant women, neonates, or children; it is a narrative commentary for practitioners. While it mentions vertical transmission, it provides no data on these groups.",no,"The paper does not include participants who received vaccines during the specified life stages (pregnancy, childhood, etc.).",yes,The paper mentions the status of smallpox vaccination.,Exclude,"This article is a clinical commentary aimed at gastroenterologists and does not contain primary research or an evidence synthesis design. It does not include or provide data on the target populations of pregnant women or children, failing several mandatory criteria.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""There is also evidence of vertical transmission.2Centers""",,"  - ""This commentary provides a brief overview of Mpox infection and reviews the presentations of infection because gastroenterologists are uniquely positioned to detect Mpox during routine examination""",,"  - ""This commentary provides a brief overview of Mpox infection and reviews the presentations of infection because gastroenterologists are uniquely positioned to detect Mpox during routine examination""
