"Safety, Immunogenicity and Efficacy of NVX-CoV2373 in Adolescents in PREVENT-19: A Randomized, Phase 3 Trial","BACKGROUND Over 20% of cases and 0.4% of deaths from Covid-19 occur in children. Following demonstration of safety and efficacy of the adjuvanted, recombinant spike protein vaccine NVX-CoV2373 in adults, the PREVENT-19 trial enrolled adolescents. METHODS Safety, immunogenicity, and efficacy of NVX-CoV2373 were evaluated in adolescents aged 12 to <18 years in an expansion of PREVENT-19, a phase 3, randomized, observer-blinded, placebo-controlled trial in the United States. Participants were randomized 2:1 to two doses of NVX-CoV2373 or placebo 21 days apart, and followed for a median of 2 months after second vaccination. Primary end points were serologic non-inferiority of neutralizing antibody (NA) responses compared with young adults (18 to <26 years) in PREVENT-19, protective efficacy against laboratory-confirmed Covid-19, and assessment of reactogenicity/safety. RESULTS Among 2,247 participants randomized between April-June 2021, 1,491 were allocated to NVX-CoV2373 and 756 to placebo. Post-vaccination, the ratio of NA geometric mean titers in adolescents compared to young adults was 1.5 (95% confidence interval [CI] 1.3 to 1.7). Twenty Covid-19 cases (all mild) occurred: 6 among NVX-CoV2373 and 14 among placebo recipients (vaccine efficacy [VE]: 79.5%, 95% CI, 46.8 to 92.1). All sequenced viral genomes (11/20) were identified as Delta variant (Delta variant VE: 82.0% [95% CI: 32.4 to 95.2]). Reactogenicity was largely mild-to-moderate, transient, and more frequent in NVX-CoV2373 recipients and after the second dose. Serious adverse events were rare and evenly distributed between treatments. CONCLUSIONS NVX-CoV2373 was safe, immunogenic, and efficacious in the prevention of Covid-19 and those cases caused by the Delta variant in adolescents. (Funded by the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Preparedness and Response, Biomedical Advanced Research and Development Authority and the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID), National Institutes of Health; PREVENT-19 ClinicalTrials.gov number, NCT04611802).","G. Áñez, L. Dunkle, C. Gay, K. Kotloff, J. Adelglass, B. Essink, J. Campbell, S. Cloney-Clark, M. Zhu, J. Plested, Pavitra Roychoudhury, A. Greninger, N. Patel, A. McGarry, W. Woo, I. Cho, G. Glenn, F. Dubovsky",10.1101/2022.09.20.22279903,https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.09.20.22279903,medRxiv,6,2022,yes,The study focuses specifically on adolescents aged 12 to <18 years.,no,"While the study reports safety and effectiveness outcomes, they are for the prevention of COVID-19, not mpox.",yes,This is a phase 3 randomized controlled trial.,yes,The study was conducted in human participants (adolescents).,yes,The study includes participants under 18 years of age (12 to <18).,yes,Study participants received the vaccine during adolescence.,no,"The study involves a recombinant spike protein vaccine (NVX-CoV2373) for COVID-19, not a vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"This study is a randomized trial of the NVX-CoV2373 COVID-19 vaccine in adolescents. It does not evaluate mpox vaccines, nor does it report outcomes related to mpox infection, and therefore fails critical inclusion criteria for vaccine type and relevant outcomes.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Safety, immunogenicity, and efficacy of NVX-CoV2373 were evaluated in adolescents aged 12 to <18 years""",,"  - ""protective efficacy against laboratory-confirmed Covid-19,""",,"  - ""phase 3, randomized, observer-blinded, placebo-controlled trial""",,"  - ""Safety, immunogenicity, and efficacy of NVX-CoV2373 were evaluated in adolescents aged 12 to <18 years""",,"  - ""Safety, immunogenicity, and efficacy of NVX-CoV2373 were evaluated in adolescents aged 12 to <18 years""",,"  - ""Safety, immunogenicity, and efficacy of NVX-CoV2373 were evaluated in adolescents aged 12 to <18 years""",,"  - ""recombinant spike protein vaccine NVX-CoV2373 in adults, the PREVENT-19 trial enrolled adolescents.""",
Smallpox: the old and the new,"Current concerns about bioterrorism have catapulted into the limelight variola, a virus long thought to be extinct. Extensive work has adapted the classic vaccinia strain into cell culture allowing 250 million doses to be prepared for the USA ‘one dose for every citizen’. Immunisation using vaccinia is not as well tolerated as are modern liveattenuated vaccines such as MMR. Furthermore, contemporary clinical studies are more comprehensive and demanding than those conducted in the past, so it is not surprising that vaccinia vaccination has been associated with a previously unrecognised side effect. Probable or suspected myocarditis was reported [1,2] in 22/38 257 (0.06%) civilian and 63/515 000 (0.01%) military vaccinees. Although uncommon, this side effect is clinically serious and greatly stimulated the refusnic attitude among health care workers and emergency staff scheduled to receive vaccine. The programme of immunising ‘first responders’ is thus in abeyance. Irrespective of side effects, there has been a robust debate about just how much protection is provided by vaccinia vaccination. Reviews in Medical Virology has informed this discussion by reprinting archival reports, together with contemporary commentary, under the banner of ‘Classic Papers’. Thus, Baxby [3] reviewed the 1913 paper by Hanna showing that inoculation of a single dose of vaccinia as a neonate provided substantial protection against death from smallpox (although not necessarily against smallpox itself) when variola virus was subsequently introduced into a community in Liverpool. Likewise Mortimer [4] reviewed the evidence from McVail that receipt of two lifetime vaccinations with vaccinia provided extensive protection against smallpox during an outbreak in Glasgow in 1900–1902. Such reports were often written in the style of the day, more as political polemics than as dispassionate objective recordings of epidemiological data. However, these two Classic Papers show convincingly that vaccination with vaccinia provides substantial protection against death and (especially with re-vaccination) against smallpox. In the current issue of Reviews in Medical Virology we extend coverage of the natural history of smallpox by reprinting the first paper to evaluate the community wide population dynamics of this virus [5]. Remarkably, this paper dates from 1766 and is the first to use mathematical modelling to attempt to understand transmission dynamics. Blower reviews the background and understanding of disease transmission in the 18th century and illustrates why this paper was ahead of its time and so truly is a classic. Moving forward three centuries, this issue of Reviews in Medical Virology also includes a summary of the antiviral compounds which have activity against poxviruses in vitro. Among the list of chemical moieties reviewed by De Clercq and Neyts [6] are compounds which undoubtedly could protect communities against a bioterrorist outrage; but which ones should be prioritised for clinical development? The authors emphasise that clinical evaluation of efficacy is limited because smallpox is no longer a current problem, and the related monkeypox appears only intermittently and in parts of the globe where systematic studies are difficult. The leading contender compound must be cidofovir because there is clinical evidence of its use in humans with CMV infection and corresponding evidence of how to manage renal toxicity. Cidofovir has been shown to have activity against variola in an animal model, including animals where therapy was delayed until the first signs of smallpox [7]. Cidofovir also has activity against monkeypox [7], so, taken together, these results may well lead to licensure of cidofovir under the Food and Drug Administration’s ‘animal rule’ which posits that control of related animal viruses will be accepted as evidence of efficacy if human data cannot be obtained. However, cidofovir has to be given by iv infusion and the current schedule of once weekly induction followed by alternate week maintenance is dictated E D I T O R I A L",P. Griffiths,10.1002/rmv.447,https://doi.org/10.1002/rmv.447,Reviews in Medical Virology,3,2004,yes,The paper discusses outcomes in neonates and general populations based on historical records.,yes,The paper reports on protection against death from smallpox.,maybe,"The paper is an editorial summarizing/reviewing other historical papers and contemporary data. While it functions like a review/synthesis of classic evidence, it does not clearly identify as a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis in the formal sense.",yes,The paper discusses human vaccination data and historical human outbreaks.,yes,The study mentions vaccination of neonates.,yes,The study explicitly references vaccination during the neonatal period.,yes,The study evaluates vaccinia vaccination.,Include,"This editorial synthesizes historical data and classic papers regarding vaccinia vaccination, specifically mentioning neonatal vaccination and protection against death. However, its nature as an editorial commentary rather than a primary research study or formal systematic review may affect its classification under study design criteria.",,,3.6,"  - ""inoculation of a single dose of vaccinia as a neonate""
