Smallpox vaccination discontinuation and monkeypox incidence in an African endemic region: a reanalysis on the relationship between the withdrawal of smallpox vaccine and subsequent morbidity.,"BACKGROUND
Monkey pox has expanded across Europe as a result of the widespread outbreak, creating a severe public health risk. Monkey pox is an uncommon pox infection that has reappeared due to zoonosis. Monkey pox has spread over Europe and North America, posing a serious public health risk. The regular smallpox vaccine has been shown to be effective against monkeypox. The suspension of smallpox immunization is currently being debated due to the possibility of a connection with the current monkeypox outbreak. In clinical immunology, the link between a desire for smallpox vaccination, low population immunity, and a higher incidence of monkeypox is an intriguing topic.
METHODS
This is a descriptive analysis done in the past. The writers investigate the situation in West Africa in this research. The available data on monkeypox incidence in an African endemic area was reassessed.
RESULTS
Based on a recent analysis of epidemiological data from an endemic area, there is no indication of a yearly ongoing increase in monkeypox incidence following the discontinuation of the smallpox vaccine, and incidence varies.
CONCLUSION
There is no evidence of an annual increase in monkeypox incidence following the withdrawal of smallpox immunization.","R. Mungmunpuntipantip, V. Wiwanitkit",,,American Journal of Clinical and Experimental Immunology,2,2022,yes,"The study examines population-level incidence in an African endemic region, which is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study reports on mpox incidence as a measure of morbidity related to the withdrawal of vaccination.,yes,The study is a descriptive analysis and reanalysis of epidemiological data.,yes,This is a human epidemiological study investigating incidence in a geographical region.,no,"The study abstract does not mention the inclusion or specific analysis of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; it focuses on general population data.",no,"The study focuses on the discontinuation and withdrawal of smallpox immunization in the population and does not specify if participants received the vaccine during the required windows (pregnancy, childhood, etc.).",yes,The study addresses the smallpox vaccine.,Exclude,"This study is an epidemiological reanalysis of mpox incidence in West Africa following the withdrawal of the smallpox vaccine. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not specifically include or report on the target populations of interest, such as children or pregnant women.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""descriptive analysis done in the past. The writers investigate the situation in West Africa in this research. The available data on monkeypox incidence in an African endemic area was reassessed.""",,"  - ""there is no indication of a yearly ongoing increase in monkeypox incidence following the discontinuation of the smallpox vaccine, and incidence varies.""",,"  - ""descriptive analysis done in the past. The writers investigate the situation in West Africa in this research. The available data on monkeypox incidence in an African endemic area was reassessed.""",,"  - ""descriptive analysis done in the past. The writers investigate the situation in West Africa in this research. The available data on monkeypox incidence in an African endemic area was reassessed.""",,"  - ""descriptive analysis done in the past. The writers investigate the situation in West Africa in this research. The available data on monkeypox incidence in an African endemic area was reassessed.""
