Neurologic Complications of Smallpox and Monkeypox: A Review.,"Importance
Orthopox viruses include smallpox virus, a once feared but now eradicated virus, as well as monkeypox virus. Monkeypox is an emerging virus initially isolated in 1958, previously unrecognized outside sub-Saharan Africa until a worldwide outbreak in May 2022. It is important to review known neurologic consequences of both these viruses, as complications of smallpox may be relevant to monkeypox, though complications of monkeypox may be rarer and perhaps less severe.
Observations
This was a literature review of the known neurologic complications of smallpox, which include encephalitis, transverse myelitis, and acute disseminated encephalomyelitis among others; historical complications of smallpox vaccination, including postvaccinal encephalomyelitis; and the known neurologic complications of monkeypox, which include headaches and mood disturbances, as well as rare presentations of encephalitis, transverse myelitis, and seizures. Of concern is the possibility of viral persistence and systemic complications in immunocompromised individuals. Also provided were considerations for diagnosis, current treatment, and prevention of monkeypox.
Conclusions and Relevance
Monkeypox should be considered in high-risk populations who present with neurologic syndromes. Diagnosis may require serology and polymerase chain reaction testing of blood and spinal fluid. Antiviral therapy should be initiated early in the course of the illness.","B. Billioux, Oliver Tshiani Mbaya, J. Sejvar, A. Nath",10.1001/jamaneurol.2022.3491,https://doi.org/10.1001/jamaneurol.2022.3491,JAMA Neurology,55,2022,yes,"The study focuses on individuals with neurologic complications of monkeypox and smallpox, rather than exclusively healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on safety outcomes and complications such as encephalitis, myelitis, and postvaccinal encephalomyelitis.",no,"The paper identifies as a 'literature review' and does not indicate that it is a primary research study, systematic review, or meta-analysis.",yes,The study is a clinical review of neurologic syndromes in humans.,no,"The abstract does not specify that the review includes pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"The study does not indicate that vaccine administration occurred during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence for a specific sub-population in this abstract.",yes,The study discusses smallpox vaccination and its historical complications.,Exclude,"This article is a narrative literature review rather than a systematic review or primary research study. Additionally, it does not specify the inclusion of pediatric or pregnant populations, which are required under the population criteria.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""This was a literature review of the known neurologic complications of smallpox,""
