Clinical and serologic study of four smallpox vaccines comparing variations of dose and route of administration. Summary.,"Because of increasing concern about morbidity and mortality associated with smallpox vaccination, the Vaccine Development Branch of the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID) called together on January 12, 1970 an advisory group to discuss what could be done by the NIAID to improve smallpox vaccines or procedures for vaccination The group included experts and consultants from academic medicine, the National Center for Disease Control, the Bureau of Biologics, the military, the National Institutes of Health, and the World Health Organization (WHO).","G. Galasso, M. Mattheis, J. Cherry, J. Connor, K. Mcintosh, A. Benenson, D. Alling",10.1093/INFDIS/135.1.183,https://doi.org/10.1093/INFDIS/135.1.183,Journal of Infectious Diseases,34,1977,maybe,"The text does not specify the age or pregnancy status of participants, mentioning only an advisory group with experts from the military and other organizations.",yes,The paper discusses morbidity and mortality related to vaccination.,maybe,"While the title mentions a 'study', the provided text describes an advisory group meeting report rather than primary research data.",yes,"The title refers to a clinical study, which involves humans.",no,"The text contains no evidence that the study included pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,There is no information regarding the timing of vaccination relative to pregnancy or childhood stages.,yes,The study involves historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This paper is a summary of an advisory group meeting regarding smallpox vaccine improvements. It fails to provide evidence that the target populations—pregnant women, children, or adolescents—were included in the clinical observations, and thus fails the mandatory population criteria.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""called together on January 12, 1970 an advisory group to discuss what could be done by the NIAID to improve smallpox vaccines""
