Mpox (Monkeypox) Infection in Children.,"Mpox is an enveloped double-stranded DNA virus belonging to the Orthopoxvirus genus in the Poxviridae family. This family also includes variola, cowpox, and vaccinia viruses. In November 2022, the World Health Organization recommended using the name mpox instead of monkeypox due to racist and stigmatizing language. Mpox was first identified in macaque monkeys in 1958, hence the name; however, rodents are hypothesized to be its primary host and natural reservoir. The disease is endemic to rainforests of several Western and Central African countries, infecting some mammalian species. Two genetic groups evolved from a common ancestor or clades (I and II) have been identified.Human infection was first reported in 1970 in a 9-month-old in Zaire. Since then, sporadic cases and regional outbreaks have been reported in endemic areas, with an increasing number of cases in younger patients during the past 2 decades. This increase has been attributed to a decrease in those vaccinated for smallpox after its eradication in 1980, an increase in the animal reservoir, and an increase in consumption of and contact with rodents and squirrels due to repurposing of forested areas in endemic countries. Before 2022, only sporadic cases were reported in nonendemic countries. In 2003, 53 cases were reported in the United States in 6 Midwestern states after contact with infected prairie dogs.In May 2022, a significant number of cases were reported across the world, leading to the World Health Organization declaring this outbreak to be a public health emergency of international concern in July 2022. Since then, more than 80,000 cases have been reported in more than 50 countries possibly as a result of human-to-human transmission via close contact of infected individuals. Clade IIb has been responsible for this latest outbreak, causing less severe disease, with a fatality rate of less than 1%. It caused less severe disease than what was previously reported, as clade I was reported to have a fatality rate of 10%. Most cases were reported in young adult males who have sex with males, presenting with a predominance of anogenital lesions. Infection in children and adolescents was infrequent (<0.3% of total cases), and disease was generally not severe. The overall fatality rate in adults and children has been reported to be less than 0.2% in the United States.Animal-to-human transmission via direct physical contact or via ingestion has been extensively described and has been responsible for previous sporadic cases and small outbreaks. However, human-to-human transmission, mainly via skin-to-skin contact, has been the main mechanism of transmission for the recent outbreak. Transmission through large respiratory droplets, indirect contact through fomites, bites, scratches, contact with blood, or bodily fluids, has also been described. In addition, vertical transmission and secondary fetal deaths have been reported. To date, there is no evidence of transmission through seminal/vaginal fluids or human milk.The incubation period is 3 weeks (median, 9 days), and the illness lasts 2 to 4 weeks. Prodromal symptoms are fever, chills, fatigue, and lymphadenopathy. In previous outbreaks, these symptoms were described in most cases (80%) and would appear 1 to 5 days before cutaneous lesions. During the 2022 outbreak, they were less frequently observed (50%–60%), and cutaneous lesions were a more common presenting symptom. Cutaneous and mucosal lesions develop simultaneously, can appear anywhere (but most commonly on the face and extremities), and can include the palms and soles. In the current global outbreak, lesions are often anorectal, genital, and oral. Lesions progress through stages: macular, papular, vesicular, pustular, and eventually scabbing and crusting. The lesions are typically well circumscribed, deep, and umbilicated. The infectious period starts from the onset of symptoms, including prodromal symptoms even if cutaneous lesions are absent. Physical contact with mucocutaneous lesions, however, has been described to be the most efficient route of transmission. The virus has been cultured from crusts, raising the hypothesis that the infectious period lasts until crusts fall. Mucosal lesions have been reported to ulcerate and can become confluent. Rectal symptoms (pain, bleeding) have been reported in the current global outbreak. Respiratory symptoms (congestion, cough) can also occur.The differential diagnosis in young children can be challenging. Chickenpox is an important consideration because lesions can appear similar to mpox in the vesicular phase. In contrast to chickenpox, the lesions in mpox are typically not seen at different stages. Coinfection of mpox and chickenpox has been occasionally described in African countries, so consider testing for both if papular and vesicular lesions at different stages are present in the setting of known epidemiologic risk factors. Disseminated cutaneous herpes simplex virus infection, particularly in the neonatal period or in patients with eczema, should also be considered. Other infections to consider in the differential diagnosis are molluscum contagiosum, measles, hand-foot-mouth disease, impetigo, syphilis, drug-associated eruptions, and scabies.Mpox should be considered in children with papular, vesicular, or pustular umbilicated lesions suspicious for disease, especially if epidemiologic criteria are met. Specifically in adolescents, mpox should be considered if a sexually active patient presents with genital or anal lesions, particularly in males who have sex with males. In these cases, testing for other sexually transmitted infections (STIs), including human immunodeficiency virus, should be performed because coinfection with other STIs has been reported in 30% of patients during the 2022 outbreak. In a suspected case of mpox infection, diagnosis is performed by obtaining a sample from at least 2 lesions, preferably from different locations, for molecular diagnostics. Sterile, synthetic, noncotton swabs should be used to collect specimens. Swabs of the lesion surface, exudate, or crusts are the recommended specimen types for laboratory testing, and procedures depend on the phase of the rash. Unroofing or aspiration of the lesions is not necessary, and vigorous swab should be sufficient. Real-time polymerase chain reaction is the preferred diagnostic test because it allows rapid detection with discrimination from other Orthopoxvirus species. Samples should be sent to a local response network laboratory or an authorized commercial laboratory for analysis.Complications have been more frequently reported in children younger than 8 years and in immunocompromised hosts, including bacterial superinfection such as cellulitis and proctitis, bronchopneumonia, encephalitis, and keratitis that can cause blindness. Permanent scarring has been described as another complication, often with bacterial superinfection rather than mpox infection alone. Historically, severity of disease was significant, particularly among children younger than 8 years, with mortality rates of 10% to 28% in children infected with clade I virus. However, during the recent 2022 outbreak with clade II, this rate was found to be lower worldwide (1%–5%), with no deaths reported in children in the United States.Treatment of suspected or confirmed cases should be individualized according to severity of disease and risk of poor outcomes. Treatment should be initiated in children younger than 1 year, those with severe immunocompromising conditions (leukemia, lymphoma, solid organ transplant, hematopoietic cell transplant, generalized malignancy, high-dose corticosteroids, tumor necrosis factor α inhibitors, immunodeficiency, poorly controlled human immunodeficiency virus), pregnancy, and conditions affecting skin integrity (eczema, atopic dermatitis, burns, impetigo, varicella-zoster virus coinfection, psoriasis, severe diaper dermatitis). Children younger 8 years are considered to be at higher risk, so treatment can also be considered in this age group. A pediatric infectious diseases expert can be consulted to guide treatment in high-risk patients. Tecovirimat, an antiviral approved by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for treating smallpox, is the current first-line therapy for mpox. However, safety studies have not been performed in children, and efficacy for mpox has not been established in the absence of clinical trials published to date. Treatment can be initiated in the setting of severe disease or with the previously reported risk factors while awaiting confirmation if there is a high degree of suspicion. The oral route is preferred because intravenous infusion can lead to renal dysfunction, particularly in children younger than 2 years. Other available options that have demonstrated antiviral activity in animal models are cidofovir and brincidofovir, but they should be considered only for severe cases with progression despite tecovirimat use. Last, vaccinia immunoglobulin was FDA approved to treat smallpox vaccination complications and has been used in select patients with severe disease or as postexposure prophylaxis in very high-risk patients. Two vaccines have been FDA approved for smallpox vaccination: ACAM2000 and JYNNEOS. Both are live vaccines, but only JYNNEOS has been FDA approved for mpox immunization. It has not been licensed or rigorously evaluated in children, but, since August 2022, standard dosing was authorized in children older than 1 year in the United States under an Emergency Use Authorization. Finally, if mpox is suspected, physical contact with other people should be avoided and caregivers should be limited to 1. If required to leave isolation, all lesions of the patient should be covered, and a well-fitting face mask should be worn.In conclusion, mpox infection should be suspected in sexually active adolescents with genital or perianal lesions. Testing for mpox together with other STIs should be perf","Sandra Castejon-Ramirez, Jason A. Yaun, B. Bagga",10.1542/pir.2023-006049,https://doi.org/10.1542/pir.2023-006049,Pediatrics in review,0,2024,yes,The focus of the paper is specifically on the pediatric population.,yes,"The paper discusses disease severity and outcomes in children, such as complications and hospitalization potential.",no,"This is a narrative review or clinical update article, not primary research (RCT, cohort, etc.) or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,The article focuses on human infection and pediatric clinical care.,yes,The study explicitly includes children and adolescents.,maybe,"The paper discusses vaccine authorization for children older than 1 year, but does not present original data from a specific study population vaccinated during childhood.",yes,The paper mentions ACAM2000 and JYNNEOS.,Exclude,"This article is a clinical review focused on mpox infection in children and adolescents. While it covers the relevant population and vaccine types, it does not meet the study design requirements as it is a narrative update rather than a primary research study or systematic review.",Study Design,Study Design,0.1,,,"  - ""Infection in children and adolescents was infrequent (<0.3% of total cases), and disease was generally not severe.""
  - ""Complications have been more frequently reported in children younger than 8 years""",,"  - ""efficacy for mpox has not been established in the absence of clinical trials published to date.""",,"  - ""Human infection was first reported in 1970 in a 9-month-old in Zaire.""",,"  - ""Infection in children and adolescents was infrequent (<0.3% of total cases), and disease was generally not severe.""",,"  - ""since August 2022, standard dosing was authorized in children older than 1 year in the United States under an Emergency Use Authorization.""",,"  - ""Two vaccines have been FDA approved for smallpox vaccination: ACAM2000 and JYNNEOS.""",
"Monkeypox in neonates: A narrative review on clinical presentations, vertical transmission, and treatment challenges","Monkeypox (Mpox), a zoonotic infection caused by the Mpox virus, has re-emerged as a global public health concern, with unique implications for neonatal health. Although rare in newborns, cases of neonatal Mpox pose significant diagnostic and therapeutic challenges due to limited clinical data and the vulnerability of this population. This narrative review explores the pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnostic barriers, and therapeutic strategies associated with Mpox in neonates. Vertical and perinatal transmission have been identified, with some cases presenting with vesicular rashes, fever, lymphadenopathy, and systemic involvement such as respiratory or neurological complications. Diagnosis often relies on polymerase chain reaction testing, yet resource limitations and symptom overlap with other neonatal infections can hinder accurate identification. Antiviral treatments like tecovirimat and cidofovir are considered in severe cases, but dosing in neonates remains uncertain due to a lack of robust safety data. Supportive care, including hydration, fever management, and prevention of secondary infections, is critical. Breastfeeding decisions require individualized assessment due to the unknown risk of viral transmission through breast milk. Preventive strategies emphasize early isolation, surveillance, and infection control measures in neonatal care settings. The review highlights significant research gaps in understanding neonatal Mpox, especially concerning long-term outcomes and optimal treatment protocols. A coordinated global effort is essential to improve diagnostics, develop safe therapeutic options, and establish evidence-based guidelines tailored to neonates.","Ezza Ikram, Abaidullah Shaukat, Muhammad Shoaib Qureshi, Muneeb Saifullah, M. Aslam, A. Mehdi",10.5495/wjcid.v14.i1.109806,https://doi.org/10.5495/wjcid.v14.i1.109806,World Journal of Clinical Infectious Diseases,0,2025,yes,"The study focuses on neonates and vertical transmission, rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.",maybe,"The review discusses clinical presentations, systemic involvement, and therapeutic strategies, although it mentions a lack of robust safety data.",no,"The study identifies itself as a narrative review, which is not a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,The focus is on human clinical presentations and transmission in neonates.,yes,The study specifically focuses on neonates and vertical transmission from mothers.,no,"The abstract discusses antiviral treatments (tecovirimat, cidofovir) but does not mention vaccination of these populations.",no,The abstract focuses on antiviral treatments and makes no mention of vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,This is a narrative review focused on neonatal clinical presentation and antiviral treatments for Mpox. It fails the study design criterion and does not address vaccination or vaccine outcomes in the target population.,"Study Design, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""This narrative review explores the pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnostic barriers, and therapeutic strategies associated with Mpox in neonates.""
