A Look Back at the Recent Smallpox Vaccination Program,"In late 2002, smallpox vaccination was reinstated temporarily for military personnel (590,400 doses); in early 2003, the program was expanded to include civilians at high risk (64,600 doses, mostly for health care and emergency service workers). In two studies, researchers have assessed adverse events (AEs) that were …",T. Schwenk,10.1056/JW200601100000001,https://doi.org/10.1056/JW200601100000001,NEJM Journal Watch,1,2006,no,"The study population consists of military personnel and health care or emergency service workers, which are groups typically composed of healthy adults.",yes,The text explicitly mentions that researchers assessed adverse events (AEs).,no,The article is from NEJM Journal Watch and serves as a summary/commentary on other studies rather than a primary research study or a formal systematic review.,yes,The study involves human participants (military personnel and civilians).,no,"The study identifies its participants as military personnel, health care workers, and emergency service workers, none of whom are identified as pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"The vaccinations occurred in military and emergency service contexts for adults, not during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,The study explicitly concerns smallpox vaccination.,Exclude,"This article is a commentary/summary from NEJM Journal Watch that describes smallpox vaccination adverse events in adult populations, specifically military personnel and high-risk civilian workers. It fails the inclusion criteria because it does not target pregnant women, neonates, or children, and it is not a primary research study.","Population Focus, Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""smallpox vaccination was reinstated temporarily for military personnel (590,400 doses);""
