"Maternal COVID-19, vaccination safety in pregnancy, and evidence of protective immunity","Over the past 18 months, the world has seen the largest pandemic, caused by the severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2). As of June 28, 2021, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) reported 98,948 cases of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) infection in pregnancy and 109 related maternal deaths in the United States alone.1Villar J, Ariff S, Gunier RB, Thiruvengadam R, Rauch S, Kholin A, et al. Maternal and neonatal morbidity and mortality among pregnant women with and without COVID-19 infection: the INTERCOVID multinational cohort study [published online ahead of print April 22, 2021]. JAMA Pediatr. https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapediatrics.2021.1050.Google Scholar As the pandemic continues to evolve, the rapid and overwhelming increase in available evidence on the impact in pregnancy has resulted in studies of varying degrees of bias and quality. In this brief review, we seek to fill some of the knowledge gaps regarding maternal care considerations and answer some key questions about vaccination safety in pregnancy and evidence of protective immunity (Fig 1). Maternal COVID-19 varies widely, but clinical course, laboratory findings, and radiological patterns found in pregnancy (Table I) are similar to those in the nonpregnant population.3Molteni E. Astley C.M. Ma W. Sudre C.H. Magee L.A. Murray B. et al.SARS-CoV-2 (COVID-19) infection in pregnant women: characterization of symptoms and syndromes predictive of disease and severity through real-time, remote participatory epidemiology.medRxiv. 2020; (2020.08.17.20161760)Google Scholar Although some patients may be asymptomatic, presence of any COVID-19 symptoms was found to be associated with increased maternal morbidity and mortality.1Villar J, Ariff S, Gunier RB, Thiruvengadam R, Rauch S, Kholin A, et al. Maternal and neonatal morbidity and mortality among pregnant women with and without COVID-19 infection: the INTERCOVID multinational cohort study [published online ahead of print April 22, 2021]. JAMA Pediatr. https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapediatrics.2021.1050.Google ScholarTable IClinical findings, laboratory parameters, and radiologic findings of frequency in pregnancies affected by COVID-19Sign and symptomsRange of frequency in pregnancyClinical findings Fever32.8%-78%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google Scholar Cough34%-70%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google Scholar Dyspnea7.3%-35.6%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google Scholar Asymptomatic8%-32.6%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google Scholar Myalgia6%-24.4%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google Scholar Sore throat3.4%-22.2%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google Scholar Fatigue9.5%-18.5%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google Scholar Diarrhea4%-10.4%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google ScholarLaboratory parameters Elevated C-reactive protein40.8%-70.3%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google Scholar Lymphopenia29%-68.2%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google Scholar Leukocytosis13%-45.8%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google Scholar Leukopenia<45.3%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google Scholar Abnormal liver function test result8%-27.3%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google Scholar Thrombocytopenia2.7%-8.4%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google ScholarRadiologic findings Ground-glass opacities41.5%-81.6%2Papapanou M. Papaioannou M. Petta A. Routsi E. Farmaki M. Vlahos N. et al.Maternal and neonatal characteristics and outcomes of COVID-19 in pregnancy: an overview of systematic reviews.Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021; 18: 596Crossref PubMed Scopus (97) Google Scholar Open table in a new tab Maternal COVID-19 is associated with consistent and substantial increases in morbidity and mortality when infected pregnant versus nonpregnant individuals are compared.1Villar J, Ariff S, Gunier RB, Thiruvengadam R, Rauch S, Kholin A, et al. Maternal and neonatal morbidity and mortality among pregnant women with and without COVID-19 infection: the INTERCOVID multinational cohort study [published online ahead of print April 22, 2021]. JAMA Pediatr. https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapediatrics.2021.1050.Google Scholar A large study conducted by the Maternal-Fetal Medicine Unit Network including 1219 patients reported that mothers with severe or critical COVID-19 and their neonates are at increased risk for a number of perinatal complications, including cesarean birth, hypertensive disorders of pregnancy, preterm birth, venous thromboembolism, neonatal intensive care unit admission, and lower birth weight, compared with asymptomatic mothers.4Metz T.D. Clifton R.G. Hughes B.L. Sandoval G. Saade G.R. Grobman W.A. et al.Disease severity and perinatal outcomes of pregnant patients with coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19).Obstet Gynecol. 2021; 137: 571-580Crossref PubMed Scopus (274) Google Scholar Pregnancy is also independently associated with an increased risk for intensive care unit admission, needing extracorporeal membrane oxygenation, and maternal death among patients with symptomatic COVID-19 infection. Moreover, comorbidities (body mass index higher than 35 kg/m2, diabetes, and cardiovascular disorders) and advanced maternal age appear to have an independent risk for adverse maternal outcomes. Vertical transmission is defined as evidence of transmission of the SARS-CoV-2 virus from the mother to the fetus or newborn. Studies have evaluated SARS-CoV-2 viral concentrations in umbilical cord blood and placenta. Real-time RT-PCR has been used to evaluate amniotic fluid, newborn blood, urine, nasopharyngeal, fecal, and rectal swabs. Positive samples have been rare, and significant neonatal respiratory disease, even in the presence of SARS-CoV-2 positivity, is even more infrequent.5Amaral W.N.D. Moraes C.L. Rodrigues A. Noll M. Arruda J.T. Mendonca C.R. Maternal coronavirus infections and neonates born to mothers with SARS-CoV-2: a systematic review.Healthcare (Basel). 2020; 8: 511Crossref PubMed Scopus (45) Google Scholar The CDC reported that transmission of SARS-CoV-2 virus to neonates occurred primarily through respiratory droplets during the postnatal period when neonates are exposed to mothers or other caregivers with COVID-19. Overall, the risk of vertical transmission of the SARS-CoV-2 virus is approximately 3.2%.6Kotlyar A.M. Grechukhina O. Chen A. Popkhadze S. Grimshaw A. Tal O. et al.Vertical transmission of coronavirus disease 2019: a systematic review and meta-analysis.Am J Obstet Gynecol. 2021; 224: 35-53.e3Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (412) Google Scholar In utero fetal production of IgG and IgM antibodies starts in the 20th week of gestation; therefore, most neonatal IgG is of maternal origin. IgG positivity cannot support or refute vertical transmission. IgM antibodies do not cross the placenta, and therefore IgM presence","A. Pham, D. Aronoff, J. Thompson",10.1016/j.jaci.2021.07.013,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaci.2021.07.013,Journal of Allergy and Clinical Immunology,15,2021,yes,The study focuses specifically on pregnant women and neonates rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.,yes,The study discusses safety (complications) and protective immunity/effectiveness.,no,"The paper describes itself as a 'brief review'. This is typically a narrative review rather than primary research (RCT, cohort) or an evidence synthesis study (systematic review, meta-analysis).",yes,The study reviews human data related to maternal and neonatal outcomes.,yes,The study explicitly includes pregnant women and neonates as the primary subjects of concern.,yes,The study addresses vaccination safety specifically during pregnancy.,no,"The paper is exclusively about the COVID-19 (SARS-CoV-2) pandemic and associated vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,This paper is a brief narrative review focused on SARS-CoV-2 (COVID-19) infection and vaccination safety in pregnant women. It is excluded because it does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines and does not follow a systematic review or primary research design.,"Study Design, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""In this brief review, we seek to fill some of the knowledge gaps regarding maternal care considerations and answer some key questions about vaccination safety in pregnancy and evidence of protective immunity""
