Phase 3 Efficacy Trial of Modified Vaccinia Ankara as a Vaccine against Smallpox.,"BACKGROUND
Many countries have stockpiled vaccines because of concerns about the reemergence of smallpox. Traditional smallpox vaccines are based on replicating vaccinia viruses; these vaccines have considerable side effects.
METHODS
To evaluate the efficacy of modified vaccinia Ankara (MVA) as a potential smallpox vaccine, we randomly assigned 440 participants to receive two doses of MVA followed by one dose of the established replicating-vaccinia vaccine ACAM2000 (the MVA group) or to receive one dose of ACAM2000 (the ACAM2000-only group). The two primary end points were noninferiority of the MVA vaccine to ACAM2000 with respect to the peak serum neutralizing antibody titers and attenuation of the ACAM2000-associated major cutaneous reaction by previous MVA vaccination, measured according to the maximum lesion area and the derived area attenuation ratio.
RESULTS
A total of 220 and 213 participants were randomly assigned and vaccinated in the MVA group and ACAM2000-only group, respectively, and 208 participants received two MVA vaccinations. At peak visits, MVA vaccination induced a geometric mean titer of neutralizing antibodies of 153.5 at week 6, as compared with 79.3 at week 4 with ACAM2000 (a ratio of 1.94 [95% confidence interval {CI}, 1.56 to 2.40]). At day 14, the geometric mean titer of neutralizing antibodies induced by a single MVA vaccination (16.2) was equal to that induced by ACAM2000 (16.2), and the percentages of participants with seroconversion were similar (90.8% and 91.8%, respectively). The median lesion areas of the major cutaneous reaction were 0 mm2 in the MVA group and 76.0 mm2 in the ACAM2000-only group, resulting in an area attenuation ratio of 97.9% (95% CI, 96.6 to 98.3). There were fewer adverse events or adverse events of grade 3 or higher after both MVA vaccination periods in the MVA group than in the ACAM2000-only group (17 vs. 64 participants with adverse events of grade 3 or higher, P<0.001).
CONCLUSIONS
No safety concerns associated with the MVA vaccine were identified. Immune responses and attenuation of the major cutaneous reaction suggest that this MVA vaccine protected against variola infection. (Funded by the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Preparedness and Response Biomedical Advanced Research and Development Authority of the Department of Health and Human Services and Bavarian Nordic; ClinicalTrials.gov number, NCT01913353.).","P. Pittman, M. Hahn, Heechoon Lee, Craig D Koca, N. Samy, D. Schmidt, Joachim Hornung, H. Weidenthaler, C. Heery, T. Meyer, G. Silbernagl, J. Maclennan, P. Chaplin",10.1056/NEJMoa1817307,https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1817307,New England Journal of Medicine,213,2019,maybe,"The study was a Phase 3 efficacy trial involving 440 participants. While it does not explicitly state the age range, standard Phase 3 trials for smallpox vaccines in this context typically involve healthy adults, and there is no mention of non-adult or pregnant populations. However, it does not provide enough evidence to confirm it excluded healthy non-pregnant adults solely.",yes,"The study reports safety outcomes, specifically adverse events of grade 3 or higher, and effectiveness outcomes such as neutralizing antibody titers.",yes,"The study is a Phase 3 efficacy trial and randomized participants, making it an RCT (primary research).",yes,The study involved 440 human participants.,no,"There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents being included. Standard vaccine efficacy trials of this nature usually recruit healthy adults unless otherwise specified.",no,"There is no evidence that vaccinations occurred during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The study involves Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA) and ACAM2000 (replicating-vaccinia vaccine).,Exclude,"This study is a Phase 3 RCT evaluating MVA and ACAM2000 vaccines in humans. However, it does not include the target population of pregnant women or children, and thus fails the specific population requirements of the screening criteria.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""randomly assigned 440 participants to receive two doses of MVA followed by one dose of the established replicating-vaccinia vaccine ACAM2000""",,"  - ""There were fewer adverse events or adverse events of grade 3 or higher after both MVA vaccination periods""
  - ""The two primary end points were noninferiority of the MVA vaccine to ACAM2000 with respect to the peak serum neutralizing antibody titers and attenuation of the ACAM2000-associated major cutaneous reaction""",,"  - ""randomly assigned 440 participants to receive two doses of MVA followed by one dose of the established replicating-vaccinia vaccine ACAM2000""",,"  - ""randomly assigned 440 participants to receive two doses of MVA followed by one dose of the established replicating-vaccinia vaccine ACAM2000""",,"  - ""randomly assigned 440 participants to receive two doses of MVA followed by one dose of the established replicating-vaccinia vaccine ACAM2000""",,"  - ""randomly assigned 440 participants to receive two doses of MVA followed by one dose of the established replicating-vaccinia vaccine ACAM2000""",,"  - ""randomly assigned 440 participants to receive two doses of MVA followed by one dose of the established replicating-vaccinia vaccine ACAM2000""",
US military service members vaccinated against smallpox in 2003 and 2004 experience a slightly higher risk of hospitalization postvaccination.,"This study explores adverse events severe enough to warrant hospitalization that may have been associated with receiving the smallpox vaccine in conjunction with military service. Cox proportional hazards modeling was used to identify the risk of hospitalization among US active-duty military personnel during a 1-year period following receipt of the smallpox vaccine. The reference group consisted of active-duty military personnel who also received the smallpox vaccine after the conclusion of their health care observation period, allowing for comparison to a temporally and demographically similar population. The risk of hospitalization was slightly elevated among the postvaccine group for any-cause hospitalization and for hospitalization in several broad diagnostic categories. Hospitalizations for asthma, autoimmune diseases, and myopericarditis, were more likely in the postvaccine group. The increased risk of hospitalization for varied outcomes does not necessarily imply a cause-effect relationship, but it does offer areas for more focused study, using longitudinal data to explore the long-term impact of smallpox vaccination on the health of young adults.","I. Jacobson, T. Smith, Besa Smith, T. Wells, R. Reed, M. Ryan",10.1016/j.vaccine.2008.05.044,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2008.05.044,Vaccine,17,2008,no,"The study is exclusively limited to active-duty military personnel, who are characterized as young adults. There is no mention of pregnant individuals or non-adult populations.",yes,"The study reports on safety outcomes, specifically risks of hospitalization and conditions like myopericarditis.",yes,"The study uses Cox proportional hazards modeling, indicating a cohort study design.",yes,The study involves human US military personnel.,no,"The study participants are active-duty US military personnel, which excludes children and neonates, and there is no mention of pregnant women.",no,"The vaccination occurred in 2003 and 2004 during military service, which corresponds to adulthood rather than childhood, pregnancy, or the neonatal period.",yes,The study investigates the smallpox vaccine.,Exclude,"This study is excluded because it focuses exclusively on a healthy adult population of active-duty military personnel rather than the required target populations of pregnant women, neonates, or children. Furthermore, the vaccination took place during adult military service rather than during the required childhood or pregnancy windows.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""US active-duty military personnel""
