"Analysis of cases reported as generalized vaccinia during the US military smallpox vaccination program, December 2002 to December 2004.","BACKGROUND
We evaluated military personnel who developed dermatologic reactions suggestive of generalized vaccinia (GV) after smallpox vaccination.
METHODS
We conducted surveillance and retrospective analysis of cases from the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (a passive reporting system managed by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention), and the military's preventive medicine channels, vaccine healthcare centers, clinical laboratory network, dermatology clinics, and pathology departments from December 2002 to December 2004.
RESULTS
Of 74 cases investigated in 753,226 vaccinations, 50 (67.6%) met the case definition of possible GV (rate 66/million), 95% confidence interval (49-88/million), consistent with historically reported rates. Cases of possible GV occurred more frequently in primary vaccinees (81/million) than in those revaccinated (32/million) (relative risk 2.6, 95% confidence interval 1.2-5.9, P = .013). None met the case definition of probable or confirmed GV, including 15 with virologically negative laboratory evaluations (eg, culture, skin biopsy, or polymerase chain reaction).
LIMITATIONS
The methods of case collection and retrospective nature of this study are its limitations. The clinical diagnosis of possible GV was made on the basis of the authors' interpretation of clinical notes and adverse events submitted by more than 100 different providers. Only 15 of the 74 cases of possible GV had laboratory attempts for virological confirmation.
CONCLUSION
GV is still a rarely reported complication of smallpox vaccination. True GV, strictly defined, may be even less common than previously reported. We named one self-limited dermatologic manifestation confused with GV ""postvaccinial nonviral pustulosis."" Properly screened individuals considering smallpox vaccination may be assured most exanthemata after vaccination are benign.","F. Lewis, Scott A. Norton, R. Bradshaw, Joyce A. Lapa, John D. Grabenstein",10.1016/J.JAAD.2006.04.017,https://doi.org/10.1016/J.JAAD.2006.04.017,Journal of American Academy of Dermatology,16,2006,no,"The study evaluated military personnel who received the smallpox vaccine. In the context of the US military smallpox vaccination program during 2002-2004, the participants are exclusively healthy, non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on safety outcomes, specifically dermatologic adverse events like generalized vaccinia.",yes,The study is a retrospective analysis of surveillance data and medical records.,yes,The study describes clinical outcomes in human military personnel.,no,"The study population consists of US military personnel, who are adults. There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"The participants were active-duty military personnel at the time of vaccination, not children or pregnant women.",yes,The study involves the US military smallpox vaccination program.,Exclude,"This study focuses exclusively on healthy adult military personnel and does not include the target populations of interest such as children, neonates, or pregnant women. While it effectively evaluates safety outcomes of the smallpox vaccine, it fails to meet the population-specific inclusion criteria.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""smallpox vaccination.""",,"  - ""smallpox vaccination.""
  - ""Of 74 cases investigated in 753,226 vaccinations, 50 (67.6%) met the case definition of possible GV""",,,,"  - ""smallpox vaccination.""",,"  - ""smallpox vaccination.""",,"  - ""smallpox vaccination.""",,"  - ""smallpox vaccination.""",
"Safety of Ancestral Monovalent BNT162b2, mRNA-1273, and NVX-CoV2373 COVID-19 Vaccines in US Children Aged 6 Months to 17 Years","Key Points Question Were statistical signals detected for health outcomes after ancestral monovalent COVID-19 vaccination in US children aged 6 months to 17 years? Findings In this cohort study of 4 102 106 vaccinated enrollees, 2 outcomes met the statistical threshold for a signal compared with historical rates: myocarditis or pericarditis after BNT162b2 (ages 12-17 years) and seizure after BNT162b2 (ages 2-4 years) and mRNA-1273 (ages 2-5 years) vaccinations. Meaning Near-real-time monitoring detected a previously identified statistical signal for myocarditis or pericarditis and a new statistical signal for seizure; however, these results should be interpreted cautiously because the methods only screened for potential statistical signals and have several limitations.","M. Hu, A. Shoaibi, Yuhui Feng, P. Lloyd, H. Wong, Elizabeth R. Smith, K. Amend, Annemarie Kline, D. Beachler, J. Gruber, Mahasweta Mitra, John D. Seeger, Charlalynn Harris, A. Secora, J. Obidi, Jing Wang, Jennifer Song, C. McMahill-Walraven, Christian Reich, Rowan McEvoy, Rose Do, Y. Chillarige, Robin Clifford, Danielle D. Cooper, R. Forshee, Steven A. Anderson",10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2024.8192,https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2024.8192,JAMA Network Open,12,2024,yes,"The study is specifically focused on children and adolescents aged 6 months to 17 years, rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports safety outcomes including myocarditis, pericarditis, and seizures.",yes,The study is described as a cohort study.,yes,"The study involves a cohort of 4,102,106 human enrollees.",yes,The participants are children and adolescents aged 6 months to 17 years.,yes,Participants received the vaccines during childhood or adolescence (ages 6 months to 17 years).,no,"The study evaluates COVID-19 vaccines (BNT162b2, mRNA-1273, and NVX-CoV2373) rather than vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the safety of COVID-19 vaccines in a pediatric population. While it includes the target age groups and reports relevant safety outcomes, it fails the inclusion criteria because it does not involve vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""US children aged 6 months to 17 years?""",,"  - ""myocarditis or pericarditis after BNT162b2 (ages 12-17 years) and seizure after BNT162b2 (ages 2-4 years) and mRNA-1273 (ages 2-5 years) vaccinations.""",,"  - ""cohort study of 4 102 106 vaccinated enrollees,""",,"  - ""cohort study of 4 102 106 vaccinated enrollees,""",,"  - ""US children aged 6 months to 17 years?""",,"  - ""US children aged 6 months to 17 years?""",,,
Serological responses to the MVA-based JYNNEOS monkeypox vaccine in a cohort of participants from the Democratic Republic of Congo,"The current worldwide monkepox outbreak has reaffirmed the continued threat monkeypox virus (MPXV) poses to public health. JYNNEOS, a Modified Vaccinia Ankara (MVA)-based live, non-replicating vaccine, was recently approved for monkeypox prevention for adults at high risk of MPXV infection in the United States. Although the safety and immunogenicity of JYNNEOS have been examined previously, the clinical cohorts studied largely derive from regions where MPXV does not typically circulate. In this study, we assess the quality and longevity of serological responses to two doses of JYNNEOS vaccine in a large cohort of healthcare workers from the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC). We show that JYNNEOS elicits a strong orthopoxvirus (OPXV)-specific antibody response in participants that peaks around day 42, or 2 weeks after the second vaccine dose. Participants with no prior history of smallpox vaccination or exposure have lower baseline antibody levels, but experience a similar fold-rise in antibody titers by day 42 as those with a prior history of vaccination. Both previously naïve and vaccinated participants generate vaccinia virus and MPXV-neutralizing antibody in response to JYNNEOS vaccination. Finally, even though total OPXV-specific IgG titers and neutralizing antibody titers declined from their peak and returned close to baseline levels by the 2-year mark, most participants remain IgG seropositive at the 2-year timepoint. Taken together, our data demonstrates that JYNNEOS vaccination triggers potent OPXV neutralizing antibody responses in a cohort of healthcare workers in DRC, a monkeypox-endemic region. MPXV vaccination with JYNNEOS may help ameliorate the disease and economic burden associated with monkeypox and combat potential outbreaks in areas with active virus circulation.","L. Priyamvada, W. Carson, E. Ortega, Terese Navarra, S. Tran, T. Smith, E. Pukuta, E. Muyamuna, J. Kabamba, B. Nguete, T. Likafi, G. Kokola, R. S. Lushima, J. M. Tamfum, E. Okitolonda, D. Kaba, B. Monroe, A. McCollum, B. Petersen, P. Satheshkumar, M. Townsend",10.1016/j.vaccine.2022.10.078,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2022.10.078,Vaccine,58,2022,no,"The study is exclusively limited to healthcare workers, who are adults. The abstract notes the vaccine is approved for adults.",no,"The study reports serological/immunogenicity outcomes rather than clinical safety or effectiveness (prevention of infection, hospitalization, or death) as required.",yes,This is a cohort study.,yes,The study involves human healthcare workers.,no,"The study cohort consists of healthcare workers, which excludes children, neonates, and pregnant women.",no,"The participants received the vaccine as healthcare workers, implying adulthood, rather than during childhood or pregnancy.",yes,The study evaluates the JYNNEOS MVA-based vaccine.,Exclude,"The study is excluded as it focuses exclusively on a cohort of adult healthcare workers and does not include the target population of children, adolescents, or pregnant women. Additionally, the study reports serological data rather than the required safety or clinical effectiveness outcomes.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""assess the quality and longevity of serological responses to two doses of JYNNEOS vaccine in a large cohort of healthcare workers from the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC).""
