Medically Attended Acute Adverse Events in Pregnant People After Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19) Booster Vaccination,"Receipt of an mRNA monovalent coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) booster vaccination in pregnancy was not associated with increased risk for serious acute adverse events. In this multisite, observational, matched cohort study of more than 80,000 pregnant people, receipt of an mRNA monovalent coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) booster vaccination in pregnancy was not associated with increased risk for thrombocytopenia, myocarditis, venous thromboembolism, ischemic stroke, or other serious adverse events within 21 or 42 days after booster vaccination. The mRNA monovalent COVID-19 booster in pregnancy was associated with an increased risk for medically attended malaise or fatigue within 7 days of vaccination (adjusted rate ratio [aRR] 3.64, 95% CI 2.42–5.48) and lymphadenopathy or lymphadenitis within 21 days (aRR 3.25, 95% CI 1.67–6.30) or 42 days (aRR 2.18, 95% CI 1.33–3.58) of vaccination. Our findings are consistent with prior evaluations of the primary COVID-19 vaccine series and are reassuring with respect to COVID-19 booster vaccination in pregnancy.","M. DeSilva, J. Haapala, G. Vazquez-Benitez, T. Boyce, C. Fuller, M. Daley, D. Getahun, S. Hambidge, H. Lipkind, A. Naleway, Jennifer C. Nelson, K. Vesco, Eric S. Weintraub, Joshua T. B. Williams, O. Zerbo, E. Kharbanda",10.1097/AOG.0000000000005241,https://doi.org/10.1097/AOG.0000000000005241,Obstetrics and Gynecology,8,2023,yes,The study focuses specifically on pregnant people.,yes,The study reports on serious acute adverse events following vaccination.,yes,"This is described as a multisite, observational, matched cohort study.",yes,"The study involves a cohort of more than 80,000 human participants.",yes,"The study explicitly includes more than 80,000 pregnant people.",yes,The study evaluates vaccination that occurred during pregnancy.,no,"The study evaluates mRNA monovalent COVID-19 booster vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study focuses on safety outcomes following mRNA COVID-19 booster vaccination in a large cohort of pregnant individuals. While it meets the criteria for population and timing, it fails the intervention requirement as it does not investigate vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""multisite, observational, matched cohort study of more than 80,000 pregnant people,""",,"  - ""increased risk for thrombocytopenia, myocarditis, venous thromboembolism, ischemic stroke, or other serious adverse events""",,"  - ""multisite, observational, matched cohort study of more than 80,000 pregnant people,""",,"  - ""multisite, observational, matched cohort study of more than 80,000 pregnant people,""",,"  - ""multisite, observational, matched cohort study of more than 80,000 pregnant people,""",,"  - ""Receipt of an mRNA monovalent coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) booster vaccination in pregnancy""",,"  - ""Receipt of an mRNA monovalent coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) booster vaccination in pregnancy""",
"Monkeypox outbreak in the Netherlands in 2022: public health response, epidemiological and clinical characteristics of the first 1000 cases and protection of the first-generation smallpox vaccine","In early May 2022 a global outbreak of monkeypox (MPX) started among persons without a travel history to regions known to be enzootic for monkeypox-virus. On August 8 2022, the Netherlands reported its 1000th monkeypox case representing a cumulative incidence of 55 per million population, one of the highest cumulative incidences worldwide. Here we describe the epidemiological characteristics and clinical presentation of the first 1000 monkeypox cases in the Netherlands, within the context of the public health response. Additionally, we explored risk factors for and estimated the protective effect of first-generation smallpox vaccine against more severe MPX. The first 1000 MPX cases, reported between May 20 and August 8 2022, were predominantly MSM aged 31-45 years. The vast majority of infections were acquired through sexual contact with casual partners in private or recreational settings including LGBTQIA+ venues in the Netherlands. This indicates that, although some larger upsurges occurred from point-source and/or travel related events, the outbreak is mainly characterised by sustained transmission within the Netherlands. More severe MPX was associated with having one or more comorbidities as well as having participated in more (3+) different sexual activities 21 days before symptom onset. We found a vaccine effectiveness of the prior first-generation smallpox vaccine against more severe MPX of 58% (95% CI 17-78%), suggesting moderate protection against more severe MPX symptoms on top of any possible protection by this vaccine against MPXV infection and disease.","C. V. van Ewijk, F. Miura, G. Rijckevorsel, H. D. de Vries, M. Welkers, O. E. van den Berg, I. Friesema, P. van den Berg, T. Dalhuisen, J. Wallinga, D. Brandwagt, B. V. van Cleef, H. Vennema, B. Voordouw, M. Koopmans, A. A. van der Eijk, C. Swaan, M. T. te Wierik, T. Leenstra, E. O. D. Op de Coul, E. Franz, the Dutch Monkeypox Response Team",10.1101/2022.10.20.22281284,https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.10.20.22281284,medRxiv,13,2022,yes,"The study focuses on individuals with monkeypox infection, not healthy adults.",yes,The study reports on vaccine effectiveness against more severe MPX symptoms.,yes,The study is a cohort/descriptive study of the first 1000 cases.,yes,The study involves human cases of monkeypox.,no,"The study describes the population as predominantly MSM aged 31-45 years. It does not explicitly mention inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents; however, the term 'predominantly' suggests there may be others, but doesn't confirm the target groups.",maybe,"The vaccine studied is 'prior first-generation smallpox vaccine', which usually refers to childhood vaccination (given the age range 31-45), but the text does not explicitly detail the timing of vaccination for children/adolescents in this specific cohort.",yes,The study specifically evaluates the first-generation smallpox vaccine.,Exclude,"The study provides evaluation of first-generation smallpox vaccine effectiveness against severe monkeypox in a cohort that was predominantly adult MSM aged 31-45. It fails the target population criterion because it does not demonstrate the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, or children.",Target Population,Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Here we describe the epidemiological characteristics and clinical presentation of the first 1000 monkeypox cases in the Netherlands,""",,"  - ""We found a vaccine effectiveness of the prior first-generation smallpox vaccine against more severe MPX of 58%""",,"  - ""Here we describe the epidemiological characteristics and clinical presentation of the first 1000 monkeypox cases in the Netherlands,""",,"  - ""The first 1000 MPX cases, reported between May 20 and August 8 2022, were predominantly MSM aged 31-45 years.""",,"  - ""The first 1000 MPX cases, reported between May 20 and August 8 2022, were predominantly MSM aged 31-45 years.""",,"  - ""We found a vaccine effectiveness of the prior first-generation smallpox vaccine against more severe MPX of 58%""",,"  - ""estimated the protective effect of first-generation smallpox vaccine against more severe MPX.""",
Neurological Manifestations of Human Monkeypox: A Systematic Review (P11-10.003),"<h3>Objective:</h3> To characterize the neurological manifestations and adverse events related to the monkeypox virus. In an effort to better inform clinicians on the rare but devastating neurological manifestations in human monkeypox. <h3>Background:</h3> The monkeypox virus (MPXV) belongs to the family of orthopoxviruses. MPXV is the causative agent of human monkeypox. Traditionally human monkeypox has been confined to the African continent however a growing number of monkeypox cases have been reported by the Centers of Disease Control (CDC) starting in May 2022. This systematic review aims to characterize the neurological manifestation of human monkeypox. <h3>Design/Methods:</h3> A systematic review was conducted across three databases PubMed/PubMedCentral/MEDLINE, ScienceDirect, and Scopus. The review was conducted in accordance with PRISMA guidelines and was prospectively registered on PROSPERO (Registration ID: CRD42022336088). <h3>Results:</h3> A total of 8 cases are identified describing neurological manifestations secondary to human monkeypox. Five cases of encephalitis are identified and one of conjunctivitis leading to severe vision impairment. Notably, during the current 2022 outbreak two cases of encephalomyelitis have been identified within the United States. Cases involving children are believed to be of greater severity and two deaths were identified. Most cases were managed with high-dose intravenous methylprednisolone and immunomodulators such as IVIG. <h3>Conclusions:</h3> Although rare, neurological manifestations and secondary neuroinflammatory conditions are possible secondary to human monkeypox. Cases in children are likely to carry greater severity with increased morbidity and mortality. As cases continue to emerge through the 2022 global outbreak clinicians must remain vigilant in monitoring for neurological complications from the monkeypox virus. <b>Disclosure:</b> Dr. Srichawla has nothing to disclose. Dr. Garcia-Dominguez has nothing to disclose.","B. Srichawla, Maria Garcia-Dominguez",10.1212/wnl.0000000000201855,https://doi.org/10.1212/wnl.0000000000201855,"Wednesday, April 26",1,2023,yes,"The study includes children, who are not healthy non-pregnant adults.",maybe,"The study reports on manifestations of the virus and complications such as encephalitis and death, but it focuses on the virus manifestations and related adverse events rather than vaccine effectiveness or vaccine-related safety outcomes.",yes,The study is a systematic review.,yes,The study focuses on human cases of monkeypox.,yes,The study explicitly mentions identifying and analyzing cases involving children.,no,The paper focuses on the manifestations of the monkeypox virus itself rather than individuals who received a vaccine during the specified periods. There is no evidence of vaccine administration in the abstract.,no,"The paper characterizes the neurological manifestations and adverse events related to the monkeypox virus infection, but does not discuss vaccinia-based or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This systematic review investigates the neurological manifestations of human monkeypox infection, including data on children. However, it does not evaluate vaccination or vaccine-related outcomes, failing the criteria for vaccine type and vaccination timing.","Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Cases involving children are believed to be of greater severity and two deaths were identified.""",,"  - ""To characterize the neurological manifestations and adverse events related to the monkeypox virus.""
  - ""This systematic review aims to characterize the neurological manifestation of human monkeypox.""",,"  - ""A systematic review was conducted across three databases""",,"  - ""This systematic review aims to characterize the neurological manifestation of human monkeypox.""",,"  - ""Cases involving children are believed to be of greater severity and two deaths were identified.""",,"  - ""To characterize the neurological manifestations and adverse events related to the monkeypox virus.""
