Monkeypox in human pregnancy: an overview,"Monkeypox is a viral zoonosis caused by the monkeypox virus, an enveloped, double-stranded DNA virus belonging to the Orthopoxvirus genus in the Poxviridae family. Monkeypox has become a disease of global public health significance. Pregnant women are unfortunately among the those at an increased risk for exposure to monkeypox because their immune system is altered during pregnancy. They may also be at risk for more severe disease or a worse outcome than others. During pregnancy or while breastfeeding when consideration is given for pre-exposure or postexposure vaccination, nonreplicating (Modivied Vaccinia Ankara – Bavarian Nordic) or minimally replicating (LC16, KM Biologics) vaccines are preferred. The ACAM2000 vaccine is contraindicated in pregnancy because it contains live virus particles that can cause fetal vaccinia and fetal death. There are no data to support the use of tecovirimat in pregnant women. However, no fetal adverse effects were noticed when tecovirimat was used in animal studies.","Sulaiman Muhammad Daneji, A. Aminu, A. Borodo, F. Dayyab",10.1016/j.xagr.2022.100130,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xagr.2022.100130,AJOG Global Reports,5,2022,yes,"The study focuses specifically on monkeypox in pregnant women, making its focus not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The paper discusses established safety outcomes like fetal death in relation to vaccine contraindications, but it does not report new outcomes or data from a primary research cohort or a formal evidence synthesis.",no,"The paper identifies itself as an 'overview', which refers to a narrative review rather than one of the required designs like an RCT, cohort study, or systematic review.",yes,The paper discusses human pregnancy and infection in humans.,no,"While the paper focuses on pregnant women, it is a narrative overview and does not include them as participants in a study.",no,The paper discusses vaccination during the pregnancy and neonatal periods but does not actually observe participants receiving the vaccine as part of a study design.,yes,"The paper specifically mentions vaccinia-based vaccines such as MVA-BN, LC16, and ACAM2000.",Exclude,"This paper is a narrative overview of monkeypox in pregnancy and does not fulfill the required study designs such as primary research or systematic reviews. Furthermore, it does not include participants or report primary data on outcomes, instead providing general summaries of risks and vaccination guidelines.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Pregnant women are unfortunately among the those at an increased risk for exposure to monkeypox""",,"  - ""The ACAM2000 vaccine is contraindicated in pregnancy because it contains live virus particles that can cause fetal vaccinia and fetal death.""",,,,,,,,"  - ""nonreplicating (Modivied Vaccinia Ankara – Bavarian Nordic) or minimally replicating (LC16, KM Biologics) vaccines are preferred.""
  - ""The ACAM2000 vaccine is contraindicated in pregnancy because it contains live virus particles that can cause fetal vaccinia and fetal death.""",,"  - ""nonreplicating (Modivied Vaccinia Ankara – Bavarian Nordic) or minimally replicating (LC16, KM Biologics) vaccines are preferred.""
  - ""The ACAM2000 vaccine is contraindicated in pregnancy because it contains live virus particles that can cause fetal vaccinia and fetal death.""",
Influenza vaccination during pregnancy: a systematic review of effectiveness and adverse effects.,"Review question/objective
Objectives:
1. To synthesise the best available evidence on the effectiveness of influenza vaccination during pregnancy, for pregnant women, her foetus and the child up to six months of age.
2. To synthesise the best available evidence on the types and frequency of adverse events for pregnant women, her foetus and the child up to six months of age, associated with influenza vaccination during pregnancy.
Review questions:
1. What are the beneficial effects of influenza vaccination during pregnancy for pregnant women, her foetus and child up to six months of age?
2. What are the adverse effects of influenza vaccination during pregnancy for pregnant women, her foetus and child up to six months of age?
Inclusion criteria
Types of participants
This study will focus on pregnant women with or without risk factors for complications from influenza infection, their unborn child that is being carried at the time of the vaccination, and children up to the age of 6 months.
Types of intervention(s)
Inactivated influenza vaccination, irrespective of antigenic configuration or adjuvant, administered via any route, any dose, to pregnant women of any trimester.
Studies investigating the monovalent H1N1 vaccine will be assessed separately, as well as in combination with the standard trivalent vaccine.
Comparator: Pregnant women not vaccinated against influenza.
Types of outcomes
•Number and severity of influenza and influenza-like episodes and hospitalisation from influenza for pregnant women and their infant less than six months.
•Adverse effects for pregnant women, including but not limited to local reaction, fever, anaphylaxis, Guillain Barre Syndrome and maternal death.
•Effects on the foetus, including but not limited to spontaneous abortion, stillbirth, premature birth, birth weight, foetal growth and congenital malformation.
•Adverse effects of maternal immunisation on the infant up to six months of age.
The case definition of influenza will be accepted as a collection of respiratory and systemic symptoms as defined by the author, and/or laboratory confirmed influenza using viral isolation and/or serology.
Severity for the mother and infants will be assessed by hospitalisation and/or death, and/or severe disease such as pneumonia.",,10.11124/JBISRIR-2012-264,https://doi.org/10.11124/JBISRIR-2012-264,JBI library of systematic reviews,6,2012,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women and their infants.,yes,The study reports on effectiveness (prevention of influenza) and safety (adverse events).,yes,This is a protocol or review aiming to synthesise evidence (systematic review).,yes,The study focuses on human populations (pregnant women and infants).,yes,The focus is explicitly on pregnant women and children up to 6 months of age.,yes,The study focuses on vaccination 'during pregnancy'.,no,"The study is exclusively about the 'Inactivated influenza vaccination', not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,This systematic review focusing on pregnant women and infants is excluded because it evaluates influenza vaccines rather than the required vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.,Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""This study will focus on pregnant women""
