Has Human Papillomavirus (HPV) Vaccination Prevented Adverse Pregnancy Outcomes? Population-Level Analysis After 8 Years of a National HPV Vaccination Program in Australia.,"BACKGROUND
Human papillomavirus (HPV) infection, and its sequelae of precancerous cervical lesions and their subsequent treatment, have been linked with an increased risk of adverse pregnancy outcomes. Publicly funded HPV vaccination of female adolescents began in Australia in 2007 with initial catch-up to age 26 years.
METHODS
Using data from the National Perinatal Data Collection we compared rates of preterm births and small-for-gestational-age infants born in Australia 2000-2015. We used generalized linear models, assuming a Poisson distribution and log link function, with single-year categories of infant birth year, maternal age, and age-specific HPV vaccination coverage as independent variables.
RESULTS
In maternal cohorts with 60%-80% HPV vaccination coverage as achieved in Australia, there was a relative rate reduction of 3.2% (95% confidence interval, 1.1%-5.3%) in preterm births and 9.8% (8.2% to 11.4%) in small-for-gestational-age infants, after adjustment for infant's birth year and maternal age.
CONCLUSION
This analysis provides provisional population-level evidence of a reduction in adverse pregnancy outcomes in cohorts of women offered HPV vaccination. Confounding by smoking or other variables and/or ecological analysis limitations, however, cannot be excluded. These findings indicate potential broader benefits of HPV vaccination than have been documented to date.","Susan Yuill, Susan Yuill, S. Egger, Megan A. Smith, Megan A. Smith, L. Velentzis, L. Velentzis, L. Velentzis, C. D. Wrede, C. Wrede, D. Bateson, K. Canfell, K. Canfell",10.1093/infdis/jiaa106,https://doi.org/10.1093/infdis/jiaa106,Journal of Infectious Diseases,25,2020,yes,The study focuses on pregnant women and their infants.,no,"The study reports on pregnancy outcomes such as preterm births and small-for-gestational-age infants, but does not report mpox-related outcomes.",yes,The study is a population-level analysis using registries (cohort-style).,yes,The study involves human birth records and maternal data.,yes,The study includes pregnant women and neonates.,yes,The study examines women who were offered vaccination as adolescents/young adults (before or potentially during pregnancy).,no,"The study specifically evaluates the Human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccine, not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the impact of HPV vaccination on pregnancy outcomes using Australian national data. It fails the inclusion criteria because it focuses on the HPV vaccine rather than mpox or smallpox vaccines, and it does not report on mpox-specific effectiveness or safety outcomes.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""Human papillomavirus (HPV) infection, and its sequelae of precancerous cervical lesions and their subsequent treatment, have been linked with an increased risk of adverse pregnancy outcomes.""
