Viral Immunology (VIR)","Jameson Crandell, V. Monteiro, Lauren Pischel, Zhenhao Fang, Luciana Conde, Yi Zhong, Lauren Lawres, M. Breban, C. Vogels, Nathan D. Grubaugh, Lydia A Aoun-Barakat, A. Grifoni, A. Sette, Sidi Chen, Inci Yildirim, Andre M Vale, Saad B. Omer, Carolina Lucas",10.1093/jimmun/vkaf283.326,https://doi.org/10.1093/jimmun/vkaf283.326,Journal of Immunology,0,2025,yes,"The study includes Mpox-infected individuals in addition to vaccine recipients, and therefore is not exclusively limited to healthy adults.",no,"The study reports only immunological surrogate markers, specifically antibody and T-cell reactivity, and does not provide clinical effectiveness data regarding the prevention of infection, hospitalization, or death.",yes,The study is a primary research cohort study examining immune responses in vaccine recipients and convalescents.,yes,The study examines cross-reactive memory 'in humans'.,no,"The study participants are described as being up to 80 years old, and there is no mention of current children, neonates, or pregnant women as participants.",maybe,"Some participants were vaccinated up to 80 years ago, which implies they received the vaccine during infancy or childhood. However, this is not the focus of the study's population comparison.",yes,The study evaluates first-generation (Dryvax) and third-generation (JYNNEOS) vaccines.,Exclude,The paper investigates long-term immune memory in adults vaccinated up to 80 years ago or recovered from Mpox. It fails inclusion because it does not specifically include children or pregnant women as study participants and focuses on immunological markers rather than clinical effectiveness or safety outcomes.,"Relevant Outcomes, Target Population",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""first- (Dryvax) and/or third-generation (JYNNEOS) vaccine recipients (vaccinated 1 week to 80 years ago) and Mpox-infected individuals.""",,"  - ""The vaccines elicited strong VACV- and MPXV-reactive antibodies; VACV-neutralizing antibodies persisted for decades""",,"  - ""first- (Dryvax) and/or third-generation (JYNNEOS) vaccine recipients (vaccinated 1 week to 80 years ago) and Mpox-infected individuals.""",,,,"  - ""sustained VACV neutralization across ages, with strong responses even in 80-year-olds.""",,"  - ""first- (Dryvax) and/or third-generation (JYNNEOS) vaccine recipients (vaccinated 1 week to 80 years ago) and Mpox-infected individuals.""",,"  - ""first- (Dryvax) and/or third-generation (JYNNEOS) vaccine recipients (vaccinated 1 week to 80 years ago) and Mpox-infected individuals.""",
Characterising the background incidence rates of adverse events of special interest for covid-19 vaccines in eight countries: multinational network cohort study,"Abstract Objective To quantify the background incidence rates of 15 prespecified adverse events of special interest (AESIs) associated with covid-19 vaccines. Design Multinational network cohort study. Setting Electronic health records and health claims data from eight countries: Australia, France, Germany, Japan, the Netherlands, Spain, the United Kingdom, and the United States, mapped to a common data model. Participants 126 661 070 people observed for at least 365 days before 1 January 2017, 2018, or 2019 from 13 databases. Main outcome measures Events of interests were 15 prespecified AESIs (non-haemorrhagic and haemorrhagic stroke, acute myocardial infarction, deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, anaphylaxis, Bell’s palsy, myocarditis or pericarditis, narcolepsy, appendicitis, immune thrombocytopenia, disseminated intravascular coagulation, encephalomyelitis (including acute disseminated encephalomyelitis), Guillain-Barré syndrome, and transverse myelitis). Incidence rates of AESIs were stratified by age, sex, and database. Rates were pooled across databases using random effects meta-analyses and classified according to the frequency categories of the Council for International Organizations of Medical Sciences. Results Background rates varied greatly between databases. Deep vein thrombosis ranged from 387 (95% confidence interval 370 to 404) per 100 000 person years in UK CPRD GOLD data to 1443 (1416 to 1470) per 100 000 person years in US IBM MarketScan Multi-State Medicaid data among women aged 65 to 74 years. Some AESIs increased with age. For example, myocardial infarction rates in men increased from 28 (27 to 29) per 100 000 person years among those aged 18-34 years to 1400 (1374 to 1427) per 100 000 person years in those older than 85 years in US Optum electronic health record data. Other AESIs were more common in young people. For example, rates of anaphylaxis among boys and men were 78 (75 to 80) per 100 000 person years in those aged 6-17 years and 8 (6 to 10) per 100 000 person years in those older than 85 years in Optum electronic health record data. Meta-analytic estimates of AESI rates were classified according to age and sex. Conclusion This study found large variations in the observed rates of AESIs by age group and sex, showing the need for stratification or standardisation before using background rates for safety surveillance. Considerable population level heterogeneity in AESI rates was found between databases.","Xintong Li, A. Ostropolets, R. Makadia, A. Shoaibi, G. Rao, A. Sena, E. Martínez-Hernandez, A. Delmestri, K. Verhamme, P. Rijnbeek, T. Duarte-Salles, M. Suchard, P. Ryan, G. Hripcsak, D. Prieto-Alhambra",10.1136/bmj.n1435,https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.n1435,British medical journal,157,2021,yes,"The study includes children and adolescents aged 6-17 years, meaning it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study reports background rates of adverse events of special interest (AESIs) like anaphylaxis or stroke, but these are not the results of mpox vaccination or effectiveness outcomes against mpox.",yes,"The study is a multinational network cohort study, which is a primary research design.",yes,The study is a human study involving over 126 million people.,yes,The study includes participants aged 6-17 years.,no,"The study evaluates background rates using data from before 2019, and participants did not receive mpox or smallpox vaccinations during the observation periods.",no,The study focuses on background rates relevant to COVID-19 vaccines and does not involve vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.,Exclude,"This study is excluded because it characterizes background rates of adverse events for COVID-19 vaccines using data collected prior to 2019 and does not involve mpox or smallpox vaccinations. While it includes pediatric populations, it fails the required criteria for vaccine type and vaccination timing.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""rates of anaphylaxis among boys and men were 78 (75 to 80) per 100 000 person years in those aged 6-17 years""",,"  - ""rates of anaphylaxis among boys and men were 78 (75 to 80) per 100 000 person years in those aged 6-17 years""
