"Mpox Surveillance Based on Rash Characteristics — 13 Emergency Departments, United States, June–December 2023","In 2022, a global mpox outbreak occurred, primarily affecting gay and bisexual men who have sex with men (GBMSM). To screen for mpox's reemergence and investigate potentially unsuspected cases among non-GBMSM, prospective surveillance of patients aged ≥3 months with an mpox-compatible rash (vesicular, pustular, ulcerated, or crusted) was conducted at 13 U.S. emergency departments (EDs) during June-December 2023. Demographic, historical, and illness characteristics were collected using questionnaires and electronic health records. Lesions were tested for monkeypox virus using polymerase chain reaction. Among 196 enrolled persons, the median age was 37.5 years (IQR = 21.0-53.5 years); 39 (19.9%) were aged <16 years, and 108 (55.1%) were male. Among all enrollees, 13 (6.6%) were GBMSM. Overall, approximately one half (46.4%) and one quarter (23.5%) of enrolled persons were non-Hispanic White and non-Hispanic Black or African American, respectively, and 38.8% reported Hispanic or Latino (Hispanic) ethnicity. Unstable housing was reported by 21 (10.7%) enrollees, and 24 (12.2%) lacked health insurance. The prevalence of mpox among ED patients evaluated for an mpox-compatible rash was 1.5% (95% CI = 0.3%-4.4%); all persons with a confirmed mpox diagnosis identified as GBMSM and reported being HIV-negative, not being vaccinated against mpox, and having engaged in sex with one or more partners met through smartphone dating applications. No cases were identified among women, children, or unhoused persons. Clinicians should remain vigilant for mpox and educate persons at risk for mpox about modifying behaviors that increase risk and the importance of receiving 2 appropriately spaced doses of JYNNEOS vaccine to prevent mpox.","C. T. Berdahl, A. Krishnadasan, Kavitha Pathmarajah, Gregory J. Moran, Jesus R. Torres, Matthew C. Waxman, William R. Mower, O. Garner, Lorenzo P Duvergne, A. Rimoin, P. Gorbach, David A. Talan, Brett A. Faine, Jon K. Femling, James Galbraith, Derek Isenberg, J. Jui, Frank LoVecchio, Johanna C. Moore, U. Nandi, Richard E. Rothman, Howard Smithline, Mark T. Steele, Amy M. Stubbs, Sam S. Torbati",10.15585/mmwr.mm7322a1,https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm7322a1,MMWR. Morbidity and mortality weekly report,2,2024,yes,"The study population consists of symptomatic patients with mpox-compatible rashes, not healthy adults. Additionally, it includes children and infants as young as 3 months.",no,"The study reports on the prevalence of mpox infection. However, it does not provide effectiveness or safety data specifically following vaccination for the target populations, as the confirmed mpox cases were all unvaccinated adults and no cases (or vaccination statuses) were reported for children.",yes,"The study is a prospective surveillance study conducted at 13 U.S. emergency departments, which equates to a primary research study (cross-sectional or case series).",yes,This is a human study involving 196 enrolled persons.,yes,The study specifically includes participants aged <16 years.,no,"While demographics and medical history were collected, the study does not report that any participants received the vaccine during childhood or adolescence. It only describes the vaccination status of adult male cases, who were recorded as not being vaccinated.",yes,The study references the JYNNEOS vaccine and collects data on mpox vaccination status.,Exclude,"This prospective surveillance study includes a pediatric cohort, but it fails to report any study participants receiving a vaccine during childhood or adolescence. Because it does not offer safety or effectiveness data specifically for vaccinated individuals within the target populations, it does not meet the specified inclusion requirements.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccination Timing",Vaccination Timing,0.1,"  - ""prospective surveillance of patients aged ≥3 months with an mpox-compatible rash""
