Progress in the evaluation of modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine against mpox.,"Vaccination is one of the tools used against the 2022 outbreak of mpox (formerly known as monkeypox), considered a Public Health Emergency of International Concern by WHO until May, 2023. The third-generation, live, non-replicating, modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine MVA-BN (also known as JYNNEOS) has been approved for use against mpox on the basis of data from animal studies and similar immunogenicity to second-generation vaccines (ACAM2000) in humans. No randomised clinical trials of its efficacy are currently available, and, although real-world data estimate vaccine effectiveness to be around 85%,1–3 immunological correlates of protection are still unknown.","V. Mazzotta, G. Matusali, A. Oliva, F. Maggi, A. Antinori",10.1016/s1473-3099(23)00369-9,https://doi.org/10.1016/s1473-3099(23)00369-9,Lancet. Infectious Diseases (Print),3,2023,maybe,The abstract discusses general human and animal data without specifying that the population is not exclusively healthy adults.,yes,The study mentions effectiveness estimates from real-world data.,no,"The article appears to be a narrative review or commentary on 'progress' rather than primary research or a systematic review, and explicitly states no RCTs are available.",yes,The paper discusses vaccine use and immunogenicity in humans.,no,"There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents in the provided text.",no,The text does not specify vaccination timing within pregnancy or childhood.,yes,The study involves the MVA-BN vaccine.,Exclude,This paper is a narrative progress review regarding the MVA-BN vaccine. It fails to meet the inclusion criteria as it does not target pregnant women or pediatric populations and is not a primary research study.,"Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""approved for use against mpox on the basis of data from animal studies and similar immunogenicity to second-generation vaccines (ACAM2000) in humans.""",,"  - ""real-world data estimate vaccine effectiveness to be around 85%,1–3""",,"  - ""No randomised clinical trials of its efficacy are currently available,""",,"  - ""approved for use against mpox on the basis of data from animal studies and similar immunogenicity to second-generation vaccines (ACAM2000) in humans.""",,"  - ""modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine MVA-BN (also known as JYNNEOS)""",,"  - ""approved for use against mpox on the basis of data from animal studies and similar immunogenicity to second-generation vaccines (ACAM2000) in humans.""",,"  - ""modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine MVA-BN (also known as JYNNEOS)""",
"The emergence of Monkeypox Virus: its complications, connection with smallpox and its future aspects - a short review","Monkeypox Virus (MPXV), a zoonotic orthopox DNA virus related to the smallpox virus, was first identified in humans in 1970. The World Health Organization (WHO) declared monkeypox an ""evolving hazard of moderate public health concern"" on 23 June 2022. Case reports included in this study showed that all patients with typical rashes and risky sexual behaviour should be encouraged to seek immediate medical attention. If a patient has had sexual contact, a diagnosis of monkeypox should be considered even if the patient does not travel to other countries. According to authentic proof from Africa, smallpox immunization is at least 85% successful at avoiding monkeypox. Given the inadequate health infrastructure in Low and Middle-Income Countries (LMIC), it would be extremely challenging to implement vaccination as a regular component of immunization programs. Patients with monkeypox may experience complications, including bacterial superinfection, encephalitis, pneumonia, and conjunctivitis/keratitis. Seldom-recorded complications incorporate bronchopneumonia, spewing, and corneal scarring, leading to lasting visual deficiency. Lowering the risk of transmission from person to person, reducing the risk of zoonotic transmission, preventing monkeypox by limiting the trade in animals, engaging the LGBTQIA community, and vaccination may reduce and control monkeypox.",Dronesh Chettri,10.4081/idhm.2024.388,https://doi.org/10.4081/idhm.2024.388,Infectious Diseases and Herbal Medicine,0,2024,maybe,"The study provides a broad overview of monkeypox in humans and does not state that it is exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults, though it does not explicitly characterize its population further.",yes,The paper reports effectiveness outcomes regarding smallpox immunization and safety-related complications like encephalitis and pneumonia.,no,"The article is self-described as a ""short review"", which typically refers to a narrative review rather than a primary research study or a systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,The study focuses on the emergence of the virus in humans.,no,"The paper content does not mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as included participants or specific focuses of the review.",no,"There is no information regarding the timing of vaccination specifically for children, adolescents, or pregnant women.",yes,The study specifically references smallpox immunization.,Exclude,"This paper is a narrative 'short review' that summarizes general information about monkeypox complications and smallpox vaccine effectiveness. It fails to meet inclusion criteria because it is not a primary study or systematic review, and it does not specifically address or include data for the target populations of pregnant women, children, or adolescents.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""first identified in humans in 1970.""",,"  - ""smallpox immunization is at least 85% successful at avoiding monkeypox.""
  - ""complications, including bacterial superinfection, encephalitis, pneumonia, and conjunctivitis/keratitis.""",,,,"  - ""first identified in humans in 1970.""",,,,,,"  - ""smallpox immunization is at least 85% successful at avoiding monkeypox.""",
Adverse drug reaction profile of third-generation smallpox vaccines used in France during the 2022 monkeypox epidemic,"Due to the start of the monkeypox epidemic in 2022, we retrospectively analyzed the adverse drug reactions (ADRs) reported in France after monkeypox vaccinations with the third-generation smallpox vaccine. Ninety-eight cases, representing 172 ADRs, were reported. ADRs were mostly expected reactogenicity reactions occurring within days after the first dose of vaccine and having a quick favorable outcome. Unexpected facial palsy and vaccination failure are discussed.","Audrey Fresse, Nathalie Massy, Dorine Fournier, Sylvine Pinel, Mathilde Beurrier, M. Antoine, Nadine Petitpain, Pierre Gillet",10.1097/QAD.0000000000003838,https://doi.org/10.1097/QAD.0000000000003838,AIDS (London),0,2024,maybe,The abstract does not provide details on whether the population was exclusively healthy non-pregnant adults or included other groups.,yes,The study reports on adverse drug reactions (safety outcomes).,yes,The study is a retrospective analysis of reported cases (case series).,yes,The study describes clinical adverse reactions in human cases in France.,no,"There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents being included in the 98 cases analyzed.",no,"The training does not indicate that vaccination occurred during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The study evaluates a third-generation smallpox vaccine used for monkeypox.,Exclude,"While the study evaluates safety outcomes (ADRs) of the third-generation smallpox vaccine in France, it does not report the inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents. Consequently, it fails the specific target population and vaccination timing requirements for this review.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""retrospectively analyzed the adverse drug reactions (ADRs) reported in France after monkeypox vaccinations with the third-generation smallpox vaccine.""
