MVA-BN as monkeypox vaccine for healthy and immunocompromised,"Background: Over 100 monkeypox (MPX) cases were reported in Nigeria since 2017, with travelers to the UK, Israel, and Singapore becoming ill and incidents of secondary transmission further raising concerns. MVA-BN (JYNNEOS) is the only approved MPX vaccine, with nonclinical and clinical evidence supporting its safety and efficacy. Methods and materials: In a nonclinical study, cynomolgus macaques were vaccinated with placebo (N = 5), 1 standard MVA-BN dose (N = 6), or 2 standard MVA-BN doses 4 weeks apart (N = 6) before intravenous challenge with MPX virus 5 weeks later. In a Phase 2 clinical study evaluating safety and immunogenicity of MVA-BN in humans with a history of AIDS (CD4 nadir ≤ 200 cells/μL), participants were randomized to receive 2 standard (N = 27) or double doses (N = 29) 4 weeks apart or 2 standard doses on the same schedule with a 12-week booster (N = 31). In both studies, safety was monitored and neutralizing antibody levels and seroconversion (antibody appearance or ≥2× baseline level) assessed. Standard dose was ≥0.5 × 108 TCID50. Results: In macaques, 1 or 2 doses of MVA-BN was 100% protective against MPX compared with those receiving placebo, who all succumbed to infection. A single dose induced a rapid neutralizing response in macaques with 100% seroconversion after 2 weeks. In immunocompromised humans, seroconversion with the standard dose was 60.0% four weeks post-dose 1 and 100% two weeks post-dose 2. Doubling the dose did not strengthen the neutralizing response, but a booster resulted in 1.7-fold higher antibody levels compared to the standard schedule at 12 months. In both macaques and humans, antibody levels post-dose 2 were higher compared with post-dose 1 (by approximately 10- and 20-fold, respectively) and comparable to levels previously correlated with orthopox virus protection. In humans, injection site pain was the most common AE (66.7%) and no vaccine-related SAEs or AEs of special interest were reported. Conclusion: MVA-BN was immunogenic and reached levels considered protective in both macaques and humans. FDA approved MVA-BN (September 2019) for use in adults aged ≥18 years (including immune compromised individuals) at high risk for smallpox or MPX infection.","E. Overton, S. Lawrence, J. Stapleton, H. Weidenthaler, D. Schmidt, K. Nopora, T. Meyer, J. Maclennan, B. Koenen, G. Silbernagl, A. Volkmann, P. Chaplin",10.1016/J.IJID.2020.09.1217,https://doi.org/10.1016/J.IJID.2020.09.1217,International Journal of Infectious Diseases,8,2020,yes,"The study focuses on humans with a history of AIDS (CD4 nadir ≤ 200 cells/μL), which fits the requirement of not being exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports safety outcomes (adverse events, SAEs) in humans.",yes,The study describes a Phase 2 clinical study which is a primary research design.,yes,The study includes a clinical study in humans.,no,"The clinical study was conducted in adults aged ≥18 years and does not mention containing pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"The vaccination was administered to adults aged ≥18 years, not during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,"The study evaluates MVA-BN (JYNNEOS), which is a vaccinia-based mpox vaccine.",Exclude,"While the study evaluates the relevant MVA-BN vaccine and its safety in immunocompromised humans, it specifically focuses on adults aged ≥18 years. It fails the mandatory criteria for including pregnant women or pediatric populations.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Phase 2 clinical study evaluating safety and immunogenicity of MVA-BN in humans with a history of AIDS (CD4 nadir ≤ 200 cells/μL),""",,"  - ""In humans, injection site pain was the most common AE (66.7%) and no vaccine-related SAEs or AEs of special interest were reported.""",,"  - ""Phase 2 clinical study evaluating safety and immunogenicity of MVA-BN in humans with a history of AIDS (CD4 nadir ≤ 200 cells/μL),""",,"  - ""Phase 2 clinical study evaluating safety and immunogenicity of MVA-BN in humans with a history of AIDS (CD4 nadir ≤ 200 cells/μL),""",,"  - ""FDA approved MVA-BN (September 2019) for use in adults aged ≥18 years (including immune compromised individuals)""",,"  - ""FDA approved MVA-BN (September 2019) for use in adults aged ≥18 years (including immune compromised individuals)""",,"  - ""MVA-BN (JYNNEOS) is the only approved MPX vaccine,""",
"Preventing the Next Pandemic: Is Live Vaccine Efficacious against Monkeypox, or Is There a Need for Killed Virus and mRNA Vaccines?","(1) Background: The monkeypox virus (MPV) is a double-stranded DNA virus belonging to the Poxviridae family, Chordopoxvirinae subfamily, and Orthopoxvirus genus. It was called monkeypox because it was first discovered in monkeys, in a Danish laboratory, in 1958. However, the actual reservoir for MPV is still unknown. (2) Methods and Results: We have reviewed the existing literature on the options for Monkeypox virus. There are three available vaccines for orthopoxviruses—ACAM2000, JYNNEOS, and LC16—with the first being a replicating vaccine and the latter being non- or minimally replicating. (3) Conclusions: Smallpox vaccinations previously provided coincidental immunity to MPV. ACAM2000 (a live-attenuated replicating vaccine) and JYNNEOS (a live-attenuated, nonreplicating vaccine) are two US FDA-approved vaccines that can prevent monkeypox. However, ACAM2000 may cause serious side effects, including cardiac problems, whereas JYNNEOS is associated with fewer complications. The recent outbreaks across the globe have once again highlighted the need for constant monitoring and the development of novel prophylactic and therapeutic modalities. Based on available data, there is still a need to develop an effective and safe new generation of vaccines specific for monkeypox that are killed or developed into a mRNA vaccine before monkeypox is declared a pandemic.","A. Abdelaal, A. Reda, B. Lashin, B. Katamesh, A. Brakat, Balqees Al-Manaseer, Sayanika Kaur, Ankush Asija, Nimesh K. Patel, S. Basnyat, A. Rabaan, S. Alhumaid, Hawra Albayat, M. Aljeldah, B. A. Shammari, Amal H Al-Najjar, Ahmed K. Al-Jassem, S. T. AlShurbaji, F. Alshahrani, Ahlam Alynbiawi, Zainab H. Alfaraj, Duaa H. Alfaraj, Ahmed H. Aldawood, Y. Sedhai, Victoria Mumbo, A. Rodríguez-Morales, R. Sah",10.3390/vaccines10091419,https://doi.org/10.3390/vaccines10091419,Vaccines,82,2022,maybe,The article is a general review of literature and does not specify a study population beyond discussing existing vaccines in general terms.,yes,"The paper discusses general safety outcomes (side effects, cardiac problems) and the prevention of monkeypox.",no,The study describes itself as a review of existing literature rather than a systematic review or primary research.,no,"The paper is a literature review, not a primary human study.",no,"There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents as specific participants in this review.",no,"As the study is a general literature review and not a primary study of a specific cohort, it does not confirm vaccination timing for a study population.",yes,"The paper discusses ACAM2000, JYNNEOS, and LC16, which are vaccinia-based vaccines.",Exclude,"This paper is a general literature review discussing the status of monkeypox vaccines rather than a primary research or systematic review study. It fails several population and study design criteria, including the requirement for specific target populations such as children or pregnant women.","Study Design, Study Type, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""We have reviewed the existing literature on the options for Monkeypox virus.""",,"  - ""However, ACAM2000 may cause serious side effects, including cardiac problems, whereas JYNNEOS is associated with fewer complications.""",,"  - ""We have reviewed the existing literature on the options for Monkeypox virus.""",,"  - ""We have reviewed the existing literature on the options for Monkeypox virus.""",,"  - ""We have reviewed the existing literature on the options for Monkeypox virus.""",,"  - ""We have reviewed the existing literature on the options for Monkeypox virus.""",,"  - ""There are three available vaccines for orthopoxviruses—ACAM2000, JYNNEOS, and LC16—with""",
"Outcomes of post-exposure vaccination by modified vaccinia Ankara to prevent mpox (formerly monkeypox): a retrospective observational study in Lyon, France, June to August 2022","Modified vaccinia virus Ankara vaccine (MVA-BN; Bavarian Nordic) is recommended to contacts of mpox cases up to 14 days post-exposure but the effectiveness of this strategy is unknown. Among 108 adults (≥ 18 years old) who received one dose of MVA-BN after exposure to mpox, 11 (10%) cases of breakthrough mpox were observed. Sexual exposure was associated with the risk of breakthrough mpox (p = 0.0179). Samples taken from vaccinated breakthrough mpox cases had similar rates of infectious virus isolation than unvaccinated mpox cases.","Y. Merad, A. Gaymard, L. Cotte, T. Perpoint, Dulce Alfaiate, M. Godinot, A. Becker, O. Cannesson, A. Batalla, Fatima Oria-Yassir, S. Landré, F. Morfin, M. Bouscambert, F. Valour, F. Ader, A. Conrad",10.2807/1560-7917.ES.2022.27.50.2200882,https://doi.org/10.2807/1560-7917.ES.2022.27.50.2200882,Euro surveillance : bulletin Europeen sur les maladies transmissibles = European communicable disease bulletin,24,2022,no,The study is exclusively limited to adults (≥ 18 years old).,yes,"The study reports on breakthrough mpox infections, which is an effectiveness outcome.",yes,This is a retrospective observational study.,yes,The study involves human participants (108 adults).,no,"The study participants are exclusively adults (≥ 18 years old) and does not mention pregnant women, neonates, or children.",no,"The participants received the vaccine as adults, not during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The vaccine used is the Modified vaccinia virus Ankara vaccine (MVA-BN).,Exclude,"The study is excluded because it focuses exclusively on an adult population (≥ 18 years old) and does not include the target populations of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents. This fails the mandatory target population and vaccination timing criteria.