Vaccine effectiveness of 3rd generation mpox vaccines against mpox and disease severity: A systematic review and meta-analysis.,"INTRODUCTION
Before the global mpox outbreak which began in 2022, the real-world vaccine effectiveness (VE) of mpox vaccines was unknown. We quantified the VE in the global population of 3rd generation or later mpox vaccines (MVA-BN, LC16m8, OrthopoxVac) compared with unvaccinated or other vaccinated states for infection, hospitalization and death. VE was stratified by 1-dose and 2-doses and post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP).
METHODS
Studies were included if they measured vaccine efficacy or effectiveness in humans. Animal studies and immunogenicity studies were excluded. MEDLINE, Web of Science, Google Scholar, Embase, MedRxiv and grey literature were searched from January 1st, 1970, with the last search run on November 3, 2023 (Prospero, CRD42022345240). Risk of publication bias was assessed via funnel plots and Egger's test, and study quality via Newcastle-Ottawa scales.
RESULTS
A total of 11,892 records were identified via primary search, 3,223 via citation chasing. Thirty-three studies were identified of 3rd generation vaccines, 32 of which were MVA-BN. Two additional studies were re-analysis of existing data. Most of these studies were focused on gay, bisexual, or other men who have sex with men between the ages of 18-49 in May to October of 2022. VE of 1 dose of MVA-BN was 76% (95%CI 64-88%) from twelve studies. VE of 2 doses was 82% (95%CI 72-92%) from six studies. VE of MVA-BN PEP against mpox was 20% (95%CI -24-65%) from seven studies. All VE are calculated from random effects estimates. 18/33(55%) studies were rated as poor, 3/33(9%) as fair and 12/33(36%) as good. Studies included in the meta-analysis had higher quality: 11/16 (69%) were rated as good quality.
CONCLUSION
Both 1 and 2 doses of MVA-BN are highly effective at preventing mpox. Effectiveness estimates, specifically of PEP are limited by immortal time bias, predominant mode of mpox transmission, and real-world vaccine timing of administration.","Lauren Pischel, Brett A. Martini, Natalle Yu, David Cacesse, Mahder Tracy, K. Kharbanda, Noureen Ahmed, K. Patel, A. Grimshaw, Amyn A. Malik, G. Goshua, Saad B. Omer",10.1016/j.vaccine.2024.06.021,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2024.06.021,Vaccine,84,2024,no,"The study population consists primarily of healthy adults aged 18-49. While it analyzes outcomes like hospitalization, it does not clearly focus on or include populations other than non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on vaccine effectiveness for infection, hospitalization, and death.",yes,The study is a systematic review and meta-analysis.,yes,The study inclusion criteria were limited to human studies.,no,"The abstract specifies that the included studies focused on participants between the ages of 18-49, and there is no mention of data for pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Because the study focuses on adults aged 18-49 and does not report on pediatric or pregnant populations, it does not meet the vaccination timing criteria for those groups.",yes,"The study evaluates 3rd generation mpox vaccines including MVA-BN and LC16m8, which are vaccinia-based.",Exclude,"This is a systematic review and meta-analysis evaluating the effectiveness of third-generation mpox vaccines. However, the study is excluded because the evidence synthesized is derived from adult populations (18-49 years) and does not include the target populations of pregnant women, neonates, or children.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Most of these studies were focused on gay, bisexual, or other men who have sex with men between the ages of 18-49""",,"  - ""quantified the VE in the global population of 3rd generation or later mpox vaccines (MVA-BN, LC16m8, OrthopoxVac) compared with unvaccinated or other vaccinated states for infection, hospitalization and death.""",,,,"  - ""Studies were included if they measured vaccine efficacy or effectiveness in humans.""",,"  - ""Most of these studies were focused on gay, bisexual, or other men who have sex with men between the ages of 18-49""",,"  - ""Most of these studies were focused on gay, bisexual, or other men who have sex with men between the ages of 18-49""",,"  - ""quantified the VE in the global population of 3rd generation or later mpox vaccines (MVA-BN, LC16m8, OrthopoxVac) compared with unvaccinated or other vaccinated states for infection, hospitalization and death.""",
Adverse events following administration to pregnant women of influenza A (H1N1) 2009 monovalent vaccine reported to the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System.,"OBJECTIVE
The objective of the study was to evaluate and summarize reports to the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS), a spontaneous reporting system, in pregnant women who received influenza A (H1N1) 2009 monovalent vaccine to assess for potential vaccine safety problems.
STUDY DESIGN
We reviewed reports of adverse events (AEs) in pregnant women who received 2009-H1N1 vaccines from Oct. 1, 2009, through Feb. 28, 2010.
RESULTS
VAERS received 294 reports of AEs in pregnant women who received 2009-H1N1 vaccine: 288 after inactivated and 6 after the live attenuated vaccines. Two maternal deaths were reported. Fifty-nine women (20.1%) were hospitalized. We verified 131 pregnancy-specific outcomes: 95 spontaneous abortions (<20 weeks); 18 stillbirths (≥20 weeks); 7 preterm deliveries (<37 weeks); 3 threatened abortions; 2 preterm labor; 2 preeclampsia; and 1 each of fetal hydronephrosis, fetal tachycardia, intrauterine growth retardation, and cleft lip.
CONCLUSION
Review of reports to VAERS following H1N1 vaccination in pregnant women did not identify any concerning patterns of maternal or fetal outcomes.
Published by Mosby, Inc.","P. Moro, K. Broder, Y. Zheteyeva, N. Revzina, N. Tepper, D. Kissin, Faith Barash, Jorge E Arana, M. Brantley, H. Ding, J. Singleton, Kimp Walton, P. Haber, Paige W. Lewis, X. Yue, F. Destefano, C. Vellozzi",10.1016/j.ajog.2011.06.047,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajog.2011.06.047,American Journal of Obstetrics and Gynecology,114,2011,yes,The study focuses specifically on pregnant women rather than healthy non-pregnant adults.,no,"The study reports safety outcomes (adverse events) but they are related to the H1N1 influenza vaccine, not mpox.",yes,"The study is a review of reports from a spontaneous reporting system (VAERS), which counts as a primary research case series.",yes,The study involves human clinical data reported to VAERS.,yes,The study's participants are pregnant women.,yes,Participants received the vaccine during pregnancy.,no,"The study evaluates the influenza A (H1N1) 2009 monovalent vaccine, not vaccinia-based or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study is excluded because it evaluates the influenza A (H1N1) vaccine rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines. While it focuses on a relevant population (pregnant women) and reports safety outcomes, it is irrelevant to mpox vaccination.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""evaluate and summarize reports to the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS), a spontaneous reporting system, in pregnant women who received influenza A (H1N1) 2009 monovalent vaccine""
