"Safety of Hepatitis B, Pneumococcal Polysaccharide and Meningococcal Polysaccharide Vaccines in Pregnancy","Immunization during pregnancy has the potential to protect the mother and the newborn from preventable diseases. Current recommendations suggest that inactivated vaccines might be considered during pregnancy when the benefits outweigh the risks. In this review, we aimed to evaluate the safety of hepatitis B (HB) vaccine, pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine (PPSV) and meningococcal polysaccharide vaccine (MPSV) administration during pregnancy by systematically reviewing the available evidence in PubMed and Scopus databases, as well as postmarketing surveillance data (including the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System [VAERS] database). A total of 18 studies were eligible for inclusion in the review. Six studies provided data on HB vaccine, six on PPSV and three on MPSV; three additional studies compared PPSV with MPSV. Additionally, 91 reports on vaccinations of pregnant women were identified from postmarketing surveillance data (88 on HB vaccine, 2 on PPSV, 1 on MPSV). The most common complaints were local reactions, including tenderness and swelling. Overall, immunization during pregnancy did not seem to be associated with a teratogenic effect on the fetus, preterm labour or spontaneous abortion. However, the lack of randomized, placebo-controlled trials, or even large cohort studies, in addition to the inherent limitations of the reviewed observational studies with small statistical power, precluded safe conclusions. Large, prospective, population-based cohort studies are needed to elucidate this issue.","M. Makris, K. Polyzos, M. Mavros, S. Athanasiou, P. Rafailidis, M. Falagas",10.2165/11595670-000000000-00000,https://doi.org/10.2165/11595670-000000000-00000,Drug Safety,44,2012,yes,"The study focuses on pregnant women and their newborns, rather than non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports on safety outcomes such as teratogenic effects, preterm labor, spontaneous abortion, and local reactions.",yes,The study is a systematic review of available evidence and postmarketing surveillance data.,yes,The study evaluates vaccines administered to pregnant women and outcomes in their fetuses/newborns.,yes,The participants are pregnant women.,yes,The vaccination occurred during pregnancy.,no,"The study evaluates hepatitis B, pneumococcal, and meningococcal vaccines, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"While the study evaluates the safety of various vaccines in a relevant target population (pregnant women), it does not include vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines. Consequently, it does not meet the specified vaccine type inclusion criterion.",Vaccine Type,Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""we aimed to evaluate the safety of hepatitis B (HB) vaccine, pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine (PPSV) and meningococcal polysaccharide vaccine (MPSV) administration during pregnancy by systematically reviewing the available evidence""",,"  - ""Overall, immunization during pregnancy did not seem to be associated with a teratogenic effect on the fetus, preterm labour or spontaneous abortion.""",,"  - ""we aimed to evaluate the safety of hepatitis B (HB) vaccine, pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine (PPSV) and meningococcal polysaccharide vaccine (MPSV) administration during pregnancy by systematically reviewing the available evidence""",,"  - ""we aimed to evaluate the safety of hepatitis B (HB) vaccine, pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine (PPSV) and meningococcal polysaccharide vaccine (MPSV) administration during pregnancy by systematically reviewing the available evidence""
