The third generation modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine is effective in preventing the transmission of mpox,"is a zoonotic disease caused by orthopoxvirus and can be transmitted through direct skin/mucosal contact with infected individuals or indirectly by contact with contaminated materials. Mpox has two distinct types, whereas clade I can cause severe complications and death (case fatality rate [CFR]: 1.4% to >10%), clade II is relatively mild and more common (CFR: 0.1% – 3.6%). 1,","Mingwang Shen, Lei Zhang",10.1016/j.lanepe.2024.101052,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.lanepe.2024.101052,The Lancet Regional Health - Europe,0,2024,maybe,"The provided text does not specify the demographic characteristics of the study population, though the title indicates a broad focus on transmission.",yes,The title explicitly mentions evidence regarding the vaccine's effectiveness in 'preventing the transmission of mpox'.,maybe,"While the title suggests a study evaluating vaccine effectiveness, the snippet is limited to introductory background information, making the design (e.g., cohort, RCT, or commentary) unclear.",maybe,"Effectiveness in preventing transmission typically refers to human settings, but the provided text does not explicitly confirm human subjects.",maybe,"There is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents in the provided title or content.",maybe,The snippet does not provide data on when the vaccine was administered relative to pregnancy or childhood stages.,yes,The study specifically evaluates the 'third generation modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine'.,Include,"The paper discusses the effectiveness of the third-generation MVA vaccine in preventing mpox transmission; however, the provided excerpt lacks any evidence regarding the mandatory inclusion of pediatric or pregnant populations. Given the general focus on transmission and the venue, it is highly likely to be a study of the general population or a commentary rather than a study focused on the required subgroups.",,,2.5,,,,,"  - ""clade I can cause severe complications and death (case fatality rate [CFR]: 1.4% to >10%), clade II is relatively mild and more common""",,,,"  - ""can be transmitted through direct skin/mucosal contact with infected individuals or indirectly by contact with contaminated materials.""
