Mpox breakthrough infections: concerns and actions.,"There have been more than 88 000 cases of mpox (formerly known as monkeypox) disease, caused by monkeypox virus (MPXV), since May, 2022. In 2019, the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved the modified vaccinia Ankara–Bavaria Nordic (MVA–BN) vaccine as a two subcutaneous dose regimen to prevent smallpox and mpox infection.1US FDAJYNNEOS. Package insert.https://www.fda.gov/media/131078/downloadDate: March, 2023Date accessed: August 9, 2023Google Scholar During the 2022–23 outbreak, the pooled estimated vaccine effectiveness in preventing mpox infection was 89%.2Xu M Liu C Du Z Bai Y Wang Z Gao C Real-world effectiveness of mpox (monkeypox) vaccines: a systematic review.J Travel Med. 2023; (published online April 11.)https://doi.org/10.1093/jtm/taad048Crossref Scopus (4) Google Scholar Despite that good effectiveness, breakthrough infections can occur, due to various factors, including the host immune response variations or exposure to high viral loads. A recent study in the UK reported that eight of 363 mpox cases assessed were likely to have occurred at least 14 days after receiving a single-dose MVA–BN vaccine.3Bertran M Andrews N Davison C et al.Effectiveness of one dose of MVA–BN smallpox vaccine against mpox in England using the case-coverage method: an observational study.Lancet Infect Dis. 2023; 23: 828-835Summary Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (28) Google Scholar Notably, 32 cases occurred within two weeks of vaccination. In another study,4Hazra A Rusie L Hedberg T Schneider JA Human monkeypox virus infection in the immediate period after receiving modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine.JAMA. 2022; 328: 2064-2067Crossref PubMed Scopus (30) Google Scholar 77% of post-vaccination cases occurred within two weeks. In another study,5Agunbiade S Burton F Muirhead J Whitlock GG Girometti N Clinical characteristics of mpox infection in individuals who received a first dose of modified vaccinia Ankara immunisation.Sex Transm Infect. 2023; 99: 198-199PubMed Google Scholar prodromal symptoms, skin lesions, and the need for symptomatic treatment were similar between cases of mpox diagnosed after vaccination with a single MVA–BN dose and those in individuals who were not vaccinated. It was suggested that these cases were more likely incubating during vaccine administration as there was a short interval (median of 4 days) between vaccination and initial symptoms. Interestingly, three (20%) of 15 diagnosed people were HIV positive. This finding was further supported by studies in the UK3Bertran M Andrews N Davison C et al.Effectiveness of one dose of MVA–BN smallpox vaccine against mpox in England using the case-coverage method: an observational study.Lancet Infect Dis. 2023; 23: 828-835Summary Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (28) Google Scholar and in the USA,4Hazra A Rusie L Hedberg T Schneider JA Human monkeypox virus infection in the immediate period after receiving modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine.JAMA. 2022; 328: 2064-2067Crossref PubMed Scopus (30) Google Scholar which found that 50% and 36% of mpox cases occurring within 2 weeks of vaccination, respectively, were in people who were HIV positive, raising concerns about lower protection among this group. The fact that people with HIV can be immunocompromised and can have an insufficient immune response, as shown by the US study, in which 96% of cases in people with HIV occurring within 2 weeks and all cases in people with HIV occurring 28 days after vaccination were virologically suppressed, supports this hypothesis.4Hazra A Rusie L Hedberg T Schneider JA Human monkeypox virus infection in the immediate period after receiving modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine.JAMA. 2022; 328: 2064-2067Crossref PubMed Scopus (30) Google Scholar A previous trial6Overton ET Stapleton J Frank I et al.Safety and immunogenicity of modified vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic smallpox vaccine in vaccinia-naive and experienced human immunodeficiency virus-infected individuals: an open-label, controlled clinical phase II trial.Open Forum Infect Dis. 2015; 2ofv040Crossref PubMed Scopus (77) Google Scholar comparing the safety of MVA–BN in people with HIV and people without HIV demonstrated significantly higher total antibody titres in people without HIV compared with those living with HIV. Notably, high seropositivity rates were observed in groups after two doses of MVA–BN, supporting the conclusion that immune responses associated with protection were induced in this immunocompromised population.6Overton ET Stapleton J Frank I et al.Safety and immunogenicity of modified vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic smallpox vaccine in vaccinia-naive and experienced human immunodeficiency virus-infected individuals: an open-label, controlled clinical phase II trial.Open Forum Infect Dis. 2015; 2ofv040Crossref PubMed Scopus (77) Google Scholar However, no statistically significant association was found in real-world data when analysing the link between immunosuppression and HIV status and the effectiveness of vaccines.7Merad Y Gaymard A Cotte L et al.Outcomes of post-exposure vaccination by modified vaccinia Ankara to prevent mpox (formerly monkeypox): a retrospective observational study in Lyon, France, June to August 2022.Euro Surveill. 2022; 272200882Crossref PubMed Scopus (9) Google Scholar, 8Wolff Sagy Y Zucker R Hammerman A et al.Real-world effectiveness of a single dose of mpox vaccine in males.Nat Med. 2023; 29: 748-752Crossref PubMed Scopus (49) Google Scholar A retrospective study7Merad Y Gaymard A Cotte L et al.Outcomes of post-exposure vaccination by modified vaccinia Ankara to prevent mpox (formerly monkeypox): a retrospective observational study in Lyon, France, June to August 2022.Euro Surveill. 2022; 272200882Crossref PubMed Scopus (9) Google Scholar assessed the effectiveness of a single dose of MVA–BN vaccine in 108 individuals exposed to mpox within 14 days of receiving the vaccine. Of them, 11 (10%) had breakthrough mpox infection, with mild symptoms around 5 days postvaccination. The authors found that sexual exposure was significantly associated with breakthrough infection, whereas immunosuppression, HIV infection, history of smallpox vaccination, and age did not show a significant association.7Merad Y Gaymard A Cotte L et al.Outcomes of post-exposure vaccination by modified vaccinia Ankara to prevent mpox (formerly monkeypox): a retrospective observational study in Lyon, France, June to August 2022.Euro Surveill. 2022; 272200882Crossref PubMed Scopus (9) Google Scholar Another study8Wolff Sagy Y Zucker R Hammerman A et al.Real-world effectiveness of a single dose of mpox vaccine in males.Nat Med. 2023; 29: 748-752Crossref PubMed Scopus (49) Google Scholar reported an effectiveness rate of 86% for the MVA–BN vaccine in men at high risk of mpox. However, due to limitations in the study design, such as significant characteristics differences between study groups, this effectiveness rate might have been overestimated. The authors found no significant association between HIV pre-exposure prophylaxis, HIV history, recent sexually transmitted infections, and mpox.8Wolff Sagy Y Zucker R Hammerman A et al.Real-world effectiveness of a single dose of mpox vaccine in males.Nat Med. 2023; 29: 748-752Crossref PubMed Scopus (49) Google Scholar Given these findings, breakthrough infection should be considered. Clinical suspicion still needs to be high, even in people who are vaccinated, and both clinicians and patients need to be informed of this. Unlike most cases in individuals who are vaccinated that tend to be mild or moderate, another report9Berens-Riha N De Block T Rutgers J et al.Severe mpox (formerly monkeypox) disease in five patients after recent vaccination with MVA–BN vaccine, Belgium, July to October 2022.Euro Surveill. 2022; 272200894Crossref PubMed Scopus (7) Google Scholar showed five severe breakthrough mpox cases following recent MVA–BN vaccination. All five patients experienced localised anogenital symptoms. Four had proctitis requiring antibiotics and analgesics, including opioids. The two patients who received a second dose had less severe symptoms than the others.9Berens-Riha N De Block T Rutgers J et al.Severe mpox (formerly monkeypox) disease in five patients after recent vaccination with MVA–BN vaccine, Belgium, July to October 2022.Euro Surveill. 2022; 272200894Crossref PubMed Scopus (7) Google Scholar The genetic variations found in the MPXV outbreak viruses might impact the effectiveness of vaccinia virus-based vaccines by affecting immune responses. Therefore, four patients' viral DNA was sequenced and analysed, revealing that the genomes belonged to both existing and distinct MPXV clade IIb lineages. However, no common mutation pattern was identified among the four viral genomes, indicating no clear genomic traits associated with potential immune escape.9Berens-Riha N De Block T Rutgers J et al.Severe mpox (formerly monkeypox) disease in five patients after recent vaccination with MVA–BN vaccine, Belgium, July to October 2022.Euro Surveill. 2022; 272200894Crossref PubMed Scopus (7) Google Scholar A single-centre study10Thy M Peiffer-Smadja N Mailhe M et al.Breakthrough infections after postexposure vaccination against mpox.N Engl J Med. 2022; 387: 2477-2479Crossref PubMed Scopus (25) Google Scholar reported two non-severe proctitis cases within 12 PCR-confirmed breakthrough infections among 276 participants who were vaccinated. The lack of a standardised definition of disease severity might hinder the comparability of studies. Additionally, breakthrough cases will need thorough tests to identify the risk factors, ideally, including taking samples for viral sequencing. It is crucial to emphasise that these isolated breakthrough infections should not be interpreted as undermining the effectiveness of the mpox vaccine in reducing disease burden at a population level;4Hazra A Rusie L Hedberg T Schneider JA Human monkeypox virus infection in the immediate peri","A. M. Taha, A. Rodríguez-Morales, R. Sah",10.1016/s1473-3099(23)00546-7,https://doi.org/10.1016/s1473-3099(23)00546-7,Lancet. Infectious Diseases (Print),5,2023,yes,"The study includes immunocompromised individuals (HIV-positive), so it is not exclusively limited to healthy adults.",yes,The study reports on vaccine effectiveness and safety/breakthrough complications.,no,"The article is a commentary or narrative summary ('concerns and actions') of other studies, not a primary research study (RCT, cohort, etc.) or a formal systematic review/meta-analysis.",yes,The paper discusses findings from human clinical trials and real-world human data.,no,"The study describes populations of 'men at high risk' and HIV-positive individuals but makes no mention of children, adolescents, pregnant women, or neonates.",no,"There is no indication that participants were vaccinated during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence; the focus is on the 2022-23 adult outbreak vaccination campaign.",yes,The study focuses on the MVA-BN vaccine.,Exclude,"This paper is a commentary on breakthrough mpox infections and vaccine effectiveness in adult populations, specifically discussing HIV-positive individuals and men at high risk. It fails several inclusion criteria as it is not a primary research or systematic review study, and it does not include pregnant women, children, or adolescents.","Study Design, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Safety and immunogenicity of modified vaccinia Ankara-Bavarian Nordic smallpox vaccine in vaccinia-naive and experienced human immunodeficiency virus-infected individuals:""
