Clinical and Immunological Response to Attenuated Tissue-Cultured Smallpox Vaccine LC 16 m 8,"THE THREAT OF SMALLPOX BIOterrorism has prompted reconsideration of the need for smallpox vaccination. Serious adverse events associated with firstgeneration vaccines such as the New York City Board of Health (Dryvax; Wyeth, Madison, New Jersey), Lister, and Ikeda strains have raised obstacles to vaccination campaigns in the United States. Second-generation vaccines such as ACAM2000 (Acambis, Cambridge, Massachusetts) that use a first-generation seed virus but are grown in tissue culture are also usually accompanied by a high frequency of adverse events. Developing a vaccine that is safer than first-generation vaccines yet highly immunogenic is crucial to constructing a prevention plan in the event of bioterrorist attack. LC16m8 is a live, attenuated, tissuecultured third-generation vaccine comprising attenuated vaccinia virus strains as well as subunit vaccines made from viral proteins or DNA. It is a desirable candidate for routine vaccination because of its low reactogenicity and reasonable safety profile. Vaccination can be conveniently accomplished with a single intraepidermal scarification alone, which usually results in a visible major skin reaction (“take”) similar to those resulting from firstand second-generation vaccines. LC16m8 was derived from the Lister strain used for the Intensified Smallpox Eradication Programme of the World Health Organization by temperature sensitivity and pock size See also Patient Page. Author Affiliations: Bio-preparedness Research Laboratory, Department of Tropical Medicine and Parasitology, School of Medicine, Keio University, Tokyo, Japan (Drs Saito and Takeuchi); Department of Internal Medicine (Dr Fujii) and Center for Health Management (Dr Kuwabara), Japan SelfDefense Forces Central Hospital, Tokyo; Research Institute, National Defense Medical College, Saitama, Japan (Dr Kanatani); Department of Virology I, National Institute of Infectious Diseases, Tokyo (Drs Saijo and Morikawa); and Chemo-SeroTherapeutic Research Institute (Kaketsuken), Kumamoto, Japan (Mr Yokote). Corresponding Author: Yasuhiro Kanatani, MD, PhD, National Defense Medical College, 3-2 Namiki, Tokorozawa-city, Saitama 359-8513, Japan (ykanatan @ndmc.ac.jp). Context The attenuated, tissue-cultured, third-generation smallpox vaccine LC16m8 was administered to vaccinia-naive infants in Japan during the 1970s without serious adverse events. It is a good candidate for use as part of a prevention plan for bioterrorism.","Tomoya Saito, T. Fujii, Y. Kanatani, M. Saijo, S. Morikawa, H. Yokote, T. Takeuchi, Noriyuki Kuwabara",,,,17,2009,no,The current study described in the title and affiliated with the Japan Self-Defense Forces Central Hospital appears to be exclusively limited to healthy adults. Historical data on infants is only mentioned as background context.,yes,The paper reports on serious adverse events and the clinical/immunological response to the vaccine.,yes,"The title indicates a study evaluating 'Clinical and Immunological Response', which constitutes primary research.",yes,"The study involves human participants, specifically evaluating the response to a vaccine and referencing historical use in infants.",no,"While the paper mentions that the vaccine was administered to infants in the 1970s, these historical subjects are background context. The current study is being conducted at the Japan Self-Defense Forces Central Hospital, implying adult recruitment.",no,"There is no evidence the participants in the current study received the vaccine during the specified periods (childhood, pregnancy, etc.), as the setting suggests recruitment of adult personnel.",yes,"The study evaluates the LC16m8 vaccine, which is described as a third-generation smallpox vaccine.",Exclude,"Although the paper mentions the historical safety of the LC16m8 smallpox vaccine in infants during the 1970s, the clinical research described in the title is affiliated with the Japan Self-Defense Forces Central Hospital, indicating that its participants are healthy adults rather than the required pediatric or pregnant populations.","Population Focus, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Population Focus,0.1,"  - ""Japan SelfDefense Forces Central Hospital,""
