"Adverse Events Following Measles, Mumps, and Rubella Vaccine in Adults Reported to the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS), 2003-2013.","BACKGROUND
Limited data exist on the safety of the measles, mumps, and rubella (MMR) vaccine in adults. We reviewed reports of adverse events (AEs) to the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS) to assess safety in this previously understudied group.
METHODS
VAERS is the national spontaneous vaccine safety surveillance system coadministered by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention and the US Food and Drug Administration. We searched the VAERS database for US reports of adults aged ≥19 years who received the MMR vaccine from 1 January 2003 to 31 July 2013. We clinically reviewed reports and available medical records for serious AEs, pregnancy reports, and reports for selected prespecified outcomes.
RESULTS
During this period, VAERS received 3175 US reports after MMR vaccine in adults. Of these, 168 (5%) were classified as serious, including 7 reports of death. Females accounted for 77% of reports. The most common signs and symptoms for all reports were pyrexia (19%), rash (17%), pain (13%), and arthralgia (13%). We did not detect any new safety findings in empirical Bayesian data mining. We identified 131 reports of MMR vaccine administered to a pregnant woman; the majority of these vaccinations were in the first trimester and in 83 (62%), no AE was reported.
CONCLUSIONS
In our review of VAERS data, we did not detect any new or unexpected safety concerns for MMR vaccination in adults. We identified reports of pregnant women exposed to MMR, which is a group in whom the vaccine is contraindicated, suggesting the need for continued provider education on vaccine recommendations and screening.","Lakshmi Sukumaran, M. McNeil, P. Moro, Paige W. Lewis, Scott K. Winiecki, T. Shimabukuro",10.1093/cid/civ061,https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/civ061,Clinical Infectious Diseases,50,2015,yes,"The study includes pregnant women, meaning it is not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"While the study reports safety outcomes (adverse events), they are specific to the MMR vaccine rather than mpox prevention, hospitalization, or death related to mpox.",yes,"The study is a review of VAERS database reports, which constitutes primary research in the form of a case series or surveillance study.",yes,The study involves human participants reported to VAERS.,yes,The study explicitly includes reports of vaccinations in pregnant women.,yes,The study identified 131 reports where the vaccine was administered to women during their pregnancy.,no,"The study focuses exclusively on the measles, mumps, and rubella (MMR) vaccine, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study is excluded because it investigates the safety of the MMR vaccine rather than vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines. Although it includes a target population (pregnant women receiving the vaccine during pregnancy), it does not meet the specified vaccine type or disease outcome requirements.","Relevant Outcomes, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""131 reports of MMR vaccine administered to a pregnant woman;""",,"  - ""reviewed reports of adverse events (AEs)""
