"Safety Monitoring of JYNNEOS Vaccine During the 2022 Mpox Outbreak — United States, May 22–October 21, 2022","JYNNEOS (Modified Vaccinia Ankara vaccine, Bavarian Nordic) is recommended in the United States for persons exposed to or at high risk for exposure to Monkeypox virus during the 2022 monkeypox (mpox) outbreak (1). JYNNEOS is a live, nonreplicating viral vaccine licensed for the prevention of smallpox and mpox in adults aged ≥18 years, administered as a 0.5-mL 2-dose series given 28 days apart by subcutaneous injection (2). On August 9, 2022, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) issued an Emergency Use Authorization (EUA) for administration of 0.1 mL doses by intradermal injection for adults aged ≥18 years as a strategy to increase vaccine supply, and administration of 0.5 mL doses subcutaneously for persons aged <18 years (3). During May 22-October 21, 2022, a total of 987,294 JYNNEOS vaccine doses were administered in the United States. CDC has monitored JYNNEOS vaccine safety using the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS) and the Vaccine Safety Datalink (VSD) for vaccine recipients of all ages, and through single-patient emergency Investigational New Drug (EIND) procedures for persons aged <18 years vaccinated before August 9, 2022. The most common adverse health events reported to VAERS for adults were nonserious and included injection site reactions, which was consistent with the prelicensure studies. Adverse health events were reported at similar rates for doses received by intradermal and subcutaneous administration. Serious adverse events were rare in adults, and no serious adverse events have been identified among persons aged <18 years. Overall, postlicensure and postauthorization surveillance to date support JYNNEOS vaccine safety.","J. Duffy, P. Marquez, P. Moro, Eric S. Weintraub, Yon C. Yu, Peter Boersma, J. Donahue, J. Glanz, Kristin Goddard, S. Hambidge, Bruno J Lewin, N. Lewis, Douglas Rouse, T. Shimabukuro",10.15585/mmwr.mm7149a4,https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm7149a4,MMWR. Morbidity and mortality weekly report,53,2022,yes,"The study includes children and adolescents under 18 years of age, and is not limited to healthy adults.",yes,The study reports on adverse health events and safety outcomes.,yes,The study is a post-authorization safety surveillance report (primary research).,yes,The study involves human vaccine recipients.,yes,The study explicitly includes persons aged <18 years.,yes,The participants aged <18 years received the vaccine during childhood or adolescence during the 2022 outbreak.,yes,The study involves the JYNNEOS (Modified Vaccinia Ankara) vaccine.,Include,This study provides safety surveillance data for the JYNNEOS vaccine in both adults and children <18 years of age. It specifically reports on the lack of serious adverse events in the pediatric population and focuses on vaccinia-based mpox vaccination.,,,5,"  - ""CDC has monitored JYNNEOS vaccine safety using the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS) and the Vaccine Safety Datalink (VSD) for vaccine recipients of all ages, and through single-patient emergency Investigational New Drug (EIND) procedures for persons aged <18 years""",,"  - ""no serious adverse events have been identified among persons aged <18 years.""
  - ""The most common adverse health events reported to VAERS for adults were nonserious and included injection site reactions,""",,"  - ""postlicensure and postauthorization surveillance""",,"  - ""CDC has monitored JYNNEOS vaccine safety using the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS) and the Vaccine Safety Datalink (VSD) for vaccine recipients of all ages, and through single-patient emergency Investigational New Drug (EIND) procedures for persons aged <18 years""",,"  - ""CDC has monitored JYNNEOS vaccine safety using the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS) and the Vaccine Safety Datalink (VSD) for vaccine recipients of all ages, and through single-patient emergency Investigational New Drug (EIND) procedures for persons aged <18 years""
