"Evaluation of Public Health Contact Tracing for Mpox Among Gay, Bisexual, and Other Men Who Have Sex With Men-10 US Jurisdictions, May 17-July 31, 2022.","Objectives. To examine the potential impact of contact tracing to identify contacts and prevent mpox transmission among gay, bisexual, and other men who have sex with men (MSM) as the outbreak expanded. Methods. We assessed contact tracing outcomes from 10 US jurisdictions before and after access to the mpox vaccine was expanded from postexposure prophylaxis for persons with known exposure to include persons at high risk for acquisition (May 17-June 30, 2022, and July 1-31, 2022, respectively). Results. Overall, 1986 mpox cases were reported in MSM from included jurisdictions (240 before expanded vaccine access; 1746 after expanded vaccine access). Most MSM with mpox were interviewed (95.0% before vaccine expansion and 97.0% after vaccine expansion); the proportion who named at least 1 contact decreased during the 2 time periods (74.6% to 38.9%). Conclusions. During the period when mpox cases among MSM increased and vaccine access expanded, contact tracing became less efficient at identifying exposed contacts. Public Health Implications. Contact tracing was more effective at identifying persons exposed to mpox in MSM sexual and social networks when case numbers were low, and it could be used to facilitate vaccine access. (Am J Public Health. Published online ahead of print May 4, 2023:e1-e4. https://doi.org/10.2105/AJPH.2023.307301).","A. Cope, R. Kirkcaldy, P. Weidle, D. A. Jackson, Nicholas Laramee, Robyn Weber, Julie L. Rowse, Anil T. Mangla, Brian Fox, Katharine E. Saunders, Kristen Taniguchi, Lauren Usagawa, Megan E. Cahill, P. Harrington, Erin K. Ricketts, Khalil Harbi, L. Malec, Tingting Gu Templin, D. Drociuk, Terri Hannibal, R. Klos, K. Bernstein",10.2105/AJPH.2023.307301,https://doi.org/10.2105/AJPH.2023.307301,American Journal of Public Health,8,2023,yes,"The study focuses on gay, bisexual, and other men who have sex with men (MSM). While it does not explicitly exclude non-pregnant adults, the primary focus is a specific high-risk adult population, and the study doesn't indicate it is exclusively limited to healthy individuals, as it includes mpox cases.",no,"The study reports on contact tracing outcomes and potential prevention of transmission, rather than specific clinical effectiveness outcomes (like rates of infection prevention post-vaccination) or safety outcomes for the vaccine itself.",yes,"The study is a primary research piece, specifically an evaluation of contact tracing outcomes across 10 jurisdictions.",yes,The study involves human jurisdictions and case reports of MSM.,no,"The abstract specifies gay, bisexual, and other MSM and does not mention pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"There is no information suggests that any participants were vaccinated during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence. It focuses on adult sexual and social networks.",maybe,"While the study mentions mpox vaccine expansion, it does not specifically evaluate the vaccine effects or types in the target populations of interest (children/pregnant women).",Exclude,This study evaluates public health contact tracing among MSM in the US. It fails several required criteria as it does not include the target population of pregnant women or children and focuses on contact tracing efficiency rather than vaccine effectiveness or safety outcomes.,"Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""To examine the potential impact of contact tracing to identify contacts and prevent mpox transmission among gay, bisexual, and other men who have sex with men (MSM)""
