Protecting pregnancy during pandemics: What recent outbreaks teach us,"ABSTRACT Public health emergences often place pregnant people at a greater risk of severe disease. In this commentary, we highlight some of the insights from recent public health emergencies, spanning the 2009 influenza A/H1N1 pandemic to the recent mpox outbreaks. These epidemics have resulted in a large amount of perinatal pharmacovigilance expertise and capacity, improving the ability to conduct post-authorization evaluation of the safety of vaccines and other therapeutics during pregnancy. Although pregnant people have not historically been included in the clinical development of vaccines and other therapeutics intended for the general population, experiences from more recent public health emergencies show this trend is changing, with ongoing clinical trials either including or specifically targeting pregnant people. Pandemics and other public health emergencies that strain the healthcare system can disrupt routine prenatal care, birthing, and post-delivery care practices and this should be considered as part of pandemic planning. Finally, experiences from several epidemics show that vaccination during pregnancy is achievable, but vaccination rates during pregnancy are often suboptimal. A better understanding of vaccine hesitancy and acceptance during pregnancy and evidence-based strategies to address such hesitancy remain needed to better protect pregnant people against emerging infectious disease threats.","Annette K. Regan, Flor M. Muñoz",10.1080/21645515.2025.2595779,https://doi.org/10.1080/21645515.2025.2595779,Human Vaccines & Immunotherapeutics,0,2025,yes,The study explicitly focuses on pregnant people and the risks they face during pandemics.,no,"While the paper discusses the capacity to evaluate safety and effectiveness outcomes during pregnancy, as a commentary, it does not report primary data or systematic synthesis of safety outcomes or mpox prevention effectiveness.",no,"The authors state this is a 'commentary', which is not a primary research study (e.g., RCT, cohort) or a systematic review as required.",yes,The focus is on human pregnancy and public health emergencies affecting people.,yes,The study's primary focus is on pregnant people.,yes,The text discusses vaccination during pregnancy.,yes,The text mentions recent mpox outbreaks and the evaluation of vaccines during these periods.,Exclude,"This article is a commentary discussing insights from recent pandemics, including mpox, specifically regarding pregnant people. It fails the inclusion criteria because it is not a primary research study or a systematic review and does not report primary outcomes for vaccine safety or effectiveness.","Relevant Outcomes, Study Design",Study Design,0.1,"  - ""In this commentary, we highlight some of the insights from recent public health emergencies,""
