Levels of antibodies against the monkeypox virus compared by HIV status and historical smallpox vaccinations: a serological study,"ABSTRACT Men who have sex with men and people living with HIV are disproportionately affected in the 2022 multi-country monkeypox epidemic. The smallpox vaccine can induce cross-reactive antibodies against the monkeypox virus (MPXV) and reduce the risk of infection. Data on antibodies against MPXV induced by historic smallpox vaccination in people with HIV are scarce. In this observational study, plasma samples were collected from people living with and without HIV in Shenzhen, China. We measured antibodies binding to two representative proteins of vaccinia virus (VACV; A27L and A33R) and homologous proteins of MPXV (A29L and A35R) using an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay. We compared the levels of these antibodies between people living with and without HIV. Stratified analyses were performed based on the year of birth of 1981 when the smallpox vaccination was stopped in China. Plasma samples from 677 people living with HIV and 746 people without HIV were tested. A consistent pattern was identified among the four antibodies, regardless of HIV status. VACV antigen-reactive and MPXV antigen-reactive antibodies induced by historic smallpox vaccination were detectable in the people born before 1981, and antibody levels reached a nadir during or after 1981. The levels of smallpox vaccine-induced antibodies were comparable between people living with HIV and those without HIV. Our findings suggest that the antibody levels against MPXV decreased in both people living with and without HIV due to the cessation of smallpox vaccination.","Dapeng Li, Haiyan Wang, Liqin Sun, Jiamin Feng, Wenting Li, Lin Cheng, X. Liao, Yang Zhang, Zhongxiang Xu, X. Ge, Bing Zhou, Juanjuan Zhao, B. Ju, Hongzhou Lu, Zheng Zhang",10.1080/22221751.2024.2356153,https://doi.org/10.1080/22221751.2024.2356153,Emerging Microbes and Infections,9,2024,no,"The study population consists of adults born around or before 1981, as it analyzes the long-term effects of historic smallpox vaccination in China before it was stopped in 1981. There is no indication that the study focuses on anything other than healthy or HIV-positive adults.",no,"The study measures antibody levels (serology), not clinical outcomes such as infection prevention, hospitalization, death, or safety adverse events.",yes,This is an observational study using plasma samples from participants.,yes,The study involved human participants from whom plasma samples were collected.,no,"The participants were categorized by birth year (pre- and post-1981) to assess the effects of a vaccination program that ended in 1981. This implies the participants are adults (born in/before 1981 would be 40+ years old), and there is no mention of pregnant women, neonates, or children being included in the current study sample.",no,"The study assesses antibodies in adults who received vaccines decades ago (prior to 1981). While some may have received it as children, the current study does not explicitly monitor a pediatric or adolescent population during or immediately following vaccination, nor does it include pregnancy-related timing.",yes,The study evaluates antibodies induced by historic smallpox vaccination.,Exclude,"This is a serological study evaluating antibody levels in adults based on their HIV status and historical smallpox vaccination status prior to 1981. It fails several required criteria, most notably the exclusion of the target populations (pregnant women, children, neonates) and the lack of primary clinical effectiveness or safety outcomes.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Stratified analyses were performed based on the year of birth of 1981 when the smallpox vaccination was stopped in China.""
