"Ischemic cardiac events during the Department of Health and Human Services Smallpox Vaccination Program, 2003.","Ten ischemic cardiac events (ICEs) were reported among 37,901 initial US Department of Health and Human Services (DHHS) smallpox vaccinees. Symptoms developed a median of 10 days after vaccination (range, 0-28 days). The median age of case patients was 56 years (range, 42-65 years), and 60% were male. Seven (70%) of the case patients had >/=3 cardiac risk factors or probable coronary artery disease before vaccination. Two women, 55 and 57 years of age, experienced acute myocardial infarction and fatal cardiac arrests. Background rates of ICEs during a 3-week period for civilian populations that were age and sex matched to DHHS vaccinees were estimated. The observed number of myocardial infarctions exceeded estimated expectations (5 vs. 2) but remained within the 95% predictive interval (PI) (0.6-5.4). New onset angina was observed significantly less frequently than estimated expectations (1 vs. 10; 95% PI, 3.5-15.7). After persons with >/=3 cardiac risk factors or known heart disease were deferred from vaccination, no ICEs were reported among an additional 6638 vaccinees.","D. Swerdlow, M. Roper, J. Morgan, R. Schieber, L. Sperling, Mercedes M Sniadack, L. Neff, Jacqueline W. Miller, C. R. Curtis, M. Marín, J. Iskander, P. Moro, P. Hightower, N. Levine, M. McCauley, J. Heffelfinger, I. Damon, T. Török, M. Wharton, E. Mast, G. Mootrey",10.1086/524745,https://doi.org/10.1086/524745,Clinical Infectious Diseases,20,2008,yes,"The study population consists of adults with varying health statuses, including many with multiple cardiac risk factors or coronary artery disease, which means the study is not exclusively limited to healthy individuals.",yes,"The study reports safety outcomes, specifically ischemic cardiac events such as myocardial infarction and angina.",yes,This is a primary research study analyzing data from a vaccination program cohort.,yes,The study involves human participants within the US DHHS smallpox vaccination program.,no,"The study focuses on adults in the DHHS vaccination program; the reported age range of case patients was 42-65 years, and there is no evidence of inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Participants received the vaccine as adults during the 2003 DHHS program, not during pregnancy, the neonatal period, or childhood.",yes,The study evaluates outcomes for the smallpox vaccine.,Exclude,"This study monitors ischemic cardiac events in a cohort of 37,901 adult smallpox vaccinees. It fails the inclusion criteria because the participants were exclusively adults (age 42-65 in reported cases) and the study did not include pregnant women, children, or adolescents.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""Seven (70%) of the case patients had >/=3 cardiac risk factors or probable coronary artery disease before vaccination.""",,"  - ""Ten ischemic cardiac events (ICEs) were reported among 37,901 initial US Department of Health and Human Services (DHHS) smallpox vaccinees.""",,"  - ""Ten ischemic cardiac events (ICEs) were reported among 37,901 initial US Department of Health and Human Services (DHHS) smallpox vaccinees.""",,"  - ""Ten ischemic cardiac events (ICEs) were reported among 37,901 initial US Department of Health and Human Services (DHHS) smallpox vaccinees.""",,"  - ""The median age of case patients was 56 years (range, 42-65 years),""",,"  - ""Ten ischemic cardiac events (ICEs) were reported among 37,901 initial US Department of Health and Human Services (DHHS) smallpox vaccinees.""
