The effect of human papillomavirus vaccination during adolescence or early adulthood on incidence of spontaneous abortion in adulthood.,"While human papillomavirus (HPV) infection has been associated with spontaneous abortion (SAB), there are limited data on HPV vaccination and SAB risk. We analyzed data from Pregnancy Study Online (PRESTO), a North American preconception cohort study (2013-2025). We followed 11,770 females aged 21-45 years for 12 months or until pregnancy, whichever came first. On baseline questionnaires, participants reported their HPV vaccination status and age at HPV vaccination. Participants reported pregnancy outcomes on follow-up questionnaires. We used Cox regression to estimate hazard ratios (HRs) and 95 % confidence intervals (CIs) adjusted for potential confounders. The prevalence of HPV vaccination was 52.9 %, and 20.4 % of participants experienced SAB. There was no appreciable association between HPV vaccination and SAB overall (HR = 0.95, 95 % CI: 0.87-1.04) or by age at vaccination (<18 years: HR = 1.03, 95 % CI: 0.92-1.16; ≥18 years: HR = 0.90, 95 % CI: 0.81-1.00). Results did not vary appreciably by timing of SAB (<6 vs. ≥6 weeks).","Alexandra Hunt, Annette K. Regan, Rebecca Perkins, Lauren A Wise",10.1016/j.vaccine.2025.128152,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.vaccine.2025.128152,Vaccine,0,2025,yes,"The study includes participants who were pregnant, as evidenced by the tracking of spontaneous abortion (SAB) outcomes, and excludes the study from being limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,"The study reports safety outcomes in the form of spontaneous abortion (SAB), which is a pregnancy complication.",yes,"The study is a North American preconception cohort study, which is a type of primary research study.",yes,"The study involves human participants (11,770 females).",yes,The study includes pregnant women (indicated by reporting on SAB outcomes) and individuals who received vaccination during adolescence (<18 years).,no,Participants in the study received the vaccine during adolescence (at age <18 years).,no,"The study explicitly focuses on the human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccine, not vaccinia-based mpox or historical smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study is excluded from the review because it investigates the safety of the human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccine, failing the criteria regarding vaccine type. While it includes relevant populations such as pregnant women and those vaccinated during adolescence, the intervention is not an mpox or smallpox vaccine.","Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""20.4 % of participants experienced SAB.""",,"  - ""20.4 % of participants experienced SAB.""",,,,"  - ""We followed 11,770 females aged 21-45 years""",,"  - ""20.4 % of participants experienced SAB.""",,,,,
"Transmission of vaccinia virus, possibly through sexual contact, to a woman at high risk for adverse complications.","Severe adverse events, including eczema vaccinatum (EV), can result after smallpox vaccination. Persons at risk for EV include those with underlying dermatologic conditions, such as atopic dermatitis. We investigated a case of vaccinia infection, possibly acquired during sexual contact with a recently vaccinated military service member, in a female Maryland resident with atopic dermatitis. The U.S. Department of Defense's Vaccine Healthcare Centers Network (VHCN) and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) worked in conjunction with the patient's physician and the Maryland Department of Health and Mental Hygiene (DHMH) to confirm the diagnosis, ensure treatment, and prevent further transmission. Specimens collected from the patient were tested at the DHMH laboratories and were positive by real-time polymerase chain reaction for nonvariola orthopoxvirus. Testing at the CDC verified the presence of vaccinia-specific DNA signatures. Continuing spread of the patient's lesions led to the administration of vaccinia immune globulin and strict infection control measures to prevent tertiary transmission to vulnerable family members, also with atopic dermatitis. VHCN contacted the service member to reinforce vaccination site care and hygiene. This case underscores the importance of prevaccination education for those receiving the smallpox vaccine to protect contacts at risk for developing severe adverse reactions.","M. Said, Charles Haile, Venkataraman Palabindala, Naomi Barker, R. Myers, Ruth J Thompson, L. Wilson, Frances Allan-Martinez, Jay R. Montgomery, B. Monroe, D. Tack, M. Reynolds, I. Damon, D. Blythe",10.7205/MILMED-D-13-00233,https://doi.org/10.7205/MILMED-D-13-00233,Military Medicine,9,2013,yes,The study focuses on a female with atopic dermatitis who experienced an adverse event from secondary transmission.,yes,The study reports on the transmission of vaccinia virus and subsequent clinical complications (lesions) requiring treatment.,yes,This is a case report of a specific patient's infection and treatment.,yes,The study describes a clinical case involving human residents and family members.,no,"While the paper mentions 'vulnerable family members', the primary subject is described only as a 'Maryland resident' or 'woman' with no indication that she or other participants were pregnant, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"The study involves secondary transmission from a vaccinated military service member. There is no evidence any participant received a vaccine during pregnancy, childhood, or adolescence.",yes,The study involves the vaccinia virus from a smallpox vaccination.,Exclude,"This is a case report describing secondary transmission of vaccinia virus to an adult female with atopic dermatitis. It fails the target population criteria because it does not include pregnant women, children, or adolescents.","Target Population, Vaccination Timing",Target Population,0.1,"  - ""investigated a case of vaccinia infection, possibly acquired during sexual contact with a recently vaccinated military service member, in a female Maryland resident with atopic dermatitis.""",,"  - ""investigated a case of vaccinia infection, possibly acquired during sexual contact with a recently vaccinated military service member, in a female Maryland resident with atopic dermatitis.""
  - ""Continuing spread of the patient's lesions led to the administration of vaccinia immune globulin and strict infection control measures""",,"  - ""investigated a case of vaccinia infection, possibly acquired during sexual contact with a recently vaccinated military service member, in a female Maryland resident with atopic dermatitis.""
  - ""This case underscores the importance of prevaccination education""",,"  - ""investigated a case of vaccinia infection, possibly acquired during sexual contact with a recently vaccinated military service member, in a female Maryland resident with atopic dermatitis.""
