Smallpox Vaccination: a Review of Adverse Events for Contemporary Health Care Providers,"This chapter deals with the smallpox vaccine, its benefits, its risks, and the current state of its use. To ensure the future availability of appropriate amounts of smallpox vaccine, the U.S. government has funded the manufacture of tissue culture vaccine; the most likely candidate is an Acambis/Baxter Laboratories’ product, grown in Vero monkey kidney cell culture. It is critical to emphasize that the vast majority of vaccinees (nearly all) undergo vaccination without serious complications. The potential vulnerabilities to adverse events after smallpox vaccination are described. It is known that in the era when smallpox vaccination was routine, many patients with hypogammaglobulinemia but with intact T-cell function underwent smallpox vaccination without incidents, presumably because their T-cell immunity was sufficient to contain the virus and to rid the body of infected skin cells, preventing further spread to other areas of the body. It also known that a few individuals with progressive vaccinia virus infection had a lack of T-cell function but intact antibody capacity to various degrees. These individuals had lesser degrees of progression, often without viremic spread. Due to our lack of knowledge of the degree of susceptibility, if any, of individuals with non-T-cell immunodeficiencies, the recommendation has evolved to exclude such individuals from elective smallpox vaccination. The minimal reactions are split into noninfectious consequences of smallpox vaccination and infectious complications.",V. Fulginiti,10.1128/9781555815585.CH18,https://doi.org/10.1128/9781555815585.CH18,,1,2007,yes,"The paper is a general review of smallpox vaccination and its risks for health care providers, not exclusively limited to healthy non-pregnant adults.",yes,The study specifically reviews adverse events and complications (safety outcomes) associated with smallpox vaccination.,no,The text describes itself as a review chapter or summary of contemporary knowledge regarding adverse events. It is not clearly a primary research study or a systematic evidence synthesis.,yes,The paper discusses outcomes in human vaccinees.,maybe,"The abstract/chapter excerpt does not explicitly mention inclusion of pregnant women, neonates, or children; it focuses on vaccinees generally and patients with immunodeficiencies.",maybe,"There is no specific mention of the timing of vaccination relative to pregnancy, childhood, or adolescence in the provided text.",yes,The study involves historical smallpox vaccines and tissue culture vaccines.,Exclude,"This paper is a review chapter focusing on the benefits and risks of smallpox vaccination. It fails the study design criterion as it is a narrative review/chapter rather than primary research or a systematic review, and it does not specifically describe data for the target populations of interest (pregnant women or children) in the available text.",Study Design,Study Design,0.1,"  - ""This chapter deals with the smallpox vaccine, its benefits, its risks, and the current state of its use.""",,,,"  - ""This chapter deals with the smallpox vaccine, its benefits, its risks, and the current state of its use.""",,"  - ""This chapter deals with the smallpox vaccine, its benefits, its risks, and the current state of its use.""
  - ""many patients with hypogammaglobulinemia but with intact T-cell function underwent smallpox vaccination without incidents,""",,"  - ""This chapter deals with the smallpox vaccine, its benefits, its risks, and the current state of its use.""
  - ""many patients with hypogammaglobulinemia but with intact T-cell function underwent smallpox vaccination without incidents,""",,,,"  - ""This chapter deals with the smallpox vaccine, its benefits, its risks, and the current state of its use.""",
Age-patterns of severity of clade I mpox in historically endemic countries,"Background The last two years have seen mpox emerge as a notable pandemic threat. The global 2022-23 outbreak of clade II mpox caused over 93,000 cases worldwide, while the 2023-24 epidemic of the more severe clade I virus in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC) has caused over 14,000 cases and over 650 deaths thus far. Of particular concern is that both case incidence and mortality in the DRC outbreak are concentrated in children. However, quantification of age variation in severity for clade I infections has been lacking to date. Methods Using Bayesian binomial regression models, we analysed data from systematically reviewed clade I outbreaks to estimate case fatality ratios (CFR) by age, smallpox vaccination status and over time. We compared model predictive performance using leave-one-out cross-validation and compared our findings to the ongoing DRC outbreak. Findings The CFR had a near-reciprocal relationship with age, declining from 9.7% (95% credible interval: 6.9%-13.0%) among 5-year-olds to 1.2% (95%CrI: 0.3%-3.7%) by age 30. Accounting for vaccination status in addition to age did not improve model fit, but posterior parameter estimates suggest substantial vaccine-protection against death. Reanalysis incorporating cases from the ongoing DRC outbreak suggested less steep declines in severity with age and a protective effect of vaccination against death of 64% (95%CrI: -3.4%-95.6%) and reduction in severity over time. Interpretation We provide estimates of the mpox clade I CFR in historically endemic settings by vaccine status, and find the highest risk of death in the youngest children. Funding: Wellcome, NIHR, MRC, Community Jameel","L. Whittles, P. Mbala-Kingebeni, N. Ferguson",10.1101/2024.04.23.24306209,https://doi.org/10.1101/2024.04.23.24306209,medRxiv,6,2024,yes,"The study explicitly focuses on children, noting that both incidence and mortality are concentrated in this group.",yes,The study reports case fatality ratios (CFR) and the protective effect of vaccination against death.,yes,The study is an evidence synthesis (meta-analysis/review) that analysed data from systematically reviewed clade I outbreaks.,yes,The study uses clinical data from human outbreaks in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC) and elsewhere.,yes,"The study includes and focuses on children, providing specific estimates for 5-year-olds.",yes,"The study evaluates smallpox vaccination status in a population involving children and adolescents. For children to have a smallpox vaccination status in these outbreaks, they would have been vaccinated during childhood or as part of response efforts.",yes,The study specifically examines 'smallpox vaccination status' as a predictor of protection.,Include,"This study is an evidence synthesis analyzing age-specific severity and the effectiveness of smallpox vaccination in Clade I mpox outbreaks. It specifically highlights and estimates mortality rates in children and reports on the protective effect of vaccination against death, meeting all inclusion criteria.",,,4.9,"  - ""both case incidence and mortality in the DRC outbreak are concentrated in children.""
