Safety of Covid-19 vaccination during pregnancy – The Finnish TIS experience,"Streptococcus pneumoniae infections have periodically caused significant morbidity and outbreaks among military personnel, especially trainees. This study evaluated the effectiveness of the 23-valent polysaccharide pneumococcal vaccine (PPV23) in reducing pneumonia in healthy military trainees.From 2000–2003, 152 723 military trainees from 5 US training camps were enrolled in a double-blind, placebo-controlled trial of PPV23. Participants were closely monitored during basic training for radiographically confirmed pneumonia etiology and loss-of-training days. Participants were also followed using electronic medical encounter data until 1 June 2007 for three additional outcomes: any-cause pneumonia, any acute respiratory disease, and meningitis.Comparison of demographic data by study arm suggested the randomization procedures were sound. During basic training, 371 study participants developed radiographically confirmed pneumonia. None had evidence of S. pneumoniae infection, but other etiologies included adenovirus (38%), Chlamydophila pneumoniae (9%), and Mycoplasma pneumoniae (8%). During the follow-up period, many study participants, in both the vaccine and placebo groups, had clinical encounters for the medical outcomes of interest. However, Cox's proportional hazard modeling revealed no evidence of a protective vaccine effect during recruit training (radiographically confirmed pneumonia) or up to 6.7 years after enrollment (any-cause pneumonia, any acute respiratory disease, or meningitis).Data from this large, double-blind, placebo controlled trial do not support routine use of PPV23 among healthy new military trainees. This clinical trial was registered at clinicaltrials.gov (registration number NCT02079701, http://www.clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT02079701?term=NCT02079701&rank=1).","Melinda Gonzalez, Maria Ellfolk, Julia Reponen, E. Huvinen, H. Malm",10.1016/j.ntt.2023.107237,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ntt.2023.107237,Neurotoxicology and Teratology,0,2023,no,"The study was conducted exclusively on healthy military trainees, which are generally healthy non-pregnant adults.",no,"The study reports on pneumonia, respiratory disease, and meningitis outcomes, which are not 관련 to mpox infection, hospitalization, or death.",yes,"The study is a double-blind, placebo-controlled trial, which is a primary research design.",yes,The study involves human participants (military trainees).,no,"The study focuses on military trainees, not pregnant women, neonates, children, or adolescents.",no,"Participants received the vaccine during military training (adulthood), not during pregnancy, the neonatal period, childhood, or adolescence.",no,"The study evaluates the 23-valent polysaccharide pneumococcal vaccine (PPV23), not vaccinia-based mpox or smallpox vaccines.",Exclude,"This study evaluates the pneumococcal vaccine (PPV23) in healthy military trainees, rather than mpox or smallpox vaccines. It fails several required criteria, most notably the target population and vaccine type requirements.","Population Focus, Relevant Outcomes, Target Population, Vaccination Timing, Vaccine Type",Vaccine Type,0.1,"  - ""healthy military trainees.From""",,"  - ""during recruit training (radiographically confirmed pneumonia) or up to 6.7 years after enrollment""",,"  - ""152 723 military trainees from 5 US training camps were enrolled in a double-blind, placebo-controlled trial""",,"  - ""152 723 military trainees from 5 US training camps were enrolled in a double-blind, placebo-controlled trial""",,"  - ""healthy military trainees.From""",,"  - ""healthy military trainees.From""
